9303,voice-commands-classification-2025,"# Overview

As part of this competition, participants are invited to develop a robust classifier of voice commands. It is assumed that the developed classifier will be resistant to malicious distortions of the input data.

# Evaluation.
## Goal.
The goal of this competition is to classify speech commands correctly. As a result, each of the samples must be associated with a command.

## Evaluation Metric.
$$Accuracy = \frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}$$

## Submission File.
The csv file must contain a header and be in the following format:
```
image_id, label 
1, right
2, happy
3, no
4, happy
```","# Dataset Description

File **train.zip** contains samples represented as numpy arrays saved in npy format. In addition, the archive contains a file **metadata.csv**, which holds information about each sample. The file name stores its identifier. For example, 12.npy means that the unique identifier is 12.

File **test.zip** contains samples for testing purposes; the archive includes a file **metadata.csv** that holds information about each sample.

## Files

*   **train** - training data.
*   **adv_test** - testing data.

## Columns

*   `id` - unique sample identifier.
*   `label` - class to which the sample belongs.",False,True
9304,hkustgz-aiaa-5032-hw-2-spring-2025,"# Overview

This is the Kaggle page for homework2, enjoy the competition!
----------------------
# Description

Homework 2 for AIAA-5032 2025 Spring. Please refer to the Github page for instructions.
[https://github.com/hkust-aiaa5032/homework/tree/main/hw2](https://github.com/hkust-aiaa5032/homework/tree/main/hw2)
In this homework we will perform a video classification task using CNN. 

Rules: You can use any or all of the 30 images to train and test your model. **You are allowed to use pretrained models and weights (only ImageNet-1K pertaining is allowed).** See this [post](https://hkust-gz.instructure.com/courses/1795/discussion_topics/12025).
----------------------
# Evaluation

We will use accuracy as the evaluation metric. The submission file should contain the classification labels (0-9) for each video.","# Dataset Description

### Files

- trainval.csv - the training and validation set label
- test_for_student.csv - the test set with label column(all set 0), it's also a sample submission file in the correct format
- test_for_student.label - the test set without label column, used for CNN_Classifier_test2csv.py
- video_frames_30fpv_320p.tgz - the processed video frames. Under each subfolder, there are 30 images from one video, we use the middle image for classification

### Columns
id - video id
Category - video category

### Note
before downloading the data, you need to set up your kaggle API.
ref: [https://adityashrm21.github.io/Setting-Up-Kaggle/](https://adityashrm21.github.io/Setting-Up-Kaggle/)",False,True
9305,the-4th-repss-t1,"# Overview

**Notice**: 
Please check the Workshop Paper submission details [here](https://repss-w.github.io/4th-repss/workshop.html).

**Final Leaderboard**
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F25544624%2F5ff14f5b40ee5092727bb0006e82565f%2Ffinal_leaderboard.png?generation=1748018681941612&alt=media)

The 4th Vision-based Remote Physiological Signal Sensing (RePSS) workshop will be held in conjunction with the [International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI 2025)](https://2025.ijcai.org/) on August 28th, 2025.

**VIPL-HR Test Set Data Update**: **New Uncropped Version Available**. Several participants have reported difficulties in reconstructing videos due to inconsistent frame sizes and the absence of some frames in the previously distributed version. To address these concerns and facilitate smoother video reconstruction, we have now made available **an uncropped version** of the VIPL-HR test set. This updated dataset can be accessed via the same OneDrive link previously provided. To reconstruct, please set frame rates to **25fps**. However, that some frames are **still missing** in this original data, which may result in certain clips being slightly shorter than 10 seconds. The **average clip length** is 9.74s at 25fps. Image size: RGB images: 960¡Á540.Infrared images: 640¡Á480. **To test your results on VIPL-HR test set, you can choose either previously shared face-centered cropped version or newly uploaded un-cropped frames.**

**Submission Guidelines**: Please ensure your submission team name exactly matches the name provided on your team registration form. Each team (with the registered team name) can submit up to?five times per day.





**Overview**: 
RePSS (Remote Physiological Signal Sensing Challenge) is a platform that unites multidisciplinary researchers to advance remote photoplethysmography (rPPG), a technology that extracts physiological signals¡ªsuch as heart rate (HR), heart rate variability (HRV), breathing rate (BR), and others¡ªfrom facial videos. These signals are vital for applications in remote healthcare, gaming, and educational research. Since its inception in 2020, RePSS has been held in conjunction with leading conferences, including CVPR2020, ICCV2021, and IJCAI2024, featuring workshops, challenges, and invited talks. The event aims to foster fair competition, resource sharing, and technological progress in the field of remote physiological sensing.

The 4th RePSS Challenge and Workshop will be held in conjunction with the International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI 2025) in August 2025. The 4th RePSS Challenge, hosted alongside IJCAI 2025, centers on ""RGB-NIR Fusion for Robust rPPG Measurement."" It invites participants to develop innovative data fusion techniques that integrate RGB and Near-Infrared (NIR) facial videos to improve the accuracy and robustness of rPPG measurements, particularly in challenging scenarios such as varying lighting conditions or diverse skin tones. The motivation for this challenge stems from the limitations of traditional RGB-based rPPG methods, which are often susceptible to environmental noise like lighting variations. To address this limitation and explore the performance differences between single-modal and fusion-based approaches, the challenge incorporates NIR videos, which offer greater resilience to such variations while retaining physiological signal information. This challenge aims to advance the frontiers of multimodal fusion techniques, paving the way for more reliable remote physiological sensing in real-world applications.


----------------------
# Description

The 4th RePSS focuses on multimodal fusion of RGB and NIR videos for robust heart rate (HR) measurement.

Competitors will use the VIPL-HR dataset as the training set, featuring diverse RGB and NIR facial videos. The VIPL-HR and OBF datasets will serve as the test set to assess the model¡¯s robustness in HR estimation across different data. The aim is to develop innovative fusion strategies to improve accuracy and reliability.
----------------------
# Schedule

Important Dates (may adjust later)
* March 24: Challenge website online
* April 1: Release training and resource data
* **April 30: Team registration deadline**
* May 3: Testing data release
* May 17: Final test submission deadline
* May 24: Challenge results release
* May 31: Paper submission deadline
* June 6: Notification to authors
* August 28 : RePSS workshop in Guangzhou
----------------------
# Team registration

**Note**:You can participate the challenge as a team of one to ten members. Every team (including single member or multi-members) must fill in a team registration form ([onedrive](https://1drv.ms/f/c/2d8763f435503032/EqPjptxpOmpGt9KV6b9IE80BHXVwXMK9CX2tGZa6wu_pYA?e=Y2mNPa)), and send the form together with data LAs to us (see the Data tab) before the registration DDL. The teams are fixed by registration and adding new members are not allowed after the registration DDL.
**Team Name**: the team name should be a fixed Kaggle account ID, which will be used for challenge result submission of the team. Ensure the name follows Kaggle's naming rules (such as appropriate length, no special characters). Note that only results submitted from registered team name will be recognized as valid for ranking.
![](https://repss-w.github.io/4th-repss/team_information400.png)



----------------------
# Evaluation

We use root **mean squared errors (RMSE)** as the evaluation metrics. the RMSE between ground truth heart rates y and submitted heart rates y' is calculated.
![](https://repss-w.github.io/4th-repss/rmse.png)
----------------------
# Instruction about test submission

Your submission should be a .csv file with the following columns: **ids, HR_RGB, HR_NIR, and HR_FUSION**. The ids column represents the video file name, HR_RGB is the predicted heart rate using RGB data, HR_NIR is the predicted heart rate using near-infrared (NIR) data, and HR_FUSION is the predicted heart rate based on a fusion of both RGB and NIR data. Note that single-modal results are collected for discussion purpose, and **only the fusion results HR_FUSION** will be used for the final ranking.The figure below illustrates the required submission format. You can download the submission example file submission_example.csv via this [OneDrive link](https://1drv.ms/f/c/2d8763f435503032/EqPjptxpOmpGt9KV6b9IE80BHXVwXMK9CX2tGZa6wu_pYA?e=1lOkVB). 
Each team (with the registered team name) can submit up to **five times per day**, from the 'testing data release' date to the 'test submission deadline' date.
![](https://repss-w.github.io/4th-repss/submission_example_new.png)","# Dataset Description

1. **Training data**
We use the **VIPL-HR** dataset as the training data. Paired RGB and NIR videos collected from 107 subjects are segmented into 10-second clips, which will be shared with participants together with the corresponding ground truth PPG signals measured with an oximeter. 
The dataset comprises video data from four distinct modalities, collected using the following devices and labeled as (a), (b), (c), and (d):
Modality (a): Captured by a Logitech C310 camera, providing standard visible-light video data;
Modality (b): Recorded by a RealSense F200 color camera, producing high-quality color video data;
Modality (c): Obtained from a RealSense F200 near-infrared (NIR) camera, capturing video data in the near-infrared spectrum;
Modality (d): Collected by the frontal camera of a HUAWEI P9, representing video data from a mobile device.
It is particularly noteworthy that modalities (b) and (c) constitute paired data.The test set of the VIPL-HR dataset includes data from **modalities (b) and (c) exclusively**.
![](https://repss-w.github.io/4th-repss/vipl_introduction_1.png)

2. **Test data**
 We use **VIPL-HR** and **OBF** datasets as the test set. Paired RGB-NIR videos of 100 subjects (reserved, different from the training set) from VIPL-HR and 100 subjects from OBF dataset (one pair of sample images are shown below) are segmented into 10-second clips, which will be shared as the testing data. VIPL-HR contains variant lighting conditions, and OBF contains subjects of various skin tones. Due to protocol restrictions, the OBF videos provided to participants have been anonymized by applying mosaics to sensitive areas, safeguarding the privacy of individuals within the dataset. To support the development of robust algorithms despite this anonymization, we will supply participants with facial landmarks corresponding to the video frames. These landmarks enable participants to precisely locate and analyze facial features, ensuring effective algorithm training and evaluation while maintaining compliance with privacy requirements.
![](https://repss-w.github.io/4th-repss/obf1_200.png)![](https://repss-w.github.io/4th-repss/obf2_200.png)



3. **Data Sharing Agreement**
The validation data and test data are from the organizers¡¯ teams, i.e., the VIPL-HR dataset and the OBF dataset. **Two license agreements (LA)** and a **team registration** form **must** be filled before participants can get access to any data. The documents can be downloaded from [Onedrive](https://1drv.ms/f/c/2d8763f435503032/EqPjptxpOmpGt9KV6b9IE80BHXVwXMK9CX2tGZa6wu_pYA?e=ELwIde). Please follow the following instructions carefully for preparing the documents:
1). The LA must be signed by a person with an email that affiliated with an institution or company (e.g., xx.xx@mit.edu, xx.xx@microsoft.com ), which means that the person has a fixed position in the institution or company (e.g., a professor from a university, or an employee from a company). Personal emails (e.g., xxx@gmail.com, xxx@qq.com ) are NOT valid. For students please ask your supervisor to sign the LA.
2). One signer can be associated with multiple registered competition IDs, i.e., one professor can sign the LA and multiple students from his/her group can register to the competition.
3). The signer must read through the LA carefully, and only sign the document when he/she fully understand and agree to all the items listed in the LA.
4). Participants should send ONE email with all the signed LA and the team registration form in PDF format, named as IJCAI 2024 data agreement_track1_Yourname.pdf to Ms. Huiyu Yang (Huiyu.Yang@oulu.fi), Mr. Yunchi Zhang (zhangyunchi19@mails.ucas.ac.cn),Dr. Xiaobai Li(xiaobai.li@zju.edu.cn), and Dr. Hu Han (hanhu@ict.ac.cn). We will send you the download link after receiving the signed LA and the team registration form.
5). The signer is fully responsible (for all users whose IDs are associated with him/her) to make sure that all associated ID users are fully aware of the LA contents, and the data is accessed and used in the proper way according to LA. Data users have no right to distribute the data in any form.
6). The test data is shared only for this competition usage but not for any other usage. **All test data must be deleted after the competition by 06.06.2025. The participants should contact the organizers again if planning to use any part of the test data for other research purpose**. 

Participants are **required to cite** the following articles in their workshop & challenge papers::
[1] Xuesong Niu, Shiguang Shan*, Hu Han, and Xilin Chen, ""RhythmNet: End-to-end Heart Rate Estimation from Face via Spatial-temporal Representation,"" IEEE Transactions on Image Processing (T-IP), vol. 29, pp. 2409-2423, 2020.?
[2] X. Li et al., ¡°The OBF database: A large face video database for remote physiological signal measurement and atrial fibrillation detection,¡± in Proc. IEEE 13th Int. Conf. Autom. Face Gesture Recognit., 2018, pp. 242¨C249.",False,True
