9318,geology-forecast-challenge-open,"# Overview

## Geological layer-depth sequences

Subsurface geology is inherently uncertain due to the limited availability of direct measurements and the complexity of geological processes over time. Data from wells, seismic surveys, and logging tools provide partial and sometimes ambiguous information, leaving gaps in our understanding of the subsurface. 
Predicting geological scenarios enables us to integrate available data and reduce uncertainty. Thus, predictive methods enable informed decision-making in applications like drilling, resource extraction, and risk mitigation, ensuring more efficient and safer operations.

Most geological systems are composed of thin layers with different properties deposited on top of each other, forming a flat layer cake of constant thickness in the simplest case. As these layers are buried deeper into subsurface, different physical processes might bend the layers or break them, creating geological faults. 


**Your task is to create a data-driven model that predicts likely 1D layer-depth sequences based on formations where lateral wells are typically drilled.** The model should generate the geological deformations based on the **geology in the well up to the current drilling location** (input).


Figure: 1. Geology with parallel horizontal layers.
![Geology with parallel horizontal layers](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F832185%2Fb4d93f531a66de875bce3e526d2c3c2c%2Fimage%20(6).png?generation=1736785314112560&alt=media)

Figure 2. Actual 1D geology with faults and dips.
![Actual 1D geology with faults and dips](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F832185%2F44fc83c8159022d4062502205e3a4248%2Fimage%20(8).png?generation=1736785363139707&alt=media)


If, at a local scale, the assumption of the constant thickness layer cake holds, geology can be defined by delta_z(X), the deformation at each location. 
The data in this competition is delta_z(x) along the lateral (horizontal) wells, creating 1D layer-depth sequences.



Such reconstruction would help with predictions for **geosteering** - the real-time process of directional changes of a well while drilling. The predicted geological realizations can be compared to acquired data to reduce uncertainty, further informing decision-making. One of the possibilities is using this model instead of a proposal distribution in a method similar to a particle filter [Muhammad et al. 2024](https://doi.org/10.2118/218444-MS).

## Competition Financial Sponsor
This competition is supported by [ROGII Inc](https://www.rogii.com/). **ROGII** provides software solutions for geology, drilling, and completions teams to use on a customized cloud-based platform.

We acknowledge ROGII for providing the field data and the monetary prizes.

----------------------
# Description

## Task

Task is to learn to predict realizations of geological layer depth sequence. 

We ask to predict **10 realizations** (with **300 positions** each) with best possible accuracy. 
**10 realizations** are required to account for uncertainty of predictions. We encourage submissions to have capability of changing the number of realizations to any number.
Diverse predictions would likely increase the score.

Note: if your model uses randomness to predict uncertainty, please **fix the seed** for reproducibility.  

## Columns
* Position number (in column names) is ""**X**"" (vertical section) position in feet relative to the current location of the drill bit.
* Values in cells are **Z**  (oriented up, in feet) coordinates of a layer boundary near a drilled well 

### Column **geology_id**
Hash-based id of a specific row - unique ID string. 

### Columns **-299**..**0**
Inferred (known) geology **Z** (oriented up) coordinate of a layer up to the current drilling location along **X** (vertical section).

The data is normalized so that **Z(0) = 0**. 

Note: not all columns **-299**..**0** contain a value. However, we guarantee that the 
values for columns **-49**..**0** are present with a value in all the rows. 
The values will not have gaps starting from the column **-k** (first with data) until **0**. 

### Columns **1**..**300**
Geology to be predicted (realization 0 out of 10, zero-indexed). 

In the `train.csv` these columns contains the true geology of layer segments.

### Columns **r_1_pos_1**..**r_1_pos_300** ... **r_9_pos_1**..**r_9_pos_300**
Geology to be predicted (realizations 1 to 9 out of 10, zero-indexed). 

Note: The order of the realizations **0 to 10** does not change the score. 
This includes realization 0 described above.  

In the `train.csv` these columns contains the true geology of layer segments same as Columns **1**..**300**. 



----------------------
# Evaluation


Submissions are evaluated using negative log likelihood metric.

Let:

- *x* be horizontal location along the well which in the task is replaced by its index 1..300,
- **b^{T}(x)** be the true geological boundary,
- *b_{i}(x)* be a predicted geology realization number *i*,
- **D_T^{-1}(x)**  is an approximation of a diagonal of an idealized covariance matrix.

We can compute a simple misfit vector between  a single prediction and the truth as:

$$
e_{i}(x) = b^{T}(x) - b_{i}(x)
$$

Assuming that each geological predicion defines a center line of a Gausian kernel with varience **D_{T}**
we can define ""Gaussian"" or exponential misfit **E_{i,G}** as follows  

$$
  E_{i,G} = exp (e_{i}(x) \cdot D_T^{-1}(x) e_{i}(x)),  
$$
where $\cdot$ is the inner product, and **E_{i,G}** are 10 numbers corresponding to the respective predctions. 

Finally, we can write the weighted negative log likelihood loss formula used for the evaluation:

$$
  loss = - log (\sum_{i=0..9} p_i * E_{i,G}),
$$
where *p_i* is the probability of each kernel; 
the realizations are assumed equiprobable: *p_i = 1/10*.

The system returns the mean of the *loss* over the test data.

### Idealized covariance matrix

**D_T^{-1}(x)** is derived by curve-fitting based on the training and test data.
Each part can be represented as:

$$
D_T^{-1}(x) = exp \left( \log(x) a_k + b_k] \right),
$$
where **a_k** (`log_slopes`) and **b_k** (`log_offsets`) are imperical constants depending on the growth mode **k**:
- **k=1** Close prediction with exponential growth of errors, *x = 1..60*
- **k=2** Region with stable errors *x = 61..244*
- **k=3** Region with further growth of errors *x = 245..300*

The constants for *a* and *b* are provided by these arrays:

```python
log_slopes = [1.0406028049510443, 0.0, 7.835345062351012]
log_offsets = [-6.430669850650689, -2.1617411566043896, -45.24876794412965]
```

### References 

For description of the NLL loss see 
1. [NLL concept, Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negative_log_predictive_density)
2. [Gaussian function (error), Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaussian_function)
3. [Discussion of applying this loss to geological boundaries in Alyaev and Elsheikh 2022](https://doi.org/10.1029/2021EA002186) 
4. [The current evaluation-metric code](https://www.kaggle.com/code/alin256/nll-for-1d-geology-metric-v5)

### Output format
#### Columns **1**..**300**
Geology to be predicted (realization 0 out of 10, zero-indexed). 

#### Columns **r_1_pos_1**..**r_1_pos_300** ... **r_9_pos_1**..**r_9_pos_300**
Geology to be predicted (realizations 1 to 9 out of 10, zero-indexed). 

Note: The order of the realizations **0 to 10** does not change the score. 
This includes realization 0 described above.  
----------------------
# Prizes

The total monetary pot of 1000$ is provided by ROGII. NORCE will **not** deliver the monetary prizes. Top-performing competitors will be invited to write an article summarizing the findings with NORCE co-authors. 

The organisers will recommend the top 3 participating teams for the prizes from ROGII Inc., who has allocated a fund of 1000$. The prizes will be delivered as digital gift cards from ROGII Inc. or equivalent upon agreement.

* 1st place: $500 Gift Card or equivalent
* 2nd place: $270 Gift Card or equivalent
* 3rd place: $230 Gift Card or equivalent",,False,True
9319,bpl-ai4good-wheat-price-forecasting,"# Overview

Welcome to the time series forecasting competition from the AI for Good course. For more context and materials from the course, check out the GitHub repo [here](https://github.com/Better-Planet-Laboratory/ai4good).

In this competition, we give you monthly grain commodity prices such as wheat, maize, rice, and barley from markets across the world going as far back as the 1990s. The wheat prices come from the [World Food Program (WFP)](https://www.wfp.org/market-analysis), which tracks food prices in over 1500 markets, and the other grain prices come from the [International Grains Council (IGC)](https://www.igc.int/en/markets/marketinfo-prices.aspx). 

In February of 2022, Russia invaded Ukraine in a major escalation of the Russio-Ukrainian war ([Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine)). The invasion, the largest and deadliest conflict in Europe since World War II, has caused hundreds of thousands of military casualties and tens of thousands of Ukrainian civilian casualties. The ripples of the conflict have reverberated around the world, including major disruptions to global grain markets, due to among other things, Ukraine being the 5th largest wheat exporter in the world.

For this competition, imagine you are an analyst for the WFP in March 2022, right after the start of the invasion, tasked with forecasting how this conflict will impact grain prices in markets around the world for the next 3 months.

**Goal:** Use the historical data on grain prices in markets around the world up through March 2022 to forecast the price of wheat flour in USD in each market for the months of April, May, and June of 2022.
----------------------
# Description

Welcome to the time series forecasting competition from the AI for Good course. For more context and materials from the course, check out the GitHub repo here.

In this competition, we give you monthly grain commodity prices such as wheat, maize, rice, and barley from markets across the world going as far back as the 1990s. The wheat prices come from the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) of the United Nations, and the other grain prices come from the International Grains Council (IGC).

In February of 2022, Russian invaded the Ukraine in a major escalation of the Russio-Ukrainian war (Wikipedia). The invasion, the largest and deadliest conflict in Europe since World War II, has caused hundreds of thousands of military casualties and tens of thousands of Ukrainian civilian casualties. The ripples of the conflict reverberated around the world, including major disruptions to global grain markets.

For this competition, imagine you are an analyst for the FAO in March 2022, right after the start of the invasion, tasked with forecasting how this conflict will impact grain prices in markets around the world for the next 3 months.

Goal: Use the historical data on grain prices in markets around the world up through March 2022 to forecast the price of wheat in USD in each market for the months of April, May, and June of 2022.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using [Root Mean Square Error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_mean_square_deviation) for each of the predictions per market per month. 

## Submission File
For each market and month/year, you must predict a price of 1kg of `Wheat flour` in USD, as found in the training data as `price_usd` where `commodity` is equal to `Wheat flour`. 

Your file should have the following headers and format:

For each `market_year_month` in the test set, you must predict a `price_usd` value. The file should contain a header and have the following format. Be sure your submission has 951 rows.

    market_year_month, price_usd
    Badakhshan_2022_4, 0.0123
    Badakhshan_2022_5, 0.4567
    Badakhshan_2022_6, 0.8910
    Badghis_2022_4, 0.1112
    Badghis_2022_5, 0.1314
    Badghis_2022_6, 0.1516
    Bamyan_2022_4, 0.1718
    Bamyan_2022_5, 0.1920
    Bamyan_2022_6, 0.2122
    etc.",,False,True
9320,admissions-department-problem,"# Overview

During the COVID-19 pandemic, the University suspended its SAT requirement for admissions. While this policy change aimed to improve accessibility, the admissions committee still prefers to evaluate candidates using familiar metrics. To address this, the goal of this competition is to predict two key scores for each applicant: 

- **Verbal Intelligence Score** ¡ª reflecting language and communication abilities
- **Mathematical Intelligence Score** ¡ª reflecting quantitative reasoning skills

Participants will build models that predict these scores using student grades in various subjects:

- Algebra
- Geometry
- Precalculus
- Biology
- Chemistry
- Physics
- Environmental Science
- English
- World History
- US History
- Geography

Competitors are encouraged to explore innovative approaches that effectively capture both subject-specific and cross-disciplinary patterns to generate accurate predictions.
----------------------
# Description

The admissions department is not only interested in point predictions for the verbal and mathematical intelligence scores but also requires an interval estimate for each score. This additional requirement ensures that the predicted range reflects the uncertainty in the model's predictions.

Once the University obtains the actual scores, participants will be evaluated based on two criteria:  
1. **Accuracy** ¡ª How close the predicted point estimates are to the true scores.  
2. **Coverage** ¡ª Whether the true score falls within the predicted interval.

To assess both aspects, the competition will use the **Mean Winkler Interval Score**. This scoring metric is defined as follows:

Given a prediction interval \\( [L\_i, U\_i] \\) for the true score \\( y\_i \\), with a target coverage probability of 95% (i.e., \\( 1 - \alpha = 0.95 \\)), the Winkler score for that observation is:

$$
W\_i = (U\_i - L\_i) + \frac{2}{\alpha} \max(0, L\_i - y\_i) + \frac{2}{\alpha} \max(0, y\_i - U\_i)
$$

The Mean Winkler Interval Score is the average of these scores across all observations:

$$
\text{Mean Winkler Interval Score} = \frac{1}{n} \sum\_{i=1}^n W\_i
$$

A lower score indicates better performance, rewarding both narrow intervals and correct coverage of the true values.

### Standardization of Scores
The predicted verbal and mathematical intelligence scores must be standardized on a scale with:  
- Mean = 100  
- Standard deviation = 10  
----------------------
# Evaluation

Competitors will be graded based on their performance on the leaderboard. Specifically, a participant's grade will be calculated as follows:

\\[
\text{Grade} = \frac{\text{Best Score}}{\text{Your Score}}
\\]

Where:  
- **Best Score** refers to the lowest Mean Winkler Interval Score achieved by any participant.  
- **Your Score** refers to the participant's own Mean Winkler Interval Score.  

### Submission File Format
Your submission file must follow this structure:  
- The first rows should contain the intervals for **Verbal Intelligence** scores.  
- The subsequent rows should contain the intervals for **Mathematical Intelligence** scores.  
- Each row should have three columns, named exactly as follows:
  - **First Column:** Column Id. `colID`.  
  - **Second Column:** Lower bound of the interval . `score_lower`.
  - **Third Column:** Upper bound of the interval. `score_upper`.  

For example, if there are only 2 students, your submission file should be:

    colID, score_lower, score_upper
    1, 95, 103
    2, 92, 101
    3, 99, 107
    4, 104, 112

Where the first 2 rows are the Verbal Intelligence Interval Scores of the 2 students and the next 2 rows are the Mathematical Intelligence Interval Scores.",,False,True
9321,regression-models-and-polynomial-features,"# Overview

**Welcome to our first Bootcamp Competition**

**Your Goal:** The goal of this competition is to predict the price of used cars based on various attributes.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
Submissions are scored on the root mean squared error. RMSE is defined as:

$$
\textrm{RMSE} =  \left( \frac{1}{N} \sum\_{i=1}^{N} (y\_i - \widehat{y}\_i)^2 \right)^{\frac{1}{2}}
$$

where \\( \widehat{y}\_i \\) is the predicted value and \\( y_i \\) is the original value for each instance \\(i\\).

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the `price` of the car. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,price
    188533,43878.016
    188534,43878.016
    188535,43878.016
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - March 12, 2025
* **Entry Deadline** - March 21, 2025
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  March 21, 2025

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Used Car Price Prediction Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/taeefnajib/used-car-price-prediction-dataset). Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `price` is the continuous target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the value of `price` for each row
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9322,2025-spring-stat-3612-group-project,"# Overview

In this group project, your task is to develop statistical machine learning models for hospital readmission prediction using only EHR or multimodal data. You have the freedom to choose feature engineering techniques and machine learning algorithms without any restrictions. It¡¯s important to note that additional training data is not permitted for this project.
----------------------
# Description


Reducing avoidable hospital readmissions is a primary goal for healthcare systems worldwide. In the United States, readmission rates serve as critical indicators of healthcare quality. Unplanned readmissions within a short timeframe after discharge, typically occurring within 30 days, impose substantial burdens on both patients and healthcare systems. Machine learning, with its capacity to learn from extensive datasets encompassing diverse modalities, including patient clinical and imaging data, has demonstrated remarkable progress in various fields. This project aims to leverage machine learning techniques to predict 30-day hospital readmissions, contributing to improved patient care and healthcare system efficiency.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored using AUROC score.","# Dataset Description

In this project, we employ three modalities of patient data for hospital readmission prediction:

1. Electronic Health Record Data (Tabular Data)
We utilize Electronic Health Record (EHR) data from MIMIC-IV v1.0 [1], which encompasses key patient
information:
? demographics (age, gender, and ethnicity)
? comorbidities recorded as ICD-10 codes
? laboratory test results
? medications administered during hospitalizations
2. Chest Radiographs (Imaging Data)
Corresponding radiographs can be obtained from MIMIC-CXR-JPG v2.0 [2].
3. Clinical notes (Text Data)
The deidentified free-text clinical notes of each patient can be accessed from MIMIC-IV-Note [1]
Our dataset consists of 13,763 hospital admissions, encompassing 82,465 chest radiographs from 11,041 unique patients. Among these, 2,379 admissions resulted in readmission within 30 days of discharge, including cases where patients passed away during their hospital stay.

Files
train.csv:

? id - (string): a unique id for each admission. This is the minimum unit of your prediction.
? subject_id - (string): id for each patient.
? hadm_id - (string): hospital admission id.
? dicom_id - (string): an unique id for each image.
? study_id - (string): the id of the study for each image.
? ViewPosition - (string): the view position of the chest x-ray. Find more details in https://ce4rt.com/positioning/radiographic-positioning-of-the-chest/.
image_path - (string): the path of the image.
readmitted_within_30days - (string): (groud truth) whether this admission is readmitted.
valid.csv, test.csv have the same format except that the test.csv doesn't have the ground truth.

ehr_preprocessed_seq_by_day_cat_embedding.pkl

? feat_dict: a dictionary, {""id"": EHR features}.
? feat_cols: the names of these 171 features.
? cat_idxs: the index of the categorical features.
? cat_dims: the dimension of each categorical feature.
? demo_cols: the names of demographic features.
? icd_cols: the names of ICD features.
? lab_cols: the names of lab test features.
? med_cols: the names of medications administered features.",False,True
