9325,team-bike,"# Description

Los sistemas de bicicletas compartidas pueden contribuir significativamente a la movilidad urbana sostenible en las grandes ciudades, pero a pesar de este potencial, su planificaci¨®n y operaci¨®n no est¨¢n exentas de dificultades. Uno de sus principales problemas operativos es la distribuci¨®n desequilibrada de bicicletas en la zona de servicio, lo que genera ¨¢reas con escasez de bicicletas y otras con acumulaci¨®n excesiva y, para garantizar un nivel de servicio aceptable, el operador debe realizar movimientos de redistribuci¨®n, los cuales son costosos. Por esa raz¨®n, se han desarrollado soluciones de optimizaci¨®n para la redistribuci¨®n de bicicletas basadas en la estimaci¨®n del n¨²mero esperado de solicitudes y devoluciones en cada ubicaci¨®n, sin embargo, los errores en esta predicci¨®n se traducen directamente en soluciones de redistribuci¨®n sub¨®ptimas, por lo cual, el desarrollo de metodolog¨ªas capaces de prever con precisi¨®n el uso de las bicicletas compartidas es un tema de gran importancia.

Por otra parte, los constantes desajustes y da?os que sufren las bicicletas disminuyen considerablemente su disponibilidad en el momento que son requeridas por los usuarios, lo que provoca su frustraci¨®n cuando no pueden recoger una bicicleta en el punto de inicio de su viaje debido a estaciones vac¨ªas o con veh¨ªculos no operacionales. Este aspecto del problema requiere de una cuadrilla de operarios que pueda realizar los mantenimientos correctivos de forma eficiente y oportuna para no causar disrupciones en el sistema.

En este sentido, la compa?¨ªa TeamBike desea obtener un modelo que le permita estimar con una alta precisi¨®n los requerimientos de mantenimientos y reparaciones de su flota de bicicletas en la ciudad de Jersey en la primera semana de marzo del 2025.




----------------------
# Evaluation

En la base de datos que se encuentra en https://citibikenyc.com/system-data, usted podr¨¢ encontrar las demandas de viajes para todo el mes de febrero del 2025 en el sistema de bicicletas compartidas de la ciudad de Jersey, para cada uno de los dos tipos de bicicletas (cl¨¢sicas y el¨¦ctricas). Usted deber¨¢ utilizar esos datos y cualquier otra informaci¨®n adicional para pronosticar la demanda de viajes totales para los primeros 7 d¨ªas del mes de marzo del 2025 y el n¨²mero de viajes por franja horaria para el primero de marzo de ese mismo a?o, de tal forma que se minimice el MAPE ponderado, es decir, el promedio ponderado de los dos MAPEs considerando un peso del 30% para las demandas diarias y un 70% para las demandas por franjas horarias

Formato de respuesta:

Para esta primera parte del caso, cada usted deber¨¢ subir todos los pron¨®sticos en una ¨²nica columna. Primero los 7 pron¨®sticos para d¨ªas entre el 1 y el 7 de marzo y, luego, los 24 pron¨®sticos para las franjas horarias del primero de marzo del 2025.","# Dataset Description

En la p¨¢gina https://citibikenyc.com/system-data, se debe buscar el enlace *Download Citi Bike trip history data* y, una vez all¨ª, localizar el archivo correspondiente al reporte de viajes en Jersey City en el mes de febrero del a?o 2025 **JC-202502-citibike-tripdata.csv.zip**. Este archivo contiene informaci¨®n de cada uno de los viajes que iniciaron en una estaci¨®n de la ciudad de Jersey durante el mes de febrero, por lo tanto, utilizando la informaci¨®n de la columna **started\_at** su equipo de trabajo deber¨¢ determinar:

- El n¨²mero de viajes diarios realizado en la ciudad de Jersey para cada d¨ªa del mes de febrero de 2025.
- El n¨²mero de viajes por franja horaria durante este mismo mes

Para reportar sus resultados, descargue el archivo TeamBike.csv y complete all¨ª sus pron¨®sticos. Tenga en cuenta que sus pron¨®sticos deber¨¢n estar en n¨²mero de viajes, es decir, no deber¨¢ usar cifras decimales.

Aseg¨²rese de no da?ar el formato del archivo cvs, los separadores deber¨¢n seguir configurados como comas, no como punto y comas y el archivo deber¨¢ tener las dos columnas con los mismos nombres. En cada participaci¨®n, nombre su archivo con su n¨²mero clase, su n¨²mero de grupo y la versi¨®n, por ejemplo ""*1706Grupo1V01.csv*"", de no seguir estas indicaciones, su participaci¨®n no ser¨¢ v¨¢lida.",False,True
9326,open-ai-x-data-supervised-pillar-spring-2025,"# Overview

# Competition Overview

Predict the average disbursed FFEL Loan per recipient at a university based on university data, as well as university zip and county data. Participants will develop regression models to estimate loan amounts, with the best-performing models minimizing Mean Squared Error (MSE).

----------------------
# Description

The dataset includes information on U.S. colleges and universities, incorporating factors such as school diversity, tuition and housing prices, and county employment data. Contestants will use this data to predict the average FFEL Loan disbursed per student at each university.

### Dataset Details:
- **Dataset Name:** N/A (2009-2010 Award Year FFEL Volume by School + many others)
- **Dataset Author:** U.S. Department of Education + many others
- **Dataset Description:** This dataset provides university-level details such as demographics, tuition, and local economic factors. The goal is to leverage these features to predict the average FFEL Loan disbursed per student.

----------------------
# Evaluation


Submissions will be evaluated using the **Mean Squared Error (MSE)** metric:
$$
MSE = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2
$$
A lower MSE indicates a more accurate model.

# Submission File

Your submission file should be a CSV containing two columns:

| id  | avg_disbursed_loan |
|-----|--------------------|
| 1   | 5000.50           |
| 2   | 6200.75           |
| ... | ...              |

- **id**: The unique identifier for each university.
- **avg_disbursed_loan**: The predicted average disbursed FFEL Loan per recipient.

Ensure your submission follows this format to be correctly evaluated.","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description

This section provides an overview of the dataset used in this competition, including file structure, column definitions, and key information about the features. Understanding this information will help participants preprocess the data and build accurate models.

## Files

- **train.csv** - The training dataset containing university-level data along with the target variable (`avg_disbursed_loan`).
- **test.csv** - The test dataset for which participants must predict `avg_disbursed_loan`.
- **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file illustrating the correct format for predictions.

## Columns

The dataset consists of various features related to U.S. universities, including demographic, financial, and regional economic data. Below are the column descriptions:

- **`num_recipients`** - Number of students who received an FFEL Loan at the university.
- **`school`** - Name of the university.
- **`school_type`** - Type of institution (e.g., Public, Private).
- **`state`** - U.S. state where the university is located.
- **`zip_code`** - University¡¯s zip code.
- **`avg_disbursed_loan`** - The target variable, representing the average FFEL Loan disbursed per recipient at the university.
- **`zip_median_household_income`** - Median household income in the university¡¯s zip code area.
- **`degree_length`** - Typical program length (e.g., 4-Year, 2-Year).
- **`room_and_board_price`** - Estimated cost of room and board at the university.
- **`in_state_tuition`** - Tuition fees for in-state students.
- **`out_of_state_tuition`** - Tuition fees for out-of-state students.
- **`early_career_pay_estimate`** - Estimated salary for graduates early in their careers.
- **`mid_career_pay_estimate`** - Estimated salary for graduates in mid-career.
- **`stem_percent`** - Percentage of students enrolled in STEM programs.
- **`total_enrollment`** - Total number of students enrolled at the university.
- **`american_indian_alaska_native_enrolled`** - Number of enrolled students identifying as American Indian or Alaska Native.
- **`asian_enrolled`** - Number of enrolled students identifying as Asian.
- **`black_enrolled`** - Number of enrolled students identifying as Black.
- **`hispanic_enrolled`** - Number of enrolled students identifying as Hispanic.
- **`native_hawaiian_pacific_islander_enrolled`** - Number of enrolled students identifying as Native Hawaiian or Pacific Islander.
- **`non_resident_foreign_enrolled`** - Number of enrolled students who are non-resident foreign nationals.
- **`total_minority_enrolled`** - Total number of enrolled students identifying as a minority.
- **`two_or_more_races_enrolled`** - Number of enrolled students identifying as two or more races.
- **`unknown_enrolled`** - Number of enrolled students with unknown race/ethnicity.
- **`white_enrolled`** - Number of enrolled students identifying as White.
- **`women_enrolled`** - Number of enrolled female students.
- **`county_name`** - Name of the county where the university is located.
- **`county_labor_force`** - Total labor force in the university¡¯s county.
- **`county_employed`** - Number of employed individuals in the university¡¯s county.
- **`county_unemployed`** - Number of unemployed individuals in the university¡¯s county.

Participants will use these features to build regression models that predict the `avg_disbursed_loan` for each university.",False,True
9327,open-ai-x-data-unsupervised-pillar-spring-2025,"# Overview

# Competition Overview

Welcome to the **Health Data Insights Challenge**! In this competition, contestants will explore a comprehensive dataset on health-related behaviors across the United States. Your task is to uncover meaningful insights by applying **unsupervised learning techniques** such as clustering or dimensionality reduction.  

There is no predefined objective¡ª**you define your own goal** based on the dataset! Whether you aim to identify health trends, categorize populations based on physical activity, or discover regional dietary patterns, the direction of your analysis is entirely up to you.  

----------------------
# Description


Participants will work with real-world health data collected from **2011 to 2023** across all **50 U.S. states**. The dataset, sourced from the **Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System (BRFSS)**, provides insights into **nutrition, physical activity, and obesity** trends among adults.  

### Dataset Details:
- **Dataset Name:** Nutrition, Physical Activity, and Obesity - Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System  
- **Dataset Author:** U.S. Department of Health & Human Services  
- **Dataset Description:** This dataset includes data on an adult's diet, physical activity, and weight status collected via surveys.  

Participants must apply **at least one unsupervised learning technique** (e.g., **clustering, PCA, t-SNE, or other dimensionality reduction methods**) in their analysis. The findings should be presented in a structured Kaggle Notebook, which will be reviewed for evaluation.  

----------------------
# Evaluation

Unlike traditional Kaggle competitions, this challenge has **no fixed evaluation metric**. Instead, participants will be **judged live** based on the depth of their insights, creativity in analysis, and proper application of unsupervised learning techniques.  

However, since Kaggle requires a submission system, a **dummy CSV file** will be provided. Submitting this file will grant **100% accuracy**, ensuring successful participation.  

## Submission  

Your submission should include:  

1. **A Kaggle Notebook** ¨C Your exploratory analysis, methodology, and insights must be clearly documented in a Jupyter Notebook.  
2. **A CSV Submission** ¨C A pre-provided CSV file must be submitted to meet Kaggle¡¯s submission requirements.  

By the end of this competition, you¡¯ll have hands-on experience working with real-world health data, applying machine learning techniques, and deriving actionable insights. **Good luck! ??**","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description

This dataset provides insights into **nutrition, physical activity, and obesity trends** in the United States, collected through the **Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System (BRFSS)**. The data spans from **2011 to 2023** across all **50 states**, capturing information on health-related behaviors and demographic factors.  

## Files

- **train.csv** ¨C The main dataset containing surveyed health-related data.
- **sample_submission.csv** ¨C A required submission file for Kaggle, ensuring a 100% accuracy submission.

## Data Format

The dataset is structured as a **CSV file**, where each row represents a surveyed data point for a specific year, location, and demographic group. It includes **health behavior metrics**, **demographic stratifications**, and **geographical identifiers**.

## Columns

- `YearStart` ¨C The starting year of the survey data.
- `YearEnd` ¨C The ending year of the survey data.
- `LocationAbbr` ¨C The two-letter abbreviation for the U.S. state or location.
- `LocationDesc` ¨C The full name of the U.S. state or location.
- `Datasource` ¨C The source of the data (e.g., BRFSS).
- `Class` ¨C The broad category of health data (e.g., Obesity / Weight Status, Physical Activity).
- `Topic` ¨C A more specific classification of the health data.
- `Question` ¨C The specific health-related survey question asked.
- `Data_Value_Unit` ¨C The unit of measurement (e.g., percentage).
- `Data_Value_Type` ¨C The type of value recorded (e.g., Value, Estimate).
- `Data_Value` ¨C The primary numerical value recorded for the observation.
- `Data_Value_Alt` ¨C An alternative representation of the recorded value.
- `Data_Value_Footnote_Symbol` ¨C A symbol indicating special notes or exclusions.
- `Data_Value_Footnote` ¨C Additional context or notes about the data.
- `Low_Confidence_Limit` ¨C The lower bound of the confidence interval for the data value.
- `High_Confidence_Limit` ¨C The upper bound of the confidence interval for the data value.
- `Sample_Size` ¨C The number of individuals surveyed for the data point.
- `Total` ¨C The total count associated with the response (if applicable).
- `Age(years)` ¨C The age range of respondents (if applicable).
- `Education` ¨C The education level of respondents (if applicable).
- `Sex` ¨C The gender of respondents (if applicable).
- `Income` ¨C The income category of respondents (if applicable).
- `Race/Ethnicity` ¨C The racial or ethnic background of respondents (if applicable).
- `GeoLocation` ¨C The geographical coordinates of the surveyed location.
- `ClassID` ¨C The identifier for the health data category.
- `TopicID` ¨C The identifier for the health topic.
- `QuestionID` ¨C The identifier for the specific survey question.
- `DataValueTypeID` ¨C The identifier for the data value type.
- `LocationID` ¨C The identifier for the location.
- `StratificationCategory1` ¨C The primary stratification category (e.g., Race, Sex, Age).
- `Stratification1` ¨C The specific stratification value (e.g., Female, Hispanic, 18-24 years).
- `StratificationCategoryId1` ¨C The identifier for the stratification category.
- `StratificationID1` ¨C The identifier for the specific stratification value.

## What to Expect

- The dataset contains **health-related metrics** measured across various demographics and time periods.
- Each row corresponds to **one observation**, representing a particular survey result for a specific location, demographic group, and year.
- Participants are encouraged to apply **unsupervised learning techniques** (e.g., clustering, PCA, t-SNE) to uncover trends and insights in the data.

This dataset provides an **open-ended exploration** of public health trends, allowing participants to formulate their own research questions and findings. ??",False,True
9328,open-ai-x-data-vision-pillar-spring-2025,"# Overview

# Competition Overview

The goal of this competition is to build or fine-tune a computer vision model to correctly classify sports video game images into one of five different sports genres: **Basketball, Volleyball, Tennis, Soccer, and American Football**. The contestants will be provided with a dataset consisting of video game images, along with a CSV file that maps the image filenames to their respective sports labels. Contestants will need to develop a model capable of identifying the sport category of each image.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F19320325%2F7fc46967a0b3e366a3a286f4bc3b3688%2F757.jpg?generation=1741430890058945&alt=media)
---
----------------------
# Description

In this competition, participants will use the **Sports10** dataset, which contains video game images categorized into five sports genres. Each genre includes images from various games within that sport. The objective is to create or fine-tune a computer vision model that can accurately classify each image into one of the five categories:  
- **Basketball**
- **Volleyball**
- **Tennis**
- **Soccer**
- **American Football**

The images will contain diverse visual representations of these sports, as they are sourced from different video games. The dataset will be split into a training set and a test set, with participants tasked with labeling the test images based on their model's predictions.

For further information on the dataset and methodology used in its creation, please refer to the paper: [Contrastive Learning of Generalized Game Representations](https://paperswithcode.com/paper/contrastive-learning-of-generalized-game).

### Dataset Details:
- **Dataset Name**: Sports10
- **Dataset Author**: Chintan Trivedi, Antonios Liapis, Georgios N. Yannakakis
- **Dataset Description**: A collection of images representing five different sports genres within video games, including Basketball, Volleyball, Tennis, Soccer, and American Football.

----------------------
# Evaluation


## Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition will be **accuracy**. Submissions will be assessed based on how well the model correctly classifies the sports images in the test set. The higher the percentage of correct predictions, the better the model¡¯s performance.

Accuracy will be calculated as the proportion of correctly predicted labels for the test dataset.

---

## Submission File

Participants are required to submit a CSV file that includes the following:
- **id**: The identifier for each test image.
- **class**: The predicted label corresponding to the image's sport genre.

The format of the CSV file should be as follows:

| id         | class          |
|------------|----------------|
| 1001  | Basketball     |
| 1002  | Soccer         |
| 1003  | Volleyball     |
| ...        | ...            |

Make sure to label the columns correctly
---
Good luck, and we look forward to seeing your innovative solutions for classifying sports video game images!","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition consists of images representing video game sports, with the goal of classifying each image into one of five sport genres: Basketball, Volleyball, Tennis, Soccer, or American Football. Participants will be provided with three primary files: `train.csv`, `test.csv`, and a folder containing the images. 

---

## Files

### **train.csv**  
This file contains the training data used to train the computer vision model. It provides the filename of each image along with the corresponding sports label. The format of the file is as follows:

| filename     | class         | id  |
|--------------|---------------|-----|
| images/1.jpg | AmericanFootball | 1  |
| images/2.jpg | AmericanFootball | 2  |
| images/3.jpg | AmericanFootball | 3  |
| ...          | ...           | ... |

- `filename`: The path to the image file.
- `class`: The label for the sports genre of the corresponding image.
- `id`: A unique identifier for the image.

### **test.csv**  
This file contains the test data used for evaluation. It follows the same structure as the `train.csv` file, but without labels. The test set will be used for the final model evaluation.

| filename     | id  |
|--------------|-----|
| images/1001.jpg | 1001 |
| images/1002.jpg | 1002 |
| images/1003.jpg | 1003 |
| ...          | ... |

- `filename`: The path to the image file.
- `id`: A unique identifier for the image (used for the submission file).

### **sample_submission.csv**  
This is a sample submission file to help you understand the required format for your predictions. It contains two columns: `id` (the identifier of the test images) and `label` (the predicted label for each test image). The `label` column will contain the sports genre prediction for each test image, which you will submit for evaluation.

| id        | label        |
|-----------|--------------|
| 1001      | AmericanFootball |
| 1002      | Soccer       |
| 1003      | Basketball   |
| ...       | ...          |

### **images**  
This folder contains 1129 images of various video games from five distinct sports genres. Each image corresponds to a unique `filename` listed in the `train.csv` and `test.csv` files. The images are categorized into the following sports genres:
- **Basketball**
- **Volleyball**
- **Tennis**
- **Soccer**
- **American Football**

---

## Data Format

### **CSV File Columns:**
1. `filename`: The image file path.
2. `class`: The actual sports genre (only in `train.csv`).
3. `id`: A unique identifier for each image.

### **Image Format:**
- The images are JPEG files.
- The images represent scenes from video games and cover a wide range of in-game representations of the five sports.

---

## What Are You Predicting?

You are predicting the **sports genre** of the test images. The test dataset does not include labels, and your model¡¯s predictions for these images will be evaluated based on accuracy. Your task is to predict one of the following categories for each image:
- **Basketball**
- **Volleyball**
- **Tennis**
- **Soccer**
- **American Football**

Your submission should include a CSV file with the image `id` and the predicted `label` for each test image.

---

## Acronyms You May Encounter

- **CSV**: Comma-Separated Values
- **id**: A unique identifier for each image
- **class**: The sports genre label for an image (only in training data)",False,True
9329,pml-m-3-makeup-hackathon,"# Overview

Hopkins is one of the great hospital systems of the world. It is therefore fitting that we work with healthcare data. The data (SUPPORT2) is hosted at the University of California, Irvine: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/880/support2.

Your purpose:

To develop and validate two properly-fitted models to predict charges for services. The two models you using should be a) a regression tree (DecisionTreeRegressor), and b) a LightGBM ensemble. Your performance metric is MSE. Your target is charges.
To include a learning curve (to assess fit) and graph of actuals vs predicted values (to assess performance) for both models. Contrast the performance and fit of the two models.
----------------------
# Description

Data Dictionary:

| Variable Name | Role    | Type        | Demographic       | Description                                                                                          | Units | Missing Values |
|--------------|--------|------------|------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------|---------------|
| id           | ID     | Integer    |                  |                                                                                                  |       | no            |
| age         | Feature | Continuous | Age              | Age of the patients in years                                                                      | years | no            |
| sex         | Feature | Categorical | Sex              | Gender of the patient. Listed values are {male, female}.                                         |       | no            |
| slos        | Other   | Continuous |                  | Days from Study Entry to Discharge                                                              |       | no            |
| d.time      | Other   | Continuous |                  | Days of follow-up                                                                              |       | no            |
| dzgroup     | Feature | Categorical |                  | The patient's disease subcategory amongst ARF/MOSF w/Sepsis, CHF, COPD, Cirrhosis, Colon Cancer, Coma, Lung Cancer, MOSF w/Malig. |       | no            |
| dzclass     | Feature | Categorical |                  | The patient's disease category amongst ""ARF/MOSF"", ""COPD/CHF/Cirrhosis"", ""Cancer"", ""Coma"".      |       | no            |
| num.co      | Feature | Continuous  |                  | The number of simultaneous diseases (or comorbidities) exhibited by the patient. Values are ordinal with higher values indicating worse condition and chances of survival. |       | no            |
| edu         | Feature | Categorical | Education Level  | Years of education                                                                              | years | yes           |
| income      | Feature | Categorical | Income           | Income of the patient. Listed values are {""$11-$25k"", ""$25-$50k"", "">$50k"", ""under $11k""}.       |       | yes           |
| scoma       | Feature | Continuous  |                  | SUPPORT day 3 Coma Score based on Glasgow scale (predicted by a model).                         |       | yes           |
