9334,da514-default-prediction,"# Overview

This project is designed to help you gain experience in exploring and analyzing data for a specific goal, building and validating a machine learning model to predict the customer default on a personal loan. This is a classification problem with a binary target class for customer default/non-default.

All details are given in the notebook which you can download from SUCourse.
----------------------
# Description

Details you'll need to start the project are provided in the notebook you can download from SUCourse.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on F1 score.

Submission File:

For each ID in the test set, you must report the prediction on the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

You are supposed to train your model on the training set provided. All operations on hyperparameter tuning should be executed on the training data set. Remember that the test data presented here does not contain the target variable. Please keep in mind that any preprocessing you applied on the training data should be applied to the test data as well. 

## Files

*   **train-kaggle.csv** - the training set
*   **test-kaggle.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
* **macro_data.xlsx** - Economic indicators for the relevant yers in the train/test data (such as inflation and interest rates)
* **unemployment.xlsx** - Unemployment rates for the years relevant to the train/test data
These files are also available on SUcouse.",False,True
9335,kaggle-community-olympiad-unmasking-fakes,"# Overview

**Unmasking Fakes: Classify Statements as True or False**

**Why Participate?**  
- **Expand Your Knowledge**: Dive into natural language processing (NLP) and binary classification techniques, mastering their use in detecting deception.  
- **Make an Impact**: Advance the fight against misinformation by building models that distinguish truth from falsehood, contributing to a more informed world.  
- **Showcase Your Skills**: Compete with peers, highlight your machine learning expertise, and shine in the evolving field of data science.  
- **Earn Rewards**: Top contributors receive exclusive swags from TFUG Prayagraj!  

Join us on this thrilling journey to explore the intersection of machine learning and misinformation detection. Let¡¯s harness the power of AI to unmask fakes and promote truth!  

**To Get an Invitation for Graduation Day**:  
- Be a top contributor and fill out this form: [https://forms.gle/JczUHVXic28F4P2MA](https://forms.gle/JczUHVXic28F4P2MA)  

**MLOlympiad**  
**Email**: tfugprayagraj@gmail.com

**Goal:**  
The goal of ""Unmasking Fakes"" is to empower participants to build machine learning models that accurately classify statements as ""true"" or ""false"". By tackling this real-world challenge, we aim to enhance skills in NLP, promote critical thinking about misinformation, and foster a community-driven effort to combat fake news with AI.

\#KaggleCommunityOlympiad

----------------------
# Description

![KCO](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F19972470%2Fbfd85bcfad7c06131eb4cab6ccaa904c%2FKCO_square_message.png?generation=1742129560427756&alt=media)Join TFUG Prayagraj¡¯s ML Olympiad 2025 to classify statements as ""true"" or ""false"" using the dataset. Build NLP models, submit predictions, and compete for accuracy¡ªand swags!!

\#KaggleCommunityOlympiad
----------------------
# Evaluation

In the ""Unmasking Fakes"" competition, your model's performance is judged by accuracy¡ªthe percentage of statements correctly classified as ""true"" or ""false."" For example, 80 out of 100 correct gives you 80% accuracy.","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description: 

#### Overview
This dataset is a collection of political statements that has been processed to exclude entries originally classified as ""half-true"" and to simplify historical count metrics into two categories: ""True Count"" and ""False Count."" Each row represents a unique political statement with associated metadata and historical accuracy counts, treated independently without cross-row data aggregation.

#### Dataset Transformation
The original data included statements with five historical count categories: ""Barely True,"" ""False,"" ""Half True,"" ""Mostly True,"" and ""Pants on Fire."" For this version:
- *Filtering*: Rows originally marked as ""half-true"" are excluded.
- *Counts*: 
  - Historical counts are combined into two categories:
    - ""True Count"" = original ""Mostly True"" count.
    - ""False Count"" = sum of original ""False,"" ""Barely True,"" and ""Pants on Fire"" counts.
  - The ""Half True"" count is discarded as these rows are removed.
- *ID*: The "".json"" suffix is removed from file IDs for cleaner identification.
- The resulting dataset is stored in CSV format with quoted fields for consistency.

#### Structure
- *Format*: CSV (Comma-Separated Values)
- *Rows*: 224 (after exclusion of ""half-true"" entries from an unspecified original total)
- *Columns*: 10
- *Encoding*: UTF-8
- *Delimiter*: Comma (,)
- *Quoting*: All fields are enclosed in double quotes ("")

#### Column Descriptions
| Column Name                | Data Type | Description                                                                                   | Example Value                                      |
|----------------------------|-----------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| *ID*                     | String    | Unique identifier for the statement, derived from original filename without "".json"" suffix.   | ""2635""                                            |
| *Statement*              | String    | The full text of the political statement or claim.                                            | ""Says the Annies List political group supports third-trimester abortions on demand."" |
| *Subject*                | String    | Topic(s) of the statement, comma-separated if multiple.                                       | ""abortion""                                        |
| *Speaker*                | String    | Name of the individual or entity making the statement.                                        | ""dwayne-bohac""                                    |
| *Speaker Job Title*      | String    | Professional title or role of the speaker (may be empty).                                     | ""State representative""                            |
| *State*                  | String    | U.S. state associated with the speaker or statement (may be empty).                           | ""Texas""                                           |
| *Political Party Affiliation* | String    | Political party of the speaker (e.g., ""republican,"" ""democrat,"" ""none"").                      | ""republican""                                      |
| *True Count*             | Integer   | Historical count of ""mostly true"" ratings for this speaker, from original data.               | ""0""                                               |
| *False Count*            | Integer   | Sum of historical ""false,"" ""barely true,"" and ""pants on fire"" ratings for this speaker.       | ""1""                                               |
| *Context*                | String    | Where or when the statement was made (e.g., speech, debate, ad).                              | ""a mailer""                                        |

#### Data Characteristics
- *Size*: 224 rows, reduced from an unspecified original total by the exclusion of ""half-true"" entries (approximately 33% of rows may have been removed if ""half-true"" was evenly distributed).
- *Missing Values*: 
  - Fields like ""Speaker Job Title,"" ""State,"" or ""Context"" may be empty ("""") if not provided in the original data.
  - No numerical missing values; counts are always present as integers.
- *Uniqueness*: Each row is treated as a standalone entity with its own metadata and historical counts, not influenced by other rows.
- *Diversity*: 
  - Speakers range from prominent political figures (e.g., ""barack-obama,"" ""hillary-clinton"") to organizations (e.g., ""chain-email,"" ""blog-posting"").
  - Subjects cover a wide array of topics, including ""abortion,"" ""economy,"" ""health-care,"" ""immigration,"" and more.

#### Usage
- *Purpose*: Designed for analysis of political statements with associated historical accuracy metrics.
- *Features*: 
  - *Textual*: ""Statement"" (primary text data), ""Subject,"" ""Speaker,"" ""Speaker Job Title,"" ""Context.""
  - *Categorical*: ""State,"" ""Political Party Affiliation.""
  - *Numerical*: ""True Count,"" ""False Count"" (historical speaker credibility metrics).
- *Potential Applications*: 
  - Natural Language Processing (NLP) for statement analysis.
  - Political analysis of speaker credibility based on historical counts.
  - Sentiment or bias analysis based on party affiliation or subject matter.

#### Limitations
- *Ambiguity Removal*: Exclusion of ""half-true"" rows reduces dataset size and removes nuanced cases.
- *Historical Counts*: ""True Count"" and ""False Count"" reflect only past ""mostly true"" and combined ""false/barely true/pants on fire"" ratings, respectively, not the full spectrum of original ratings.
- *Data Gaps*: Empty fields in ""Speaker Job Title"" or ""State"" may limit analysis for some rows.
- *Context Variability*: The ""Context"" field varies widely in specificity (e.g., ""a mailer"" vs. ""an interview on Fox News""), which may affect consistency in analysis.

#### Source and Processing
- *Original Source*: A collection of political statements with historical count data, exact origin unspecified.
- *Processing*: Processed to remove "".json"" from IDs, exclude ""half-true"" rows, and combine counts into ""True Count"" and ""False Count"" using a custom script.
- *Date*: Processed as of March 15, 2025 (based on system context), though original data timestamps are not preserved.",False,True
9336,kcvanguard-deep-learning-assignment,"# Overview

?? Welcome to the KCVanguard Sentiment Analysis Classification competition! ?? This competition is designed to evaluate participants' understanding of deep learning concepts covered in the workshops organized by Lab Komputasi Cerdas dan Visi (KCV). ??

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F19646647%2F709e982b564c2c0c07a4d310f4256c86%2FWhatsApp%20Image%202025-03-08%20at%2010.42.57_b63a78f0.jpg?generation=1741962123844868&alt=media)
----------------------
# Description

Participants will work with a dataset consisting of **restaurant reviews** collected from Google Maps in Indonesia. ?? The dataset contains only text data, where each review expresses either a **positive ?? or negative ??** sentiment. The challenge is to develop a robust machine learning model that can accurately classify these reviews based on their sentiment. ??

The goal of this competition is to assess how well participants can apply deep learning techniques to a real-world sentiment analysis problem. By engaging in this challenge, participants will:
- ??? Gain hands-on experience in text classification using deep learning.
- ?? Develop models that can distinguish between positive and negative restaurant reviews.
- ??? Enhance their ability to preprocess, train, and evaluate machine learning models effectively.

Name Format : **NRP_Name**
----------------------
# Evaluation

?? Submissions will be evaluated using **accuracy score** as the primary metric. The goal is to maximize the accuracy of sentiment predictions on the test set.","# Dataset Description

The dataset provided contains:

- Review Text: ?? Raw customer reviews of restaurants in Indonesia.
- Sentiment Label: Either Positive ? or Negative ? (target variable).

No additional metadata (e.g., ratings, restaurant names, timestamps) is included to focus the competition purely on text-based sentiment classification.",False,True
