9356,icdar-2025-glyph-detection,"# Overview

This is a competition on the detection and classification of glyphs in 15th-Century European printed documents. We provide over 200 document images with a sparse labeling. The test set consists of 60 fully-labeled pages.
----------------------
# Description

Our competition is about glyph detection and classification. Our training data is provided in the widely-used COCO format.

## Input
The training data is provided at the following address:
[https://faubox.rrze.uni-erlangen.de/getlink/fi53t9EmHRryikPEK9o7Lu/icdar25-glyph-detection](https://faubox.rrze.uni-erlangen.de/getlink/fi53t9EmHRryikPEK9o7Lu/icdar25-glyph-detection)
It consists of:
- A set of images (25plus) with a higher density of annotations,
- A set of images (25minues) with a lower density of annotations,
- A fully labeled images and a test script, so that you can test locally methods
Here, annotations are in the COCO format.

## Output
Submissions have to be in a CSV format (Kaggle accepts only this). We provide a Python3 script which turns COCO files in the required CSV format. Some more details are given in the ""Data"" tab.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation, and ranking, is done on Kaggle.

For debugging and test purpose, we also provide:
- An image labelled in the same way as the test data,
- An evaluation script for this image.","# Dataset Description

This test dataset is composed of:
- 60 images,
- A template COCO file to fill
- A Python script to produce a Kaggle-friendly CSV file
Details are given below.

**Test images**
The test images are of a similar diversity as the training data. They have various resolutions and aspect ratios.

**Template COCO file**
This file has the same structure as the training ground truth. Your method should ideally populate the ""annotations"" entry of this file with entries of this structure:

{
    ""id"": UNIQUE_INT_ID,
    ""image_id"": IMAGE_ID,
    ""category_id"": CAT_ID,
    ""bbox"": [TOP, LEFT, WIDTH, HEIGHT],
    ""iscrowd"": 0,
    ""area"": WIDTH*HEIGHT
}

Where:
- UNIQUE_INT_ID is the annotation's unique ID. You can simply increment an integer.
- IMAGE_ID is the ID of the image containing the annotation (see field ""id"" in the image list)
- CAT_ID is the category ID, aka the class label
- TOP, LEFT, WIDTH, and HEIGHT define the bounding box

**json_to_csv.py**
In short, Kaggle accepts only results in a CSV format which is not great for object detection tasks. We circumvent this issue by asking participants to:
1) produce an output in the standard COCO format, which is great for object detection,
2) use our script json_to_csv.py which turns the result file into a CSV file which can be accepted by Kaggle.

The CSV file has the following structure:
`image_id, annotations_dumped_as_json_string`",False,True
9357,sss-analytics-rug-pull,"# Overview

