9369,principios-de-machine-learning-regresion-2501,"# Overview

Se tiene informaci¨®n sobre 1600 zonas ordenadas de manera contigua durante un a?o de un detallista de ropa y joyer¨ªa que vende por cat¨¢logo.

De acuerdo con lo indicado por el cuerpo directivo, el objetivo es realizar un pron¨®stico de ventas anual para la ropa de mujer basado en la informaci¨®n de la sucursal. De gran importancia es saber si los recursos que se asignan tienen poder para modelar la demanda en las zonas respectivas.

Para ello desean que usted haga un pron¨®stico para las 400 zonas que se encuentran en el archivo de sometimiento (ver datos), cuya informaci¨®n corresponde al mismo a?o de las 1600 zonas originales. El pron¨®stico ser¨¢ evaluado con base en la ra¨ªz del error cuadr¨¢tico medio (RMSE) que se obtenga en estas 400 sucursales.

## ?Qu¨¦ deben presentar?

* Talleres individuales acorde a CRISP-DM con longitud m¨¢xima de 10 p¨¢ginas: 50%.
* Predicci¨®n: 50%.
Se dar¨¢ bonificaci¨®n adicional por la realizaci¨®n y respuesta de preguntas en los foros, entrega de notebooks o c¨®digo p¨²blico en la plataforma, valoraciones de los aportes de otros, y en general, por la colaboraci¨®n.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La mejor predicci¨®n obtendr¨¢ un 50 en el 50% del taller, y todos aquellos que igualen un benchmark ingenuo obtendr¨¢n 20. Valores intermedios se ubicar¨¢n de acuerdo a una escala. Si no se supera el benchmark ingenuo la nota ser¨¢ inferior a 20. La predicci¨®n ser¨¢ valorada basada en el ingenuo ya publicado y usando la m¨¦trica RMSE. La colaboraci¨®n en los kernels y foros en el espacio del concurso puede darle puntos adicionales, valorados por los profesores. Colaborar no necesariamente es responder, tambi¨¦n preguntar y comentar. S¨®lo se permitir¨¢n notebooks de exploraci¨®n/visualizaci¨®n. El taller corresponde al otro 50% de la nota.","# Dataset Description

El archivo de datos que se entrega est¨¢ en formato Excel (xlsx) y tiene dos hojas: Train, para desarrollar el modelo (1600 registros) y Test (400 registros) con todas las variables independientes necesarias para predecir la variable de respuesta (ropamujer). Las predicciones deben ser sometidas a kaggle en formato .csv con dos columnas con encabezados idloc y ropamujer (esta ¨²ltima contiene su predicci¨®n). La separaci¨®n de las columnas es con coma y el decimal debe marcarse con punto(.).

Campos de datos
ropamujer: ventas de prendas para mujer en millones de pesos durante el a?o.
correo: n¨²mero de cat¨¢logos enviados durante el a?o.
p¨¢ginas: n¨²mero de p¨¢ginas del cat¨¢logo.
tel¨¦fono: n¨²mero promedio de l¨ªneas para llamada abiertas para pedidos.
impresa: cantidad gastada en publicidad impresa.
servicio: n¨²mero de representantes del servicio al cliente.
idmercado: tipo de mercado. Solo se listan n¨²meros de clases o tipos de mercado que se desea atacar. No se proveer¨¢ informaci¨®n adicional.
tama?omer: tama?o potencial del mercado, proyectado de acuerdo a cifras del ¨¢rea de marketing.
Idloc: ID de la tienda.
Edadloc: a?os transcurridos desde la llegada a la zona.
promo (promoci¨®n): tipo de promoci¨®n que se llev¨® a cabo durante el a?o. De nuevo, informaci¨®n descriptiva sobre estas promociones es reservada.
nomina: valor total de la n¨®mina durante el a?o.",False,True
9370,retencion-en-telefonia-movil-2501,"# Overview

.
----------------------
# Description

Las comunicaciones por tel¨¦fono celular siempre han sido objeto de una feroz competencia, en la que, usualmente, hay un n¨²mero reducido de competidores (operadores) en el mercado que oscila entre cuatro y seis. Los operadores deben hacer fuertes inversiones de entrada para poder operar y prestar un servicio adecuado. El mercado de telefon¨ªa celular est¨¢ usualmente saturado (poca posibilidad de ampliaci¨®n del mercado) y, por tanto, el conjunto de consumidores disponibles es reducido, por lo que los operadores usualmente cambian su estrategia de adquisici¨®n de clientes a una de retenci¨®n, pues en el mundo se calcula que el costo de adquirir un nuevo cliente es cinco veces superior al de retenerlo, sin contar con el posible CLTV (customer lifetimevalue) del cliente, esto es, el retorno a largo plazo que se genera por un cliente que contin¨²a consumiendo los servicios de un operador. Otra cifra clave es que la tasa de deserci¨®n (churn) suele rondar el 2% mensual promedio para un operador de telecomunicaciones. Desde el punto de vista de la inteligencia de negocios, bajo la perspectiva de CRM hay dos esfuerzos fundamentales de Anal¨ªtica: a) predecir la deserci¨®n (churn) de manera adecuada y b) evaluar la manera m¨¢s costo/efectiva en la que un operador puede hacer para la retenci¨®n (incluyendo no hacer nada).

Un operador de telecomunicaciones les ha contratado para resolver las dos inquietudes anal¨ªticas presentadas anteriormente. Para la primera desea que, a partir de una base de datos de entrenamiento (que es una muestra aleatoria simple de la base de datos total de la compa?¨ªa) que contiene el resultado de retenci¨®n en los ¨²ltimos seis meses, se realice la mejor predicci¨®n posible (basada en el ¨¢rea bajo la curva COR) para una base de datos de prueba en un concurso de Kaggle. Para la segunda parte, adicional a un reporte con metodolog¨ªa CRISP-DM (de m¨¢ximo 12 p¨¢ginas) de los modelos realizados, desea que se le presente una estrategia (incluyendo puntos de corte, costos y posibles ganancias) para atacar la retenci¨®n.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La m¨¦trica de evaluaci¨®n es el ¨¢rea bajo la curva caracter¨ªstica de operaci¨®n (AUC bajo la curva COR) en la base de datos de test. Los sometimientos deben ser archivos separados por comas, con un encabezado que contenga las variables ""id"" y ""resultado"".","# Dataset Description

La base de datos de entrenamiento tiene una muestra aleatoria simple de los clientes de la compa?¨ªa. Las variables de consumo representan los ¨²ltimos seis meses del cliente con el operador. Si el cliente no ha entrado en churn, corresponden al segundo semestre de 2018 (el corte es primero de enero de 2019). Si el cliente ha entrado en churn, corresponden a los ¨²ltimos seis meses con el operador, y el churn pudo haber ocurrido en cualquier momento dentro de la ventana de tiempo del segundo semestre de 2018. Todos los clientes tienen contratos de suscripci¨®n (son clientes pospago).

La base de datos de prueba contiene una muestra no aleatoria adicional de clientes de la compa?¨ªa. Las variables de consumo representan los ¨²ltimos seis meses del cliente con el operador. Si el cliente no ha entrado en churn, corresponden al segundo semestre de 2018 (el corte es primero de enero de 2019). Si el cliente ha entrado en churn, corresponden a los ¨²ltimos seis meses con el operador, y el churn pudo haber ocurrido en cualquier momento dentro de la ventana de tiempo del segundo semestre de 2018. Todos los clientes tienen contratos de suscripci¨®n (son clientes pospago). La variable de posible churn (resultado) no se incluye, pues es la variable a predecir.

**Archivos**
traintelco.xlsx - La base de datos de entrenamiento
testelco.xlsx - La base de datos de prueba
samplesubtelco.csv - Archivo muestra de submission


**Campos de la base de datos**
id - id an¨®nimo que identifica al cliente.
Fecha de nacimiento - Fecha de nacimiento del cliente, dd/mm/yyyy.
tipo cliente - Tipo de cliente: 1 - Hombre, 2 - Mujer, 3 - Cliente empresarial.
Factura online - Binario que indica si el cliente recibe ¨²nicamente su factura online.
Antiguedad equipo - Meses de antig¨¹edad del equipo.
Plan de datos - Binario. 1 = el cliente tiene plan premium de datos.
Facturaci¨®n - Total facturaci¨®n de los ¨²ltimos seis meses (suma).
Mora - D¨ªas de mora acumulados.
Fecha inicio contrato - Fecha de inicio de su primer contrato de plan pospago en la compa?¨ªa.
minutos - total minutos consumidos en los ¨²ltimos seis meses.
resultado - 0 = No hubo deserci¨®n; 1 = Hubo deserci¨®n (churn)",False,True
9371,garbage-guru-challenge-2-0,"# Overview

Welcome to the second edition of the **Garbage Guru Challenge**! Building on the success of the first competition, we aim to push the boundaries of AI-powered waste classification and recycling technology. This challenge provides an opportunity for participants to leverage machine learning techniques to create robust models for identifying and sorting different types of waste efficiently.
----------------------
# Description

**What's New in 2.0?**

**Expanded Dataset:** The dataset now includes more diverse waste categories, improved annotations, and additional real-world images.

**Enhanced Evaluation Metrics:** We've refined our scoring system to better reflect real-world classification performance.

**New Baseline Models:** Participants can access updated starter notebooks to help kickstart their models.

**Improved Labeling Quality:** Data annotations have been enhanced for better accuracy and consistency.



----------------------
# Challenge

Craft an AI model that can:

- **Ace the Sorting Exam**: Pinpoint the correct category for each garbage item with impressive precision and recall.
- **Handle the Messy Real World**: Deal with diverse image variations like different lighting, angles, and cluttered backgrounds.
- **No More Peek-a-Boo Trash**: Be robust to occlusions and partial visibility of waste items.
- **Think Beyond the Training Ground**: Generalize well to unseen data and real-world scenarios, becoming a true waste-sorting master.
----------------------
# Dataset

- The dataset consists of images labeled with different waste categories such as plastic, paper, glass, metal, organic, and more.
- Each image is accompanied by its corresponding class label.
- A detailed data description and sample code are provided to help you get started.
----------------------
# Rewards

- **Knowledge is Power**: Gain valuable experience and insights into AI for environmental applications.
- **Sharpen Your Portfolio**: Showcase your skills and expertise to potential employers and collaborators.
- **Contribute to a Greener Future**: Your learnings can pave the way for smarter waste sorting systems, promoting a more sustainable world.
----------------------
# Evaluation

- **Macro F1 score**: The heart of the matter! We'll measure how often your model correctly classifies each type of garbage using Macro F1-Score. See https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html.
- **Robustness**: Can your model handle real-world challenges like blurry images and mixed waste streams? We'll test its adaptability.
- **Interpretability**: Bonus points for models that explain their reasoning, helping us understand and improve their decision-making.

----------------------
# Bonus Quests 

- **Transfer Learning Mastery**: Utilize pre-trained image classification models and fine-tune them for garbage sorting.
- **Data Augmentation Guru**: Unleash the power of data augmentation techniques to boost your model'sgeneralizability.
- **Explainable AI**: Develop models that reveal their reasoning behind each classification, fostering trust and understanding.
----------------------
# Submission

For each id in the test set, you must predict a type of class (or label). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```csv
Id,Expected
paper/paper_1775.jpg,0
paper/paper_1749.jpg,0
.....
```

The expected numeric class labels are assigned based on a predefined order rather than alphabetical sorting. The class labels are mapped as follows:
Paper ¡ú 0
Clothes ¡ú 1
Metal ¡ú 2
Cardboard ¡ú 3
Trash ¡ú 4
Glass ¡ú 5
Biological ¡ú 6
Battery ¡ú 7
Plastic ¡ú 8
Shoes ¡ú 9
----------------------
# Summary

Are you ready to take on the challenge and make an impact on sustainability? Join now and be part of the movement towards smarter waste management!

Let¡¯s innovate for a cleaner planet! ????","# Dataset Description

Collection of images of garbages grouped into 10 classes (metal, glass, biological, paper, battery, trash, cardboard, shoes, clothes, and plastic). This data is a subset of a [Garbage dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/sumn2u/garbage-classification-v2). The number of files in respective classes is as follows: 

- **Metal**: 1000
- **Glass**: 1000
- **Biological**: 1000
- **Paper**: 1000
- **Battery**: 1000
- **Trash**: 1000
- **Cardboard**: 1000
- **Shoes**: 1000
- **Clothes**: 1000
- **Plastic**: 1000",False,True
9372,skku-aai-3010-2025-spring-lab-assignment-1,"# Overview

Task Description:
The goal of this assignment is to complete a regression task to predict the appropriate number of shared bicycle rentals on a certain day. You will build a regression model by analyzing the given feature data of each data, completing the entire process from data preprocessing to feature selection, model training, and evaluation.

We provide you with a basic model as a starting point. You can modify and optimize this model or build your own model to improve performance. The dataset contains missing values (NA), which you need to handle before training the model. By analyzing and processing the data, you will learn how to address real-world data challenges and optimize your model¡¯s performance
----------------------
# Description

Assignment Requirements:

**Basic Model:**
We provide a basic regression model as a starting point. You can use this model to train on the dataset, or you can modify and optimize it for better performance.
Alternatively, you can build your own model from scratch.

**Data Preprocessing:**
You need to handle the missing values (NA) in the dataset. You can choose to fill NA values with zeros, mean values, or use other reasonable methods.
Analyze the relationship between different features and the target variable (cnt) to identify key features for prediction.

**Feature Selection and Engineering:**
The dataset contains many features, but not all of them are useful for prediction. You need to perform feature selection to retain features that are beneficial for the model.
You can also apply feature engineering by creating new features or transforming existing ones to further improve model performance.

**Model Training and Evaluation:**
Train your model using PyTorch, either by modifying the provided basic model or by building your own.
You can experiment with hyperparameter tuning (e.g., learning rate, batch size) or apply other regression techniques to improve results.
Once the model is trained, predict the cnt values for the test set and submit your predictions.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated one the Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE). The RMSLE is calculated as
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F8378313%2F599fad85f2d2f84a0a226ff3a49e4098%2Frmse.png?generation=1742731968954019&alt=media)

Where:
- n: the number of days in the test set
- p_i:  your predicted count
- a_i: the actual count
- log(x): the natural logarithm","# Dataset Description

Case description: A certain shared bicycle was launched on the market and has been in operation for a period of time. We have obtained a series of data on factors that affect bicycle rentals and the number of bicycles rented on that day. Please conduct data analysis based on this data to predict the appropriate number of shared bicycle rentals on a certain day in the future, so that the company can invest and obtain maximum profits.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set, incuding feature information for each data and cnt ??(number of total rentals). You can use this data to train the model.
*   **test.csv** - the test set, providing the same features, but excluding cnt. You need to predict and submit the prediction results based on these features.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns
* instant: ID
* dteday: daily date + timestamp
* season:  1 = spring, 2 = summer, 3 = fall, 4 = winter 
* holiday: whether the day is considered a holiday
* workingday: whether the day is neither a weekend nor holiday
* weathersit: 
1: Clear, Few clouds, Partly cloudy, Partly cloudy
2: Mist + Cloudy, Mist + Broken clouds, Mist + Few clouds, Mist
3: Light Snow, Light Rain + Thunderstorm + Scattered clouds, Light Rain + Scattered clouds
4: Heavy Rain + Ice Pallets + Thunderstorm + Mist, Snow + Fog
* temp: temperature in Celsius
* atemp: ""feels like"" temperature in Celsius
* hum: relative humidity
* windspeed: wind speed
* cnt: number of total rentals

**Note** that there are missing values ??in the dataset, which you need to handle before starting modeling.",False,True
9373,loveu-cvpr-2025-track-1-vdc,"# Overview

This competition challenges participants to generate structured, detailed captions for videos from the VDC benchmark introduced in [AuroraCap](https://wenhaochai.com/aurora-web/). For each video, participants must generate five distinct captions focusing on different aspects of the content: camera, background, main objects, summary, and a full detailed caption.

The goal is to advance fine-grained video understanding through multi-perspective caption generation. Submissions will be evaluated using VDCScore, a QA-based LLM metric.

?? Dataset: [VDC on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/datasets/auroracap/vdc)  
?? Leaderboard: [External Link to Leaderboard](https://your-link.com)

?? **Note**:
- Submissions that are **incomplete** or **do not follow the required format** will be **ignored without evaluation**.
- Due to the nature of the evaluation pipeline, the **leaderboard is updated once every 24 hours** based on your most recent valid submission.
----------------------
# Description

## ?? Task

You are given 100 videos sampled from the VDC benchmark. For each video, your task is to generate **five structured captions**:

1. **Camera Caption** ¨C describe camera movements, transitions, angles  
2. **Short Caption** ¨C a one-sentence summary  
3. **Background Caption** ¨C describe the scene and environment  
4. **Main Object Caption** ¨C describe main entities, actions, interactions  
5. **Detailed Caption** ¨C a long, rich description integrating all perspectives

All captions should follow the provided structured prompt format.

---

## ?? Submission Content

Each submission should include:

- Five captions per video (total of 500 captions)  
- The number of sampled frames per video  
- The number of visual tokens per frame  

Format: a single JSON file (see Evaluation page for details)

---

## ?? Evaluation Metric

We use **VDCScore**, a QA-based caption evaluation metric.

- Reference captions are decomposed into QA pairs
- LLaMA-3.1-8B is used to check answer consistency
- Final score = average correctness across QA evaluations

Execution is accelerated via [SGLang](https://github.com/DachengLi1/SGLang).

---

## ?? Recognition & Workshop

Top 3 teams will be selected based on:

- Highest overall VDCScore  
- A short technical report (due by June 5, 2025)

?? Winners will be invited to present at **LOVEU@CVPR 2025 Workshop**.  
All submitted reports will be included in the official workshop summary (not proceedings).

---

## ??? Timeline

- ?? Submission Opens: **April 15, 2025**  
- ?? Submission Deadline: **June 1, 2025**  
- ?? Report Deadline: **June 5, 2025**

---

## ?? Resources

- [VDC benchmark on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/datasets/auroracap/vdc)  
- [VDC introduction video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePpGJDR1FxM)
----------------------
# Evaluation

## ?? Evaluation Metric: VDCScore

We use **VDCScore**, a QA-based evaluation framework using large language models. For each ground-truth caption, a set of QA pairs is generated. The same questions are then asked of the generated caption, and answers are checked for consistency.

- Model: `LLaMA-3.1-8B`
- Engine: [SGLang](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang)
- Final score = mean accuracy across all QA pairs and all five caption types


![VDCScore evaluation pipeline](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11288095%2F9239965875aef051918eb8bc4f08263f%2Fvdcscore.png?generation=1742748343228547&alt=media)

---

## ?? Submission Format

Your submission should be a single `.json` file with the following format:

```json
{
  ""video_id_001"": {
    ""camera_caption"": ""..."",
    ""short_caption"": ""..."",
    ""background_caption"": ""..."",
    ""main_object_caption"": ""..."",
    ""detailed_caption"": ""..."",
    ""num_sampled_frames"": 8,
    ""visual_tokens_per_frame"": 32
  },
  ""video_id_002"": {
    ...
  }
}

?? **Note**:
- Submissions that are **incomplete** or **do not follow the required format** will be **ignored without evaluation**.
- Due to the nature of the evaluation pipeline, the **leaderboard is updated once every 24 hours** based on your most recent valid submission.","# Dataset Description

