9380,isit-2025-wireless-wanderlust,"# Overview

In this year¡¯s competition¡ª**Wireless Wanderlust**¡ªwe explore a semi-supervised indoor positioning challenge. Consider an indoor environment with multiple remote antennas, each equipped with several antenna elements. These remote antennas operate over a set of orthogonal frequencies, capturing signals from users who carry communication devices. The relevant channel characteristics are collectively referred to as **Channel State Information (CSI)**.

The CSI observations are transmitted from the remote antennas to a central **fusion center** over a **grant-free random access channel**. Grant-free random access refers to a communication model in which devices transmit data **directly**, without requesting permission or waiting for explicit scheduling from a central controller. This decentralized access reduces latency and overhead but requires intelligent decision-making to ensure useful and non-redundant data reaches the fusion center.

By analyzing the received CSI from each remote antenna, the fusion center estimates the user¡¯s position. Participants are challenged to develop smart transmission and inference strategies under tight communication and model constraints.

To accommodate a wide range of participants, we have structured the competition into a series of tasks with increasing complexity. Everyone is encouraged to submit their solutions¡ªawards will be given across different levels of performance, rewarding creativity and effort from both newcomers and experienced teams alike.

The scenario under consideration is depicted below:

**[INSERT IMAGE]**
Figure 1: System setting for the ¡°Wireless Wanderlust¡±  D2I challenge. 

##Why CSI Matters
Indoor environments often present complex multipath effects, attenuation, and interference. For instance, static obstacles may affect the channel quality/multipath effects observed in the CSI. This correlation can be leveraged to localize the user. Hence, by **learning the relationship** between CSI patterns and user position (either with direct regression or more advanced neural architectures), participants can build models capable of estimating user locations from newly observed signals¡ªeven under challenging conditions.

##Why is Grant-Free Random Access?
Grant-free random access is particularly well suited for highly dynamic environments with high diversity in user participation. Instead of relying on coordination or scheduled slots, users autonomously attempt transmission based on the local dynamics. This setup mirrors realistic constraints in IoT or edge-based deployments, where centralized coordination is either impractical or too slow to respond in real-time. Also, it offers scalability and resilience as the centralized coordinator does not need to explicitly control the access to the communication resources.
----------------------
# Dataset


To facilitate accurate indoor localization, participants will use a dataset collected in a realistic indoor environment where multiple remote antennas capture **Channel State Information (CSI)**.

An example of the magnitude and phase of a CSI entry is plotted below. Different curves correspond to various transmission frequencies.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11750142%2F688beb76e2d8d536e3681c8c79086662%2Ffig1.png?generation=1743808077064789&alt=media)

Each entry in the dataset corresponds to a measurement time, along with the received signals and relevant metadata. These measurements are divided into two categories:

####1. Labeled Measurements ¨C 25%
 A set of (CSI, location, timestamp) tuples gathered during a dedicated calibration phase. The user¡¯s ground-truth position was measured via high-precision instrumentation (e.g., tachymeter or equivalent). These labeled samples demonstrate how specific CSI patterns map to known user locations.


####2. Unlabeled Measurements ¨C 75%
 A larger set of (CSI, timestamp) entries without any corresponding location label. These unlabeled data reflect real-world usage conditions, where continuous labeling isn¡¯t feasible. By learning general CSI patterns, participants can leverage these unlabeled samples to refine or regularize their models in a semi-supervised manner.


###Training, Validation, and Testing
The dataset will be split into training and validation sets that include both labeled and unlabeled samples, allowing participants to calibrate and fine-tune their models.


A separate hidden test set will evaluate final submissions to ensure fairness and robust model performance under unseen conditions.


This setup underscores the **semi-supervised** nature of the competition: you must utilize the limited labeled data effectively while also capitalizing on a broader pool of unlabeled measurements. 

Please visit the [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/isit-2025-wireless-wanderlust/data) section to download the dataset.

----------------------
# Task 1 ¨C Localization using MEDE & R90

##Overall Description of the Task
In the first task of the challenge, you must minimize two key metrics Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and the R90 which are explained in detail in the sequel. You will use both labeled and unlabeled data in a semi-supervised manner to balance accuracy and robustness. A limit of 40M trainable parameters applies, and you are encouraged to explore different training paradigm that can improve accuracy, in terms of signal processing, machine learning, and deep learning approaches. All location estimates must be derived from a single measurement data point.

##Understanding CSI Measurements:
Channel State Information (CSI) measurements characterize how wireless signals propagate from antennas through an environment.  In our scenario, a user transmits a pilot signal over multiple frequency bins which is sensed at 4 remote antenna arrays. Each remote antenna array is equipped with 8 antennas. The number of frequency bins is 16.

Accordingly, the CSI measurement is a tensor described as:
* CSI(remote antenna array idx, antenna element, frequency bin)
  * 4 remote antenna array idx
  * 8 antenna elements
  * 64 frequency bins

CSI contains rich information about position-dependent reflections, scattering, and signal strengths, making it invaluable for localization tasks.

Note that the CSI measurements are complex-valued: accordingly, the participants must decide how to manage the data format appropriately. 

##Balancing Accuracy and Reliability:
High accuracy in localization tasks is crucial, but equally critical is minimizing catastrophic failures. In many real-world applications, occasional large errors (outliers) can be far more detrimental than frequent smaller errors. Thus, it is essential to balance high precision (low MAE) with robust performance against large errors.

In Task 1, your goal is to design a deep neural network (DNN) that effectively balances two metrics:
* **Mean Absolute Error (MAE)** ¨C Average absolute distance error between true and predicted positions, reflecting overall accuracy.
* **R90** ¨C The radius of the smallest circle around the true position that encompasses 90% of the predicted locations, emphasizing reliability. Specifically, for a set of n ground-truth location coordinates and their corresponding estimated coordinates, the calculation begins by defining a circle with an initial radius of zero centered at each true position. The radius is incrementally increased until the circles collectively enclose 90% (i.e., 0.9n) of the estimated coordinates. The resulting radius, termed R90, quantifies the dispersion of the estimates and provides a robust measure of predictive accuracy and consistency.

We consider here an equal weighting of these two loss functions, giving rise to the combined loss:
Loss = ? MEDE+? R90

The performance for this task is evaluated in terms of the lowest achievable loss.

##Data and Experimental Conditions:
* **Parameter Limit:** Maximum of 40 million total parameters.
* **Data Splits:** Use a semi-supervised dataset with a labeled-to-unlabeled ratio of 25% labeled and 75% unlabeled samples.
* **Code Provided:** (i) loader, (ii) train/test split function, and (iii) loss function are provided.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11750142%2F48500e5a11bdafa0e88905a2894e353e%2FD2I%20competition%20ideas.png?generation=1747830720044735&alt=media)

----------------------
# Task 2 ¨C Trajectory-Aware  Localization

## Overall Description of the Task
In this final task, temporal continuity is incorporated into the localization process. Unlike previous tasks, positions can now be estimated using temporal CSI data, than a single data point alone.  The challenge in this task involves managing complexity arising from training over large-dimensional feature spaces, decision variables, and sequential machine learning models. Position tracking occurs over CSI observations to predict a subsequent observation. 

Task 2  extends Task 1  by incorporating temporal continuity and trajectory information into the localization process. Unlike earlier tasks, localization decisions in Task 2 are based on sequences of CSI measurements rather than single, isolated data points. This allows leveraging historical information to enhance the accuracy and reliability of position estimates.

## Temporal Continuity and Trajectory Prediction
Participants will develop models capable of processing sequences of three consecutive CSI observations to predict the subsequent (fourth) position. Effective approaches might include recurrent neural networks (RNNs), Long Short-Term Memory networks (LSTMs), Transformers, or filtering methods such as Kalman and particle filters.

## Constraints and Evaluation
* **Sequence Length:** Use sequential CSI data from consecutive measurements to predict the following position. Note that there is no constraint on the total amount of past measurements used by the model.
* **Parameter Limit:** Maximum of 100 million total parameters.
* **Code Provided:**  same as Task 1.

Participants must report performance on the same metrics as Task 1 (MAE, R90) and provide additional insights into temporal accuracy and trajectory stability. Highlight the impact of integrating temporal information on localization accuracy and robustness.

----------------------
# Task 3 ¨C Grant-free Random Access in Distributed Inference

## Overall Description of the Task
The second challenge centers on reducing the communication overhead from the remote antenna arrays to the fusion center. In particular, we assume that, upon receiving a CSI observation, each remote antenna decides whether to transmit the CSI observation to the fusion center or not. This policy should manage the total expected communication overhead toward the fusion center, meaning that the set of policies must be so that the expected number of transmissions between the remote antennas and the fusion center does not exceed a given total budget T. 

Note that the transmission policies must be decentralized, that is the policies must perform inference locally at each of the remote antennas without any further communication between antennas and/or fusion center.  The size of the local model is fixed at most 20M, while that of the central model at 60M.

Large-scale deployments can quickly overwhelm centralized systems if remote antennas transmit every measurement. Task 2 addresses this by requiring each remote antenna to independently decide whether to transmit its CSI observation to the fusion center using a decentralized local policy. The goal of this policy is to attain an expected number of features transmitted from all the remote antennas toward the fusion center. This is in order to address both communication and computational complexity constraints at the fusion center.

## Decentralized Transmission Policy
Upon receiving a CSI measurement ¨C as detailed it Task 1¨C each remote antenna implements a local neural network or heuristic to output a decision indicating whether CSI data should be transmitted to the fusion center. The decision is based solely on local data without inter-antenna or antenna-to-center communication during inference.


## Constraints and Evaluation
* **Communication Budget (T):** The expected total number of transmitted CSI observations per data point must not exceed the specified budget T. This budget is expressed in terms of features as float32. Since float32 contains 4 bytes, we can equivalently express the communication budget in terms of bytes. In this task, each feature is one entry of the CSI tensor as described it Task 1. We consider three expected communication budgets:
   * *Small Budget*: 1 transmitting remote antenna array on average 
   * *Medium Budget:* 2 transmitting remote antenna array on average
   * *Large Budget:* 3 transmitting remote antenna array on average

Note that the communication budget must be satisfied in an expected sense, that is it must be satisfied empirically over 32 inference rounds. 

* **Model Size**
   * Local Model Size: Maximum of 20M parameters per remote antenna.
   * Central Fusion Model Size: Maximum of 60M parameters.

* **Code Provided:** same as for Task 1. A function to evaluate the communication budget is also provided.

This task emphasizes practical scenarios where communication efficiency and decentralized decision-making are crucial for scalability. Remember that the expected communication budget is the total communication rate between all four antennas and the fusion center.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11750142%2Fdcd9da6cb48de221ff493b99f75940c7%2Ftask3.jpg?generation=1743811608407592&alt=media)
Fig. 3:  A visual representation of Task 3.

----------------------
# Task 4 ¨C Feature-Selection for Grant-free Random Access

## Overall Description of the Task
CSI measurements typically exhibit significant sparsity, allowing them to be compressed into fewer, more informative features. In this task, you must design a compressed representation of the CSI measurements, which is then utilized by the feature transmission policy as detailed in Task 2. In this case you can jointly design the compression rate and the transmission policy so that the total communication budget is met. 

The primary challenge here is that the CSI compression model is centrally trained on all available CSI observations and then distributed to remote antennas. This scenario reflects realistic constraints for scalability and interoperability, where data compression models are predetermined. On the contrary, local transmission politicies can be learnt on the fly.

Model sizes for this task are as follows: CSI compressor (global model) fixed at 40M parameters, local model fixed at 20M parameters, and the central model fixed at 60M parameters.

## CSI Compression and Feature Selection
Participants must design a centrally trained CSI compression model that captures the most informative aspects of the CSI measurements. The compression algorithm can consider any implementation: signal processing, machine learning, or deep learning oriented. The trained compression model is then deployed to remote antennas, while local transmission policies remain adaptable and are learned individually.
Let the compression rate of the CSI compressor be R.

## Decentralized Transmission Policy
As in Task 3, the local transmission policy at the remote antenna decides whether to transmit the compressed CSI observation. In this Task, the total transmission constraint jointly considers the  compression rate and the number of transmissions, meaning that  (i) if the compression rate is high, then more remote antennas can transmit toward the fusion center, whereas (ii) a lower transmission rate.

##Constraints and Evaluation
The communication budget is expressed in terms of features as float32. Since float32 contains 4 bytes, we can equivalently express the communication budget in terms of bytes. Note that the total size of the CSI observation at each remote antenna is obtained as (8 antennas)*(16 frequencies)(1 real+1 complex dimension)=256 features. 
The total communication rate is then obtained as the expected total number of features transmitted from all the remote antennas to the fusion center per each datapoint. 

* **Communication Budgets**
For this task, the participants are asked to evaluate the performance under three expected total communication budgets:

* *Small Budget:* **Features:** 32 features | **Bytes:** 128 bytes | **Percentage of original:** ~12.5%
* *Medium Budget:* **Features:** 64 features | **Bytes:** 256 bytes | **Percentage of original:** ~25%
* *Large Budget:* **Features:** 128 features | **Bytes:** 256 bytes | **Percentage of original:** ~50%

Note that ¨C as for Task 2 ¨C the communication budget must be satisfied empirically over 32 inference rounds.

* **Model Size**
   * Global CSI Compressor Model: Fixed at 40M parameters.
   * Local Decision Model: Fixed at 20M parameters per remote antenna.
   * Central Fusion Model: Fixed at 60M parameters.

* **Code Provided:** Same as Task 2.

Participants must present results highlighting the trade-offs between communication budget and localization accuracy, emphasizing the balance between communication efficiency and effective localization.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11750142%2F7544d3477edbf74d5d5670068281042d%2Ftask4.jpg?generation=1743811943604295&alt=media)
Fig. 4: A visual representation of Task 4.

----------------------
# Task summary table

| Task | Task Name                                                          | Model Constraints            |
|------|------------------------------------------------|------------------------------|
| 1       | Localization using MAE & R90                            | 40M total         |
| 2      | Trajectory-aware Localization                            | 100M total       |
| 3      | Grant-free Random Access in Distributed Inference | 20M local, 60M central   |
| 4      | Feature-Selection and Grant-free Random Access | 40M global compressor, 20M local, 60M central |

----------------------
# Further Scenario Details

In this scenario, each user is equipped with a **single transmit antenna**, while we have four remote antennas, each with eight elements, operating across 16 OFDM frequencies. The dataset¡ªcalled **dichasus-cf0x**¡ªcomes from the University of Stuttgart¡¯s DICHASUS channel sounder. All measurements are phase synchronous, offering a realistic environment for advanced signal processing and semi-supervised learning. The x coordinate is in the range (-12, 2) and the y coordinate is in the rate (-14, -2), all coordinates are given in meters, and full diagrams will be provided to illustrate sensor placement and user paths.
----------------------
# Submission Requirements

1. **Performance Metrics:** Submit results that show the performance metrics (MAE and R90) you achieve with your approach.
2. **Code:** Provide `.py` files only. Clearly comment your solutions.
3. **Trained Models:** Save them in `models/`, e.g., `model.save(...)` for TensorFlow or `torch.save(...)` for PyTorch.
4. **Dependencies:** Include a simple `packages.txt` (e.g., `pip freeze > packages.txt`).
5. **README:** Briefly explain how to set up and run your code, and describe how you respect constraints (e.g., parameter limits, communication budgets).

We look forward to your creative strategies that unify accuracy, communication efficiency, and temporal coherence. Good luck!
----------------------
# References

* https://dichasus.inue.uni-stuttgart.de/tutorials/tutorial/dissimilarity-metric-channelcharting/
* https://github.com/Jeija/ToA-AoA-Augmented-ChannelCharting/
* https://github.com/Jeija/Geodesic-Uncertainty-Loss-ChannelCharting

