**Contact Information**
* For general inquiries regarding competition organization: contact Luca, Stefano, or Farhad.
* For dataset and evaluation-related questions: reach out to Florian and Phillip.
* For technical issues related to Kaggle or registration: contact Marian or Esteban.","# Dataset Description

The DICHASUS dataset provides real-world wireless channel measurements collected in an industrial environment, specifically designed for research in distributed antenna systems and channel characterization. The dataset was captured at the Arena2036 research factory campus, creating a unique opportunity to study both line-of-sight (LoS) and non-line-of-sight (NLoS) channels in an authentic industrial setting.

This dataset features measurements from four distributed antenna arrays, each containing 8 antennas, positioned at the corners of an L-shaped area with a scatterer between them.


The full measurement dataset is provided online [here](https://dichasus.inue.uni-stuttgart.de/datasets/data/dichasus-cf0x/)

Various tutorials are provided [here](https://dichasus.inue.uni-stuttgart.de/tutorials/)


## DataLoader
We provide code that participants may use to load the dataset and generate basic visualizations. Additionally, a function is included to compute the MAE and R90 localization performance metrics. The code is available at the following links: 

- [Getting Started Notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/tebs89/gettingstarted)
- [dichasus-cf0x.py](https://www.kaggle.com/code/tebs89/dichasus-cf0x-py)
- [dichasus-evaluation.py](https://www.kaggle.com/code/tebs89/dichasus-evaluation-py)

>We express our gratitude to the the Professor Stephan ten Brink, Florian Euchner, and Phillip Stephan for all their effort in building and documenting this dataset and the DataLoader code, and for their willingness to share it with the community.",False,True
9381,taxi-trips-wash-u-scot-573-spring-2025,"# Overview



----------------------
# Description

Welcome to the Spring 2025 SCOT 573 Kaggle data competition!

Your goal is to predict the number of taxi trips originating during each hour of the year by a major taxicab company/network operating in an unspecified major world city. I've compiled (and cleaned) a dataset covering one actual year's worth of data for you to explore, model, and make predictions. See the Data tab and the Rules tab for more details.

P.S.: I won't tell you just now which city, which year, or which taxicab company/network the dataset comes from (you can ask me after the competition is over :). I recommend you don't spend time trying to identify the source of the dataset - in real life the unknown data is future data that we don't know at the time we perform the analysis.

The purpose of this exercise is to provide you with an opportunity to try out your awesome data analysis / data science - in particular, predictive analytics - on a real-life dataset. Try to be creative, practice some coding, and have fun. 

Here is a handy link to [Kaggle's competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

----------------------
# Evaluation

You'll need to upload a csv file with your predictions on the test set. The submission format must be identical to that of the sample_submission.csv file provided. Kaggle will automatically score the submission for accuracy based on a ""root mean square error"" metric. Kaggle can score your submissions because I have provided it with a hidden ""solutions"" file that contains the true num_trips for each hour in the test set. In fact, the test set hours are randomly divided into two halves: Public and Private. While the competition is live, only the public set is used to calculate the root mean square error metric. That determines the public leaderboard displayed throughout the competition. You can use that to fine tune your approach and compare with other teams. You are allowed 5 new submission every 24 hour period (following UTC time) between the launch of the competition and its deadline. 

At the deadline you get to choose, or Kaggle chooses, your best submission to date. This is then evaluated on the private test observations to determine the private leaderboard. It is that private leaderboard that determines the competition winners. The purpose of this setup is to minimize the role of luck and random trial and error. 

The competition deadline is Sunday, March 30th, at 11:59 p.m. St. Louis time. The private leaderboard will be published on Monday, March 31st, at noon. The leading teams will discuss their prediction approaches during class that afternoon. When the competition launches, there is one solution already evaluated based on the sample_submission file. This is essentially a random prediction file so it shouldn't be too hard to beat that benchmark!","# Dataset Description

You have access to a train dataset that constitutes a 70% random sample of all hours in the year. Each row corresponds to an hour and each column represents a possibly relevant temporal or weather-related information. Your team can do whatever analysis it deems useful to explore and model this dataset. I recommend using Python on your computer instead of Kaggle's built-in coding ""kernels"".

You also have access to a test dataset that contains the remaining 30% of the hours. In the test set, you have exactly the same columns as the train set except that it doesn't include the number of trips. This is the sample you need to make predictions for.

Ultimately you'll need to upload a csv file with your predictions (see the instructions below and the file). Kaggle will automatically score the submission for accuracy based on a ""root mean square error"" metric. See the evaluation section for more details.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9382,mlx-2-0-classification,"# Overview

**Welcome to the *mlX 2.0 Classification Challenge*!**  

In this competition, your task is to predict which team (A or B) will win a CSGO round based on in-game statistics at a given moment. Using machine learning, you¡¯ll analyze real match data¡ªincluding player health, economy, weapons, and round state¡ªto forecast the outcome before the round concludes.  

The dataset captures actual competitive matches, and your goal is to build the most accurate predictive model possible. Will your algorithm call the round winner before the final bullet is fired?  

Good luck, and may the odds be in your favour!  

---
**P.S.: Haven¡¯t registered yet?**
Secure your spot now at: [https://bit.ly/4hQ17NS](https://bit.ly/4hQ17NS)
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **F1-Score**, which balances both precision and recall. The F1-Score is calculated as:

<pre>
F1 = 2 ¡Á (Precision ¡Á Recall) / (Precision + Recall)
</pre>

Where:
- **Precision** = TP/(TP + FP)
- **Recall** = TP/(TP + FN)
- \(TP\) = True Positives
- \(FP\) = False Positives  
- \(FN\) = False Negatives

You can easily compute the F1-Score using scikit-learn:

```python
from sklearn.metrics import f1_score
f1_score(y_true, y_pred)
```

**Note:** The F1-Score will be calculated on the **private leaderboard** to determine the final rankings.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict a probability for the `target` variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,target
    2,A
    5,B
    6,A
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files Provided
1. **train.csv** - The training set containing historical CS:GO round data with known outcomes
2. **test.csv** - The test set containing CS:GO round data without outcomes (for prediction)
3. **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file demonstrating the required format

## Prediction Target
Your goal is to predict which team (A or B) will win the current CS:GO round based on the given in-game statistics. The target variable is:
- `target`: Binary classification (A or B) indicating the predicted winning team

## Data Columns
Each row represents a snapshot of a CS:GO round at a specific moment in time.

### Identifier
- `id`: Unique identifier for each round observation

### Temporal Features
- `remaining_time`: Time remaining in the round (seconds)
- `B_score`: Current score of Team B
- `A_score`: Current score of Team A

### Map Features
- `location`: Map name (e.g., de_nuke)
- `device_active`: Boolean indicating if a bomb/device is currently active
- `map_anomaly_detected`: Indicator of unusual map events

### Team Statistics
For both Team A and Team B:
- `_health`: Total health points of alive players (sum)
- `_armor`: Total armor points of alive players (sum)
- `_money`: Team's total money available
- `_helmets`: Number of helmets equipped
- `_players_alive`: Number of players currently alive
- `_defuse_kits`: Number of defuse kits (Team B only)

### Weapon Inventory
For both Team A and Team B:
- `_Pistol`: Number of pistols equipped
- `_Shotgun`: Number of shotguns equipped
- `_SMG`: Number of SMGs equipped
- `_Sniper`: Number of sniper rifles equipped
- `_Heavy`: Number of heavy weapons equipped
- `_Rifle`: Number of rifles equipped
- `_total_grenades`: Total number of grenades carried by team

### Audio/Environmental Features
- `player_footstep_volume`: Audio intensity of player footsteps
- `voice_line_frequency`: Frequency of in-game character voice lines
- `chicken_deaths`: Number of chicken deaths (environmental feature)

## Data Format
- All files are in CSV format
- Missing values are represented by empty cells or NaN
- Boolean values are represented as True/False
- Weapon counts are integers (when present)
- Health/armor/money values are continuous

## Important Notes
1. The data represents real competitive CS:GO matches
2. Each row is an independent observation from different rounds/matches
3. Team A and Team B designations are arbitrary and don't indicate any specific team
4. Some features may have missing values that require handling
5. The `target` column is only present in the training data

## Evaluation Metric
Submissions will be evaluated using the **F1-Score**, which balances precision and recall in binary classification. The metric will be calculated as:
```
F1 = 2 ¡Á (Precision ¡Á Recall) / (Precision + Recall)
```",False,True
9383,mlx-2-0-regression,"# Overview

**Welcome to *mlX 2.0 Regression Challenge*!**  

In this competition, your mission is to **predict a song¡¯s popularity score (0-100)** using real-world music data¡ªfrom audio features and artist stats to track metadata. Using machine learning, you¡¯ll analyze trends in danceability, energy, valence, and more to train a model that forecasts how listeners will react before a song even hits the charts.  

The dataset includes **real Billboard-tracked tracks**, and your goal is to build the most accurate regression model possible. Can your algorithm decode the secret formula behind viral hits, or will it flop harder than a one-hit wonder?  

**The Challenge:**  
- Predict continuous popularity scores (not just hits/flops).  
- Uncover hidden patterns in audio and artist data.  

**Good luck, and may your model top the (leaderboard) charts!**

---
**P.S.: Haven¡¯t registered yet?**
Secure your spot now at: [https://bit.ly/4hQ17NS](https://bit.ly/4hQ17NS)
----------------------
# Evaluation

The competition will be judged using **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)**, which measures the difference between predicted and actual popularity scores. Lower RMSE values indicate better performance.  

### **RMSE Formula**:
<pre>
RMSE = ¡Ì(mean((y_true - y_pred)2)
</pre>

Where:  
- **y_true** = Actual popularity scores (0-100)  
- **y_pred** = Your model¡¯s predictions  

### **Implementation**:
```python
from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error
import numpy as np

rmse = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_true, y_pred))
```
**Note**:  The final leaderboard will rank submissions by RMSE on a **hidden test set**. 

---

## **Submission Format**  
Your submission file must contain two columns with a header:  
1. `id` (identifier for each test sample)  
2. `target` (your predicted popularity score, 0-100)  

Example:  
```
id,target
1001,67
1002,42
1003,89
```","# Dataset Description

