2. **test.csv** - The test set containing music track data without popularity scores (for prediction)
3. **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file demonstrating the required format

## Prediction Target
Your goal is to predict the popularity score of music tracks/albums based on their audio characteristics and metadata. The target variable is:
- `target`: Continuous popularity score (higher values indicate more popular tracks/albums)

## Data Columns
Each row represents a single music release with its audio features and derived characteristics.

### Track Identification
- `track_identifier`: Official title of the primary track or album
- `creator_collective`: List of credited artists
- `publication_timestamp`: Release date (YYYY-MM-DD format)
- `album_component_count`: Total tracks in the release

### Core Audio Features (Tracks 0-2)
For the first three tracks on the release:
- `composition_label_[0-2]`: Track titles
- `duration_ms_[0-2]`: Duration in milliseconds
- `rhythmic_cohesion_[0-2]`: Danceability (0-1)
- `intensity_index_[0-2]`: Energy level (0-1)
- `harmonic_scale_[0-2]`: Musical key (0-11)
- `tonal_mode_[0-2]`: Modality (0=minor, 1=major)
- `organic_texture_[0-2]`: Acousticness (0-1)
- `beat_frequency_[0-2]`: Tempo in BPM
- `time_signature_[0-2]`: Meter (e.g., 3=3/4, 4=4/4)

### Derived Audio Metrics
- `emotional_charge_[0-2]`: Valence ¡Á Energy product
- `groove_efficiency_[0-2]`: Energy/Danceability ratio
- `organic_immersion_[0-2]`: Acousticness ¡Á Duration
- `duration_consistency`: Std dev of track durations
- `tempo_volatility`: BPM range across tracks
- `key_variety`: Unique keys among first 3 tracks

### Contextual Features
- `album_name_length`: Character length of track title
- `artist_count`: Number of credited artists
- `weekday_of_release`: Day of week (Monday-Sunday)
- `season_of_release`: Seasonal quarter
- `lunar_phase`: Moon phase at release

## Data Format
- CSV format with header row
- Missing values represented as empty cells or NaN
- Continuous features scaled 0-1 unless otherwise noted
- Temporal features in ISO 8601 format (YYYY-MM-DD)
- Categorical features as strings

## Important Notes
1. Data represents diverse music releases across genres and eras
2. All audio features are extracted from signal processing algorithms
3. The `target` column is only present in training data

## Evaluation Metric
Submissions will be evaluated using **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)**:
```
RMSE = ¡Ì(1/n ¦²(y_pred - y_true)2)
```
Where:
- `y_pred` = your predicted popularity scores
- `y_true` = actual popularity values
- `n` = number of predictions

Lower RMSE values indicate better model performance.",False,True
9384,udea-ai-4-eng-20251-pruebas-saber-pro-colombia,"# Overview

Las Pruebas Saber Pro son ex¨¢menes estandarizados que se administran en Colombia para evaluar la calidad y el nivel de conocimiento y competencias de los estudiantes de educaci¨®n superior, es decir, de instituciones de educaci¨®n superior como universidades y tecnol¨®gicos. Estas pruebas son parte de los esfuerzos del Gobierno de Colombia para monitorear y mejorar la calidad de la educaci¨®n superior en el pa¨ªs.

Estas Pruebas constan cinco componentes gen¨¦ricos, Ingl¨¦s, Lectura Cr¨ªtica, Competencias Ciudadanas, Razonamiento Cuantitativo y Comunicaci¨®n Escrita.

**Tu tarea ser¨¢ crear un modelo de clasificaci¨®n que para cada estudiante prediga qu¨¦ desempe?o va a tener:** *bajo*, *medio-bajo*, *medio-alto* o *alto*.
----------------------
# Description

El conjunto de datos contiene m¨¢s de 50 columnas que describen de manera distintos aspectos de cada estudiante, incluyendo:

- *Informaci¨®n sociecon¨®mica*: Describen caracter¨ªsticas sociecon¨®micas del estudiante como su estrato, educaci¨®n de sus padres, estrato, entre otras.

- *Informaci¨®n de instituciones*: Describen las instituciones de donde provienen los estudiantes.

- *Informaci¨®n del estudiante*: Describe particularidades del estudiante como su edad, que programa estudian, la modalidad de estudio, etc.

- *Informaci¨®n estad¨ªstica*: Describe algunos coeficientes que equipos de estudio han desarrollado que podr¨ªa ayudar a la clasificaci¨®n.

As¨ª como muchos otros datos que ayudan a clasificar de manera precisa los niveles de desempe?o
----------------------
# Evaluation

Esto es una tarea de clasificaci¨®n multi-clase con 4 clases.

La m¨¦trica de desempe?o ser¨¢ el **accuracy**, es decir, el porcentaje de aciertos que tiene tu modelo


## Submission File

Por cada **ID** en el fichero `test.csv`, se debe clasificar el desempe?o para la variable **RENDIMIENTO_GLOBAL**. El archivo debe contener un encabezado y tener el siguiente formato:

```python
ID,RENDIMIENTO_GLOBAL
550236,bajo
98545,alto
499179,medio-bajo
etc.

```
Ten en cuenta que su submission file debe tener los **MISMOS IDs** que estan en el `test.csv`, pues son estos los estudiantes con los que se evaluar¨¢ el rendimiento de su modelo","# Dataset Description

# Files
**train.csv** - Este archivo contiene todas las columnas que describen al estudiante, junto con las etiquetas de clasificaci¨®n de desempe?o RENDIMIENTO_GLOBAL, y el ID de cada estudiante ID

**test.csv** - Este archivo contiene todas las columnas que describen al estudiante, sin las etiquetas de clasificaci¨®n de desempe?o, pues son estos los estudiantes que tienen que clasificar, contiene ID de cada estudiante ID

**sample_submission.csv** - Este archivo muestra un ejemplo que ilustra como deben formatear su archivo de env¨ªo.

# Columns
`ID` - ID ¨²nico de cada estudiante
`RENDIMIENTO_GLOBAL` - Rendimiento del estudiante en la prueba",False,True
9385,stocks-return-prediction-shor-term,"# Overview

Use the given features to predict future return.
----------------------
# Description

You will get 2 dataset (training set and test set). Use the training set to train your model and predict with the features data in the test set.

When you think you've got a pretty good version, you can submit it. Everyone can submit repeatedly, with no limit on the number of submissions.
----------------------
# Evaluation

When we get your submitting, we will use Rank Information Coefficient (Rank IC) to evaluate the algorithm:

$$IC = \frac{\sum_{t=1}^TCorr(rank(\hat{y_t}), rank(y_t))}{T}$$","# Dataset Description

There are three files.

## Files

###    **train_data.pkl** - the training set
###    **test_data.pkl** - the test set

#### Columns

*   `code` - stock codes (totally random)
*   `date` - dates in order
*   `f_0~f_18`?- features
*   `y`?- targets (only in the training set)


###   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

#### Columns

*   `id` - range(len(sample_submission))
*   `code` - stock codes
*   `date` - dates in order
*   `y_pred`?- results of prediction",False,True
9386,valorant-agent-detection,"# Overview

Valorant is a tactical first-person shooter where players control unique characters called **agents**. In this competition, your task is to build a computer vision model that can **detect and classify four key agents** ¡ª **Phoenix**, **Jett**, **Brimstone**, and **Sage** ¡ª in gameplay frames.

You'll be working with a curated dataset of screenshots extracted from gameplay, accompanied by **Pascal VOC-style annotations** that define bounding boxes around visible agents. The bounding boxes are provided as **separate XML files**, not drawn on the images themselves.

This challenge is perfect for those interested in object detection, video understanding, and gaming analytics.

Can your model accurately spot the agents amidst the chaos of battle?
----------------------
# Description

## ?? Introduction

In this competition, your task is to build an object detection model that identifies Valorant agents (Phoenix, Jett, Brimstone, and Sage) in gameplay screenshots. This problem involves computer vision techniques and is designed to give you hands-on experience with real-world image data.

## ?? Objective

Create a model that accurately detects the four agent classes in test images using bounding boxes. The model's performance will be evaluated using mean Average Precision (mAP@0.5).

## ?? Dataset

The dataset consists of 2825 images taken from custom Valorant game sessions. Each image contains one or more visible agents, annotated in Pascal VOC XML format. Only the following classes are used:

- Phoenix
- Jett
- Brimstone
- Sage

All images are resized to 640x640 pixels.

## ?? Evaluation
The evaluation metric is mean Intersection over Union (mean IoU).
For each predicted bounding box that matches a ground-truth box (same class and not previously matched), the IoU is computed.
All matched and unmatched predictions and ground-truth boxes are included in the calculation.

## ?? Submission Format

Submit a `.csv` file with two columns:

| Id         | PredictionString                                         |
|------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| img_2303   | Phoenix 354 73 474 389 Sage 175 77 249 356               |
| img_2314   | Brimstone 305 264 398 577                                |
 
<br />
See more details are in the data description. 

## ? Timeline

- Competition Start: April 15, 2025  
- Final Submission Deadline: May 10, 2025  
- Results Announcement: May 11, 2025

## ????? Organizers

This competition is organized as part of the **University of Tartu** course **Deep Learning for Computer Vision** (Spring 2025).
----------------------
# Evaluation


Submissions are evaluated using mean Intersection over Union (mean IoU), computed across all test images and all predicted bounding boxes.

For each predicted box, its IoU (Intersection over Union) is computed with a ground truth box of the same class. A prediction is considered matched if its class label matches and it has the highest IoU with an unused ground truth box. Unmatched predictions and ground truth boxes are penalized with IoU = 0.

The final score is the mean IoU across all predicted and ground truth boxes in the evaluated dataset.

#Rules
- Valid classes: Phoenix, Jett, Brimstone, Sage
(All other classes are ignored)

- Confidence scores are not considered during evaluation ¡ª all bounding boxes are assumed to have a fixed confidence of 1.0. Only the class labels and bounding box coordinates are used to calculate Intersection over Union (IoU).

- Predictions that do not match any ground-truth box (by class or IoU) are counted as false and assigned IoU = 0

- Missing predictions for ground-truth objects are also penalized as IoU = 0

- Predictions with invalid coordinates are not discarded, but will result in low or zero IoU","# Dataset Description


## ?? Files

The dataset includes the following:

- `train.zip` ¡ª contains annotated training images and corresponding Pascal VOC `.xml` annotation files.
- `test.zip` ¡ª contains test images without annotations.
- `sample_submission.csv`¡ª CSV file with 2 columns:  
`Id`, `PredictionString` ¡ª where `PredictionString` contains space-separated predictions in the format:  
`ClassName xmin ymin xmax ymax` (one or more times).  
Includes two example rows for two test images.
 
Each image is a screenshot from the game **Valorant**, and has resolution **640x640** pixels.

## ?? Annotation Format

The training annotations are provided in **Pascal VOC XML** format. Each `.xml` file describes the objects (agents) detected in the image.

Each object in the annotation file has:

- `name` ¡ª the class label: one of `Phoenix`, `Jett`, `Brimstone`, or `Sage`
- `bndbox` ¡ª the bounding box coordinates: `xmin`, `ymin`, `xmax`, `ymax`

Example (simplified):

```xml
<annotation>
    <folder>images</folder>
    <filename>image_001.png</filename>
    <source>
        <database>MyDatabase</database>
        <annotation>COCO2017</annotation>
        <image>flickr</image>
        <flickrid>NULL</flickrid>
        <annotator>1</annotator>
    </source>
    <owner>
        <flickrid>NULL</flickrid>
        <name>Label Studio</name>
    </owner>
    <size>
        <width>640</width>
        <height>640</height>
        <depth>3</depth>
    </size>
    <segmented>0</segmented>
    <object>
        <name>Sage</name>
        <pose>Unspecified</pose>
        <truncated>0</truncated>
        <difficult>0</difficult>
        <bndbox>
            <xmin>473</xmin>
            <ymin>312</ymin>
            <xmax>552</xmax>
            <ymax>474</ymax>
        </bndbox>
    </object>
</annotation>
```

## ?? What are you predicting?

You are predicting bounding boxes and class labels for all agents in each test image. Predictions must be submitted in CSV format (see below).

## ?? Submission Format

Submit a `.csv` file with two columns:

| Id         | PredictionString                                         |
|------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| img_2303   | Phoenix 354 73 474 389 Sage 175 77 249 356               |
| img_2314   | Brimstone 305 264 398 577                                |

- `Id` ¡ª image filename without extension.
- `PredictionString` ¡ª a string containing all predictions for that image.
 
Each prediction must include:

`<label>` `<xmin>` `<ymin>` `<xmax>` `<ymax>`

Multiple predictions for one image are separated by spaces.

Each row corresponds to a single image.

## ?? Class labels

There are exactly 4 classes in the dataset:

- `Phoenix`
- `Jett`
- `Brimstone`
- `Sage`

## ? What about empty predictions?

If your model has **no prediction for a given image**, you must still include a **placeholder** in the submission to follow the format and avoid errors during evaluation.

For an empty prediction, submit: `None 0 0 1 1`

### Example of empty row in `submission.csv`:

| Id    | PredictionString              |
|----------|---------------------------------|
| img_0001   | None 0 0 1 1                    |",False,True
9387,the-new-era-of-competition,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the yield of wild blueberries. Good luck!

## NOTE:

You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error),

![MAE](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visipedia/iwildcam_comp/master/assets/MAE.png)

where each `x_i` represents the predicted target, `y_i` represents the ground truth, and `n` is the number of rows in the test set.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `yield`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,yield
    15289,6025.194
    15290,1256.223
    15291,357.44
    etc.",,False,True
