9390,bert-classification-ioai,"# Description

<h1> Task </h1>
This is a binary classification task that requires determining whether a tweet contains a mention of an adverse drug reaction (ADR) to a medication (labeled as ""1"") or not (labeled as ""0"").

<h1> Dataset </h1>
The train portion of the dataset consists of 9,515 tweets related to health conditions. These tweets have been labeled based on whether they mention an adverse side effect associated with medication. The dataset was created as part of the Social Media Mining for Health Applications (#SMM4H) Shared Tasks ¡ª a track focused on automatically extracting information about drug side effects from tweets. The dataset was prepared in collaboration with the UPenn HLP Center and the Laboratory of Chemoinformatics and Molecular Modeling (KFU). The dataset is publicly available on the Yandex Toloka website (https://toloka.ai/ru/datasets). The test portion of the dataset contains 1,504 tweets and was specifically annotated for this competition. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Evaluation metric: <strong>F1-measure</strong></p>

<hr>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every tweet in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: tweet id and class. 
</p><p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,class<br>760402871867367424,0<br></pre>
----------------------
# Baseline

The source code for the weak baseline based on Recurrent Neural Networks can be downloaded from
[link](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1GsGundHdGZ0NyGx769IYLZJOmOPCXKzf/view?usp=sharing)","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.tsv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.tsv</strong> - the test set</li>
</ul>

<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - tweet id</li>
<li><strong>tweet</strong> - the text of a tweet</li>
<li><strong>class</strong> - the label 1 (ADR) or 0 (no-ADR)</li>
</ul>",False,True
9391,team-16-codebreakers,"# Overview

# **???? Fruit Similarity Challenge: Object Detection Competition**

Fruits may look simple, but distinguishing between visually similar ones is a challenge, even for humans! In this competition, your goal is to build an object detection model that accurately identifies and classifies nearly identical fruits in images.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Mean Average Precision (mAP)](https://www.v7labs.com/blog/mean-average-precision). For each class, Average Precision (AP) is calculated by plotting the precision-recall curve and computing the area under the curve. mAP is then obtained by averaging the AP scores across all object classes. This metric captures how well the predicted bounding boxes match the ground truth, both in terms of spatial overlap (measured by Intersection over Union, IoU) and correct class labels. A higher mAP indicates better overall performance in detecting and classifying objects.

The evaluation is done on the testing set that is equally divided into public and private sets. Public test set results are available immediately after the submission. Private test set results will be shown after the competition deadline.

You are allowed to make 5 submissions per day.

## Submission File
For submission file structure, look it up in the [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/team-16-codebreakers/data) tab.","# Dataset Description

The dataset consist of images of 8 different fruits/berries: **peach, nectarine, orange, mandarin, lingonberry, cranberry, plum and apricot** (mapped 0 to 7 in this order). It is guaranteed that each image contains exactly one type of fruit/berry. There are roughly 250 images per each class. And the total dataset of 2018 images has been divided randomly into train and test set. 
As berries are usually handled in bunch, it would be impractical to detect them one by one. So **each image has exactly one bounding box**, which contains all of the fruits/berries in that picture. The images were collected and labelled by different individuals: one person labelled peaches and nectarines, another handled oranges and mandarins, a third lingonberries and cranberries, and another plums and apricots.
Some examples of bounding boxes:
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11603593%2Fde748025a4724a5bcd10c128fe10f625%2Fnectarine.png?generation=1744273439929666&alt=media)
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11603593%2F04b022b0ce0fdcd29d461d596d45234f%2Flb.png?generation=1744273546931855&alt=media)

Image files have different sizes, so keep that in mind while processing the data.

## Files

*  **data/train/images/** - Contains the training images. Each in .jpg or .png format.
*  **data/test/images/** - Contains the testing images. Each in .jpg or .png format.
*   **train.csv** - Bounding box info of all training images.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file in the correct format.

## Columns in train.csv and sample_submission.csv
*   `filename` - The name of the image file the bounding box corresponds to (e.g., 7d23404a-image_4.jpg).
*   `xmin`?- The x-coordinate of the top-left corner of the bounding box.
*   `ymin`?- The y-coordinate of the top-left corner of the bounding box.
*   `xmax`?- The x-coordinate of the bottom-right corner of the bounding box.
*   `ymax`?- The y-coordinate of the bottom-right corner of the bounding box.
*   `class`?- The class label of the fruits/berries within the bounding box (integer-encoded).",False,True
9392,robot-predictive-maintenance-2025-i,"# Overview

**IMPORTANT Notice: There is an update on the testing dataset on 16/02/2024. Please update your dataset and resubmit your results.**

This data challenge is used for the evaluation of the course ""Predictive Maintenance"" at Centralesup¨¦lec, Universit¨¦ Paris-Saclay. This is the phase I of a two-phase data challenge.

In this phase, the aim is to develop models to predict the remaining useful life of an industrial robot, based on a synthetic dataset containing three failure modes, including degradation failure and also random failure. As an evaluation of this phase, you will be given a testing dataset of the same format as the training dataset, and you have to use your model to predict for each sample in the testing dataset, if it can survive for the next $6$ months.

In the next phase, you will make use of the model you developed to make optimal operation planning for a fleet of $12$ robots: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/predictive-maintenance-of-a-robot-ii
----------------------
# Description

## Failure modes

From historical knowledge, the robot has three main failure modes:

- Infant mortality: This failure mode is mainly due to manufacturing defects. Only a small fraction of the population might be subject to this failure mode. But if infant mortality occurs, the sample fails very early in its lifecycle.
- Failure of control boards: This failure mode appears to be random and can only be described by a probabilistic distribution.
- Fatigue crack growth: This failure mode is due to the generation and growth of fatigue cracks. When the length of the crack grows beyond a threshold, a failure occurs. This is a degradation failure and the length of the crack can be monitored periodically, i.e., every month.

## Physical model for the crack growth process

It is assumed that the crack growth process has a shape of logistic function:

$$y(t) = \frac{\beta_2}{1+e^{-(\beta_0 + \beta_1 t)}} $$

where $y(t)$ is the crack length in arbitrary units, $t$ is time in months and $\beta_0, \beta_1, \beta_2$ are parameters related to material properties. 

A failure occurs when 

$$y(t) > y_{th}$$

where $y_{th}=0.85.$

## Process noise, observation noise, and state space models

Due to process noise, the parameters $\beta_0, \beta_1, \beta_2$ might change values slightly overtime. Also, due to the limitation of the measurement equipment, the measured crack length is affected by large measurement noise. 

The crack length is measured every $1$ month. The following state space model is used to capture the process and observation uncertainty:

$$z_k = y_k + \epsilon_k $$

where

$$y_k = \frac{\beta_{2,k}}{1+e^{-(\beta_{0,k} + \beta_{1,k} t_k)}} $$ and

$$ \beta_{2,k} = \beta_{2,k-1} + \omega_{2, k-1}$$
$$ \beta_{1,k} = \beta_{1,k-1} + \omega_{1, k-1}$$
$$ \beta_{0,k} = \beta_{0,k-1} + \omega_{0, k-1}$$

The observation noise 

$$\epsilon_k \sim Normal(0, 0.05)$$

The process noise

$$\omega_{2, k-1} \sim Normal(0, 0.01)$$
$$\omega_{1, k-1} \sim Normal(0, 0.001)$$
$$\omega_{0, k-1} \sim Normal(0, 0.01)$$
----------------------
# Evaluation

You will be given a testing dataset of $50$ robots, where each robot is measured for crack growth at a frequency of every 1 month. You will have to predict the remaining useful life of each robot in the testing dataset, and make a decision whether the robot will survive the next $6$ months based on your predicted RUL.

## Submission format

For each ID in the test set, you must predict a label of 0/1 where:
- $1$ means it will fail in the next $6$ months,
- $0$ means otherwise.

## Evaluation metric

The participant is asked to predict whether the RUL of an item is less than 6 months: 
- label = 1, if RUL<=6 and 
- label = 0, otherwise.

If the predicted label matches the ground truth, a reward of $2$ will be given.
If it does not match, then,
- A penalty of -4 will be given, if truth is 1 and prediction is 0;
- A penalty of -1/60*true_rul will be given, if truth is 0 and prediction is 1.

The evaluation metric is calculated as following:

$$perf = \sum_{i=1}^n Reward_i$$

where $Reward_i$ is calculated as mentioned before.

The following function calculate the evaluation metric:

```
import pandas as pd
import pandas.api.types

def score(solution: pd.DataFrame, submission: pd.DataFrame, row_id_column_name: str) -> float:
    '''
    This metric is customized to measure the performance of remaining useful life prediction. 
    The participant is asked to predict whether the RUL of an item is less than 6 months: 1 - if RUL<=6 and 0 otherwise.
    In the ground truth file ""Solution.csv"", there will be a column ""true_rul"" as well as a column ""label"".
    If the predicted label matches the ground truth, a reward of 2 will be given.
    If it does not match, then,
    - A penalty of -4 will be given, if truth is 1 and prediction is 0;
    - A penalty of -1/60*true_rul will be given, if truth is 0 and prediction is 1.

    TODO: Add unit tests. We recommend using doctests so your tests double as usage demonstrations for competition hosts.
    https://docs.python.org/3/library/doctest.html
    # This example doctest works for mean absolute error:
    >>> import pandas as pd
    >>> row_id_column_name = ""item_index""
    >>> solution_data = {'item_index': [0, 1, 2, 3], 'label': [1, 0, 1, 0], 'true_rul': [5, 20, 1, 6]}
    >>> submission_data = {'item_index': [0, 1, 2, 3], 'label': [1, 0, 0, 0]}
    >>> solution = pd.DataFrame(solution_data)
    >>> submission = pd.DataFrame(submission_data)
    >>> score(solution.copy(), submission.copy(), row_id_column_name)
    2
    '''

    # Initialize rewards and penalties
    reward = 2
    penalty_false_positive = -1/60
    penalty_false_negative = -4

    # Compare labels and calculate rewards/penalties
    rewards_penalties = []
    for _, (sol_label, sub_label, true_rul) in enumerate(zip(solution['label'], submission['label'], solution['true_rul'])):
        if sol_label == sub_label:
            rewards_penalties.append(reward)
        elif sol_label == 1 and sub_label == 0:
            rewards_penalties.append(penalty_false_negative)
        elif sol_label == 0 and sub_label == 1:
            rewards_penalties.append(penalty_false_positive * true_rul)
        else:
            rewards_penalties.append(0)  # No reward or penalty if labels don't match   
    
    return sum(rewards_penalties)
```","# Dataset Description

In this page, you will find the training dataset, testing dataset, and a csv file which you can use as a template for submitting your result.

