The dataset for the proposed challenge is available. The MiGA challenge is planned as a continuous annual event. This year¡¯s challenge will use the **iMiGUE dataset**, published in CVPR 2021.

The Micro-Gesture Understanding and Emotion Analysis (iMiGUE) dataset comprises 359 long video sequences¡ªranging from 0.5 to 26 minutes in duration¡ªcollected from post-match press conferences of professional tennis players. In total, the dataset contains approximately 3,765,600 frames, capturing a wide range of human behavioral cues, including facial expressions, body gestures, and micro-gestures.

For this competition, participants are permitted to use RGB video data to train their models.

**[Details about iMiGUE dataset](https://github.com/linuxsino/iMiGUE)**

