9413,cas-bdai-cup-2025,"# Overview

Happy Coding!
----------------------
# Description

Craigslist is the world's largest collection of used vehicles for sale, yet it's very difficult to collect all of them in the same place. I built a scraper for a school project and expanded upon it later to create this dataset which includes every used vehicle entry within the United States on Craigslist.

Can you predict the price of the used cars listed on craigslist? Build a model that minimizes RMSE!
----------------------
# Evaluation

The submissions get rated by RMSE. The submissions with the lowest RMSE wins!","# Dataset Description

The dataset has bee collected from craigslist. For the purpose of the CAS BDAI Cup the data has been shortened and pre-processed a bit. The original dataset can be found here
## Descriptions of the data sets
- **train_data.csv** Complete training data (X and y) for building models
- **test_data.csv**: X values for the testing data, y values (are ""unknown"" and saved within kaggle for scoring)
## Descriptions of the variables
- **Id** Instance ID
- **price** Price of the car in USD
- **year** Production date of car
- **brand** String indicating the car manufacturer
- **model** String indicating the model name 
- **transmission** String indicating the transmission type (automatic vs. manual etc.)
- **mileage** Total distance the car has been running in miles
- **fuelType** String indicating type of fuel the car runs with
- **tax** Road tax to be paid for using the car on public streets
- **mpg** MilesPerGallon indicating how many miles a car can travel for every gallon (4.55 litres)
- **engineSize** Size of the engine in liters",False,True
9414,amia-public-challenge-2025,"# Overview

Challenge based on a sub-set of the Ving Big Data Chest X-ray Challenge 

https://www.kaggle.com/c/vinbigdata-chest-xray-abnormalities-detection/

----------------------
# Description

In this competition, you have to automatically localize and classify 14 types of thoracic abnormalities from chest radiographs. The subset is based on a dataset consisting of a total of 15,000 scans that have been annotated by experienced radiologists. From those, 8,573 images have been used for training and 6,427 images for testing.  The train csv contains 45,925 rows and the test csv 21,989. 

Details on building the dataset can be found in our recent paper ¡°VinDr-CXR: An open dataset of chest X-rays with radiologist's annotations¡±.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The challenge uses the standard PASCAL VOC 2010 mean Average Precision (mAP) at IoU > 0.4.

## Submission File
Images in the test set may contain more than one object. For each object in a given test image, you must predict a class ID, confidence score, and bounding box in format xmin ymin xmax ymax. If you predict that there are NO objects in a given image, you should predict 14 1.0 0 0 1 1, where 14 is the class ID for ""No finding"", 1.0 is the confidence, and 0 0 1 1 is a one-pixel bounding box.

The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
image_id,PredictionString
004f33259ee4aef671c2b95d54e4be68,14 1 0 0 1 1
004f33259ee4aef671c2b95d54e4be69,11 0.5 100 100 200 200 13 0.7 10 10 20 20
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

In this competition, we are classifying common thoracic lung diseases and localizing critical findings. This is an object detection and classification problem.

For each test image, you will be predicting a bounding box and class for all findings. If you predict that there are no findings, you should create a prediction of ""14 1 0 0 1 1"" (14 is the class ID for no finding, and this provides a one-pixel bounding box with a confidence of 1.0).

The images are in PNG format (already converted from DICOM for you) and resized to 1024x1024 pixels.

## Dataset information
The dataset comprises 15,000 postero-anterior (PA) CXR scans in PNG format.
All images were labeled by a panel of experienced radiologists for the presence of 14 critical radiographic findings as listed below:

```
0 - Aortic enlargement
1 - Atelectasis
2 - Calcification
3 - Cardiomegaly
4 - Consolidation
5 - ILD
6 - Infiltration
7 - Lung Opacity
8 - Nodule/Mass
9 - Other lesion
10 - Pleural effusion
11 - Pleural thickening
12 - Pneumothorax
13 - Pulmonary fibrosis
```
The ""No finding"" observation (14) was intended to capture the absence of all findings above.

Note that a key part of this competition is working with ground truth from multiple radiologists.

## Files
* **train.csv** - the train set metadata, with one row for each object, including a class and a bounding box. Some images in both test and train have multiple objects (several rows with respect the same image).

* **test.csv** - the test set metadata, only containing the filenames.
* **img_size.csv** - the original dimensions of the files: dim0 is the height and dim1 is the width.
* **sample_submission.csv** - sample submission (similar to test.csv) with dummy results. 

## Columns
```
image_id - unique image identifier
class_name - the name of the class of detected object (or ""No finding"")
class_id - the ID of the class of detected object
rad_id - the ID of the radiologist that made the observation
x_min - minimum X coordinate of the object's bounding box
y_min - minimum Y coordinate of the object's bounding box
x_max - maximum X coordinate of the object's bounding box
y_max - maximum Y coordinate of the object's bounding box
```",False,True
9415,student-at-risk-subject-topic-risk-analysis,"# Overview

Education is a fundamental right, yet millions of students drop out or fail courses due to systemic barriers. In this competition, you¡¯ll use anonymized educational data to predict at-risk students early, empowering institutions to provide timely support. Whether it¡¯s tutoring, mentorship, or financial aid, your model could help bridge the equity gap in education!
----------------------
# Description

### Kaggle Community Olympiad - ML Hajipur hosts Kaggle Competition

# Welcome to the 2025 edition of the Kaggle Community Olympiad! ??

## Problem Title:
Student At-Risk - Subject & Topic Risk Analysis


### Problem Overview:
Education is a crucial pillar for future success, but many students struggle due to various socio-economic and academic challenges. Not all students struggle equally. Some may need help in math, others in literature, or even specific topics like algebra or grammar. In this competition, you¡¯ll predict not only if a student is at risk of failing or dropping out but also which subjects and specific topics they need support in. Participants will develop a machine learning model to predict students at risk of underperforming or dropping out based on academic records and personal factors. This challenge is designed for beginners in ML/AI and aims to introduce them to real-world predictive modeling.



### Problem Statement:
Given a dataset containing students' academic performance, attendance, socio-economic factors, and engagement levels, your task is to develop a predictive model that identifies students who are at risk of failing or dropping out. Additionally, participants should analyze performance subject-wise and topic-wise to gain deeper insights into areas where students struggle the most.

This isn't just a binary prediction¡ªyou're building a *multi-output classification model* capable of identifying risks at three levels:  

**Task - 1**
*Overall Risk* ¨C Will the student struggle overall? (Binary classification: *at_risk = 1/0*)  
**Task - 2**
*Subject Risk* ¨C What core subjects need attention? (e.g., *math_risk, science_risk, literature_risk*)  
**Task - 3**
*Topic Risk* ¨C Which specific topics pose challenges? (e.g., *Algebra, Chemical Reactions, Grammar*)  

### *Why This Matters*  
**Precision Learning** ¨C Go beyond generic ""at-risk"" labels and provide *granular, topic-level insights*.  
**Actionable Insights** ¨C Teachers can *prioritize interventions* for each student based on their specific weaknesses.  
**Real-World Impact** ¨C Your model could help educators *reduce dropout rates* and improve student success!  

### *Who Should Join?*  
- Gain hands-on experience in machine learning for education analytics.
- Work on real-world student data to make a meaningful impact.
- Compete with peers and enhance your Kaggle profile.
- Learn best practices in data preprocessing, feature engineering, and model evaluation.



### Expected Results:
In this competition, your goal is to *predict not just whether a student is at risk of failing or dropping out but also which subjects and specific topics they need the most support in.*  



### Dataset :
Total 10,000 student records


### Acknowledgments:
Thanks to Google Developers and the ML Developer Programs team (Nari Yoon, Hee Jung, and Soonson Kwaon) for their guidance and support.

### Organizer:
- Machine Learning Hajipur

#KaggleCommunityOlympiad #MLHajipur #ML #AI

### Show some excitement on socials
Share your progress using this hashtag: #KaggleCommunityOlympiad #MLHajipur
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Metrics:
- F1 Score (Primary Metric)
- Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) (Secondary Metric)
- R-squared (R2) (Complementary Metric)

### Evaluation Criteria: 

- Analyzing student performance across different subjects
- Studying the relationship between attendance and grades
- Investigating the impact of test preparation courses
- Analyzing performance patterns across different departments
- Gender-based performance analysis
- Age-based performance analysis
----------------------
# Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the Kaggle Community Olympiad?
An associated Kaggle Community Competitions hosted by Google ML communities (AI/ML GDEs and ML communities), supported by Google Developers.

### What is the Kaggle Community Competition?
Kaggle¡¯s community running competitions. The feature was announced in 2022, as an extended form of ¡°InClass competitions¡±. It lets anyone can create and host a competition at no cost.
https://www.kaggle.com/c/about/community


### Are there any restrictions?
This program follows Kaggle¡¯s Terms; see ¡®the rules for competitions¡¯ for more information.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains comprehensive student performance information for 10,000 students. Each student record includes basic information (ID, name, email, gender, age, department) and detailed academic performance metrics. The academic metrics include attendance, midterm and final scores, assignment and quiz averages, participation scores, project scores, and overall grades. Additionally, the dataset includes subject-specific scores in mathematics, reading, writing, and science, along with information about whether students took test preparation courses. The data spans various departments, including engineering, CS, business, and mathematics. This combined dataset allows for analysis of student performance across different subjects, the impact of test preparation, and performance patterns across departments, genders, and age groups. The synthetic data maintains the statistical properties of the original dataset while ensuring a complete dataset for analysis.


## Columns

1. Student Identification Information:
`Student_ID`: Unique identifier (S1000 to S9999)
`First_Name`: Student's first name
`Last_Name`: Student's last name
`Email`: Student's email address
`Gender`: Student's gender
`Age`: Student's age
`Department`: Student's academic department (e.g., Engineering, CS, Business, Mathematics)

2. Academic Performance Metrics:
`Attendance (%)`: Class attendance percentage
`Midterm_Score`: Score in midterm examination
`Final_Score`: Score in final examination
`Assignments_Avg`: Average score in assignments
`Quizzes_Avg`: Average score in quizzes
`Participation_Score`: Score for class participation
`Projects_Score`: Score in projects
`Total_Score`: Overall score
`Grade`: Final grade (A, B, C, D, F)

3. Subject-Specific Scores:
`test_preparation_course`: Whether the student took a test preparation course (0 or 1)
`math_score`: Score in mathematics
`reading_score`: Score in reading
`writing_score`: Score in writing
`science_score`: Score in science",False,True
9416,heart-disease-prediction-dataquest,"# Overview

This is the first Kaggle competition supported by Dataquest, to provide hands-on experience on real-world datasets. In this competition, you'll develop a machine-learning model to predict heart disease as a binary classification problem.
----------------------
# Description

The World Health Organization (WHO) estimates that 17.9 million people die from cardiovascular diseases (CVDs) every year.

Multiple risk factors could contribute to CVD in an individual, such as an unhealthy diet, lack of physical activity, or mental illness. Identifying these risk factors early on could help prevent many premature deaths.

The goal of this competition is to create a community of learning and a supported environment to use expertise and show in a leaderboard, which set the building blocks of your data science journey.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Accuracy score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision) between the predicted  and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the value of the TARGET variable (0 or 1). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,HeartDisease
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

Source
This dataset was created by combining different datasets already available independently but not combined before. In this dataset, 5 heart datasets are combined over 11 common features which makes it the largest heart disease dataset available so far for research purposes. The five datasets used for its curation are:

Cleveland: 303 observations
Hungarian: 294 observations
Switzerland: 123 observations
Long Beach VA: 200 observations
Stalog (Heart) Data Set: 270 observations
Total: 1190 observations
Duplicated: 272 observations

Final dataset: 918 observations

## Files
- heart_train.csv - the training set
- heart_test.csv - the test set
- sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

- ``Age``: age of the patient [years]
- ``Sex``: sex of the patient [M: Male, F: Female]
- ``ChestPainType``: chest pain type [TA: Typical Angina, ATA: Atypical Angina, NAP: Non-Anginal Pain, ASY: Asymptomatic]
- ``RestingBP``: resting blood pressure [mm Hg]
- ``Cholesterol``: serum cholesterol [mm/dl]
- ``FastingBS``: fasting blood sugar [1: if FastingBS > 120 mg/dl, 0: otherwise]
- ``RestingECG``: resting electrocardiogram results [Normal: Normal, ST: having ST-T wave abnormality (T wave inversions and/or ST elevation or depression of > 0.05 mV), LVH: showing probable or definite left ventricular hypertrophy by Estes' criteria]
- ``MaxHR``: maximum heart rate achieved [Numeric value between 60 and 202]
- ``ExerciseAngina``: exercise-induced angina [Y: Yes, N: No]
- ``Oldpeak``: oldpeak = ST [Numeric value measured in depression]
- ``ST_Slope``: the slope of the peak exercise ST segment [Up: upsloping, Flat: flat, Down: downsloping]
- ``HeartDisease``: output class [1: heart disease, 0: Normal]",False,True
