9446,damage-classification-wall,"# Overview

?? Overview

This is a group competition, where your goal is to correctly classify the damage type of each structural component: beam, column, and wall. Each of them will have its corresponded Kaggle link, and the baseline are 0.9 for each of them.
The data is collected mostly from Roboflow and post-earthquake images from Hualien by Buddhist Compassion Relief Tzu Chi Foundation. Each image has been carefully evaluated and classified into three damage types: A, B, and C by a civil engineer.

The criteria we mainly refer to is on this website: 
https://www.ncree.org/safehome/ncr07/ncr1.htm

You can also find the detailed English version here: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1i6p4RIRHobUfNhKgVl_tmRLURJCeH8hI/view?usp=sharing

Also, because our next competition is related to this one, I'd like to give you a preview of what's coming. This will allow you to experiment and observe how to extend the results of this competition to the next one, or you can start thinking about how to approach the next challenge given your current results.
For the next competition, the goal will be to correctly explain the reason behind each classification. For example, if a wall image is classified as damage level A, you should provide the reasonable explanation for that level. For instance, a wall classified as damage level A might be due to a large area of falling debris or exposed rebar. If you classify it as A but explain that it's because of continuous horizontal cracks, your answer won't count.

??Scoring Policy

Normal baseline: 0.9 accuracy
Teams meeting this threshold will receive 50% of the total score

Strong baseline: 0.95 accuracy
Teams meeting this threshold will receive 80% of the total score

Top 5 teams will receive an additional 20% bonus
Top 3 teams will receive an additional 30% bonus
Note: Bonuses are not cumulative, i.e. Top3 won't get (20+30)%
Maximum possible score: 110 points
Final score will be the average of scores in three structural component (beam, column, wall)

Should you have any questions or encounter difficulties, please feel free to reach out via the TA's email address.

?? Submission deadline : 2025/04/24 12:20 p.m
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Submission Requirements
For each image ID in the test set, you must predict:

1. The class label of the image

Your submission file must include a header and follow this format:

    id,class
    1,A
    2,B
    3,C
    ...","# Dataset Description

??Contained Files

beam_damage - Image dataset for model training and validating (needs to be further split)
test - Image data for which you need to generate predictions
submission_example.csv - Template for formatting your predictions before submission


??Data Structure
The dataset contains the following 3 classes:
A
B
C

Each image is NOT in fix size, but in RGB format.


??Instructions

Download the provided ZIP file containing all necessary materials for this assignment.
Split the datasets/ folder into appropriate training and validation sets. We recommend an 80:20 or 90:10 split, but you may experiment with different ratios.
Develop and train your classification model using the training data.
Validate your model's performance using your validation set.
Generate predictions for all images in the test/ folder.
Format your predictions according to the submission_example.csv template (each image ID should have exactly one class label).
Submit your predictions to Kaggle to receive your accuracy score.


??Evaluation
Your submission will be evaluated based on classification accuracy on the test set. The leaderboard on Kaggle will display your performance relative to other submissions.
Additional Guidelines

You may use any deep learning framework (PyTorch, TensorFlow, etc.) for implementation.
While transfer learning is permitted, we encourage you to experiment with designing your own architecture.
You must document your approach, including model architecture, hyperparameters, and data augmentation techniques (if used).

Good luck with your implementations!",False,True
