9452,log-instance-segmentation,"# Overview

Welcome to the Log Face Instance Segmentation competition. This is a challenge for the Spring 2025's Deep Learning for Computer Vision course participants, which aims to advance the field of forestry logistics by automating the process of log face detection and segmentation. Participants will develop models to segment individual log faces from images accurately.

**Goal**
To develop a deep learning model that can perform log face instance segmentation accurately.

**Dataset**
Participants will be provided with a dataset consisting of images of log piles, each containing multiple annotations of individual log faces. The dataset is split into training and test sets.

**Evaluation Metrics**
Submissions will be evaluated based on the mean Average Precision (mAP) score for the segmented log faces. Higher mAP scores indicate better segmentation performance.

**Steps**
1. Download the datasets from the Data page: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/log-instance-segmentation/data
2. Build your model: Use any neural network techniques for instance segmentation task
3. Test your model: Make predictions from test images and convert the results into RLE
3. Submit predictions: Upload a CSV file consisting of `image_id`, `width`, `height` and `encoded_mask` for evaluation

**Starter Notebook**
We provide a starter notebook containing URLs to download datasets (which are also available on the Data page), visualize the data, convert predictions into the appropriate submission format, and upload your submission via Kaggle API
https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1cLm_xXBKbJqnTnXmq1c1Mxepy57p67jf?usp=sharing or https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/log-instance-segmentation/code

**Contact**
Please contact the competition organizers through the Kaggle discussion forums for any queries.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on mAP.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict instance segments of the log faces. The file should contain a header and have the following format (semicolons are used to separate between segmentation instances for the same image):

    image_id, width, height, encoded_mask
    img0001, 4000, 2250, 302829 8 304367 5;120134 10 121665
    img0002, 4000, 2250, 302829 8 304367 5;120134 10 121665
    img0003, 4000, 2250, 302829 8 304367 5;120134 10 121665
    etc.
----------------------
# ?? What about empty predictions?

If your model has no prediction for a given image, you must still include a placeholder in the submission to follow the format and avoid errors during evaluation.

For an empty prediction, submit: `None 0 0 1 1`

Example of empty row in submission.csv:
|image_id  | width  | height | encoded_mask |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| img_0001 | 4000  | 2250 | None 0 0 1 1 |",,False,True
9453,multi-label-classification-competition-2025,"# Overview

Welcome to University of Sydney, School of Computer Science, COMP5329, 2025 Semester1, Assignment2, Competition Track!

In this Assignment, you are going to participate in a Multi-label Classification Competition. 

Good Luck!
----------------------
# Description

In this assignment, you are to solve the multi-label classification task. Each sample in this dataset includes:

* an image,
* one or more labels, range: 1-19 (12 is missing)
* a short caption that summarizes the image.

Your goal is to implement an image classifier that predicts the labels of the image data samples. You may optionally include the caption in the input of your classifier -- it's up to you!

Please submit your submission file via Kaggle. Remember: the ranking contributes to 20% of your assignment mark. Please use the following name format for your submission.

`{unikey1}_{unikey2}_{unikey3}`

Happy coding!
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the **Mean F1-Score**. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision $p$ and recall $r$.

Precision is the ratio of true positives $tp$ to all predicted positives $(tp + fp)$. Recall is the ratio of true positives tp to all actual positives $(tp + fn)$. The F1 score is given by:

$$F1=2\frac{p\cdot r}{p+r}, \text{where} p = \frac{tp}{tp+fp}, r = \frac{tp}{tp+fn}$$

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favoured over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **data/*.jpg** - the images

## Data fields

* **ImageID** - The file name of the images
* **Labels** - The labels of the images
* **Caption** - The caption of the images

You can get started by importing the training file names and labels.

```
import re
import pandas as pd
from io import StringIO
FILENAME = 'train.csv'
with open(FILENAME) as file:
lines = [re.sub(r'([^,])""(\s*[^\n])', r'\1/""\2', line) for line in file]
df = pd.read_csv(StringIO(''.join(lines)), escapechar=""/"")
```",False,True
9454,hack-rush-deep-fake-detection,"# Overview

In this hackathon, participants are tasked with developing a robust, efficient, and explainable model capable of accurately distinguishing between real photographs and AI-generated images. As generative AI tools continue to evolve in realism and sophistication, detecting synthetic content has become a critical concern in areas such as media integrity, digital forensics, and online security.

----------------------
# Description

Dataset
The dataset includes approximately 60,000 images, comprising a balanced mix of real-world photographs and AI-generated images created using various state-of-the-art generators.
The training set is labeled and organized by image source.
The test set includes a mix of clean images and adversarially manipulated versions designed to test the robustness of your model against real-world tampering and perturbations.
Participants are encouraged to find additional training data if they see fit. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on the following criteria:

1. Accuracy and Robustness (65%)
Real vs. AI Classification accuracy as per the leaderboard
2. Inference Speed (15%)
Model execution time on the standardized test hardware
Efficiency of your implementation
3. Explainability and Presentation (20%)
Participants must incorporate at least one explainability method:
Attention visualization (e.g., Grad-CAM)
Natural language explanations/captions for classifications
Feature importance visualization
Decision boundary analysis

Your final submission must be a CSV file with the following format:

```filename,class```
```image_001.png,0```
```image_002.png,1```
Note: 0 corresponds to the label ai and 1 corresponds to real images
...
A sample file (sample.csv) has been provided for reference.","# Dataset Description

The training dataset can be accessed here:
https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shreyansjain04/ai-vs-real-image-dataset

The testing dataset can be accessed here:
https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shreyansjain04/ai-vs-real-image-test-dataset

Sample of the submission format has been given below in the sample.csv. 

**Submission Format**
Your submission should be a CSV file with the following format:
filename,class
image_001.png,0
image_002.png,1

The class 0 corresponds to ai generated and 1 corresponds to real images

A sample has been given as sample.csv",False,True
9455,gen-ai-competition,"# Overview

??? **Image2Caption2Embedding**: From Images to Pixels and then to Vectors
Transform Images into Language, Then into Vectors. Can You Capture the Essence?

Welcome to Caption2Embedding, a hands-on machine learning competition where your mission is to build an intelligent pipeline that goes beyond pixels ¡ª all the way to powerful representations.

**?? Challenge Overview**

You are given a set of mystery images. Your task?

1. Caption each image using a vision-to-language model (e.g., BLIP, MiniGPT-4, or anything else you fancy).

1. Transform the generated captions into 512-dimensional embeddings using CLIP's text encoder.

1. Submit the final matrix of embeddings ¡ª one 512-dim vector per image.

Think of this as a multi-step challenge: vision ¡ú language ¡ú vector space. Your goal is to generate embeddings that truly capture the soul of each image.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation will be done by comparing the submitted text embeddings with the image embeddings using mean cosine similarity.","# Dataset Description

You¡¯ll receive a dataset of uncaptioned images.
There is no ground truth provided

Hint: The better your captions, the more meaningful your CLIP embeddings. Focus on generating captions that are descriptive, relevant, and varied.",False,True
9456,ele-8453-devoir-2-question-2-a-2025-extended,"# Overview

Le but est d'apprendre ""energie_totale_consommee"" en fonction des autres features  pour chaque entr¨¦e.
