# Evaluation

Vous serez ¨¦valuez sur le root mean square error (RMSE) entre vos pr¨¦dictions et les valeurs r¨¦elles de l'ensemble de test.","# Dataset Description

Ensemble de donn¨¦es anonymis¨¦es sur la consommation ¨¦lectrique de la client¨¨le qui participe ¨¤ un programme de gestion locale de la demande de puissance. Les points de donn¨¦es, enregistr¨¦s toutes les heures, d¨¦montrent les variations de la consommation d¡¯¨¦lectricit¨¦ en fonction de la m¨¦t¨¦o et de l¡¯heure de la journ¨¦e. Les donn¨¦es, qui proviennent des compteurs intelligents d¡¯Hydro-Qu¨¦bec, des thermostats intelligents d¡¯Hilo ainsi que des informations m¨¦t¨¦orologiques pour la r¨¦gion de Montr¨¦al, regroupent notamment :

la consommation horaire par poste ;
la temp¨¦rature int¨¦rieure moyenne ;
la temp¨¦rature de consigne moyenne des thermostats ;
le nombre de clientes et clients connect¨¦s ;
le nombre de thermostats intelligents connect¨¦s ;
la pr¨¦sence d¡¯un ¨¦v¨¦nement de gestion de la demande ;
les donn¨¦es du calendrier ;
la temp¨¦rature de l¡¯air ;
la vitesse du vent ;
le niveau d¡¯ensoleillement ;
l¡¯humidit¨¦ relative ;
les pr¨¦cipitations de neige.
M¨¦thodes
Consommation horaire par poste ¨¦lectrique
La consommation horaire d¡¯un poste ¨¦lectrique est calcul¨¦e en agr¨¦geant la consommation individuelle horaire de la client¨¨le desservie par ce poste. Ces donn¨¦es de consommation sont recueillies ¨¤ l¡¯aide des compteurs intelligents d¡¯Hydro-Qu¨¦bec.

Temp¨¦rature int¨¦rieure moyenne et temp¨¦rature de consigne moyenne des thermostats 
La temp¨¦rature int¨¦rieure moyenne et la temp¨¦rature de consigne moyenne des thermostats sont mesur¨¦es ¨¤ l¡¯aide des thermostats intelligents d¡¯Hilo. Les donn¨¦es sont ensuite agr¨¦g¨¦es sur une base horaire et par poste ¨¦lectrique.

#M¨¦t¨¦o
Les donn¨¦es m¨¦t¨¦orologiques, c¡¯est-¨¤-dire la temp¨¦rature ext¨¦rieure moyenne, la vitesse du vent, le niveau d¡¯ensoleillement, l¡¯humidit¨¦ relative et les pr¨¦cipitations de neige, sont recueillies toutes les heures au moyen de l¡¯API Weatherbit.

Pr¨¦sence d¡¯un ¨¦v¨¦nement de gestion de la demande d¡¯¨¦lectricit¨¦ (d¨¦fi Hilo)
L¡¯indicateur de pr¨¦sence d¡¯un ¨¦v¨¦nement de gestion de la demande d¡¯¨¦lectricit¨¦ dans l¡¯heure est d¨¦termin¨¦ par l¡¯historique des d¨¦fis Hilo.

Un d¨¦fi Hilo consiste en un ¨¦v¨¦nement ponctuel au cours duquel Hilo aide sa client¨¨le ¨¤ r¨¦duire sa consommation ¨¦lectrique ¨¤ domicile. Chaque d¨¦fi se d¨¦roule en trois phases, habituellement aux heures suivantes :

d¨¦fi en matin¨¦e : phase de pr¨¦chauffage optionnelle de 4 h ¨¤ 6 h, phase de r¨¦duction de 6 h ¨¤ 10 h, phase de reprise de 10 h ¨¤ 11 h ;
d¨¦fi en soir¨¦e : phase de pr¨¦chauffage optionnelle de 15 h ¨¤ 17 h, phase de r¨¦duction de 17 h ¨¤ 21 h, phase de reprise de 21 h ¨¤ 22 h.
Renseignements additionnels
Fr¨¦quence de mise ¨¤ jour : non planifi¨¦e
Fr¨¦quence d¡¯¨¦chantillonnage : horaire
Notices et conditions d¡¯utilisation : les donn¨¦es fournies sont brutes, sans garantie de qualit¨¦, et peuvent changer sans pr¨¦avis.",False,True
9457,skku-aai-3010-2025-spring-lab-assignment-1-04-06,"# Overview

Task Description:
The goal of this assignment is to complete a regression task to predict the appropriate number of shared bicycle rentals on a certain day. You will build a regression model by analyzing the given feature data of each data, completing the entire process from data preprocessing to feature selection, model training, and evaluation.

We provide you with a basic model as a starting point. You can modify and optimize this model or build your own model to improve performance. The dataset contains missing values (NA), which you need to handle before training the model. By analyzing and processing the data, you will learn how to address real-world data challenges and optimize your model¡¯s performance
----------------------
# Description

Assignment Requirements:

**Basic Model:**
We provide a basic regression model as a starting point. You can use this model to train on the dataset, or you can modify and optimize it for better performance.
Alternatively, you can build your own model from scratch.

**Data Preprocessing:**
You need to handle the missing values (NA) in the dataset. You can choose to fill NA values with zeros, mean values, or use other reasonable methods.
Analyze the relationship between different features and the target variable (cnt) to identify key features for prediction.

**Feature Selection and Engineering:**
The dataset contains many features, but not all of them are useful for prediction. You need to perform feature selection to retain features that are beneficial for the model.
You can also apply feature engineering by creating new features or transforming existing ones to further improve model performance.

**Model Training and Evaluation:**
Train your model using PyTorch, either by modifying the provided basic model or by building your own.
You can experiment with hyperparameter tuning (e.g., learning rate, batch size) or apply other regression techniques to improve results.
Once the model is trained, predict the cnt values for the test set and submit your predictions.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated one the Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE). The RMSLE is calculated as
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F8378313%2F599fad85f2d2f84a0a226ff3a49e4098%2Frmse.png?generation=1742731968954019&alt=media)

Where:
- n: the number of days in the test set
- p_i:  your predicted count
- a_i: the actual count
- log(x): the natural logarithm","# Dataset Description

Case description: A certain shared bicycle was launched on the market and has been in operation for a period of time. We have obtained a series of data on factors that affect bicycle rentals and the number of bicycles rented on that day. Please conduct data analysis based on this data to predict the appropriate number of shared bicycle rentals on a certain day in the future, so that the company can invest and obtain maximum profits.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set, incuding feature information for each data and cnt ??(number of total rentals). You can use this data to train the model.
*   **test.csv** - the test set, providing the same features, but excluding cnt. You need to predict and submit the prediction results based on these features.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns
* instant: ID
* dteday: daily date + timestamp
* season:  1 = spring, 2 = summer, 3 = fall, 4 = winter 
* holiday: whether the day is considered a holiday
* workingday: whether the day is neither a weekend nor holiday
* weathersit: 
1: Clear, Few clouds, Partly cloudy, Partly cloudy
2: Mist + Cloudy, Mist + Broken clouds, Mist + Few clouds, Mist
3: Light Snow, Light Rain + Thunderstorm + Scattered clouds, Light Rain + Scattered clouds
4: Heavy Rain + Ice Pallets + Thunderstorm + Mist, Snow + Fog
* temp: temperature in Celsius
* atemp: ""feels like"" temperature in Celsius
* hum: relative humidity
* windspeed: wind speed
* cnt: number of total rentals

**Note** that there are missing values ??in the dataset, which you need to handle before starting modeling.",False,True
