9459,musical-instrumemts-sound-classification,"# Overview

This competition seeks to develop a classification model that can accurately identify different types of sounds produced by various Khasi musical instruments. The audio data has already been preprocessed, and Mel spectrograms have been extracted for each sample.

Participants are required to use these extracted Mel spectrograms to build a deep learning model capable of classifying the different instruments based on their unique audio signatures.


----------------------
# Description

The Khasi tribe, located in the Northeastern part of India, has a rich tradition of music and a diverse collection of unique musical instruments. However, with the passage of time, these traditional sounds have slowly begun to fade. Many of these instruments are no longer widely used, and only a few individuals today know how to play them or even recognize their distinct sounds.

To preserve this cultural heritage, this competition aims to bring traditional Khasi music into the digital age. By building a deep learning classification model that can identify different Khasi musical instruments based on their sound (represented as Mel spectrograms), participants can contribute to the revival and documentation of these traditional instruments.

This initiative is not just a technical challenge¡ªit¡¯s an opportunity to engage the younger generation with the rich musical legacy of the Khasi people and to take a meaningful step toward cultural preservation through technology.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation for this competition will be based on the Macro F1 Score. A confusion matrix will also be provided at the end of the competition to help analyze how well models perform across different classes.

---




## Submission File
For each test image (Mel spectrogram), you must predict the corresponding instrument name.

Your submission file should be a CSV file with the following format:

    Filename,Instrument_name
    qbhssia.jpg,Pdiah
    agjashusb.jpg,Kynshaw
    etc.","# Dataset Description

All the data has been labeled and organized into separate folders for each class. The train, validation, and test data are split and stored in clearly named folders, making navigation easier.



## Files

*   **train** - the training set
*   **test** - the test set
*   **val** - the validation set",False,True
