9461,nlp-2025-math,"# Overview

The Competition below is a skills-based competition to evaluate the effectiveness of natural language processing techniques in solving multiple-choice math problems. 

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on **overall accuracy**, i.e., the proportion of multiple-choice questions for which the predicted answer index matches the correct one.

Each test sample represents a math multiple-choice question with several answer choices. Your task is to predict the **index (starting from 0)** of the correct answer choice.

### Submission File

Your submission file should be a **CSV** file with two columns:  
- `ID`: the unique identifier for each question in the test set  
- `label`: your predicted choice index (e.g., 0 if you select the first answer, 1 for the second, etc.)

Make sure to include the **header row** in your file. The format should look like this:

```
ID,label
1001,2
1002,0
1003,1
...
```

### Notes:
- Each row must correspond to a question from the test set.
- Labels must be integers: `0`, `1`, `2`, etc., depending on the number of choices.
- The order of rows should **not** matter, but IDs must match exactly with those provided in the test set.","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Description

The dataset consists of multiple-choice math questions designed to test natural language understanding and mathematical reasoning abilities. Each question is accompanied by four answer choices, only one of which is correct.

Participants are expected to build models that can accurately select the correct answer index (starting from `0`) for each question based solely on the question content and provided choices.

### ?? Files
#### `train.jsonl`
Contains the train set questions for training.

Example:
```
{""Question"": ""Martin spends 2 hours waiting in traffic, then four times that long trying to get off the freeway. How much time does he waste total?"", ""Choices"": [620, 10, 536, 379], ""label"": 1}
```

#### `test.csv`
Contains the test set questions for evaluation.

| Column Name     | Description                                                                 |
|------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `ID`             | Unique identifier for each question.                                       |
| `question`       | The text of the math question.                                              |
| `choice_0` to `choice_n` | The answer choices list.              |

The `label` column is **not provided** in the test set. Your task is to predict this value.",False,True
9462,dl-4-cv-competition,"# Overview

Welcome to our SUPER MARIO COMPETITION
----------------------
# Description 

In this competition, you¡¯re challenged to build a model that is capable of **detecting**  3 different types of mobs in the Mario Universe. You will be using a dataset that consists of images from different Mario versions. The dataset contains screenshots from the game and corresponding annotation files.

The types of mobs that your model will learn to detect:

| Skelerex | Koopa | Goomba |
| --- | --- | --- |
| <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F12229823%2F74da796ddfa818c6c074571d16f3a112%2FScreenshot%202025-04-09%20at%2019.43.33.png?generation=1744217264837663&alt=media"" height= ""100""> | <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F12229823%2F7a30eb89c617db4f4e384b99e9ec9563%2Fscreenshot1.png?generation=1744366798554071&alt=media"" height= ""100""> | <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F12229823%2F5bde7788074f3f667663f0b518d8c16e%2FScreenshot%202025-04-09%20at%2019.45.12.png?generation=1744217293380497&alt=media"" height= ""100""> |
________

|  An example of the image from the dataset |  |
| --- | --- |
|  <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F12229823%2Ff0b211ce56266191ff8e619c91a8fd90%2FScreenshot%202025-04-09%20at%2020.37.55.png?generation=1744220285156164&alt=media"" height= ""200"">| 


*More details about the dataset can be found from [here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/dl-4-cv-competition/data).

**Goal:** Build a model for object detection, that can detect different types of mobs in Mario Universe.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on Mean Average Precision at IoU=0.5 (mAP@0.5, VOC-style)
This evaluation script computes mean Average Precision (mAP) at a fixed Intersection over Union (IoU) threshold of 0.5, following the Pascal VOC evaluation protocol.
- Bounding boxes are matched if they have the same class and IoU ¡Ý 0.5.
- Precision-recall curves are computed per class, then integrated to obtain Average Precision (AP).
- The final metric is the mean of the APs over all classes (mAP@0.5).
- No confidence scores are used: all predictions are considered equally ranked.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict bounding boxes and classes. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,PredictionString
    test_image_001.jpg,""<class_id> <x_center> <y_center> <width> <height>""
    test_image_002.jpg,""<class_id> <x_center> <y_center> <width> <height>""

Submitted file must be CSV.","# Dataset Description

# ?? Dataset Description

This dataset is designed for an object detection competition using the YOLO (You Only Look Once) format. It includes images and corresponding annotation files, split into training and test sets. Participants must train a model to detect and classify objects in the test images and submit their predictions in a specific CSV format.

## ?? Files

- **dataset_kaggle.zip**  
  Contains the training data. Inside:
  - `images` ¡ª training images (`.jpg`)
  - `labels` ¡ª YOLO annotation files (`.txt`)
  - `classes.txt` ¡ª list of class names (one per line)
  - `notes.json` ¡ª Metadata

- **test_set_without_label**  
  Contains the test images. Inside:
  - `images/` ¡ª test images without labels

- **starter-notebook.ipynb**  
  A short notebook to help you submit your solution.

- **sample_submission.csv**  
  A template showing the expected submission format. Each row matches one image and contains predicted objects as a flat string in YOLO format.

## ?? Data Format

Each `.txt` file in `labels/` corresponds to one image and contains lines like:

<class_id> <x_center> <y_center> <width> <height>

- `class_id` refers to the index in `classes.txt` (starting from 0)
- All coordinates are **normalized** between `0` and `1`
- `x_center` and `y_center` are the center of the bounding box
- `width` and `height` are the size of the box relative to the image


**Example:**

1 0.45 0.50 0.20 0.15
0 0.70 0.75 0.10 0.12

## ?? Submission Format

Your final submission must be a CSV file with two columns:

Id,PredictionString
test_image_001.jpg,""0 0.5 0.5 0.2 0.2 2 0.7 0.7 0.1 0.1""
test_image_002.jpg,""""

Where `PredictionString` contains zero or more objects for the image, each represented as:
<class_id> <x_center> <y_center> <width> <height>

Multiple objects are space-separated in a single string.

## ? FAQ

**What files do I need?**  
Use the training archive to train your model and the test archive to generate predictions.

**What am I predicting?**  
You are predicting bounding boxes and classes for all objects in each test image.

**What format should my submission be in?**  
A single CSV file like `sample_submission.csv`, with predicted bounding boxes in YOLO format.

**What is `classes.txt`?**  
A list of class names, one per line. The line number = `class_id`.


Good luck!",False,True
