9483,vehicle-type-classification-challenge,"# Overview

Welcome to the Vehicle Type Classification Challenge!

Your goal in this competition is to build a machine learning model that can classify vehicles captured in urban street images into one of four categories:

- Sedan
- SUV
- Bus
- Other (e.g., motorcycle, van, bicycle)

This dataset is custom-built using real-world vehicle images collected and labeled by the organizer. Images in the test set are anonymized and shuffled. The task is multi-class classification, and submissions will be evaluated based on accuracy.
----------------------
# Description

In this competition, you are provided with a dataset of street-view vehicle images. Each training image is labeled with one of four vehicle types:

- Sedan  
- SUV
- Bus
- Other (includes motorcycle, van, bicycle, etc.)

Your goal is to build a classifier that predicts the correct category of each vehicle in the test set.

All images have been resized to 256x256 pixels. The dataset was collected, cleaned, and labeled by the competition host. The test images are unlabeled and shuffled. Predictions should be submitted in the format specified in `sample_submission.csv`.

You can start from the provided [starter notebook](#) which includes:
- Data loading
- Image visualization
- Training a ResNet18 baseline
- CSV prediction output

Make sure your final submission has the correct format and filename as required. All submissions will be evaluated based on *accuracy*.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric

Your model will be evaluated using **accuracy**:

$$
Accuracy = \frac{\text{Correct}}{\text{Total}}
$$


## Submission File

For each image in the test set, you must predict one of the following labels:

- Sedan
- SUV
- Bus
- Other

The submission file must be a **CSV file** with the following two columns:

- `filename`: the image file name (e.g., `test1.jpg`, `test2.jpg`, ...)
- `label`: the predicted class label

The file should include a header and follow this format:

```
filename,label
test1.jpg,Bus
test2.jpg,Bus
test3.jpg,Other
test4.jpg,Other
test5.jpg,Sedan
```


Make sure the filenames in your submission exactly match those in the test set. All submissions will be evaluated using **accuracy**.

### Leaderboard

The leaderboard is split as follows:

- **50% Public Leaderboard**: visible during the competition
- **50% Private Leaderboard**: used for final evaluation after the competition ends","# Dataset Description

This competition provides a custom dataset for multi-class vehicle type classification.

Participants must classify each test image as one of the following categories:

- **Sedan**
- **SUV**
- **Bus**
- **Other**

---

## ?? Files Provided

- **train.zip**  
  A folder of training images organized in subfolders by class:  
  `train/Sedan/`, `train/SUV/`, `train/Bus/`, `train/Other/`  
  All images are in `.jpg` format and resized to **256x256**.

- **test.zip**  
  A folder of unlabeled test images named `test1.jpg`, `test2.jpg`, ..., `testXXX.jpg`.  
  All images are **shuffled** and have no class information.

- **sample_submission.csv**  
  A sample file that shows the correct format for submissions.  
  It contains two columns: `filename` and `label`.

---

## ?? Submission Format

Submissions must be `.csv` files with the following structure:

```
filename,label
test1.jpg,Bus
test2.jpg,Bus
test3.jpg,Other
test4.jpg,Other
test5.jpg,Sedan
```

Predicted labels must be **exactly one** of: `Sedan`, `SUV`, `Bus`, `Other`.

---

If you have any questions, you can contact us directly in Slack: 
Zekang Chen,
Naveen Kumar Kandasamypalayam kulandhaivel, 
Hanna-Maria Kukk,
 Yang Sheng.",False,True
9484,dl-4-cv-aircraft,"# Overview

This is the in class competition for the [dl4cv](https://courses.cs.ut.ee/2025/DL4CV/spring/Main/HomePage) course.

The goal is to train an object detection model to recognize aircafts such as:
* Airplanes
* Helicopters
* Gliders
* Paragliders
----------------------
# Evaluation

IoU on a private test set

## Submission File
For each image in the test set, you must predict the bounding boxes on it and output them as a list of `;` separated YOLO format boxes. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```csv
image,boxes
airplane_1,0 0.5 0.1 0.7 0.3
helicopter_18,2 0.1 0.2 0.5 0.1;3 0.6 0.1 0.6 0.23
...
```

You can also look at `starter_notebook.ipynb` or `example_submission.csv` uploaded with the dataset!","# Dataset Description

Our dataset comes in the **YOLO** format

*From label-studio*: Popular TXT format is created for each image file. Each txt file contains annotations for the corresponding image file, that is object class, object coordinates, height & width.

For example *images/airplane_17.jpg* could come with a text file *labels/airplane_17.txt* which looks like
```csv
0 0.648333 0.470238 0.563333 0.869048
0 0.650000 0.023810 0.000000 0.023810
```
where rows denote bounding boxes and columns are `class` followed by normalized `center-x, center-y, width, height`.
You can find class label mappings in `notes.json`.",False,True
