9492,where-is-valdis,"# Overview

## ?? Where is Valdis?! ¨C Object Detection Challenge
Welcome to LTAT.02.028: Where is Valdis?!, a wild ride into the heart of Estonian cult cinema and a chance to flex your computer vision muscles.
This ain¡¯t your average COCO dataset. You¡¯re about to dig through scenes from the legendary underground film ¡°Tulnukas ehk Valdise p??semine 11 osas¡± (translation: Alien or: Valdis¡¯ Escape in 11 Chapters), a bizarre, iconic flick that dives deep into the small-town chaos of 90s Estonia. Your mission? Find Valdis ¡ª our mysterious, often partially-visible protagonist ¡ª using object detection.
##?? The Dataset
The footage was bravely yoinked from YouTube for strictly educational purposes (don¡¯t @ us), then chopped into 25 FPS frames using CVAT. NSFW scenes were purged during preprocessing, so Valdis is the only nudity-free star here. Bounding boxes were drawn when Valdis appears in all his glory (or at least 30% of him). Frames where he¡¯s barely peeking or totally missing? Consider those background noise.
## ???¡â? What You¡¯re Dealing With
This is a custom, never-seen-before dataset. It¡¯s quirky, messy, and full of eastern European charm. Your job is to train an object detection model that can reliably spot Valdis in each frame.
Think ¡°Where¡¯s Waldo¡±, but instead of a striped shirt in a crowd, it¡¯s M?rt Avandi in an orange vest and blue cap.
## ?? Challenges & Quirks
Bounding box consistency might not be perfect ¡ª Valdis doesn¡¯t always make it easy.
The data was prepped on a budget. Some manual syncing may have happened between extracted frames and annotations.
Some frames may feel like deleted scenes from a fever dream. Training your model to handle weird lighting, odd angles, and low-budget Estonian cinematography is part of the fun.

## ?? TL;DR ¨C Your Task
Train a model that detects Valdis in each frame. That¡¯s it. No facial recognition, no classification ¡ª just classic object detection. Submit your bounding box predictions and prove that you can out-Valdis even the most die-hard Tulnukas fans.
If you need help or want to geek out about Estonian cult cinema, hit up the discussion boards.

----------------------
# Description

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F22550501%2Ff0cfc37c922ecf7b499d8f3033c1839e%2Fto_kaggle_1.png?generation=1744564397385427&alt=media)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F22550501%2Fbb34d675bb1782efe06cc67d4443beb7%2Fto_kaggle_2.png?generation=1744564410322720&alt=media)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F22550501%2F552c806ee6f9c7a38722423252d29a02%2Fto_kaggle_3.png?generation=1744564417745445&alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

We use IoU as the evaluation metric. The format of your submission.csv is the following:

| image_ID        | xmin  | ymin  | xmax  | ymax  |
|------------------|-------|-------|--------|--------|
| frame_69420.png  | 0.43  | 500.1 | 70.2   | 100.2  |
| frame_42069.png  | -1    | -1    | -1     | -1     | 


Values have to be in the original image size coordinate system (1280X720). If you resize the data for training and inference, make sure to resize it back to original dimensions.
If there is no object detected and no bounding box to draw, then all columns should be assigned value -1.","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description

Welcome to the official dataset for the **Where is Valdis?! ¨C Object Detection Challenge**.

This dataset consists of video frames annotated with bounding boxes indicating the location of *Valdis*¡ªa single individual of interest who appears sporadically throughout the footage. The goal of the competition is to detect Valdis in each frame where he appears.

## Overview

- **Frame resolution:** 1280 ¡Á 720  
- **Color:** Yes (RGB)  
- **Total frames:** 7020  
- **Frames with Valdis:** 1970  
- **Frames without Valdis:** 5050  
- **Estimated Valdis presence:** ~28% of frames  
- **File types:**
  - Images: `.PNG`
  - Annotations: `.xml` (PASCAL VOC format)
- **Annotations:** Bounding boxes only  
- **Average Valdis bbox size:**
  - Width: 491.87 px  
  - Height: 562.98 px  
  - Area: 299570.48 px2  

## Data Split

The dataset has been split into two parts:

- **Train set (85%)**
- **Test set (15%)**

Importantly, the Valdis/no-Valdis ratio is preserved in both subsets to ensure a balanced distribution for training and evaluation. All images in the test set are included in a sample submission file with default or true bounding boxes (if available).

---

## Files

- `train/` ¡ª contains training data  
  - `images/` ¡ª training images  
  - `labels/` ¡ª corresponding annotation XMLs  
- `test/` ¡ª contains the test set  
  - `images/` ¡ª test images (annotations not provided)

- `sample_submission.csv` ¡ª a sample submission in the correct format:

| ID         | xmin  | ymin  | xmax   | ymax   |
|------------------|-------|-------|--------|--------|
| frame_008926.PNG | 0.69  | 36.87 | 517.83 | 708.61 |
| frame_001234.PNG | -1  | -1  | -1   | -1   |

- If Valdis is **not present**, use `-1,-1,-1,-1` as a placeholder.
- If Valdis is **detected**, provide bounding box coordinates rounded to 2 decimal places.",False,True
