9494,ml-competition-gd-go-c-its,"# Overview

Dalam kompetisi ini, peserta diberikan data dengan jenis data Tabular, dengan data bernama Fraud Detection Dataset. Dataset ini berasal dari *Fraud Detection Dataset*. Kumpulan data tersebut dibuat menggunakan pustaka Faker untuk mensimulasikan data transaksi. Kumpulan data tersebut berisi beberapa kolom yang mewakili informasi pengguna dan transaksi, termasuk fitur untuk mendeteksi aktivitas penipuan. Data tersebut mencakup campuran nilai kategoris, numerik, dan datetime, yang perlu diproses untuk proses prediksi menggunakan model Machine Learning nantinya. Dengan menerapkan teknik data science, peserta diharapkan dapat mengetahui hubungan antar data untuk memprediksi kapan data tersebut fraud atau tidak.


**Permasalahan**

Adanya kasus fraud dalam kartu kredit menimbulkan banyak permasalahan kenyamanan pengguna dalam menggunakan kartu kredit. Dalam kompetisi ini, peserta ditantang untuk memprediksi klasifikasi kartu kredit termasuk *fraud* atau tidak. Peserta diharapkan dapat melakukan tugas flow data science dan machine learning sederhana dari mulai data preprocessing, EDA, modelling, dan lain-lain. Peserta dilarang untuk mengirimkan submisi dengan **teknik manual** input dari data Kaggle karena nantinya 3 terbesar akan disuruh untuk presentasi mempresentasikan hasil dari project tersebut dan mendapatkan beberapa reward.

**Tujuan**
Tujuan dari kompetisi ini adalah mendorong para data scientist untuk mengasah keterampilan mereka dalam membangun model prediksi yang akurat dalam masalah klasifikasi. Dengan berpartisipasi, peserta turut berkontribusi dalam pemahaman yang lebih mendalam mengenai prediksi apakah kartu kredit mengalami fraud atau tidak. Selain meningkatkan kemampuan teknis, kompetisi ini juga berfokus pada pentingnya pencegahan fraud kartu kredit yang terjadi di perusahaan untuk mencegah kerugian finansial akibat penipuan
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submisi dinilai berdasarkan Nilai Precision. Nilai Precision dalam Sci-kit Learn library didefinisikan sebagai 

**Precision** = TP / (TP + FP)


Dengan :
TP = True Positive
FP = False Positive


```python
from sklearn.metrics import precision_score
precision = precision_score(y_true, y_pred)

print(""Precision:"", precision)
```

**Mengapa Precision?**
Precision dipilih karena menyediakan metrik evaluasi yang sesuai dengan kasus prediksi fraud ini. Dalam perusahaan diharapkan terjadinya fraud haruslah seminimal mungkin. Metriks ini digunakan untuk tujuan meminimalkan False Positive, karena menganggap transaksi non-fraud menjadi fraud bisa sangat merugikan perusahaan dan seorang data scientist beradaptasi dengan kondisi permasalahan seperti ini.","# Dataset Description

Dalam dataset ini, terdapat beberapa kolom data yang didapatkan dari suatu transaksi dari sebuah kartu credit dalam bank tertentu untuk mendeteksi klasifikasi dari suatu kartu kredit apakah terdeteksi **fraud** atau tidak.

Dataset ini memiliki beberapa jenis data seperti berikut
1. Train, merupakan data latih yang terdiri dari beberapa *feature* dan kolom target yaitu *Fraud*
2. Test, merupakan data test yang terdiri dari beberapa *feature* seperti di data train tanpa adanya kolom target karena kolom tersebut yang akan diprediksi

Data train memiliki 5965 data point sedangkan data test memiliki 2000 data point

Penjelasan atribut kolom :
`#` : Kolom Id, Nomor data pada tiap data point, pasti berbeda tiap barisnya
`Profession` : Data profesi tiap pengguna kartu kredit
`Income` : Data penghasilan tiap pengguna kartu kredit
`Credit_card_number` : Data nomor kartu kredit pengguna
`Expiry` : Tanggal kadaluarsa kartu kredit
`Security_code` : Kode keamanan kartu kredit
`Fraud` : Kolom target, Data kartu kredit fraud atau tidak dengan *fraud* bernilai 1 dan *non-fraud* bernilai nol",False,True
9495,object-detection-of-football-matches-objects,"# Overview

This is an object detection competition of football match objects for Deep Learning for Computer Vision class (2025 Spring). The creator of this competition is team Neural Network Ninjas.

The goal of the competition is to detect various objects from images taken from a football match. The classes are: ball, player, referee, coach, goalkeeper.
----------------------
# Description

Accurate detection of players, referees, coaches, and the ball can enhance tactical analysis, player tracking, and automated highlight generation in football. Existing datasets related to football usually only have players and/or the ball annotated. Our dataset has more classes, which means it can be used for a variety of purposes.

The dataset is derived from a publicly available football match video from YouTube. Both the public and private datasets are from the same video. 

Keep in mind that the data is naturally unbalanced because there are much more players on the field compared to referees, balls, coaches, goalkeepers. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

####Overview

This competition is evaluated on the mean Average Precision (mAP) metric across multiple Intersection over Union (IoU) thresholds. mAP is a comprehensive metric that balances precision (the accuracy of positive predictions) and recall (the ability to detect all relevant instances) across different levels of detection difficulty.

The evaluation is done on the testing set that is equally divided into public and private sets. Public test set results are available immediately after the submission. Private test set results will be shown after the competition deadline.

You are allowed to make 5 submissions per day. You can choose 2 submissions for the final evaluation, so the best score out of two will be used in the private leaderboard.

####Metric Details

The mAP metric for this competition is calculated by averaging the area under the Precision-Recall curve at different IoU thresholds, specifically ranging from 0.5 to 0.95 with a step size of 0.05. For a predicted bounding box to be considered a correct detection, its IoU with a ground truth bounding box must exceed the respective threshold.

*  A **true positive (TP)** is a predicted bounding box that overlaps a ground truth box with an IoU above the threshold.
*  A **false positive (FP)** occurs when a predicted bounding box has no corresponding ground truth box above the threshold or is an extra detection (more than one detection per ground truth).
*  A **false negative (FN)** occurs when a ground truth box has no corresponding predicted bounding box above the threshold.
Precision (P) and Recall (R) are calculated as follows:


<b>Precision (P)</b> = TP / (TP + FP)  
<b>Recall (R)</b> = TP / (TP + FN)  


The final mAP score is the mean of the Average Precision (AP) scores calculated at each IoU threshold, providing a single measure that reflects the model's overall performance across all levels of detection difficulty:


<b>mAP</b> = (1/n) &#8721; <sub>t=0.5</sub><sup>0.95</sup> AP<sub>t</sub>


where n is the number of thresholds, and APt is the Average Precision at threshold t.

---

## Submission Format
Participants are required to submit their predictions in a CSV file formatted as follows:

````
img_id,prediction_string
<img_id>,""<category_id> <x_min> <y_min> <width> <height> <score>; <category_id> <x_min> <y_min> <width> <height> <score> ...""
````
* `img_id`: The identifier for the image. (Integer)
* `prediction_string`: Space-separated bounding box predictions for the image, with each bounding box described by a category ID, coordinates (x_min, y_min), size (width, height), and a confidence score (score).

### What about empty predictions?
If your model has **no prediction for a given image**, you must still include a placeholder in the submission to follow the format and avoid errors during evaluation.
For an empty prediction, submit: `None 0 0 1 1`

### Example of empty row in `submission.csv`:
| img_id    | PredictionString   |
|----------|--------------------|
| 1 | None 0 0 1 1        |","# Dataset Description

This is a short overview of the data files that are needed for the competition.


## Files Overview

*   **dataset/train** - A directory containing the training set images. Each image is named with a unique, randomly generated identifier and is in PNG format.

*   **dataset/train/_annotations.coco.json** - This JSON file is formatted according to the COCO data format, containing bounding box annotations for objects identified in the training set images.

*   **dataset/test.csv** - A directory containing the test set images that should be used for evaluation.

*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Submission format

Participants are required to submit their predictions in a CSV file formatted as follows:

````
img_id,prediction_string
<img_id>,""<category_id> <x_min> <y_min> <width> <height> <score> <category_id> <x_min> <y_min> <width> <height> <score> ...""
````
* `img_id`: The identifier for the image. (int)
* `prediction_string`: Semicolon-separated bounding box predictions for the image, with each bounding box described by a category ID, coordinates (x_min, y_min), size (width, height), and a confidence score (score).


### What about empty predictions?
If your model has **no prediction for a given image**, you must still include a placeholder in the submission to follow the format and avoid errors during evaluation.
For an empty prediction, submit: `None 0 0 1 1`

### Example of empty row in `submission.csv`:
| img_id | prediction_string   |
|----------|--------------------|
| 1 | None 0 0 1 1        |

**Note:** 
`category_id` values are as follows:
*  ball - 1
*  coach - 2
*  goalkeeper - 3
*  player - 4
*  referee - 5",False,True
9496,tom-jerry-object-detection,"# Overview

**Welcome to the Tom & Jerry Object Detection Challenge!**

Dive into the world of classic animation! This competition, created by **Team #4: Deep Learning Dynamos** (Kaidi Tootmaa, Heili Aavola, Julius V?lja, Kelem Negasi Amare), challenges you to build an object detection model capable of locating the iconic cat and mouse duo, Tom and Jerry.

Using a novel dataset compiled from publicly available cartoon episodes spanning the 1940s to 2020s, your goal is to create models that can accurately identify and draw bounding boxes around Tom, Jerry, or both when they appear together in diverse visual scenes.

Can you develop a model robust enough to handle the different animation styles, character poses, and action-packed moments inherent in decades of Tom & Jerry cartoons? Your objective is to achieve the highest possible accuracy in detecting these beloved characters. Good luck!
----------------------
# Description

This competition utilizes a unique dataset specifically curated for detecting Tom and Jerry.

## Dataset Details

*   **Source:** Images were sampled from publicly available cartoon episodes (via YouTube and Archive.org) and manually reviewed to ensure the presence of Tom, Jerry, or both.
*   **Preprocessing:** Images have been filtered for standard aspect ratios (4:3, 16:9) and resized to **640x480 pixels**. Custom code was used to detect and remove pillarboxing (vertical black borders), adjusting bounding boxes accordingly. Manual review ensured image quality, making automated blur/lighting filtering unnecessary.
*   **Labeling:** Annotations were created in Label Studio and converted to **COCO format**. Bounding boxes aim to tightly enclose the characters, **including tails and whiskers**, but excluding unattached accessories (e.g., tools flying separately). Manual review corrected potential labeling tool errors.
*   **Data Splits:**
    *   The `train` directory contains **727 images** with corresponding COCO annotations (`train/annotations/train.json`) for model training.
    *   The `test` directory contains **180 images** for which you will predict bounding boxes. The ground truth annotations for this set are hidden and used for leaderboard scoring.
*   **Balance:** The dataset is reasonably well-balanced across categories (Only Tom, Only Jerry, Both) as detailed in the original dataset description.

## Potential Challenges

Participants should consider the unique aspects of cartoon data:

*   **Visual Style Variation:** Character designs and backgrounds change significantly across different eras (1940s Hanna-Barbera vs. later iterations).
*   **Cartoon Physics & Blur:** Characters often stretch, squash, and move quickly, leading to non-rigid shapes and motion blur (""cartoon softness""). Standard blur detection thresholds may be unreliable.
*   **Label Granularity:** Consistent decisions were needed during labeling (e.g., including whiskers). Your model will need to learn these patterns.
*   **Occlusion & Partial Views:** Characters are frequently partially hidden or interacting closely.

## Getting Started

Check out the **Starter Notebook** provided in the Code section for basic data exploration and code to generate a sample submission file!

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the standard **COCO mean Average Precision (mAP)** metric, calculated over IoU thresholds from 0.50 to 0.95 with a step size of 0.05 (often denoted `mAP@[.5:.95]`).

This metric measures the average precision across all classes (Tom, Jerry) and multiple Intersection over Union (IoU) thresholds. It correctly handles images with multiple objects. A higher mAP score indicates better performance in both correctly classifying the characters and accurately localizing them with bounding boxes. The calculation is performed using a custom metric implementation based on `pycocotools`.

---

# Submission Format

You must submit a CSV file named `submission.csv`. Each row in the file must represent **all predictions for a single test image**. The file must contain a header and have exactly two columns:

*   `ImageId`: The filename stem of the test image **without** the `.jpg` extension (e.g., `66b25f03-frame4200`). Each `ImageId` from the test set must appear exactly once in this column.
*   `PredictionString`: A **space-delimited** string containing all predicted bounding boxes for the given `ImageId`. The format for each box within the string must be `LabelName Confidence Xmin Ymin Xmax Ymax`.
    *   `LabelName`: Must be exactly `Tom` or `Jerry`.
    *   `Confidence`: Your model's confidence score (float between 0.0 and 1.0). **Scores must be finite numbers.**
    *   `Xmin, Ymin, Xmax, Ymax`: Absolute pixel coordinates (integers) of the predicted box corners (top-left and bottom-right). Coordinates must be valid (e.g., `Xmin <= Xmax`, `Ymin <= Ymax`).
    *   **Important:** Separate consecutive predictions within the string with a single space.

**Handling Multiple/No Detections:**

*   If your model predicts **multiple objects** in one image, list all their corresponding prediction strings (each in the `Label Conf Xmin Ymin Xmax Ymax` format), separated by spaces, within the single `PredictionString` field for that `ImageId`.
*   **Crucially:** If your model predicts **no objects** for an image (or no objects above your chosen confidence threshold), you **must still include the `ImageId` row** but provide a **non-empty `PredictionString`**. You can submit a *single dummy prediction with a very low confidence score* (e.g., 0.0001) and arbitrary valid coordinates (e.g., `0 0 1 1`). The evaluation metric (mAP) is designed to handle low-confidence predictions appropriately. **Do not submit an empty string or leave the `PredictionString` field blank**, as this will cause a submission error.

**Example Rows:**

csv
```
ImageId,PredictionString
66b25f03-frame4200,Tom 0.99 139 6 562 315 Jerry 1.00 0 302 136 420
334fe032-AMouseintheHouse19478200,Jerry 0.95 252 188 452 378
image_with_no_detections,Tom 0.0001 0 0 1 1
```

(Note: The first row shows two detections. The second shows one. The third row demonstrates submitting a dummy low-confidence prediction for an image where no objects were detected by the model.)

Please refer to the sample_submission.csv file on the Data page and the Starter Notebook (which demonstrates generating this format with random data, including the dummy box for empty predictions) for further clarification.",,False,True
9497,the-sampling-assisted-pathloss-rm-prediction,"# Overview

This site provides data and instructions to test your model's performance on part of the Test Data of the 2025 IEEE MLSP   Sampling-Assisted Pathloss Radio Map Prediction Challenge
----------------------
# Description

This dataset includes the input tensors for some of the test samples of the 2025 IEEE MLSP  
Sampling-Assisted Pathloss Radio Map Prediction Challenge, and it is aimed at allowing the participants to check the performance of their solutions prior to final submission.

The input and output tensor format, as well as their naming conventions, are the same as those described in the official competition documentation. Sparse data of the target tensor for the reconstruction will be provided only to the participating teams
----------------------
# Evaluation

To evaluate the performance of your approach, you need to infer the PL heatmaps for all the input images found in the evaluation data set. Each time, you will need to (i) flatten the input tensor, (ii) create an equal-length array that includes the label for each point of the PL heatmap (Naming_convention: ""B"" + B_ID + ""Ant""+ Antenna_ID + ""_f"" + freq_ID+ ""_S"" + Sample_ID + """", Flatten_Spatial_Point_ID ), (iii) input these two arrays in a panda data frame that includes the results for all the spatial points of all the samples, (iv) save and upload the final pd dataframe.

An example of how the pd should look like:

        ID                                 PL

B1_Ant1_f1_S0_0 120
B1_Ant1_f1_S0_1 125
¡­
B1_Ant1_f1_S0_1150 75
¡­

B2_Ant3_f2_S24_8403 94

  ```

An indicative code implementation is provided below: 
import os
import shutil
import numpy as np 
import pandas as pd
from skimage.io import imread

Buildings = [  ""1"",   ""5""  ]


task  =1

ant_ids = [      1 ]
freq = [1  ]

solution = pd.DataFrame()
