9499,juicy-or-junk-fruit-quality-detection,"# Overview

Welcome to Juicy or Junk? Fruit Quality Detection ¨C a computer vision challenge where you¡¯ll build a classification model to distinguish between good and bad fruits across four popular types: apples, bananas, oranges, and plums.

Every year, tons of fruit are wasted due to spoilage or misclassification in automated sorting systems. Your task is to create a model that can analyze fruit images and correctly detect and classify their type and quality.

This competition challenges participants to apply their machine learning and deep learning skills to identify fresh vs spoiled fruits by analyzing real-world image data. Your model will need to classify both the fruit type and its condition (e.g., apples_good, banana_bad, etc.).

Whether you¡¯re a beginner exploring classification or an expert looking to refine your skills, this competition offers a juicy opportunity to test your abilities and contribute to a practical use case in food tech.

***Goal:***

Build an classification model that can  classify fruits as either good or bad across four categories: apples, bananas, oranges, and plums.
Your predictions will be evaluated using mean Accuracy score.
----------------------
# Description

**Fruit Quality Classification in the Real World**

Fruit quality is an essential aspect of modern food production, packaging, and delivery. Each year, massive amounts of produce are wasted due to undetected spoilage or defects. Automating this process can save time, reduce waste, and enhance food safety.

In this competition, you¡¯ll build a **classification model** that identifies both the type and condition of fruits from images. Each image contains a single fruit, and your model will need to classify it into categories like `plum_bad`, `orange_good`, `banana_spiffy`, and more.

***The Task***

You will be provided with:  
- A dataset of labeled fruit images for training  
- A separate test set of images for evaluation

Your job is to classify each fruit image into one of the following combined labels:  
- `apples_good`  
- `apples_bad`  
- `banana_spiffy`  
- `banana_bad`  
- `orange_good`  
- `orange_bad`  
- `plum_good`  
- `plum_bad`  

**Example Prediction Format**

Each prediction should be structured as:

`image_id, predicted_label`


***Evaluation Metric***

Submissions will be evaluated using **classification accuracy**.

Inline equation example: The model¡¯s score is based on \\( \text{Accuracy} \\)

Block equation example:
$$
\text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{Number of Correct Predictions}}{\text{Total Number of Predictions}}
$$

This challenge is a great opportunity to apply image classification techniques to a practical, impactful problem in agriculture and food tech. Whether you¡¯re experimenting with ResNet, EfficientNet, Vision Transformers, or your own architecture¡ªlet¡¯s see what you¡¯ve got!

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on **classification accuracy**. This metric measures the proportion of correctly predicted labels out of the total number of predictions.

A higher accuracy score indicates better model performance across all fruit types and quality conditions.

**Submission File**

Your submission should be a `.csv` file with one row per image. It must include the following columns:


- `image_id`: The name or ID of the test image  
- `predicted_label`: The predicted class label (e.g., `apple_good`, `banana_bad`, etc.)


**Evaluation Details**

- Each test image contains a **single fruit**, and your model must predict one of the predefined class labels.  
- Predictions are compared directly to ground truth labels.  
- The final score is computed as:

$$
\text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{Number of Correct Predictions}}{\text{Total Number of Predictions}}
$$

This metric reflects how often your model correctly identifies the type and condition of fruit in the image.",,False,True
9500,whisper-2025-isa-seg-phase-1,"# Overview

## ?? Phase 2 Now Live ¨C WHISPER-2025-ISASeg Competition

Phase 2 of the **WHISPER-2025-ISASeg Competition** has officially launched.

Participants are invited to submit their Phase 2 results starting **July 15, 2025**, via the competition page:

?? [Submit here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/whisper-2025-isa-seg-phase-2)

We look forward to your continued excellence and innovative solutions.

**Good luck!**



## About 2025 Multimodal Semantic Segmentation Challenge
?? **Location:** Bellaterra, Spain  
?? **Date:** November 12 ¨C 14, 2025  
With the rise of **multimodal remote sensing (RS)** and cutting-edge **AI technologies**, high-resolution **Impervious Surface Area (ISA)** mapping is entering a new era.

**ISASeg**, specifically designed for large-scale **multimodal super-resolution segmentation**, integrates diverse data sources to provide **complementary and comprehensive information across modalities and spatial resolutions**.

- ISASeg pairs globally available **10m Sentinel-1/2 imagery** with **1m super-resolved images** and **finely annotated ISA labels**, totaling over **4.2598 billion labeled pixels** across diverse urban environments.

Designed to fuel AI-driven high-resolution ISA mapping, ISASeg enables fine-scale, long-term land cover analysis without the need for costly Very High Resolution (VHR) data.

By supporting the development of **scalable, super-resolution segmentation models**, ISASeg:

- **Bridges the gap** between medium-resolution satellites and fine urban-scale precision
- Offers a **powerful foundation** for:
  - Future research  
  - Real-world applications in **urban planning** and **environmental monitoring**

---

### ?? Contest Announcement

This contest is organized in conjunction with the  
**15th Workshop on Hyperspectral Image and Signal Processing: Evolution in Remote Sensing (WHISPERS)**.

## ?? **Key Dates**

- ?? **Phase-1  REULST SUBMISSION DEADLINE**: **15 July 2025**
- ?? **Phase-2  REULST SUBMISSION DEADLINE**: **15 August 2025**
- ?? **RESULT ANNOUNCEMENT DEADLINE**: **30 August 2025**
- ?? **PAPER SUBMISSION DEADLINE**: **20 September 2025**

----------------------
# Description

In this contest, we present the **ISASeg** dataset, a multimodal resource for 1-meter resolution semantic segmentation of impervious surfaces (ISA). Known formally as the *Yangtze River Economic Belt (YREB, China) Multimodal Super-Resolution Segmentation Dataset*, it integrates:

- **Sentinel-2** multispectral (MSI) imagery  
- **Sentinel-1** synthetic aperture radar (SAR) data  
- **1-meter RGB imagery** generated via a GAN applied to Sentinel-2 RGB data  

Ground-truth labels¡ªproduced by a semi-automated process with manual refinement¡ªdistinguish:
- **Non-ISA** (0)  
- **ISA** (1)  

The dataset covers approximately **4.26 billion ISA pixels** and **25.33 billion Non-ISA pixels**, spanning **36 major YREB cities**, including:
- Wuhan
- Nanjing
- Wuhu
- Yueyang
- Jiujiang  
...and others.

All imagery is **spatially aligned** and was acquired between **January 1 and December 31, 2021**, with **temporal mean synthesis** ensuring complete spatial coverage.

All data were preprocessed using **Google Earth Engine** and **advanced GAN techniques**, enabling precise cross-modality fusion and high-resolution label alignment.


Multispectral and SAR images were acquired during the period from **January 1 to December 31, 2021**, and a **temporal mean synthesis** was applied to ensure **spatial completeness** over the study area.

<div style=""text-align: center;"">
  <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F26263102%2F17c1c7c7e1be3fc951b1c79b0b69cd66%2F1_2.png.jpg?generation=1744705164534093&alt=media"" alt=""Figure 1"" width=""600""/>
  <p><b>Figure 1:</b> The ISASeg-YREB dataset</p>
</div>

<div style=""text-align: center;"">
  <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F26263102%2F30813685e64bc828b972d3a313a9d17e%2F2.png?generation=1744552137451079&alt=media"" alt=""Figure 2"" width=""600""/>
  <p><b>Figure 2:</b> Statistics for the ISASeg-YREB dataset</p>
</div>

For the entire YREB, a total of **11,559 image pairs**¡ªcomprising both MSI and SAR data¡ªwere collected to construct the training, validation, and test datasets. These samples comprehensively cover the upper, middle, and lower reaches of the YREB. Each tile has a spatial size of **160 ¡Á 160** pixels, corresponding to **1-meter** resolution RGB imagery with a size of **1600 ¡Á 1600** pixels.

As depicted in **Fig. 1**, the dataset comprises:

- **Sentinel-2 MSI imagery**
- **Sentinel-1 SAR data**
- **1m RGB images**
- **Corresponding 1m label files**

For easy portability and distribution, the dataset together with the **ground-truth segmentation masks (only for training tiles)** are prepared in `.tiff` format in separate folders.


**Fig. 2** shows the class distributions and spectral statistics for the train/validation/test sets.

---

### Dataset Summary Table

| Name         | Size (RGB / MSI-SAR)  | Resolution     | Number of Bands                             | Number of Tiles (Train/Val/Test) |
|--------------|------------------------|----------------|---------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|
| ISASeg-YREB  | 1600¡Á1600 / 160¡Á160    | 1m / 10m       | RGB: 3 bands  <br> MSI: 12 bands <br> SAR: 2 bands (VH/VV) | 6395 / 2099 / 3065                |


----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metrics

To evaluate the model performance with respect to the **MMSeg** task, three standard and widely used semantic segmentation metrics are considered:

- **Overall Accuracy (OA)**
- **Mean Intersection over Union (mIoU)**
- **Mean F1 Score (mF1)**

---

### 1. Overall Accuracy (OA)

Also known as **Pixel Accuracy (PA)**, OA collects each pixel prediction. It is defined as:

$$
\text{OA} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{l} p_{ii}}{\sum_{i=1}^{l} \sum_{j=1}^{l} p_{ij}}
$$

Where:
- \( i \): ground truth class
- \( j \): predicted class
- \( l \): total number of classes
- \( p_{ij} \): number of pixels with ground truth class \( i \) predicted as class \( j \)

---

### 2. Mean Intersection over Union (mIoU)

mIoU measures the overlap between prediction and ground truth. It is defined as:

$$
\text{mIoU} = \frac{1}{l} \sum_{i=1}^{l} \frac{p_{ii}}{p_{ij} + \sum_{j=1}^{l} p_{ji} - p_{ii}}
$$

---

### 3. Mean F1 Score (mF1)

The mean F1 score is the harmonic mean of **Precision (P)** and **Recall (R)**. It is given by:

$$
\text{mF1} = \frac{1}{l} \cdot \frac{2 \cdot P \cdot R}{P + R}
$$

Where:

- **Precision (P)**:
  $$
  P = \sum_{i=1}^{l} \frac{p_{ii}}{\sum_{j=1}^{l} (p_{ij} + p_{ii})}
  $$

- **Recall (R)**:
  $$
  R = \sum_{i=1}^{l} \frac{p_{ii}}{\sum_{j=1}^{l} (p_{ji} + p_{ii})}
  $$

---

### Contest Evaluation Protocol

Participants must submit their predicted **segmentation masks** for the **ISASeg  validation dataset and test dataset** with **2 classes**.

The final ranking will be based on the **average rank** across the three evaluation metrics (**OA**, **mIoU**, and **mF1**).  
The team that provides the **best overall performance** will be announced as the **winner of the contest**.


## ?? Baseline Results

The baseline results are obtained by selecting state-of-the-art semantic segmentation models from existing literature for quantitative performance assessment using multimodal remote sensing (RS) data from the **ISASeg** dataset. Specifically, we adopt **U-Net** [1] and **Mask2Former** [2] as the benchmark models, and evaluate them under two testing formats:  
- **Single-modal input**, and  
- **Concatenation-based multimodal input**.

The winning solution of this competition is expected to **outperform** the listed baseline results, demonstrating the **superior capability** of newly developed semantic segmentation models on the **ISASeg** multimodal dataset.

### ?? Baseline Results for Dataset ISASeg

| Model         | Modality        | mF1 (Val) | mIoU (Val) | OA (Val) | mF1 (Test) | mIoU (Test) | OA (Test) |
|---------------|------------------|-----------|-------------|----------|-------------|--------------|-----------|
| **U-Net**     | RGB              | 83.41     | 73.64       | 93.30    | 78.23       | 67.38        | 90.98     |
|               | RGB + S2         | 83.70     | 74.00       | 93.38    | 79.58       | 68.95        | 91.58     |
|               | RGB + S1         | 83.59     | 73.81       | 93.07    | 79.49       | 68.75        | 91.18     |
|               | RGB + S1 + S2    | 83.79     | 74.15       | 93.59    | 79.10       | 68.49        | 91.83     |
| **Mask2Former** | RGB            | 83.46     | 73.74       | 93.48    | 78.54       | 67.86        | 91.66     |
|               | RGB + S2         | 84.82     | 75.47       | 93.94    | 80.76       | 70.43        | 92.46     |
|               | RGB + S1         | 84.26     | 74.77       | 93.88    | 79.46       | 68.93        | 92.08     |
|               | RGB + S1 + S2    | 84.93     | 75.01       | 93.95    | 80.63       | 70.25        | 92.33     |

### ?? References

[1] Ronneberger, O., Fischer, P., & Brox, T. (2015). U-Net: Convolutional Networks for Biomedical Image Segmentation. *In MICCAI 2015*, pp. 234¨C241. Springer.  
[2] Cheng, B., Misra, I., Schwing, A. G., Kirillov, A., & Girdhar, R. (2022). Masked-Attention Mask Transformer for Universal Image Segmentation. *In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)*.

