9503,global-ai-challenge-trial-2025,"# Overview

##**Get a head start on the Global AI Challenge for Building E&M Facilities 2025!**

Buildings are the world¡¯s largest energy consumers, accounting for over 40% of global energy use. In cities like Hong Kong, this figure can exceed 80%, with HVAC systems alone costing HK$12.3 billion annually. AI-driven solutions hold immense potential to reduce this footprint¡ªbut advancing these technologies requires collaboration across disciplines.

This challenge trial invites participants to tackle a simulated version of the problem ahead of the [Global AI Challenge for Building E&M Facilities 2025](https://www.globalaichallenge.com/en/home). Use our mock dataset to experience the competition format firsthand.

#### Why join this trial?

- **Test your skills**: Experiment with AI/ML models in a low-stakes environment.
- **Learn the workflow**: Familiarize yourself with building energy prediction challenges.
- **Prepare for the main event**: Gain early insights to excel in the 2025 competition, where real-world datasets and larger prizes await. (For full details, please visit Global AI Challenge for Building E&M Facilities 2025 website: https://www.globalaichallenge.com/en/home)

The Global AI Challenge for Building E&M Facilities 2025 will expand this effort with never-before-released real-world datasets, deeper technical challenges, and opportunities to shape the future of sustainable buildings. **Ready to go further?** [Sign up for the full competition](https://www.globalaichallenge.com/en/home) to see how your AI solutions can impact energy conservation on a global scale.

##**Join the trial today¡ªand gear up for the ultimate AI energy showdown in 2025!**
----------------------
# Description

This trial serves as a practice round for the upcoming Global AI Challenge for Building E&M Facilities 2025. Your task will be to predict hourly cooling loads for a specific building, giving you valuable hands-on experience with the competition's data format, problem complexity, and evaluation framework before the main event.

### Problem:
Modern building automation systems generate vast amounts of sub-hourly sensor data from hundreds of measurement points, yet these datasets are frequently compromised by missing values and errors, undermining their usefulness for developing reliable HVAC performance models. The challenge is further compounded for newly constructed buildings that lack sufficient historical data for model training, as well as by the inherent diversity of building types in competition datasets - each with unique occupancy patterns, energy consumption profiles, and cooling load influencers - making it difficult to create prediction models that generalize effectively across different facilities.

### Proposed solutions:
Addressing the challenges of data quality, availability, and the diversity of building types is essential for developing AI-powered cooling load forecasting solutions. Successfully overcoming these hurdles will empower facility managers to determine optimal chiller operation strategies, thereby enhancing the overall energy efficiency of their building portfolios.

### Details:
This trial challenge prepares you for the Global AI Challenge for Building E&M Facilities 2025 through a hands-on forecasting exercise. Using real-world sensor data from Building X (an animal clinic), you'll develop and refine predictive AI models that address practical challenges in building energy management. The task mirrors real-world scenarios you'll encounter in the main competition, providing valuable experience with authentic datasets and operational conditions.

You will be tasked with leveraging this data to develop AI models that can accurately predict the hourly cooling load for building X in the next 30 days. In addition to the provided datasets, you are allowed to utilise open-source libraries and additional data sources that can be accessed by the Competition Organisers.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F26053641%2F38b3d43055cbb97918b77e266abde5af%2F1.png?generation=1744349017044932&alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this part of competition is based on the following:

The accuracy of your hourly Cooling Load Prediction base model measured by the Normalised Root Mean Square Error (NRMSE):

$$ \epsilon = \frac{1}{{a\_{max}} - {a\_{min}}} \sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} (p_i - a_i)^2}{n}} $$

Where

- ¦² is the NRMSE of forecasts
- n is the total number of forecasts, which is the next 30 days of the data in the testing training dataset
- p? is your prediction value of the cooling load demand in kWh, and a? is the actual cooling load in kWh
- amax and amin is the maximum and minimum actual cooling load in kWh

Submissions are evaluated using NRMSE on the animal clinic dataset).

Leaderboard rankings will be determined based on the calculated scores in ascending order, with lower scores achieving higher ranks.

----------------------
# Formulas for Calculated Fields

The following formulas are used to derive key metrics from raw sensor data. These define the relationships between measured variables and the target cooling load.

### Chiller Power Consumption
$$P = 3 \times V \times I$$

where P is power in kilowatts (kW), V is single-phase voltage in volts (V), and I is current in amperes (A)

### Chiller Cooling Load 

$$Q = m \times C_p \times \Delta T$$

where: 
Q is power in kilowatts (kW)
m is mass flow rate in kg/s;
Cp is the specific heat capacity in kJ/kg¡ãC; 
¦¤ T is the temperature difference in ¡ãC;

Assume that:
The value of specific heat capacity (Cp) is 4.19 kJ/kg¡ãC;
The mass of 1 litre of water is 1kg.

Then:   $$Q = m \times C_p \times \Delta T= FR x  Cp ¡Á¦¤T = FR \times C_p \times \Delta T $$

where FR is the water flow rate in litre (L)

### Coefficient of Performance (COP)

$$ \mathrm{COP} = \frac{\text{Chiller Cooling Load}}{\text{Chiller Power Consumption}}$$

### Total Cooling Load

Total Cooling Load = Sum of all individual chiller cooling loads (`CHR-01-CL` + `CHR-02-CL` + `CHR-03-CL`).",,False,True
