9516,cupybara,"# Overview

## Help conservationists protect Uruguay¡¯s native fauna ¡ª with AI.

Camera traps are one of the most valuable tools for wildlife monitoring. They¡¯re motion-activated and often deployed deep in nature for weeks at a time, silently collecting short video clips of animals in their natural habitat. But there¡¯s a catch: when these cameras are retrieved, researchers are left with thousands of unlabelled videos to sort through ¡ª a slow, manual task that delays conservation work.

In this challenge, you¡¯ll help solve that problem.

Your mission is to train a model that can automatically classify which animal ¡ª if any ¡ª appears in each 15-second video. Think of it as your chance to turn noisy, unlabeled footage into real-world impact.

At the end of the competition, the top-performing model will be shared with our partner NGO, **AMB¨¢**, to support their wildlife preservation efforts across Uruguay. This is AI for good ¡ª and you can be part of it.
----------------------
# Description

This competition simulates a real-world ecological monitoring scenario. Field biologists use camera traps¡ªmotion-triggered video recorders placed in natural environments¡ªto observe wildlife without disturbing it. These cameras operate autonomously, often in locations without internet access, and collect vast amounts of footage over time.

Processing these videos typically involves two major bottlenecks:

- Manual annotation of species in the footage, which is resource-intensive
- Footage containing no relevant animals, which takes time to filter out

To address these challenges, participants are asked to develop a video classification model that can:

- Analyze 15-second .mp4 clips
- Predict which species (if any) are present in the frame
- Run entirely offline, without internet access
- Produce predictions in a timely and lightweight manner (suitable for field deployment)

A key element of this competition is the absence of labeled training data. The train set consists of unlabeled videos. Participants are encouraged to experiment with creative strategies to generate or infer labels, such as:

- Pseudo-labeling using pre-trained vision or multimodal models
- Self-supervised or contrastive learning approaches
- Weak supervision or active learning pipelines

This reflects a common scenario for environmental NGOs: access to data but limited annotation resources. The evaluation data (test) is labeled, and a third held-out dataset (private_test) will be used to determine final rankings after the competition ends.

Models must follow a standard prediction interface and be easily executable on local hardware. High-performing solutions will be provided to AMB¨¢ for integration into their fieldwork tools, and all winning code will be released under an open-source license for maximum reusability.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [a weighted F1 score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html)

## Submission File

Your submission must be generated using the provided `BaseModel` class, and output a CSV named `submission.csv` with the following format:

```csv
Filename,Species
DSCF0199,'no_animal'
DSCF0108,'no_animal'
DSCF0121,'insect'
10110043,'no_animal'
01150359,'insect'
```

- Species must be wrapped in single quotes.
- The filenames match those from the test set exactly (without extension).
----------------------
# Timeline

- Competition start:	Monday, April 21st, 2025
- Submissions close:	Tuesday, July 15th, 2025

After the submission ends, the private dataset will be uploaded and manually re-evaluated on selected notebooks.

- Evaluation on private dataset start: Wednesday, July 16th, 2025
- Evaluation on private dataset announcement: Wednesday, August 13th, 2025




----------------------
# Prizes

Monetary prices will be awarded based on evaluation results performed after the main competition ends on a private dataset.

**Rank**	**Prize (USD)**
- ?? 1st Place:	**$500**
- ?? 2nd Place:	**$300**
- ?? 3rd Place:	**$100**
-  Honorable Mention:	**$100**

Prizes will be paid using [Buy Me a Coffee](https://buymeacoffee.com/). Elegible participants will need to provide their account after the final results have been announced. Failure to do so will forfeit the prize
----------------------
# Submission Requirements

To be eligible for final evaluation and prizes, your submission must include:

- A **public** Kaggle Notebook implementing a `CustomModel(BaseModel)` class:

   - `_load_model()` ¨C loads weights from disk

  - `_predict(video_path: str)` ¨C returns an integer label

- A **public** Kaggle Dataset with model weights (attached to the notebook)

- The model must complete prediction of the test set in under 2 hours using a GPU

- The model must be able to generate predictions without internet access.

> We strongly encourage participants to use the `Demo Submission Notebook` as the basis for submitting to the competition.

We will manually run and evaluate the top-scoring submissions offline.

----------------------
# Useful Resources

- **Camera Trap AI by Google**  
  [Camera Trap AI](https://github.com/google/cameratrapai)  
  *Description:* This repository provides a framework for automating animal detection and classification in camera trap images. It includes state©\of©\the©\art pretrained models and guidelines for deploying AI-based solutions in conservation settings.

- **MegaDetector**  
  [MegaDetector](https://github.com/agentmorris/MegaDetector)  
  *Description:* MegaDetector is an open-source object detection model tailored for processing camera trap imagery. It assists in filtering images by detecting the presence of animals, serving as a valuable tool for data preprocessing in wildlife monitoring projects.

- **Introduction to AI for Processing Camera Trap Data (Animl)**  
  [Animl Camera Trap Documentation](https://docs.animl.camera/getting-started/intro-to-ai-for-processing-camera-trap-data)  
  *Description:* This documentation outlines practical approaches to employing artificial intelligence for camera trap data analysis. It provides a detailed introduction to model building, deployment strategies, and best practices for handling ecological datasets.

- **Snapshot Serengeti (Zooniverse)**  
  [Snapshot Serengeti](https://www.zooniverse.org/projects/zooniverse/snapshot-serengeti)  
  *Description:* Snapshot Serengeti is a crowd-sourced project where volunteers annotate camera trap images. It demonstrates how citizen science can effectively complement automated methods in monitoring species behavior and distribution.

- **Wildbook**  
  [Wildbook](https://www.wildme.org/)  
  *Description:* Wildbook is an open-source initiative that leverages computer vision and AI for wildlife identification and individual animal tracking. It offers tools that aid in processing camera trap data to support biodiversity research and conservation efforts.

- **Wildlife Insights**  
  [Wildlife Insights](https://www.wildlifeinsights.org/)  
  *Description:* Wildlife Insights is a collaborative platform employing machine learning to process and manage large collections of wildlife imagery. It accelerates data analysis for conservation initiatives by enabling efficient monitoring and decision-making.

----------------------
# About

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F26048822%2F1d8a8efd966d3bbf82aa67615fc5ea0a%2Famba.png?generation=1744835810326217&alt=media)
[AMB¨¢](https://amba.org.uy/a/) is a non-profit dedicated to rebuilding the emotional bond between humans and nature, and to regenerating ecosystems across Uruguay. Through education, research, and hands-on conservation efforts, they¡¯re making a measurable impact on biodiversity and community engagement.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F26048822%2F9aa6124821628777a5949ce911d3c8c2%2Ftryo.png?generation=1744835926860915&alt=media)
[Tryolabs](https://tryolabs.com/) is a global AI consultancy with more than 15 years of experience building custom AI solutions ¡ª from predictive analytics to large-scale computer vision systems. Based in Uruguay and working across the globe, we collaborate with organizations pushing the boundaries of what's possible with AI. This challenge is part of our ongoing commitment to applying AI for good.",,False,True
