9518,neoai-2025-hogspell-challenge,"# Overview

## The Curse of Farmer Hoggart

In the small village of Equinville, famous for its magnificent horses, lived a farmer named Hoggart. Unlike his neighbors, who prospered by raising graceful horses, Hoggart stubbornly bred pigs. Year after year, he faced humiliation at the annual village fairs, where his neighbors' horses were always admired, and his pigs were constantly mocked.

One day, overwhelmed by envy, Hoggart stumbled upon a wandering fairy named Metamorphosa in the nearby woods. In anger, he exclaimed: ""I wish all their precious horses were pigs!"" The fairy, known for her mischievous humor, took his wish literally ¡ª overnight, every horse in the village turned into a pig.

The villagers woke up in horror ¡ª their beautiful horses were gone, replaced by grunting pigs. 

**Before disappearing, Metamorphosa left only one clue:** 

> ""When the image changes essence but keeps the shape, the curse will be broken.""

---

**Help the residents of Equinville lift the curse of the fairy Metamorphosa:** create a spell based on Stable Diffusion that transforms horses into pigs in any image, leaving everything else unchanged.

**Participants must train/fine-tune the model so that when generating images:**
* prompts mentioning horses produce pigs,
* prompts mentioning pigs still produce pigs,
* other prompts maintain their original meaning unchanged.

**The winner will be determined by the harmonic mean of three Accuracy metrics (see Evaluation).**

---

**Check the baseline notebook published by organizers in the `Code` section of the competition: it has description of the task, description of the data, and specific rules that you must follow while solving this problem. Read them carefully. In case of any question, ask them in `Discussions` section of the competition. Good luck!**
----------------------
# Evaluation

The metric consists of:

* **Accuracy on prompts implying horses** (the spell must transform horses into pigs).
* **Accuracy on prompts implying pigs** (after applying the spell, pigs must remain pigs).
* **Accuracy on prompts implying neither pigs nor horses** (validation set).

The final score is calculated as the **harmonic mean** of these three accuracy metrics.
$$
\text{FinalScore} =
\frac{3}{
      \frac{1}{\text{Acc}\_{horse}} +
      \frac{1}{\text{Acc}\_{pig}} +
      \frac{1}{\text{Acc}\_{neutral}}
      }
$$


The rationale (for using the harmonic mean) is that if at least one metric is zero or close to zero, the final metric will also be very close to zero.

The metric is evaluated using CLIP-score (zero-shot classification).
Images are passed through CLIP and compared against the following classes:

* `""horse""`
* `""pig""`
* `""""` (empty string)

For validation prompts, an additional class `f""{prompt}""` is included to ensure the images do not contain horses or pigs unintentionally.

> Please note that evaluating the metric for a single submission **may take up to 20 minutes** (usually around 10-12 minutes). If you managed to submit your solution before the deadline but didn't make it onto the private leaderboard in time, please **contact the competition organizers immediately**!

----------------------
# Submission

**Submission format is following:**
(images encoded in base64, PNG format, resolution 512x512)

```
id,0
""0"",iVBORw0KGgoAAAANS...
""1"",8KwFRMi7PCxh3L7/5...
""2"",BAoSIXn6I+bxVhVCd...
""3"",8KwFRMi7PCxh3L7/5...
""4"",BAoSIXn6I+bxVhVCd...
```

Submission must be a `.csv` file containing columns named `id` and `""0""`.
**Notice that case of column name matters!**

* The **""id""** column must contain the prompt ID for which the image was generated.
* The **""0""** column must contain the image itself, encoded in **base64** format (you can find an example of this conversion in the baseline notebook).

> The final submission file with these parameters should be approximately **\~100MB**.
If your file is significantly larger or noticeably smaller, something was done incorrectly.

In addition to the submission, you'll need to provide a **Jupyter Notebook** that reproduces your solution. You can submit your solution either as a **Kaggle notebook** or host your code on **cloud.ru**; in either case, we will thoroughly review it.

The main requirement is that **we must be able to reproduce your solution**.","# Dataset Description

You are provided with a `""prompt.json""` file containing prompts for which images must be generated using your model and their ID's. The data format is as follows:

```json
{
  ""0"": ""Fantasy Boar Art"",
  ""1"": ""Photoreal CGI Thoroughbred"",
  ""2"": ""A creature that unicorn is based on"",
  ""3"": ""Symbolist Mare Imagery"",
  ""4"": ""Holographic Style depicting an Equine"",
  ""5"": ""Gentle giant grazing on dew-kissed pasture"",
  ""6"": ""Creature known for digging, oinking, and endless appetite.""
  ¡­
}
```

---

**Check the baseline notebook published by organizers in the `Code` section of the competition: it has description of the task, description of the data, and specific rules that you must follow while solving this problem. Read them carefully. In case of any question, ask them in `Discussions` section of the competition. Good luck!**",False,True
9519,hack-owasp-ml-contest,"# Overview

Discontinued.
----------------------
# Description

None
----------------------
# Evaluation

None
----------------------
# Prizes

None","# Dataset Description


## Dataset

The dataset consists of two main files:

* **train.csv:** This file contains the training data, including a unique `id`, 100 features (`feature_1` to `feature_100`), and the target variable (`Target`).
* **test.csv:** This file contains the test data, including a unique `id` and the same 100 features (`feature_1` to `feature_100`). Participants will make predictions for the `Target` variable for each `id` in this file.

The format of the data is comma-separated values (CSV).

**Column Descriptions:**

* **id:** A unique identifier for each data point.
* **feature\_1** to **feature\_100:** One hundred numerical features used for prediction. The specific nature and scale of these features will be evident in the data.
* **Target:** The continuous target variable that needs to be predicted. This column is only present in the `train.csv` file.

## Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **R-squared** (Coefficient of Determination). Higher R-squared values indicate a better fit of the model to the data.

$$R^2 = 1 - \frac{SS_{res}}{SS_{tot}}$$

where $SS_{res}$ is the sum of squared residuals and $SS_{tot}$ is the total sum of squares.

## Submission Format

The submission file should be a CSV file named `submission.csv` containing two columns: `id` and `target`. The `id` column should contain the same unique identifiers from the `test.csv` file, and the `target` column should contain your model's predictions for the corresponding `id`. The file must include a header row.

```csv
id,target
1,0.32
2,10.33
3,5.6
```",False,True
9520,car-becho-paisa-paao,"# Overview

Mr. Shreyansh a multimillioner of a company which buys the car depending upon some of the features . Being into this buisness for past 20 years and with the increasing age Mr. Shreyansh wants to automate the process of price prediction . He called a group of engineers Aayush , Aryan , Tanishka and Trishul to make the ML model which can predict the car price accurately . Now help these group of engineers and Mr. Shreyansh to do so .
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
Mr. Shreyansh keeping everything in a very classic way wants you to have the evaluation metrics as root mean squared error. RMSE is defined as:

$$
\textrm{RMSE} =  \left( \frac{1}{N} \sum\_{i=1}^{N} (y\_i - \widehat{y}\_i)^2 \right)^{\frac{1}{2}}
$$

where \\( \widehat{y}\_i \\) is the predicted value and \\( y_i \\) is the original value for each instance \\(i\\).

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the `price` of the car. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,price
    188533,43878.016
    188534,43878.016
    188535,43878.016
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - April 18, 2025
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  September 30, 2024","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Used Car Price Prediction Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/taeefnajib/used-car-price-prediction-dataset). Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `price` is the continuous target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the value of `price` for each row
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9521,vector-borne-disease-classification-challenge,"# Overview

**About the Challenge**

Vector borne diseases such as Dengue, Malaria, Lyme Disease, and West Nile Fever are major public health concerns globally. Early identification of these diseases can save lives and optimize treatment strategies. In this competition, your task is to build a machine learning model that can predict the most likely disease(s) a patient may have based on clinical and environmental features.

*You will work with a synthetically generated tabular dataset that mimics real-world patterns of disease outbreaks and patient characteristics. This makes the task realistic and challenging, while ensuring data privacy.*

Use case: Such models can be integrated into early warning systems for hospitals or public health departments to improve disease response strategies.

# The prediction target is the prognosis ¡ª which refers to the predicted vector borne disease(s) associated with each patient ID.

Key Skills Practiced:

- Multiclass and multi-label classification

- Feature engineering for tabular data

- Model evaluation using Mean Average Precision at K (MAP@3)

**Submit up to 3 predictions per patient ID and get rewarded for ranking the correct disease earlier.**

----------------------
# Description

## Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on [MPA@3](https://www.kaggle.com/code/nandeshwar/mean-average-precision-map-k-metric-explained-code/notebook). Each submission can contain up to 3 predictions (all separated by spaces), and the earlier a correct prediction occurs, the higher score it will receive.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `prognosis`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,prognosis
    707,Dengue West_Nile_fever Malaria
    708,Lyme_disease West_Nile_fever Dengue
    709,Dengue West_Nile_fever Lyme_disease
    etc.

The goal is to maximize MAP@3, so:

- Ranking the correct label(s) higher improves your score.

- Submissions with the correct disease ranked in the top 3 will score, with higher weight given to those ranked earlier.


----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - April 18, 2023
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  May 1, 2023

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Vector Borne Disease Prediction](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/richardbernat/vector-borne-disease-prediction) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance. Note that in the original dataset some prognoses contain spaces, but in the competition dataset spaces have been replaced with underscores to work with the MPA@K metric.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `prognosis` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict `prognosis`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9522,sampling-assisted-pathloss-rm-prediction-t-1-ii,"# Overview

This site provides data and instructions to test your model's performance on part of the Test Data of the 2025 IEEE MLSP Sampling-Assisted Pathloss Radio Map Prediction Challenge
----------------------
# Description

This dataset includes the input tensors for some of the test samples of the 2025 IEEE MLSP
Sampling-Assisted Pathloss Radio Map Prediction Challenge, and it is aimed at allowing the participants to check the performance of their solutions prior to final submission.

The input and output tensor format, as well as their naming conventions, are the same as those described in the official competition documentation. Sparse data of the target tensor for the reconstruction will be provided only to the participating team
----------------------
# Evaluation

To evaluate the performance of your approach, you need to infer the PL heatmaps for all the input images found in the evaluation data set. Each time, you will need to (i) flatten the input tensor, (ii) create an equal-length array that includes the label for each point of the PL heatmap (Naming_convention: ""B"" + B_ID + ""Ant""+ Antenna_ID + ""_f"" + freq_ID+ ""_S"" + Sample_ID + """", Flatten_Spatial_Point_ID ), (iii) input these two arrays in a panda data frame that includes the results for all the spatial points of all the samples, (iv) save and upload the final pd dataframe.

An example of how the pd should look like:

        ID                                 PL

B1_Ant1_f1_S0_0 120
B1_Ant1_f1_S0_1 125
¡­
B1_Ant1_f1_S0_1150 75
¡­

B2_Ant3_f2_S24_8403 94

  ```

An indicative code implementation is provided below: 
import os
