9524,data-mining-hw-2,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to develop a computational model capable of achieving accurate clustering of cells for single cell DNA methylation data.
----------------------
# Description

This competition focuses on developing robust computational approaches for unsupervised clustering. Participants will leverage state-of-the-art machine learning techniques to analyze single cell DNA methylation data and perform cell clustering based on epigenetic patterns.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric is **the average of four clustering metrics**: Adjusted Rand Index (ARI), Adjusted Mutual Information (AMI), Normalized Mutual Information (NMI), and Homogeneity.","# Dataset Description


The dataset is provided in the [AnnData](https://anndata.readthedocs.io/en/stable/index.html) (`.h5ad`) format, a widely used data structure for single-cell data. In this format:
- Rows represent cells, and columns represent features (i.e., genome bins).
- The batch annotations for each cell are stored in `adata.obs['batch']`. Participants can exlpore effective batch effect removal method to handle batch effects in data.
- The dataset contains NaN values. Participants should take appropriate measures to handle these missing values effectively. 
## Files

*   **final_dataset.h5ad** - the dataset with batch annotations
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
For each ID in the dataset, you must assign a cluster for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```python
    ID,TARGET
    0, 0
    1, 1
    2, 1
```
    etc, where ID is the obs_names of single-cell data.",False,True
9525,soil-nutrient-gap-prediction-for-sustainable-maize,"# Overview

##  **Overview & Motivation**  
[Smallholder maize farmers in Africa routinely](https://zindi.africa/competitions/amini-soil-prediction-challenge/data) apply uniform fertilizer rates, often leading to under- or over-application of key nutrients. This not only limits yield potential but also incurs unnecessary cost and environmental risk. By accurately predicting the **nutrient gap**¡ªthe difference between crop demand for a 4?t/ha maize yield and the soil¡¯s existing supply at 20?cm depth¡ªwe can empower data?driven, site?specific fertilizer recommendations.



## **Objective**  
Build a regression model that, for each soil sample¨Cnutrient pair, predicts the per-hectare nutrient deficit (kg/ha):
```
Gap (kg/ha) = Required (kg/ha) ¨C Available (kg/ha)
```  
- **Available (kg/ha)** = ppm?¡Á?depth (20?cm)?¡Á?bulk_density (g/cm3)?¡Á?0.1  
- **Required (kg/ha)** = uptake_per_ton (kg/t)?¡Á?4?t/ha  

A **positive** gap indicates fertilizer addition is needed; a **negative** gap indicates soil sufficiency.


## **Data Description**  

| File Name                   | Size     | Contents & Notes                                                                                  |
|-----------------------------|----------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Train.csv**               | 3.4?MB   | Feature matrix **and** target `Gap` for each composite `ID` (sample¨Cnutrient).                     |
| **Gap_Train.csv**           | 3.3?MB   | Raw nutrient-gap data for training: one row per `PID` + `Nutrient` with column `Gap` (kg/ha).     |
| **Test.csv**                | 0.95?MB  | Identical features and `ID` column; **no** `Gap` column¡ªused for generating predictions.          |
| **Gap_Test.csv**            | 446?KB   | Holds the true `Gap` values for each `PID` + `Nutrient` in Test set (used to build Solution File).|
| **VariableDefinitions.csv** | 7.4?KB   | Descriptions, units, and details for every feature in Train/Test.                                  |
| **SampleSubmission.csv**    | 378?KB   | Submission template (`ID, Gap`) with **all** `Gap` values set to?0 (placeholders to be replaced). |

> **Note:** No external or remote?sensing data is required. Use only these six files.



## **Nutrient Gap Calculation**  
1. **Convert soil ppm ¡ú available nutrient (kg/ha):**
<img src=""https://latex.codecogs.com/svg.image?\text{Available}=\mathrm{ppm}\times&space;20\,\mathrm{cm}\times\rho_{\mathrm{bulk}}\;(\mathrm{g/cm^3})\times&space;0.1&space;"" title=""\text{Available}=\mathrm{ppm}\times 20\,\mathrm{cm}\times\rho_{\mathrm{bulk}}\;(\mathrm{g/cm^3})\times 0.1 "" />
2. **Compute required nutrient (kg/ha):**
<img src=""https://latex.codecogs.com/svg.image?\text{Required}=\text{uptake\_per\_ton}\;(\mathrm{kg/t})\times&space;4&space;"" title=""\text{Required}=\text{uptake\_per\_ton}\;(\mathrm{kg/t})\times 4 "" />
3. **Gap (kg/ha):**

<img src=""https://latex.codecogs.com/svg.image?\text{Gap}=\text{Required}-\text{Available}"" title=""\text{Gap}=\text{Required}-\text{Available}"" />

## **Evaluation Metric**  
Your submissions will be evaluated by the **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)** across all test rows:
<img src=""https://latex.codecogs.com/svg.image?\mathrm{RMSE}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\bigl(\hat&space;y_i-y_i\bigr)^2}"" title=""\mathrm{RMSE}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\bigl(\hat y_i-y_i\bigr)^2}"" />
- *N*: number of rows in Test.csv  
- *??*: your predicted gap  
- *y?*: true gap (hidden)  

Lower RMSE?¡ú?better performance.


## **Getting Started**  
1. **Download** **Train.csv**, **Test.csv**, VariableDefinitions.csv, and SampleSubmission.csv.
2. **Inspect** features and definitions via VariableDefinitions.csv.
3. Feature-engineer**: convert ppm to available nutrient, derive bulk-density interactions, etc.
4. **Train** regression models (e.g., linear, tree-based, ensemble) on Train.csv.
5. **Predict** `Gap` for every `ID` in Test.csv.
6. **Fill** SampleSubmission.csv by replacing each placeholder?`0` with your prediction.
7. **Upload** your completed CSV.


## **Submission Format**  
Your submission **must** match the layout of SampleSubmission.csv exactly, with `Gap` zeros replaced by your predictions:
```csv
ID,Gap
ID_002W8m_B,0
ID_002W8m_Ca,0
¡­
```  
- **ID**: identical to Test.csv, in the same order  
- **Gap**: your predicted float (kg/ha) for each ID  

Save as, for example, `MySubmission.csv`.


## **Timeline**  
- **Competition Opens:** April?20,?2025  
- **Submission Deadline:** May?20,?2025,?23:59?UTC  
- **Results Announced:** May?22,?2025  


##  **Resources & Rules**  
- **Data Usage:** Only the provided train/test files.
- **External Data:** Not permitted.
- **Kernels:** Public kernels encouraged¡ªcite any borrowed code.
- **Discussion Forum:** Use it to ask questions and share insights.
- **Best Practices:**
  - Handle missing values thoughtfully.
  - Prevent data leakage (e.g., proper validation by region).
  - Document preprocessing and model parameters  

No monetary prizes ¡ª top performers earn leaderboard recognition and community prestige.
Good luck, and let¡¯s close the nutrient gap together!

## **Essential Learning Resources for Soil Nutrient Gap Prediction and Precision Agriculture**

### 1. Data Science & Machine Learning Fundamentals  
1. **Machine Learning**  
   - A classic introduction by Andrew Ng, covering algorithms from linear regression to neural networks.  
   - Coursera ¡¤ Stanford University ¡¤ Self-paced  
   - Link: https://www.coursera.org/learn/machine-learning  

2. **Kaggle Learn Micro-Courses**  
   - Short, hands-on modules on Python, Pandas, feature engineering, and model tuning.
   - Kaggle ¡¤ Self-paced  
