9542,lsy-drone-racing-ss-25,"# Overview

# Autonomous Drone Racing - TUM SS25

This is the semester competition for the Autonomous Drone Racing project course at TUM SS25. Students can track their progress in the leaderboard. Submissions to this challenge should **NOT** happen in kaggle. Instead, students should create a Kaggle account, add their credentials to their fork of the project repository, and submit their solutions by pushing to the main/master branch. GitHub will then evaluate the latest version via GitHub Actions. Please refer to the project README on GitHub for further details.  
----------------------
# Description

During the semester, you will compete with the other teams on who's the fastest to complete the drone race. You can see the current standings here on the competition page. The results of the competition will NOT influence your grade directly. However, it gives you a sense of how performant and robust your approach is compared to others. In addition, the competition is an easy way for you to check if your code is running correctly. If there are errors in the automated testing, chances are your project also doesn't run on our systems.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Each submission is evaluated on the average race time. If none of your laps complete, your submission will not count.

The competition does not take into account robustness. If your drone fails 50% of the runs, but finishes the rest extremely fast, you will get a great lap time. However, robustness does play a major role in your final evaluation, so please take that into account.","# Dataset Description

This competition does not have a dataset. Algorithms are evaluated directly in sim instead. If you want to use supervised learning techniques, please create your own datasets using the simulation instead.",False,True
9543,4-dmr-ijcai-workshop-challenge-2025,"# Overview

1st Challenge and Workshop for 4D Micro-Expression Recognition for Mind Reading (4DMR).
In this competition participants will be tasked with micro expression recognition from sequences of 3D meshes of the [4DME dataset](https://doi.org/10.1109/TAFFC.2022.3182342)

# NEWS 
**We ask all participants to please submit their team member names and affiliations by May 19th to the competition hosts. The submission should include the signature of the responsible person. Only verified teams will be counted in the final ranking and be eligible for the Top-3 certificate, for fairness of the competition.**
The final leaderboard is now released, submissions are being rescored.
----------------------
# Description

#4D micro-expression recognition challenge
## Task

Micro-expressions (MEs) are subtle, rapid, and involuntary facial movements that often occur in high-stakes scenarios or when individuals attempt to gain advantages or conceal their true emotions. Due to their extremely short duration and low intensity, MEs are difficult to detect and demand high-precision facial data.

This challenge leverages the power of 4D facial analysis¡ªcapturing the temporal evolution of facial expressions in 3D space¡ªto uncover the complex dynamics of facial muscle movements over time. Unlike traditional 2D or static 3D approaches, 4D analysis (3D mesh + temporal sequence) excels at identifying fleeting, involuntary micro-expressions by incorporating both spatial depth and motion cues. This multi-view, temporal information enriches the data and significantly improves recognition accuracy and robustness.

In this challenge, participants are tasked with recognizing five categories of micro-expressions: Negative£¬Positive£¬Repression£¬ Surprise£¬Others.  
Note: The task is framed as a multi-label classification problem, where a single expression instance may exhibit features from multiple emotion categories. This better reflects the complexity of real human emotions and provides a more nuanced benchmark for recognition systems.

## Dataset

The challenge is based on the 4DME dataset, introduced in:
Li, Xiaobai, et al.""4DME: A spontaneous 4D micro-expression dataset with multimodalities."" IEEE Transactions on Affective Computing (2022).
We will release a registered version of the 4D micro-expression dataset containing spontaneous expressions labeled with the five target categories listed above. The dataset includes high-resolution 3D facial meshes over time, enabling precise analysis of facial dynamics.

## Evaluation
The chosen evalutation metric is the F1-score. The F1-score is defined as 
$$
F1 = 2 \cdot \frac{{\text{{precision}} \cdot \text{{recall}}}}{{\text{{precision}} + \text{{recall}}}}
$$

Specifically, the macro F1-score will be computed :

$$
F1_{macro} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} F1_i
$$

where \\(N\\) is the number of classes and \\(F1_i\\) is the F1-score for class \\( i \\).


Participants' models will be evaluated on the public set, which is a subset of the testing set containing only half of the subjects, for the public leaderboard. The models will then be evaluated on the full testing set to compute the final scores and rankings.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a multiclass label of the ME categories
There are 5 categories in the label :  Negative, Positive, Repression, Surprise, Others

    ID,Negative, Positive, Repression, Surprise ,Others
    1,0,1,0,1,1
    2,1,0,0,1,0
    .
    .
    .
    99,0,1,1,0,0


----------------------
# Bibliography

X. Li et al., ""4DME: A Spontaneous 4D Micro-Expression Dataset With Multimodalities,"" in IEEE Transactions on Affective Computing, vol. 14, no. 4, pp. 3031-3047, 1 Oct.-Dec. 2023, doi: 10.1109/TAFFC.2022.3182342.","# Dataset Description

The data is stored as a private kaggle dataset, please sign the licence agreement on the [IJCAI challenge page](https://jingjingchen-pro.github.io/4DMR2025/) to gain access. 
Return the signed licence agreement to our data chair : Mengting Wei Mengting.Wei{at}oulu.fi

The dataset is composed of 4D sequences taken fom high-resolutions scans.
Each sequence is composed of multiple 3D meshes, material file and material source ; in .obj, .mtl and .jpg format respectively.
The dataset contains 24 subject from different cultures, each of them containing a different number of sequences.
Sequences are cut to contain only the micro-expression(s).

The train.csv file will contain the paths of the sequences and their label in the following format :

    path_new, Negative, Positive, Repression, Surprise ,Others, Id
    train\seq0,0,1,0,1,1,1
    train\seq1,1,0,0,1,0,2
    .
    .
    .
    train\seq99,0,1,1,0,0,99


Each participant's submission will be tested on a subset of the test data, the final results will be tested on the full test set.

You can find a sample submission in the sample_submission.csv file.",False,True
9544,itmo-acoustic-event-detectin-2025,"# Description

### Event classification competition of the course ""Acoustic Events Detection"".

### Baseline: https://github.com/Tvicker/itmo-kaggle-inclass

You will achive scores: 
 5 : beat random baseline 

get 'passed' grade when you beat the :
5: when you beat 0.1 F1-score          //It is strongly better then random (1/42) - you on the right way!
10: when you beat 0.4 F1-score       //It is usualy achivable if you do everything correct
15: when you beat 0.5 F1-score       // It is achvable if you pay attention to both: preprocessing and good training process  
20: when you beat the F1-baselines  //Excellent! Now try to win others ;)

Your solutions will be scored with average class-based [ F1-score ](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_score).

Please, ensure to set the seed when you do any randomness in your notebook. These lines of code must help:

```
import random
import numpy as np

random.seed(42)
np.random.seed(42)
torch.manual_seed(42)
torch.cuda.manual_seed(42)
torch.backends.cudnn.deterministic = True
```
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have included below:

 ---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `AuthorId` and `DuplicateAuthorIds`. `DuplicateAuthorIds` should be a space-delimited list. Every `AuthorId` counts as its own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids. For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list `A,A B C`, `B,B A C`, `C,C A B`.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

Files `train.csv` and `sample_submission.csv` contain `label` and `fname`  as columns.

`fname` is filename, and `train.csv` describes the files in `audio_train` folder, while `sample_submission.csv`contains all the names from the test set. 

To make a submission you should change zeroes in `sample_submission.csv` to meaningful class names as in `train.csv`.",False,True
