9561,icip-act-2025-spring-classification,"# Overview

**Text Level Reading** is a key application of natural language processing in education. Against this backdrop, we focuses on the classification of Classical Chinese texts.  We provide linguistic features and a small corpus of Ancient Chinese Text from textbooks and exam papers to build our training and test set.

Contestants can use these features, combined with feature engineering and machine learning algorithms, to predict the actual level of the text.

Finally, we use **Macro F1** to evaluate your model performances.

***We do not provide any specific text content in our datasets. Only the values of the linguistic features given by our self-designed algorithm are provided.***
----------------------
# Description

# About the label of the text:
- **Level 0**: **Beginner**. Short, concise texts using simple vocabulary and common sentence structures. Content is life-oriented and easily comprehensible, matching the difficulty of middle school textbooks or standardized exams.
- **Level 1**: **Intermediate**. Longer excerpts from classical cultural works, featuring archaic expressions, phonetic loan characters, and special grammatical constructions. Requires foundational cultural knowledge, comparable to high school curricula or national college entrance exams.
- **Level 2**: **Advanced**. Sophisticated classical texts with specialized vocabulary, complex rhetorical structures, and profound conceptual depth, requiring scholarly expertise for comprehension.

# Features
In this competition, we provide four categories of features:

1.**Dataset Characteristics**
- **ID** :  Randomly generated strings composed of characters and digits to distinguish different texts.

2.**Count information**
- **Word Level**
   - **Number of words in the text**: The total word count of the text, irrespective of word type.
   - **Number of Words of Different Parts of Speech**: The count of words categorized by parts of speech, such as content words (including nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, and pronouns) and functional words (including prepositions, determiners, and conjunctions).

- **Sentence Level**
    - **Average Sentence Length** : The average of the sentence length in the text. 
    - **Variability in Sentence Length** : The Variance of the sentence length. We compute it in both full sentences and clauses.

3.**Lexical Features**
- **Single word ratio** : The ratio of words that only exist once in the text.
- **Moving Average Type-Token Ratio**: Number of unique words divided by the total number of words. We compute this on character level and bigram level, and compute TTR in specific window to mitigate the influence of context length.
- **Average Sense Count**: The average sense numbers of the character in the context.

4.**Content Features**
- **Content Perplexity**:  The perplexity of the content evaluated by GPT2-Modern and GPT2-Ancient.

--- 


**TTR** stands for **Type-Token Ratio**. This is a measure used in linguistics to assess the lexical diversity of a text. The TTR is calculated by dividing the number of unique word types (distinct words) by the total number of words (tokens) in the text.

**Formula**:

$$TTR=\frac{Number\ of\ Unique\ Word\ Types}{Total\ Number\ of\ Words}$$

**The Importance of TTR in text classification**
- **Lexical Variety** : A higher TTR indicates a greater variety of vocabulary used in the text, which is often associated with a higher level of language proficiency.
- **Text Complexity**:  Text with a higher lexical diversity might also indicate more complex thoughts.

**Limitation**
- **Text Length Sensitivity** : TTR tends to be higher in shorter texts and decreases as text length increases. This is because, in longer texts, the likelihood of using the same words multiple times increases. 
    - To mitigate this side effect, we compute the TTR in a moving window, and calculate the mean of the window TTR. 

--- 

**Perplexity** (PP) is a fundamental metric for evaluating language models. It quantifies how well a probability model predicts a given sample, with lower values indicating better predictive performance.

**Formula**:

$$P P(W)=\exp \left(-\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \log P\left(w_{i} \mid w_{1}, \ldots, w_{i-1}\right)\right)$$



----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation Metrics For Classification

In the task of classification, **Accuracy**, **Precision**, **Recall** and **F1** are four common evaluation metrics.

**Accuracy**: The ratio of true prediction in all predictions.
**Precision**: Identifying true positives within the model's correct predictions.
**Recall**: Identifying true positives within the true labels.
**F1**: Harmonic Mean of Precision and Recall.

**Macro F1** computes the unweighted mean of F1 scores across all labels, treating each label equally regardless of class distribution.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **actc-train-2025spring.csv** - the training set
*   **actc-test-2025spring.csv** - the test set without `label`
*   **actc-test-submission-sample-2025Spring.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

- **word_token**: Count of characters in the text.
- **word_type**: Unique num of characters in the text.
- **single_word_ratio**: The ratio of characters exists once in the text among all words.
- **word_MATTR**: Moving average TTR in characters level.
- **word_log_meanFre**: The mean frequency of word in log form.
- **word_avg_meaning**: The mean count of sense of characters in the text.
- **common_word_ratio**: The ratio of common word in the text. This is extract by a specific common word table in middle and high school.
- **common_particles_ratio**: The ratio of particles in the text. This is extract by a specific particles table in middle and high school.
- **highfre_word_ratio_500**: The ratio of high frequency characters(Rank 500) in the text. The frequency table is based on a huge Ancient Chinese Corpus.
- **highfre_word_ratio_1000**: The ratio of high frequency characters(Rank 1000) in the text.
- **highfre_word_ratio_1500**: The ratio of high frequency characters(Rank 1500) in the text.
- **highfre_word_ratio_2000**: The ratio of high frequency characters(Rank 2000) in the text.
- **bigram_mattr**: MATTR in bigram level.
- **bigram_type**: Unique num of bigrams in the text.
- **bigram_log_meanFre**: The mean frequency of bigram in log form.
- **highfre_bigram_ratio_500**: The ratio of high frequency bigram(Rank 500) in the text.
- **highfre_bigram_ratio_1000**: The ratio of high frequency bigram(Rank 1000) in the text.
- **highfre_bigram_ratio_1500**: The ratio of high frequency bigram(Rank 1500) in the text.
- **highfre_bigram_ratio_2000**: The ratio of high frequency bigram(Rank 2000) in the text.
- **sen_avg_len**: The mean of sentence length in the text.
- **sen_var**: The variance of sentence length in the text.
- **sen_svar**: The variance of clause length in the text.
- **common_am_diff**: The ratio of words that have different sense in Modern Chinese and Ancient Chinese. 
- **modern_perplexity**: Content perplexity computed by GPT2-Modern.
- **tradition_perplexity**: Content perplexity computed by GPT2-Ancient.",False,True
9562,gene-expression-and-biological-age-re-launch,"# Overview

One widely studied **continuous health indicator** that can be predicted from gene expression is **Biological Age** (also called **Epigenetic Age** or **Transcriptomic Age**).  

### **Why Biological Age?**
- Unlike chronological age, **biological age** reflects the physiological state of an individual, capturing aging-related changes at the molecular level.
- It can be estimated using **gene expression data**, particularly from transcriptomic or epigenetic markers.
- Studies have developed **transcriptomic age predictors** using machine learning on large-scale gene expression datasets.
----------------------
# Description

### **Why Biological Age Varies Across Populations & Why Prediction Models Differ**  

#### **1. Biological Age vs. Chronological Age**  
Chronological age is simply the number of years since birth, but **biological age** reflects the physiological and molecular state of an individual. It is influenced by:  
- **Genetics**  
- **Epigenetics**  
- **Environmental factors** (diet, pollution, stress)  
- **Lifestyle choices** (exercise, smoking, sleep)  

Biological aging can progress at different rates depending on these factors, making it **population-dependent**.

---

#### **2. Population-Specific Differences in Biological Aging**  

1. **Genetic Variability**  
   - Different populations have unique genetic backgrounds, which can influence aging-related pathways.  
   - For example, **genes associated with inflammation and oxidative stress** may vary in significance across ethnic groups.  

2. **Epigenetic Differences**  
   - DNA methylation-based biological clocks (e.g., Horvath¡¯s clock) show differences in accuracy across populations.  
   - Some methylation sites may be **more predictive** in certain populations than others.  

3. **Environmental & Lifestyle Factors**  
   - Access to healthcare, nutrition, exposure to pollution, and socioeconomic factors all contribute to **differences in biological aging** across populations.  
   - For instance, **urban vs. rural populations** may have different stress-related aging patterns.  

---

#### **3. Why Biological Age Prediction Models Differ Across Populations**  

1. **Different Genes Matter in Different Groups**  
   - A gene predictive of aging in **one population** may not be as relevant in another due to genetic drift or selection pressures.  
   - Example: Genes linked to **oxidative stress** may be more critical in populations exposed to high pollution levels.  

2. **Differential Expression of Aging-Related Genes**  
   - Gene expression signatures of aging may **differ between populations** due to genetic and environmental interactions.  
   - A universal model trained on one population might fail when applied to another.  

3. **Epigenetic Clocks May Need Recalibration**  
   - Epigenetic aging models trained on **European ancestry populations** may not work as well for **African, Asian, or Indigenous populations** due to different baseline methylation patterns.  
   - This is why **population-specific clocks** (e.g., GrimAge, PhenoAge) are sometimes developed.  

---

### **4. Modeling Restriction**

For this project, we have simulated data from linear models for each population. But as always, you are welcome to try anything.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The objective of this project is to identify the populations from using simulated Gene Expression Data and Biological age. You will be evaluated using the Adjusted Rand Index of **ARI**. This index goes from up to 1, with 1 being a perfect match.

Competitors will be graded based on their performance on the leaderboard. Specifically, a participant's grade will be calculated as follows:

\\[
\text{Grade} = \frac{\text{Your Score}}{\text{Best Score}}
\\]

Where:  
- **Best Score** refers to the highest ARI achieved by any participant.  
- **Your Score** refers to the participant's own highest ARI.  

### Submission File Format
Your submission file must follow this structure:
- First Column: colID
- Second Column: Cluster Membership a number for 1 to K, where K your number of clusters.

Submission Example:

    colID, cluster
    1, 2
    2, 4
    3, 5
    4, 1
    5, 3
    6, 5
    etc.",,False,True
9563,2025bmi707-assignment-2-q5,"# Overview

<h2>Question 5: Multiclass classification</h2>
<p>In this question, we will build multiclass classifiers to distinguish different types of lung diseases using the NIHCXR8 data.</p>
<p>Please download the training set and the validation set from the Data tab (https://www.kaggle.com/c/2025bmi707-assignment-2-q5/data). Please note that this dataset is different from the one we used in Questions 3 and 4. Please <b>DO NOT</b> use any additional dataset to train or augment your models.</p> 
<p>Feel free to use any (ImageNet or any custom) architecture to classify all available classes.</p>
<p>In addition to participating in this BMI707 internal Kaggle contest, you would need to provide your answers to the questions and codes in the assignment write-up.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.tar</strong> - the training set (Dropbox link: <a href=""https://www.dropbox.com/s/feaakvusdvkep3n/train.tar"">https://www.dropbox.com/s/feaakvusdvkep3n/train.tar)</a></li>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the labels of the training set (Dropbox link: <a href=""https://www.dropbox.com/s/9kebfecemhfkj7k/train.csv"">https://www.dropbox.com/s/9kebfecemhfkj7k/train.csv)</a></li>
<li><strong>testPublic.tar</strong> - the public test set (the labels are not given). This dataset is used to generate the evaluation results for the leaderboard. (Dropbox link: <a href=""https://www.dropbox.com/s/20d8b9z8znc2lmp/testPublic.tar"">https://www.dropbox.com/s/20d8b9z8znc2lmp/testPublic.tar</a>) </li>
<li><strong>testPrivate.tar</strong> - the private test set (the labels are not given). This dataset is used to generate the final evaluation. (Dropbox link: <a href=""https://www.dropbox.com/s/lun96wxiq84z7eg/testPrivate.tar"">https://www.dropbox.com/s/lun96wxiq84z7eg/testPrivate.tar</a>) </li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format. (Dropbox link: <a href=""https://www.dropbox.com/s/a9o0lkszeusdmr9/sampleSubmission.csv"">https://www.dropbox.com/s/a9o0lkszeusdmr9/sampleSubmission.csv</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields in the submission</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Id</strong> - the file name of the image</li>
<li><strong>Category</strong> - the diagnostic category of the image</li>
</ul>",False,True
