9577,hill-of-towie-wind-turbine-power-prediction,"# Overview

## Background and motivation
Predicting power at a turbine in the absence of reliable wind measurements (e.g. power performance mast or LiDAR) is very challenging but increasingly necessary. One application of power prediction is in measurement of the effectiveness of a turbine upgrade. Examples of such analysis are shown in [this repository](https://github.com/resgroup/hill-of-towie-open-source-analysis).

Upgrading existing wind farms is a highly effective way to unlock additional clean energy without requiring large-scale infrastructure investments. Transparent and reliable measurement of upgrade value is key to scaling these activities and supporting the rapid expansion of wind energy. Increasing the accuracy of wind turbine power modelling is fundamental to this goal.

## Challenge goal
The goal of this challenge is to predict power as accurately as possible for one turbine at Hill of Towie given the data from the nearby turbines. The goal is to minimize Mean Absolute Error of the 10-minute predicted active power timeseries.

This challenge is similar to [this competition](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/predict-the-wind-speed-at-a-wind-turbine) except the target feature is power instead of wind speed and the setup of the challenge is structured to be representative of a commercial turbine upgrade measurement.
----------------------
# Description

To simplify the process of getting started, we've created a Kaggle Notebook showing how to load the data using python and create a very simplistic model and use it to prepare a submission file.

The notebook can be found here: https://www.kaggle.com/code/gabecalvo/hill-of-towie-power-prediction-getting-started
----------------------
# Evaluation

The submissions will be evaluated using the **Mean Absolute Error** metric.

The predicted Active Power of Turbine 1 (target turbine) will be compared to the actual recorded power for the period in the submission dataset (year 2020).

The periods in the submission dataset where the target turbine is not operating normally (e.g. downtimes) will not be considered when calculating the metric; these rows are indicated by the `is_valid` column (True means used for scoring, False means ignored for scoring).

## Submission File
For each timestamp in the submission dataset, you must predict the value of Active Power of Turbine 1. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```csv
id,prediciton
0,1790.092578125
1,1883.399609375
...
```","# Dataset Description

This dataset was generated from the [Hill of Towie wind farm open dataset](https://zenodo.org/records/14870023).

The data has been collated, pre-processed and formatted to make it easier to work with within the context of this competition:
- filtered: only data from Turbines 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 7; only relevant data fields.
- standardized: OEM specific StationId to simpler turbine_id (1-7); Timestamp standardized as UTC start-format (the timestamp indicates the start of the 10min period).
- duplicates removed.
- joined with ShutdownDuration and reference data from the closest ERA5 reanalysis node.
- reshaped to wide format (unique timestamp column; turbine id included in field column).
- added `id`, `is_valid` and `target` columns (see ""Fields"" section below)

The code used to collate the dataset for this competition can be inspected [here](https://github.com/resgroup/hill-of-towie-open-source-analysis/blob/main/scripts/wedowind_challenge/s02_generate_datapack.py). Participants can use more data if they would like (eg more fields and more distant turbines) but please do not peak at the answers, ie do not look at T1 active power in 2020.

## Files
- **training_dataset.parquet** - the training dataset (contains data for 2016, 2017, 2018 and 2019 for all turbines)
- **submission_dataset.parquet** - the submission dataset (contains data for 2020 for all turbines apart from the target one: turbine 1)
- **sample_model_submission.csv** - example of file formatted for submission.

## Fields
All information about the columns and their meaning can be found at the original zenodo dataset (see ""Hill_of_Towie_turbine_fields_description.csv""). The data has been reshaped so the columns are in the format `{field_name};{turbine_id}`.

On top of those, a few columns were added:
- `TimeStamp_StartFormat` is the UTC timestamp referring to the start of the 10 minute period.
- `id`: an integer column used as unique identifier for the sole purpose of allowing the Kaggle to correctly score the submission. The field counts the 10min timestamp since the start of the submission dataset start (2020-01-01T00:00+00:00), so the number will be negative for the training dataset.
- `is_valid`: a boolean column to mark when the target turbine is in normal operation (ShutdownDuration=0, wtc_ScReToOp_timeon=600) and the active power is not null.
- `target`: which is active power column of turbine 1, with the lower bound clipped to 0.
- `ERA5_...`: ERA5 reanalysis data from Open Meteo. The data has been downloaded under their non-commercial licence, which still covers it. Individual field description is available [here](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-weather-api#hourly_parameter_definition)",False,True
9578,blueberry-production-classification-competition,"# Overview

Blueberry is a profitable fruit exported from South American countries like Peru, Argentina, and Chile to different continents worldwide. Understanding the crop to have the capacity to estimate or predict how many tons or kilograms of blueberry the farm will produce is critical for the business to attract customers and meet the deadlines. Wrong estimations could lead to important economic losses and impact the prestige of the farm to close future contracts.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4420288%2Fd0c103228269358b8a9b440b6a549737%2Fproduction-over-time.png?generation=1743670364258287&alt=media)
Figure 1. Weekly yield per plant for two blueberry cultivars. Figure taken from M. E. Cortes, P. A. Mesa, C. M. Grijalba, and M. M. Perez. (2016). Yield and Fruit Quality of the Blueberry Cultivars Biloxi and Sharpblue in Guasca, Colombia.

Agronomists usually take into consideration many sources of variables to determine the yield per week (Fig. 1). Sources such as irrigation, plant nutrition, soil quality, NDVI, and weather, among others, are some of the most common. However, as the crop fields increase in size, the burden of calculating manually the yield per sector becomes harder, so calculations tend to be biased or mistaken. 

In order to make this process easier, we require a tool to assist the agronomist in making better decisions and have a whole picture of the production of his fields. With Artificial Intelligence we can predict the class of the productions as **low, medium and high**.
----------------------
# Description

In this challenge, you will be tasked with the classification of blueberry production using data from different sources. The dataset provides information about blueberry growth characterization, fertilizers, irrigation, and weather stations from 2021 to 2022. Three classes are defined: **low, medium, and high production**.

- WEEK: Week of the current year
- H20: Amount of water in M3 provided to crop
- NDVI: How green is the crop index
- EVO: Evaporation of water index
- LR: Irrigation sheet
- N: Nitrogen
- P: Phosphorous
- K: Potassium
- TEMPERATURE: Temperature of the crop field (C)
- HUMIDITY: Relative humidity of the crop field (%)
- WIND_VELOCITY: Velocity of wind in the crop field (km/s) 
- RAIN: Rain level in mm
- SOLAR_RADIATION: Measurement in W/m2
- STEMS_PER_M: Number of stems per meter
- TOTAL_BUDS: Number of buds in every blueberry plant
- BUDS_PER_BUNCHES: Number of buds per bunch
- MATURATION_LEVEL: Level of blueberry maturation (%)
- BRIX_DEGRESS: % average BRIX level in blueberries
- PERCENTAGE_OF_BIG_BUDS: % of buds relatively large
- PERCENTAGE_OF_MEDIUM_BUDS: % of buds relatively medium
- PERCENTAGE_OF_SMALL_BUDS: % of buds relatively small
- ABORTED_PERCENTAGE: % of aborted blueberries in a harvest

The primary goal is to implement a robust machine learning model to accurately predict the class of production (low, medium, high) out of a diverse dataset of selected variables listed above.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Accuracy
Accuracy measures how close a model's predictions are to the actual values. It is commonly used in classification tasks and is defined as the proportion of correctly predicted labels to the total number of instances.

Formally, accuracy is expressed as:
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4420288%2F23a1ac9193ec21d3f9805c9209e0a80e%2Feq.png?generation=1743675585511609&alt=media)

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.",,False,True
9579,fiber-content-in-wheat-and-barley-grains-cropss,"# Overview

Fibers are essential components for humans, as part of a healthy diet, and one of the most critical fibers is Arabinoxylan, which can be found in wheat and barley grains, two massive cereal crops for human consumption. According to Korge et. al. (2023), the levels of arabinoxylan are seasonal; winter and spring might influence the final quality of the fiber in both crops (Figure 1).

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4420288%2F1b4944f90ab75eb02abf423d99f5ff7b%2Ffiber%20content%20over%20time.png?generation=1745852579242489&alt=media)
Figure 1. The AX content as an average of N treatments in winter wheat and spring barley flours in different years. 

However, Korge et. al. (2023) found that not only does temperature correlate with higher levels of arabinoxylan, but also the kind of fertilizer treatment and crop system influence it. This opens up an opportunity to improve the crop quality and benefit consumers' health. 

**Goal:** To help farmers produce high-quality crops, we require an AI model to predict the level of arabinoxylan using a 4-year dataset related to fertilizer treatment, crop system, and temperature.
----------------------
# Description

In this challenge, you will be tasked with predicting the arabinoxylan level using data from different sources. The dataset provides information about fertilizer treatment, crop system, and seasonal temperature from 2014 to 2021.

The dataset is based on the following columns:
YEAR: Year of the crop.
CROPPING_SYSTEM: farming system used for the crop.
TREATMENT: fertilizer treatment for the crop.
AVR_APRIL_TEMP: weather temperature in April in C degrees.
AVR_MAY_TEMP: weather temperature in May in C degrees.
AVR_JUNE_TEMP: weather temperature in June in C degrees.
AVR_JULY_TEMP: weather temperature in July in C degrees.
AVR_AUGUST_TEMP: weather temperature in August in C degrees.
TOTAL_PRECIPITATION:  total rainfall between April and August in mm.
PRECIPITATION_APRIL: rainfall in April in mm.
PRECIPITATION_MAY: rainfall in May in mm.
PRECIPITATION_JUNE: rainfall in June in mm.
PRECIPITATION_JULY: rainfall in July in mm.
PRECIPITATION_AUGUST: rainfall in August in mm.
BARLEY_YIELD: total amount of barley produced in tons/ha.
AX_CONTENT: Arabinoxylan content in g/100g.

The primary goal is to implement a robust machine learning regressor model to accurately predict the quantity of arabinoxylan (AX_CONTENT) out of a diverse dataset of selected variables listed above.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.",,False,True
