9581,dont-flatten-shrink-image-classification,"# Overview

You are challenged to tackle the image classification on the CIFAR-10 dataset with the smallest possible model using NdLinear, link: https://github.com/ensemble-core/NdLinear 

A PyTorch module that replaces the standard linear layer in neural networks.

- Plug-and-play replacement for nn.Linear
- Lightweight, parameter-efficient
- Preserves multivariate structure natively
----------------------
# Goals

- Build an image classifier using CIFAR-10 with NdLinear.
- Track performance, speed, and model efficiency in a leaderboard format.

----------------------
# Evaluation

All submissions will undergo an initial screening to verify the correct and consistent usage of the NdLinear module within the model architecture. Only submissions that pass this correctness check will proceed to the next evaluation stage, where they will be assessed using a comprehensive set of metrics designed to capture both model effectiveness and efficiency.

- **The key evaluation metric is the Top-1 accuracy your model achieves.**

- **Model Shrinkage (%):**
 Model Shrink (%) = ((Your Model Parameter Count - Baseline Model Parameter Count) / Baseline Model Parameter Count) * 100

  This measures the reduction in model size, as indicated by the decrease in the number of parameters, compared to the baseline reference model.

- **Model Complexity:**  
  In addition to the parameter count, we will also consider the computational complexity of the model, typically measured in FLOPs (floating-point operations). Lower complexity with high performance is strongly encouraged.

- **Creativity and Novelty:**  
  The extent to which your approach introduces original ideas, novel adaptations, or creative solutions in leveraging NdLinear will be qualitatively assessed.

**Submission Requirements:**

- Each submission must include detailed instructions along with fully functional scripts for model training, testing, and evaluation.  
- Ensure that your documentation is clear enough for others to run your code and replicate your results without ambiguity.

Submissions that fail to meet the correctness criteria in using the NdLinear module will not advance for further evaluation. We encourage thorough documentation and well-commented code to facilitate the review process and maximize reproducibility.
----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: 100","# Dataset Description

### Dataset: CIFAR-10

Section 3 in this technical report describes the CIFAR10 dataset and the methodology followed when collecting it in much greater detail. Please cite the following work in your report. 
*Learning Multiple Layers of Features from Tiny Images, Alex Krizhevsky, 2009.*

- 60,000 32x32 color images in 10 classes, with 6,000 images per class.
- Official split: 50,000 training + 10,000 testing.
- Classes: airplane, automobile, bird, cat, deer, dog, frog, horse, ship, truck.

```python
from torchvision.datasets import CIFAR10
train_set = CIFAR10(root='./data', train=True, download=True)
test_set = CIFAR10(root='./data', train=False, download=True)
```",False,True
9582,faim-sgp-25-d-sarah-gets-a-diamond,"# Description

Case: ¡°Sarah Gets a Diamond¡±, UVA-QA-0702 + data

The file ¡°Sarah Gets a Diamond data.xls¡± contains data about various attributes for over 9000 diamonds. For the first 6000 you also have the price. 

The goal of this competition is to use the concepts and tools you've learned in Sessions 2-4 to predict the prices of the remaining diamonds (i.e., those with IDs 6001 and above). 

When done, create a spreadsheet containing two columns: ID and Price. For IDs, put 6001, 6002, etc., and for the prices, put the numerical values of your predictions (without dollar signs, or any other strings), e.g., 12345.6, 23456.7, etc.

Save the resultant file in the CSV format and submit it. Make sure the column names follow the uppercase and lowercase format and are exactly ID and Price:
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1489777%2F9cc19c90fa3db1214edc371261ef142c%2FSarah%20Gets%20a%20Diamond%20solution%20-%20sample.JPG?generation=1734625749156314&alt=media)

Your submission will be immediately scored on a fraction of the diamonds according to the Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) metric that we will discuss in class. The lower, the better. 

## Acknowledgements

http://store.darden.virginia.edu/sarah-gets-a-diamond 
