9659,datascience-4-competition,"# Overview

Predict the synthetic category for each data record from 64 generated data points.

Training rows: 564

Test rows: 143

Features: 64 (feature0 ¡­ feature63) ¨C binary (0?/?1) flags for a yes/ no synthesized data

Target column: label ¨C integer target code 0-10 (11 classes)

ID column: ID

Use your favourite classification techniques¡ªtree ensembles, neural nets, calibrated linear models, or clever feature engineering¡ªto climb the leaderboard. The winning solution will showcase skill at handling sparse, high-dimensional, multi-class medical data. **Note: only models with result higher than 40 percent will be scored as bouns**
Your bonus score will be computed by the following formula and added directly to your practical grade!
$$
\text{Bonus} \;=\; \frac{1}{1 + 0.5 * (\text{rank} - 1)}
$$
----------------------
# Description

This competition provides an **anonymised, synthetic clinical dataset**. Each row represents a unique patient encounter; each of the 64 features is a binary flag indicating the presence (`1`) or absence (`0`) of a specific sign, symptom, diagnostic code, or laboratory threshold breach.
Your goal is to map these indicators to the correct disease category in `label`.

#### Files

| File                       | Shape    | Contents                                |
| -------------------------- | -------- | --------------------------------------- |
| **train.csv**              | 564 ¡Á 66 | 64 features + `label` + ID              |
| **test.csv**               | 143 ¡Á 65 | 64 features + ID (no `label`)           |
| **sample\_submission.csv** | 143 ¡Á 2  | `ID` and dummy `label`; use as template |

The public leaderboard score is based on **50%** of the hidden labels; the final private score (revealed at competition end) uses the remaining 50% so your rank in the leaderboard might be a little different after competition ends.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored on **overall classification accuracy**

$$
\text{Accuracy} \;=\; \frac{\text{Number of correct predictions}}{\text{Total predictions}}
$$

The maximum possible score is **1.000** (100 % correct).

#### Submission format

Create a CSV with exactly these two columns:

```
ID,label
```

and one line per row in **test.csv**, for example:

```
ID,label
17,3
18,0
19,7
...
```

* **ID** ¡ú value from id column  in *test.csv*
* **label** ¡ú your predicted disease code (integer 0 ¨C 10)

Upload the file via **¡°Submit Predictions¡±** to receive an instant accuracy score.","# Dataset Description

## ?? Files

| File                       | Rows ¡Á Cols | What it contains                                                        |
| -------------------------- | ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **train.csv**              | 564?¡Á?66    | 64 binary clinical features, an **ID** column, and the target **label** |
| **test.csv**               | 143?¡Á?65    | Same 64 features?+?ID (no label)                                        |
| **sample\_submission.csv** | 143¡Á?2     | Template you will upload to Kaggle (`ID`, `label`)                      |

---

## ?? Column Definitions

| Column                   | In files         | Type       | Description                                                                                                                         |
| ------------------------ | ---------------- | ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **ID** (`ID`)    | all              | int        | Unique patient identifier                                                                                                           |
| **feature0 ¡­ feature63** | all              | int (0/1)  | Binary flags for anonymised clinical indicators (symptoms, lab thresholds, codes). A value of **1** means the indicator is present. |
| **label**                | *train.csv* only | int (0¨C10) | Disease category to predict                                                                                                         |

No missing values are present; every feature is either **0** or **1**.

---

## ?? Prediction Target

Your model must predict **`label`** for every row in **test.csv**.

---

## ?? Submission Format

Upload a CSV named however you like but with **exactly two columns**:

```
ID,label
```

* `ID` ¨C copy of the **ID** from *test.csv*
* `label` ¨C integer prediction 0?¡­?10

A valid first few lines look like:

```
ID,label
1,7
3,0
4,6
...
```

Good luck, and happy modeling!",False,True
