9677,bakery-sales-prediction-summer-2025,"# Overview

This project is part of the [introductory course](https://edu.opencampus.sh/course/411) to data science and machine learning at [opencampus.sh](https://opencampus.sh).  

The dataset was provided by meteolytix. For more then 10 years meteolytix was specializing on machine learning based sales forecasting, with models using more than 400 input variables and doing over one million predictions each day.
----------------------
# Description

## Goal of the Competition

In this practical competition, you are tasked with forecasting bakery product sales using a dataset from a specific bakery branch. Your challenge is to develop a model that accurately predicts sales for six different bakery product categories: Bread, Rolls, Croissants, Confectionery, Cakes, and Seasonal Bread.

You'll delve into a dataset containing sales records from July 1, 2013, to July 30, 2018, encompassing various factors influencing bakery sales. The aim is to harness your machine learning skills to create a model that identifies and leverages patterns within this dataset to forecast future sales.

## Get Started

To kick off, consider beginning with a baseline linear regression model to understand fundamental sales trends and then evolve your approach to incorporate more complex machine learning or neural network methods for refined predictions. Participants are encouraged to engage in comprehensive data preparation and visualization, using tools and techniques best suited for time-series forecasting.

## Context

Sales forecasting is a critical component in the bakery business, influencing inventory management, staffing decisions, and customer satisfaction. The ability to predict sales accurately has far-reaching impacts, from reducing waste to ensuring customer demands are met effectively. The challenge becomes more intriguing as you consider factors like seasonal variations, changing consumer preferences, and the introduction of new products.

## Potential Impact

If successful, you'll have flexed some new skills in a real world example. For bakeries, it could lead to more efficient inventory control, reduced food waste, and improved customer satisfaction by ensuring the availability of products. Participants will have the opportunity to apply and showcase their skills in a real-world scenario, contributing to advancements in data-driven decision-making in retail.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The performance of the models will be evaluated using the Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) metric, which provides a clear and understandable measure of forecast accuracy.

The Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) is a measure of prediction accuracy in a forecasting model. It calculates the average of the absolute percentage errors of the forecasts. The formula for MAPE is as follows:

\[ \text{MAPE} = \frac{100\%}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left| \frac{y_i - \hat{y}_i}{y_i} \right| \]

where:
- \( n \) is the total number of observations,
- \( y_i \) is the actual value of the observation,
- \( \hat{y}_i \) is the predicted value.

The MAPE gives a simple percentage which makes it easy to interpret and understand. A lower MAPE value indicates better predictive accuracy of the model.

## Submission File

For each `id` in the test set, you must predict a value for the `umsatz` variable. The file should contain a header and be formatted as follows:
```
id,umsatz
201808011,0.0
201808012,0.0
201808013,0.0
```","# Dataset Description

In this competition, you will predict sales for six product categories of a bakery branch located somewhere in Kiel. The training data includes dates, Product categories, and sales numbers. Additional files include supplementary information that may be useful in building your models.

## File Descriptions and Data Field Information

### train.csv
The training data, comprising daily time series data with the sales (`Umsatz`) for each product category (`Warengruppe`).
- `id` is a unique identifier for the recorded sales.
- `Datum` gives the date of the recorded sales.
- `Warengruppe` identifies the product categories according to following scheme:
    - `1`: Bread
    - `2`: Rolls
    - `3`: Croissant
    - `4`: Confectionery
    - `5`: Cake
    - `6`: Seasonal Bread
- `Umsatz` gives the total sales for a product category at a given date.

### test.csv
The test data, having the same feature variables as the training data. You will predict the target sales for the dates in this file.
The dates in the test data are for the 12 months after the last date in the training data.

### sample_submission.csv
A sample submission file in the correct format.

### wetter.csv
Weather data for Kiel from January 1, 2012 to August 1, 2019.
- `Datum` gives the date of the observed weather.
- `Bewoelkung` describes the cloud coverage. Check [this site](https://www.wetter.net/wetterlexikon/eintrag/bewoelkungsgrad) for more info on the values.
- `Temperature` gives the temperature in Celsius.
- `Windgeschwindigkeit` gives the wind speed in meters per second.
- `Wettercode` describes the type of weather. Check [this site](http://www.seewetter-kiel.de/seewetter/daten_symbole.htm) for more info on the values.

### kiwo.csv
Dates of the ""Kieler Woche"" for the years 2013 to 2019.
These 10 days each year have a significant effect on the bakery sales in Kiel.",False,True
9678,aikc-course-final-competition,"# Overview

In this competition, you are challenged to predict the genres of video games based on their metadata and visual content. Each game is represented by its title, description, and one  in-game screenshot. Since a single game can belong to multiple genres (e.g., ¡°Action¡±, ¡°RPG¡±, ¡°Multiplayer¡±), this is a multi-label classification problem.

Participants are expected to develop multimodal models that combine both textual and visual features to accurately assign all relevant genres to each game.

**Goal:** Build a model that predicts all applicable genres for each game using its title, description, and screenshot.

AI Knowledge Club Links: [tg](https://t.me/aiknowledgeclub), [youtube](https://www.youtube.com/@AIKnowledgeClub)
----------------------
# Evaluation

In this competition, your task is to predict one or more genres for each game in the test set. For each game ID, your prediction must be a single string containing the list of genres, separated by commas. This format should match how labels are provided in the train.csv file.

Submissions will be evaluated using the macro-averaged F1-score, which computes the F1-score independently for each genre and then takes the unweighted average across all genres.

This means that rare genres are treated equally to frequent ones, encouraging models that perform well across the full range of genre labels.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a genres variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,genres
    0,¡±RPG¡±
    1,¡±RPG, Action¡±
    2,¡±RPG¡±
    etc.","# Dataset Description

### Files

- train.csv ¨C the training set. Each row contains information about a video game, including its title, description, an image URL, and the list of genres (target labels).
- test.csv ¨C the test set. Same format as train.csv, but without the genres field. Your task is to predict the genres for each game in this file.
- sample_submission.csv ¨C a sample submission file in the correct format. Use this as a template for formatting your predictions.

### Format and Columns

- ID ¨C a unique identifier for the game.
- title ¨C the game's title (short text).
- description ¨C a textual description of the game (can be multiple sentences).
- image ¨C a URL pointing to a representative image (screenshot or cover) of the game.
- genres ¨C (train only) a string of one or more genre labels, separated by commas. This is the multi-label target you need to predict.",False,True
9679,machine-learning-4-sbu,"# Overview

Predict whether an employee will leave the company using a variety of organizational, personal, and behavioral features.

You are provided with anonymized HR records for a set of employees. Your task is to build a binary classification model that predicts the likelihood of an employee leaving the company.

Use your best classification techniques to climb the leaderboard.

A bonus will be awarded for achieving high predictive performance!



----------------------
# Description

This challenge provides an anonymized HR dataset from a synthetic corporate environment. Each row corresponds to an individual employee, including information such as:

* Age, salary, and education
* Work division, job role, and commute distance
* Job satisfaction, work-life balance, and promotion history

Your goal is to predict the binary `left_company` label¡ªwhether the employee left (`1`) or stayed (`0`).

This competition simulates a real-world business use case where accurate employee retention prediction can inform HR strategies and reduce turnover costs.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored using the **F1 Score** for binary classification:

$$
F1 = 2 \times \frac{precision \times recall}{precision + recall}
$$

The F1 score balances **precision** (how many selected items are relevant) and **recall** (how many relevant items are selected), making it ideal for datasets with class imbalance.

- The public leaderboard is based on 50% of the test data.
- The final private leaderboard (revealed after the competition ends) uses the remaining 50%.

**Bonus Criteria**: Only models with F1 score above **0.70** will qualify for bonus points in the final grade.","# Dataset Description

## ?? Files

| File                       | Rows ¡Á Cols | What it contains                                                       |
| -------------------------- | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **train.csv**              | 1341¡Á 35    | 33 feature columns, an **ID** column, and the target **left\_company** |
| **test.csv**               | 336¡Á 34    | Same 33 features + ID (no `left_company`)                              |
| **sample\_submission.csv** | 336¡Á 2     | Template for your submission (`ID`,`left_company`)                     |

---

## ?? Column Definitions

| Column                         | In files         | Type        | Description                                                                                   |
| ------------------------------ | ---------------- | ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **ID**                         | all              | int         | Unique employee identifier                                                                    |
| **age\_years**                 | all              | int         | Employee age in years                                                                         |
| **travel\_freq**               | all              | categorical | Travel frequency (`frequent_travel`, `rare_travel`, `non_travel`)                             |
| **daily\_salary**              | all              | int         | Daily salary in local currency units                                                          |
| **work\_division**             | all              | categorical | Department of employment (e.g., `rnd`, `sales`, `hr`, `management`, etc.)                     |
| **commute\_distance**          | all              | int         | Commute distance category (e.g., number of miles/km to office)                                |
| **education\_level**           | all              | int         | Education level code (e.g., 1 = below college, 2 = college, 3 = bachelor, 4 = master & above) |
| **degree\_field**              | all              | categorical | Field of highest degree (`life_sci`, `medical`, `marketing`, etc.)                            |
| **headcount**                  | all              | int         | Number of direct reports (team size)                                                          |
| **env\_satisfaction**          | all              | int (1¨C4)   | Environmental satisfaction rating (1 = Low ¡­ 4 = Very High)                                   |
| **job\_involvement**           | all              | int (1¨C4)   | Level of job involvement                                                                      |
| **job\_level**                 | all              | int         | Hierarchical job level                                                                        |
| **job\_satisfaction**          | all              | int (1¨C4)   | Job satisfaction rating                                                                       |
| **performance\_rating**        | all              | int (1¨C4)   | Performance rating                                                                            |
| **relationship\_satisfaction** | all              | int (1¨C4)   | Satisfaction with workplace relationships                                                     |
| **stock\_level**               | all              | int         | Stock option level                                                                            |
| **work\_life\_balance**        | all              | int (1¨C4)   | Work¨Clife balance rating                                                                      |
| **std\_work\_hours**           | all              | int         | Standard weekly work hours                                                                    |
| **career\_years**              | all              | int         | Total years of professional experience                                                        |
| **years\_current\_role**       | all              | int         | Years spent in current role                                                                   |
| **years\_post\_promotion**     | all              | int         | Years since last promotion                                                                    |
| **years\_with\_manager**       | all              | int         | Years with current manager                                                                    |
| **left\_company**              | *train.csv* only | int (0/1)   | **Target**: 1 = left the company, 0 = remained                                                |

*No missing values: every field is fully populated.*

---

## ?? Prediction Target

Your model must predict **`left_company`** (0 or 1) for each row in **test.csv**.

---

## ?? Submission Format

Upload a CSV file with **exactly two columns**:

```
ID,left_company
```

* `ID` ¨C copied from the **ID** column in *test.csv*
* `left_company` ¨C your predicted binary label (0 = stay, 1 = leave)

A valid submission starts like:

```
ID,left_company
0,0
1,1
2,0
...
```

Good luck, and happy modeling!",False,True
9680,binary-prediction-of-smoker,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use binary classification to predict a patient's smoking status given information about various other health indicators. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the probability for the target variable `smoking`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,smoking
    159256,0.5
    159257,0.5
    159258,0.5
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Smoker Status Prediction using Bio-Signals](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/gauravduttakiit/smoker-status-prediction-using-biosignals) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `smoking` is the binary target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of positive `smoking`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9681,data-mining-2025-spring-homework-1-test,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to develop a computational model capable of achieving automatic and accurate cell type annotation for single cell DNA methylation data.
----------------------
# Description

This competition focuses on developing a robust computational model for automatic cell type annotation. Participants will leverage state-of-the-art machine learning techniques to model single cell DNA methylation data and accurately annotate cell types.


----------------------
# Evaluation


Submissions are evaluated on [Cohen¡¯s kappa score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cohen%27s_kappa) between the predicted label and the true label.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a cell type for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```python
    ID,TARGET
    0, IT-L23
    1, IT-L23
    2, ODC
```
    etc, where ID is the obs_names of single-cell data.

## Notes
- Maximum daily submission: 5 times
- If there is any question or concern, you may discuss with us on Feishu.","# Dataset Description


The training set and test set are provided in the [AnnData](https://anndata.readthedocs.io/en/stable/index.html) (`.h5ad`) format, a widely used data structure for single-cell data. In this format:
- Rows represent cells, and columns represent features (i.e., genome bins).
- The cell type annotations for each cell are stored in `adata.obs['cell_type']`.
- The training and test sets contain NaN values. Participants should take appropriate measures to handle these missing values effectively.

## Files

*   **train_adata.h5ad** - the training set with cell type annotations
*   **test_adata_noLabel.h5ad** - the test set without cell type annotations
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a cell type for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```python
    ID,TARGET
    0, IT-L23
    1, IT-L23
    2, ODC
```
    etc, where ID is the obs_names of single-cell data.",False,True
9682,alpha-dent,"# Overview

In this task, we provide participants with a set of high-quality dental intraoral photographs. The goal is to annotate them into 9 classes using instance segmentation. An example of the annotation is shown in the figure below as well as every class examples.

![Dataset AlphaDent example 1](https://soloro.ru/images/2025/AlphaDent_Example_v1.jpg)
![Dataset AlphaDent example 2](https://soloro.ru/images/2025/AlphaDent_Example_v2.jpg)
![Dataset AlphaDent classes examples](https://soloro.ru/images/2025/AlphaDent_Classes.jpg)
----------------------
# Evaluation

Participants' solutions are evaluated using the [mAP@50](https://learnopencv.com/mean-average-precision-map-object-detection-model-evaluation-metric/) metric. The metric code is available [at this link](https://www.kaggle.com/code/zfturbo/map-50-for-instance-segmentation).

## Submission File
For each test image (135 in total), you need to predict a set of masks for each of the 9 pathology classes. Each test image may have multiple predicted masks. Each prediction should be written on a separate line and consist of 4 fields:

`patient_id` - image ID (filename without extension)
`class_id` - class ID (ranging from 0 to 8, inclusive)
`confidence` - model confidence score
`poly` - polygon defined by a set of points in the format: x1 y1 x2 y2 ... xN yN. Each point is normalized to the [0, 1] range. Polygons follow the YOLO format specification.

The submission file must adhere to the following format:

```csv
patient_id,class_id,confidence,poly
test_000,0,0.963867,""0.100101 0.900101 0.100102 0.101010 0.901011 0.101012 0.901013 0.901041""
test_001,1,0.845342,""0.200101 0.800101 0.200102 0.201010 0.801011 0.201012 0.801013 0.801041""
...
```
----------------------
# Citation

More details in paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.22512

```
@misc{sosnin2025alphadent,
  title={AlphaDent: A dataset for automated tooth pathology detection},
  author={Evgeniy I. Sosnin and Yuriy L. Vasilev and Roman A. Solovyev and Aleksandr L. Stempkovskiy and Dmitry V. Telpukhov and Artem A. Vasilev and Aleksandr A. Amerikanov and Aleksandr Y. Romanov},
  journal={Computer Optics},
  volume={49},
  pages={6},
  year={2025},
}
```","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of images (.jpg), annotations (.txt), and a YAML file named yolo_seg_train.yaml. The dataset format follows the [Ultralytics/YOLO annotation format for Instance Segmentation tasks](https://docs.ultralytics.com/datasets/segment/). The dataset is divided into three parts: training, validation, and test. For this task, you need to predict masks for the test portion, which has no annotations in the dataset.

A total of 1,320 photographs from 295 patients were used in the study (each patient has multiple photographs available). Nine classes were annotated, as shown in the table below.

## Classes description

| Class ID | English Name       | Description                                                                 |
|----------|--------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| 0        | Abrasion           | Teeth with mechanical wear of hard tissues                                  |
| 1        | Filling            | Dental fillings of various types                                            |
| 2        | Crown              | Dental crown (restoration)                                                 |
| 3        | Caries Class 1     | Caries in fissures and pits (occlusal surfaces of molars/premolars, buccal surfaces of molars, lingual surfaces of upper incisors). |
| 4        | Caries Class 2     | Caries on proximal surfaces of molars and premolars.                       |
| 5        | Caries Class 3     | Caries on proximal surfaces of incisors/canines *without* incisal edge involvement. |
| 6        | Caries Class 4     | Caries on proximal surfaces of incisors/canines *with* incisal edge involvement. |
| 7        | Caries Class 5     | Cervical caries (buccal/lingual surfaces).                                 |
| 8        | Caries Class 6     | Caries on incisal edges of anterior teeth or cusps of molars.              |

The dataset was split by patients, not by images. 273 patients were included in the training set, and 22 patients were allocated to the validation set. Detailed class statistics for the training and validation sets are provided in the table below.

## Train and validation split

| Class Name       | Training Data                   || Validation Data                ||
|------------------|--------------------------------|--------------------------------|
|                  | Images Containing the Class    | Annotated Masks for the Class  | Images Containing the Class    | Annotated Masks for the Class  |
| Abrasion         | 1,065                         | 5,957                          | 73                             | 409                            |
| Filling          | 695                           | 2,187                          | 48                             | 186                            |
| Crown            | 210                           | 570                            | 9                              | 19                             |
| Caries Class 1   | 341                           | 742                            | 30                             | 62                             |
| Caries Class 2   | 471                           | 1,026                          | 41                             | 73                             |
| Caries Class 3   | 238                           | 474                            | 23                             | 33                             |
| Caries Class 4   | 30                            | 43                             | 3                              | 4                              |
| Caries Class 5   | 328                           | 981                            | 21                             | 81                             |
| Caries Class 6   | 33                            | 52                             | 4                              | 5                              |


## Dataset Statistics

    Number of male patients: 131
    Number of female patients: 162
    Average number of images per patient: 4.5
    Most images have a resolution exceeding 5000 ¡Á 3000 (15 megapixels).
    Average number of bounding boxes per image: 9.77

Below is the directory structure of the dataset. It is organized to allow direct training of YOLO models without modifications.

## Directory structure 
```
AlphaDent  
©¦  
©¸©¤©¤ yolo_seg_train.yaml ¨C YAML file containing class descriptions  
©À©¤©¤ images ¨C contains all dataset images, divided into three folders  
