9684,histopathology,"# Overview

Histopathology plays a vital role in the diagnosis and classification of various diseases, especially cancers. In this competition, participants are challenged to develop a Deep Learning model that can classify histopathology images into one of four classes, each representing a different tissue or disease category.

This is a multi-class image classification problem, and participants will work with real histopathology image data. The dataset has been curated to reflect real-world variability in slide quality, staining, and tissue structure, making the task both challenging and meaningful.

Participants are expected to build models that can accurately learn patterns and differentiate between subtle differences across the four categories. The ultimate goal is to push the boundaries of computer vision in medical imaging and contribute toward automating histopathological analysis.

----------------------
# Description

In the medical field, histopathology is the gold standard for diagnosing many diseases, especially various forms of cancer. However, manual examination of tissue samples under a microscope is time-consuming and subject to variability between pathologists. This competition aims to leverage the power of machine learning to assist in automating this critical task.

Participants are provided with a labelled dataset of histopathology images, each belonging to one of four classes. These classes represent different types of tissue samples or pathological conditions. The objective is to train a classification model that can predict the correct class of a given image with the highest possible accuracy.

The competition is designed to simulate a real-world medical image classification problem, where model performance can have significant implications. Competitors must focus on model robustness, efficient preprocessing, and effective learning of visual features within the images.

Only individual participation is allowed, and external datasets or pre-trained models are not permitted. The winning solution will be the one that achieves the highest classification accuracy on the unseen test set.

This competition provides an excellent opportunity for participants to apply their skills in deep learning, computer vision, and medical imaging while also contributing to a problem of practical significance in healthcare.
----------------------
# Evaluation

To assess the performance of your classification model, we will consider multiple evaluation metrics. Although Accuracy will determine the final ranking, other metrics are useful for model development and deeper analysis.

# **1. Accuracy**
The proportion of correctly predicted samples over the total number of samples.
$$
\text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{Number of Correct Predictions}}{\text{Total Number of Predictions}} = \frac{TP + TN}{TP + TN + FP + FN}
$$

# **2. Precision**
Measures the proportion of true positives among all predicted positives for a class.

# **3. Recall**
Measures the proportion of true positives among all actual positives for a class.

# **4. F1-Score (per class and macro average)**
The harmonic mean of precision and recall.
$$
F1 = 2 \cdot \frac{\text{Precision} \cdot \text{Recall}}{\text{Precision} + \text{Recall}}
$$","# Dataset Description

The dataset used in this competition is sourced from real-world colon histopathology slides provided by Chaoyang Hospital. It contains high-resolution image patches labelled into four distinct categories:

- Normal (Label-0)
- Serrated (Label-1)
- Adenoma (Label-2)
- Adenocarcinoma (Label-3)

Each image patch is of size 512 ¡Á 512 pixels, extracted from whole-slide images (WSIs) scanned at 20¡Á objective magnification.

# **Labeling Procedure**
Each image patch was independently labeled by 3 expert pathologists.

The test set contains only those samples where all 3 pathologists reached a consensus, ensuring high-quality labels.

**The training set includes:**

Patches with consensus from all three pathologists.

Patches with disagreements (~40%), where one label was randomly selected from the three opinions to simulate real-world noise.

This design introduces label noise into the training data, reflecting real clinical uncertainty and making the task more challenging and realistic.",False,True
