9688,social-sim-challenge,"# Overview

Welcome to the **SocialSim Challenge**! This competition invites participants to build models that simulate **user behavior on social media platforms** using **large language models (LLMs)**. Your task is to develop systems that can accurately emulate how different user personas interact, post, and respond in online environments.

This competition is inspired by the growing research on **agent-based modeling**, **behavioral evaluation**, and the **ethical simulation of social systems**. By participating, you'll help push forward the frontier of using AI to understand and replicate digital social behavior¡ªan essential step for designing safer and more responsive online platforms.

----------------------
# Description

This competition supports the [**SocialSim'25**](https://sites.google.com/view/social-sims-with-llms/home#h.yup5e9zbopt4) workshop at [COLM 2025](https://colmweb.org/cfw.html), and is hosted in collaboration with leading researchers in AI ethics, NLP, and computational social science. It draws on the [BluePrint dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/your-team/blueprint), a novel resource for persona-based language modeling. 

?? Task Objective
Given a user persona and a history of interactions, your model should predict the next plausible action or message. The goal is to emulate realistic social media behavior, capturing the nuances of different user personas. Participants will work with structured data derived from a large-scale, anonymized social media archive, complete with **clustered user personas**, **interaction traces**, and **temporal dynamics**.

----------------------
# Evaluation

To be updated! 

* F1-score on the action space
* co-sine similarity
* JS divergence",,False,True
9689,heads-or-tails-image-classification,"# Overview

The [coin toss](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coin_flipping) is a common touchpoint for many when discussing statistics and probability, but why stop there?

In this competition, classifying the outcome of a coin toss is presented as an approachable computer vision task for beginners. 800 images are provided for training a machine learning model, to be tested on 200 unlabelled samples. 

This problem necessitates attention to detail, with subtle features being presented in a variety of orientations. It also presents additional challenge in the limited number of training samples and deliberate imbalances within the dataset...

**Goal:** Using a machine learning model, predict the outcome of a coin toss for each of the 200 unlabelled images.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probabilities and the ground truth targets. This gives a score between 0.0 and 1.0, where higher is better.


## Submission File
For each image sample in the test set, you must predict a probability for the class ```heads```. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    prediction_id,probability_of_heads
    1,0.5
    2,0.5
    3,0.5
    etc.",,False,True
