9721,bin-ego-360-challenge-tal,"# Overview

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F16342273%2F54806d6ca5c3298b740c930a9360103f%2FScreenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2013.49.19.png?generation=1748609376448475&alt=media)

> **Important Notice:**
> **New Final Submission Deadline????10 August 2025 (AOE)**
> We are delighted to let you know that the challenge deadline has been extended! Now you have more time to cook your good stuff! 

> Because the initial Kaggle competition could no longer be re?opened after its closing date, **a new competition page has been created to host the extended track**:
https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/bin-ego-360-challenge-tal-ext

> All rules, evaluation metrics, and prizes remain unchanged (with an additional prize of registration fee!); only the schedule has shifted, and you can submit via kaggle's sandbox. If you submitted to the first competition, simply re?upload your final model predictions here before the new deadline. ?

## Goal of the [BinEgo-360](https://x360dataset.github.io/BinEgo-360/#challenge) Challenge

What does it take for a model to understand a scene¡ªis it enough to see it? To hear it? Or is there more?

We present an exciting new challenge at the frontier of panoptic video understanding. The goal is to explore **multi-perspective learning**¡ªunderstanding how the fusion of diverse viewpoints and modalities, such as egocentric vision, 360¡ã third-person context, audio, and location cues, can lead to a more complete and holistic representation of complex scenes.

Join us in pushing the boundaries of multi-view learning. Your solution could help shape the future of perception in robotics, AR/VR, and human-centric video intelligence.

~~~~

>**This challenge is only for the ICCV 2025 BinEgo360 workshop and Challenge, and all the prizes will be issued at the workshop**

## Context

This challenge is part of the [BinEgo-360](https://x360dataset.github.io/BinEgo-360/#challenge) workshop. **Winners of the challenge will be invited to jointly write a paper about the workshop and challenge to be included in the ICCV proceedings.** The objective of this track is to detect the start and end time of every action instance for a given video clip. For more details about the dataset, tracks, timeline, and submission rules, please visit: https://x360dataset.github.io/BinEgo-360/#challenge.

We primarily incorporate two types of videos:

- **First-person (egocentric) video** captures the visual experience from the capturer's point of view. It includes direct observations of objects, hands, and interactions at close range, often reflecting the actor¡¯s focus and activity. The content is typically constrained by a limited field of view and may include frequent occlusions due to body movement or object handling.
- **360¡ã third-person video** provides an omnidirectional view of the environment from an external perspective. It records the full spatial layout and captures multiple agents within the scene. This perspective remains external to the actor, without access to internal states or viewpoint-specific information.




----------------------
# Prizes

### Awards & Prizes

To recognize outstanding solutions, we will award top-performing teams based on final leaderboard rankings.

**Total Prizes Available:** 3 winning spots

- **1st Place** ¨C Insta360 X5 panoramic camera + GPU Cloud Credit (worth ¡ê5-15k) on SCAN 
- **2nd Place** ¨C Amazon / Taobao vouchers + GPU Cloud Credit (worth ¡ê5-15k) on SCAN
- **3rd Place** ¨C Amazon / Taobao vouchers + GPU Cloud Credit (worth ¡ê5-15k) on SCAN


All awards are sponsored by **Insta360**, **SCAN**, and **Allsee Technologies**.
----------------------
# Description

In this track, participants are tasked with detecting the start and end time of every action instance inside a clip. Each clip is annotated with human-labeled label, reflecting the action instance in the clip.

To solve this task, participants may leverage any subset or fusion of the following modalities provided in the dataset:

- **360¡ã panoramic video**: Omnidirectional video capturing the entire surrounding scene from a fixed location.
- **Third-person frontal video**: A narrow-field view extracted from the 360¡ã video, offering less distortion but limited spatial coverage.
- **Egocentric monocular and binocular videos**: First-person views recorded alongside the 360¡ã video, ranging from zero to multiple clips, spatially distributed within the 360¡ã video.
- **Spatial 6-channel audio (360¡ã video)**: Multichannel spatial audio captured along with the 360¡ã video, preserving directional sound information.
- **Spatial 2-channel audio (egocentric video)**: Stereo audio recorded from the egocentric cameras.
- **GPS and weather metadata**: Geolocation and environmental context associated with each video clip.
- **Scene-level textual descriptions**: High-level summaries of the scene. *(Not provided for the validation set.)*


The objective is to explore **multi-view, multi-modal learning** for temporal action localisation. This setting encourages models that integrate spatial, temporal, auditory, and textual cues for robust classification across diverse real-world environments.


----------------------
# Evaluation

### Scoring with mAP

Mean Average Precision (**mAP**) is used as the scoring function by evaluating the quality of temporal detection predictions. The model must identify the correct action label, along with the corresponding start and end timestamps within each video clip.

For each detection, the output should be in the following JSON format:

```json
{
  ""video_id"": ...,
  ""t_start"": ...,
  ""t_end"": ...,
  ""label"": ...
}
```

Predictions are evaluated against ground-truth annotations using **mean Average Precision (mAP)**, averaged over a set of temporal Intersection over Union (**IoU**) thresholds:

$$
\text{IoU} \in \{0.5, 0.75, 0.95\}
$$

For each IoU threshold, **Average Precision (AP)** is computed per class by ranking predictions according to confidence scores, computing precision-recall curves, and integrating the area under the curve. The **mean** is then taken across all classes and all specified IoU thresholds to yield the final mAP score.

This scoring method rewards both high localization precision (via IoU) and correct classification, and is well-suited for evaluating temporal detection tasks.

### Leaderboard Ranking

Final leaderboard ranking is based on the overall mAP score:

$$
\text{Final Score} = \frac{1}{|T|} \sum_{t \in T} \frac{1}{C} \sum_{c=1}^{C} AP_{c}^{(t)}
$$

Where:

- $T = \{0.5, 0.75, 0.95\}$ is the set of IoU thresholds  
- $C$ is the total number of classes  
- $AP_c^{(t)}$ is the Average Precision for class $c$ at IoU threshold $t$  

This metric encourages models to produce temporally precise and class-accurate predictions across a range of detection difficulties.","# Dataset Description

The challenge uses our public dataset 360+x for training/validation, and a held-out test set for the evaluation. The full dataset can be fetched: [https://huggingface.co/datasets/quchenyuan/360x_dataset_HR](https://huggingface.co/datasets/quchenyuan/360x_dataset_HR)


![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F16342273%2F645b624e0405ed841981d6377c0e9498%2Fteaser_video.gif?generation=1748609310125434&alt=media)

Dataset Overview:
360+x captures real-world environments from four synchronised viewpoints (360¡ã panoramic, binocular egocentric, monocular egocentric, third-person front) together with 6-channel spatial audio, GPS, weather and text metadata. The dataset is designed to study how an embodied agent could perceive, localise actions and recognise scenes when immersed in a space, while also observing itself from an external vantage.",False,True
9722,detect-leaking-data,"# Overview

   
----------------------
# Summary ??

This competition's main task is to detect data leakage in the given texts. Each text originally came from [The Messenger] (https://messenger.eso.org) journal but has been significantly modified using large language models (LLMs). Only after these modifications was a hidden message inserted into each text.

To solve the task, you have access to:
1) Three specially curated texts created for this competition, each containing a hidden message.
2) A notebook demonstrating how to build a sample solution.

If needed, additional hints will be publicly provided during the hackathon ¡ª see *Discussion* tab for more details and rules.
----------------------
# Description ??

ESA's European Space Operations Centre initiated a project to outline a clear direction for data strategy and AI implementation in mission operations. This initiative led to the creation of the [DataX] (https://iafastro.directory/iac/paper/id/89097/summary/) [1] strategy, developed to improve the scalability of advanced analytics applications, including AI systems and to increase the value of data within the organization. One of the key elements of developing an AI system is the **security of AI applications** addressed within the [**Assurance for Space Domain AI Applications** project](https://assurance-ai.space-codev.org/) and its [Catalogue of Security Risks for AI Applications in Space](https://zenodo.org/records/14762574). This competition is based on a real-life AI security threat identified within the project - **data leakage** [2].

## Story

There was unauthorized access reported in your company's network. Your job is to investigate the incident and check if the data from the recent important discovery is safe. No file transfer has been observed, but some of the latest PDFs of the [""The Messenger"" magazine from the ESO webpage](https://messenger.eso.org/) were irreversibly replaced on servers with their slightly modified versions. You are aware of [steganography](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steganography) and different [techniques](https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/9/21/2829) of hiding information in text, so you suspect that some sensitive data may have been hidden in the articles from the magazine. Your next task is to check this hypothesis and determine what information was breached.

## Task description

Find the secret message hidden in the changed ""The Messenger"" files. Both contaminated files and messages are text. We provide changed PDF files in [Discussion tab](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detect-leaking-data/discussion). 

You are given three texts, with three messages hidden, one in each. The difficulty increases with increasing numbers in the file names.

The hackathon can be solved in teams of up to 3 members. You have a maximum of 10 submissions per team, so use them wisely.

### Data

Data contains ""The Messenger"" articles intentionally modified using LLMs, so they are not the same as the ones available online, and you do not have access to their versions from before the attack.

More details regarding the data are provided in the [Data tab](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detect-leaking-data/data).

### Code
The competition offers a [notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detect-leaking-data/code) to show how to read PDF files and create a sample solution format.

----------------------
# Awards ??

<img title=¡±aistar¡± src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6945783%2F79f4cfb74edc38bebd6be5a7dc8779f1%2Fobraz_2025-10-25_215736469.png?generation=1761422258677122&alt=media""; style=""float:right; height:200px; width:350px"">

The award ceremony and best teams presentations will take place during the EASi workshop ECAI 2025, on 26th October 2025 at 5 PM.

Please check the [Rules tab] (https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detect-leaking-data/rules) for further details about eligibility and criteria.

----------------------
# Evaluation metric ?

Submissions are evaluated using the **averaged normalised Levenshtein distance** (also known as the edit distance) [3]. Normalisation is performed by dividing the classical edit distance by the length of the longer string. You can think about the classical edit distance as a number of operations (removing, swapping, and inserting) performed on individual characters to transform your answer into the correct solution. **Thus, if your answer is correct, the distance equals 0. If, on the other hand, nothing in your answer matches (everything should be either removed, swapped or inserted), the distance is equal to 1.**

**Tip:** Because the distance is averaged over all tasks, and the distance from the "" "" string to the correct answer is always 1, you can set all solutions to the "" "" string except the one you are currently solving and check if the metric is equal to ""1 - (1/N)"", where N is the number of tasks. If this is true, you predicted this task correctly!

In the case of identical scores for two teams on the final leaderboard, priority will be given to the team that solved tasks with higher numbers (they are more difficult). Later, the faster team. [This further evaluation will be done manually by the organisers, before the Award Ceremony.] ?

**Please have in mind, that the lower the score the better.**
----------------------
# Submission file ??

Provide a string with the found hidden message for each index representing a file number from [Discussion tab](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detect-leaking-data/discussion). The important part is that the value in the second column is of type string, which cannot be empty. You can also provide "" "" in the second column for test purposes. An example can be seen below:

| id | hidden_message|
| --- | --- |
|1|     |
|2|Some answer 1|
|3|Some answer 2|
|4|Some answer 3|
|5|Some answer 4|

The file should be saved in CSV.
----------------------
# Timeline ?

* **Start Date** - 26 October 2025 at 11:00 AM
* **Entry deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final submission deadline** -   26 October 2025 at 4:00 PM
* **Private leaderboard release** -  during Award Ceremony

All deadlines are in CET on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if deemed necessary.
----------------------
# Organizers Team ?

The competition is organized on behalf of ESA (Evridiki Ntagiou) by [MI Space Team/ Centre for Credible AI Team from Warsaw University of Technology](http://credibleai.eu/) (Przemys?aw Biecek, Artur Janicki, Agata Kaczmarek, Dawid P?udowski and Piotr Wilczy¨½ski) and [KPLabs](https://www.kplabs.space) (Krzysztof Kotowski, Jakub Nalepa and Ramez Shendy). 
----------------------
# References ??

[1] E. Ntagiou, J. Eggleston, K. Cichecka, and P. Collins, ""DataX: a state of the art data strategy for mission operations"", in 75th International Astronautical Congress 2024. Available: https://iafastro.directory/iac/paper/id/89097/summary/

[2] K. Kotowski, P. Wilczy¨½ski, D. P?udowski, A. Kaczmarek, R. Shendy, J. Nalepa, P. Biecek, and E. Ntagiou, ""Towards Explainable and Secure AI for Space Mission Operations"", SpaceOps 2025. Available: https://star.spaceops.org/2025/user_manudownload.php?doc=150__traivhwa.pdf

[3] Levenshtein, Vladimir I. ""Binary codes capable of correcting deletions, insertions, and reversals."" Soviet physics doklady. Vol. 10. No. 8. 1966.",,False,True
9723,map-charting-student-math-misunderstandings,"# Overview

In this competition, you¡¯ll develop an NLP model driven by ML to accurately predict students¡¯ potential math misconceptions based on student explanations in open-ended responses. This solution will suggest candidate misconceptions for these explanations, making it easier for  teachers to identify and address students¡¯  incorrect thinking, which is critical to improving student math learning.
----------------------
# Description

Students are often asked to explain their mathematical reasoning. These explanations provide rich insight into student thinking and often reveal underlying misconceptions (systematic incorrect ways of thinking).

For example, students often think 0.355 is larger than 0.8 because they incorrectly apply their knowledge of whole numbers to decimals, reasoning that 355 is greater than 8. Students develop a range of misconceptions in math, sometimes because they incorrectly apply prior knowledge to new content and sometimes because they are trying to make sense of new information but misunderstand it. To read more about these definitions and framework, please see the [linked report here]( https://the-learning-agency.com/news-press/ai-in-math-improving-error-identification-and-feedback/).This competition aims to explore such possibilities by encouraging participants to develop models that can distinguish between these types of  conceptual errors in students¡¯ responses, paving the way for improved feedback and better learning outcomes.

Tagging students¡¯ explanations as containing potential misconceptions is valuable for diagnostic feedback but is time-consuming and challenging to scale. Misconceptions can be subtle, vary in specificity, and evolve as new patterns in student reasoning emerge.
Initial efforts to use pre-trained language models have not been successful, likely due to the complexity of the mathematical content in the questions. Therefore, a more efficient and consistent approach is needed to streamline the tagging process and enhance the overall quality.

The MAP (Misconception Annotation Project) competition challenges you to develop a Natural Language Processing (NLP) model driven by Machine Learning (ML) that predicts students¡¯ potential math misconceptions based on student explanations. The goal is to create a model that identifies potential math misconceptions that generalize across different problems. 
Your work could help improve the understanding and management of misconceptions, enhancing the educational experience for both students and teachers.

Vanderbilt University and The Learning Agency have partnered with Kaggle to host this competition.

### Acknowledgments 

Vanderbilt University and The Learning Agency would like to thank the Gates Foundation and the Walton Family Foundation for their support in making this work possible, as well as [Eedi ](https://eedi.com/us) for providing the data. Eedi is an edtech platform that helps students ages 9 to 16 master math by identifying and resolving misconceptions using diagnostic questions, AI-powered insights, and access to live one-on-one tutoring to boost understanding and confidence.

<a href=""https://www.waltonfamilyfoundation.org/""><img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F16341929%2Fb39e4ed86f6986a0b69d521ccea1c406%2Fwff-logo.webp?generation=1750869647492153&alt=media"" alt=""Walton Logo"" width=""250""></a>
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated according to the Mean Average Precision @ 3 (MAP@3):

$$\text{MAP@3} = \frac{1}{U} \sum\_{u=1}^{U} \sum\_{k=1}^{min(n,3)} P(k) \times rel(k)$$

where \\( U \\)  is the number of observations, \\( P(k) \\) is the precision at cutoff \\( k \\), \\( n \\) is the number predictions submitted per observation, and \\( rel(k) \\) is an indicator function equaling 1 if the item at rank \\( k \\) is a relevant (correct) label, zero otherwise. 

Once a correct label has been scored for *an observation*, that label is no longer considered relevant for that observation, and additional predictions of that label are skipped in the calculation. For example, if the correct label is `A` for an observation, the following predictions all score an average precision of `1.0`.

    [A, B, C, D, E]
    [A, A, A, A, A]
    [A, B, A, C, A]

There is only one correct label per observation (hence no divisor term in front of the inner summation.)

## Submission File
For each `row_id` in the test set, you must predict the corresponding `Category` and `Misconception`, concatenated with a colon (`:`). You can predict up to 3 `Category:Misconception` values per row (any predictions beyond the third are ignored), and the predictions should be space-delimited. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
row_id,Category:Misconception
36696,True_Correct:NA False_Neither:NA False_Misconception:Incomplete
36697,True_Correct:NA False_Neither:NA False_Misconception:Incomplete
36698,True_Correct:NA False_Neither:NA False_Misconception:Incomplete
etc.
...
```
----------------------
