9734,diabetes-risk-forecast,"# Overview

Diabetes is a major global health concern, with rising prevalence and substantial impacts on individuals and healthcare systems. Early identification of at-risk individuals allows for preventive interventions that can significantly reduce disease burden.

In this competition, participants are challenged to develop machine learning models to predict whether an individual has diabetes based on their demographic information, medical history, and basic lab test results.

This binary classification task will help demonstrate the potential of predictive models in supporting early diabetes screening and risk assessment.
----------------------
# Description

Diabetes is one of the most common chronic diseases in the world. According to the International Diabetes Federation (IDF), more than 500 million people were affected by diabetes in 2021, and this number is projected to rise in the coming decades. Early detection of high-risk individuals through health screening data is crucial for timely intervention.

In this competition, your task is to develop a machine learning model that predicts the probability of a person having diabetes based on demographic and medical features.

The dataset consists of 100,000 records and includes features such as:

- Age

- Gender

- BMI (Body Mass Index)

- Hypertension

- Heart Disease History

- Smoking History

- HbA1c Level (Glycated Hemoglobin)

- Blood Glucose Level

The target variable is diabetes (0 = no diabetes, 1 = has diabetes). Your model should output the probability that a person has diabetes.

This challenge is ideal for students and machine learning enthusiasts who are interested in public health, healthcare data science, and predictive modeling.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on the Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC ROC) between the predicted probabilities and the actual outcomes.

AUC is a common metric for binary classification problems and measures how well your model separates the positive class (diabetes = 1) from the negative class (diabetes = 0).

?? Submission Format
Your submission file must be a CSV file with the following format:

ID,diabetes_probability
0,0.128
1,0.843
2,0.547
...

- ID corresponds to the row index in the test dataset.

- diabetes_probability should be a value between 0 and 1.

- The file must include a header row.","# Dataset Description

The dataset used in this competition was originally published on Kaggle and contains 100,000 anonymized health records. Each row represents an individual's medical and demographic information, along with their diabetes status.",False,True
9735,become-a-kaggle-master-2025-hw-3,"# Description

# Context
This is homework 3 of the course <a href=""https://www.lamsade.dauphine.fr/~ebenhamou/beccome_a_kaggle_master/index.html"">Become a Kaggle Master</a>
This course is part of the <a href=""https://dauphine.psl.eu/en/training/masters-degrees/computer-science/m2-artificial-intelligence-systems-data"">IASD Master 2 in Machine Learning, Artificial Intelligence, and Data Science</a> program, jointly run by <a href=""https://dauphine.psl.eu/en/"">Universit¨¦ Paris Dauphine</a>, <a href=""https://www.ens.psl.eu/en""> ENS</a>  and <a href=""https://mines-paristech.eu/""> Mines ParisTech</a>.

# Goal
This competition is a classification exercise. You are called upon to help the economic team of a leading consulting firm. The objective of the competition is to determine the economic regime predicted by the best economists for the emerging world compared to the rest of the world. Economists have identified 3 regimes for emerging countries versus the rest of the world:
- 1: growing faster
- 0: similar pace
- -1: slower



----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation is the AUC

You are asked to provide in your submission **2 columns**

- **Date**:  the date for the predicted return
- **Market_Regime**: the predicted market_regime of your model

Make sure you have the right columns as a submission not respecting this format will be considered invalid","# Dataset Description

We provide four files

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set. It contains features (columns 1 to 25 corresponding to GDP of countries and region, as well some currencies indexes as well VIX and Oil index) + the forecasted market regime (last column)
*   **test.csv** - the test set containing only the 25 features
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9736,io-t-data-preprocessing-kaggle-summer-school-urjc,"# Overview

El objetivo de esta competencia es desarrollar un pipeline de preprocesamiento eficaz para datos de ciberseguridad en entornos IoT, utilizando el CIC IoT 2023 Dataset. Este conjunto de datos contiene tr¨¢fico de red generado por m¨¢s de 100 dispositivos, incluyendo tanto tr¨¢fico benigno como ataques maliciosos clasificados en diferentes categor¨ªas.

- La competici¨®n se centra principalmente en la etapa de preprocesamiento, es decir:

- Selecci¨®n y transformaci¨®n de caracter¨ªsticas.

- Codificaci¨®n de variables categ¨®ricas.

- Normalizaci¨®n o estandarizaci¨®n.

- Tratamiento de valores binarios (como TRUE / FALSE).

- Preparaci¨®n adecuada para modelos de Machine Learning.

**?Por qu¨¦ es importante el preprocesamiento?**
En ciberseguridad, el preprocesamiento de datos puede marcar la diferencia entre un modelo funcional y uno completamente in¨²til. Este reto pretende concienciar sobre la importancia de una limpieza cuidadosa, especialmente con variables como protocolos, banderas TCP, y frecuencias de paquetes.

Adem¨¢s, se busca analizar c¨®mo diferentes enfoques de preprocesamiento afectan al rendimiento predictivo de los modelos en tareas como la detecci¨®n de tr¨¢fico malicioso.

----------------------
# Description

?? **Formato de los datos**
Los archivos disponibles son:

train.csv: contiene ejemplos con la variable objetivo (benign) indicando si el tr¨¢fico es benigno (TRUE) o malicioso (FALSE).

test.csv: contiene ejemplos sin etiqueta, sobre los cuales deber¨¢s predecir.

sample_submission.csv: muestra el formato que debe tener tu archivo de predicciones.

**?? ?Qu¨¦ se espera de ti?**
Preprocesar los datos del conjunto de entrenamiento y test.

Entrenar un modelo de clasificaci¨®n binaria (benigno vs. malicioso).

Generar un archivo submission.csv con tus predicciones sobre el conjunto de test.

Subir tu archivo y observar tu puntuaci¨®n en el leaderboard.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Las predicciones ser¨¢n evaluadas seg¨²n la accuracy (exactitud) del modelo sobre un conjunto oculto de etiquetas. La columna objetivo es benign, y se espera una predicci¨®n por cada id en el conjunto de test.

Ejemplo de archivo de env¨ªo:

id,benign
1,FALSE
2,TRUE
3,FALSE
...",,False,True
