# Evaluation

##Evaluation Metric

Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) is a commonly used evaluation metric for regression problems in Kaggle competitions. It measures the average magnitude of the errors between the predicted and actual values, providing an indication of the model's accuracy.

RMSE is calculated by taking the square root of the mean of the squared differences between the predicted and actual values. The formula for RMSE is as follows:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F21861189%2F7e45d48afb70f6830d227d43c4ff869e%2FScreenshot%202025-06-10%20222724.png?generation=1749574701457452&alt=media)

RMSE has the following characteristics:

- It is always non-negative since it involves squaring the differences.
- It penalizes larger errors more than smaller errors due to the squaring operation.
- It has the same unit as the target variable, making it easy to interpret.


When comparing models in a Kaggle competition, a lower Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) indicates better performance. The model with the lowest RMSE score is considered the most accurate in terms of minimising the differences between the predicted and actual values.

It's important to note that RMSE is sensitive to outliers since larger errors have a greater impact on the overall score. Therefore, it is crucial to preprocess the data and handle outliers appropriately to obtain reliable RMSE scores.

In summary, RMSE is a popular evaluation metric for regression problems in Kaggle competitions. It provides a measure of the average magnitude of the errors, allowing participants to assess and compare the accuracy of their models.

Participants in this competition can leverage a structured approach to gain profound insights into the dataset, crucial for developing accurate and resilient models. The steps of dataset overview, descriptive statistics, data visualizations, and feature engineering play pivotal roles in achieving this goal.
