# Deliverables

Participants are expected to submit the following:

* **Round 1 Submission** : A solution.csv file containing predicted race outcomes (lap time in seconds for each rider) submitted via Kaggle by the Round 1 deadline (06/14/2025, 10:00 PM IST). Maximum 3 submissions allowed per team, with the best submission automatically selected for evaluation. ??
* **Jupyter Notebook** : A fully reproducible Jupyter Notebook detailing your analysis, including Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA), feature engineering, and model development, submitted alongside the solution.csv. ??
* **Round 2 Presentation** : A PowerPoint (PPT) presentation with exactly 10 slides (including title and thank you slides), submitted via a Google Form link 30 minutes before the scheduled presentation time on 06/15/2025. Each team will present for up to 12 minutes, covering their MotoGP analytics model and answering judges¡¯ questions.??

----------------------
# Success Criteria

Your submission will be evaluated based on the following criteria:

* **Accuracy of Predictions** : How well your model predicts MotoGP race outcomes, measured by a 50-50 combination of public and private leaderboard scores on Kaggle. ??
* **Understanding of the Problem Statement** : Demonstrated grasp of the MotoGP racing dynamics and the challenges outlined in the problem statement. ??
* **Type of Solution Used** : Appropriateness and innovation of the modeling techniques applied (e.g., regression, ensemble methods). ???
* **Modeling Techniques and Justification** : Quality of the modeling approach, including feature engineering, and the rationale behind your choices. ??
* **Insights Generated** : Relevance and depth of insights into racing dynamics derived from your analysis. ??
* **Presentation Quality** : Clarity, structure, design, and delivery of your Round 2 presentation, ensuring effective communication of your findings. ??
----------------------
# Code Requirements

Submissions must be made through Jupyter Notebooks on Kaggle. To enable the ""Submit"" button after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

* **CPU Notebook < 12 hours run-time** 
* **GPU Notebook < 12 hours run-time** 

All work must be fully reproducible by the contest organisers. Offline pretraining (on external computers) is allowed; just ensure to document your process so we can reproduce it if you win.

**Language**: Kaggle notebooks allow you to write code in Python and R, but participants can virtually code in any language other than that (if they are that brave). All that matters is the submission file that the participants have to upload.

Participants are required to submit their solutions in a solution.csv file format directly on the Kaggle platform or through a notebook. Ensure the submission adheres to the specified format and includes all necessary predictions or analyses.

By following these steps meticulously, participants can uncover hidden insights, refine their models, and ultimately contribute to the advancement of predictive modeling in motorsports. ??

