ÎÒ‚ƒ•þÊ¹ÓÃAUROC×÷žéÔu¹ÀÖ¸˜Ë¡£Ã¿Ìì×î¶à¿ÉÒÔÌá½»10´Î¡£","# Dataset Description

This dataset is from the ""Default of Credit Card Clients DataSet "" on UCI Machine Learning Repository. https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/default+of+credit+card+clients

## File descriptions
data_train.csv - Training Data X
data_test.csv - Testing Data X
answer_train.csv - Training Data Y
answer_sample.csv - Sample Submission

## Data fileds
ID - ID of each client
LIMIT_BAL - Amount of given credit in NT dollars (includes individual and his/her family/supplementary credit)
SEX - Gender (1=male, 2=female)
EDUCATION - (1 = graduate school; 2 = university; 3 = high school; 0, 4, 5, 6 = others)
MARRIAGE - Marital status (1 = married; 2 = single; 3 = divorce; 0=others)
AGE - Age in years
PAY_0 - Repayment status in September, 2005 (-2: No consumption; -1: Paid in full; 0: The use of revolving credit; 1=payment delay for one month, 2=payment delay for two months, ¡­ 8=payment delay for eight months, 9=payment delay for nine months and above) -2 = Balance paid in full and no transactions this period (we may refer to this credit card account as having been 'inactive' this period)
-1 = Balance paid in full, but account has a positive balance at end of period due to recent transactions for which payment has not yet come due
0 = Customer paid the minimum due amount, but not the entire balance. I.e., the customer paid enough for their account to remain in good standing, but did revolve a balance

PAY_2 - Repayment status in August, 2005 (scale same as above)
PAY_3 - Repayment status in July, 2005 (scale same as above)
PAY_4 - Repayment status in June, 2005 (scale same as above)
PAY_5 - Repayment status in May, 2005 (scale same as above)
PAY_6 - Repayment status in April, 2005 (scale same as above)
BILL_AMT1 - Amount of bill statement in September, 2005 (NT dollar)
BILL_AMT2 - Amount of bill statement in August, 2005 (NT dollar)
BILL_AMT3 - Amount of bill statement in July, 2005 (NT dollar)
BILL_AMT4 - Amount of bill statement in June, 2005 (NT dollar)
BILL_AMT5 - Amount of bill statement in May, 2005 (NT dollar)
BILL_AMT6 - Amount of bill statement in April, 2005 (NT dollar)
PAY_AMT1 - Amount of previous payment in September, 2005 (NT dollar)
PAY_AMT2 - Amount of previous payment in August, 2005 (NT dollar)
PAY_AMT3 - Amount of previous payment in July, 2005 (NT dollar)
PAY_AMT4 - Amount of previous payment in June, 2005 (NT dollar)
PAY_AMT5 - Amount of previous payment in May, 2005 (NT dollar)
PAY_AMT6 - Amount of previous payment in April, 2005 (NT dollar)
default.payment.next.month - Default payment (1=yes, 0=no)",False,True
9750,the-happy-prince,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the yield of wild blueberries. Good luck!

## NOTE:

You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error),

![MAE](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visipedia/iwildcam_comp/master/assets/MAE.png)

where each `x_i` represents the predicted target, `y_i` represents the ground truth, and `n` is the number of rows in the test set.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `yield`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,yield
    15289,6025.194
    15290,1256.223
    15291,357.44
    etc.",,False,True
9751,blueberry-yield,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the yield of wild blueberries. Good luck!

## NOTE:

You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error),

![MAE](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visipedia/iwildcam_comp/master/assets/MAE.png)

where each `x_i` represents the predicted target, `y_i` represents the ground truth, and `n` is the number of rows in the test set.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `yield`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,yield
    15289,6025.194
    15290,1256.223
    15291,357.44
    etc.","# Dataset Description

**NOTE:** You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Wild blueberry Yield Prediction Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shashwatwork/wild-blueberry-yield-prediction-dataset). (Since this is Playground 3.14, it seems like we need a Blueberry Pie joke here?) Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `yield` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the `yield` given the other features
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9752,sas-viya-for-learners-challenge-2025,"# Overview

Welcome to the 2025 SAS Viya for Learners Challenge, a unique opportunity to collaborate, innovate, and solve real-world problems using data and analytics. Whether you're a beginner or an experienced data scientist, this event is your chance to explore the power of the SAS Viya platform and apply advanced techniques to meaningful challenges.

We hope you have fun!

DISCLAIMER: THIS COMPETITION COMMENCES ON MONDAY THE 14TH OF JULY 2025
----------------------
# Description


The **SAS Viya for Learners Challenge**, hosted online through **Kaggle InClass**, is geared towards students who are looking to get into and engage with machine learning problems. Students will walk through the data science process and learn how to build machine learning models on a simulated business problem, with a chance to prove their skills on the live leaderboard.

**SAS Viya for Learners** is a full suite of cloud-based software that supports the entire analytics life cycle ¨C from data, to discovery, to deployment ¨C and it also lets you code in **SAS**, **Python**, or **R**. **Viya Workbench** is a low-cost, cloud-based development environment that lets developers, data scientists, and modelers code in SAS, Python, or R.

Machine learning is about using a combination of programming for model building, as well as visual, interactive interfaces to explore data and test assumptions quickly.

## To Enter the Challenge:

* In order to participate in the challenge, you need to register in the link [SAS Viya for Learners 5 day challenge page](https://www.sas.com/en_au/events/viya-for-learners-challenge.html#register)
* Click on the [Rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/sas-viya-for-learners-challenge-2025/rules) tab and accept the competition rules in order to participate.

# Challenge Structure & Getting Started

##Challenge description
The student_performance dataset is a sample collected from a diverse group of students across various academic institutions. Each record in the data represents a student and captures key demographic, academic, and lifestyle attributes relevant to their daily routines and educational engagement.

The challenge involves analyzing these attributes to understand the factors influencing academic performance, particularly during final examinations. The goal is to model the final exam score of each student, which serves as a proxy for their academic achievement.

The purpose of this project is to train a predictive model that can estimate a student¡¯s final exam score based on a range of features, including study habits, sleep patterns, diet quality, mental health, and extracurricular participation. This model can help educators and institutions identify students who may be at risk of underperformance and provide them with targeted support and intervention.

## Getting Started and Entering

To start developing the ML models for this challenge on **SAS Viya for Learners**, all participants need access to the environment.

If you came here from the landing page, you should have received an email with credentials to access the environment. If you are signing up through Kaggle, please refer to the **SAS Viya For Learners Registration** section on the left for more details.

?? [SAS Skill Builder for Students](https://www.sas.com/en_au/learn/academic-programs/campaigns/skill-builder-for-students.html)

Once you have access to the environment, please refer to the [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/sas-viya-for-learners-challenge-2025/data) section to understand and walk through the dataset used in the challenge.

## Rules

Please refer to the **Evaluation** and **Submission Requirements** sections for more details.

# SAS Viya Resources

To get you started, refer to the [Notebooks](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/sas-viya-for-learners-challenge-2025/code) section of this Kaggle competition. There you¡¯ll find a python programming templates that walk through a full machine learning pipeline and build a simple model to help you get started.

To access SAS Viya for Learners and SAS Viya Workbench for Learners visit the [SAS Viya Skill Builder for Students](https://www.sas.com/en_au/learn/academic-programs/campaigns/skill-builder-for-students.html).

# Contact & Acknowledgements

* Please use the **Discussion** and **Notebooks** sections for discussion on the challenge.
* For questions, feel free to reach out to the SAS Academic team at **Academic@oz.sas.com**

Thanks for playing!

----------------------
# Evaluation

# Submission to Kaggle

## Submission Requirements

All submissions must include the following components to be eligible:

- Viya: **CSV file** named submission.csv upload to kaggle + **email model** card to academic@oz.sas.com
- Workbench: **CSV file** named submission.csv upload to kaggle + **email screenshot** of workbench environment with code present.


---

## Submission CSV Evaluation

All entries are evaluated based on a final submission CSV file that contains **two columns**:

* `id`: The unique identifier of each candidate in the test set.
* `score`: Your model¡¯s predicted final score for the corresponding id.



## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

id, score  
S1023, 87.5  
S1024, 72.0  
S1025, 66.3  

##SAS Model Code (SAS Viya submissions)

Along with your submission CSV, you must submit and provide accompanying model files so your submission can be replicated and you demonstrated that you used the SAS Viya procedures to build your model. The model score code, which is either a .sas file or a combination of .sas & ASTORE. These are scored representations of the model used to score new data. These files can be emailed to Academic@oz.sas.com with an email titled ""SAS Viya for Learners Submission ###"" where ### is your Kaggle user or your team-name.

##Workbench Screenshot (SAS Viya Workbench submissions)

Along with your submission CSV, you must take a screenshot of your workbench environment with a snippet of your code. A screenshot with all your code is preferable. This screenshot can be emailed to Academic@oz.sas.com with an email titled ""SAS Viya for Learners Submission ###"" where ### is your Kaggle user or your team-name.


----------------------
# Prizes

Participants get a chance to hone their skills and receive:

**1) SAS Badge for their resume that can also be displayed on LinkedIn. 
These are digital badges through Acclaim and are a way to display your skills with your LinkedIn network and prospective employers looking for SAS skills.**  
There will be two badges offered:

* A Participant's Badge for entering the challenge and making an eligible submission.  
![Participant's Badge](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11084107%2F895f5a6f413c05f67b9793034003435a%2Finbox_1570418_2e74bf1c45d23b8ede6da5f9c5998d28_ParticipantBadge.png?generation=1686109383610333&alt=media)

* Participants Badge

* A Winner's Badge for the final Top 3 participants on the Leaderboard at the end of the challenge.  
![Winner's Badge](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11084107%2Ff984513a267cd8dd66c52e93c8347db3%2Finbox_1570418_c14af378f7d501afe09cf97fda8550e6_WinnerBadge.png?generation=1686109402287353&alt=media)

* Winner's Badge  
An email will be sent to the Top 3 Winners on the Leaderboard from the SAS Academic Team (Academic@oz.sas.com) on Monday 21st of July 2025.

**2) 3-month voluntary internship for only the Top 3 eligible individual entries on the Leaderboard with SAS Institute Australia.  SAS Institute is a global leader in analytics, with an award-winning culture, which during this time you will:**

* Work alongside senior engineers to assess and troubleshoot issues currently being reported by customers.

* Recreate customer issues in test environments using our SAS software, hands on use of SAS environments.

* Understand process of raising issues internally through to achieving fixes and achieving acceptable outcomes for customers.

* Internship period is for 3 months in total and must be taken in from 12th of January 2026, either full time or part-time.

* The recipient must also take the following 2 certifications before the commencement:  
  https://www.sas.com/en_au/certification/credentials/foundation-tools/viya-programming-associate.html  
  https://www.sas.com/en_au/certification/credentials/foundation-tools/programming-fundamentals.html

* Upskill by learning base SAS which will set you up to gain your SAS Base Programming certification  
  https://www.sas.com/en_au/certification/credentials/foundation-tools/base-programming-specialist.html  
  which is to be completed at the completion of the internship

* The internship is a voluntary 3-month program with the primary focus of providing firsthand knowledge and exposure into the real working environment to help you develop your career in analytics.

# Prize Eligibility  
As stated in the Prizes section, you will be awarded:

**1) A Digital SAS Badge - The Top 3 individual entries on the Leaderboard will receive a Winners Badge for submitting an eligible entry (in accordance with the Timeline and Rules stated on the Kaggle competition page) and a Participants badge for all others.**

* To be eligible to receive the Winner's badge,?your Kaggle account must be registered with an Australian university student email.

**2) 3-month voluntary internship for only the Top 3 eligible individual entries on the Leaderboard with SAS Institute Australia**

* To ensure the SAS experience, the internship will be conducted in a hybrid format where applicable.  
* If any of the Top 3 individual entries are located in either Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane or Canberra, they are welcome into the SAS local office.  
* The internship will be conducted remotely from the convenience of your own home for those outside of these states.

----------------------
# Timeline


## Competition Timeline & Dates

- 9AM Monday 14th July, 2025: Challenge begins. The test dataset will be revealed on the Kaggle page and activated on the environment. Participants can then submit to the Leaderboard. Notebook and modelling templates will also be shared to help get participants started.
- 6PM Friday 18th July, 2025** - Competition ends and submission deadline. All deadlines will be AEST (UTC+10).
- Monday 21st July, 2025**- Email will be sent out announcing the winners

If there are any technical issues please use the SAS Chatbox or email sasviyaforlearners@sas.com.
