9763,google-gemma-3n-hackathon,"# Overview

Your mission is to leverage the unique capabilities of Gemma 3n to create a product that addresses a significant real-world challenge. Think bigger than a simple chatbot. How can a private, offline-first, multimodal model make a tangible difference in people's lives?

----------------------
# Description

Hello World! The future of AI is personal, private, and compact enough to run in the palm of your hand. With the launch of [Gemma 3n](https://www.kaggle.com/models/google/gemma-3n/), we are putting the next generation of on-device, multimodal AI into your hands. Now, we challenge you to use this groundbreaking technology to build products that create meaningful, positive change in the world.

This is your opportunity to tackle real-world problems in areas like accessibility, education, healthcare, environmental sustainability, and crisis response. With a total prize pool of $150,000, we're looking for projects that aren't just technically brilliant, but are truly built for impact.

Gemma 3n is Google's first open model built on a new, cutting-edge architecture designed for mobile-first AI. It allows for highly capable, real-time AI to operate directly on phones, tablets, and laptops, enabling experiences that are both personal and private.

##What is Gemma 3n?

Here¡¯s what makes Gemma 3n a game-changer for developers:
- **Optimized On-Device Performance**: Gemma 3n is engineered for speed and efficiency. Thanks to innovations like Per-Layer Embeddings (PLE), the 5B and 8B parameter models run with a memory footprint comparable to 2B and 4B models, making them perfect for resource-constrained devices.
- **Many-in-1 Flexibility**: A single 4B model natively includes a 2B submodel, allowing you to dynamically trade off performance and quality on the fly. You can even use the ""mix¡¯n¡¯match"" capability to create custom-sized submodels for your specific use case.
- **Privacy-First & Offline Ready**: By running locally, Gemma 3n enables applications that protect user privacy and function reliably, even without an internet connection¡ªa critical feature for accessibility and use in remote areas.
- **Expanded Multimodal Understanding**: Gemma 3n understands and processes interleaved audio, text, and images, with significantly enhanced video understanding. This unlocks powerful capabilities like real-time transcription, translation, and rich, voice-driven interactions.
- **Improved Multilingual Capabilities**: The model features strong performance across multiple languages, including Japanese, German, Korean, Spanish, and French, breaking down communication barriers.

##The Challenge: Your Mission to Build for Impact

Your mission is to leverage the unique capabilities of Gemma 3n to create a product that addresses a significant real-world challenge. Think bigger than a simple chatbot. How can a private, offline-first, multimodal model make a tangible difference in people's lives?

Consider products that:
- **Enhance Accessibility**: Build tools for real-time translation or transcription for the hearing-impaired, or visual description apps for the blind.
- **Revolutionize Education**: Create interactive, offline-ready learning experiences for students in low-connectivity regions.
- **Improve Health & Wellness**: Develop on-device apps that can provide mental health support through voice analysis or act as a personal wellness coach.
- **Promote Environmental Sustainability**: Design an app that uses image and audio recognition to identify local plant diseases, track biodiversity, or promote recycling.
- **Aid in Crisis Response**: Build tools that can operate offline to provide critical information or facilitate communication during natural disasters.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Your project will be judged primarily on your video demo. This is your chance to create something exciting, compelling, and with the potential to be seen by millions. We want to see the ""wow"" factor. Your video should tell a story, demonstrate the real-world impact of your product, and leave the judges inspired.

While the video is the star of the show, all projects must be backed by real, functional technology. The accompanying writeup and code repository will be used by our judges to verify that your product is not just a concept but a working proof-of-concept built on [Gemma 3n](https://www.kaggle.com/models/google/gemma-3n/).

## Submission Requirements

You will submit a Writeup to one of the hackathon¡¯s tracks which should minimally include:

1. **The Video Demo** (3 minutes or less): This is the most important part of your submission. Create a dynamic, engaging, and high-quality video that demonstrates your project in action.
 - Your Goal: Tell a story. Show us the problem and how your Gemma 3n app solves it in a powerful way.
 - Format: You can post your video to YouTube, X (Twitter), TikTok, or any other public platform. You will provide a direct link to the video. It must be viewable by the judges without requiring a login.
2. **The Technical Writeup** (The Proof of Work): This paper- or blog-style report is your technical verification. It must clearly explain the architecture of your app, how you specifically used Gemma 3n, the challenges you overcame, and why your technical choices were the right ones. Its primary purpose is to prove to the judges that your video demo is backed by real engineering.
3. **Public Code Repository** (The ""Source of Truth""): Provide a link to a public repository (e.g., GitHub, Kaggle Notebook). The code must be well-documented and clearly show the implementation of Gemma 3n. This is non-negotiable and will be used to validate the authenticity of your project.
4. **Public Project Link** (The Live Demo): A URL to your working product or interactive demo. This allows judges to experience your project firsthand, if applicable. It should be publicly accessible and not require a login or paywall.

## Judging Criteria
Your video demo will be the primary lens through which judges evaluate your project, with your writeup and code serving as verification and providing technical depth. Submissions will be scored based on the following criteria:
- **Impact & Vision (40 points)**: As demonstrated in your video, how clearly and compellingly does your project address a significant real-world problem? Is the vision inspiring and does the solution have a tangible potential for positive change?
- **Video Pitch & Storytelling (30 points)**: How exciting, engaging, and well-produced is the video? Does it tell a powerful story that captures the viewer's imagination? Does it clearly and effectively demonstrate the product in action, showcasing a great user experience? Does it have viral potential?
- **Technical Depth & Execution (30 points)**: As verified by the code repository and writeup, how innovative is the use of Gemma 3n's unique features (on-device performance, multimodality, mix'n'match, etc.)? Is the technology real, functional, well-engineered, and not just faked for the demo?

For the **Special Technology Prizes**, these same criteria will be applied with a focus on how that specific technology (LeRobot, Jetson, Ollama, Unsloth, Google AI Edge) was pivotal in achieving the project's impact and technical excellence.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **June 26, 2025** - Start Date. 
* **August 6, 2025** - Final Submission Deadline. 

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# judges

This is placeholder content. This will not be displayed, but exists to satisfy launch checklist conditions.
----------------------
# tracks-and-awards

This is placeholder content. This will not be displayed, but exists to satisfy launch checklist conditions.
----------------------
# Submission Instructions

Your submission must be a [Kaggle Writeup](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/google-gemma-3n-hackathon/projects) and it must be attached to this page. To create a new Writeup, click on the ""New Writeup"" button [here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/google-gemma-3n-hackathon/projects). After you have saved your Writeup, you should see a ""Submit"" button in the top right corner. 

Each team is limited to submitting only a single Writeup, but that same Writeup can be un-submitted, edited, and re-submitted as many times as you'd like. Your Writeup should contain a summary of your overall project along with links to supporting resources. 

**Your final Submission must be received prior to the deadline. Any un-submitted or draft Writeups by the competition deadline will not be considered by the Judges.**

Note: If you attach a private Kaggle Resource to your public Kaggle Writeup, your private Resource will automatically be made public after the deadline.",,True,False
9764,applications-of-deep-learning-wustl-summer-2025,"# Overview

# Kaggle Community Competition: Predicting Preservation Score Performance

## Overview

score of ancient treasure items. The preservation score reflects how well an artifact can be expected to endure over time, taking into account factors like material composition, craftsmanship, and environmental exposure. This is a regression problem where students must draw on both structured and unstructured data to estimate the item's preservability. Success will require not only understanding the physical and historical features of each artifact, but also effectively integrating multimodal inputs such as images and text descriptions.

Each record in the dataset describes a unique treasure item, including its physical dimensions (weight, height, width, depth), materials used (primary and secondary materials, gem type, gem count), and aesthetic or symbolic features (color, surface texture, inscription, pattern type). Historical and contextual details such as origin culture, creation year, restoration status, and authentication status provide additional depth. Categorical variables like item type, rarity level, and environment type may correlate with preservation potential, while flags such as cursed status or magic enchanted add a layer of fantasy-themed complexity. Students are also provided with a free-text description and a photograph of each artifact, enabling them to experiment with computer vision or natural language processing techniques to extract predictive signals from unstructured data.

### What is the Preservation Score?

The **preservation_score** is a single numeric value (0¨C100) that estimates how well an ancient treasure item is likely to withstand the test of time. This score reflects a combination of environmental exposure, material resilience, and current condition. Items found in protected environments like tombs or caves tend to score higher, while those from corrosive or exposed locations such as oceans or battlefields may score lower. Additional factors influencing the score include the artifact¡¯s primary material, level of oxidation, restoration efforts, craftsmanship quality, surface texture, and even size. While the exact scoring formula is not provided, students can assume it reflects a weighted combination of these attributes, with some random noise added to simulate real-world measurement variability.

Each treasure item in the dataset is described using a rich set of features. These include physical dimensions (weight, height, width, depth), material properties (e.g., gold, bronze, gem count), aesthetic qualities (surface texture, shine factor), and contextual data such as origin culture, creation year, and restoration status. Categorical fields like item_type, rarity_level, and magic_enchanted provide further nuance. To support multimodal modeling, each item also comes with a textual description and a photograph, enabling students to explore natural language processing and computer vision techniques alongside traditional tabular modeling.

## The Dataset

The competition dataset is rich and diverse, incorporating information from three distinct sources:

1. **Tabular Data:** A structured dataset containing detailed specifications for each item, including physical characteristics and other information.
2. **Item Images:** Each item is visually represented by an AI-generated image. However, please note that these images may not always reflect the most up-to-date specifications, as errors can occur.
3. **Descriptive Text:** Unstructured textual descriptions of each item, offering qualitative insights into its intended purpose and functionality. For example:

   *""Forged in the shadowy workshops of a legendary pirate stronghold, this rare bronze amulet whispers tales of maritime adventure and hidden treasures. Adorned with three emeralds and bearing intricate geometric patterns, the piece captures the raw elegance of maritime craftsmanship against the opulent backdrop of a forgotten palace.""*

## Challenge Details

Your task is to analyze the provided dataset and build a predictive model that can accurately estimate the **preservation_score** for new items. The competition will test your ability to handle structured, unstructured, and visual data sources to make well-informed predictions.

### Key Considerations:
- **Feature Engineering:** The dataset includes both numerical and categorical features that impact performance. Exploring interactions between them may improve your model.
- **Multi-Modal Learning:** While the main target variable is derived from tabular data, leveraging image and text descriptions might enhance prediction accuracy.
- **Generalization:** Your model should perform well on unseen items, meaning it must capture general trends rather than overfitting to existing examples.

## Get Started
Explore the dataset, experiment with different modeling approaches, and see how well you can predict the **preservation_score**. This is your opportunity to apply machine learning techniques to item scoring problem!

We look forward to seeing your innovative solutions¡ªgood luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using the root mean square ([RMSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_mean_square)) metric, which measures how closely the predicted preservation_score values align with the actual scores. A lower RMSE indicates more accurate predictions. While RMSE is the official evaluation metric, participants may also consider tracking the R2 score during development to assess how much variance in preservation can be explained by their models. To improve performance, participants are encouraged to experiment with feature engineering, multimodal fusion (text and image), and a variety of machine learning techniques.

## Submission File
For each id in the test set, you must predict a probability for the preservation_score variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
id,preservation_score
8001,74.59488588167487
8002,74.59488588167487
8003,74.59488588167487
8004,74.59488588167487
8005,74.59488588167487
8006,74.59488588167487
8007,74.59488588167487
8008,74.59488588167487
8009,74.59488588167487
8010,74.59488588167487
```",,False,True
9765,californiahousing,"# Overview

In this competition, participants will develop regression models to predict median house values using a highly engineered and preprocessed version of the California Housing dataset. This isn't the standard dataset you're used to ¡ª it¡¯s been extensively transformed through rigorous feature engineering. The result is what may be one of the most feature-dense housing datasets ever curated.

The goal is to accurately model the relationship between geographic, economic, and engineered signals and the median house value in California. This serves as both a machine learning challenge and an opportunity to explore the limits of tabular model performance under heavy preprocessing.

Participants are encouraged to explore ensemble models (e.g., XGBoost, CatBoost, LightGBM), neural networks, or even hybrid modeling strategies. Strong performance will hinge on navigating the high-dimensional feature space effectively, leveraging regularization, and understanding the engineered features.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between the predicted and actual median house values. RMSE is a commonly used metric for regression tasks, particularly when larger errors should be penalized more heavily.
---
 Why RMSE?
Sensitive to large errors: Makes it ideal for identifying models that underperform on outliers or high-variance regions.
Standardized in regression: Aligns with common practices across housing price prediction and economic forecasting tasks.
Lower RMSE indicates better model performance. Final rankings will be determined based on RMSE calculated on a hidden test set. Ties will be broken based on leaderboard submission time.


## Submission File
For each row in the test set, your submission must contain a predicted value for the median house value. The submission file should be a CSV with a header and the following structure:
ID,TARGET
0,179000.0
1,241300.5
2,126700.0
...

id: A unique identifier corresponding to each row in the test set.
TARGET: Your model's predicted median house value for that row (a continuous numeric value, not rounded or bucketed).

Make sure:
Your file includes no missing values
The column names are exactly id and TARGET (case-sensitive)",,False,True
9766,cram-cv-educational-round,"# Overview

# Description
This is a deep image hallucination classification challenge in which competitors train deep classification models on a data set containing images generated by deep generative models. Competitors are scored based on the classification accuracy on a given test set. For each test image, the participants have to predict its class label.
----------------------
# Description

# Evaluation
## Metric
The evaluation measure for this competition is the classification accuracy computed on the test set. The classification accuracy is given by the number of correctly predicted labels divided by the total number of test samples.

## Public versus Private Test Evaluation
The public leaderboard is calculated on approximately 50% of the test data. These examples are randomly chosen. The final results will be based on the other 50%, so the final standings may be different. In this context, you will have to choose 2 submissions that you think will attain the best performance on the 50% of the test data that is not used for the public leaderboard. 

## Submission Format
For every sample in the test set, submission files should contain two columns: Image and Class. The Image is the file name of an image sample. The Class should be the class label, 0 to 95, predicted for the corresponding data sample.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

Image,Class
16865.png,0
10070.png,0
...","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description
## Task
Participants have to train a model for deep hallucination classification. This is a multi-way classification task in which an image must be classified into one of the 96 classes.

The training data is composed of 12,000 image files. The validation set is composed of 1,000 image files. The test is composed of 5,000 image files.

## File Descriptions
train.csv - the training metadata file containing the training image file names and the corresponding labels (one example per row)
val.csv - the validation metadata file containing the validation image file names and the corresponding labels (one example per row)
test.csv - the test metadata file containing only the test image file names (one sample per row without label)
sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format
## Data Format
### Metadata Files
The metadata files are provided in the following format based on comma separated values:

Image,Class
12953.png,38
