9773,mave-db-amino-acid-substitution-prediction,"# Overview

In this competition we invite you to build a model that predicts the outcomes of Multiplexed Assays of Variant Effects (MAVEs). Given an amino acid substitution in a protein, you will predict the measurement of protein function that one would get if the protein were assayed in the lab.
----------------------
# Description

Understanding the functional consequences of genetic variants is a cornerstone of modern genomics. Multiplexed Assays of Variant Effect (MAVEs) provide high-throughput measurements of how thousands of variants impact gene function, and [MaveDB](https://www.mavedb.org/) is a growing repository of these datasets. However, predicting variant effects across different genes, assays, and experimental conditions remains a major challenge.

In this competition, your task is to develop machine learning models that can **predict the functional outcomes of genetic variants** based on MAVE data hosted in MaveDB.

## Why It Matters

Accurate prediction of variant effects has profound implications for:
- **Clinical genomics**: Interpreting variants of uncertain significance (VUS)
- **Protein engineering**: Designing proteins with desired properties
- **Basic biology**: Understanding structure-function relationships in proteins

This competition offers a unique opportunity to contribute to the development of generalizable models that can learn from diverse experimental data and help bridge the gap between genotype and phenotype.

## Background

[Proteins](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protein) are the molecular machines that living cells rely on for various functions, including structural support, signaling, and metabolism. They are made up of chains of [amino acid residues](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amino_acid), which makes it possible to represent a protein as a sequence of symbols, where each symbol represents an amino acid, for example:
```
MPLYSVTVKWGKEKFEGVELNTDEPPMVFKAQLFALTGVQP
```
What happens to the protein¡¯s function if one of the amino acid residues is substituted for another?
```
MPLYSVTVKWGKEKFEGVELNTDEPPMVFKAQLFALTGVQP
         ¡ý
MPLYSVTVKDGKEKFEGVELNTDEPPMVFKAQLFALTGVQP
```
Here, the reference amino acid, Tryptophan (W) at position 10 is substituted with an alternate amino acid Aspartic Acid (D). Does this change the function of the protein? And if so, how?

The MAVEs from MaveDB that we use in this challenge are experiments in which many such substitutions are tested, and their effects are measured in various ways. The measurements can be something as general as cell growth, to something as specific as the rate of transport of a particular molecule. Ultimately, each substitution in each MAVE gets a single numerical score representing the functional change, but note that the same number can mean different things in different experimental studies!

## What you'll do

The model you develop will need to be able to predict the MAVE numerical score of a substitution given the reference sequence, position of the amino acid substituted, and the new alternate amino acid. You are encouraged to use any additional external information you find helpful for your model.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation section describes how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.

---

Submissions are evaluated on [mean squared error loss](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error) between the predicted values and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `accession` identifier in the test set, you must predict a probability for the `score` variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    accession,score
    urn:mavedb:00000069-a-2#1,-0.7004083010102047
    urn:mavedb:00000069-a-2#2,-0.8618222710913744
    urn:mavedb:00000069-a-2#3,-0.9651334791478582
    urn:mavedb:00000069-a-2#4,-0.7652028740216816
    urn:mavedb:00000069-a-2#5,-0.781604387066486
    etc.

See `sample_submission.csv` for an example in the available data files.",,False,True
