9802,proposal-for-connecting-ai-to-well-being,"# Overview

We are looking for applications or solutions  that connect machine learning and AI to well-being.

For example, you could use open data and machine learning to identify what makes you feel happy, or create an app that can refresh you when you're feeling down. Any idea is welcome.

**Prize**: 100,000 yen (Japanese yen)
*For overseas transfers, a bank transfer fee will be deducted.



**You can also apply via the link below.**
https://forms.gle/qqJjmrnEq4jfM5F8A

**Information in Japanese**
https://sites.google.com/view/20250803/%E3%83%9B%E3%83%BC%E3%83%A0/ai-grand-prix


This is the home page of the competition. In this section, you should aim to succinctly introduce the competition.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Judges

Takaya Endo
Former Chief Engineer at NTT Advanced Technology Corporation. Representative of ABC Center at M-SAKU Networks. BMX (Business Model ¡Á AI) strategy engineer. Judge for ""AI Prompt Grand Prix"" ('23, '24). Technical Advisor at AI Innovation Hub at Ignition Point Inc.

Hiroko Kano
Associate Professor at Yamagata University. Researches various issues related to the information society, such as cybercrime and cyberbullying, and the impact of AI on education and society. Recent publications include ""Information Education in the AI ??Era,"" ""The Disaster Legacy of the New Coronavirus on Human Society,"" and ""UNESCO Handbook for Dealing with Fake News."" AI-related papers include research on the development of AI counselors and misconceptions about AI.

Masayoshi Yasumoto
Lecturer at Kinki University's School of Social Sciences, PhD (Engineering). Engaged in research on environmental analysis using satellite data and class management using ICT and generative AI. He has received the Japan Society of Remote Sensing Paper Encouragement Award (2002) and the 1st AI Prompt Grand Prix Corporate Organization Award (2023). His publications include ""First Step: Starting Data Science from the Basics"" (2022) and ""AI Utilization Skills for Problem Solving"" (2024).


Keirah Comstock
Dr. Keirah Comstock is an educational technologist at the Simon School of Business, University of Rochester. Her research focuses on creating and fair use of custom AI models in education, and teaching AI guidelines. Her major works include How K-12 Teachers Make Sense of Differentiating Instruction and Technology Integration Through a Unique Tech-PD Approach and Innovating Education: Creating Custom ChatGPT Solutions for Enhanced Teaching and Learning Experiences.

---

