9804,draft-like-a-pro-predict-nba-player-success,"# Overview

# Welcome to the NBA Draft Machine Learning Challenge!
In this competition, you'll step into the shoes of a professional basketball scout and use historical NBA draft data to predict how successful newly drafted players will be.

From raw stats to advanced analytics, this dataset contains rich career performance data for NBA players drafted between 1989 and 2021. Your job is to predict the long-term value of recent draft picks ¡ª before their careers have fully unfolded.

## Goal:
Use historical data available at the time of the draft (e.g. draft pick, college, pre-draft stats) to predict the Value Over Replacement Player (VORP) ¡ª a metric that captures a player's overall career contribution.

You¡¯ll train your models on past drafts and try to accurately estimate the VORP of more recent draftees (2016¨C2021), whose careers are still in progress.
----------------------
# Description

Predicting success in professional sports is hard ¡ª especially during the draft. NBA franchises invest millions in scouting players, yet even lottery picks can become busts, while others rise unexpectedly to stardom.

In this competition, you'll work with a dataset covering over 30 years of NBA Draft history, using player information and career statistics to model long-term performance. The goal is to predict a player¡¯s Value Over Replacement Player (VORP) ¡ª an advanced metric that summarizes their contribution throughout their career.

The dataset has been carefully compiled to include:

Draft position and scouting rank

College or international team

Career statistics such as games played, points per game, assists, rebounds, and more

Advanced performance metrics like Win Shares, Box Plus Minus, and VORP

**Why This Matters**
From a data science perspective, this challenge combines:

Real-world prediction under uncertainty

Regression modeling with mixed data types

Feature engineering around career trajectories

Applications in sports analytics and talent evaluation

From a basketball perspective: it's your chance to prove you could have out-drafted actual NBA GMs.

Good luck ¡ª and may your models find the next MVP hiding in plain sight.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on the R**oot Mean Squared Error (RMSE)** between the predicted value_over_replacement and the actual observed values.

The RMSE is calculated as:

????????=1??¡Æ??=1??(??^???????)2

Where:

??^?? = is your prediction for the i-th player

???? = is the actual value over replacement

?? = is the total number of players in the test set

Lower RMSE values indicate better performance.

## Submission File
For each id in the test set, you must predict a numeric value for the value_over_replacement. The submission file should be a **CSV** with a header and the following format:
  
id,value_over_replacement
1563,3.2
1564,0.0
1565,7.8
...

## Final Scoring
The public leaderboard is based on a random subset of the test set.

The private leaderboard (used for final ranking) is based on the remaining hidden portion.

You may select up to 3 final submissions before the deadline.

Submissions beyond the daily limit of 5 will not be accepted.","# Dataset Description

# Files:
**train_NBA.csv** ¡ª Historical NBA draft data from 1989 to 2015, including player stats and the target variable: value_over_replacement.

**test_NBA.csv** ¡ª Draft data from 2016 to 2021. The target variable is hidden; your model must predict it.

**submission_NBA.csv** ¡ª Shows the correct submission format. Contains id and placeholder values for value_over_replacement.

## What You¡¯re Predicting:
Your task is to predict the value_over_replacement (VORP) for players in the test set ¡ª a numerical value that reflects a player¡¯s total career contribution relative to a replacement-level player.

## Columns
Below is a description of the most important columns in the dataset:

Column Name,	Description
id:	Unique identifier for each player (used in submission)
year:	Year the player was drafted
rank:	Prospect scouting rank prior to the draft
overall_pick:	Overall draft position (e.g., 1st pick, 2nd pick, etc.)
team:	NBA team that drafted the player
player:	Player's full name
college:	College or international team the player came from
years_active:	Number of seasons played in the NBA
games:	Total games played in career
minutes_played:	Total career minutes played
points:	Total points scored
total_rebounds:	Total rebounds recorded
assists:	Total assists made
field_goal_percentage:	FG% (career shooting accuracy from the field)
3_point_percentage:	3P% (career shooting accuracy from 3-point range)
free_throw_percentage:	FT% (career shooting accuracy from the free-throw line)
average_minutes_played:	Minutes played per game over career
points_per_game:	Average points per game
average_total_rebounds:	Average rebounds per game
average_assists:	Average assists per game
win_shares:	Total career win shares (advanced metric)
win_shares_per_48_minutes:	Win shares normalized per 48 minutes played
box_plus_minus:	Advanced stat estimating performance vs. league average
value_over_replacement:	**Target variable** ¡ª estimated value above a replacement-level player

## Notes:
Some players may have short careers, impacting totals.

Shooting percentages are expressed as decimals (e.g., 0.455 for 45.5%).

Not all players played in the NBA (e.g., international draftees who didn¡¯t sign).",False,True
