9810,schwifty-nlp-kaggle-competition,"# Overview

Ready to get schwifty with NLP? In this bonus challenge of CSE 676, you'll dive into interdimensional text analysis of Rick and Morty to identify which character said what in the series.

----------------------
# Description

In this competition, you¡¯ll:

Train your model on the Rick and Morty transcript data to learn each Smith family member's unique speech patterns and vocabulary quirks.

Use your model to identify which character said each line in the test set ¡ª can your model tell a classic Rick rant from Morty's anxious stammering?

Submit your predictions as a CSV file matching the specified format ¡ª nothing fancy, just line_id and speaker pairs.

Want to learn more about Kaggle competitions and submission guidelines? Check out Kaggle's competition documentation.
----------------------
# Evaluation

We'll score based on accuracy score ¡ª the more speakers you get right, the higher you climb on the leaderboard.

Please note that the Leaderboard you see during the competition does not represent your final ranking. Kaggle has two leaderboards ¡ª the public and private leaderboard. The solution is split into two parts, using one part for the public leaderboard and another part for the private leaderboard. You generally will now know which samples are public vs private. The private leaderboard is kept a secret until after the competition deadline and is used as the official leaderboard for determining the final ranking.","# Dataset Description

You'll be working with a transcript dataset containing dialogue from the show, formatted like this:

line_id,episode_id,speaker,dialogue
1,1,Rick:,"" Alright, Morty. You ready for our adventure to the lost city of Atlantis?""
2,1,Morty:,"" *gives a thumbs-up* Ready as I'll ever be, Rick!""
Files
train.csv - Training dataset containing labeled dialogue lines with known speakers
test.csv - Test dataset containing dialogue lines without speaker labels
sample_submission.csv - Example submission file in the correct format with random placeholder labels
Columns
line_id - Unique identifier for each line of dialogue
episode_id - Identifier for the episode the dialogue appears in
speaker - Character who spoke the line (only in train.csv)
dialogue - The actual text spoken by the character
Your Goal
Your task is to classify each line in the test set as being spoken by one member of the main family or others:

Rick, Morty, Summer, Beth, Jerry, Others
Submission File
Your submission should match the format of the sample submission file, with predictions for each line_id in the test set.

For each line_id in the test set, you must identify a character for the speaker column. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

line_id,speaker
6725,Morty
6726,Rick
6727,Others
Interdimensional Pro Tip
The multiverse is complicated, so to make things easier for you: only the main Smith family from C-137 (or whatever our ""main"" reality is) counts as themselves. That means all those alternate reality versions like Evil Morty, Decoy Jerry are classified as Others ¡ª though they might look the same.

And before you ask ¡ª yes, Home Beth in the transcript is simply classified as Beth and Space Beth goes into the Others category, despite we'll never know whether she is Beth's clone or the original Beth that this Beth was cloned from. Just don't overthink it!",False,True
9811,heart-failure-prediction-comp-3220-summer-2025,"# Description

Welcome to the heart failure prediction competition for the COMP3220 course. Your job is to use medical data to predict if someone has heart failure. 

## Context
Cardiovascular diseases (CVDs) are the number 1 cause of death globally, taking an estimated 17.9 million lives each year, which accounts for 31% of all deaths worldwide. Four out of 5CVD deaths are due to heart attacks and strokes, and one-third of these deaths occur prematurely in people under 70 years of age. Heart failure is a common event caused by CVDs and this dataset contains 11 features that can be used to predict a possible heart disease.

People with cardiovascular disease or who are at high cardiovascular risk (due to the presence of one or more risk factors such as hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidaemia or already established disease) need early detection and management wherein a machine learning model can be of great help. 

## Objective

Use your data science skills to create a model that can predict whether someone has heart disease. Submit your submission file and compete to find the most accurate model.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Scoring

Your submission will be score based on accuracy.

## Submission Format

Your submission file should be formatted as shown below. You have been given a sample submission file, make sure your submission file is formatted the same.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
PatientId,HeartDisease
810,1
```","# Dataset Description


## Source
This dataset was created by combining different datasets already available independently but not combined before. In this dataset, 5 heart datasets are combined over 11 common features which makes it the largest heart disease dataset available so far for research purposes. The five datasets used for its curation are:

Cleveland: 303 observations
Hungarian: 294 observations
Switzerland: 123 observations
Long Beach VA: 200 observations
Stalog (Heart) Data Set: 270 observations

## Files

*   **heart_train.csv** - the training set
*   **heart_test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

* Age: age of the patient [years]
* Sex: sex of the patient [M: Male, F: Female]
* ChestPainType: chest pain type [TA: Typical Angina, ATA: Atypical Angina, NAP: Non-Anginal Pain, ASY: Asymptomatic]
* RestingBP: resting blood pressure [mm Hg]
* Cholesterol: serum cholesterol [mm/dl]
* FastingBS: fasting blood sugar [1: if FastingBS > 120 mg/dl, 0: otherwise]
* RestingECG: resting electrocardiogram results [Normal: Normal, ST: having ST-T wave abnormality (T wave inversions and/or ST elevation or depression of > 0.05 mV), LVH: showing probable or definite left ventricular hypertrophy by Estes' criteria]
* MaxHR: maximum heart rate achieved [Numeric value between 60 and 202]
* ExerciseAngina: exercise-induced angina [Y: Yes, N: No]
* Oldpeak: oldpeak = ST [Numeric value measured in depression]
* ST_Slope: the slope of the peak exercise ST segment [Up: upsloping, Flat: flat, Down: downsloping]
* HeartDisease: output class [1: heart disease, 0: Normal]",False,True
