9829,brain-to-text-25,"# Overview

People with ALS or brainstem stroke can lose the ability to move and speak. Speech brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) can restore communication by decoding what someone is trying to say directly from their brain activity. Once decoded, the person¡¯s intended message can be spoken for them or typed as text on a computer. Following up on prior speech decoding work, we [recently](https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2314132) demonstrated that a ¡°brain-to-text¡± speech BCI can decode the attempted speech of a man with ALS and severe dysarthria into words with 97.5% accuracy.

The goal of this competition is to foster the development of new and improved algorithms for decoding speech from brain activity. Improved accuracies will make it more likely that a speech BCI can be clinically translated, improving the lives of those with vocal tract paralysis. We hope that this competition can also serve as an indicator of progress in the field and provide a standardized way to compare algorithm performance.

In the prior [Brain-to-Text '24 challenge](https://eval.ai/web/challenges/challenge-page/2099/evaluation), competitors successfully developed improved speech decoding algorithms, reducing the word error rate from 11.06% to as low as 5.77%. Here, we present a similar challenge but with a new dataset from a different research participant collected by the [UC Davis Neuroprosthetics Lab](https://neuroprosthetics.science/).
----------------------
# Description

## Computational problem

The decoding problem consists of mapping a variable-length time series of neural activity to text with no ground-truth alignment between the two. We provide a [baseline algorithm](https://github.com/Neuroprosthetics-Lab/nejm-brain-to-text) that first decodes neural activity into phoneme probabilities and then uses an n-gram language model plus LLM rescoring to decode the phoneme probabilities into text (see the diagram below). Our baseline algorithm achieves a 6.70% word error rate on the held-out *test* set. We may provide additional example approaches as the competition progresses, based on our own ideas as well as the lessons learned from the [Brain-to-Text '24 challenge](https://eval.ai/web/challenges/challenge-page/2099).

![Speech neuroprosthesis overview](https://github.com/Neuroprosthetics-Lab/nejm-brain-to-text/raw/main/b2txt_methods_overview.png)

Some potential avenues for improvement beyond the baseline algorithm include:
- Data augmentation (e.g., temporal masking)
- Model architecture (e.g., transformer, end-to-end)
- Different loss function (e.g., transducer)
- Using different language models for sentences from [different corpora](https://github.com/Neuroprosthetics-Lab/nejm-brain-to-text/blob/main/data/t15_copyTaskData_description.csv) (e.g., perhaps a 1-gram language model would do better than a 5gram model on the ""random word"" sentences)
     - Keep in mind that, with the goal of generalization in mind, you ***cannot*** tell your model what corpus to use at test-time. If your model can automatically detect which corpus to use, that's fine. The intention is for models to generalize to all types of speech during real-world BCI usage. Submissions that violate this rule are not eligible for prize money.
- Different language modeling approach (e.g., neural LM instead of ngram)
- Model ensembling
- Test time augmentation
- Test time adaptation (i.e., continuous finetuning)
- Different tokenization strategies (e.g., syllables or BPE instead of phonemes)
- Transfer learning from other datasets (e.g., ASR datasets or the [Brain-to-Text '24](https://eval.ai/web/challenges/challenge-page/2099) T12 neural dataset)

## Dataset

The dataset used in this competition consists of 10,948 sentences spoken by a single research participant as described in [Card et al. ¡°An Accurate and Rapidly Calibrating Speech Neuroprosthesis¡± (2024) *New England Journal of Medicine*](https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2314132). For each sentence, we provide the transcript of what the participant was attempting to say, along with the corresponding time series of neural spiking activity recorded from 256 microelectrodes in speech motor cortex. The dataset contains predefined ¡°*train*¡±, ¡°*val*¡±, and ¡°*test*¡± partitions. The train and val partitions include the sentence labels, and you may repartition them if desired. The ¡°*test*¡± partition does not include sentence labels and will be used for competition evaluation. The dataset can be downloaded either from this Kaggle competition page (see `Data` tab) or from [Dryad](https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.dncjsxm85).

The dataset contains a mixture of speaking strategies and sentence corpuses (see table, below) on a block-by-block basis. A description of which blocks correspond to which corpuses and data splits can be found [here](https://github.com/Neuroprosthetics-Lab/nejm-brain-to-text/blob/main/data/t15_copyTaskData_description.csv). To make the competition more challenging and better approximate real-world use, we do not provide labels as to the speaking strategy for each block.

There are some notable differences between this dataset and the one used in the Brain-to-Text 2024 challenge:
| <img width=300/> | Brain-to-Text '24 | Brain-to-Text '25 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Participant** | 'T12' | 'T15' |
| **Neural recordings** | 128 intracortical electrodes in speech motor cortex, 128 intracortical electrodes in inferior frontal gyrus | 256 intracortical recording electrodes in speech motor cortex |
| **Dataset period** | 25 sessions spanning 4 months | 45 sessions spanning 20 months |
| **Number of Sentences** | 12,100 | 10,948 |
| **Sentence corpus** | Switchboard | 50-word vocabulary, Switchboard, Openwebtext2, Harvard sentences, custom high-frequency word sentences, random word sentences |
| **Speaking strategy** | Attempted vocalized | Attempted vocalized or attempted silent |
| **Speaking rate** | ~62 words per minute | ~30 words per minute (attempted vocalized) or ~50 words per minute (attempted silent) |

## Competition Timeline

Competitors may submit their submission `.csv` files until the competition ends on December 31st, 2025 at 11:59 pm (Pacific Standard Time). After the competition ends, entrants who would like to be considered for the cash prize will have until January 31st, 2026, to submit their one-page summary and code. Details on how to submit this information will be provided at a later date. Winners will be chosen and then announced in February 2026.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The aggregate [word error rate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_error_rate) metric will be used to evaluate decoding performance across the 1,450 held-out test sentences contained in this dataset (specifically, the `data_test.hdf5` files for each session). Word error rate is the ""edit distance"" between the decoded sentence and the actual sentence, computed over words (i.e., the number of substitutions, insertions and deletions of words needed to make the decoded sentence match the true sentence). The aggregate word error rate across all sentences is equal to the summed edit distance over all sentences, divided by the total number of words across all true transcriptions. ""Words"" are sequences of characters separated by spaces. To be counted as correct, all characters in the decoded word must match the true word (including apostrophes for contractions like ""don't""). Punctuation marks (such as periods, question marks and commas) are not evaluated and should not be included in the decoded transcripts. 

The test set consists of 1,450 sentences. Not that all sessions do not include *val* or *test* partitions. 1/3 of the *test* sentences will be used for the public leaderboard and the remaining 2/3rds will be used for the private leaderboard. The public leaderboard will be visible throughout the competition, but the private leaderboard will only be revealed after the competition ends on December 31, 2025. Prizes will be based on the private leaderboard.

Your submission file should be a `.csv` file with an `id` and a `text` column. Sentences should appear in chronological order (by session date, block, and trial number) from the *test* partition, with `id` incrementing from 0 to 1449. For example, your `.csv` submission file should first have the held out *test* trials from session `t15.2023.08.13`, followed by `t15.2023.08.18`, then `t15.2023.08.20`, and so on.

See our [example evaluation script](https://github.com/Neuroprosthetics-Lab/nejm-brain-to-text/blob/main/model_training/evaluate_model.py) for how to load data, do inference with pretrained models, and create a correctly-formatted submission file. See our [example baseline submission file](https://github.com/Neuroprosthetics-Lab/nejm-brain-to-text/blob/main/model_training/rnn_baseline_submission_file_valsplit.csv) for what your decoded transcripts should look like (note that the linked example submission file corresponds to the *val* partition, not the *test* partition).

Please feel free to discuss decoding strategies and questions on the *Discussion* tab of this Kaggle competition.
----------------------
# Prizes

This challenge has a cash prize associated with it, generously provided by [Blackrock Neurotech](https://blackrockneurotech.com/):
- **1st Place**?-?$5,000
- **2nd Place** - $3,000
- **Most Innovative Approach** - $1,000

To be considered eligible for cash prizes, competitors must submit a one-page summary of their approach as well as the code required to reproduce it (see competition timeline, below). The prize will be fully administered and paid out by Blackrock Neurotech, subject to applicable laws, rules, and conditions. We (the academic organizers of this competition) are not directly involved in the prize but will provide Blackrock with the names and contact information of the winners.
----------------------
# Competition Organizers

This competition was organized by [Nicholas Card](https://x.com/NS_Card) and the [UC Davis Neuroprosthetics Lab](https://neuroprosthetics.science/), which is part of the [BrainGate consortium](https://www.braingate.org/).

For questions or comments, please reach out via the discussion board here, or via email, Twitter/X, or LinkedIn:
- Email: nscard@health.ucdavis.edu
- Twitter/X: https://x.com/NS_Card
- LinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/in/nicholas-card/
----------------------
# Terms and Conditions

The [data](https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.dncjsxm85) is licensed under a CC0 1.0 Universal (CC0 1.0) Public Domain Dedication license. Please cite [Card et al. ¡°An Accurate and Rapidly Calibrating Speech Neuroprosthesis¡± (2024) *New England Journal of Medicine*](https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2314132) in any work that uses the data.","# Dataset Description

## Overview
The [dataset](https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.dncjsxm85) used in this competition consists of 10,948 sentences spoken by a single research participant as described in [Card et al. ¡°An Accurate and Rapidly Calibrating Speech Neuroprosthesis¡± (2024) *New England Journal of Medicine*](https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2314132). For each sentence, we provide the transcript of what the participant was attempting to say, along with the corresponding time series of neural spiking activity recorded from 256 microelectrodes in speech motor cortex. The dataset contains predefined *train*, *val*, and *test* partitions. The train and val partitions include the sentence labels, and you may repartition them if desired. The *test* partition does not include sentence labels and will be used for competition evaluation. Your goal will be to train a model to predict spoken words from neural data using the *train* and *val* data splits, and then use that model to predict the words being spoken during each *test* trial.

The dataset contains a mixture of speaking strategies and sentence corpuses (see table, below) on a block-by-block basis. A description of which blocks correspond to which corpuses and data splits can be found [here](https://github.com/Neuroprosthetics-Lab/nejm-brain-to-text/blob/main/data/t15_copyTaskData_description.csv). To make the competition more challenging and better approximate real-world use, we do not provide labels as to the speaking strategy for each block.

## Differences from Brain-to-Text '24
There are some notable differences between this dataset and the one used in the Brain-to-Text 2024 challenge:
| <img width=300/> | Brain-to-Text '24 | Brain-to-Text '25 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Participant** | 'T12' | 'T15' |
| **Neural recordings** | 128 intracortical electrodes in speech motor cortex, 128 intracortical electrodes in inferior frontal gyrus | 256 intracortical recording electrodes in speech motor cortex |
| **Dataset period** | 25 sessions spanning 4 months | 45 sessions spanning 20 months |
| **Number of Sentences** | 12,100 | 10,948 |
| **Sentence corpus** | Switchboard | 50-word vocabulary, Switchboard, Openwebtext2, Harvard sentences, custom high-frequency word sentences, random word sentences |
| **Speaking strategy** | Attempted vocalized | Attempted vocalized or attempted silent |
| **Speaking rate** | ~62 words per minute | ~30 words per minute (attempted vocalized) or ~50 words per minute (attempted silent) |

## Data format
The dataset can be downloaded either from this Kaggle competition page (see below) or from [Dryad](https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.dncjsxm85). There are 10,948 sentences from 45 sessions spanning 20 months. Data is stored in `.hdf5` files. **An example of how to load this data using the Python `h5py` library is provided on our GitHub repository, [here](https://github.com/Neuroprosthetics-Lab/nejm-brain-to-text/blob/main/model_training/evaluate_model_helpers.py#L29)**.

Each trial of data includes: 
- The session date, block number, and trial number
- 512 neural features (2 features [-4.5 RMS threshold crossings and spike band power] per electrode, 256 electrodes), binned at 20 ms resolution. The data were recorded from the speech motor cortex via four high-density microelectrode arrays (64 electrodes each). The 512 features are ordered as follows in all data files: 
    - 0-64: ventral 6v threshold crossings
    - 65-128: area 4 threshold crossings
    - 129-192: 55b threshold crossings
    - 193-256: dorsal 6v threshold crossings
    - 257-320: ventral 6v spike band power
    - 321-384: area 4 spike band power
    - 385-448: 55b spike band power
    - 449-512: dorsal 6v spike band power
- The ground truth sentence label (for train and val splits)
- The ground truth phoneme sequence label (for train and val splits)

## Loading the data in Python

You can load the data using the `h5py` Python library. See example code below, and also on our GitHub repository, [here](https://github.com/Neuroprosthetics-Lab/nejm-brain-to-text/blob/main/model_training/evaluate_model_helpers.py#L29).

```python
import h5py

def load_h5py_file(file_path):
    data = {
        'neural_features': [],
        'n_time_steps': [],
        'seq_class_ids': [],
        'seq_len': [],
        'transcriptions': [],
        'sentence_label': [],
        'session': [],
        'block_num': [],
        'trial_num': [],
    }
    # Open the hdf5 file for that day
    with h5py.File(file_path, 'r') as f:

        keys = list(f.keys())

        # For each trial in the selected trials in that day
        for key in keys:
            g = f[key]

            neural_features = g['input_features'][:]
            n_time_steps = g.attrs['n_time_steps']
            seq_class_ids = g['seq_class_ids'][:] if 'seq_class_ids' in g else None
            seq_len = g.attrs['seq_len'] if 'seq_len' in g.attrs else None
            transcription = g['transcription'][:] if 'transcription' in g else None
            sentence_label = g.attrs['sentence_label'][:] if 'sentence_label' in g.attrs else None
            session = g.attrs['session']
            block_num = g.attrs['block_num']
            trial_num = g.attrs['trial_num']

            data['neural_features'].append(neural_features)
            data['n_time_steps'].append(n_time_steps)
            data['seq_class_ids'].append(seq_class_ids)
            data['seq_len'].append(seq_len)
            data['transcriptions'].append(transcription)
            data['sentence_label'].append(sentence_label)
            data['session'].append(session)
            data['block_num'].append(block_num)
            data['trial_num'].append(trial_num)
    return data
```

## Data fields
- `neural_features`: Temporally binned (20 ms) neural features for each trial (512 X T).
- `n_time_steps`: Number of time steps per trial.
- `seq_class_ids`: Integer phoneme sequence labels for each trial. Integers correspond to phonemes using the following mapping:
```python
LOGIT_TO_PHONEME = [
'BLANK',    # ""BLANK"" = CTC blank symbol
'AA', 'AE', 'AH', 'AO', 'AW',
'AY', 'B', 'CH', 'D', 'DH',
'EH', 'ER', 'EY', 'F', 'G',
'HH', 'IH', 'IY', 'JH', 'K',
'L', 'M', 'N', 'NG', 'OW',
'OY', 'P', 'R', 'S', 'SH',
'T', 'TH', 'UH', 'UW', 'V',
'W', 'Y', 'Z', 'ZH',
' | ',    # ""|"" = silence token
]
```
- `seq_len`: Number of phoneme labels per trial.
- `transcriptions`: ASCII representation of sentence label for each trial.
- `sentence_label`: Raw text sentence label for each trial.
- `session`: Date that the trial's data was collected. Each date has a number of blocks, each block has a number of trials.
- `block_num`: Research block number that the trial is sourced from.
- `trial_num`: Trial number that the trial is sourced from.

## More examples
You can refer to our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/Neuroprosthetics-Lab/nejm-brain-to-text) for thorough examples on how to download and unzip the data, where to put it, and how to train and evaluate a baseline RNN model with it.",False,True
9830,coral-diversity-at-reef-sites,"# Overview

Coral reefs host a diverse mix of species, shaped by environmental conditions and geography. Knowing which coral species are found at different sites can reveal patterns in reef structure, change over time, and responses to local conditions.

**Coral Diversity at Reef Sites** is a machine learning competition focused on predicting which coral species are present at different reef locations. Participants are given site-level environmental and spatial data, along with species presence/absence labels for training.

The goal is to build models that can perform multi-label classification to predict species composition at 1600 test sites.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Hamming Loss](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.hamming_loss.html) - the fraction of the wrong labels to the total number of labels. A lower score is better.

$$
\text{HammingLoss} = \frac{1}{N \cdot L} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \sum_{j=1}^{L} \mathbb{1}\left[ y_{ij} \ne \hat{y}_{ij} \right]
$$



## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a binary value for each of the coral species classes (0=absent, 1=present).

 ```
id,species_Porites,species_Fungia,species_Favia...
7789,1,1,0...
6069,1,1,0...
```",,False,True
9831,street-food-image-classification,"# Overview

**Overview**

Dive into the Multiclass Food Image Dataset, a curated collection of high-quality photographs representing everyday dishes from six popular food categories. Created to support computer-vision research in nutrition analysis, smart-kitchen applications, and automatic menu tagging, this dataset offers a practical sandbox for training and benchmarking image-classification models.

**Context**

Accurate food recognition is essential for calorie-tracking apps, automated checkout systems, and dietary studies. By providing labeled examples of Burgers, Pizzas, Salads, Sushi, Desserts, and Soups, the dataset helps machine-learning practitioners explore challenges such as class imbalance, intra-class variation (different plating styles), and domain adaptation (restaurant vs. homemade photos). Whether you are testing data-augmentation pipelines or comparing CNN architectures, this resource lets you focus on the core problem¡ªteaching models to see what¡¯s on the plate.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train_images/** - training images grouped into 10 class-named sub-folders
*   **test_images/** - unlabeled test images in one flat folder with anonymous file names
*   **train.csv** - has two columns, containing relative path inside *train_images/* and the class name that image belongs to
*   **test.csv** - each file in *test_images/*
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
