# Dataset Description

## ?? Files Provided

```plaintext
/Starter_Dataset
©À©¤©¤ /testImages/                        # Real-world test images (you'll be judged on)
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ /images/                       
©À©¤©¤ /Dataset/                           # Synthetic data to start training, create more on FalconCloud
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ /train/
©¦   ©¦   ©À©¤©¤ /images/  
©¦   ©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ /labels/
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ /val/                               #Synthetic and real-world data for validation, create more synthetic on FalconCloud
©¦       ©À©¤©¤ /images/
©¦       ©¸©¤©¤ /labels/

©À©¤©¤ convert_preds_to_csv.py            # Script to convert YOLO predictions to Kaggle CSV
©À©¤©¤ predict.py                         # YOLOv8 inference script
©À©¤©¤ yolo_params.yaml                   # Configuration file for training/inference
©À©¤©¤ Kaggle_3LC.ipynb           # Notebook that connects your data to the 3LC analysis software
```

### ?? Key Files:
- **Starter_Dataset.zip**: Includes all synthetic training/validation data folders and the real-world test set.
- Dataset contains:
  - `/train/images` and `/train/labels` ¨C for training
  - `/val/images` and `/val/labels` ¨C for validation # includes real-world data ONLY for validation, not training
- `/TestImages/images`: Contains real-world test images used for scoring.
- **classes.txt**: Defines class names (`cheerios`, `soup`).
- **yolo_params.yaml**: Used by training and prediction scripts to set dataset paths and options.
- **convert_preds_to_csv.py**: Converts YOLO prediction `.txt` files into Kaggle submission format.
- **predict.py**: Creates the predictions for scoring
-**Kaggle_3LC.ipynb**: A notebook that will connect your data to the 3LC software

---

## ?? Data Format

### 1. **Images**  
- Format: `.jpg` or `.png`  
- Resolution: All synthetic images are `1920x1080px` , some portrait and some landscape
- Located in `/images/` subfolder

### 2. **Labels (YOLO Format)**  
Each image has a corresponding `.txt` file inside the matching `/labels/` folder.  
Each line in the file follows this format:
- class_id confidence x_center y_center width height
- Coordinates are **normalized** (0 to 1) relative to image dimensions  
- Example (`IMG_0001.txt`): 
a. Single object: 0 0.95 0.45 0.50 0.20 0.15
b. Two objects: 1 0.95 0.45 0.50 0.20 0.15 0 0.92 0.70 0.30 0.10 0.12

### 3. **Class ID**  
-  **class 0**: cheerios,  **class 1**: soup  
- There are no other object categories in this challenge.

---

## ??**IMPORTANT!** 
 
The **Scenario set up on Falcon Cloud** offers a way for you to generate a **richer set of high-variability synthetic images**, for helping **improve model generalization** and **real-world performance.**  

Leverage the tool and create your own targeted datasets for better results and a competitive edge on the leaderboard!

?? [Start generating your own data here!](https://falcon.duality.ai/secure/scenarios/edit/9e90e036-8af9-41e4-8af0-1343b8e8f467?utm_source=kaggle&utm_medium=kaggle_competition&utm_campaign=competition_4)

---

## ?? What You're Predicting
Predict **bounding boxes** for real-world test images in YOLO format:  
- csv file of all predictions with 2 major entries being, ""image_id"", ""prediction_string""  
- Each line: `image_id"", "" object_class confidence x_center y_center width height""`  
- **Confidence scores** must be between 0 and 1

---

## ?? Acronyms
| Term | Definition |  
|------|------------|  
| **YOLO** | You Only Look Once (object detection architecture) |  
| **mAP@50** | Mean Average Precision at 50% IoU |  
| **IoU** | Intersection over Union (box overlap metric) |  

---

## ? Frequently Asked Questions

**Q: Can I use external data for training?**  
A: Only if it's created using FalconEditor or FalconCloud.  

**Q: What image sizes and formats should I expect?**  
A: Synthetic images are 1980x1080px in .png format. Real test images will be 1920x1080px. 

**Q: How should I improve model performance and results beyond a certain point?** 
A: Use the 3LC software to identify trends in difficult data, then create more data to address the model's weaknesses. [Check out our tips and tricks page](https://falcon.duality.ai/secure/documentation/model-and-data-improvements?sidebarMode=learn?utm_source=kaggle&utm_medium=kaggle_competition&utm_campaign=competition_4) for improving performance through scenario changes, data manipulation, model tuning, and more!",False,True
9851,prediciton-of-ocean-temperature-profile-2025,"# description

# You get 3 files about competetion.
The file named ""train_data_target.npz"" include data of training and corresponding target. You can use the program `np.load(filename)[part needed]` to get the data you need.
Data represents the temperature profiles of a ocean region about 138¡ã-141¡ãE and 1¡ã-4¡ãN. The max depth of the data is 2000m.
The shape of every data is  (625, 37, 12) which represents number of horizontal vertices, number of vertical profiles and number of time strides used to predict future region temperature respectively. **You can reshape it in (25, 25, 37, 12) datasize to observe or utilize the spatial feature.**
*Attention:*
(25, 25, 37, 12) is just the shape of one piece of data. There is an dimension of num of data at position 0. Just use the `data_train.reshape(-1, 25, 25, 37, 12)`
The corresponding target is (625, 37, 1).
If you decide to use torch structure to make your deeplearning net model, we advice you to use the program to transform the numpydata to torch tensor:

```
train_x_tensor = torch.from_numpy(data_train).type(torch.FloatTensor)  # (B, N, F, T)

train_target_tensor = torch.from_numpy(target_train).type(torch.FloatTensor)  # (B, N,F T)
```


Then, you can use the program to make your data set by your needs

```
train_dataset = torch.utils.data.TensorDataset(train_x_tensor, train_target_tensor)

train_loader = torch.utils.data.DataLoader(train_dataset, batch_size=batch_size, shuffle=True/Flase)
```

# test data
The shape of test data is (304, 625, 37, 12), the target is (304, 625, 37, 1)(*Attention: The first location represents the number of all test data, and the last one is the prediction time. The others are as same as the training data.*)  **which is the data you need to submit but you need to reshape it as the (304, -1) using the program.**
```
reshaped_data = target.reshape(304, -1)

df = pd.DataFrame(reshaped_data)

#add ID
df.insert(0, 'ID', range(1, 304+1))

df.to_csv('output.csv', index=False)
```


