    0,123.5
    1,123123.12
    2,123456.78
    etc.",,False,True
9880,mlolympiadbd2025,"# Overview

Every day, maternal complications threaten the lives of pregnant women ¡ª but early risk detection can save them.  
In this competition, your challenge is to build a machine learning model that predicts a pregnant woman¡¯s maternal health risk level: **Low Risk**, **Mid Risk**, or **High Risk**.

Using physiological data such as blood pressure, blood sugar, heart rate, body temperature, and age, your model should learn to classify each patient¡¯s risk level.  
Accurate predictions could help healthcare providers prioritize care, improve outcomes, and potentially save lives.

This competition is an opportunity to apply data science skills to a meaningful real-world problem in healthcare.

----------------------
# Description

Maternal health complications pose significant challenges in healthcare, often requiring timely intervention to prevent severe outcomes. Accurate prediction of a pregnant woman¡¯s risk level can support medical decision-making and resource prioritization.

In this competition, your objective is to develop a multi-class classification model that predicts the maternal health risk level ¡ª **Low Risk**, **Mid Risk**, or **High Risk** ¡ª using structured physiological data.  

The dataset includes the following numerical features:
- `Age`: Age of the pregnant woman (years)
- `SystolicBP`: Systolic (upper) blood pressure (mmHg)
- `DiastolicBP`: Diastolic (lower) blood pressure (mmHg)
- `BS`: Blood sugar level (mmol/L)
- `BodyTemp`: Body temperature (¡ãC)
- `HeartRate`: Heart rate (beats per minute)

Your trained model should learn to generalize from the provided `train.csv` and make accurate predictions on the unseen `test.csv`.  
Final submissions will be evaluated based on **Accuracy Score**, which ensures balanced performance across all three risk classes ¡ª an important consideration given the potential for class imbalance.

This competition combines real-world healthcare context with practical machine learning challenges such as dealing with imbalanced data, feature engineering, and optimizing multi-class classification models.

**For queries contact**:
Email: hasanulbannahimel2003@gmail.com
Linkedin: https://www.linkedin.com/in/hasanul-banna-himel/

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using the **Accuracy Score**, which calculates the Accuracy score independently for each class (Low, Mid, High) and then averages them.  

This metric ensures that your model performs well across all risk levels, rather than focusing only on the majority class.  
Higher Accuracy Score indicates better balanced and more reliable predictions.

> ?? Note: Class imbalance may exist in the dataset, so designing your model to balance precision and recall across classes is important.

---

## ?? Submission Instructions

To participate, submit a CSV file in the following format:

| Id | RiskLevel |
|----|-----------|
| 0  | Low Risk  |
| 1  | Mid Risk  |
| 2  | Low Risk  |
|... | ...       |

- `Id`: Unique identifier matching the `Id` column from `test.csv`.
- `RiskLevel`: Your model's predicted risk level (`Low Risk`, `Mid Risk`, or `High Risk`).

You can find an example format in `sample_submission.csv`.  
Make sure your predictions cover all rows in the test set, and that the column names and order match exactly.

Once you have prepared your submission, upload it on the competition page to see your score on the leaderboard.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains physiological measurements of pregnant women collected to assess maternal health risk levels. Each row represents a single patient and includes basic medical features used to predict whether the woman is at **Low Risk**, **Mid Risk**, or **High Risk** of complications. It captures critical maternal health indicators gathered from hospitals, community clinics, and maternal healthcare centers in rural areas of Bangladesh to help predict the risk level during pregnancy.

The dataset is split into:
- `train.csv`: includes feature columns and the target column `RiskLevel`
- `test.csv`: includes feature columns only (without `RiskLevel`), used for evaluation

---

### ?? Columns

| Column       | Description                                                           |
|--------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Id           | Unique identifier for each patient sample                              |
| Age          | Age of the pregnant woman (years)                                      |
| SystolicBP   | Systolic blood pressure (upper value, mmHg)                            |
| DiastolicBP  | Diastolic blood pressure (lower value, mmHg)                           |
| BS           | Blood sugar level (mmol/L)                                             |
| BodyTemp     | Body temperature (¡ãC)                                                  |
| HeartRate    | Heart rate (beats per minute)                                          |
| RiskLevel    | Target variable: `Low Risk`, `Mid Risk`, or `High Risk` (only in train.csv) |

---

### ?? Target Variable

The goal is to predict `RiskLevel`, which categorizes maternal health risk into:
- **Low Risk**
- **Mid Risk**
- **High Risk**

Your task is to use the provided physiological measurements to classify each patient into one of these categories.

---

### ?? Files Included

| File                    | Description                                                        |
|------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
| train.csv              | Training data with features and target `RiskLevel`                   |
| test.csv               | Test data without target column                                      |
| sample_submission.csv  | Example submission file showing required format                       |
| metaData.csv           | Supplemental information describing each column                      |

---

This dataset is intended for educational purposes to explore predictive modeling in maternal healthcare.",False,True
9881,mmloso2025,"# Overview

Welcome to **[MMLoSo Language Challenge 2025](https://mmloso-workshop.github.io/#contact)**!
India is land of unmatched diversity, especially in terms of spoken languages. Many of these languages are tribal languages, which have a sizable number of speakers. But often, these languages are poorly documented and thus, lack the massive annotated corpora that power today¡¯s NLP breakthroughs. Bhili, being one of them. Limited digital presence means the native speakers face barriers in health-care messaging, disaster alerts, e-governance, and educational resources¡ªall of which increasingly rely on text mining and machine translation. 
----------------------
# Description

## Task 
The task is the develop a **Unified Translation Model** that can convert High Resource Languages (Hindi / English) to Low Resource Languages (Bhilli / Mundari / Santali / Gondi) and visa-versa.

## Call for Papers

We focus on bridging the gap between the growing capabilities of multimodal machine learning models and the urgent needs of real-world applications in under-resourced, marginalized, or data-constrained settings. This includes scenarios where data is scarce, modalities are incomplete or imbalanced, and computational or human infrastructure may be limited.

The topics of interest for the workshop include, but are not limited to:

* Learning with Missing or Incomplete Modalities
    Techniques for modality dropout, hallucination, and imputation when input signals are sparse or missing at training or inference time.

* Few-Shot, Zero-Shot, and Transfer Learning in Multimodal Contexts
    Approaches that allow models pre-trained on high-resource datasets to adapt effectively to novel, low-resource domains and languages.

* Multilingual and Multimodal Representation Learning
    Unifying language, vision, audio, and other modalities across multiple languages, especially those underrepresented in current benchmarks.

* Ethical, Interpretable, and Responsible AI for Multimodal Systems
    Auditing and mitigating bias in multimodal systems; developing transparent models that explain decisions across modalities in high-stakes domains.

* Benchmarking and Evaluation for Real-World Robustness
    Proposing new datasets, metrics, and evaluations that reflect deployment challenges in regions with limited resources or infrastructure.

* Applications in Social Good, including:
        Ecological and biodiversity monitoring (e.g., combining satellite, image, and audio data)
        Public health and epidemiology in underserved regions
        Language documentation and cultural preservation
        Crisis response, misinformation detection, and social justice

----------------------
# Evaluation

#### NOTE: Test Data will be released within a month
The Evaluation for multiple translation tasks is the following: 
```
0.6 x 
  ( 0.6 x 
   (Bleu(Hindi->Bhilli)  + Bleu(Hindi->Mundari) + Bleu(Hindi->Gondi) + Bleu(English->Santali))
  + 0.4 x 
   (Bleu(Bhilli->Hindi)  + Bleu(Mundari->Hindi) + Bleu(Gondi->Hindi) + Bleu(Santali->English))
  )

+ 0.4 x 
  ( 0.6 x 
   (chrF(Hindi->Bhilli)  + chrF(Hindi->Mundari) + chrF(Hindi->Gondi) + chrF(English->Santali))
  + 0.4 x 
   (chrF(Bhilli->Hindi)  + chrF(Mundari->Hindi) + chrF(Gondi->Hindi) + chrF(Santali->English))
  )
 
```


## Submission File
For each `ROW ID` in the test set, you must predict the Translation for `Source Sentence` and save it in the `Target Sentence`. The `submission.csv` should have two coloumns  `Row ID` & `Target Sentence`. 

## Submission Guidelines

The workshop invites submissions in two formats. All page limits exclude unlimited pages for references.

    Long papers (up to 8 pages): This format is for presenting a substantial and complete version of research.
    Short papers (up to 4 pages): This format is ideal for presenting more focused contributions, including preliminary work, work-in-progress, position papers, or descriptions of systems.

## Submission link: 
[OpenReview Submission Portal](https://openreview.net/group?id=aclweb.org/AACL-IJCNLP/2025/Workshop/MMLoSo)
## Review process: 
Review Process: Each paper will be reviewed by at least three program committee members. The submissions will be evaluated based on their technical soundness, relevance, significance, originality, and clarity","# Dataset Description

## Files

*  **bhiili-train.csv** - Hindi - Bhilli training set
*  **gondari-train.csv** - Gondari - Hindi training set
*  **mundari-train.csv** - Mundari - Hindi training set
*  **santali-train.csv** - Santali - English training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set


## Columns 
The following Column(s) are required in submission.csv
*  `Row ID`
*  `Source Lang`
*  `Source Sentence`
*  `Target Lang`
*  `Target Sentence`

**NO WHITE SPACES**",False,True
