9884,penyisihan-bdc-ua,"# Overview

**SELEKSI INTERNAL #2 BDC Satria Data 2025**

Berdasarkan sosialisasi dan pedoman resmi Satria Data 2025, setiap instansi hanya dapat mengirimkan maksimal 15 tim untuk mengikuti satria data. Oleh karena itu, kami mengadakan seleksi internal kembali untuk menentukan 15 tim terbaik yang akan mewakili unair di cabang lomba BDC. Seleksi internal akan dilakukan dengan menggunakan kompetisi di platform kaggle. 

May the best team win.

----------------------
# Description

Data ini merupakan data komentar twitter mengenai Indonesia Emas mulai tahun 2020-2024. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation section describes how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.

---

Submissions are evaluated on Accuracy


## Submission File
For every author in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: `id`, `target_feature`. 
The file should contain a header and have the following format (please note that it's a csv):
id,target_feature
1,0","# Dataset Description

Data yang tercantum disini merupakan komentar dari Twitter hasil scraping dan telah di-label sentimennya secara manual oleh manusia. Kolom pada tabel tersebut adalah created_at, username, full_text, label_fix
Data yang diproses merupakan full_text dan kolom label_fix sebagai Label.

testdata.csv merupakan soal yang harus diberikan sentiment labelnya. Untuk format submission, mengikuti sample_submit.csv

sampledata.csv merupakan contoh file yang harus disubmit dalam kaggle untuk penilaian penyisian BDC ini. Kolom yang diminta hanya id,text, dan label_sentiment (label sentimen hasil prediksi).

Files

data_train.csv - the training set - Komentar dengan Label Sentimen tentang Indonesia Emas
data_test.csv - the exam - Komentar tanpa Label Sentimen untuk di prediksi
sample_submit.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9885,kaggle-community-olympiad-customer-deflection,"# Overview

Retaining customers is just as important as acquiring new ones. In today¡¯s competitive landscape, businesses must understand the subtle signals that indicate when a customer is likely to leave. That¡¯s where Machine Learning steps in!

This competition invites you to harness the power of AI to predict the churn probability of customers based on historical data. By identifying at-risk customers early, your model can help businesses take proactive steps to improve retention and satisfaction. #KaggleCommunityOlympiad

**Goal:** Develop a machine learning model that predicts the likelihood of a customer churning based on the provided dataset.
----------------------
# Description

We provide a dataset containing customer profiles from a subscription-based service. Your challenge is to develop a machine learning model that predicts whether a customer is likely to churn. Churn refers to when a customer stops using the service¡ªsomething every business wants to prevent.

The dataset includes features such as customer age, gender, tenure, usage frequency, support call history, payment delays, and more. Your model should analyze these behavioral and demographic patterns to accurately predict customer churn.

### Explainability is crucial!
>Go beyond just high accuracy¡ªstrive to build interpretable models that help reveal why a customer might churn. This adds real-world value by helping businesses take meaningful actions to retain their users.


## Who can Participate?
This competition is open to everyone passionate about solving real-world problems using Machine Learning. Whether you're just getting started or already have experience, this is a great opportunity to grow your skills and contribute to practical business insights.

**#KaggleCommunityOlympiad** is a series of community competitions organized by the Google AI Community, with support from the Google AI Developer Programs team.

Ready to tackle customer retention with ML? Join the challenge and build models that make a real impact in the world of customer experience!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your model's performance will be evaluated based on its ability to accurately predict whether a customer will churn. The primary evaluation metric for this competition is Log Loss, which measures the uncertainty of your predictions.

---

Lower Log Loss values indicate better performance. Remember, it's not just about predicting churn correctly, but also about how confident your model is in its predictions.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,Churn
    2,0.0
    5,1.0
    6,1.0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset contains customer records from a subscription-based service and is designed to help participants build models that predict whether a customer is likely to churn.

The training set (`train.csv`) should be used to build your machine learning models. For this set, the outcome (Churn) is provided for each customer. You¡¯ll base your model on features such as age, usage frequency, support calls, payment delays, contract length, and more. You are encouraged to perform feature engineering to derive additional insights and improve model performance.

The test set (`test.csv`) should be used to evaluate your model's ability to generalize to unseen data. The Churn outcome is not provided in the test set¡ªyou are expected to predict it using your trained model. For each customer in the test set, submit a predicted probability of churn.

Check the `sample_submission.csv` file for an example of how to format your predictions for submission and evaluation.

### Files

*   **train.csv** -The training set (Contains 404,165 records)
*   **test.csv** - The test set (Contains 101,042 records)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file in the expected format

### Columns

| Column Name | Description |
| --- | --- |
| Id         | Unique identifier for each customer |
| Age                | Age of the customer |
| Gender             | Gender of the customer (e.g., Male, Female, Other) |
| Tenure             | Number of months the customer has been subscribed |
| Usage Frequency    | Frequency of service usage (e.g., number of logins or interactions) |
| Support Calls      | Number of customer service or support calls made                            |
| Payment Delay      | Number of delayed payment instances                                         |
| Subscription Type  | Type of subscription (e.g., Basic, Standard, Premium)                       |
| Contract Length    | Duration of the customer¡¯s contract in months                               |
| Total Spend        | Total amount spent by the customer to date                                  |
| Last Interaction   | Number of days since the customer¡¯s last interaction with the platform      |
| Churn              | Target variable: 1 if the customer churned, 0 otherwise",False,True
