![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F8898627%2F686668bdc32afd62cf6b4aeb74f76dc6%2FJD_pottery%20AI%20competition%20400.png?generation=1753668147425132&alt=media)

----------------------
# Notice on the Results of the Great Daxinzhuang Pottery Puzzle Challenge

The Kaggle competition ¡°Great Daxinzhuang Pottery Puzzle Challenge¡± has now officially concluded. The core mission of this challenge was to develop powerful and innovative AI models capable of addressing two key problems: (1) Fracture Edge Matching and Assembly, by extracting and matching fracture-edge features to infer sherd adjacency; (2) Vessel 3D Reconstruction, aiming to restore assembled sherds as closely as possible to their original vessel forms.

A total of 94 submissions were received. We sincerely thank all participating teams for their enthusiasm and commitment. After the competition, the organizing committee, together with archaeological specialists, conducted a detailed manual review of all submitted pairing results. Overall, the assembled results fell far short of the technical goals and archaeological reconstruction standards originally set for the challenge. After careful deliberation, the committee decided that no First Prize or Second Prize would be awarded in this round. To recognize exploratory efforts that achieved meaningful partial progress on this highly challenging task, the committee decided to award a Third Prize to the team that successfully assembled 78 pottery sherd groups, demonstrating the best overall performance.   

We once again thank all participating teams. Your work has greatly deepened our understanding of the true complexity of automated pottery reconstruction at the intersection of archaeology and artificial intelligence and has provided valuable insights for future research and competition design.    

The next round of the competition is currently being planned. We warmly invite everyone to stay tuned and participate in future rounds of the challenge as we continue advancing the application of artificial intelligence in archaeology and cultural heritage research.   
   
Organizing Committee
----------------------
