----------------------
# Verification of the Results from the Daxinzhuang Pottery Puzzle AI Challenge

**Background and Challenge Overview**   
To explore the feasible pathways for public participation in archaeological research and to examine the research potential of open competition for studying pottery fragment matching, the Institute for Advanced Study of the Beijing Normal¨CHong Kong Baptist University (BNBU) and the School of Archaeology (Institute of Cultural Heritage) at Shandong University jointly organized the ¡°Daxinzhuang Pottery Puzzle AI Challenge¡±. The competition utilized pottery fragments excavated from the Daxinzhuang site, publicly releasing matching tasks and encouraging participants to propose matching solutions based on fragment images and catalog numbers.

Officially launched on July 26, 2025, the challenge closed for submissions on November 25, 2025. Using an online participation and submission format, the organizers provided participants with nearly 20,000 curated pottery fragment images and related basic information. Participants were required to complete and submit their Assembly Groups within the stipulated time. Submissions needed to clearly list combinations of pottery fragment numbers identified as matching, accompanied by necessary matching rationale or illustrative notes. Post-competition, the organizers arranged for physical verification of results that met the submission criteria and presented clear matching relationships.

**Objectives**   
To verify the scientific validity, accuracy, and reproducibility of the results submitted for the ¡°Daxinzhuang Pottery Puzzle AI Challenge¡±, and to further identify and confirm high-quality matches, the project team organized an on-site verification of the submitted results at the Daxinzhuang Site Museum on December 27, 2025. The verification focused on all the 17 sets of submitted results.

The primary objectives of this work included:   
1. Verifying the authenticity and logical validity of the matching relationships proposed by participants;   

2. Assessing the physical fit of the matched fragments;   

3. Providing a reliable basis for subsequent research, display, or documentation.   

**Verification Subjects and Material Sources**   
- Verification Subjects   
The competition received results from 17 teams. Among these, 2 submitted results met the requirements and proceeded to on-site verification. Each verification subject comprised selected pottery fragments and corresponding matching documentation (including images, catalog numbers, and matching illustrations). Other teams submitted results that consisted of fragment grouping or clustering results without explicit, verifiable one-to-one matches. Thus, they did not meet the submission criteria for on-site verification.

- Physical Materials   
The pottery fragments used for verification originated from specimens excavated at the Daxinzhuang site and are currently stored at the Daxinzhuang Site Museum.

All fragments retained their original catalog numbers and information labels to ensure traceability during the verification process.


**Organization of the On-site Verification**   
Time and Location   
Time: December 27 ¨C 28, 2025   
Location: Storage Room, the Daxinzhuang Site Museum   
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F27481087%2F83690017ad9fde5d76bbc8e5601f8d1f%2F1.jpeg?generation=1767861154516551&alt=media)
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F27481087%2F452cf1cc4f1f30d557a82981491d1c8a%2F2.jpeg?generation=1767861169627348&alt=media)

*On-site verification in progress (Storage Room, Jinan Daxinzhuang Site Museum)*

Participants   
Project director and professional archaeological researchers.

Work Groups and Division of Labor   
Verification was conducted group by group according to the result submission numbers, with conclusions reached by consensus.


**On-site Verification Methodology and Process**   
This on-site verification employed a combined method of ¡°physical comparison + technical observation + comprehensive judgment¡±. The specific process was as follows:

1.Documentation Review   
Cross-referencing the submitted documentations to verify pottery fragment catalog numbers, quantities, and corresponding relationships;   
Confirming that submitted materials matched the physical specimens, with no catalog number confusion or omissions.

2.Direct Physical Matching   
Physically assembling the relevant pottery fragments under natural or stable artificial light;   
Focusing on observing whether fracture edges were continuous and fit together naturally.

3.Observation of Key Technical Indicators   
Systematic comparison from the following aspects:   
Fracture morphology: Compatibility of surface topography and edge alignment;   
Body fabric: Clay color, texture, and temper;   
Surface features: Decoration, surface treatment marks;   
Wall Properties: Thickness and curvature consistency.   

4.Recording and Photography   
Each proposed match was documented in writing and photographed.

**Assessment Criteria**   
Based on the on-site verification, matching results were assessed and categorized as follows:

1.Confirmed Match: Continuous breaks, highly consistent technical features; definitively from the same vessel.

2.Basically Confirmed Match: Some discrepancies exist between the proposed match and the physical fit, but a matching relationship is partially demonstrable.

3.Match Not Confirmed: Non-continuous breaks; significant differences in fabric, surface, or firing features; from different vessels.;

