9890,binary-biplob-can-you-guess-the-chess-opening,"# Overview

This competition focuses on an image classification task within the chess domain. Participants will develop machine learning models to classify chessboard images representing the final position at the end of the opening phase. The goal is to correctly assign each position to one of five ECO (Encyclopaedia of Chess Openings) volume categories labeled A through E, based solely on the visual layout of the board.
----------------------
# Description

In this challenge, you will build and train a computer vision model that takes high-resolution images of chessboard positions and classifies them into the appropriate ECO volume category (A, B, C, D, or E). The images represent the final board state after the opening moves, and your model must learn to distinguish subtle differences in piece placement corresponding to different opening families.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your model will be evaluated based on classification accuracy on a stratified held-out test set.

Metric:
`Accuracy = (Number of Correct Predictions) / (Total Number of Predictions)`

The model with the highest accuracy on the test set will be ranked top.",,False,True
9891,big-oai-final-course-1,"# Overview

Welcome to the **BigOAI Final Exam**. This exam is customized exclusively for BigOAI students.

----------------------
# Description


**Your Goal**: The objective of this challenge is to predict insurance premiums based on various factors.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the [Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE)](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.root_mean_squared_log_error.html#sklearn.metrics.root_mean_squared_log_error).


## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict the continuous target `Premium Amount`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:



| id | Premium Amount |
| --- | --- |
| 1200000 | 1102.545 |
| 1200001 | 1102.545 |
etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was a combination of [Insurance Premium Prediction dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/schran/insurance-premium-prediction) and [Regression with an Insurance Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/c/playground-series-s4e12/data). 


## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set; `Premium Amount` is the continuous target
*   **test.csv** - the test set; your objective is to predict target `Premium Amount` for each row
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9892,iisc-ibm-crop-id-segmentation,"# Overview

Crop identification using satellite observations is an age-old problem and remains challenging, especially when trying to generalize model predictions across India's fragmented small farms with diverse cropping practices. This situation is different from regions like the USA or Europe, where farms are larger and practices are more homogeneous. Therefore, there is a significant opportunity to contribute by using geospatial foundation models as a backbone, fine-tuned with satellite observations and field-collected label data for a variety of crops across India. 
----------------------
# Description

In this hackathon, the goal is to classify crop types in agricultural fields across Northern India using Sentinel-2 satellite data as input features. Fields are located in various districts across the states of Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan, Odisha, and Bihar. The AgriFieldNet India training dataset was originally generated by the Radiant Earth Foundation (Radiant Earth Foundation & IDinsight (2022) AgriFieldNet Competition Dataset, Version 1.0, Radiant MLHub. https://doi.org/10.34911/rdnt.wu92p1) using ground reference data collection, and the IBM Research team adopted, modified for this hackathon. To balance the crop classes, we have pruned the crop with fewer samples and finally number of classes reduce from 13 to 6.  

Crop Types Included: Gram, Maize, Mustard, Sugarcane, Wheat, and Other Crop. More detail about data and their split described in the data section. 

**How to Use Them**
Model Fine-Tuning: TerraTorch
Given a task related to Earth observations - such as segmentation, classification, or regression - users can leverage IBM¡¯s open-source TerraTorch tools to streamline their workflow: https://github.com/IBM/terratorch

**Example Workflow:**
? Preprocessing: After curating the dataset, users should follow data preparation examples from the TerraTorch repository
? Model Training: Use TerraTorch for model fine-tuning

**Model Training with TerraTorch**
1. Selecting a Pre-Trained Model:
TerraTorch supports various pre-trained models, including IBM¡¯s Prithvi series.
Model checkpoints are also directly accessible from Hugging face : https://huggingface.co/collections/ibm-nasa-geospatial/prithvi-for-earth-observation-6740a7a81883466bf41d93d6
2. Configuring the Training Setup:
Users must create a configuration file tailored to their task, for example YAML file. 
An example configuration file for similar task for USA region is available at: examples/confs/multi_temporal_crop.yaml along with data sources.  Users can copy and modify this file according to their dataset and objectives.
3. Running Fine-Tuning:
Once configured, users can initiate fine-tuning using TerraTorch. 

**Boiler Plate Notebook:** To make adoption easier for hackathon participants, we have customized an end-to-end notebook that starts with environment setup, data loading, model configuration, fine-tuning, and concludes with evaluation on the test data. This notebook serves as a great starting point for solving the crop ID problem for Indian farms.

**Possible Contributions from Hackathon Participants**
There are multiple opportunities for participants to enhance crop segmentation models for the Indian dataset. Some potential contributions include:
1. Additional data: Adding appropriate dataset can improve accuracy for crop identification. For example, student may consider adding SAR data along with Optical dataset. Weather data can also consider and along many other relevant dataset. 
2. Selective Loss Computation:
Label data values are not fully populated for all pixels in a chip. Hence, loss function computation should be restricted to only those pixels with available label data.
3. Novel Loss Functions:
Since this is a segmentation task, designing novel loss functions during fine-tuning could significantly enhance model performance.
4. Feature Engineering:
Not all feature layers have the same impact on crop segmentation tasks. Participants can experiment with band combinations and create meaningful vegetation indices (e.g., NDVI - Normalized Difference Vegetation Index) to enrich the feature set for better crop identification.



----------------------
# Evaluation

Each submission will be evaluated based on the following performance metrics on the test data:
- Accuracy
- F1 Score
- Jaccard Index
- Jaccard Index Micro``
- Loss value 
- Accuracy for each crop class
- Jaccard Index for each crop class

---

## Submission File
For submission and evaluation in the hackathon, we use Run-Length Encoding (RLE) to represent predicted segmentation masks efficiently. Each test image may contain up to six classes (indexed from 1 to 6), and the RLE format encodes the positions of pixels belonging to each class in a compact form.

The submission file is structured as a CSV with the following columns:
id: Filename of the test image (e.g., agrifieldnet_0276d_label_c6.tif)
class: Class index (from 1 to 6)
predicted: RLE string representing pixel locations for that class in the image, using 1-based row-major order
Each RLE string consists of space-separated pairs:
start length, indicating the starting pixel position and the number of consecutive pixels belonging to the class.

If a class is not present in the image, the corresponding RLE string is left blank. Below example of one test image: 
```
id	class	predicted
agrifieldnet_0276d_label_c6.tif	1	
agrifieldnet_0276d_label_c6.tif	2	
agrifieldnet_0276d_label_c6.tif	3	
agrifieldnet_0276d_label_c6.tif	4	
agrifieldnet_0276d_label_c6.tif	5	1 2 49603 2 49605 3 49859 2 49861 4 50114 2 50117 3 50118 3 50120 3 50369 2 50373 2 50374 2 50376 2 50625 3 50628 2 50629 4 50632 2 50881 3 50884 3 50885 3 50887 2 51141 2 51143 2
```",,False,True
