9919,california-homelessness-prediction-challenge,"# Overview

Homelessness affects more than 187,000 people across California on any given night (HUD, 2024), making it both a profound human crisis and a persistent policy challenge. But despite billions in spending and countless programs, we still can't answer the most basic question: Why can't we solve this?

I built a new dataset to help us find out. The dataset spans 10 California counties¡ªfrom San Diego to Sacramento¡ªcovering about 200 specific regions and 5,000 Census tracts. It combines U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) Point-in-Time homelessness counts with detailed demographic features from the US Census American Community Survey¡ªintegrated into a dataset ready to put your AI skills to the test.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F28162416%2F897e127832bc8fab8a52035103ef76e0%2FCalifornia%20Homelessness%20Bubble%20Chart.png?generation=1754097847515848&alt=media)


**The Data Challenge**
HUD Point-in-Time counts provide detailed information about people experiencing homelessness in specific geographies, but report raw counts rather than population rates. Census data provides population proportions and rates, but doesn't publicly report on homelessness or many of the risk factors HUD measures. Additionally, HUD collects data using geographic boundaries that don't cleanly align with Census boundaries, making it difficult to calculate homelessness rates.

This dataset bridges that gap by creating Census-based demographic profiles that match HUD's geographic boundaries, allowing for rate-based analysis of homelessness patterns. In addition, I mapped 48 of the 58 HUD Point-in-Time demographic categories to their Census American Community Survey equivalents. However, not all HUD categories could be matched due to Census privacy policies that restrict publication of behavioral health indicators, detailed gender classifications, and other sensitive individual characteristics. This allows for comparative analysis of which demographic factors correlate with homelessness rates across HUD's Continuum of Care (CoC) regions and other community boundaries such as County Supervisory Districts.

**Dataset Overview**
HUD characteristics mapped to Census data across the following 10 California metropolitan areas:

Los Angeles City
Santa Clara County
Alameda County
Sacramento County
San Diego County
San Francisco County
Orange County
San Bernardino County
Riverside County
Sonoma County

**The Research Question**
Can demographic data predict homelessness rates, or do the unmappable behavioral health or other factors represent the true drivers of homelessness?

**Competition Goals**
Through this Kaggle competition, we invite the data science community to test whether advanced analytical techniques can identify meaningful patterns in Census demographic data that can predict homelessness rates across California communities.
?
