9925,f-1-racer-diet-planning,"# Overview


Formula 1 drivers push their bodies to the limit under extreme speeds, g-forces and race durations. Proper nutrition and precise calorie tracking are essential to keep them at peak performance.

In this competition, **you are challenged to build a model that predicts the number of calories burned by F1 racers** during a simulated training session. Using biometric and workout data derived from the Calories Burnt Prediction dataset, your predictions will help inform personalized diet plans for each driver.

**Your goal:**  
- Train on `train.csv` (features + `Calories` target)  
- Predict `Calories` on the unseen `test.csv`  
- Optimize for lowest Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)

**What you get:**  
- **Data**: biometric readings (heart rate, speed, duration, etc.) for each session  
- **Starter notebooks**: data exploration, baseline models  
- **Evaluation metric**: RMSE, as shown in the Evaluation tab

Whether you¡¯re a seasoned competitor or new to Kaggle, dive in¡ªyour work could fuel the next generation of F1 nutrition science!  

----------------------
# Description

## Background

Formula 1 is as much a test of human endurance as it is of engineering. Drivers experience sustained high G-forces, rapid accelerations and decelerations, and cockpit temperatures exceeding 50 ¡ãC. To stay sharp and avoid fatigue or heat exhaustion, teams meticulously plan each driver¡¯s diet and hydration.

## Problem Statement

In this competition, you will predict the number of calories an F1 driver burns during a simulated training session, given biometric and workout data. Accurate calorie estimates allow teams to:

- ?? Tailor pre- and post-session meals  
- ?? Optimize hydration strategies  
- ?? Monitor recovery and recovery needs  

Your model must generalize across drivers with different physiologies, session intensities, and durations.

## Data Details

You are provided three files:

- **train.csv** (35.46 MB, 9 columns, 600 000 rows)  
- **test.csv** (same 8 feature columns, no `Calories`)  
- **sample_submission.csv** (`id`, `Calories`)

**train.csv columns**  
| Column       | Type    | Description                               |
|--------------|---------|-------------------------------------------|
| `id`         | int     | Unique session identifier                 |
| `Sex`        | string  | `male` or `female`                        |
| `Age`        | int     | Driver¡¯s age (years)                      |
| `Height`     | float   | Driver¡¯s height (cm)                      |
| `Weight`     | float   | Driver¡¯s weight (kg)                      |
| `Duration`   | float   | Session length (minutes)                  |
| `Heart_Rate` | float   | Average heart rate (beats per minute)     |
| `Body_Temp`  | float   | Average body temperature (¡ãC)             |
| `Calories`   | float   | Total calories burned (target variable)   |

**sample_submission.csv**  
| Column   | Description                      |
|----------|----------------------------------|
| `id`     | Session identifier (matches train/test) |
| `Calories` | Your predicted calories burned |

## Feature Insights

- **Sex, Age, Height, Weight** capture physiological baselines.  
- **Duration** and **Heart_Rate** drive most of the metabolic estimate.  
- **Body_Temp** reflects thermal stress¡ªhigher temperatures correlate with extra calorie expenditure.



----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions are scored by **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)** on the `Calories` target:","# Dataset Description

## Files

- **train.csv** ¨C the training set (35 MB, 600 000 rows, 9 columns). Contains all feature columns plus the target `Calories`.  
- **test.csv** ¨C the test set (same 8 feature columns, no `Calories`). Use this to generate your predictions.  
- **sample_submission.csv** ¨C a sample submission in the correct format. Two columns: `id` and `Calories`.

## Columns

| Column       | Type    | Description                                       |
|--------------|---------|---------------------------------------------------|
| `id`         | int     | Unique session identifier                         |
| `Sex`        | string  | Driver¡¯s sex (`male` or `female`)                 |
| `Age`        | int     | Driver¡¯s age in years                             |
| `Height`     | float   | Driver¡¯s height in centimeters (cm)               |
| `Weight`     | float   | Driver¡¯s weight in kilograms (kg)                 |
| `Duration`   | float   | Session duration in minutes                       |
| `Heart_Rate` | float   | Average heart rate during session (beats per min) |
| `Body_Temp`  | float   | Average body temperature during session (¡ãC)      |
| `Calories`   | float   | Total calories burned in session (target)         |

## What to expect

- **train.csv** includes all 9 columns above.  
- **test.csv** includes the first 8 columns¡ªyour model must predict `Calories`.  
- **sample_submission.csv** has two columns (`id`, `Calories`); make sure your submission uses exactly these column names and order.  

## Prediction Task

You are predicting the continuous target `Calories` for each `id` in `test.csv`.

---

Feel free to refer back to this section for quick reference on file names, formats, and column meanings. Good luck!",False,True
9926,du-ai-challenge-demo,"# Overview

The Street View Object Detection Challenge is an intensive computer vision competition where participants develop AI models to detect and classify common urban objects in street-level images. Teams will compete to achieve the highest mean Average Precision (mAP) on a dataset featuring 5 object classes including vehicles and pedestrians commonly found in city environments.

----------------------
# Description

## Competition Challenge
Detect and classify vehicles and pedestrians in street-level urban images. This dataset captures the complexity of city traffic from a ground perspective, including various vehicle types and pedestrians in diverse urban scenarios.

## Dataset Overview
- **Training Set**: 200 street-level images with YOLO format annotations
- **Test Set**: 100 images requiring object detection predictions  
- **Classes**: 5 traffic participant categories (bicycle, bus, car, motorbike, person)
- **Image Resolution**: 640¡Á640 pixels (standardized)
- **Average Objects per Image**: Multiple objects per scene (dense urban environments)

## Competition Format

### Phase 1: Model Development
- Access to training dataset
- Teams can train and iterate on their models
- Use provided baseline notebooks and examples

### Phase 2: Final Testing  
- Test dataset available (labels withheld)
- Submit predictions for evaluation
- Multiple submissions allowed

## Resources Provided
- Training data with annotations
- Sample submission files
- Baseline YOLOv8 notebook
- Dataset metadata and statistics
- YOLO configuration files

## Evaluation
Submissions are evaluated using mean Average Precision (mAP) at IoU threshold 0.5 across all 5 object classes.

The mAP metric measures both the accuracy of object detection (localization) and classification. For each class, Average Precision (AP) is calculated, and the mean of all APs provides the final score.

### Leaderboard Split
- **Public Leaderboard**: 50% of test data (50 images)
- **Private Leaderboard**: 50% of test data (50 images)

## Submission File Format
Submissions must be a CSV file with the following columns:

- **image_id**: Test image filename without extension
- **PredictionString**: Space-separated predictions for all detected objects

Each prediction in the PredictionString follows this format:
```
<class_id> <confidence> <x_min> <y_min> <x_max> <y_max>
```

### Example submission.csv:
```csv
image_id,PredictionString
aguanambi-1000_png_jpg.rf.7179a0df58ad6448028bc5bc21dca41e,2 0.95 100 200 300 400 3 0.87 500 100 600 200
aguanambi-1240_png_jpg.rf.7e110b54d205ef0537ddc5dec81a79c2,4 0.92 50 60 170 180
```

## Important Notes
- Coordinates must be in pixels (not normalized)
- Confidence scores between 0 and 1
- Multiple objects separated by spaces on the same line
- Empty detections should have empty PredictionString

## Ranking
- Teams ranked by mAP score (higher is better)
- Final ranking based on private leaderboard scores

## Object Classes
The 5 object classes with their corresponding IDs:

| ID | Class     | Description                    |
|----|-----------|--------------------------------|
| 0  | bicycle   | Two-wheeled pedal vehicle      |
| 1  | bus       | Large passenger vehicle        |
| 2  | car       | Standard passenger car         |
| 3  | motorbike | Motorcycle/scooter            |
| 4  | person    | Pedestrian/people             |

## Technical Specifications

### Image Characteristics
- **Perspective**: Street-level view (not aerial)
- **Lighting**: Various conditions including day/night
- **Occlusions**: Objects may be partially visible
- **Dense scenes**: Multiple overlapping objects

### Annotation Format
- YOLO format: `class_id x_center y_center width height`
- All annotation values normalized (0-1)
- One `.txt` file per image

### Recommended Approaches
- Object detection frameworks: YOLOv8, Faster R-CNN, RetinaNet
- Data augmentation for varied lighting conditions
- Handle occlusions and partial objects
- Consider small object detection techniques

## Tips for Success
1. **Understand the perspective**: Street-level images have different challenges than aerial views
2. **Handle occlusions**: Many objects are partially hidden behind others
3. **Edge cases**: Objects at image boundaries may be cut off
4. **Class balance**: Dataset is balanced across all 5 classes
5. **Augmentation**: Use appropriate augmentations for street scenes
6. **Post-processing**: Non-maximum suppression tuning can improve results

## Dataset Files Structure
```
street_view_dataset_small/
©À©¤©¤ train/
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ images/     # 200 training images
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ labels/     # YOLO format annotations
©À©¤©¤ test/
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ images/     # 100 test images
©À©¤©¤ data.yaml       # YOLOv8 configuration
©À©¤©¤ class_names.txt # List of classes
©À©¤©¤ sample_submission.csv   # Submission template
```

## Getting Started
1. Download and extract the dataset
2. Review the baseline notebook for a simple YOLOv8 example
3. Explore the training data and annotations
4. Train your model using the provided splits
5. Generate predictions for test images
6. Submit your predictions in the required CSV format

Good luck with your object detection models!","# Dataset Description

# Street View Object Detection Challenge

## Dataset Description
This competition challenges participants to detect and classify various objects in street-level images. The dataset contains diverse urban street scenes with bounding box annotations for 5 different object classes commonly found in city environments.

## What am I predicting?
You need to detect objects in street-level images and predict:
- Object class (one of 5 categories)
- Bounding box coordinates (x_min, y_min, x_max, y_max)
- Confidence score for each detection

## Files

### ?? train/
- `images/` - 200 street-level images in JPEG format
- `labels/` - 200 corresponding annotation files in YOLO format (one .txt file per image)

### ?? test/
- `images/` - 100 test images requiring object detection predictions

### ?? Core Files
- `sample_submission.csv` - Example showing the correct submission format
- `metadata.csv` - Image dimensions and statistics for all images
- `class_names.txt` - List of 5 object classes with their IDs
- `data.yaml` - YOLO configuration file with paths and class names

## Data Format

### Training Annotations (YOLO Format)
Each .txt file in `train/labels/` contains one line per object:
```
<class_id> <x_center> <y_center> <width> <height>
```
- All values are normalized (0-1) relative to image dimensions
- Example: `2 0.5432 0.3421 0.0521 0.0832` (car at center of image)

### Images
- **Format**: JPEG (.jpg)
- **Resolution**: 640¡Á640 pixels (standardized)
- **Perspective**: Street-level view
- **Naming**: Each image has a unique identifier (e.g., `aguanambi-1000_png_jpg.rf.7179a0df58ad6448028bc5bc21dca41e`)

## Submission Format
Your submission file must be a CSV with these columns:

### Columns
- `image_id` - The test image filename without extension (must match exactly)
- `PredictionString` - Space-separated predictions for all objects detected

### PredictionString Format
Each object: `<class_id> <confidence> <x_min> <y_min> <x_max> <y_max>`
- Multiple objects are space-separated on the same line
- Coordinates in pixels (not normalized)
- Confidence between 0 and 1

### Example
```csv
image_id,PredictionString
aguanambi-1000_png_jpg.rf.7179a0df58ad6448028bc5bc21dca41e,2 0.95 100 200 300 400 3 0.87 500 100 600 200
aguanambi-1240_png_jpg.rf.7e110b54d205ef0537ddc5dec81a79c2,4 0.92 50 60 170 180
```

## Classes (5 total)

| ID | Class     | Description                    |
|----|-----------|--------------------------------|
| 0  | bicycle   | Two-wheeled pedal vehicle      |
| 1  | bus       | Large passenger vehicle        |
| 2  | car       | Standard passenger car         |
| 3  | motorbike | Motorcycle/scooter            |
| 4  | person    | Pedestrian/people             |

## Metadata Columns
The `metadata.csv` file contains:
- `image_id` - Unique image identifier
- `split` - Whether image is in train or test set
- `width` - Image width in pixels (640)
- `height` - Image height in pixels (640)
- `file_size_bytes` - File size
- `num_objects` - Object count (training images only)
- `aspect_ratio` - Width/height ratio (1.0 for all images)

## Important Notes
- **Street perspective**: All images are captured at street level, not aerial
- **Dense urban scenes**: Images contain multiple objects typical of city environments
- **Standardized size**: All images are resized to 640¡Á640 for consistency
- **Varied lighting**: Dataset includes different times of day and weather conditions

## Evaluation
Submissions are evaluated using mean Average Precision (mAP) at IoU threshold 0.5 across all 5 classes.

## Tips
- Consider the challenges of street-level object detection with occlusions
- Pay attention to partially visible objects at image boundaries
- Some objects may be heavily occluded by others
- Use the provided YOLO format labels for training
- Ensure your predictions use pixel coordinates, not normalized values
- The dataset is balanced across all 5 classes",False,True
9927,2026-icassp-hyper-object-challenge-track-1,"# Overview

Hyperspectral imaging captures rich spectral signatures, enabling precise material analysis. But dedicated HSI cameras are expensive and bulky. Meanwhile, RAW mosaic sensors (Bayer-like) are cheap and everywhere.

This challenge aims to reconstruct a 61-band hyperspectral cube (400¨C1000 nm, 10 nm step) from a single-channel mosaic input captured at commodity-camera resolution. High-fidelity spectral recon from low-cost sensors would unlock applications from counterfeit detection to agriculture, recycling, and cultural heritage.
----------------------
# Description

## Your Task
- **Input**: single-plane mosaic image (H¡ÁW¡Á1) with a 2¡Á2 CFA pattern (RGGB).
- **Output**: hyperspectral reflectance cube (H¡ÁW¡Á61) in [0, 1].

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F28174013%2Fa921d2cc46d65d2bfa71bbd15ce7c468%2FTRACK%201%20DIAGRAM%201.jpg?generation=1756184233599041&alt=media)

## Data
- Diverse everyday objects, indoor lighting near D65.
- Calibrated reflectance ground truth (61 bands, 400¡­1000 nm).
- Train/validation splits provided; hidden test for final leaderboard.

## Helper functions
- You can use our provided functions here ([https://github.com/hyper-object/2026-ICASSP-SPGC](https://github.com/hyper-object/2026-ICASSP-SPGC)) to load the data
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are ranked by Spectral-Spatial-Color (SSC) score, range in [0,1].
- Spectral: SAM, SID, ERGAS
- Spatial: PSNR, SSIM on a standardized sRGB render (D65, CIE 1931 2¡ã)
- Color: ¦¤E00 on the same sRGB render

## ?? What are the key dates?

- ?? Training Data, Public Test Set, Private Test Set Release: **Now available**  
- ?? Final Code Submission Deadline: **~~November 25, 2025~~**  **November 29, 2025**  
- ?? Winners Announced: **November 30, 2025**

## Submission File
To make a valid submission, you must upload a single `.zip` file.  
This `.zip` file must contain:  
1. **`submission.csv`**  
   - A CSV file with the following columns:  
     - `id` ¡ú the filename (without extension) of each test sample (e.g. `0001`, `0002`, ¡­).  
     - `prediction` ¡ú set to `0` (placeholder column required by Kaggle).  
   - Example:
     ```csv
     id,prediction
     Category-1_a_0007, 0
     Category-2_a_0009, 0
     ```

2. **Reconstructed hyperspectral results**  
   - For every test ID in the solution file, include a file named **`<id>.npz`** inside the zip.  
   - Each `.npz` must contain an array called **`cube`** of shape `(H, W, 61)` with reflectance values in `[0,1]`.  
   - Example for saving:
     ```python
     import numpy as np
     
     # assume `cube` is your reconstructed HSI of shape (H, W, 61)
     np.savez(""Category-1_a_0007.npz"", cube=cube)
     ```

## Example structure of your `.zip` file
```
submission.zip
©À©¤©¤ submission.csv
©À©¤©¤ Category-1_a_0007.npz
©À©¤©¤ Category-2_a_0009.npz
©À©¤©¤ Category-3_a_0035.npz
©¸©¤©¤ ...
```

## Important notes
- **Filenames must match exactly** with the test IDs provided in the private-test folder (no extensions, no extra folders).  
- `submission.csv` must be at the root of the zip, not inside a subfolder.  
- Each `.npz` must contain the key `cube`. Any other keys will be ignored.  
- Submissions with missing IDs, wrong filenames, or wrong array shapes will fail.",,False,True
9928,lex-ai-kaggle-june-comp-1-digit-recognizer,"# Description

<h3>Start here if...</h3>
<p>You have some experience with R or Python and machine learning basics, but you¡¯re new to computer vision. This competition is the perfect introduction to techniques like neural networks using a classic dataset including pre-extracted features.</p>


<h3>Competition Description</h3>
<p>MNIST (""Modified National Institute of Standards and Technology"") is the de facto ¡°hello world¡± dataset of computer vision. Since its release in 1999, this classic dataset of handwritten images has served as the basis for benchmarking classification algorithms. As new machine learning techniques emerge, MNIST remains a reliable resource for researchers and learners alike.</p>
<p>In this competition, your goal is to correctly identify digits from a dataset of tens of thousands of handwritten images. We¡¯ve curated a set of tutorial-style kernels which cover everything from regression to neural networks. We encourage you to experiment with different algorithms to learn first-hand what works well and how techniques compare.</p>
<h3>Practice Skills</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Computer vision fundamentals including simple neural networks</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Classification methods such as SVM and K-nearest neighbors</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>Acknowledgements&nbsp;</h3>
<p>More details about the dataset, including algorithms that have been tried on it and their levels of success, can be found at <a href=""http://yann.lecun.com/exdb/mnist/index.html"" target=""_blank"">http://yann.lecun.com/exdb/mnist/index.html</a>. The dataset is made available under a <a href=""https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/"" target=""_blank"">Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 3.0 license</a>.</p>
----------------------
# Tutorial

<h3>Kaggle Learn</h3>
<p><a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/learn"" target=""_blank""><b>Kaggle Learn</b></a> offers hands-on courses for most data science topics. These short courses prepare you with everything you need to start your own projects, including deep learning and computer vision projects. </p>
<p>The <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/learn/intro-to-deep-learning"" target=""_blank""><b>Deep Learning Course</b></a> will give you everything you need to succeed in this competition and others like it.</p> 

<h3>Other Python Tutorials</h3>

<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/code/poonaml/deep-neural-network-keras-way"" target=""_target""><b>Deep neural network the Keras way</b></a>
<ul>
<li>Covers pre-processing including feature standardization and one-hot encoding
</li><li>Implements an artificial neural network approach using Keras
</li></ul>

<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/code/fchollet/simple-deep-mlp-with-keras"" target=""_target""><b>Simple deep MLP with Keras</b></a>
<ul>
<li>A straightforward implementation of MLP (multi-layer perceptron) in Keras
</li><li>Learn Keras from the author himself, Francois Chollet!
</li></ul>

<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/code/arthurtok/interactive-intro-to-dimensionality-reduction"" target=""_target""><b>An introduction to dimensionality reduction</b></a>
<ul>
<li>Introduces and compares PCA, LDA, and t-SNE dimensionality reduction techniques
</li><li>Uses the Plotly library for intuitive, interactive visualizations
</li></ul>

<h3>Other R Tutorials</h3>
<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/code/benhamner/random-forest-benchmark-r"" target=""_target""><b>Random forest benchmark</b></a>	
<ul>
<li>A minimal example implementing the random forest algorithm
</li></ul>

<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/code/russwill/build-your-own-neural-network-in-r"" target=""_target""><b>Build your own neural network in R</b></a>
<ul>
<li>Implements a simple 2-layer neural network from scratch
</li><li>Based on the CS231n course offered by Stanford
</li></ul>

<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/code/olhacher/minimum-distance-classifier"" target=""_target""><b>Minimum distance classifier</b></a>	
<ul>
<li>Uses minimum distance as a simple approach to classification
</li></ul>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<h3>Goal</h3>
The goal in this competition is to take an image of a handwritten single digit, and determine what that digit is.<br>For every  in the test set, you should predict the correct label. 

<h3>Metric</h3>
<p>This competition is evaluated on the categorization accuracy of your predictions (the percentage of images you get correct).</p>

<h3>Submission File Format</h3>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ImageId,Label<br>1,0<br>2,0<br>3,0<br>etc.</pre>
----------------------
# Frequently Asked Questions

### What is a Getting Started competition? 

Getting Started competitions were created by Kaggle data scientists for people who have little to no machine learning background. They are a great place to begin if you are new to data science or just finished a MOOC and want to get involved in Kaggle.

Getting Started competitions are a non-competitive way to get familiar with Kaggle¡¯s platform, learn basic machine learning concepts, and start meeting people in the community. They have no cash prize and are on a rolling timeline.

### How do I create and manage a team?

When you accept the competition rules, a team will be created for you. You can invite others to your team, accept a merger with another team, and update basic information like team name by going to the [Team page](https://www.kaggle.com/c/digit-recognizer/team).

We've heard from many Kagglers that teaming up is the best way to learn new skills AND have fun. If you don't have a teammate already, consider asking if anyone wants to team up in the [discussion forum](https://www.kaggle.com/c/digit-recognizer/discussion).

### What are Notebooks?

Kaggle Notebooks is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Kernels supports scripts in R and Python, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. Go to the [Notebooks tab](https://www.kaggle.com/c/digit-recognizer/notebooks) to view all of the publicly shared code on this competition. For more on how to use Notebooks to learn data science, visit Kaggle's [Learn Courses](https://www.kaggle.com/learn/overview).

### What¡¯s the difference between a private and public leaderboard?

In this competition, because it is a Getting Started competition, there is no difference. We're scoring the entire test set on the Public Leaderboard. And we will refresh the competition every three months, so the Private Leaderboard is irrelevant.

For non-Getting Started Kaggle competitions, there is the concept of a public and private leaderboard to prevent participants from ¡°overfitting¡± to the leaderboard. If your model is ¡°overfit¡± to a dataset then it is not generalizable outside of the dataset you trained it on. This means that your model would have low accuracy on another sample of data taken from a similar dataset.

### Why are there perfect scores on the leaderboard?

This competition's test set labels are completely public. So it's likely that some participants will submit perfect submissions. Since there are no prizes, medals, or points associated with your leaderboard ranking in this competition, these scores have little consequence. The rankings are purely for the benefit of users to learn and see how their approach is improving. 

*""Then can you just clear them out? They're distracting.""* It's a heavily time-intensive process to manage removals from a continuously live leaderboard. The threshold for what should be removed also becomes arbitrary -- if you remove 1.00 scores, then .99 scores will quickly appear. Since there is no prize consequence, we have chosen to leave the scores as an open sandbox.

### Why did my team disappear from the leaderboard?

To keep with the spirit of getting-started competitions, we have implemented a two month rolling window on submissions. Once a submission is more than two months old, it will be invalidated and no longer count towards the leaderboard.

If your team has no submissions in the previous two months, the team will also drop from the leaderboard. This will keep the leaderboard at a manageable size, freshen it up, and prevent newcomers from getting lost in a sea of abandoned scores.

*""I worked so hard to get that score! Give it back!""* Read more about our decision to implement rolling leaderboards, covered in the [Titanic Getting Started Competition forum here](https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/discussion/6240).

### How do I contact Support?

Kaggle does not have a dedicated support team so you¡¯ll typically find that you receive a response more quickly by asking your question in the appropriate forum. (For this competition, you¡¯ll want to use [this competition's discussion forum](https://www.kaggle.com/c/digit-recognizer/discussion).

Support is only able to help with issues that are being experienced by all participants. Before contacting support, please check the discussion forum for information on your problem. If you can¡¯t find it, you can post your problem in the forum so a fellow participant or a Kaggle team member can provide help. The forums are full of useful information on the data, metric, and different approaches. We encourage you to use the forums often. If you share your knowledge, you'll find that others will share a lot in turn!

If your problem persists or it seems to be effective all participants then please [contact us](https://www.kaggle.com/contact).","# Dataset Description

<p>The data files train.csv and test.csv contain gray-scale images of hand-drawn digits, from zero through nine.</p>
<p>Each image is 28 pixels in height and 28 pixels in width, for a total of 784 pixels in total. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255, inclusive.</p>
<p>The training data set, (train.csv), has 785 columns. The first column, called ""label"", is the digit that was drawn by the user. The rest of the columns contain the pixel-values of the associated image.</p>
<p>Each pixel column in the training set has a name like pixelx, where x is an integer between 0 and 783, inclusive. To locate this pixel on the image, suppose that we have decomposed x as x = i * 28 + j, where i and j are integers between 0 and 27, inclusive. Then pixelx is located on row i and column j of a 28 x 28 matrix, (indexing by zero).</p>
<p>For example, pixel31 indicates the pixel that is in the fourth column from the left, and the second row from the top, as in the ascii-diagram below.</p>
<p>Visually, if we omit the ""pixel"" prefix, the pixels make up the image like this:</p>
<pre>000 001 002 003 ... 026 027
028 029 030 031 ... 054 055
056 057 058 059 ... 082 083
 |   |   |   |  ...  |   |
728 729 730 731 ... 754 755
756 757 758 759 ... 782 783 
</pre>
<p>The test data set, (test.csv), is the same as the training set, except that it does not contain the ""label"" column.</p>
<p>Your submission file should be in the following format: For each of the 28000 images in the test set, output a single line containing the ImageId and the digit you predict. For example, if you predict that the first image is of a 3, the second image is of a 7, and the third image is of a 8, then your submission file would look like:</p>
<pre>ImageId,Label<br />1,3<br />2,7<br />3,8 
(27997 more lines)</pre>
<p>The evaluation metric for this contest is the categorization accuracy, or the proportion of test images that are correctly classified. For example, a categorization accuracy of 0.97 indicates that you have correctly classified all but 3% of the images.</p>",False,True
9929,hull-tactical-market-prediction,"# Overview

Your task is to predict the stock market returns as represented by the excess returns of the S&P 500 while also managing volatility constraints.  Your work will test the Efficient Market Hypothesis and challenge common tenets of personal finance.
----------------------
# Description

Wisdom from most personal finance experts would suggest that it's irresponsible to try and time the market. The Efficient Market Hypothesis (EMH) would agree: everything knowable is already priced in, so don¡¯t bother trying. 

But in the age of machine learning, is it irresponsible to *not* try and time the market? Is the EMH an extreme oversimplification at best and possibly just...false?

This competition is about more than predictive modeling. Predicting market returns challenges the assumptions of market efficiency. Your work could help reshape how investors and academics understand financial markets. Participants could uncover signals others overlook, develop innovative strategies, and contribute to a deeper understanding of market behavior¡ªpotentially rewriting a fundamental principle of modern finance. Most investors don¡¯t beat the S&P 500. That failure has been used for decades to prop up EMH: If even the professionals can¡¯t win, it must be impossible. This observation has long been cited as evidence for the Efficient Market Hypothesis  the idea that prices already reflect all available information and no persistent edge is possible. This story is tidy, but reality is less so. Markets are noisy, messy, and full of behavioral quirks that don¡¯t vanish just because academic orthodoxy said they should.

Data science has changed the game. With enough features, machine learning, and creativity, it¡¯s possible to uncover repeatable edges that theory says shouldn¡¯t exist. The real challenge isn¡¯t whether they exist¡ªit¡¯s whether you can find them and combine them in a way that is robust enough to overcome frictions and implementation issues.

Our current approach blends a handful of quantitative models to adjust market exposure at the close of each trading day. It points in the right direction, but with a blurry compass. Our model is clearly a sub-optimal way to model a complex, non-linear, adaptive system. This competition asks you to do better: to build a model that predicts excess returns and includes a betting strategy designed to outperform the S&P 500 while staying within a 120% volatility constraint. We¡¯ll provide daily data that combines public market information with our proprietary dataset, giving you the raw material to uncover patterns most miss.

Unlike many Kaggle challenges, this isn¡¯t just a theoretical exercise. The models you build here could be valuable in live investment strategies. And if you succeed, you¡¯ll be doing more than improving a prediction engine¡ªyou¡¯ll be helping to demonstrate that financial markets are not fully efficient, challenging one of the cornerstones of modern finance, and paving the way for better, more accessible tools for investors.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The competition's metric is a variant of the Sharpe ratio that penalizes strategies that take on significantly more volatility than the underlying market or fail to outperform the market's return. The [metric code is available here](https://www.kaggle.com/code/metric/hull-competition-sharpe).

## Submission File

You must submit to this competition using the provided evaluation API, which ensures that models do not peek forward in time. For each trading day, you must predict an optimal allocation of funds to holding the S&P500. As some leverage is allowed, the valid range covers 0 to 2. See [this example notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/sohier/hull-tactical-market-prediction-demo-submission/) for more details.

----------------------
# Timeline

### This is a forecasting competition with an active training phase and a separate forecasting phase where models will be run against real market returns.

### Training Timeline: 

* **September 16, 2025** - Start Date. 
* **${competition.ProhibitNewEntrantsExplicitDeadline}** -  Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete. 
* **${competition.TeamMergerExplicitDeadline}** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams. 
* **December 15, 2025** - Final Submission Deadline. 

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.

### Forecasting Timeline:
Starting after the final submission deadline there will be periodic updates to the leaderboard to reflect market data updates that will be run against selected notebooks.    

 - **June 16, 2026** - Competition End Date
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - $50,000
* 2nd Place - $25,000
* 3rd Place - $10,000
* 4th Place - $5,000
* 5th Place - $5,000
* 6th Place - $5,000
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met: 
- CPU Notebook <= 8 hours run-time 
- GPU Notebook <= 8 hours run-time 
- Internet access disabled 
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models 

### Forecasting Phase
The run-time limits for both CPU and GPU notebooks will be extended to 9 hours during the forecasting phase. You must ensure your submission completes within that time.

The extra hour is to help protect against time-out failures due to the extended size of the test set. You are still responsible for ensuring your submission completes within the 9 hour limit, however. See the [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/hull-tactical-market-prediction/data) page for details on the extended test set during the forecasting phase.

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.",,False,True
9930,mmir,"# Overview

## ?? MMIR Challenge: Multimodal Inconsistency Reasoning
Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) are becoming increasingly proficient at reasoning over rich visual-textual content. However, real-world documents¡ªlike webpages, presentation slides, and posters¡ªoften contain **semantic inconsistencies** that current models struggle to detect.

The **MMIR Challenge** invites participants to build models that can **detect and localize inconsistencies** in such layout-rich, multimodal artifacts.

You¡¯ll be working with the **MMIR dataset**, which contains 534 samples, each with injected inconsistencies across five categories:

* Factual Contradiction
* Identity Misattribution
* Contextual Mismatch
* Quantitative Discrepancy
* Temporal/Spatial Incoherence

## ?? Task
For each sample, your model must **select the inconsistent element(s)** from a set of candidates.
This is a **Multiple-Choice Question (MCQ)** setting with **strict accuracy evaluation** based on exact match of predicted element IDs.
Accuracy is measured by exact match with ground-truth element ID(s).

## ?? Why This Matters
This challenge highlights a critical weakness in today¡¯s MLLMs: failure to reason about inconsistencies in real-world multimodal inputs.
By participating, you help push the field toward **more robust, error-aware multimodal reasoning systems**.

## ?? Citation
If you use the MMIR dataset in your research, please cite:
<pre>
@inproceedings{yan-etal-2025-multimodal,
  title = ""Multimodal Inconsistency Reasoning (MMIR): A New Benchmark for Multimodal Reasoning Models"",
  author = ""Yan, Qianqi and Fan, Yue and Li, Hongquan and Jiang, Shan and Zhao, Yang and Guan, Xinze and Kuo, Ching-Chen and Wang, Xin Eric"",
  booktitle = ""Findings of ACL 2025"",
  year = ""2025"",
  url = ""https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.964/""
}
</pre>

----------------------
# Description

## ?? Input Format
Each sample includes:
* A rendered visual artifact such as a web page or poster (image file)
* A question file asking which element(s) are inconsistent, each with a list of candidate elements in the artifact

Your task is to select one or two element IDs that best explain the inconsistency in the sample.

The dataset is available for download here:
[Download MMIR Dataset](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BODkvYkjkamh-hM68h9Re69TaXsiEfDj/view?usp=sharing)

## ?? Submission Format
Submissions must be a single **CSV file** with the following columns:

| Column Name     | Type        | Description                                                   |
|------------------|-------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
| `question_id`    | int         | The ID of the question/sample (matches the input question file) |
| `answer`         | list[int]   | A list containing one or two element IDs predicted as inconsistent |

### ? Example format (`submission.csv`)
```csv
question_id,answer
0,""[15]""
1,""[25, 26]""
2,""[8]""
```
?? The answer field must be a JSON-encoded list, even for single-answer cases.
Example: ""[15]"", not 15

## ?? Scoring Metric
Submissions are evaluated using strict accuracy matching.",,False,True
