9967,human-like-ai-task-automation,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to develop an AI agent capable of performing tasks on a computer in a manner indistinguishable from a human user. Participants will create intelligent agents that can interpret instructions, navigate applications, manipulate files, interact with web interfaces, and complete workflows autonomously. Solutions must be reliable, easily testable, and runnable in a standard environment.
----------------------
# Description

Participants will receive task instructions in natural language or structured workflow prompts. The AI agent must execute these tasks in a virtual or real computer environment, performing actions such as clicking, typing, dragging, or launching applications. The challenge encourages automation of routine workflows, context understanding, error handling, and human-like decision-making. Creativity in designing adaptable, generalizable agents is highly valued.

Tasks:
Tier 1 ¨C Basic Tasks (Foundational)

These tasks establish core functionality and test fundamental interaction abilities:

Open, close, and switch between applications

Create, rename, move, copy, or delete files and folders

Write, edit, and save text documents

Copy and paste data between applications

Click buttons and navigate simple menus on websites

Follow basic natural language instructions

Tier 2 ¨C Intermediate Tasks (Multi-Step & Contextual)

These require the agent to manage sequences of actions, understand context, and handle data intelligently:

Fill forms or templates automatically

Extract, summarize, or organize information from documents

Sort, filter, and analyze data in spreadsheets or databases

Schedule calendar events and send emails or messages

Navigate multi-step workflows across applications

Handle simple errors or unexpected pop-ups gracefully

Tier 3 ¨C Advanced Tasks (Human-Like Autonomy)

These tasks push toward human-level decision-making and adaptability:

Automate repetitive web interactions (logging in, downloading reports, checking emails)

Trigger and coordinate multi-application workflows dynamically

Adjust task execution based on prior experience or learning

Prioritize tasks intelligently under time or resource constraints

Participate in collaborative environments (e.g., taking notes in virtual meetings)

Make context-driven decisions when instructions are ambiguous
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation

1. Purpose
The evaluation assesses the AI agent¡¯s ability to reliably, safely, and efficiently perform automated tasks on a PC according to user instructions, while respecting security boundaries and minimizing errors.

2. Evaluation Metrics

Task Success Rate: Percentage of assigned tasks completed correctly without errors.

Execution Time: Average time taken to complete a task compared to a baseline or human execution.

Error Handling: Ability to detect, report, and recover from errors or unexpected system states.

Resource Usage: CPU, memory, and disk consumption while executing tasks.

Security Compliance: No unauthorized access, no changes outside allowed directories, proper sandboxing of sensitive operations.

User Interaction Accuracy: For tasks requiring UI interaction, measure correct clicks, key presses, and menu navigation.

Adaptability: Ability to adjust workflows dynamically based on system state changes or missing resources.

3. Evaluation Procedure

Define a set of representative tasks (file management, program execution, web automation, etc.).

Run the agent on a controlled test environment with varied OS states and system configurations.

Log metrics for each task and compute aggregate scores for success rate, efficiency, and resource usage.

Conduct stress tests for simultaneous tasks and error scenarios.

Optionally, perform user trials to assess satisfaction, ease of use, and perceived reliability.

4. Scoring

Assign weights to each metric depending on priority (e.g., 40% task success, 20% execution time, 15% error handling, 15% resource usage, 10% adaptability).

Compute an overall performance score to rank agents or versions.","# Dataset Description

No files are provided; all that is necessary is the scripts to run the agent and see its functionality. Test it on the tasks described",False,True
9968,vanilla-optimization-2-d-branin,"# Overview

This is the first in a **series of optimization competitions**. The Branin task serves as the entry point: simple, deterministic, and designed to get participants familiar with the submission workflow before moving on to harder variants.

Your objective: optimize the **Branin function** without direct access to its formula. Interaction is only through the provided package, which enforces strict evaluation budgets.

---

## Why Branin + Why This Task

- Branin is a standard 2D benchmark with known minima ([sfu.ca](https://www.sfu.ca/~ssurjano/branin.html)).  
- Each campaign has limited evaluations, making sample-efficient approaches (e.g. Bayesian optimization) advantageous.  
- This challenge is mainly about **learning the system**: campaigns, budgets, exports, and submission.  

The leaderboard for this vanilla optimization task will reflect general trends; for example, do not use the leaderboard to say ""Bayesian optimization package X performs 5% better than Bayesian optimization package Y, so I should use package X in practice"". Think of this as more of an orientation competition. Future tasks will add complexity: multi-objective optimization, multi-task optimization, multi-fidelity optimization, noisy evaluations, culminating in a **real-world optimization task**.

---

## Rules & Constraints

- Exactly **12 campaigns**  
- **40 evaluations** maximum per campaign  
- Input bounds: **x1 ¡Ê [?5, 10]**, **x2 ¡Ê [0, 15]**  
- All interaction must go through the provided Kaggle package  
- Scoring is based on **combined performance across all campaigns**

---

## Package & Workflow

The package handles campaign setup, evaluation tracking, and CSV export.

### Example

```python
import kagglehub
package = kagglehub.package_import(""amanichabouni/branin-package/versions/17"")

# start campaign
model = package.Model()

# evaluate
value = model.predict_single(2.35, 7.5)

# export results
package.Model.export_history()
```

Sample output:

```
Created campaign 0
1195.3468438774967
History exported to submission.csv (1 total evaluations).
```

---

## Coming Next

Other tasks in the series include:

* **[Multi-objective Optimization](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/multiple-objective-optimization-branin)**
  Handle two objectives at once, exploring trade-offs.
  *This competition is scheduled to launch on **September 23, 2025 at 11:00 AM EDT.***

* **[Multi-Task Optimization](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/multi-task-optimization)**
  Optimize across multiple analytical objectives simultaneously.
  *This competition is scheduled to launch on **September 30, 2025 at 11:00 AM EDT***

* **[Multi-Fidelity Branin Optimization](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/multi-fidelity-branin-optimization)**
  Balance evaluations across cheaper low-fidelity and expensive high-fidelity versions.
  *This competition is scheduled to launch on **October 7, 2025 at 11:00 AM EDT***

* **[Noisy Vanilla Optimization (2D Branin Function)](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/noisy-vanilla-optimization-2-d-branin-function)**
  Optimize the Branin function with added noise. The benchmark will ingest user predictions.
  *This competition is scheduled to launch on **October 14, 2025 at 11:00 AM EDT***

* **[CrabNet Hyperparameter Optimization](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/crab-net-hyperparameter-optimization)**
  Tune a machine learning model's 23 hyperparameters to optimize four objectives. This problem is noisy, multi-fidelity, constrained, and multi-objective, designed to match the typical characteristics of a real-world materials optimization task.
  *This competition is scheduled to launch on **October 21, 2025 at 11:00 AM EDT***
----------------------
# Evaluation

