9980,melting-point,"# Overview

**Goal:** Your goal is to build ML models that predict melting point (¡ãC) for organic compounds given molecular descriptors.
----------------------
# Description

# ?? Melting Point Prediction Challenge

Predicting the **melting point** of organic molecules is a long-standing challenge in chemistry and chemical engineering. Melting point is critical for drug design, material selection, and process safety, yet experimental measurements are often costly, time-consuming, or unavailable.

In this competition, you¡¯ll use **machine learning** to predict melting points from **group contribution features**, subgroup counts that represent functional groups within each molecule. Your task is to build models that capture the complex, nonlinear relationships between molecular structure and melting behavior.

**Submissions are scored using Mean Absolute Error (MAE)** on a held-out test set. Lower is better.

Can you design models that generalize across diverse chemical families and push the limits of data-driven property prediction?

**Dataset Description**
- Total compounds: 3328
- Train: 2662 (80%)
- Test: 666 (20%)

**Files**
- train.csv : Features + target (Tm)
- test.csv  : Features only, no target
- sample_submission.csv : Template with columns [id,Tm]

**Columns**
- id          : unique ID
- SMILES      : molecular string
- Group 1..N  : descriptor features
- Tm          : melting point (K) [train only]

Additional information hosted on the [Machine Learning for Engineers](https://apmonitor.com/pds) course in the [Thermophysical Properties](https://apmonitor.com/pds/index.php/Main/ThermophysicalProperties) page. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Submission File**
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict a the target variable `Tm` (melting point). The file should be  in csv format, contain a header,  and have the following format:

**Submission Format**

```python
id,Tm
210,350.1
211,583.8
... etc.
```",,False,True
9981,11785-hw-1-p-2-fall-25,"# Overview

Speech recognition is an important field in deep learning with multiple applications. Speech is a fundamental form of human communication. Speech recognition involves converting spoken language into written text or data that could be understood by machines. Speech data refers to audio recordings of human speech, while phonemes represent the smallest units of sound that can convey a different meaning(bake, take: b,t). Spectrograms are visual representations of the acoustic properties of speech signals, i.e. captures the changes in the frequency over time.
----------------------
# Description

In this competition, you will create a multilayer perceptron for frame-level speech recognition. Specifically, you will create a frame-level phonetic transcription of raw Mel Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs). The MLP will learn a feature representation and nonlinear classification boundary. To discriminate between each phoneme class label, cross-entropy loss will be used to minimize the dissimilarity between output logits and the target labels. The loss will be used during gradient descent to update the parameters of the neural network, thereby minimizing the cost function.
----------------------
# Reference Model

Sparse Multilayer Perceptron for Phoneme Recognition

Note: This Is provided as a conceptual reference paper only. It is very old, and the model in the paper performs poorly given our current methods and understanding. It is otherwise a nice paper, introducing the problem of phonetic transcript generation using multilayer perceptrons.

Link: https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5734801","# Dataset Description

train-clean-100: training set
dev-clean folder: dev/validation set
test-clean folder: test set, sample_submission.csv",False,True
9982,acm-icaif-25-ai-agentic-retrieval-grand-challenge,"# Overview

# Agentic Retrieval for Financial Documents (AI Grand Challenge on FinAgentBench)

> We provide all participants with a baseline guide [HERE](https://www.kaggle.com/code/jihoonkwon/baseline-for-agentic-retrieval-grand-challenge).
> As we show in the demo, even simple changes like swapping in different LLMs or adjusting prompting could lead to clear improvements. You do not need deep LLM expertise to participate.

The **AI Agentic Retrieval Grand Challenge at ICAIF ¡¯25** is motivated by the need for financial AI systems that can move beyond what traditional methods can achieve in accuracy to deliver interpretable, evidence-grounded answers from complex SEC filings. Participants are tasked with **building retrieval systems** by ***optimizing a system prompt*** that can support complex institutional finance questions with character-level evidence attribution. This competition is built on **FinAgentBench**, a benchmark purpose-built to evaluate **multi-step, agentic retrieval**.
Each query requires reasoning across **two stages**: first, *selecting the most relevant document type* (Document-Level Ranking), and second, *identifying the most relevant passages within single document* (Chunk-Level Ranking). The dataset contains about 26K examples curated by expert financial analysts from real SEC filings (2023¨C2024). Participants must return ranked results for both **document-level and chunk-level ranking tasks**.

## Participants who impress with their solutions (and explain how they achieved them) will be invited as a presenter sharing their work at ICAIF ¡¯25. 
Further details will be announced once sponsorship is confirmed.

----------------------
# Description

In the **AI Agentic Retrieval Grand Challenge**, participants are tasked with developing systems capable of retrieving the most relevant evidence from raw SEC filings to answer institutional finance questions. The task is structured as a two-stage agentic retrieval problem: **Document-Level Ranking** and **Chunk-Level Ranking**. This two-step design prioritizes accuracy and qualities essential for deploying AI systems in investment research. Participants¡¯ systems will be evaluated on their capacity to reason across both document-type ranking and chunk-level retrieval tasks. 

*All submissions must include a **reproducible notebook implemented within Databricks Free Edition workspaces**, with the notebook link uploaded in the discussion tab as the standardized evaluation interface. Optional integration with the Upstage API (Solar LLM) is allowed.*

## Task Overview
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7863652%2F6e3455da37771a24b6003d5eed85d076%2FPipeline-4(2)%209.13.16PM.png?generation=1756021014437025&alt=media"" width=""400""/>

For each **Document-Level Ranking** and **Chunk-Level Ranking** task, you are required to **optimize a system prompt** to rank as follows:
1.	**Document-Level Ranking** ¨C Given a financial question, systems must rank candidate document types (10-K, 10-Q, 8-K, DEF-14A, earnings transcripts) by their likelihood of containing the adequate information for answering the question.
2.	**Chunk-Level Ranking** ¨C Given a single document, systems must identify and rank the most relevant passages within the provided document, returning a ranked list of candidate chunks that contain the information needed for answering the question.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Task Evaluation![task_evaluation](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7863652%2F6e5405c50f64d9f28f6be300dae18f23%2FTasks-4(2)%209.13.22PM.png?generation=1756021154576940&alt=media)

The challenge consists of two sequential retrieval tasks. In the document-type ranking task, participants must rank all candidate document types based on their relevance to the input question. In the chunk-level ranking task, given the selected document, participants must rank the top 5 most relevant paragraph-level passages that contain the information necessary for answering the question.

Both tasks will be evaluated using standard ranking metrics, averaged across runs: **MRR@5** (Mean Reciprocal Rank), **MAP@5** (Mean Average Precision), and **nDCG@5** (Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain). The leaderboard will display your **MAP@5** score.


## Submission File


For every **sample_id** in the test set, you are required to predict the probability of the **TARGET document or passage index** in the example. The submission file should contain two columns: **sample_id** and **target_index**.

Each **sample_id** should be associated with the top 5 **target_index** predictions, ordered by probability, with the most probable context listed first.

The submission file must include a header and follow this format:

```
sample_id, target_index
```

You are required to **submit a single `CSV` file** that consolidates the results from all datasets.

```
sample_id,target_index
chunk_q613509,92
chunk_q613509,91
chunk_q613509,9
chunk_q613509,94
chunk_q613509,104
    etc..
```","# Dataset Description

## Overview  

This competition is designed to evaluate *agentic (multi-step) retrieval* capabilities in financial AI. The central goal is to identify grounded evidence from large-scale SEC filings in order to answer complex institutional finance questions. To this end, participants are tasked to ***optimize a system prompt that maximize the ranking performance***. Unlike traditional retrieval tasks, this challenge is framed as a two-stage process.

In the first stage, **Document-Level Ranking**, participants must determine which type of SEC filing¡ªsuch as 10-K, 10-Q, 8-K, DEF 14A, or an Earnings Transcript¡ªis most likely to contain the relevant information for a given query. In the second stage, **Chunk-Level Ranking**, the task shifts to locating the most relevant passages within the selected document, with participants required to rank the Top-5 chunks that best support the answer.  

The dataset is drawn from expert-curated examples based on real SEC filings from the period 2023¨C2024. This ensures that questions and contexts are realistic and grounded in actual financial disclosure practices.  

All results must be submitted as a single consolidated **CSV file** that combines outputs from both retrieval tasks. Detailed specifications for the submission format are provided in a separate section.  

To ensure reproducibility, every submission must include a working notebook that can be executed in the **Databricks Free Edition** environment. The notebook link should be posted in the competition¡¯s discussion tab as the standardized evaluation interface. For participants interested in enhanced functionality, optional integration with the **Upstage Solar LLM API** is also supported.  

***Notice: The dataset for competition will be accessable at the start of the competition.***

## Files

*   **document_ranking_kaggle_dev.jsonl** - the development set of document ranking task.
*   **chunk_ranking_kaggle_dev.jsonl** - the development set of chunk ranking task.

*   **document_ranking_kaggle_eval.jsonl** - the development set of document ranking task. (containing 200 samples)
*   **chunk_ranking_kaggle_eval.jsonl** - the development set of chunk ranking task. (containing 200 samples)

## Columns - Document Ranking Task

The following columns are provided for the **document-level ranking** dataset. This task requires ranking entire SEC filing types by their relative likelihood of containing the answer to a given financial question.

- `_id`: An unique identifier for each sample.

- `messages`: A JSON-style field containing the complete **user prompt**. This includes the instruction, the financial question, and the list of candidate document types to be ranked. The prompt specifies that the output must be a list of indices (e.g., `[4, 2, 1, 0, 3]`), covering all document types in ranked order.  

- `qrel`: A dictionary mapping document indices (`0` ¡­ `N-1`) to integer scores between 0 and 4, matches roughly with the **relative importance of each document type**.  
  - `4` = most relevant document type  
  - `3` = next most relevant  
  - `2` = moderately relevant  
  - `1` = weakly relevant  
  - `0` = least relevant document type  
  - In other words, every candidate document type receives a unique score from 0¨C4, reflecting its position in the ranking.  
  - **Important**: *qrel values are provided only in the development/training set. For the official test set, `qrel` is hidden and participants must predict the ranking themselves.*  

These fields define the input (prompt + candidate document types), the relative ground-truth ranking used in development, and the contextual metadata needed to interpret each example.

## Columns - Chunk Ranking Task

The following columns are provided for the **chunk-level ranking** dataset. This task requires ranking paragraph-level chunks within a single SEC filing to identify which passages contain the supporting evidence needed to answer the question.

- `messages`: A JSON-style field containing the full user prompt. This includes a simple instruction, the financial question, and the context of the document or chunks to be ranked.  
- `qrel`: A dictionary mapping chunk indices (`0` ¡­ `N-1`) to graded relevance labels, following a TREC-style convention.  
  - `0` = non-relevant (negative chunk)  
  - `1` = relevant (positive chunk)  
  - `2` = highly relevant (strong positive chunk)  
  These labels provide a finer-grained supervision signal compared to binary annotation.  
  - **Note**: `qrel` is only included in the development/training set for evaluation and model tuning. In the official test set, `qrel` values are hidden and participants must predict the relevance scores themselves.  

These fields collectively define the input (prompt + candidate chunks), the ground-truth labels used for evaluation in development, and the contextual metadata describing the example.",False,True
9983,sweet-regression-prediction-of-chocolate-sales,"# Overview

Welcome!
----------------------
# Description

# Hello future data scientists!

This week, you have learned how to work with data along the marketing process. You now know how to clean, visualize and analyze data, and how to make predictions.

As a last assignment, you will compete, in teams, against your classmates to see who can create the best regression model.  Your submission must be uploaded via Kaggle and before the deadline.

## Context
You have just been hired at a global e-commerce retailer that sells chocolates called ¡°Chocolates 4 U¡±. Its plan is to expand to further regions in the next year. The company is established globally and has a great data analysis team (hint: it¡¯s you guys!). It¡¯s your task to help the company predict sales for the new regions given known characteristics about these regions and planned marketing spending and resulting audience reach based on GRPs.

## About the company
Chocolates 4 U is a global retailer of chocolates. The company has been in business for 10 years, with steady growth each year. Chocolates 4 U operates in various regions world wide.

## Task
In your study group, set up a regression model to predict sales in the regions Chocolates 4 U wants to expand to. Train your data with the given training data set: (1) find the best regression model for the training data. (2) use this model to predict sales for the new regions, i.e. apply the model to the test data set.

Note: There is no need to test assumptions. It¡¯s ¡°just¡± about finding the relevant predictors and specify their relationship with regional sales.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Overview

Your models will be evaluated based on how well they fit the hidden test data. The best fitting model will win a 100% on the assignment. All other submissions are graded relative to this.

Remember that you will likely have to run through the data many times and try out different variations of your model to find the best one. Good luck!

## Evaluation Metric 

The evaluation metric for this competition is the MAE (mean absolute error). The group that comes up with an MAE closest to zero wins this competition.

## Submission Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

`Id,Expected`
`134,750220`

You can find an exemplary submission file in the ""Data"" section.

**Note:**

For your submission file it is important that the column names are specified exactly as in this example.","# Dataset Description

The dataset includes regional sales of the global chocolate retailer Chocholates 4 U. It also contains information on marketing campaigns, as well as regional specifics, and some details about the customers (aggregated to the region). The data is already split into training and test dataset.

## File descriptions

- **data_training.csv** - the training set
- **data_test.csv** - the test set
- **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Data fields

- **Id** - Region the chocolate company is operating in

**Marketing Campaigns (noted in number of GRPs per region)**

**Special note on GRPs**: A GRP is a Gross Rating Point, a standard measure of media delivery in advertising. One GRP is equivalent to reaching 1% of the total potential audience with one ad. Two GRPs is the equivalent of reaching 1% of the audience twice or 2% of the audience once, and so forth. One exposure in any medium has the same value as one exposure in any other medium. This standardization means that GRPs can be added across media types to produce one aggregate measure of the impact of a given media campaign. Definition from: https://www.ipsos.com/en/ipsos-encyclopedia-gross-rating-point

- **Web_GRP** - Audience reached via web campaigns
- **TV_GRP** - Audience reached via TV campaigns
- **Facebook_GRP** - Audience reached via Facebook campaigns
- **Tone_of_Ad** - Tone of advertisement (""emotional"", ""serious"", ""funny"")
- **No_of_Web_Banners** - Number of web banners used on the home page

**Regional Specifics**

- **Weather** - The general weather in the region (i.e., ""sunny"", ""cloudy"", ""rainy"")
- **Avg_Temperature** - Average high temperature in the region in degrees celsius
- **No_of_Rabbits** - Number of rabbits in region, in thousands
- **Network_Five_G** - Existence of 5G network (0 = no, 1 = yes)
- **No_of_iPhone_14_Sold** - Number of iPhone 14 sold per year in region, in thousands
- **No_of_Big_Cities** - Number of big cities in region
- **Health_Index** - Health index of region (high values indicate high health awareness)
- **Sustainability_Index** - Sustainability index of region (high values indicate high sustainability)
- **Choc_Capital_Distance** - Distance to Brussels, Belgium, the ""Chocolate Capital of the World"", in km
- **No_of_Competitors** - Number of competitors operating in region (only big companies included)
- **Import_Regulations** - Existence of import regulations for region (0 = no, 1 = yes)
- **Time_in_Region** - Time the chocholate company is operating in that region in months

**Customers**

- **Percent_Internet_Access** - Percent of population in region that has access to the internet
- **Percent_Uni_Degrees** - Percent of population in region with a university degree
- **Percent_Unemployed** - Unemployment rate in region
- **Gender** - Gender (male = 0, female = 1)
- **Coffee_Consumption** - Coffee consumption in region (i.e., ""low"", ""medium"", ""high"")
- **Avg_No_of_Cust_Complaints** - Average number of customer complaints in year in region
- **Avg_Customer_Age** - Average age of customers in years",False,True
9984,t-2-ml-2025,"# Overview

En esta competencia, los estudiantes deber¨¢n dise?ar y entrenar su propia red neuronal convolucional (CNN) desde cero, tomando decisiones clave como el n¨²mero de capas, filtros, neuronas y funciones de activaci¨®n. El objetivo ser¨¢ resolver un problema de clasificaci¨®n de im¨¢genes enfocado en el reconocimiento de emociones faciales, distinguiendo entre las categor¨ªas: anger, disgust, fear, happy, neutral, sadness y surprise. Para mejorar el rendimiento, los estudiantes deber¨¢n experimentar con hiperpar¨¢metros y aplicar t¨¦cnicas de regularizaci¨®n, buscando el mejor equilibrio entre precisi¨®n y generalizaci¨®n.

![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/61845f7929f5aa517ebab941/664d6fec8ce56bb678b9d215_Facial%20emotion%20recognition.jpg)
----------------------
# Description

## Tarea
La tarea es construir su propia red neuronal convolucional para resolver un problema de reconocimiento de emociones a partir de im¨¢genes.

![](https://static0.colliderimages.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/michaelscott_theoffice-(1).jpg?q=50&fit=crop&w=1100&h=618&dpr=1.5)

## Instrucciones
- La tarea es individual
- Entrega mediante Kaggle
- Redes similares pueden ser consideradas copia

## Objetivo del Desaf¨ªo
El objetivo es que experimenten con diferentes configuraciones de redes neuronales, buscando la mejor configuraci¨®n que maximice el Accuracy en las im¨¢genes del conjunto de prueba.

Deben probar con (todas) las siguientes parametrizaciones:

- El n¨²mero de capas.
- La cantidad de neuronas en cada capa.
- Las funciones de activaci¨®n.
- El optimizador y la funci¨®n de p¨¦rdida.


## Env¨ªo de Predicciones
- Debe realizar al menos 30 env¨ªos en lo que dura la competencia.
- Tiene como m¨¢ximo 10 env¨ªos por d¨ªa. En caso de no cumplir con estos dos requerimientos, la calificaci¨®n de la competencia ser¨¢ cero.
- Documentaci¨®n en Jupyter Notebook
- Todo el proceso de creaci¨®n, entrenamiento y ajuste de la red neuronal debe ser documentado en un Jupyter Notebook de manera clara y ordenada. Justifiqu¨¦ bien las decisiones tomadas.

Aseg¨²rense de que el notebook est¨¦ bien estructurado, con secciones claramente diferenciadas. Una sugerencia de estructura podr¨ªa ser:

- Investigaci¨®n sobre **Emotion recognition**
- Carga de datos: Explicaci¨®n y visualizaci¨®n del dataset.
- Preprocesamiento de datos: T¨¦cnicas utilizadas para preparar los datos.
- Definici¨®n del modelo: Descripci¨®n de las capas, neuronas, y funciones de activaci¨®n escogidas, etc.
- Entrenamiento del modelo: Mostrar los resultados del entrenamiento, como las curvas de p¨¦rdida y precisi¨®n.
- Evaluaci¨®n del modelo creado: An¨¢lisis de los resultados y de las m¨¦tricas de desempe?o.
- El modelo final que los estudiantes dise?en y parametrizen (la CNN) deber¨¢ compararse con al menos otro modelo de Machine Learning como SVM, Random Forest o XGBoost, utilizando m¨¦tricas est¨¢ndar de clasificaci¨®n como matriz de confusi¨®n, precisi¨®n, recall y accuracy.
- Conclusi¨®n que detalle sobre la elecci¨®n de los par¨¢metros escogidos y la comparaci¨®n entre el modelo creado por el estudiante y el modelo cl¨¢sico de ML.

> ?? El c¨®digo debe estar acompa?ado de comentarios explicativos para facilitar la comprensi¨®n de las decisiones tomadas en cada paso.
?? Utilicen gr¨¢ficos y visualizaciones donde sea necesario para mostrar la evoluci¨®n del entrenamiento y los resultados.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La m¨¦trica de evaluaci¨®n para esta competencia es la **Precisi¨®n (Accuracy)**.  

El **Accuracy** se calcula como:

$$
\text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{N¨²mero de predicciones correctas}}{\text{N¨²mero total de predicciones}}
$$


Para el conjunto de testing entregado en el dataset, se debe predecir un valor para la variable `class`.  
El archivo de env¨ªo debe contener un encabezado y seguir el formato:

**new_id,clase**
1.jpg,surprise
2.jpg,fear
3.jpg,anger
4.jpg,neutral

---","# Dataset Description

Este conjunto de datos contiene im¨¢genes faciales etiquetadas con siete emociones: anger, disgust, fear, happy, neutral, sadness y surprise.

Fue recopilado a partir de diversas fuentes p¨²blicas y curado para ayudar a estudiantes y practicantes de IA a practicar el reconocimiento de emociones. Las im¨¢genes est¨¢n organizadas en conjuntos de entrenamiento y prueba con una divisi¨®n 80/20. Cada imagen ha sido anonimizada con identificadores gen¨¦ricos para facilitar los experimentos de aprendizaje autom¨¢tico.

## Columnas:

- train.csv: id (nombre del archivo de la imagen), label (emoci¨®n)

- test.csv: id (nombre del archivo de la imagen; las etiquetas est¨¢n ocultas para predicci¨®n)",False,True
