    id,Depression
    140700,0
    140701,0
    140702,1
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - August 28, 2025
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  Sep 7, 2025

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!",,False,True
9995,soil-carbon-g-kg-prediction-spacetime,"# Overview

Soil Organic Carbon (SOC) plays a key role in soil health, agricultural productivity, and climate regulation. Accurate SOC estimation across space and time is essential for monitoring ecosystems and supporting sustainable land management practices.

In this competition, your goal is to **predict Soil Organic Carbon (SOC) levels across Europe** using a unique combination of ground-truth measurements and environmental covariates. The dataset integrates thousands of SOC observations collected between 2017¨C2024 from multiple national and international monitoring projects, enriched with both:

- **Traditional predictors** such as vegetation indices (NDVI), precipitation, land cover, etc.
- **Google Earth Engine embeddings** ¡ª 64-dimensional feature vectors derived from Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2, and Landsat imagery, capturing rich spatial and temporal information at 10-meter resolution.

We¡¯re especially excited to see how participants creatively leverage the embeddings alongside traditional predictors. By combining classic environmental covariates with cutting-edge embeddings, you¡¯ll help push forward the science of large-scale SOC prediction.

----------------------
# Description

Soil Organic Carbon (SOC) is a cornerstone of healthy ecosystems. It influences soil fertility, water retention, and biodiversity, while also acting as one of the largest terrestrial carbon sinks. Monitoring SOC is therefore crucial for agriculture, land management, and climate change mitigation. However, SOC is highly variable in space and time, making accurate prediction a challenging task.

This competition builds on a **unique, pan-European dataset** of SOC ground-truth measurements collected between 2017¨C2024. The dataset brings together observations from multiple monitoring initiatives, including:

- **[BBDD-COS](https://www.miteco.gob.es/es/biodiversidad/servicios/banco-datos-naturaleza/informacion-disponible/cos-descargas.html) (Spain)**
- **[NABODAT](https://ccsols.ch/de/datenmanagement/bereitstellung-bodeninformationen/swiss-soil-dataset/) (Switzerland)**
- **[LUCAS](https://esdac.jrc.ec.europa.eu/projects/lucas) (EU-wide survey)**
- **[BZE-LW](https://www.thuenen.de/en/institutes/climate-smart-agriculture/projects/agricultural-soil-inventory-bze-lw) (Germany)**
- **[GLanCE](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-023-02798-5) (Global)**

Each SOC measurement is paired with a rich set of **environmental covariates**, carefully pre-processed and aligned to the sampling points. These covariates fall into two main groups:

- **Traditional predictors**: NDVI, precipitation, land cover, plant functional types, soil properties, and other variables that reflect key environmental processes.  See the ¡°Predictive Features¡± section [here](https://peerj.com/articles/19605/#material-and-methodology) for full details.
- **Google Earth Engine embeddings**: 64-dimensional feature vectors developed by DeepMind¡¯s AlphaEarth Foundations, available at [GEE](https://developers.google.com/earth-engine/datasets/catalog/GOOGLE_SATELLITE_EMBEDDING_V1_ANNUAL). These embeddings encode spectral-textural patterns from Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2, and Landsat data at 10-meter resolution, capturing seasonal and spatial context in a compact form.  A user-friendly [guide](https://book.ml4rs.com/alpha-earth-foundations.html) describing the predictors, their production, and practical usage is attached.

The embeddings are a **new type of input** that go beyond traditional remote sensing indices. We encourage participants to experiment with creative ways of using them, whether as direct model inputs, in combination with engineered features, or through dimensionality reduction and representation learning.  

Your task is to build models that predict SOC across Europe, accounting for soil depth, spatial heterogeneity, and the skewed distribution of SOC values. The challenge is not only to achieve strong predictive performance, but also to **advance methodological innovation in how we combine traditional environmental covariates with modern machine-learned embeddings**.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Your submissions will be evaluated on how well they predict SOC values across Europe. Because SOC values are highly skewed, the primary metric will be computed on **log-transformed SOC values**.

### Primary Metric
- **Concordance Correlation Coefficient (CCC)** on log-transformed SOC values.  
  This metric captures both accuracy and precision, ensuring predictions are not only close to the true values but also consistent in their variability.

### Secondary Metrics  
*(only considered if the primary metric is very close between teams; these will be checked manually)*  
- **Bias**: mean difference between predicted and observed SOC (log-scale).  
- **Organic soil detection**: precision and recall for identifying samples with SOC > 300.  

These secondary checks help ensure models are robust across the entire SOC range, especially for organic soils that are more difficult to capture.

---

### Submission Format
For each record in the validation set, you must predict the SOC value.  
Your submission file should be a **CSV** with the following columns:

- `hackathon_id` ¨C the unique identifier provided in the dataset. This has to be the first columns.
- `soc` ¨C your model¡¯s predicted SOC value (**not log-transformed**).  

So your plein csv file will look like something like this:
```
hackathon_id,soc
12464,37.84196084734241
...
4649,17.86305316164712
```","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Description

This competition provides ground-truth measurements of **Soil Organic Carbon (SOC)** collected across Europe between 2017¨C2024, along with a rich set of environmental covariates.  Your task is to use the training data to build models that predict SOC for the test set.

![Spatial data coverage](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F28576796%2F2f3d5a3a13bef93cfc743b41b0ab9e96%2FScreenshot%20from%202025-08-28%2010-54-39.png?generation=1756371335748344&alt=media)

---

### Files

- **`OGHSummerSchool2025_train_dataset.csv`** ¨C the training set, containing 20,655 SOC measurements with corresponding features.  
- **`OGHSummerSchool2025_solution_public.csv`** ¨C the test set, containing 2,576 samples where you must predict SOC.  

---

### Columns

All files share the same column structure, with the exception that the **target column `soc` is only available in the training set**.  

- **`hackathon_id`** ¨C unique identifier for each sample (used for submission).  
- **`site_id`** ¨C identifier of the measurement site.  
- **`dataset_id`** ¨C identifier for the source dataset (e.g., BBDD-COS, NABODAT, LUCAS, BZE-LW, GLanCE).  
- **`hzn_dep`** ¨C soil horizon depth of the sample (important covariate).  
- **`soc`** ¨C measured Soil Organic Carbon (target variable, training set only).  

**Features** fall into two groups:  
- **`A00` to `A63`** ¨C 64-dimensional Google Earth Engine embeddings. Values are scaled to [-1, +1].  
- **Environmental covariates** ¨C the rest columns are traditional predictors such as NDVI, precipitation, land cover, and plant functional types. 

--- 

### Vanilla model

Here is a simple model, without any tuning. Feel free to outperform this!

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F28576796%2F2f90580bd8d3abf50f669420c5d0da52%2Fnaive_model.png?generation=1756736760641592&alt=media)

---

### What You Are Predicting
You will predict using the `OGHSummerSchool2025_solution_public.csv`.
For each row in the test set, you must predict the **SOC value** (`soc`) in its original (non-log-transformed) scale.  The competition platform will automatically apply a log transformation when calculating metrics.  This adjustment is necessary because the SOC distribution is highly skewed, with most values concentrated at the lower end.
![Skewed distribution](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F28576796%2Fb1539fc05e0ba49dd00b74648a723172%2FScreenshot%20from%202025-08-28%2010-55-22.png?generation=1756371464901849&alt=media)



---

### Submission Reminder
Your submission file must contain:  
- `hackathon_id`  
- `soc` (predicted SOC value, not log-transformed)",False,True
9996,sem-eval-2026-task-13-subtask-a,"# Overview

In this subtask, participants are asked to identify **whether a given code snippet is (i) fully human-written or (ii) fully machine-generated**. While the task is a binary classification problem, it is designed with a strong focus on out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization.

**Motivation**

Our previous studies ([study 1](https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.550/), [study 2](https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.1593/)) highlight a major limitations of existing AI-content detectors: they often break down in OOD conditions. This task is designed to directly address that gap.
----------------------
# Description

**Challenge Design**
To rigorously test generalization, our evaluation spans two axes: programming languages and domains.
* Seen languages (training): C++, Python, Java
* Unseen languages (test): Go, PHP, C#, C, JavaScript
* Seen domain: Algorithmic code
* Unseen domains: Research code and generic deployed code

**Evaluation Settings**
The test set covers four complementary scenarios:
* Seen languages, seen domains ¨C test code matches training distribution.
* Unseen languages, seen domains ¨C new languages, but familiar domains.
* Seen languages, unseen domains ¨C known languages, but different domains.
* Unseen languages and unseen domains ¨C both language and domain differ, requiring robust generalization.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Submission Format**

Submit a .csv file with two columns:

* ***ID***: unique identifier of the code snippet
* ***label***: the label ID (not the string label)

**Evaluation measure**
The primary evaluation measure is macro F1-score",,False,True
9997,canopy-height-mapping-using-google-aef-embeddings,"# Overview

Hackathon hosted by the OpenGeoHub Foundation as part of the 2025 OpenGeoHub Summer School, designed to explore the potential of cutting-edge remote sensing embeddings for forest monitoring. This challenge invites students, early-career researchers, and GIS/EO practitioners to develop machine learning models that predict forest canopy height from Google DeepMind¡¯s AlphaEarth Foundations (AEF) embeddings, derived from Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2, and Landsat imagery.

Participants will compete to benchmark the accuracy and generalizability of AEF embeddings against GEDI-derived canopy height measurements, gaining hands-on experience with real-world data, open-source tools, and environmental AI workflows.

The winning team will present their results at the 2025 OpenGeoHub Summer School¡ªa non-profit event bringing together developers, scientists, and institutions committed to open environmental data and software. The 2025 edition is co-organized with EO Council, Netherlands Space Office, and SURF, and includes live demos, workshops, panel discussions, and social gatherings.

Hackathons are supported by the EU-funded Open-Earth-Monitor project, under the Horizon Europe research and innovation programme (grant agreement No. 101059548).
----------------------
# Description

The hackathon dataset contains ~50.000 quality screened GEDI L2A shots distributed across southern Netherlands and northern Belgium. For each 25m footprint we provide a single response, *rh98*, in centimeters (cm). The data extraction and stratification are documented and presented in the lecture [***Introduction of cloud-native vector format: hands-on in Python environment  by Yu-Feng Ho***](https://github.com/yu-feng-ho/OGH2025).The acronym *rh* stands for relative height and measures the height difference between the detected ground return and the point at which 98% (hence *rh98*) of the cumulative lidar-waveform energy is reached. This metric is widely used as a proxy for dominant tree height because it captures the upper canopy while trimming most noise.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F22197053%2Fa891bcaa2d715e8c2bd09dbe823ac01a%2Fphoto_5872729262128220782_y.jpg?generation=1756384822427141&alt=media) *Fig 1: Distribution of GEDI Sample points in Benelux*


![
](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F22197053%2F422cc7d890580facdff853315f786a7e%2Fphoto_5872729262128220781_y.jpg?generation=1756384559077736&alt=media) *Fig 2: Vanilla model CHM over Wageningen*

The predictor variables you will be using to measure the canopy height are the 64 Google DeepMind AlphaEarth Foundations (AEF) remote-sensing embeddings; values are in the [-1, +1] interval. They capture multi-season Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2 + Landsat spectral-texture context at a spatial resolution of 10m. Attached is a [user-friendly](https://book.ml4rs.com/alpha-earth-foundations.html) description of the predictors, their production and usage. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

The result will be evaluated by Root Mean Square Error (RMSE). This formula calculates the square root of the average of the squared differences between the predicted values *?* and the actual values *y*, providing a measure of the differences in the units of the response variable.

$$
\text{RMSE}(y, \hat{y}) = \sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i=0}^{N-1} (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2}{N}}
$$","# Dataset Description

The hackathon dataset contains ~50.000 quality screened GEDI L2A shots distributed across southern Netherlands and northern Belgium. For each 25m footprint we provide a single response, **rh98, in centimeters (cm)**. 

## Files


### train.csv

The training set contains ~39,500 points collected over southern Netherlands and northern Belgium, where each sample includes a single response variable, spatial coordinates (see below) and predictors.

### test.csv

the test set contains ~17,000 of the whole 56k points overlay and includes points available for internal validation of your models. It will help you fine-tune and assess the accuracy of your predictions during the development phase. In additional, the hidden dataset will be used that represents the final test of your model's effectiveness in real-world scenarios. Your goal is to predict these hidden values accurately. Height values for the hidden set come from trees surveyed in the field with traditional methods. Field-measured dominant heights are typically 1¨C2 m higher than GEDI RH98. If you apply a bias / linear offset to compensate, please:
- document the method (e.g. constant shift, regression, eco-type specific, ¡­);
- state the exact offset value(s);
- cite the evidence or rationale you used.

Include this note with your submission.

## Columns

*   `rh98` - height difference between the detected ground return and the point at which 98%of the cumulative lidar-waveform energy is reached
*   `lon` - longtitude of the sample point in EPSG:4326
*   `lat`?- latitude of the sample point in EPSG:4326
*   `cv_{x}_google.embedding_nl` - value of google embedding vector number *x*, from 1 - 64",False,True
9998,banana,"# Overview

Go Bananas! For 48 hours, starting on September 6, 2025, we are unlocking a **special tier of the Gemini API**, putting Gemini 2.5 Flash Image Preview (aka Nano Banana), our state-of-the-art image model, into your hands.

In collaboration with Fal and ElevenLabs, we're giving over $400,000 of prizes! We're looking forward to seeing what you build in the next two days!

Nano Banana is about dynamic creation. Edit with words, blend realities, and access Gemini's unique world knowledge. We challenge you to unleash this power. Show us an application that transforms how we interact with visuals and deliver something that wasn't possible before.


----------------------
# Description

**The Challenge:** Your mission is to build a product, application, or demo that showcases one or more of these core strengths. Think beyond simple text-to-image. How can these advanced features create a magical user experience?

Consider applications that:
- Enhance dynamic storytelling (e.g., consistent character comics).
- Revolutionize e-commerce (e.g., virtual room placement or product visualization).
- Automate creative workflows (e.g., personalized marketing assets at scale).
- Build the next generation of natural language photo editors.

**Accelerate Your Build: Leverage AI Studio Apps** 

We encourage participants to leverage AI Studio apps. In the [Build tab of AI Studio](https://aistudio.google.com/apps), you can easily and quickly build advanced applications with the Gemini API, allowing you to move rapidly from idea to function prototype. AI Studio apps can quickly be re-mixed, saved to GitHub or deploy to Cloud Run. You can also find existing templates to get started.

**API Limitations** 

Please note the special tier for the Gemini API will allow **20 images per minute and 500 requests per project per day**. Developers using a paid API key will pay for all their usage. To get the free 500 generations, please use a free tier API key.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Show, Don't Just Tell. Your project will be judged primarily on your video demo. This is your chance to create something exciting and compelling. We want to see the ""wow"" factor, the utility of your application, and the technical skill behind it.

## **Submission Requirements:**
Your complete submission via the Kaggle platform **must** include the following:

1) **The Video Demo** (2 minutes or less): An engaging video demonstrating your project in action. It must be posted publicly (YouTube, X/Twitter, etc.) and a direct link provided. It must be viewable by the judges without requiring a login.

2) **Public Project Link**:  A URL to your working product or interactive demo. Consider using AI Studio apps, which are the fastest way to build prototypes using the Gemini API. This allows judges to experience your project firsthand, if applicable. It should be publicly accessible and not require a login or paywall. If a live demo is not feasible, a link to your public code repository (e.g., GitHub) is required, including detailed setup instructions.

3) **Gemini Integration Writeup:** A brief write-up (max 200 words) detailing which Gemini 2.5 Flash Image features were used and how they are central to the application.

## **Judging Criteria:**

* **Innovation and ""Wow"" Factor (40%):**  How creative and novel is the application? Does it leverage Gemini 2.5 Flash Image in a way that wasn't previously possible?

* **Technical Execution and Functionality (30%):**  Does the application work? How effectively does it utilize the API's advanced features (consistency, fusion, editing)?

* **Potential Impact and Utility (20%):** Does the application solve a real-world problem (creative, commercial, or educational)?

* **Presentation Quality (10%):** Clarity, storytelling, and engagement of the video demo.

For the **Special Technology Prizes**, these same criteria will be applied with a focus on how that specific technology (ElevenLabs and Fal) was pivotal in achieving the project's impact and technical excellence.


