4. Please try this hack without any external help.
----------------------
# Description

 - Try yourself!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Wrapper for https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.r2_score.html
:math:R^2 (coefficient of determination) regression score function.




Best possible score is 1.0 and it can be negative (because the
model can be arbitrarily worse). In the general case when the true y is
non-constant, a constant model that always predicts the average y
disregarding the input features would get a :math:`R^2` score of 0.0.

In the particular case when ``y_true`` is constant, the :math:`R^2` score
is not finite: it is either ``NaN`` (perfect predictions) or ``-Inf``
(imperfect predictions). To prevent such non-finite numbers to pollute
higher-level experiments such as a grid search cross-validation, by default
these cases are replaced with 1.0 (perfect predictions) or 0.0 (imperfect
predictions) respectively. You can set ``force_finite`` to ``False`` to
prevent this fix from happening.

Note: when the prediction residuals have zero mean, the :math:`R^2` score
is identical to the
`Explained Variance score <explained_variance_score>`.

Parameters
----------
solution : DataFrame of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_outputs)
Ground truth (correct) target values.

submission : DataFrame of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_outputs)
Estimated target values.

weights_column_name: optional str, the name of the sample weights column in the solution file.

<https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coefficient_of_determination>`","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `target` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the `yield` given the other features
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10000,cloned-competition,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2025 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** Your goal is to predict how many calories were burned during a workout.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error**.

The **RMSLE** is calculated as:
$$
\textrm{RMSLE} =   \left( \frac{1}{n} \sum\_{i=1}^n \left(\log (1 + \widehat{y}\_i) - \log (1 + y\_i)\right) \right)^{\frac{1}{2}}
$$
where:
- \\(n\\) is the total number of observations in the test set,
- \\(\widehat{y}\_i\\) is the predicted value of the target for instance \(i\),
- \\(y\_i\\) is the actual value of the target for instance \(i\), and,
- \\(\log\\) is the natural logarithm.

<script type=""text/x-mathjax-config"">
MathJax.Hub.Register.StartupHook(""End Jax"",function () {
  var BROWSER = MathJax.Hub.Browser;
  var jax = ""SVG"";
  return MathJax.Hub.setRenderer(jax);
});
</script>

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict the continuous target, `Calories`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Calories
    750000,93.2
    750001,27.42
    750002,103.8
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - ${competition.DateEnabled}
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  ${competition.Deadline}

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!",,False,True
10001,oemc-hackathon-global-precipitation-modeling,"# Overview

This hackathon focuses on developing a fused global precipitation product at 10 km spatial resolution for the period 2000¨C2016. Participants will integrate three complementary monthly precipitation datasets, ERA5 (10 km), CHELSA (1 km), and IMERG (total accumulation, liquid accumulation, liquid rate at 10 km), to generate robust estimates of mean monthly precipitation. The training framework will leverage the GSOD station network (~15,000 stations), incorporating predictors such as ERA5, CHELSA, IMERG components, and auxiliary variables (minimum/maximum geometric temperature).

**Goal:** Develop a spacetime machine-learning model that fuses global datasets to predict monthly precipitation (2000¨C2016), validated against station time series using coefficient of determination (R2) in log-scale **(log(1 + PRCP))**.
----------------------
# Description

## Description  

Precipitation is a key driver of the global water and energy cycle, shaping ecosystems, agriculture, and climate. Reliable estimates of rainfall are essential for hydrological modeling, drought monitoring, and food security assessments. However, precipitation is highly variable across both space and time, and satellite-based products often disagree, making accurate prediction a challenging task.  

This competition builds on a **global dataset of mean monthly precipitation (2000¨C2016)** that integrates multiple sources of information:  

- **ERA5 (10 km)** ¨C Reanalysis-based monthly precipitation  
- **CHELSA (1 km)** ¨C High-resolution climatological time-series  
- **IMERGE (10 km)** ¨C Satellite-derived precipitation with three components:  
  - *Total rate*  
  - *Liquid rate*  
  - *Ice rate*  
- **Ground stations (~15,000 stations)** ¨C Ground-truth precipitation records for model training and evaluation  

Each station record is paired with a set of predictors, including precipitation estimates from multiple products and auxiliary covariates such as **minimum and maximum geometric temperature**. These predictors form the training dataset for spatio-temporal modeling of precipitation.  

Your task is to build ML models that predict **global monthly precipitation at 10 km resolution**, capturing both spatial heterogeneity and temporal dynamics. The challenge is not only to maximize predictive accuracy, but also to advance **data fusion methodologies** that leverage the strengths of reanalysis, satellite, and station data in a unified framework.  

Submissions will be evaluated using the **coefficient of determination (R2) in log-scale**, ensuring robustness across regions with different rainfall regimes.  

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the coefficient of determination (R2) between the predicted and observed precipitation values, computed on log-transformed precipitation. R2 measures the proportion of variance in the observed data that is explained by the predictions, providing an indication of overall model performance. A higher R2 on the log scale reflects better agreement with station data, particularly across regions with highly skewed precipitation distributions.

## Submission Format  

For each record in the validation set, you must predict the **precipitation value in (log(1 + PRCP)) form, not in real space**.  

Your submission file should be a **CSV** with the following columns:  

- **HACKATHON_ID** ¨C The unique station identifier provided in the dataset.  
- **PRCP_LOG** ¨C Your model¡¯s predicted precipitation value (**log-transformed**).","# Dataset Description

The Global Surface Summary of the Day (GSOD) provides long-term, quality-controlled daily weather observations from thousands of meteorological stations worldwide. For this hackathon, we use GSOD precipitation stations (~15,000 globally) as the ground-truth reference to train and validate precipitation data fusion models. Here is the spatial distribution of the GSOD precipitation stations:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F20289197%2F6ba84c7b34f806711ad7a8cbd0e0144b%2Fstations.png?generation=1756467853563902&alt=media)
## Files  

- **train.csv** ¨C the training set, containing station data overlaid with predictors  
  *(ERA5, CHELSA, IMERGE-total.accum, IMERGE-liquid.accum, IMERGE-liquid.rate, geom-temp-min, geom-temp-max).*  

- **test.csv** ¨C the test set, containing samples where participants must predict **mean monthly precipitation**.  


## Columns  

- **HACKATHON_ID** ¨C Unique station&epoch identifier  
- **DATE** ¨C Date of the observation (YYYY-MM format)  
- **TMIN_GEOM** ¨C Minimum geometric temperature  
- **TMAX_GEOM** ¨C Maximum geometric temperature  
- **PRCP** ¨C  Observed precipitation (target variable in real space [mm])  
- **PRCP_CHELSA** ¨C Monthly precipitation from CHELSA 
- **PRCP_ERA5** ¨C Monthly precipitation from ERA5 
- **PRCP_TOTAL_IMERG** ¨C IMERG total accumulated precipitation 
- **PRCP_LIQUID_IMERG** ¨C IMERG liquid accumulated precipitation 
- **PRCP_ICE_IMERG** ¨C IMERG liquid rate / ice component

## What You Are Predicting
For each row in the test set, you must predict the precipitation value (PRCP_LOG) as the natural logarithm of one plus the true precipitation (log(1 + PRCP)), since precipitation in real space is highly skewed. The competition platform will compute the evaluation metric directly in this log-transformed space.",False,True
10002,heat-code-by-fodse,"# Overview

??? Heat Code by FODSE

Welcome to Heat Code by FODSE!
Your mission is to build a machine learning model that can predict Chennai¡¯s weather for Sunday (10 AM ¨C 9 PM) using the dataset we provide.
This is a 24-hour Challenge ¡ª think fast, code smart, and keep it minimal. Models with fewer lines of code will be given preference when scores are close.

----------------------
# Description

# **Task** 
- Build a model to predict temperature for Sunday from 10 AM to 9 PM.
- Submit predictions in CSV or Excel format along with your notebook.
- Provide your public Kaggle Notebook link used to generate predictions.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated on three metrics:
## **- Mean Absolute Error (MAE): Measures the average absolute error between predicted and actual values. Lower is better.**
## **- Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE): Penalizes larger errors more strongly. Lower is better.**
## **- R2 Score (Coefficient of Determination): Measures how well your predictions explain the variance in actual values. Higher is better (closer to 1).**
The final leaderboard score will be based on a weighted average of these metrics.

---

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


