10042,utec-2025-ii-animal-sound,"# Overview

En este concurso, tu tarea es desarrollar un modelo de inteligencia artificial capaz de identificar m¨²ltiples especies animales presentes en grabaciones de audio capturadas en la selva amaz¨®nica. Cada archivo de audio puede contener m¨²ltiples sonidos, reflejando la complejidad ac¨²stica de estos ecosistemas.

El reconocimiento de sonidos en entornos naturales es una herramienta clave en la conservaci¨®n de la biodiversidad, permitiendo a los investigadores monitorear especies, estudiar sus comportamientos y detectar cambios en el ecosistema a gran escala.

Este desaf¨ªo forma parte del curso ""Deep Learning (CS5364)"", donde los estudiantes tendr¨¢n la oportunidad de aplicar t¨¦cnicas avanzadas de aprendizaje profundo, como la transformaci¨®n de audio en espectrogramas, el uso de redes neuronales convolucionales (CNNs), modelos recurrentes (RNNs) y arquitecturas modernas como Transformers para el an¨¢lisis de se?ales ac¨²sticas.

Ponte a prueba resolviendo un problema del mundo real y contribuye al desarrollo de modelos robustos capaces de reconocer la diversidad sonora de la selva.
----------------------
# Description

En este desaf¨ªo, los participantes deber¨¢n desarrollar modelos de inteligencia artificial capaces de identificar m¨²ltiples especies animales en grabaciones de audio provenientes de la selva amaz¨®nica. Cada archivo de audio puede contener una combinaci¨®n de diferentes sonidos de animales, reflejando la biodiversidad ac¨²stica de este ecosistema ¨²nico.

El reconocimiento automatizado de sonidos en entornos naturales es una herramienta clave en la conservaci¨®n de especies, la detecci¨®n temprana de cambios en la biodiversidad y el monitoreo de h¨¢bitats remotos. La capacidad de distinguir especies a partir de sus vocalizaciones permite a los investigadores realizar estudios ecol¨®gicos de gran alcance sin la necesidad de intervenci¨®n humana directa.

## Base de datos

Se proporcionan dos archivos comprimidos que contienen los datos de la competencia:

1. **`SoundAnimals_Train.zip`**  
   Contiene la carpeta `train/` con archivos de audio y el archivo `train.csv`, que presenta la siguiente estructura:

   - **`filename`**: Nombre del archivo de audio correspondiente.  
   - **Etiquetas (42 columnas restantes)**: Cada columna representa una especie animal espec¨ªfica y contiene un valor binario (0 o 1) indicando su presencia en la grabaci¨®n.

   **Ejemplo de `train.csv`:**

   ```
   filename,SPHSUR,BOABIS,SCIPER,DENNAH,LEPLAT,...
   audio_001.wav,1,0,0,1,0,...
   audio_002.wav,0,1,1,0,1,...
   ```

2. **`SoundAnimals_test.zip`**  
   Contiene ¨²nicamente la carpeta `test/` con los audios que se deber¨¢n analizar. No se proporciona un archivo de etiquetas, ya que los participantes deben generar predicciones sobre estos datos.

## Objetivo del desaf¨ªo

El objetivo del concurso es desarrollar modelos de clasificaci¨®n multi-etiqueta que puedan identificar correctamente los animales presentes en las grabaciones de la selva amaz¨®nica. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

Sus env¨ªos ser¨¢n evaluados utilizando la m¨¦trica de **Macro F1-Score**, una medida ampliamente utilizada en tareas de clasificaci¨®n multi-clase. Esta m¨¦trica calcula el F1-Score de cada clase por separado y luego promedia los valores obtenidos.  

La f¨®rmula de c¨¢lculo para el F1-Score por clase es:

$$
F1 = 2 \times \frac{\text{Precisi¨®n} \times \text{Recall}}{\text{Precisi¨®n} + \text{Recall}}
$$

Donde:  

- **Precisi¨®n:** Proporci¨®n de predicciones correctas entre todas las predicciones realizadas para una clase.  
- **Recall:** Proporci¨®n de instancias de una clase correctamente identificadas entre todas las instancias reales de la clase.

El objetivo del concurso es maximizar la puntuaci¨®n Macro F1-Score, asegurando un rendimiento equilibrado en todas las especies de animales presentes en las grabaciones.


## Formato de env¨ªo

Los participantes deben enviar un archivo CSV con el siguiente formato:

```
Id,Predicted
audio_101,5 7
audio_102,3
audio_103,0
```

Cada fila debe contener:

- **Id:** Nombre del archivo de audio sin la ruta.  
- **Predicted:** Una lista de ¨ªndices de las etiquetas activas en el audio, comenzando la numeraci¨®n desde 1 y separados por espacios.  
  - Si no hay etiquetas activas, se debe colocar `0`.

**Ejemplos de interpretaci¨®n:**

- `audio_101,5 7` ¡ú Indica que las especies en las posiciones 5 y 7 est¨¢n presentes en el audio.  
- `audio_102,3` ¡ú Indica que solo la especie en la posici¨®n 3 est¨¢ presente.  
- `audio_103,0` ¡ú Indica que no se detect¨® ninguna especie en el audio.

Es importante asegurarse de que los valores de la columna `Predicted` reflejen correctamente la presencia de las especies en el formato indicado.

## Evaluaci¨®n

La evaluaci¨®n se realizar¨¢ autom¨¢ticamente en la plataforma Kaggle utilizando el archivo de predicci¨®n enviado por los participantes. Las puntuaciones se mostrar¨¢n en el leaderboard de la competencia, permitiendo comparar el desempe?o de los modelos. 

Para obtener buenos resultados, los participantes pueden probar diferentes t¨¦cnicas de preprocesamiento de audio, arquitecturas de modelos y estrategias de regularizaci¨®n.","# Dataset Description

Se han proporcionado dos archivos comprimidos que contienen los datos necesarios para esta competencia. A continuaci¨®n, se detallan los archivos disponibles y su contenido:

1. **`SoundAnimals_Train.zip`**  
   Contiene los datos de entrenamiento organizados de la siguiente manera:

   - **`train/`**: Carpeta con los archivos de audio en formato WAV. Cada archivo representa una grabaci¨®n de la selva amaz¨®nica y puede contener m¨²ltiples sonidos de animales.  
   - **`train.csv`**: Archivo de etiquetas correspondiente a los audios de entrenamiento. Presenta la siguiente estructura:

     | filename       | SPHSUR | BOABIS | SCIPER | DENNAH | LEPLAT | ... |
     |----------------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|-----|
     | audio_001.wav  | 1      | 0      | 0      | 1      | 0      | ... |
     | audio_002.wav  | 0      | 1      | 1      | 0      | 1      | ... |

     - **`filename`**: Nombre del archivo de audio correspondiente.  
     - **42 columnas restantes**: Cada columna representa una especie animal espec¨ªfica, con valores `0` o `1` indicando la ausencia o presencia de la especie en la grabaci¨®n.

2. **`SoundAnimals_Test.zip`**  
   Contiene los datos de prueba organizados de la siguiente manera:

   - **`test/`**: Carpeta con los archivos de audio en formato WAV. Estos audios no tienen etiquetas, y los participantes deben predecir la presencia de cada especie.  

   **Nota:** No se proporciona un archivo de etiquetas para estos audios. Los participantes deber¨¢n generar un archivo de predicciones para su evaluaci¨®n.


### Formato de los datos

- **Formato de los audios:**  
  - Extensi¨®n: `.wav`  
  - Frecuencia de muestreo: 22.05 kHz  
  - Duraci¨®n promedio: 3 segundos por muestra  
  - N¨²mero de canales: 1 (mono)

- **Formato de predicciones:**  
  Los participantes deben generar un archivo CSV con la siguiente estructura:

  ```
  Id,Predicted
  audio_101,5 7
  audio_102,3
  audio_103,0
  ```

  Donde:

  - **Id:** Nombre del archivo de audio.  
  - **Predicted:** Contiene los ¨ªndices de las etiquetas activas, separados por espacios (la numeraci¨®n de columnas comienza desde 1).  
    - Si no hay ninguna etiqueta activa (todas las columnas con valor 0), se debe colocar el valor `0`.

  **Ejemplos:**
  - Si un audio tiene las especies marcadas en la columna 5 y 7, la predicci¨®n ser¨ªa: `5 7`.  
  - Si ning¨²n animal est¨¢ presente, la predicci¨®n ser¨ªa: `0`.",False,True
10043,mapple-accessories,"# Description

La compa?¨ªa Mapple desea presentar las proyecciones de ventas para el a?o 2026, en particular, para los cuatro modelos de Mypads que se fabricaran en la planta de Austin, TX. Desde esta planta se fabrican todas las unidades distribuidas en la regi¨®n de Norteam¨¦rica (Canad¨¢, USA y M¨¦xico), sin embargo, debido a la escasez de mano de obra que se est¨¢ presentando en la regi¨®n, es necesario que junto con las proyecciones de ventas, se obtenga el plan de contrataci¨®n para los 6 primeros meses del a?o 2026. 
Por esta raz¨®n, la compa?¨ªa Mapple ha contratado a su grupo de trabajo para que, a trav¨¦s de un proyecto de consultor¨ªa, le entreguen una serie de recomendaciones relacionadas con la estimaci¨®n de la demanda y la planeaci¨®n de la mano de obra.  
----------------------
# Evaluaci¨®n

En la base de datos disponible, se encuentran las ventas totales, de cada uno de los cuatro modelos, en cada mes de los ¨²ltimos 7 a?os y con ellos, se busca pronosticar las ventas para el a?o 2026. Es decir, en la primera parte del caso, cada grupo debe suministrar un pron¨®stico para los 12 meses del a?o 2026, de tal forma que se minimice el MAPE promedio, es decir, el promedio aritm¨¦tico de los cuatro MAPEs.

**Submission Format**

Para la primera parte del caso, cada grupo deber¨¢ subir los pron¨®sticos para cada uno de los modelos de Mypad, para los 12 meses del a?o 2026, en una ¨²nica columna. En las primeras 12 filas los deben ir los pron¨®sticos para el modelo Mypad-pro, luego los 12 pron¨®sticos para el modelo Mypad-mini, despu¨¦s los 12 pron¨®sticos para el modelo Mypad-air y finalmente, los 12 pron¨®sticos para el modelo tradicional de Mypad.","# Dataset Description

En el archivo mypaddata.csv usted encontrar¨¢ las demandas mensuales para las cuatro referencias de Mypads para los a?os 2018 al 2024. En el archivo mypadpronostico.csv usted encontrar¨¢ el formato adecuado para reportar sus resultados, en las filas 2 a la 13 deber¨¢ reportar, redondeado al entero m¨¢s cercano, el pron¨®stico para el modelo Mypad-pro, en las filas 14 a la 25, redondeado al entero m¨¢s cercano, el pron¨®stico para el modelo Mypad-mini, en las filas 26 a la 37, redondeado al entero m¨¢s cercano, el pron¨®stico para el modelo Mypad-air y en las filas 38 a la 49, redondeado al entero m¨¢s cercano, el pron¨®stico para el modelo Mypad.

Las ventas para cada uno de los modelos se encuentran en miles de unidades, es decir, en enero del a?o 2018 se vendieron 1.744.000 unidades del MyPad_pro. Su reporte para el a?o 2025 debe seguir este mismo formato, es decir, reportar sus cifras en miles de unidades.",False,True
10044,geoplant-at-paiss,"# Overview

This challenge aims to predict plant species in a given location and time using various possible predictors: satellite images and time series, climatic time series, and other rasterized environmental data: land cover, human footprint, bioclimatic, and soil variables.
----------------------
# Competition Description

**This challenge is all about predicting plant species presence.**
Given GPS coordinates and various predictors, e.g., satellite images, climatic time series, land cover, human footprint, etc., a participant/team must predict a set of species that should grow there. To do so, we provide observation data comprising approximately 5 million Presence-Only (PO) occurrences and around 90 thousand Presence-Absence (PA) survey records. For more info about the data, please see [the Data tab](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/geolifeclef-2025/data).

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1518097%2Fae16d54c966f59fbd6cb7ec6095667fd%2FSDMs.jpg?generation=1738747729368695&alt=media)

----------------------
# Motivation

Predicting the plant species present at a given location is helpful for many biodiversity management and conservation scenarios. It allows for building high-resolution maps of species composition and related biodiversity indicators such as species diversity, endangered species, and invasive species. In scientific ecology, the problem is known as Species Distribution Modelling. Moreover, it could significantly improve the accuracy of species identification tools - such as [Pl@ntNet](https://plantnet.org/) - by reducing the list of candidate species observable at a given site.

More generally, it could facilitate biodiversity inventories by developing location-based recommendation services (e.g., on mobile phones), encouraging citizen scientist observers' involvement, and accelerating the annotation and validation of species observations to produce large, high-quality data sets.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the samples-averaged \\(F\_1\\)-score, which measures an overlap between the predicted and actual set of species present at a given location and time. Each test PA sample \\( i \\) is associated with a set of ground-truth labels \\( Y\_i \\), i.e., the set of plant species (=speciesId). For each sample, the submission must provide a list of labels, i.e., the set of species predicted present \\( \\widehat{Y}\_{i,1}, \\widehat{Y}\_{i,2}, \\dots, {\\widehat{Y}}\_{i,R\_i} \\). The micro \\(F\_1\\)-score is then computed using

\\\[ F\_1 = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum\_{i=1}^N \\frac{\\text{TP}\_i}{\\text{TP}\_i+(\\text{FP}\_i+\\text{FN}\_i)/2} \\\\ \\quad \\text{Where} \\begin{cases} \\text{TP}\_i =\\text{ Number of predicted species truly present, i.e. }|\\widehat{Y}\_i \\cap Y\_i |\\\\ \\text{FP}\_i =\\text{ Number of species predicted but absent, i.e. } |\\widehat{Y}\_i \\setminus Y\_i | \\\\ \\text{FN}\_i =\\text{ Number of species not predicted but present, i.e. } | Y\_i \\setminus \\widehat{Y}\_i |\\\\ \\end{cases} \\\]
----------------------
# Submission Format

For each surveyId in the test set, you must predict a set of species that occur at the given location. The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
surveyId,predictions
1,1 52 10231
2,78 201 1243 1333 2310 4841
...
```
The submission format is a CSV file containing two columns for each sample (row):
*   `surveyId` column containing integers corresponding to the test sample ids, corresponding to unique combinations of patchID and dayOfYear column values.
*   `predictions` column containing space-delimited lists of the predicted species identifiers (column spId in trainingdatasets)

**For each sample (row), the predicted species identifiers must be ordered by increasing the value from left to right. No test sample is empty, and the test set only contains species from the train set.**
----------------------
# Acknowledgement

This project has received funding from the European Union¡¯s Horizon Research and Innovation program under grant agreements No. 101060639 (MAMBO project) and No. 101060693 (GUARDEN project).


<div align=""center"">
  <a href=""https://www.mambo-project.eu/""><img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1518097%2Fc8653071f2bb3fbff9447a03a0c02dc3%2Fmambo_logo%20(1).png?generation=1701781977001035&alt=media"" alt=""MAMBO_logo"" style=""width: 200px; margin-top: 15px; margin-right: 20px;""></a>
  <a href=""https://guarden.org/""><img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1518097%2F5d2e4ae70449c12176fd37926fb5e852%2Fguarden_logo.png?generation=1701781941682202&alt=media"" alt=""GUARDEN_logo"" style=""width: 230px; height: auto; margin-right: 20px;""></a>
  <a href=""https://commission.europa.eu/index_en""><img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1518097%2Fc44b08f2cabdfc04178c475dada7dbe5%2Flogo-ec--en.svg?generation=1701782006107063&alt=media"" alt=""europ_commission_logo"" style=""width: 230px; height: auto; margin-right: 20px;""></a>
</div>",,False,True
10045,nfl-big-data-bowl-2026-prediction,"# Overview

You are tasked to predict NFL player movement during the video frames after the ball is thrown.
----------------------
# Description

*Note: This Big Data Bowl 2026 has two competitions. This competition is the Prediction competition. Learn more about the Analytics competition [here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/nfl-big-data-bowl-2026-analytics).*

The downfield pass is the crown jewel of American sports. When the ball is in the air, anything can happen, like a touchdown, an interception, or a contested catch. The uncertainty and the importance of the outcome of these plays is what helps keep audiences on the edge of its seat. 

The 2026 Big Data Bowl is designed to help the National Football League better understand player movement during the pass play, starting with when the ball is thrown and ending when the ball is either caught or ruled incomplete. For the offensive team, this means focusing on the targeted receiver, whose job is to move towards the ball landing location in order to complete a catch. For the defensive team, who could have several players moving towards the ball, their jobs are to both prevent the offensive player from making a catch, while also going for the ball themselves. This year's Big Data Bowl asks our fans to help track the movement of these players.

In the Prediction Competition of the Big Data Bowl, participants are tasked with predicting player movement with the ball in the air. Specifically, the NFL is sharing data before the ball is thrown (including the Next Gen Stats tracking data), and stopping the play the moment the quarterback releases the ball. In addition to the pre-pass tracking data, we are providing participants with which offensive player was targeted (e.g, the targeted receiver) and the landing location of the pass. 

Using the information above, participants should generate prediction models for player movement during the frames when the ball is in the air. The most accurate algorithms will be those whose output most closely matches the eventual player movement of each player. 

***Competition specifics***

- In the NFL's tracking data, there are 10 frames per second. As a result, if a ball is in the air for 2.5 seconds, there will be 25 frames of location data to predict.
- Quick passes (less than half a second), deflected passes, and throwaway passes are dropped from the competition.
- Evaluation for the training data is based on historical data. Evaluation for the leaderboard is based on data that hasn't happened yet. Specifically, we will be doing a live leaderboard covering the last five weeks of the 2025 NFL season. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the [Root Mean Squared Error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_mean_square_deviation) between the predicted and the observed target. The evaluation metric for this contest can be found [here](https://www.kaggle.com/code/metric/nfl-2025/edit/run/263599139).


$$RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{2N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \left( (x_{true,i} - x_{pred,i})^2 + (y_{true,i} - y_{pred,i})^2 \right)}$$

## Submission File
You must submit to this competition using the provided evaluation API, which ensures that models perform inference on a single play at a time. For each row in the test dataframe you must predict the corresponding x and y values.
----------------------
# Timeline

###This is a forecasting competition with an active training phase and a separate forecasting phase where models will be run against player data from future NFL games.

### Training Timeline:

* **September 25, 2025** - Competition Start

* **November 26, 2025** - Team Merger deadline. This is the last day participants&nbsp;may join or merge teams.

* **November 26, 2025** - Entry deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.

* **December 3, 2025** - Final submission deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.

### Forecasting Timeline:

* **December 4, 2025 - January 5, 2026** - Watch your results play out! The Kaggle Team will refresh the leaderboard throughout the competition at the conclusion of the prior week's NFL games (NFL games are typically played on Thursdays, Sundays, and Mondays). The first week of games scored begins with those played on December 4,  December 7, and December 8. The final scheduled NFL game of the 2025-2026 regular season is Sunday, January 4, 2026.

* **January 6, 2026**&nbsp; - Competition end, results published

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - $25,000
* 2nd Place - $15,000
* 3rd Place - $10,000

----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met: 
- CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time 
- GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time 
- Internet access disabled 
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models 


Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.",,True,True
10046,nfl-big-data-bowl-2026-analytics,"# Overview

You are tasked to create a metric or video to analyze NFL player movement during the video frames after the ball is thrown.
----------------------
# Description

*Note: This Big Data Bowl 2026 has two competitions. This competition is the Analytics competition. Learn more about the Prediction competition [here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/nfl-big-data-bowl-2026-prediction).*

The downfield pass is the crown jewel of American sports. When the ball is in the air, anything can happen, like a touchdown, an interception, or a contested catch. The uncertainty and the importance of the outcome of these plays is what helps keep audiences on the edge of its seat. 

The 2026 Big Data Bowl is designed to help the National Football League better understand player movement during the pass play, starting with when the ball is thrown and ending when the ball is either caught or ruled incomplete. For the offensive team, this means focusing on the targeted receiver, whose job is to move towards the ball landing location in order to complete a catch. For the defensive team, who could have several players moving towards the ball, their jobs are to both prevent the offensive player from making a catch, while also going for the ball themselves. This year's Big Data Bowl asks our fans to help track the movement of these players.

In the Analytics Competition of the Big Data Bowl, participants are tasked with describing player movement with the ball in the air in a way that is accessible to coaches and football fans. This could be making a new metric or in the comparison of team or player strategy. As is often the case in Big Data Bowl analytics competitions,  participants are encouraged to focus on one small aspect of the play, as opposed to trying to capture every aspect of every play. This could include the change of direction of a defensive player, the acceleration of the receiver, or the way that defensive schemes free players up to make plays. 
----------------------
# Timeline

* **September 25, 2025** - Start Date. 
* **December 17, 2025** - Final Submission Deadline. 
* **December 18, 2025 - January 19, 2025** - Judging Period*
* **January 20, 2026**  - Anticipated Results Announcement.

*Note - Judging period subject to change based on the number of submissions received

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Submission Requirements

A valid submission must contain the following:

1. Kaggle Writeup  
   1. Media Gallery
   2. Attached Public Notebook (University Track)
   3. Attached Public Video (Broadcast Track) 

To create a new Writeup, click on the ""New Writeup"" button [here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/nfl-big-data-bowl-2026-analytics/projects). After you have saved your Writeup, you should see a ""Submit"" button in the top right corner. 

**Your final Submission must be made prior to the deadline. Any un-submitted or draft Writeups by the competition deadline will not be considered by the Judges.**

Note: If you attach a private Kaggle Resource to your public Kaggle Writeup, your private Resource will automatically be made public after the deadline.

All of the content on Kaggle is written in [Markdown](https://github.com/showdownjs/showdown/wiki/Showdown's-Markdown-syntax). If you are copy-pasting from another application, like Word or your browser, you may need to clean up the markdown or html for things to display properly.

### 1. Kaggle Writeup

The Kaggle Writeup serves as your project report. This should include a title, subtitle, and a detailed analysis of your submission. You must select a Track for your Writeup in order to submit.

Your Writeup should not exceed 2000 words. Submissions over this limit may be subject to penalty.

The below assets must be attached to the Writeup to be eligible.

#### a\. Media Gallery

This is where you should attach any images and/or videos associated with your submission. Writeups should contain less than ten tables/figures. Submissions over this limit may be subject to penalty.  

You are allowed to submit any tables/figures directly into your Writeup via Markdown editor if preferred, but a cover image in the media gallery is required to submit your Writeup. 

#### b\.?Public Notebook 

Your code should be submitted as a public notebook in the `Project Files` field. Your notebook should be publicly accessible and not require a login or paywall. If you use a private Kaggle Notebook, it will automatically be made public after the deadline.

#### c\.?Video (required for Broadcast Visualization Track)

Attach your video to the Media Gallery. Videos should be 3 minutes or less, and should be published to Youtube.
----------------------
# Evaluation Criteria

Participants will select one of two tracks in which to submit.

1. **University Track**. University students (college or grad students welcome) are allowed to submit to this track. Participants are challenged to generally analyze player movement, create player or team metrics for either the offensive or defensive units, and/or evaluate other passing play topics.  

2. **Broadcast Visualization Track**.  Open to any Big Data Bowl participant, the goal of this track is to create an accessible, interesting, and novel visualization that uses the movement of players when the ball is in the air. Note: Participants in this track can use the data provided, but are also encouraged to overlay film from games (e.g, [NFL YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/nfl)).


----
**Scoring**
----

All entries are evaluated on the following four components, where 0 is the lowest and 10 is the highest. 

**Football Score (30%):** 
- Would NFL teams (or the league office) be able to use these results on a week-to-week basis? 
- Does the analysis account for variables that make football data complex?
- Are the ideas unique?

**Data Science Score (30%):**
- Is the work correct? 
- Are claims backed up by data?  
- Are the statistical models appropriate given the data?
- Are the analytical applications innovative? 

**Writeup Score (20%):**
- Is the Writeup well-written?
- Is the Writeup easy to follow? 
- Is the motivation (metric, player evaluation, etc) clearly defined?

**Data Visualization Score (20%)**
- Are the charts and tables provided accessible? 
- Are the charts and tables accurate?
- Are the charts and tables innovative?

Participants are encouraged to show statistical code if it helps readers better understand their analyses. However, most code should be hidden in an Appendix.  Any Writeup that doesn¡¯t use the player tracking data will not be scored.

---
**Video requirements**
----
*Broadcast Visualization Track only.*

Create a dynamic, engaging, and high-quality video that demonstrates your metric or football concept in action. Participants are encouraged to show supporting documentation via Notebook if it helps a broadcast team better understand their analyses. 

---
**Judges Panel**
----
Judges will consist of analytics staffers that are working for (i) the 32 NFL teams, or (ii) player tracking vendors that work with NFL teams. Scores will be summarized using a weighed average (see above for each weight).
----------------------
# tracks-and-awards

This is placeholder content. This will not be displayed, but exists to satisfy launch checklist conditions.
----------------------
# judges

