10048,nst-x-contest-2-abalone-regression,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2024 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** The goal of this competition is to predict the age of abalone from various physical measurements.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error**.

The **RMSLE** is calculated as:
$$\sqrt{ \frac{1}{n} \sum\_{i=1}^n \left(\log (1 + \hat{y}\_i) - \log (1 + y\_i)\right)^2}$$
where:
- \\(n\\) is the total number of observations in the test set,
- \\(\hat{y}\_i\\) is the predicted value of the target for instance \(i\),
- \\(y\_i\\) is the actual value of the target for instance \(i\), and,
- \\(\log\\) is the natural logarithm.

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict the target, `Rings`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Rings
    90615,10
    90616,10
    90617,10
    etc.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Abalone](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/1/abalone) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.
## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `Rings` is the integer target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the value of `Rings` for each row
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10049,biotrove-clustering,"# Overview

The [BioTrove dataset](https://baskargroup.github.io/BioTrove/) is a massive collection of biodiversity images curated from iNaturalist. For this competition, you¡¯ll work with a~50,000-image subset where only family-level labels are provided.

Using image features and optional family-level priors (e.g., in contrastive learning), participants are challenged to learn clusters that approximate the hidden biological taxonomy. Evaluation is based on how well your clusters match hidden genus- and species-level groupings, measured using Normalized Mutual Information (NMI).
----------------------
# Description

## Background
Biodiversity research depends on understanding relationships across taxonomic ranks, but fine-grained annotations (genus, species) are expensive and often incomplete. Machine learning offers a way to extract structure from large image collections even when only coarse labels are available.

This challenge simulates that real-world scenario: you receive only family-level supervision, and your task is to discover deeper structure through clustering. The results will help test whether modern embedding methods can recover biological hierarchies when explicit labels are missing.

## Goal

* Learn image embeddings using contrastive learning with family priors, autoencoders, or other deep representation methods.

* Cluster images using algorithms such as k-means, hierarchical clustering, DBSCAN, or graph-based methods.

* Submit cluster assignments that will be compared against hidden genus- and species-level labels using Normalized Mutual Information (NMI).

## Data
Files provided to participants

- **metadata.csv** ¡ª metadata for the subset, containing:  
  - `hash_id` ¡ª anonymized identifier that links each row to an image in `images.zip`  
  - `family` ¡ª the only taxonomic label provided (genus and species are hidden for scoring)  
- **images.zip** ¡ª a zipped folder containing ~50,000 biodiversity images. Each file name corresponds to a unique `hash_id` listed in metadata.csv

This data was prepared from BioTrove using random sampling, with the following criteria:

* Each species has a minimum number of 10 images (median=14)
* Each genus a minimum of 2 species (median=3)
* Each family includes minimum of 3 genera (median=5)

The result is not perfectly balanced, but ensures meaningful coverage across taxonomic ranks for clustering.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on **Normalized Mutual Information (NMI)** between your cluster assignments and hidden taxonomic labels.  
NMI is in the range [0,1], where higher values indicate better agreement between your clusters and the ground truth.

A key property of NMI is that it is *cluster label agnostic*. The numeric IDs you assign to clusters do not matter (e.g., cluster `1` vs cluster `99`): only the grouping of images is evaluated. If two submissions produce the same partitioning of images into clusters but use different cluster numbers, they will receive the same score.

Scoring is performed at three levels of taxonomy:

- **Species-level NMI** ¡ª agreement between your `species_cluster` column and hidden species labels  
- **Genus-level NMI** ¡ª agreement between your `genus_cluster` column and hidden genus labels  
- **Family-level NMI** ¡ª agreement between your `family_cluster` column and provided family labels  

The final score is the arithmetic mean of NMI values across these levels.  

Both the public and private leaderboards use the same scoring procedure. The public leaderboard reports performance on a held-out subset; the private leaderboard is revealed at the end of the competition using the full hidden set.

## Submission File
For each `hash_id` in metadata.csv, you must assign a cluster number at each taxonomic level. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    hash_id,family_cluster,genus_cluster,species_cluster
    lrmeeyil643bzkhmhlhikqfq,1,1,1
    dupvalqog2ulc7dno4ml2nfa,1,2,2
    b2ielsckbk2pk7ikaraamylq,2,3,3
    4vrwrft7lp3qszcg46aqnxsw,2,3,3
    f7xdrtei5igv2jtimjhqqdy2,2,3,3
    etc.

**Notes:**  
- Participants only submit their own cluster assignments ¡ª not the true genus or species IDs.  
- Cluster numbers can be arbitrary integers or strings, as long as they are consistent within each column.  
- Each `hash_id` must appear exactly once. Duplicate or missing IDs will reduce your score or cause errors.
- `family_cluster` ¡ª your cluster assignment at the family level  (e.g., 1-F, where F is number of families). Since families are given, you can also use the actual names here.
- `genus_cluster` ¡ª your cluster assignment at the genus level  (e.g., 1-G, where G is number of genus)
- `species_cluster` ¡ª your cluster assignment at the species level (e.g., 1-S, where S is number of species)","# Dataset Description

## Files
* **metadata.csv** ¡ª metadata for the released subset
  * `hash_id` ¡ª anonymized identifier; matches image filenames in `images.zip`
  * `family` ¡ª the only taxonomic label provided. Genus and species are hidden for scoring.

* **images.zip** ¡ª ~50,000 biodiversity images. Each file is named `<hash_id>.jpg` corresponding to rows in `metadata.csv`.

* **sample_submission.csv** ¡ª example submission file in the correct format
  * `hash_id` ¡ª from `metadata.csv`
  * `species_cluster` ¡ª your cluster assignment at the species level (e.g., 1-S, where S is number of species)
  * `genus_cluster` ¡ª your cluster assignment at the genus level  (e.g., 1-G, where G is number of genus)
  * `family_cluster` ¡ª your cluster assignment at the family level  (e.g., 1-F, where F is number of families). Since families are given, you can also use the actual names here.

## Predicting cluster labels
You are not predicting explicit taxonomic labels. Instead, you assign each image to clusters at three levels (species, genus, family). Your clustering will be evaluated against hidden ground truth labels using **Normalized Mutual Information (NMI)**. 

## Data distribution 
This data was prepared from BioTrove using random sampling, with the following criteria:

* Each species has a minimum number of 10 images (median=14)
* Each genus a minimum of 2 species (median=3)
* Each family includes minimum of 3 genera (median=5)

The result is not perfectly balanced, but ensures meaningful coverage across taxonomic ranks for clustering.

## License and Attribution
The BioTrove dataset is publicly available under Creative Commons licenses via iNaturalist.  
This competition uses a ~50,000-image subset derived from BioTrove, with hashed IDs replacing original filenames.  

### BioTrove
Yang et al. (2024). BioTrove: A Large Curated Image Dataset Enabling AI for Biodiversity.
NeurIPS 2024 Datasets and Benchmarks Track.
[Dataset page](https://huggingface.co/datasets/BGLab/BioTrove) ¡¤ [Paper](https://openreview.net/forum?id=DFDCtGQs7S)",False,True
