10052,affective-computing-exercise-1-data-annotation,"# Overview

Understanding and annotating micro-gestures¡ªsubtle and short-duration movements often overlooked in daily life¡ªplays a crucial role in advancing research on human behavior analysis, affective computing, and human¨CAI interaction. Although small in scale, these micro-gestures may carry important signals of intention, emotion, or decision-making processes. By systematically labeling such gestures from video data, we are contributing to a deeper understanding of fine-grained human motion and enabling the development of intelligent systems that can perceive human behavior with higher sensitivity and accuracy.


----------------------
# ?? Notice

?? **Reference annotation samples** (File: **Reference_Tag_samples**) are available in Google Drive. You can download the videos together with their corresponding label .txt files and load them into the annotation platform to **better understand** the annotation process.

?? Please carefully review **every video** and pay close attention to **subtle** micro-gestures. Some of them are **extremely brief** or **almost hidden**, which makes them easy to miss. **Slowing down** the playback can be very helpful for spotting these details.

?? Don¡¯t forget to also consider **Option ID 99 (gestures, not micro)**  where appropriate. You can check the **sample videos** in Google Drive for reference.
 
----------------------
# Description

This competition is designed as part of our course assignment to explore the process of **micro-gesture annotation** from video data. Micro-gestures are subtle, short-duration movements that often carry critical information about human intentions, emotions, or decision-making. By carefully labeling these gestures, participants will gain first-hand experience in handling fine-grained human behavior data and in contributing to an emerging area of behavioral AI research.

Your goal is to annotate the provided videos with gesture classes and their corresponding time intervals, and then submit your annotations for evaluation. The system will automatically calculate a numerical score based on your results, and you will be able to view your score after each submission. Resubmissions are allowed until the assignment deadline, so you can improve your annotations to achieve a higher score.


By following these rules and carefully preparing your annotations, you will not only complete the assignment but also contribute to the broader effort of advancing **fine-grained motion understanding and human¨CAI interaction research.**


----------------------
# Evaluation

## Scoring Rules (Total 100 points)

1. **Submit all 11 videos**  
   - If fewer than 11 videos are submitted, your score on the **main part (up to 90 points)** will be **scaled down**.  

2. **Baseline bonus (+10 points)**  
   - If each video contains at least 10 annotations (correct or not), you will automatically receive a **10-point bonus**.  

3. **Main part (90 points)**  
   - **Class coverage (40 points):** detecting all the correct gesture classes.  
     - Missing a class costs points.  
     - Adding extra classes costs fewer points.  
   - **Temporal accuracy (50 points):** how well your annotation time segments align with the reference (smaller errors give higher scores).  

4. **Final score is capped at 100 points.**


In this assignment-competition, each participant is provided with a set of videos to annotate micro-gestures.
**The annotations must be submitted in a single CSV file (submission.csv) that contains all your annotations across videos**.

?? **Important submission format rules**:
	?	Instead of uploading multiple .txt files, you must merge all your annotations into a single submission.csv file.
	?	You can use the provided helper script to perform the merge automatically:
`python S3_merge_txt_to_csv.py input_dir submission.csv`
where input_dir is the folder containing your per-video .txt files, and submission.csv is the output file.

?	The final submission.csv must strictly follow the column format:
`ID, video_id, class, start_time, end_time`
	     ?	ID = a unique integer index for each row (e.g., 0, 1, 2, ¡­).
	     ?	video_id = the ID of the annotated video.
	     ?	class = the gesture label ID.
	     ?	start_time / end_time = segment boundaries in seconds.


Each row corresponds to one annotated gesture segment.
Only one file (submission.csv) should be uploaded as your submission.


Once submitted, the system will automatically evaluate your annotations and provide you with a **numerical score**. **You may resubmit** improved results until the deadline to try to achieve a higher score.","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of a collection of **post-interview videos**, each containing multiple instances of micro-gestures. These **micro-gestures** are subtle, short-duration movements that may occur at different points throughout a video.

The same type of micro-gesture can appear more than once, often with only slight variations. Because the gestures may be very brief and not immediately obvious, careful and attentive annotation is required to capture them accurately.

Each participant is provided with a set of such videos for annotation. The goal is to identify the gesture class and its corresponding start and end times in each video.

?? **Note**: The videos are provided solely for use in this assignment-competition. Redistribution, sharing, or any external use of the dataset is strictly prohibited. 

