10055,AI-Vs-2025,"# Overview

Offshore wind turbine (OWT) farms are growing rapidly, throughout the world. Accurate, depth-dependent modeling of soil properties like shear wave velocity, *V<sub>s</sub>*, and small-strain shear modulus, *G<sub>max</sub>*, is critical for safe designs. This challenge invites AI researchers, data scientists, and geotechnical engineers to collaborate and develop predictive models using (piezo-)cone penetration test (CPTu) data.
----------------------
# Description

Normally, a shear wave is generated at the ground surface using an impact method ¨Csuch as striking a beam with a hammer¨C to induce horizontal wave motion. This wave, known as S-wave, travels through the soil and is detected after a time delay by geophones. These geophones act as wave receivers and are embedded at various depths in the subsurface, by means of a module attached to piezocone [(Campanella and Stewart, 1992)](https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/abs/10.1139/t92-052). 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6137392%2F875432e568f11d41a9fb0c5a118ea8a3%2FSchematic%20illustration%20of%20Vs.png?generation=1757367091563307&alt=media)

In geotechnical design, *V<sub>s</sub>* is calculated from: 

$$V_S=\frac{L}{t}$$


where, 'L' is the wave 1-D travel distance toward the receiver, and 't' is the time of travel. 
And, *G<sub>max</sub>* is derived from:

\begin{equation}
    G_{max} = \rho V_s^2, \hspace{1.5cm} where \hspace{0.5cm} \rho = \frac{\gamma}{g}
\end{equation}

These values are essential for modeling soil-structure interaction, particularly under dynamic and cyclic loads (e.g. wind, waves). However, *V<sub>s</sub>* and unit weight, ¦Ã, are not readily available at all depths, and errors in *V<sub>s</sub>* estimation propagate quadratically into *G<sub>max</sub>*.

## Your mission

Utilize the so-called ""AI"" techniques to predict:
- Shear wave velocity, *V<sub>s</sub>*, and,
- Small-strain shear modulus, *G<sub>max</sub>* (for FOMLIG participants only),

using a provided real-world CPT dataset, ""Train.csv"", compiled from offshore projects. *You can add your own data sets also.*


There is the possibility to share your codes here on Kaggle, or elsewhere:
```
# code to print hello FOMLIG 2025
print(""hello FOMLIG 2025! I'm participating in Student Competition, NextGen session! ;)"")
```

## Contacts
- Mohammad Sadegh Farhadi, *mohammadsadegh.farhadi@tuni.fi*,
- Alireza Douzandeh, *alireza.duzandeh@unifi.it*,
- Arianna Pucci, *pccrnn01@uniroma2.it*, especially if you may have questions about posters format.

----------------------
# Evaluation

For evaluating the models, *accuracy* of predictions and *innovation* of models play roles. Provided that you want your model to be evaluated at FOMLIG2025, upload *at least* a two-page summary of your developed model, to present the innovation also. Though, single-page graphical abstracts are more than welcome to be presented at FOMLIG workshop in Florence, Italy, 15-17 October. If not, you can evaluate your model here on Kaggle.

Submissions are evaluated on [mean squared error (MSE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error) between the predicted values and the observed V<sub>s</sub>.

The *winners*:
- One winner from Kaggle (based on the Kaggle Leaderboard ranking),
- Two winners from FOMLIG participants (based on the accuracy and innovation criteria evaluated at FOMLIG),
- One winner from open participants who may not have the opportunity to join us in Italy (based on the accuracy and innovation criteria evaluated at FOMLIG),

will be awarded Certificates.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict V<sub>s</sub> variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format, as shown in 'Sample submission.csv':

    Project,Vs
    Test_1,0
    Test_2,0
    Test_3,0
    etc.",,False,True
10056,find-polynomial,"# Overview

In this competition, your challenge is to uncover the hidden generating function behind the data by fitting a bivariate polynomial model (that¡¯s just a fancy way of saying ¡°a polynomial with two variables¡±). But beware¡ªthe data isn¡¯t perfectly clean, it¡¯s noisy, which makes the task more challenging!

?**Your Mission**: Approximate the true parameters (the coefficients of the polynomial) as closely as possible, and in doing so, reconstruct the generating function.
----------------------
# Description

You are given data for two inputs \\(x_1\\) and \\(x_2\\), and one output \\(y\\). Your task? Build a polynomial predictor \\(h_\theta(\mathbf x) \\) that approximates \\(y\\).

Here's an example:

$$ h_\theta(\mathbf x) = x_1 + x_2^2 + 1.4 x_1 x_2 - 5.4 \approx y $$
Where \\( \mathbf x = [x_1, x_2] \\).

Train your predictor on the train data and evaluate it on the test data (check the Data tab).

**Important**: You¡¯re free to go as wild as you want with the polynomial degree¡ªthere are no restrictions! (But remember, we don't want to overfit.)

----------------------
# Evaluation

You will need to submit your predictions on the test inputs (test.csv), following the **EXACT** format shown in submission_sample.csv. Make sure the indexing lines up correctly (id column starting from 0)¡ªcheck the Data tab for details.

Your submission will be scored using Mean Squared Error (MSE), so accuracy matters!

**Important**: Along with your predictions, please also submit your code on Slack to Issar Amro (TA). Make sure to explain (through comments or markdown) your thought process, the approach you used, and the different experiments you ran along the way. This helps us understand not just what you did, but how you got there.","# Dataset Description

The training data consists of a table with 3 columns: \\(x_1, x_2, y \\):

## Columns

*   `x1` - First input
*   `x2`?- Second input
*   `y` - Output

## Format

The first 'id' column you submit should have the same index as that of the \\( (x_1, x_2) \\) pair in the test.csv file.",False,True
