*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `command` - instruction sentence
*   `command type`?- type of instruction modify(0), insert(1), delete(2)
*   `label` - label of instruction modify(0), insert(1), delete(2)",False,True
10059,mapple-accessories-2,"# Description

La compa?¨ªa Mapple desea presentar las proyecciones de ventas para el a?o 2026, en particular, para los cuatro modelos de Mypads que se fabricaran en la planta de Austin, TX. Desde esta planta se fabrican todas las unidades distribuidas en la regi¨®n de Norteam¨¦rica (Canad¨¢, USA y M¨¦xico), sin embargo, debido a la escasez de mano de obra que se est¨¢ presentando en la regi¨®n, es necesario que junto con las proyecciones de ventas, se obtenga el plan de contrataci¨®n para los 6 primeros meses del a?o 2026. 
Por esta raz¨®n, la compa?¨ªa Mapple ha contratado a su grupo de trabajo para que, a trav¨¦s de un proyecto de consultor¨ªa, le entreguen una serie de recomendaciones relacionadas con la estimaci¨®n de la demanda y la planeaci¨®n de la mano de obra.  
----------------------
# Evaluaci¨®n

Con bas en los 12 pron¨®sticos para el a?o 2026 deber¨¢ estimar la demanda agregada de productos a fabricar en la planta de Austin TX y as¨ª, obtener un plan agregado que minimice la suma de los costos totales bajo el **Escenario Eficiente**.

**Formato de respuesta:**

Para esta segunda parte del caso, se deber¨¢n subir los niveles de fuerza de trabajo para la planta, tanto para tiempo regular, como para tiempo extra, considerando el escenario cl¨¢sico. Sus respuestas deben ser n¨²meros enteros porque se trata de n¨²mero de operarios, por ejemplo:
W1 corresponde al n¨²mero de operarios para el mes de enero, mientras que W1E corresponde al n¨²mero de operarios para este mismo mes que trabajar¨¢n las **dos horas** extra.**
----------------------
# Costos Y Restricciones

El segundo problema a tratar est¨¢ relacionado con la ¨²nica etapa manual del proceso, la revisi¨®n, la que realiza un operario a cada una de la unidades producidas en la planta, para que estas puedan ser despachadas y, aunque es un proceso muy r¨¢pido, los tiempos varian seg¨²n el modelo que se deba revisar. 

Tenga en cuenta que cada operario tarda, en promedio, 4.8 segundos revisando un Mypad-pro, 2.1 segundos con un Mypad-mini, 3.0 segundos con un Mypad-air y 2.4 segundos con un Mypad tradicional. Adem¨¢s, cada uno trabaja en turnos de 8 horas, con un descanso de 30 minutos por cada turno, pero solo trabajan de lunes a viernes mientras no sean d¨ªas feriados. Cada hora de trabajo de un operario le cuesta a la compa?¨ªa US$25, pero cada uno puede trabajar un m¨¢ximo de 2 horas extras cada d¨ªa, a un costo de US$30 por hora.

Por otra parte, a la compa?¨ªa le cuesta US$3500 contratar y capacitar un nuevo operario y le cuesta US$5000 despedir a uno. Por pol¨ªticas estatales, en un mismo mes no se puede despedir a m¨¢s de 10 operarios, ni contratar m¨¢s de 50 nuevos operarios. En diciembre de 2025 la compa?¨ªa proyecta contar con 20 operarios en su n¨®mina.

Finalmente, cada unidad que se guarde en el inventario de un mes al otro le cuesta a la compa?¨ªa US$25 por cada mes, pero cada vez que no pueda cumplir con la demanda se asume un costo de US$120 por cada uno y la venta se considera p¨¦rdida. A finales de diciembre del a?o 2025, la compa?¨ªa debe contar con un inventario de 1000 unidades (incluyendo los 4 modelos), adem¨¢s, los primeros 6 meses de cada a?o se debe tener un stock de seguridad agregado de 1000 unidades y los ¨²ltimos 6 meses del a?o se debe aumentar a 2000 unidades agregadas.

Utilizando su mejor m¨¦todo de pron¨®stico, estime las demandas mensuales para todo el a?o 2026 y con ellas encuentre el plan de contrataci¨®n y despido que minimiza los costos totales bajo el **Escenario Eficiente**.","# Dataset Description

En el archivo mypaddata.csv usted encontrar¨¢ las demandas mensuales para las cuatro referencias de Mypads para los a?os 2018 al 2024. En el archivo mypadpronostico.csv usted encontrar¨¢ el formato adecuado para reportar sus resultados, en las filas 2 a la 13 deber¨¢ reportar, redondeado al entero m¨¢s cercano, el pron¨®stico para el modelo Mypad-pro, en las filas 14 a la 25, redondeado al entero m¨¢s cercano, el pron¨®stico para el modelo Mypad-mini, en las filas 26 a la 37, redondeado al entero m¨¢s cercano, el pron¨®stico para el modelo Mypad-air y en las filas 38 a la 49, redondeado al entero m¨¢s cercano, el pron¨®stico para el modelo Mypad.

Las ventas para cada uno de los modelos se encuentran en miles de unidades, es decir, en enero del a?o 2018 se vendieron 1.744.000 unidades del MyPad_pro. Su reporte para el a?o 2025 debe seguir este mismo formato, es decir, reportar sus cifras en miles de unidades.",False,True
10060,the-carnival-vision-challenge,"# Overview

The streets are alive with music, lights, and parades¡ªthe carnival is here. But beneath the glitter, hidden craters threaten to trip dancers and stall floats. Slow fixes won¡¯t do; we need a digital safeguard.
Your mission is to build a swift, precise object detection model. We¡¯ll provide images of the carnival streets¡ªyour AI must spot every crater and mark it with a bounding box.
The real test comes at night, when neon glare, confetti, and shadows make detection tougher. We don¡¯t need a model that just works in the lab; we need one that thrives in the chaos of the carnival.
Show us the code that keeps the celebration safe.

----------------------
# Description

Develop a robust computer vision model to automatically detect potholes in road imagery.

### **Your Task**
For any given image of a road, your model must identify all instances of potholes and output a list of their corresponding bounding boxes. Each bounding box must be defined by its top-left (x_min, y_min) and bottom-right (x_max, y_max) coordinates.

Your solution will be trained on a provided dataset of clear images but will be evaluated for its accuracy and robustness on a hidden test set that simulates more challenging, real-world conditions.

----------------------
# Evaluation

##**Evaluation Metric**

Submissions will be evaluated using Intersection over Union (IoU) and derived classification metrics.

Intersection over Union (IoU)

For each predicted box and ground truth box pair:

$$?????? = \frac {Area?of?Overlap}  {Area?of?Union} $$

IoU = 1 ¡ú Perfect match

IoU = 0 ¡ú No overlap

---
##**Scoring Procedure**

For each image, predicted boxes are matched to ground truth boxes using greedy IoU matching.

A prediction is considered a True Positive (TP) if it achieves IoU ¡Ý 0.5 with an unmatched ground truth box.

Predictions with IoU < 0.5 are counted as False Positives (FP).

Ground truth boxes left unmatched are counted as False Negatives (FN).

---
## **Final Metrics**

The following metrics are computed over the entire dataset:

Precision = TP / (TP + FP)

Recall = TP / (TP + FN)

F1-score = Harmonic mean of Precision and Recall

Average IoU = Mean IoU of all matched boxes


---

## Submission File
Participants must submit bounding box predictions in a CSV file with the following columns:
image_id, xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax

**image_id**: Identifier of the image.
**xmin, ymin**: Coordinates of the top-left corner of the predicted bounding box.
**xmax, ymax**: Coordinates of the bottom-right corner of the predicted bounding box.

Multiple rows may exist for the same image_id if an image contains more than one pothole.

Example submission file for reference:

    filename,width,height,class,xmin,ymin,xmax,ymax
    img-561_jpg.rf.00e55aff1b67a607a6631ac6a05bf7af.jpg,400,300,pothole,175,37,211,51
    img-561_jpg.rf.00e55aff1b67a607a6631ac6a05bf7af.jpg,400,300,pothole,189,4,246,24
    img-561_jpg.rf.00e55aff1b67a607a6631ac6a05bf7af.jpg,400,300,pothole,278,0,399,160
    img-561_jpg.rf.00e55aff1b67a607a6631ac6a05bf7af.jpg,400,300,pothole,19,86,297,190
    img-561_jpg.rf.00e55aff1b67a607a6631ac6a05bf7af.jpg,400,300,pothole,60,200,178,299

    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train folder** - It contains images of potholes and an annotations.csv file containing coordinates of potholes for each image
*   **test folder** - It contains images of potholes
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns
*   filename: The name of the image file.
*   width: The total width of the source image in pixels.
*    height: The total height of the source image in pixels.
*    class: The class label for the object, which is pothole.
*    xmin: The x-coordinate (distance from the left edge) of the top-left corner of the bounding box.
*    ymin: The y-coordinate (distance from the top edge) of the top-left corner of the bounding box.
*    xmax: The x-coordinate of the bottom-right corner of the bounding box.
*   ymax: The y-coordinate of the bottom-right corner of the bounding box.",False,True
10061,cayleypy-transposons,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to develop solving/sorting methods for  certain kind
of permutation transformations which are mathematical model for [transposons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transposable_element). 
Problem is known to be NP-hard in general, but still for limited sizes it might be possible to achieve 
optimal solution, while larger sizes just - the shorter solutions achieved the better.
Challenge can serve as one of the benchmarks for modern pathfinding methods, as well as for LLM to solve real research tasks.

The problem represents a wide class of pathfinding/planning/reinforcements learning problems, which are important in mathematics, computer science, bionformatics, communication theory, etc.  

The open conjecture https://oeis.org/A065603  predicts the worst case performance  
(also called [God's algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God%27s_algorithm) or diameter of Cayley graph)
is ceiling((n+1)/2). Such problems are known to be hard,  conjecture is open for 25 years. 

The challenge is almost identical to the [Kaggle Santa 2023 challenge ](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/santa-2023), with the only difference to focus on the single puzzle. The advanced machine learning methods are proposed e.g. in [CayleyPy project ](https://cayleypy.github.io/cayleypy-docs/api.html), [CayleyPy-Cube](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.13266), [CayleyPy-RL](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18663), [CayleyPy-Growth](https://www.arxiv.org/abs/2509.19162). CayleyPy is open-source crowd-sourcing project to develop effective AI-based library for mathematics - you are welcome to contribute - contact us. For the challenge you are welcome to use any methods you prefer, only score matters.

**Computer science perspective - it is a sorting problem**
[Pancakes sorting](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pancake_sorting) is another example (with Bill Gates involved - not a joke). 

**Broader Mathematical Context - groups and Cayley graphs**
From a mathematical  perspective  it is a typical task in mathematics: decompose an element of a group into a product of given generators (""moves""), or, in the other words, find a path on the [Cayley graph](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cayley_graph) of a group. For example - [bubble sort](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bubble_sort) algorithm provides the shortest paths on the the [permutohedron](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permutohedron) Cayley graph - with nodes all n! permutations, and edges connect permutations different only in neighbour positions - thus represents a permutation as a sequence of neighbour transpositions. Length of the decomposition (path) is called ""[word metric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_metric)"" in group theory. Research on Cayley graphs is of fundamental importance for mathematics and the field contains many notoriously hard problems, which has been addressed by renowned mathematicians such as [M. Gromov](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mikhael_Gromov_(mathematician)) ([theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gromov%27s_theorem_on_groups_of_polynomial_growth)) and [T. Tao](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terence_Tao) ([posts](https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2010/07/10/cayley-graphs-and-the-geometry-of-groups/)), among others. Moreover, it has numerous applications, ranging from bioinformatics (e.g., estimating evolutionary distance¡ª""[Transform men into mice](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/492588/)"" ??), to the optimisation of [interprocessor networks](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/21148), and beyond.  Famous computer scientist [Donald Knuth ](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Knuth) contributed to [improving the algorithm](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9201304) to solve the problems like that. Such algorithm is implemented in computer algebra systems like [GAP/SAGE](https://doc.sagemath.org/html/en/reference/groups/sage/groups/perm_gps/cubegroup.html), however these methods are [not efficient](https://mathoverflow.net/q/482661/10446) ([notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/alexandervc/gap-cannot-solve-rubik-s-cube-444)) on large groups. And the challenge is to improve them. 

**Broader RL Context - RL with severely sparse reward**
The problem is a typical reinforcement learning problem - states of puzzle (nodes of Cayley graph) is a space of states, moves (edges of Cayley graph) - are actions, while rewards (penalties) are always ""1"", unless you found the solution. 
The cumulative reward (penalty) becomes exactly the length of the path to the solution state. And the goal is to minimize it, i.e. to find the shortest path. Value function has very clear and natural interpretation - length of the path. Bellman equation for the value function comes from trivial relation on path length. (See [CayleyPy-RL](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18663) for more details). There is a severe  ""sparse reward problem"", since ""always 1"" is meaningless reward (only tiny detail that for the solution state reward is not 1, but 0, makes setup meaningful). Thus advancing the challenge you will contribute to development of RL methods overcoming the ""sparse reward problem"" on extremely huge spaces like 10^33 and beyond.

**Similar Challenges**
[Kaggle Santa 2023 challenge ](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/santa-2023) - several puzzles: cubes, rings, globes. 
LRX series - [OEIS-A186783 conjecture](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/lrx-oeis-a-186783-brainstorm-math-conjecture),  [Towards God's algorithm](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/lrx-discover-math-gods-algorithm) , [Binary states 0/1](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/lrx-binary-in-search-of-gods-number) - just three moves - L,R - left/right cyclic shifts, X - eXchange positions 0 and 1.   Questions go back to  ""one of the founding fathers of Soviet cybernetics"" [V.M. Glushkov](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_Glushkov) 1968 seminal work and are still unsolved. See survey [link1](https://library.keldysh.ru/prep_vw.asp?lg=e&pid=8508) , [link2](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/alexandervc/cayleypy-development-3-growth-computations/data?select=Glukhov_Zubov1999review.pdf) (pages 21-22).

[Pancake sorting](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/CayleyPy-pancake)
[Transposons](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-transposons)
[Reversals](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-reversals)
[Glushkov problem](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-glushkov)
[RapapportM2](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-rapapport-m2)
[Rubik's cube 444](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-444-cube)
[Professor Tetraminx](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-professor-tetraminx-solve-optimally)
[Megaminx](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-megaminx)
[Christophers jewel](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-christophers-jewel)
[SuperCube from IHES](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-ihes-cube)
----------------------
# Description

One needs to make sorting with specific transformations originating from biological ""[transposons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transposable_element)"". The notation used for these transformations ""T[i,j,k]"", with three integer parameters ""i<j<k"", which mean:  transformation takes segment from positions from i-th to j-th, and moves to k-th position.
That segment is mathematical abstract for ""transposons"", which jumps out of its position on DNA,
and places itself back to the new position - starting from ""k"".

The function generating such permutation transformations can be found [here](https://cayleypy.github.io/cayleypy-docs/generated/cayleypy.PermutationGroups.html#cayleypy.PermutationGroups.transposons). 

The number of such transformation between two such sequences (DNA) may be useful to estimate the evolutionary distance between two different genomes. 

----------------------
# Input data, Solutions, Evaluation

## Brief 

The setup can be briefly described as follows: 

- Input data - vectors with integer coordinates (""test.csv"")
- Solution -   for each input vector -  find sequences of ""moves"" such that applying a sequence to the input vector one gets the fully sorted vector: (0,1,2,3,...).  How to find - that is the challenge. 
- To apply move-vectors to state-vector -  just do numpy reindexing ([example notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/alexandervc/cayleypy-professor-tetraminx-introduction)): 
```python
state_vector[move1_vector][move2_vector]...[moveN_vector]
```  
- Move names and corresponding vectors are stored in [graphs_info.json](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-transposons/data?select=graphs_info.json). 
- Submission: should contain the sequences of move names separated by dots for each sample,
also can be ""UNSOLVED"", then scores add 100 000 for each.
- Evaluation: sum of the lengths of solutions (how many moves used in total - i.e.  first count number of moves for each test sample and then sum over all  samples, for  each ""UNSOLVED"" will be added 100 000).

## Details

Here we repeat with more details what was briefly described above.

### States and moves  are integer vectors  (permutations)

To work with such puzzles on mathematical and programming levels, we assume each tile to be indexed with a unique number. In this setting each state of the puzzle is a permutation of the indices. The moves of the puzzle are permutations as well, which tell how the locations of the indices are swapped after the move.

In the scope of this competition we assume each tile to have a unique color. The puzzle is called to be ""solved"" if the **solution state** is achieved:
 
```python
solution_state = [0, 1, 2, 3, ...]
```  
The full set of the moves can be found in  [graphs_info.json](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-transposons/data?select=graphs_info.json). 

### Input data - multiple integer vectors (""initial states"" - permutations) 

Submissions are evaluated on a set of **initial states** of the puzzle. There are 1800 states in the test set. The state have different sizes -- for each sizes there is a different set of generators is defined.

For each **intial state** the participant needs to provide a set of moves to get to the **solution state**. If there is a path in submission which doesn't lead to the **solution state**, the whole submission is considered invalid.

### Evaluation: score = number of moves

For a valid submission the final score are the sums of the lengths of all paths:

$$ score = \sum_{path \in Test}\mathrm{len}(path) $$

We also allow string ""UNSOLVED"" instead of actual solution, in that case score 100 000 would be added for each such string.

The lower the score the better the result is.

Some solutions paths used for generation the samples are provided in [sample_submission.csv](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-transposons/data?select=sample_submission.csv). 


## Submission File
For each initial_state_id in the test set, you need to generate a path from the **initial state** (from [test.csv](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-transposons/data?select=test.csv) to the **solution state** . 

The path is a string of dot-separated generator names. The generator are assumed to be applied to the **initial state** to the **solution state** starting from left to right.  The generators and the solution state can be found in [graphs_info.json](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-transposons/data?select=graphs_info.json)

Make sure that the values in  **initial_state_id** column are ordered from 0 to 1000.


| initial_state_id | path |
| :-------- | :-------: |
| 0 | T[0,1,3] |
| 1 | T[6,6,8].T[1,1,8] |
| 2 | T[1,2,5].T[2,2,8].T[2,8,9]|
|...|...|

----------------------
# Further Information

# Related fundamental mathematical conjectures by [L.Babai](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%A1szl%C3%B3_Babai), [P.Diaconis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persi_Diaconis)

Although that particular challenge is narrowly focused on a particular puzzle group, it is provides an illustration for deep mathematical problems, some which are easy to state, but they seems out of reach of the present mathematics. 

Babai-like conjecture for symmetric (permutation) group of n-letters: whatever generators (moves) you choose the God's number (diameter of the Cayley graph) is not more than n^2. I.e. any two permutations can be transformed to each other by not more than n^2 moves (whatever moves you select -- with the only condition all permutations are achievable by them). 
Babai's conjecture is actually more general, T.Tao and others made huge progress on it recent years (see [blog](https://seaneberhard.com/2020/05/21/babais-conjecture-for-at-least-three-random-generators/), [survey13](https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.0239), [survey19](https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.06308) ), but it is still open as well as n^2 conjecture above. The particular conjecture for permutation group goes back at least to V.Rubtsov who raised the question  and supported it by computational experiments in 1975(!). ([Link1](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01071362), [Link2](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/alexandervc/cayleypy-development-3-growth-computations/data?select=Rubtsov1976_MaxDiamSearch.pdf)). 

The other fundamental conjecture is due to famous ""mathematician and magician"" [Persi Diaconis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persi_Diaconis). 
It states that whatever moves you choose the mixing time would not be more than n^3 log(n) ([Question 2 page 9](https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.7754)). That roughly speaking predicts that whatever way you shuffle n cards, then n^3 log(n) random shuffles suffices to get random configuration.

# Optimal solution - typically NP-complete (and harder) problems 

It has been proved already in 80-ies that finding the optimal solutions (and diameters) for general permutation groups is hard.
NP-hardness for both optimal decomposition and diameter estimation: [The minimum-length generator sequence problem is NP-hard](https://doi.org/10.1016/0196-6774(81)90029-8), 1981, S Even, O Goldreich
Strengthening to [P-space competeness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PSPACE-complete) (conjecturally outside NP) for optimal decomposition even for two generators : [The complexity of finding minimum-length generator sequences](https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-3975(85)90047-7), 1986, Mark R. Jerrum

In 2010-ies, results were found for concrete generators, even for Rubik's cube:
[Solving the Rubik's Cube Optimally is NP-complete](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06708), 2017, Erik D. Demaine et.al.
[Pancake Flipping is Hard](https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.0434), 2011, L. Bulteau, et.al.
And many others

# God's number for most of the groups/puzzles unknown - open research problems 

Determining God's number (diameter) for the standard Rubik's cube was a long searched result  [remarkably resolved only in 2010](https://www.cube20.org/) in HTM metric, and in 2014 for [QTM metric](https://www.cube20.org/qtm/). It is not yet known for [STM](https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Metric#STM) and other metrics for 333 cube. Neither it is known for majority of other puzzles. Known estimates are collected at [speedsolving site](https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=God%27s_Algorithm#Table_of_God.27s_Numbers).  

Recent papers propose precise conjecture for 444,555 cubes [Hirata1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.07337), [Hirata2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12961). But they are not proved. 

Developing methods to estimate diameters (God's numbers) would be noteworthy achievement. 

# More math: [Poincare conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincar%C3%A9_conjecture), [Markoff triples](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markov_number), automatic theorem proving

From Poincare conjecture to Cayley graph pathfinding with Reinforcement Learning.
[Poincare conjecture in 3d](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincar%C3%A9_conjecture) was selected as one of 7 ""[Millenium Prize Problems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennium_Prize_Problems)"" -  hardest and most important problems in mathematics in 20-th century. It was ingeniously resolved by G. Perelman. However its twin brother - smooth Poincare conjecture in four dimensions  ""remains open and is thought to be very difficult""([link](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincar%C3%A9_conjecture#Dimensions)). Moreover there is no consensus in community should it be true or not. Prominent mathematicians [Selman Akbulut](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selman_Akbulut) and [Robion Kirby](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robion_Kirby) proposed conjectural counterexamples to it and several other conjectures in 1985: ¡°A potential smooth counterexample in dimension 4 to the Poincare conjecture, the Schoenflies conjecture, and the Andrews¨CCurtis conjecture¡±([link](https://doi.org/10.1016/0040-9383(85)90010-2)).  Some of these potential counterexamples  were only [refuted in 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.15332)  with the help of the reinforcement learning. Essentially the problem is almost the same as described in that challenge - find a path between two elements of a space where a group acts. However the structure of the group is more complicated - infinite group of the Andrews¨CCurtis transformations. 

[Markoff triples](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markov_number) are solutions of certain  Markov Diophantine equation, the subject going back to 19-th century and attracted attention of many prominent mathematicians. 
[J.Ellenberg](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jordan_Ellenberg) in a beautiful [blog post](https://quomodocumque.wordpress.com/2016/05/11/bourgain-sarnak-gamburd-on-markoff-triples/): ""[Bourgain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Bourgain), [Gamburd](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Gamburd), [Sarnak](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Sarnak) on [Markoff triples](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markov_number)""  emphasises  that: ""Again, I emphasize that this is on its face a question of pure combinatorial group theory.  You want to know if you can get from any pair of elements in SL_2(F_p) with negative-unipotent commutator to any other via the two moves above.  You can set this up on your computer and check that it holds for lots and lots of p (they did.)"" That is reiterated in recent ML Cayley graph pathfinding [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05558) by [M.Douglas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_R._Douglas) and K.Fraser-Taliente. Who emphasised that such problems can be approached with AI-helped pathfinding and can be considered as ""warm-up"" for previously discussed Andrews¨CCurtis conjecture work. 

So in a nutshell both problems ask about existence of a path for some elements in the space where group acts. Potential counterexamples suggesting that path does not exist - might be sometimes resolved by explicit pathfinding. 

Automatic theorem proving.
In a sense, doing mathematics - is actually a pathfinding - to prove a theorem one needs to find a sequence  of arguments which lead from it to axioms or known statements. That can be formalised with the help of e.g. [Lean](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lean_(proof_assistant)) the language to give ""formal proofs"". So formally speaking it is the same pathfinding problem, however difficulty lies in enormously larger space of possible moves and states. Although systems like AlphaGeometry were quite successful on solving geometric problems with more or less similar approach as one can use for puzzles/Cayley graphs, the gap between elementary geometry problems and real math research problems is still large. 

# Analogies with games and AlphaGo/Zero approach

Games like Go, chess represent the same class of problems - one needs to find a path on a huge graph - nodes are positions, edges are moves. One searches the path from initial position to any position marked ""win"". The difference is in two-players scenario which means that after move is done by one player, then position changes independently on his will (second player makes a move). So it is a path-finding on a graph in somewhat changing environment. Nevertheless, conceptually, it is the same class of pathfinding problems.  Group/Puzzle solving represents in that respect a more simple scenario - when environment is not changing. Success of AlphaGo handling the Go game with 10^170 positions suggests the groups/puzzles of similar size should be tractable. The difficulty of the group/puzzle case lies in the different direction - there are many different groups and ideal system should effectively work on all them (it is not obvious that same method can work e.g. for  nilpotent and simple groups - which are quire different). And, moreover, ideally, to be very fast - to enable users (mathematicians) to conduct effectively computational experiments. 

# Fractals and puzzles - beautiful fractal images from groups

Discover  beautiful fractal images from groups: ""Investigation of these groups reveals a new family of fractals, as well as a rich structure that is intriguing both mathematically and artistically."" [2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12950), even more [videos](https://www.twodisks.org/play/?r1=1.555&r2=1.48&n1=17&n2=4&zoom=1.382636655948553) (thanks to Tomas Rokicki).",,False,True
10062,floodhackathon,"# Overview

Every monsoon season, Pakistan faces devastating floods that cause massive loss of lives, displacement of communities, and destruction of infrastructure. Despite repeated disasters, gaps remain in how we prepare, respond, and recover from such emergencies.

FloodHackathon is a call to action for innovators, technologists, entrepreneurs, students, and change-makers to come together and design practical, scalable, and impactful solutions. The focus will be on three key stages of disaster management:

Readiness ¨C Early detection and forecasting systems, community awareness tools, and disaster preparedness technologies.

Response ¨C Smart systems for relief item distribution, real-time communication platforms, rescue coordination, and resource mobilization.

Recovery ¨C Solutions for rebuilding livelihoods, restoring education and healthcare access, mental health support, and creating flood-resilient infrastructure.

This hackathon will bring together experts, mentors, and innovators to co-create solutions that can strengthen Pakistan¡¯s resilience against floods and set an example for disaster innovation worldwide.
**
Join us in turning challenges into opportunities ¡ª because the next flood is not a question of if, but when.**","# Dataset Description

That¡¯s totally fine ?? Kaggle allows you to launch a competition **without a dataset at first** ¡ª but you¡¯ll need to clearly communicate it to participants. The key is to write a strong placeholder **Dataset Description** so people understand what¡¯s coming.

Here¡¯s a draft you can directly use for your **FloodHackathon Kaggle page**:

---

# ?? FloodHackathon Dataset

?? **Note:** This competition does not have a dataset yet.

We are in the process of preparing and curating a dataset that reflects the challenges of **flood prediction, response, and recovery** in Pakistan. The dataset will either be:

1. **Synthetic (simulated)** ¨C generated from historical weather and hydrology patterns to provide a training ground for models.
2. **Open-source based** ¨C built from publicly available rainfall, river flow, and satellite data.
3. **Hybrid** ¨C a combination of synthetic data and real-world references, designed to be both realistic and safe to share.

---

## ?? Planned Dataset Features

The dataset will likely include the following variables:

| Column              | Description                                                                   |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `id`                | Unique identifier for each observation                                        |
| `district`          | District or region name                                                       |
| `rainfall_mm`       | Rainfall (mm) during the period                                               |
| `river_flow_cumecs` | River discharge (cubic meters per second)                                     |
| `soil_saturation`   | Soil moisture index (0 = dry, 1 = fully saturated)                            |
| `temp_c`            | Average temperature (¡ãC)                                                      |
| `month`             | Month of observation                                                          |
| `flood`             | Target variable (1 = flood, 0 = no flood) ¡ª *available only in training data* |

---

## ?? Task

Once released, participants will:

* Train models on **Train.csv** (with labels).
* Predict flood occurrence on **Test.csv** (without labels).
* Submit predictions as **submission.csv** in the required format.

**Evaluation Metric:** ROC-AUC (Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve).

---

## ?? Why No Dataset Yet?

* We want participants to **focus first on the challenge** and ideas.
* The dataset is being finalized to ensure **quality, realism, and fairness**.
* A notification will be sent to all registered participants as soon as it is published.

---

?? Until then, participants can:

* Brainstorm possible **features and data sources**.
* Explore **open datasets** on rainfall, river flows, or satellite imagery (e.g., from NASA, NOAA, or Pakistan Meteorological Department).
* Get ready to test their solutions as soon as the dataset is live.

---

This way, your competition page looks **professional and intentional**, even without a dataset right now.

Do you want me to also create a **temporary synthetic mini-dataset (like 50 rows CSV)** that you can upload so the competition pipeline (submissions/evaluation) is already testable?",False,True
10063,machine-learning-pregrado-2025-03,"# Overview

Se tiene informaci¨®n sobre 1600 zonas ordenadas de manera contigua durante un a?o de un detallista de ropa y joyer¨ªa que vende por cat¨¢logo.

De acuerdo con lo indicado por el cuerpo directivo, el objetivo es realizar un pron¨®stico de ventas anual para la ropa de mujer basado en la informaci¨®n de la sucursal. De gran importancia es saber si los recursos que se asignan tienen poder para modelar la demanda en las zonas respectivas.

Para ello desean que usted haga un pron¨®stico para las 400 zonas que se encuentran en el archivo de sometimiento (ver datos), cuya informaci¨®n corresponde al mismo a?o de las 1600 zonas originales. El pron¨®stico ser¨¢ evaluado con base en la ra¨ªz del error cuadr¨¢tico medio (RMSE) que se obtenga en estas 400 sucursales.

?Qu¨¦ deben presentar?
Talleres individuales acorde a CRISP-DM con longitud m¨¢xima de 10 p¨¢ginas: 50%.
Predicci¨®n: 50%.
Se dar¨¢ bonificaci¨®n adicional por la realizaci¨®n y respuesta de preguntas en los foros, entrega de notebooks o c¨®digo p¨²blico en la plataforma, valoraciones de los aportes de otros, y en general, por la colaboraci¨®n.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La mejor predicci¨®n obtendr¨¢ un 50 en el 50% del taller, y todos aquellos que igualen un benchmark ingenuo obtendr¨¢n 20. Valores intermedios se ubicar¨¢n de acuerdo a una escala. Si no se supera el benchmark ingenuo la nota ser¨¢ inferior a 20. La predicci¨®n ser¨¢ valorada basada en el ingenuo ya publicado y usando la m¨¦trica RMSE. La colaboraci¨®n en los kernels y foros en el espacio del concurso puede darle puntos adicionales, valorados por los profesores. Colaborar no necesariamente es responder, tambi¨¦n preguntar y comentar. S¨®lo se permitir¨¢n notebooks de exploraci¨®n/visualizaci¨®n. El taller corresponde al otro 50% de la nota.","# Dataset Description

El archivo de datos que se entrega est¨¢ en formato Excel (xlsx) y tiene dos hojas: Train, para desarrollar el modelo (1600 registros) y Test (400 registros) con todas las variables independientes necesarias para predecir la variable de respuesta (ropamujer). Las predicciones deben ser sometidas a kaggle en formato .csv con dos columnas con encabezados idloc y ropamujer (esta ¨²ltima contiene su predicci¨®n). La separaci¨®n de las columnas es con coma y el decimal debe marcarse con punto(.).

Campos de datos
ropamujer: ventas de prendas para mujer en millones de pesos durante el a?o.
correo: n¨²mero de cat¨¢logos enviados durante el a?o.
p¨¢ginas: n¨²mero de p¨¢ginas del cat¨¢logo.
tel¨¦fono: n¨²mero promedio de l¨ªneas para llamada abiertas para pedidos.
impresa: cantidad gastada en publicidad impresa.
servicio: n¨²mero de representantes del servicio al cliente.
idmercado: tipo de mercado. Solo se listan n¨²meros de clases o tipos de mercado que se desea atacar. No se proveer¨¢ informaci¨®n adicional.
tama?omer: tama?o potencial del mercado, proyectado de acuerdo a cifras del ¨¢rea de marketing.
Idloc: ID de la tienda.
Edadloc: a?os transcurridos desde la llegada a la zona.
promo (promoci¨®n): tipo de promoci¨®n que se llev¨® a cabo durante el a?o. De nuevo, informaci¨®n descriptiva sobre estas promociones es reservada.
nomina: valor total de la n¨®mina durante el a?o.",False,True
