10105,thynk-2025,"# Overview

Toxicity, hate, and rumors are everywhere on social media. Facebook threads explode into fights, YouTube comments spiral into chaos, and Reddit? Well, it¡¯s Reddit. Big platforms claim they¡¯re fighting back, but their tools mostly work for English. In Bangla, hateful comments slip through every day.

The Notre Dame IT Club decided not to sit quietly and watch. The club announced an ambitious project: a Bangla text classifier that can keep an eye on online conversations. The idea is simple enough to explain but hard to pull off. Bangla comments don¡¯t stick to one mood at a time. A single line can be angry and funny, hateful and sarcastic, or even joyful and mocking. To separate destructive hate from sharp but fair criticism, we need to catch every emotion tangled up in a comment.

And then there¡¯s the internet factor. People type fast, furious, and without much care. Typos, broken grammar, random English words, and wild spelling are part of the mix. Models that do fine on clean Bangla quickly stumble when faced with real social media.

Here¡¯s where you come in. To prove your worth in the club of geniuses, you¡¯ve boldly promised to build the model that can crack this challenge. The executive panel has already started posting about it with great enthusiasm, so backing out isn¡¯t an option anymore.

Your mission: train a Bangla text classifier that can handle messy, real-world comments and spot the true emotions behind them. Pull it off, and you¡¯ll not only save your reputation but also give Bangla speakers a fighting chance at a healthier online space.
----------------------
# Description

In this competition, your task is to build a classifier model for Bangla comments. Given a dataset of social media comments along with some metadata, your model should assign all appropriate emotion labels to each comment. This is not a single-label classification task - a comment may belong to more than one emotion category at the same time.

The ultimate goal is to design a system that can handle noisy, real-world Bangla language data used in social media and still provide reliable predictions. A strong solution will move us closer to creating safer and healthier online spaces for Bangla speakers.

Note : You have to mail the code(.ipynb file)  of your best performing solution to misbahinan@gmail.com before the end of the contest. Otherwise, it will not be considered as a valid submission. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using Hamming loss, which measures the fraction of incorrect labels assigned. Since this is a multi-label classification task, each comment can belong to multiple emotion categories at once. Hamming loss penalizes both missed labels and extra labels, so a lower Hamming loss indicates better performance.

The formula for Hamming loss is:

$$
\text{Hamming Loss} = \frac{1}{N \times L} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \sum_{j=1}^{L} \mathbf{1}(y_{i,j} \neq \hat{y}_{i,j})
$$

where:

- N = number of samples  
- L = number of labels  
- y_{i,j}= true label for sample i, class j  
- y^_{i,j} = predicted label for sample i, class j  


**Submission File**

For each `ID` in the test set, you must predict binary values (0 or 1) for each emotion label. The submission file must include a header row with column names. The format should be:


| ID | Love | Joy | Surprise | Anger | Sadness | Fear |
|----|------|-----|----------|-------|---------|------|
| 1  | 0    | 1   | 0        | 0     | 0       | 0    |
| 2  | 1    | 0   | 1        | 0     | 0       | 0    |
| 3  | 0    | 0   | 0        | 1     | 1       | 0    |


Each row corresponds to a comment from the test set. For every emotion category, output 1 if the label applies to the comment and 0 otherwise.","# Dataset Description

### Dataset Overview

This dataset is designed for a multi-label emotion classification task, where the goal is to predict the emotion(s) associated with online comments. The data is provided in CSV format, with each row representing a single social media comment and its corresponding features.

Note : You have to mail the code(.ipynb file)  of your best performing solution to misbahinan@gmail.com before the end of the contest. Otherwise, it will not be considered as a valid submission. 

### Files

* **train.csv** - The training set, which includes the comment text and the emotion labels.

* **test.csv** - The test set, which contains comment text for which you must predict the emotion labels.

* **val.csv** - A separate validation set for model development and tuning.

* **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file showing the correct format for your predictions.

### Columns

* `ID` - A unique identifier for each comment.

* `Data` - The raw text content of the comment. This is the primary feature used for prediction.

* `Love`, `Joy`, `Surprise`, `Anger`, `Sadness`, `Fear` - These are the six target emotion labels. Each is a binary value (0 or 1), indicating the presence or absence of that emotion in the comment.

* `Topic` - The general subject or category of the comment.

* `Domain` - The platform or website where the comment was originally posted.

* `is_admin` - A boolean value indicating if the user who posted the comment is an administrator.

* `like_number` - The number of ""likes"" the comment received.",False,True
