10114,multi-class-prediction-of-cirrhosis-outcomess,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far. This is our last episode for the Season 3 and we wish you all a Happy New Year! Stay tuned for the new season next year!

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use a multi-class approach to predict the the outcomes of patients with cirrhosis. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the multi-class logarithmic loss. Each `id` in the test set had a single true class label, `Status`. For each `id`, you must submit a set of predicted probabilities for each of the three possible outcomes, e.g., `Status_C`, `Status_CL`, and `Status_D`.

The metric is calculated

$$log loss = -\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N\sum_{j=1}^My_{ij}\log(p_{ij}),$$

where \\(N\\) is the number of rows?in the test set, \\(M\\) is the number of outcomes (i.e., 3),? \\(log\\) is the natural logarithm, \\(y_{ij}\\) is 1 if row \\(i\\) has the ground truth label \\(j\\) and 0 otherwise, and \\(p_{ij}\\) is the predicted probability that observation \\(i\\) belongs to class?\\(j\\).

The submitted probabilities for a given row?are not required to sum to one because they are rescaled prior to being scored (each row is divided by the row sum). In order to avoid the extremes of the log function, predicted probabilities are replaced with \\(max(min(p,1-10^{-15}),10^{-15})\\).

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict probabilities of the three outcomes `Status_C`, `Status_CL`, and `Status_D` . The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Status_C,Status_CL,Status_D
    7905,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
    7906,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
    7907,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - December 5, 2023
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  January 1, 2024

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Cirrhosis Patient Survival Prediction](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/joebeachcapital/cirrhosis-patient-survival-prediction) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `Status` is the categorical target; `C` (censored) indicates the patient was alive at `N_Days`, `CL` indicates the patient was alive at `N_Days` due to liver a transplant, and `D` indicates the patient was deceased at `N_Days`.
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of each of the three `Status` values, e.g., `Status_C`, `Status_CL`, `Status_D`.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10115,cayleypy-reversals,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to develop solving/sorting methods for  certain kind
of permutation transformations which are quite simple - take any subsequence and flip it its order (called ""reversals""). Such problem is important in bioinformatics since it allows to estimate evolutionary distance.  For example it is believed that 70 millions years ago human and mice had a common ancestor, while humans and gorillas share a common ancestor from whom they diverged roughly 8 to 19 million years ago, with the split occurring after orangutans but before the divergence of humans and chimpanzees. One of the ways to obtain such estimates is counting number of reversal transformation between genes sequences, see e.g. ""[Transform men into mice](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/492588/)"" ??)
 
 Problem is known to be NP-hard in general, but still for limited sizes it might be possible to achieve 
optimal solution, while larger sizes just - the shorter solutions achieved the better.
Challenge can serve as one of the benchmarks for modern pathfinding methods, as well as for LLM to solve real research tasks.

The problem represents a wide class of pathfinding/planning/reinforcements learning problems, which are important in mathematics, computer science, bionformatics, communication theory, etc.  


The challenge is almost identical to the [Kaggle Santa 2023 challenge ](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/santa-2023), with the only difference to focus on the single class of transformations - reversals. The advanced machine learning methods are proposed e.g. in [CayleyPy project ](https://cayleypy.github.io/cayleypy-docs/api.html), [CayleyPy-Cube](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.13266), [CayleyPy-RL](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18663), [CayleyPy-Growth](https://www.arxiv.org/abs/2509.19162). CayleyPy is open-source crowd-sourcing project to develop effective AI-based library for mathematics - you are welcome to contribute - contact us. For the challenge you are welcome to use any methods you prefer, only score matters.

**Computer science perspective - it is a sorting problem** [Pancakes sorting](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pancake_sorting) where Bill Gates (!) proposed a suboptimal algorithm,
is related problem of prefix reversal sorting, i.e. only sequences starting from the first element
are allowed to be flipped. In the present challenge we are dealing with full reversals, or just reversals - any subsequence is allowed to be flipped. 

**Broader Mathematical Context - groups and Cayley graphs**
From a mathematical  perspective  it is a typical task in mathematics: decompose an element of a group into a product of given generators (""moves""), or, in the other words, find a path on the [Cayley graph](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cayley_graph) of a group. For example - [bubble sort](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bubble_sort) algorithm provides the shortest paths on the the [permutohedron](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permutohedron) Cayley graph - with nodes all n! permutations, and edges connect permutations different only in neighbour positions - thus represents a permutation as a sequence of neighbour transpositions. Length of the decomposition (path) is called ""[word metric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_metric)"" in group theory. Research on Cayley graphs is of fundamental importance for mathematics and the field contains many notoriously hard problems, which has been addressed by renowned mathematicians such as [M. Gromov](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mikhael_Gromov_(mathematician)) ([theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gromov%27s_theorem_on_groups_of_polynomial_growth)) and [T. Tao](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terence_Tao) ([posts](https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2010/07/10/cayley-graphs-and-the-geometry-of-groups/)), among others. Moreover, it has numerous applications, ranging from bioinformatics (e.g., estimating evolutionary distance¡ª""[Transform men into mice](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/492588/)"" ??), to the optimisation of [interprocessor networks](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/21148), and beyond.  Famous computer scientist [Donald Knuth ](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Knuth) contributed to [improving the algorithm](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9201304) to solve the problems like that. Such algorithm is implemented in computer algebra systems like [GAP/SAGE](https://doc.sagemath.org/html/en/reference/groups/sage/groups/perm_gps/cubegroup.html), however these methods are [not efficient](https://mathoverflow.net/q/482661/10446) ([notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/alexandervc/gap-cannot-solve-rubik-s-cube-444)) on large groups. And the challenge is to improve them. 

**Broader RL Context - RL with severely sparse reward**
The problem is a typical reinforcement learning problem - states of puzzle (nodes of Cayley graph) is a space of states, moves (edges of Cayley graph) - are actions, while rewards (penalties) are always ""1"", unless you found the solution. 
The cumulative reward (penalty) becomes exactly the length of the path to the solution state. And the goal is to minimize it, i.e. to find the shortest path. Value function has very clear and natural interpretation - length of the path. Bellman equation for the value function comes from trivial relation on path length. (See [CayleyPy-RL](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18663) for more details). There is a severe  ""sparse reward problem"", since ""always 1"" is meaningless reward (only tiny detail that for the solution state reward is not 1, but 0, makes setup meaningful). Thus advancing the challenge you will contribute to development of RL methods overcoming the ""sparse reward problem"" on extremely huge spaces like 10^33 and beyond.

**Similar Challenges**
[Kaggle Santa 2023 challenge ](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/santa-2023) - several puzzles: cubes, rings, globes. 
LRX series - [OEIS-A186783 conjecture](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/lrx-oeis-a-186783-brainstorm-math-conjecture),  [Towards God's algorithm](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/lrx-discover-math-gods-algorithm) , [Binary states 0/1](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/lrx-binary-in-search-of-gods-number) - just three moves - L,R - left/right cyclic shifts, X - eXchange positions 0 and 1.   Questions go back to  ""one of the founding fathers of Soviet cybernetics"" [V.M. Glushkov](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_Glushkov) 1968 seminal work and are still unsolved. See survey [link1](https://library.keldysh.ru/prep_vw.asp?lg=e&pid=8508) , [link2](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/alexandervc/cayleypy-development-3-growth-computations/data?select=Glukhov_Zubov1999review.pdf) (pages 21-22).


[Pancake sorting](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/CayleyPy-pancake)
[Transposons](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-transposons)
[Reversals](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-reversals)
[Glushkov problem](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-glushkov)
[RapapportM2](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-rapapport-m2)
[Rubik's cube 444](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-444-cube)
[Professor Tetraminx](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-professor-tetraminx-solve-optimally)
[Megaminx](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-megaminx)
[Christophers jewel](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-christophers-jewel)
[SuperCube from IHES](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayleypy-ihes-cube)
----------------------
# Description

One needs to make sorting with specific transformations called ""reversals"", denoted ""R[i,j]"", 
such transformation takes a subsequence from i-th to j-th position, and just reverts its order.

Biologically it corresponds to break of DNA sequence at two positions i and j, and then cell reparation machinery puts the fragment back to its position, but it can make mistake and can reverse the order of the genes.

The function generating such permutation transformations can be found [here](https://cayleypy.github.io/cayleypy-docs/generated/cayleypy.PermutationGroups.html#cayleypy.PermutationGroups.full_reversals). 

The number of such transformation between two such sequences (DNAs) may be useful to estimate the evolutionary distance between two different genomes. 

The task is exactly to find as short sequence of transformations as possible. 
----------------------
# Input data, Solutions, Evaluation

## Brief 

The setup can be briefly described as follows: 

- Input data - vectors with integer coordinates (""test.csv"")
- Solution -   for each input vector -  find sequences of ""moves"" such that applying it to the input vector one gets the fully sorted vector: (0,1,2,3,...).  How to find - that is the challenge. 
- To apply move-vectors to state-vector -  just do numpy reindexing ([example notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/alexandervc/cayleypy-professor-tetraminx-introduction)): 
```python
state_vector[ move1_vector][move2_vector]...[moveN_vector]
```  
- Move names and corresponding vectors are stored in [graphs_info.json](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cayley-py-transposons/data?select=graphs_info.json). 
- Submission: should contain the sequences of move names separated by dots for each sample,
also can be ""UNSOLVED"", then scores add 100 000 for each.
- Evaluation: sum of the lengths of solutions (how many moves used in total - i.e.  first count number of moves for each test sample and then sum over all  samples, for  each ""UNSOLVED"" will be added 100 000).

## Details

Here we repeat with more details what was briefly described above.

### States and moves  are integer vectors  (permutations)

To work with such puzzles on mathematical and programming levels, we assume each tile to be indexed with a unique number. In this setting each state of the puzzle is a permutation of the indices. The moves of the puzzle are permutations as well, which tell how the locations of the indices are swapped after the move.

In the scope of this competition we assume each tile to have a unique color. The puzzle is called to be ""solved"" if the **solution state** is achieved:
 
```python
solution_state = [0, 1, 2, 3, ...]
```  
The full set of the moves can be found in  ""graphs_info.json"". 

### Input data - multiple integer vectors (""initial states"" - permutations) 

Submissions are evaluated on a set of **initial states** of the puzzle. There are 1800 states in the test set. 

For each **initial state** the participant needs to provide a set of moves to get to the **solution state**. If there is a path in submission which doesn't lead to the **solution state**, the whole submission is considered invalid.

### Evaluation: score = number of moves

For a valid submission the final score are the sums of the lengths of all paths:

$$ score = \sum_{path \in Test}\mathrm{len}(path) $$

We also allow string ""UNSOLVED"" instead of actual solution, in that case score 100 000 would be added for each such string.

The lower the score the better the result is.

Some solutions paths used for generation the samples are provided in sample_submission.csv. 


## Submission File
For each initial_state_id in the test set, you need to generate a path from the **initial state** (from test.csv to the **solution state** . 

The path is a string of dot-separated generator names. The generator are assumed to be applied to the **initial state** to the **solution state** starting from left to right.  The generators and the solution state can be found in ""graphs_info.json"".

Make sure that the values in  **initial_state_id** column are ordered from 0 to 1999.


| initial_state_id | path |
| :-------- | :-------: |
| 0 | R[6,11] |
| 1 | R[2,5].R[0,8] |
| 2 | R[0,1].R[6,7].R[0,10]  |
|...|...|

----------------------
# Further Information

# Related fundamental mathematical conjectures by [L.Babai](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%A1szl%C3%B3_Babai), [P.Diaconis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persi_Diaconis)

Although that particular challenge is narrowly focused on a particular puzzle group, it is provides an illustration for deep mathematical problems, some which are easy to state, but they seems out of reach of the present mathematics. 

Babai-like conjecture for symmetric (permutation) group of n-letters: whatever generators (moves) you choose the God's number (diameter of the Cayley graph) is not more than n^2. I.e. any two permutations can be transformed to each other by not more than n^2 moves (whatever moves you select -- with the only condition all permutations are achievable by them). 
Babai's conjecture is actually more general, T.Tao and others made huge progress on it recent years (see [blog](https://seaneberhard.com/2020/05/21/babais-conjecture-for-at-least-three-random-generators/), [survey13](https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.0239), [survey19](https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.06308) ), but it is still open as well as n^2 conjecture above. The particular conjecture for permutation group goes back at least to V.Rubtsov who raised the question  and supported it by computational experiments in 1975(!). ([Link1](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01071362), [Link2](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/alexandervc/cayleypy-development-3-growth-computations/data?select=Rubtsov1976_MaxDiamSearch.pdf)). 

The other fundamental conjecture is due to famous ""mathematician and magician"" [Persi Diaconis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persi_Diaconis). 
It states that whatever moves you choose the mixing time would not be more than n^3 log(n) ([Question 2 page 9](https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.7754)). That roughly speaking predicts that whatever way you shuffle n cards, then n^3 log(n) random shuffles suffices to get random configuration.

# Optimal solution - typically NP-complete (and harder) problems 

It has been proved already in 80-ies that finding the optimal solutions (and diameters) for general permutation groups is hard.
NP-hardness for both optimal decomposition and diameter estimation: [The minimum-length generator sequence problem is NP-hard](https://doi.org/10.1016/0196-6774(81)90029-8), 1981, S Even, O Goldreich
Strengthening to [P-space competeness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PSPACE-complete) (conjecturally outside NP) for optimal decomposition even for two generators : [The complexity of finding minimum-length generator sequences](https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-3975(85)90047-7), 1986, Mark R. Jerrum

In 2010-ies, results were found for concrete generators, even for Rubik's cube:
[Solving the Rubik's Cube Optimally is NP-complete](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06708), 2017, Erik D. Demaine et.al.
[Pancake Flipping is Hard](https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.0434), 2011, L. Bulteau, et.al.
And many others

# God's number for most of the groups/puzzles unknown - open research problems 

Determining God's number (diameter) for the standard Rubik's cube was a long searched result  [remarkably resolved only in 2010](https://www.cube20.org/) in HTM metric, and in 2014 for [QTM metric](https://www.cube20.org/qtm/). It is not yet known for [STM](https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Metric#STM) and other metrics for 333 cube. Neither it is known for majority of other puzzles. Known estimates are collected at [speedsolving site](https://www.speedsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=God%27s_Algorithm#Table_of_God.27s_Numbers).  

Recent papers propose precise conjecture for 444,555 cubes [Hirata1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.07337), [Hirata2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12961). But they are not proved. 

Developing methods to estimate diameters (God's numbers) would be noteworthy achievement. 

# More math: [Poincare conjecture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincar%C3%A9_conjecture), [Markoff triples](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markov_number), automatic theorem proving

From Poincare conjecture to Cayley graph pathfinding with Reinforcement Learning.
[Poincare conjecture in 3d](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincar%C3%A9_conjecture) was selected as one of 7 ""[Millenium Prize Problems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennium_Prize_Problems)"" -  hardest and most important problems in mathematics in 20-th century. It was ingeniously resolved by G. Perelman. However its twin brother - smooth Poincare conjecture in four dimensions  ""remains open and is thought to be very difficult""([link](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincar%C3%A9_conjecture#Dimensions)). Moreover there is no consensus in community should it be true or not. Prominent mathematicians [Selman Akbulut](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selman_Akbulut) and [Robion Kirby](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robion_Kirby) proposed conjectural counterexamples to it and several other conjectures in 1985: ¡°A potential smooth counterexample in dimension 4 to the Poincare conjecture, the Schoenflies conjecture, and the Andrews¨CCurtis conjecture¡±([link](https://doi.org/10.1016/0040-9383(85)90010-2)).  Some of these potential counterexamples  were only [refuted in 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.15332)  with the help of the reinforcement learning. Essentially the problem is almost the same as described in that challenge - find a path between two elements of a space where a group acts. However the structure of the group is more complicated - infinite group of the Andrews¨CCurtis transformations. 

[Markoff triples](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markov_number) are solutions of certain  Markov Diophantine equation, the subject going back to 19-th century and attracted attention of many prominent mathematicians. 
[J.Ellenberg](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jordan_Ellenberg) in a beautiful [blog post](https://quomodocumque.wordpress.com/2016/05/11/bourgain-sarnak-gamburd-on-markoff-triples/): ""[Bourgain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Bourgain), [Gamburd](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Gamburd), [Sarnak](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Sarnak) on [Markoff triples](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markov_number)""  emphasises  that: ""Again, I emphasize that this is on its face a question of pure combinatorial group theory.  You want to know if you can get from any pair of elements in SL_2(F_p) with negative-unipotent commutator to any other via the two moves above.  You can set this up on your computer and check that it holds for lots and lots of p (they did.)"" That is reiterated in recent ML Cayley graph pathfinding [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05558) by [M.Douglas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_R._Douglas) and K.Fraser-Taliente. Who emphasised that such problems can be approached with AI-helped pathfinding and can be considered as ""warm-up"" for previously discussed Andrews¨CCurtis conjecture work. 

So in a nutshell both problems ask about existence of a path for some elements in the space where group acts. Potential counterexamples suggesting that path does not exist - might be sometimes resolved by explicit pathfinding. 

Automatic theorem proving.
In a sense, doing mathematics - is actually a pathfinding - to prove a theorem one needs to find a sequence  of arguments which lead from it to axioms or known statements. That can be formalised with the help of e.g. [Lean](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lean_(proof_assistant)) the language to give ""formal proofs"". So formally speaking it is the same pathfinding problem, however difficulty lies in enormously larger space of possible moves and states. Although systems like AlphaGeometry were quite successful on solving geometric problems with more or less similar approach as one can use for puzzles/Cayley graphs, the gap between elementary geometry problems and real math research problems is still large. 

# Analogies with games and AlphaGo/Zero approach

Games like Go, chess represent the same class of problems - one needs to find a path on a huge graph - nodes are positions, edges are moves. One searches the path from initial position to any position marked ""win"". The difference is in two-players scenario which means that after move is done by one player, then position changes independently on his will (second player makes a move). So it is a path-finding on a graph in somewhat changing environment. Nevertheless, conceptually, it is the same class of pathfinding problems.  Group/Puzzle solving represents in that respect a more simple scenario - when environment is not changing. Success of AlphaGo handling the Go game with 10^170 positions suggests the groups/puzzles of similar size should be tractable. The difficulty of the group/puzzle case lies in the different direction - there are many different groups and ideal system should effectively work on all them (it is not obvious that same method can work e.g. for  nilpotent and simple groups - which are quire different). And, moreover, ideally, to be very fast - to enable users (mathematicians) to conduct effectively computational experiments. 

# Fractals and puzzles - beautiful fractal images from groups

Discover  beautiful fractal images from groups: ""Investigation of these groups reveals a new family of fractals, as well as a rich structure that is intriguing both mathematically and artistically."" [2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12950), even more [videos](https://www.twodisks.org/play/?r1=1.555&r2=1.48&n1=17&n2=4&zoom=1.382636655948553) (thanks to Tomas Rokicki).",,False,True
