10124,ninja-neural-nets-dogs-vs-cats,"# Overview

# ?? Ninja Neural Nets?? ¨C Dogs vs Cats Challenge

**Goal:** Train a deep-learning model that predicts whether an image is a **cat (0)** or a **dog (1)**.

Anyone can join, team up, or leave at any time ¡ª no complex rules.  
Use any technique you like (CNNs, transfer learning, pure NumPy) and submit a simple CSV of predictions.  
The leaderboard ranks teams by **accuracy** on hidden test data.

> ?? Strike silently, code swiftly, and climb the leaderboard with ninja precision.

----------------------
# Description

# ?? Detailed Description

Welcome to the **??Ninja Neural Nets?? ¨C Dogs vs Cats Challenge**.

## ???? The Task
Classify each test image as **Cat (0)** or **Dog (1)** using any model you like ¡ª CNNs, transfer learning, or your own ninja technique.

## ?? Data
- **train/cats** ¨C 12,500 labeled images
- **train/dogs** ¨C 12,500 labeled images
- **test/** ¨C unlabeled images for scoring
- **sample_submission.csv** ¨C shows the required `id,label` format

## ?? How to Compete
1. **Create a Kaggle Notebook** inside this competition.  
   - Your full training & prediction code must run here.  
   - Keep it public or private during the contest; it must be shareable if you win.
2. Generate the predictions and a CSV like:
   ```csv
   id,label
   1.jpg,0
   2.jpg,1
```

3. **Submit both**:

   * **Notebook**: click **Save & Submit Notebook** so reviewers can reproduce your results.
   * **CSV File**: upload your predictions on the **Submissions** page.

## ?? Goal

Learn deep learning together ¡ª **submit a working notebook and the CSV output** to climb the leaderboard with ninja precision.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on **classification accuracy** ¡ª  
the percentage of test images correctly labeled as **cat (0)** or **dog (1)**.

## Submission File
For each image in the test set, submit a CSV file with exactly these columns and a header row:

```

id,label
1.jpg,0
2.jpg,1
...

```

- **id** ¨C test image filename (include `.jpg`, no folder path).  
- **label** ¨C predicted class: `0` for cat, `1` for dog.

## Notebook Requirement
Your final score will only count if you also **submit a Kaggle Notebook** that:
- trains your model,  
- generates the submitted CSV,  
- runs end-to-end without hidden data.

## Leaderboard
- **Public Leaderboard**: shows accuracy on a subset of test images during the competition.  
- **Private Leaderboard**: final ranking based on the full hidden test set revealed at the end.

> ?? Strike silently, code swiftly, and let your notebook + CSV prove your ninja accuracy.","# Dataset Description

# ?? Data Description ¨C ??Ninja Neural Nets?? Dogs vs Cats

This challenge uses the [Dogs vs Cats dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/princelv84/dogsvscats) (MIT License).  
It contains labeled training images and an unlabeled test set for scoring.

---

## ?? Files

* **train/**  
  - `cats/` ¨C 5,000 JPG images of cats  
  - `dogs/` ¨C 5,000 JPG images of dogs  

* **test/**  
  - Unlabeled JPG images of cats and dogs mixed together  
  - Filenames look like `1.jpg`, `2.jpg`, etc.

* **sample_submission.csv**  
  - A template showing the exact submission format:
    ```csv
    id,label
    1.jpg,0
    2.jpg,1
    ```

---

## ?? What to Predict
For every image in **test/**, predict whether it is a **cat (0)** or a **dog (1)**.

---

## ??? Data Format & Notes
- All images are standard RGB `.jpg` files.  
- No extra metadata or hidden acronyms¡ªjust clean images and filenames.  
- Use any preprocessing or augmentation techniques you like.

---

> ?? Tip: Build and run your model inside a Kaggle Notebook, generate the required CSV, and submit both notebook and CSV to compete.",False,True
10125,imputation-benchmarking-xenium-fold-3,"# Overview

Spatial transcriptomics lets us measure gene expression while keeping track of where each measurement comes from in the tissue. The data are usually organized as a *spot¨Cgene matrix*, where each spot represents a small location in the tissue and captures mRNA from that spot. 

There are two main types of SRT techniques: imaging-based methods, which have high spatial resolution and can see gene expression at the level of individual cells but detect fewer genes, and sequencing-based methods, which cover larger spots that may contain multiple cells, capturing more genes at higher throughput. Because of these differences, SRT datasets often have missing or incomplete gene expression values. 

This creates an important computational challenge: how can we accurately fill in, or impute, these missing values while keeping both the spatial layout of the tissue and the relationships between genes intact? ??

?? **Goal:** The goal of this assignment is to benchmark and evaluate algorithms designed for imputing missing gene expression in spatial transcriptomics datasets.
----------------------
# Evaluation

For evaluating the imputation accuracy, you have to use 
1. **Pearson Correlation Coefficient (PCC)** 
2. **Structural Similarity Index (SSIM)**.

PCC is evaluated on the leaderboard. You need to calculate SSIM and mention it in the report for all the methods assigned to you.

## Submission File
For each spot in the ST dataset, you must predict the expression level of the masked genes. The file should contain a *spot x gene* matrix with the imputed expressions of the missing genes.","# Dataset Description

- Spatial data is of dimension *166363¡Á313*
- Matched snRNA-seq data is of dimension *30365 ¡Á 18082*. 
- The number of shared genes is 307.


## Files

The **Xenium dataset** can be found under the name *xenium_fold_3.h5ad*

**The corresponding single-cell data can be found at the link**
https://drive.google.com/file/d/1h8hC9nwYgHkJ6ThjeGIDAXoBrScdRLCk/view?usp=sharing

*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## ?? (***IMPORTANT***) Submission file details 
- Your matrix should have the first column as ""Spot_id"" mentioning the spot index (str). 
- The subsequent columns should have the names of the missing genes in the particular fold, and they should contain the expression level as predicted by the imputation method (double).",False,True
10126,cirrhosis-outcomes-challenge-a-multi-class-prediction-task,"# Overview

**Welcome to the Kaggle Playground Series!**

The Playground Series is designed to provide the Kaggle community with approachable yet challenging machine learning problems that help participants sharpen their predictive modeling skills. Whether you are just beginning your data science journey or looking to refine advanced techniques, this competition offers an excellent opportunity to experiment, learn, and compete in a supportive environment.

### **Competition Overview**

Cirrhosis is a serious, progressive liver condition that can lead to a range of clinical outcomes depending on disease severity, treatment response, and patient-specific factors. Early and accurate prediction of these outcomes can support better clinical decision-making and improve patient care.

In this competition, you will work with structured clinical data containing demographic details, laboratory test results, and treatment-related variables. Your challenge is to develop a **multi-class classification model** capable of predicting the outcome category for each patient.

### **Your Goal**

Build and optimize machine learning models to accurately predict patient outcomes associated with liver cirrhosis. Success in this challenge will require thoughtful data preprocessing, feature engineering, model selection, and evaluation.

Participants are encouraged to explore multiple approaches ¡ª from strong baseline models to advanced ensemble methods ¡ª and uncover meaningful patterns within the dataset.

Whether you focus on model interpretability, predictive performance, or innovative feature strategies, this competition is a chance to push your skills further and gain hands-on experience with real-world healthcare-style data.

Good luck, and happy modeling!

----------------------
# Evaluation

## **Evaluation Metric**

Submissions for this competition are evaluated using **multi-class logarithmic loss (log loss)**.

Each observation in the test set corresponds to a single patient and has exactly **one true outcome label**, represented by the variable `Status`. The possible outcome classes are:

* `Status_C`
* `Status_CL`
* `Status_D`

For every `id` in the test set, competitors must submit **predicted probabilities** for **each of the three outcome classes**.

The log loss metric penalizes confident but incorrect predictions more heavily than uncertain ones, making it especially suitable for probabilistic multi-class classification tasks.

The metric is defined as:

$$
\text{log loss} = -\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{M} y_{ij}\log(p_{ij})
$$

where:

* **N** is the number of rows in the test set
* **M** is the number of outcome classes (3)
* **log** denotes the natural logarithm
* **(y_{ij})** equals 1 if observation *i* belongs to class *j*, and 0 otherwise
* **(p_{ij})** is the predicted probability that observation *i* belongs to class *j*

### **Important Notes**

* The predicted probabilities for a given row **do not need to sum to 1**.
  Before scoring, probabilities for each row are automatically **rescaled so that their sum equals 1**.

To prevent numerical instability caused by extreme values, predicted probabilities are clipped to the range:

$$
\max(\min(p, 1 - 10^{-15}), 10^{-15})
$$

Lower log loss values indicate better model performance.

---

## **Submission Format**

For each `id` in the test set, you must submit predicted probabilities for the three outcome classes: `Status_C`, `Status_CL`, and `Status_D`.

The submission file must:

* Be in **CSV format**
* Contain a **header row**
* Include **one row per `id`**

### **File Structure**

```
id,Status_C,Status_CL,Status_D
7905,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
7906,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
7907,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
...
```

Make sure all predicted probabilities are valid numeric values. Submissions with missing values or incorrect formatting may result in scoring errors.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition was synthetically generated using a deep learning model trained on the **Cirrhosis Patient Survival Prediction** dataset. While the feature distributions closely resemble those of the original data, they are not identical, providing participants with a realistic yet slightly modified modeling challenge.

Competitors are encouraged to explore the relationship between the synthetic and original datasets. Incorporating the original data into your training pipeline may help improve generalization and predictive performance.

This competition focuses on predicting patient outcomes associated with liver cirrhosis ¡ª a progressive condition with varying prognoses depending on clinical factors and treatment pathways.

---

## **Files**

### **train.csv**

The training dataset contains the target variable `Status`, which represents the patient¡¯s outcome:

* **C (Censored):** The patient was alive at `N_Days`.
* **CL (Censored due to Liver Transplant):** The patient was alive at `N_Days` following a liver transplant.
* **D (Deceased):** The patient had passed away by `N_Days`.

Use this dataset to train and validate your multi-class classification models.

---

### **test.csv**

The test dataset has the same features as the training data but does **not** include the target variable.

Your objective is to predict the probability for each possible outcome:

* `Status_C`
* `Status_CL`
* `Status_D`

---

### **sample_submission.csv**

A sample submission file demonstrating the correct structure and formatting required for leaderboard evaluation. Use this file as a reference to ensure your submission is properly formatted.",False,True
10127,fall-25-birth-weight-prediction,"# Description

I am quoting from the WebMD article:
¡°A new baby's gender, name, time of birth, and birth weight are nice information for a birth announcement, but birth weight is especially important for an obstetrician. A large size at delivery has long been associated with an increased risk of injuries to a newborn and its mom. So the better a doctor can predict birth weight, the easier the delivery may be.¡±

Ultrasound is a popular way of doing it. But, aha! You are a Data Scientist (or a ¡°going to be one¡±(!)). You can amaze people by predicting the birth weight way earlier than ultrasound, right? In this assignment, let¡¯s accomplish this.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is RMSLE, Root Mean Square Logarithmic Error. 

RMSLE is defined as : 
![RMSLE = ](https://drive.google.com/thumbnail?id=12yVAH2wZO17u4KTC1UN8kplaehvZQd-P&amp;sz=w700-h130)

where, 
* `n` is total number of samples in the evaluation dataset.
* `y_i` is the true/known target label for the `i_th` sample
* y&#770;_i is the predicted target for the input sample xi.
* `log(.)` is the natural logarithm.
<hr>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: ID and BWEIGHT </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID,BWEIGHT<br>1,10.1875<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>Dataset Description</h2>
In the ¡°dataset/¡± you will find two csv and a txt files: 
<ul>
<li>baby-weights-dataset.csv</li>
<ul><li>It has 101400 rows (samples) with 37 columns (variables). Each sample represents a case of a new-born. Very last column is ""BWEIGHT"", that tells the true weight of the new-born (in lbs unit). Actually, this needs to be considered as the target variable here. </li></ul>
<li>data-description.txt</li>
<ul><li>Here, you will find the names of the 37 variables used in the dataset above. And, the source of the dataset did not offer me description of every single of them. But, after studying about them, I could elaborate only few of them. Please pardon my laziness. Okay, this file contains few descriptions for the variables. All the rest are mostly talking about the Mother's medical history and all. No big deal, I guess, for you to work with these variables without knowing their meaning.</li></ul>
<li>judge-without-labels.csv</li>
<ul></li>This is an interesting file. It contains new samples: additional 2000 rows with 36 columns (without the BWEIGHT target column). Predict BWEIGHT values of these 2000 samples. We (me and the TA) will evaluate that as well.</li></ul>
</ul>",False,True
