10147,pig-posture-recognition,"# Overview

### ?? Important Announcement!

Thank you for your strong interest and enthusiastic participation in this challenge.

We have launched an **upgraded version of the pig posture recognition dataset**, featuring **additional training data and more realistic practical challenges**. As a result, **all prizes originally associated with this challenge have been moved to the newly launched *Multi-View Pig Posture Recognition* challenge**.

Participants who previously submitted results to this competition are kindly encouraged to **join the new challenge**, train and evaluate their models using the updated dataset, and submit their results there to be considered for prizes.

This challenge will remain active to allow users to **practice and explore the algorithm**. However, **results and leaderboard rankings from this challenge will not be considered for winner selection in the new challenge**.

Thank you for being part of this effort, and we look forward to your continued participation in the new challenge.

?? **Join the new challenge and access the updated dataset here:**
[https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/multi-view-pig-posture-recognition](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/multi-view-pig-posture-recognition)

## ?? About Challenge
Pig posture recognition is a key task in Precision Livestock Farming, enabling automated monitoring of animal health and welfare. In this competition, participants develop computer vision models to classify pig postures from 2D images captured under realistic farm conditions, including occlusion, lighting variation, and multiple camera views. Using the provided bounding boxes, participants must predict the posture of each pig instance and submit their results in a standardized submission CSV file. Submissions are automatically evaluated, ranked against private leaderboard ground-truth labels, and published on the leaderboard. 
----------------------
# Description

Accurate detection of pig posture in real farm environments remains a challenging problem in computer vision. Unlike controlled laboratory settings, commercial barns present cluttered backgrounds, frequent occlusions, variable lighting conditions, and diverse camera viewpoints. In addition, several pig postures exhibit subtle visual differences, making posture discrimination from 2D images particularly difficult.

This competition aims to advance pig posture recognition by encouraging the development and evaluation of deep learning models under realistic farm conditions. The dataset consists of 3,090 images with 22,934 annotated instances collected at a swine research center at Michigan State University (MSU). Images were acquired using eight overhead cameras and encompass multiple pig breeds (Purebred Yorkshire, Exotic crossbred, and Meishan) and production batches.

The dataset is randomly divided into training and testing subsets, with 70% of the images (2,164 images containing 16,062 instances) allocated for training and the remaining 30%, which follows the similar class distribution, reserved for testing.

<b>The camera array used in data collection</b>
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F29008476%2Fb2b6dc1d276b1698f1abb01a1c76ccec%2Fleaderboard_camera.png?generation=1767893280056824&alt=media)

<b>The training dataset statistics</b>
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F29008476%2F23c49237871155bef287db0eed014051%2Ftrain_statistics.png?generation=1766521350085777&alt=media)

Each image contains 3 to 10 pigs within a pen. For every pig instance, the dataset provides an axis-aligned bounding box (xmin, ymin, w, h) in both the training and test sets. However, posture labels are available only for the training set. Participants are required to predict the posture for each detected pig instance in the test set and submit their results using a standard submission CSV file, which will be automatically evaluated and ranked on the leaderboard.

The task is to classify the posture of each pig into one of the following five categories:

- Lateral_lying_left

- Lateral_lying_right

- Sitting

- Standing

- Sternal_lying

By participating in this challenge, you will not only test your skills in instance-level image classification, representation learning, and model generalization, but also contribute to ongoing research in Precision Livestock Farming (PLF)¡ªan emerging interdisciplinary field where machine learning, sensor technology, and animal science converge to enable smarter, more sustainable livestock production systems.

The ultimate goal is to build a robust and generalizable posture recognition model that performs consistently across different camera devices, viewpoints, pig populations, and environmental conditions.
----------------------
# ?? Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the macro-averaged F1 score.
This metric computes the F1 score independently for each posture class and then averages the scores, giving equal weight to all classes regardless of their frequency. This makes it particularly suitable for imbalanced datasets.

<pre>F1 = 2 ¡Á (precision ¡Á recall) / (precision + recall)</pre>

Scores range from 0.0 to 1.0, where 1.0 indicates perfect classification performance, and 0.0 indicates no correct predictions.

The final competition ranking is based on the private leaderboard, which is evaluated using a Solution CSV file.

## Submission Format

Each submission must be a CSV file with **two columns**:

| Column     | Description                            |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------- |
| `row_id`   | Unique pig instance ID from `test.csv` |
| `class_id` | Predicted posture class (integer 0¨C4)  |

### Example

```csv
row_id,class_id
test_00001001_0000,3
test_00001001_0001,2
```

Must exactly match the row_id of test.csv.

- label

An integer value from 0 to 4, corresponding to the following posture classes:
<hr>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Class ID</th>
      <th>Posture Name</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>0</td><td>Lateral_lying left</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Lateral_lying right</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Sitting</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Standing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Sternal_lying</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<hr>
Submissions with missing test images, incorrect labels, or formatting errors may result in a submission failure.

----------------------
# Prizes

All prizes originally associated with this challenge have been moved to the newly launched Multi-View Pig Posture Recognition challenge.

?? **Join the new challenge and access the updated dataset here:**
[https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/multi-view-pig-posture-recognition](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/multi-view-pig-posture-recognition)","# Dataset Description

### ?? Important Announcement

Thank you for your strong interest and enthusiastic participation in this challenge.

We have launched an **upgraded version of the pig posture recognition dataset**, featuring **additional training data and more realistic practical challenges**. As a result, **all prizes and awards originally associated with this challenge have been moved to the newly launched *Multi-View Pig Posture Recognition* challenge**.

Participants who previously submitted results to this competition are kindly encouraged to **join the new challenge**, train and evaluate their models using the updated dataset, and submit their results there to be considered for prizes.

This challenge will remain active to allow users to **practice and explore the algorithm**. However, **results and leaderboard rankings from this challenge will not be considered for winner selection in the new challenge**.

Thank you for being part of this effort, and we look forward to your continued participation in the new challenge.

?? **Join the new challenge and access the updated dataset here:**
[https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/multi-view-pig-posture-recognition](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/multi-view-pig-posture-recognition)

### About data

This dataset contains **RGB images of pigs captured in real farm environments**, annotated for the task of **pig posture recognition**.
The objective is to develop a computer vision model that **focuses on individual pigs using provided bounding boxes**, extracts visual features from each instance, and **classifies the posture of each pig instance**.

Images were collected under realistic farm conditions, including **multiple pigs per frame, background clutter, occlusion, and varying illumination**.

Each pig instance is annotated with an **axis-aligned bounding box** and one of five posture classes.

---

## Posture Classes

| Class ID | Posture Name        |
| -------: | ------------------- |
|        0 | Lateral_lying_left  |
|        1 | Lateral_lying_right |
|        2 | Sitting             |
|        3 | Standing            |
|        4 | Sternal_lying       |

---

## Dataset Structure

The dataset is split into **training** and **test** subsets:

```
pig_posture_recognition/
©À©¤©¤ train_images/                 # Training images
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 00000001.jpg
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 00000002.jpg
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ ...
©À©¤©¤ test_images/                  # Test images
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 00002165.jpg
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 00002166.jpg
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ ...
©À©¤©¤ train.csv                     # Training annotations (bbox + posture labels)
©À©¤©¤ test.csv                      # Test annotations (bbox only)
©À©¤©¤ sample_submission.csv         # Example submission format
©¸©¤©¤ pig_posture_classes.txt       # Mapping of class IDs to posture names
```

---

## Training Annotations (`train.csv`)

Each row in `train.csv` corresponds to **one pig instance** (one bounding box).

### Columns

| Column            | Description                                                  |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| row_id          | Unique identifier for each pig instance                      |
| image_id       | Image filename (e.g., 00000001.jpg)                            |
| width, height | Original image dimensions (pixels)                           |
| bbox            | Bounding box in [xmin, ymin, w, h] format (absolute pixel units) |
| class_id        | Posture class ID (0¨C4)                                       |

### Example

```csv
row_id,image_id,width,height,bbox,class_id
train_00000001_0000,00000001.jpg,1920,1080,[967.5,331.5,463.0,447.0],3
```

**Note:**
Bounding box coordinates are provided in **absolute pixel units** relative to the original image resolution and are **not normalized**.

---

## Test Set (`test.csv`)

All test images are provided in the `/test_images` directory.

The `test.csv` file contains **ground-truth bounding boxes only** (no posture labels).
Participants must **predict the posture class for each bounding box listed in `test.csv`**.

Ground-truth posture labels for the test set are **hidden** and used exclusively for leaderboard evaluation.

---

## Evaluation Metric

Submissions are evaluated using the **macro-averaged F1 score**, computed across the five posture classes.

A prediction is considered correct if:

* The predicted posture class matches the ground-truth label for the corresponding **pig instance (`row_id`)**

Macro-F1 assigns **equal importance to all posture classes**, making it suitable for class imbalance.

---

## Submission Format

Each submission must be a CSV file with **two columns**:

| Column     | Description                            |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------- |
| `row_id`   | Unique pig instance ID from `test.csv` |
| `class_id` | Predicted posture class (integer 0¨C4)  |

### Example

```csv
row_id,class_id
test_00002165_0000,0
test_00002165_0001,1
```

---

## Notes

* Bounding boxes are **axis-aligned** and provided for **both training and test sets**
* Multiple pigs may appear in a single image
* Participants **do not need to predict bounding boxes**
* The task focuses purely on **instance-level posture classification under real-world farm conditions**

