10180,alpha-radar-solana-sprint,"# Overview

This competition challenges you to **build a machine learning model that can identify a subset of tokens within a dataset**.  This is not a dataset that is open ended and exposes you to false positives and the frustration of engineered tokens designed to be deceptive. With this you are just looking for the best way to identify a subset of our dataset. 

**Your goal:**

Engineer features using the provided Pumpfun dataset and train a CatBoost model which reliably predicts the target tokens, while minimizing false positives.  75% minimum returned results is the baseline for a successful submission. 

**Provided:**

??  ?A 30 day dataset of Pumpfun token transactions.  This dataset has already been processed into a core set of transaction metadata and technical indicators.
??  ?A CSV of target tokens

**Requirements:**

??  ?Use the provided dataset to engineer your features, do not supplement the dataset with additional external sources. 
??  ?Use CatBoost
??  ?The prediction must be achieved within the first 30 seconds of a token lifespan (e.g. from the tokens first transaction based on dataset timestamp).
Submissions that just search for the goal data in the larger dataset to return a 100% result will not be considered. The goal is to engineer a model to predict the target tokens not just find the target tokens in the dataset by using the target tokens.. 

**Deliverables:**

??  ?A Jupiter notebook with your solution
??  ?A CSV file of all tokens which exceeded your prediction threshold. (token, threshold, prediction value, isTargetToken)

You can ask questions in the Discussion section or join the community https://discord.gg/cabApvps.
----------------------
# Description

Most Pumpfun tokens on Solana are short-lived. Some get rugged, some fade with little traction, and a rare few take off.

This competition gives you access to 30 days of Pumpfun token transactions. The dataset has already been processed into a core set of transaction metadata and technical indicators, making it easier to build features while still staying close to the raw behavior of tokens.

In addition, we provide a CSV of target tokens ¡ª those identified as profitable by a high-performing wallet¡¯s strategy.

**Your task:**

Engineer features only from the provided Pumpfun dataset

Train a CatBoost model to predict with at least 75% accuracy, which tokens are likely to be part of the profitable subset

Ensure predictions are made within the first 30 seconds of a token¡¯s lifespan (based on timestamp)

**This challenge is:**

*Beginner-friendly:* clean dataset with ready-to-use structure

*Advanced:* requires creative feature engineering and precision to minimize false positives

*Practical:* mirrors real-world token discovery pipelines in Solana

External data or alternative ML frameworks (LightGBM, XGBoost, deep learning, etc.) are not allowed.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the Jaccard Index (Intersection-over-Union), comparing your predicted positive set to the true target set on a private holdout. Provide a confusion matrix using Jaccard scoring in your results. Higher Jaccard indicates better performance.

Minimum 75% Recall on the private set is required for consideration.
----------------------
# Prizes

The participants or teams are eligible for these total prizes which will be sent as USDC to a confirmed Solana wallet after the contest is over and the winners awarded.

Total Prizes Available: 6,000 USDC

1st Place - 3,000 USDC
2nd Place - 1,500 USDC
3rd Place - 1,000 USDC
4th Place - 500 USDC","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains the first 30 seconds of post-mint transaction data for PumpFun tokens created in the month of September 2025.
The data was scraped using a proprietary parsing tool from a private Solana node.
The categories are fixed and must be adhered to.

**Data Sources**

The full 30-second dataset:
?? https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/twainayar/pumpfun-30s-september-2025

The target tokens (wallet buys):
?? https://drive.google.com/file/d/1EsqpZXPBU-6m0djDmccCrtUX07jV2fHA/view?usp=sharing

**The Goal**

The target tokens are contained within the larger dataset.
Using the available categories, engineer a CatBoost model that can isolate at least 75 % of the target tokens within the first 30 seconds of their lifespan.

**Update (October 2025)**

A new evaluation dataset has been uploaded to prevent data leakage and improve leaderboard accuracy.
All submissions must now contain exactly 64 208 rows, one per token ID in the evaluation set.

Columns

**index**	Row ID / sequence
**timestamp**	Event time (UTC)
**mint_token_id**	Token mint address
**holder**	Wallet address involved
**trade_mode**	buy / sell / other action type
**token_quantity**	Tokens bought / sold amount
**creator**	Token creator address
**creator_fee**	Creator fee (absolute)
**creator_fee_pump**	PumpFun creator fee
**market_cap_usd**	Circulating market cap (USD)
**token_delta**	Net token change
**sol_delta**	Net SOL spent / received
**buy_count**	Number of buys
**sell_count**	Number of sells
**total_count**	Total transactions (buys + sells)
**token_volume**	Traded token volume
**sol_volume**	Traded SOL volume
**liquidity_ratio**	Liquidity / volume ratio
**virtual_sol_reserves**	Virtual SOL in pool
**virtual_token_reserves**	Virtual tokens in pool
**consumed_gas**	Compute units used
**fee**	Transaction fee in SOL
**relative_strength_index**	RSI momentum indicator
**bollinger_relative_position**	Price vs Bollinger bands
**volume_oscillator**	Volume momentum measure
**rate_of_change**	% change over window
**money_flow_index**	Volume-weighted RSI
**total_holders**	All-time unique holders
**current_holders**	Current wallets holding
**top10_percent_total**	Top 10 % holdings share
**creator_balance**	Creator¡¯s current token balance
**creator_sold**	Tokens sold by creator
**holder_ratio**	Holders / supply metric
**buy_sell_ratio**	Buys / sells ratio

**Prediction Target**

Your objective is to predict whether a token appears in the Target Token Dataset.
This is a binary classification task.

Target label:

is_target = 1 ¡ú token was bought by the target wallet  
is_target = 0 ¡ú token was not bought

Submission Format

Your submission must be a CSV with the following structure:

mint_token_id,is_target
<MINT_TOKEN_ID>,<IS_TARGET>


**Example:**

mint_token_id,is_target
14XBCQLJ6A8jifCBFsp1NfW3kG9FxbY7xbiQSxfXpump,1
4shdnB4wA5TA5wphgD1gQFmbaAACtbUyiepLDUoopump,0


**Header row required
Exactly 64 208 rows (one for each token in the evaluation set)
is_target values must be binary (0 or 1)**",False,True
10181,circom-challenge-2025,"# Overview

**Circom** is a domain specific language (DSL) central to **modern zero-knowledge proof systems**, powering cryptographic protocols and privacy-preserving computations. Writing robust Circom code requires both logical reasoning and syntactic precision, something current LLMs often struggle with.

**Your task is to delve into LLMs and ML engineering for code generation - find a way to get models to generate correct Circom code!**

Your submitted notebook will then run on kaggle and your model will be evaluated against 50 problems on Circom, and it must generate the code for implementing each of them. 

If the circom code generated by your model passes all the test cases for a problem, you get one added to your score. And so, there are 50 total problems to be grabbed. The best model wins. 

**The higher you score the more points you get : )**

Originally a part of Silicon Maze 2025, now open for all : )
----------------------
# Details

### Dataset Structure
The competition includes:
- **20 Public Problems** (visible during the competition)
- **30 Private Problems** (included in final scoring)

Each problem comes with multiple test cases that will be used to verify the correctness of your generated Circom code.

### Evaluation Method
Your submitted model will be evaluated against all 50 problems:
0. Your notebook should pass the input public_dataset.json and private_dataset.json into your model
1. For each problem, your model must generate valid Circom code
2. The generated code will be compiled using Circom
3. The compiled code will be tested against multiple test cases
4. A problem is considered solved only if your code passes ALL test cases for that problem
5. Your final score is the total number of correctly solved problems (maximum: 50 problems)
6. This score will later be scaled accordingly in the Silicon Maze leaderboard

---

You are free to use any approach that can run on Kaggle environment : )
- Your notebook should not require internet access during evaluation.
- You can upload everything required as datasets / models on kaggle and import it into your notebook
- You can submit the notebooks multiple times, as you improve your submission over time
- Finetuning, which base model to use, which parameters to use etc. are all up to you. 


----------------------
# Hints

## Internet

Your submitted notebook will not have internet access during execution. Please remove any internet-dependent code before final submission. Your notebook should only generate and submit the required CSV file, without needing to compile code via Circom.

The backend environment already includes the Circom compiler, so evaluation will run smoothly without any internet access.

## Circom Setup in Kaggle Environment

To set up Circom in your Kaggle notebook, run the following code at the beginning:
**Make sure to remove it before submitting it to the competition as it involves downloading the libraries via the Internet**

```python
%%writefile install_circom.sh
#!/bin/bash
set -eux

# Ensure Rust is installed
if ! command -v cargo &> /dev/null; then
  echo ""Rust not found. Installing...""
  curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh -s -- -y
fi

# Load Rust env if exists
if [ -f ""$HOME/.cargo/env"" ]; then
  source ""$HOME/.cargo/env""
else
  export PATH=""$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH""
fi

# Verify Rust
rustc --version
cargo --version

# Install nvm (Node Version Manager) and Node.js 18
if ! command -v node &> /dev/null || [[ $(node -v | cut -d. -f1) != ""v18"" ]]; then
  echo ""Installing nvm and Node.js 18...""
  curl -o- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh/nvm/v0.39.6/install.sh | bash
  export NVM_DIR=""$HOME/.nvm""
  [ -s ""$NVM_DIR/nvm.sh"" ] && \. ""$NVM_DIR/nvm.sh""
  nvm install 18
  nvm use 18
fi

# Verify Node.js
node -v
npm -v

# Clone Circom repo if missing
cd /kaggle/working
if [ ! -d ""circom"" ]; then
  git clone https://github.com/iden3/circom.git
fi
cd circom

# Build and install Circom only if missing
if ! command -v circom &> /dev/null; then
  cargo build --release
  cargo install --path circom
else
  echo ""Circom already installed, skipping build/install.""
fi

#  Ensure Cargo bin in PATH
export PATH=""$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH""

#  Verify Circom
circom --version

#  Install snarkjs globally
npm install -g snarkjs

#  Verify snarkjs
snarkjs --help | head -n 5
```

Then give it execution permissions and run it:

```python
!chmod +x install_circom.sh
!./install_circom.sh
```

Make sure you set files and environment as ""persistent"" in the settings to prevent having to reinstall every time.",,False,True
10182,ml-approach-to-personality,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2025 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** Your objective is to predict whether a person is an Introvert or Extrovert, given their social behavior and personality traits.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using [Accuracy Score](https://www.kaggle.com/code/metric/accuracy-score) between the predicted value and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `Personality`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Personality
    18524,Extrovert
    18525,Introvert
    18526,Introvert
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - ${competition.DateEnabled}
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  ${competition.Deadline}

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!",,False,True
10183,hat-or-no-hat-that-is-the-question-fall-25,"# Overview

The problem is simple. Bring your recently learned Deep Learning knowledge to detect if a given image contains a person with a hat or not. Simple, right?
----------------------
# Description

You are not given any specific training dataset! Strange, right? But, you do need a training dataset to build model. You can do this in at least one of two approach: (i) build your own labeled training dataset, by going out taking photos, or web search, (ii) in the past I found students using the CelebA dataset ( [https://mmlab.ie.cuhk.edu.hk/projects/CelebA.html](https://mmlab.ie.cuhk.edu.hk/projects/CelebA.html) ) which coincidentally  has an attribute called `Wearing hat`, which you will find advantageous for this project. For the first approach, if it seems collecting images is easy than to label them, you may resort to using the `semi-supervised learning` technique. You may not need to go down that road, but I am just saying :) 

You need to build a deep learning model and predict all the test images for the `hat-ness` test, i.e., if image contains a person with a hat or not. This provided test set should be considered for judging, and of course is unlabeled, and used to produce the rank of your entry in the live ranklist.

My policy while constructing the `test-set` was:
1. Photos of real people, i.e., absolutely no cartoons, nor apparitions.
2. Photos may contain multiple people with their heads (with or without hat) visible. If I see at least one person wearing hat, I would label it as a `Hat` instance.
3. If a person's head is covered either with a scarf, or bandana, hijab, beanie, etc, might as well be considered person with `hat` during evaluation.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on `Categorization accuracy`. Higher the better.

Please note, a sample with `id` should contain a corresponding `class` value that belongs to this set: `{'Hat', 'No Hat'}`.


## Submission File
For each `id` corresponding to an image file in the `test-set/` directory, you must predict a class label for the `class` variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,class
    a51f75effb1ddecbdc415f2c04856dca,No Hat
    4f1a9f55fec2af7a38e425098955b53e,No Hat
    494027738fc26f214792b9c389455528,No Hat
    07af6e551654de3d522a79f87801b288,No Hat
    fc965946777a2192bd76fc768a7a87d2,No Hat
    d9cee025cb3007e59f903724fc4ebab4,No Hat
    8207a51323622887aef5c7060705f9b8,No Hat
    877c11ba4e9f4f991bdd959b9a940929,No Hat
    9427c232457eb4f35230ef9557237956,No Hat
    38b77bdf40c69d39affe50e83f9ce1dd,No Hat
    etc.

Please note:
1. The `class` values mentioned above as well as in the `sample_submission.csv` file are intentionally set to a constant value `No Hat`. It was set for illustration purposes only. True values will be used during evaluation.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **test_set/** - the test set directory containing exactly `1490` image files.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
