10195,i-liked-it,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the quality of wine based on various properties. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored based on the?quadratic weighted kappa, which measures the agreement between two outcomes. This metric typically varies from 0 (random agreement) to 1 (complete agreement). In the event that there is less agreement than expected by chance, the?metric may go below 0.

The quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as follows. First, an N?x?N histogram matrix <em>O</em> is constructed, such that <em>O<sub>i,j</sub></em> corresponds to the number of `Id`s <em>i</em> (actual) that received a predicted value <em>j</em>.?An <em>N-by-N </em>matrix of weights, <em>w</em>, is calculated based on the difference between actual and predicted values:

$$w_{i,j} = \frac{\left(i-j\right)^2}{\left(N-1\right)^2}$$

An <em>N-by-N</em> histogram matrix of expected outcomes, <em>E</em>, is calculated assuming that there is no correlation between values.? This is calculated as the outer product between the actual histogram vector of outcomes and the predicted histogram vector, normalized such that <em>E</em> and <em>O</em> have the same sum.

From these three matrices, the quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as:?

$$\kappa=1-\frac{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}O\_{i,j}}{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}E\_{i,j}}.$$


## Submission File
For each `Id` in the test set, you must predict the value for the target `quality`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,quality
    2056,5
    2057,7
    2058,3
    etc.",,False,True
10196,app-of-gen-ai-deep-learning-wustl-fall-2025,"# Overview

# Residential Property Value Prediction

## Overview

This competition challenges participants to predict residential property values based on a comprehensive dataset of home characteristics. The competition is part of two Washington University in St. Louis courses: [T81-558: Applications of Deep Neural Networks](https://github.com/jeffheaton/app_deep_learning) and [T81-559: Applications of Generative AI](https://github.com/jeffheaton/app_generative_ai), taught by instructor [Jeff Heaton](https://sites.wustl.edu/jeffheaton/).

## The Challenge

Participants will predict a property value score (0-100) that represents the relative value of residential properties. This score is influenced by various factors including:

* Major factors: environment type, total area, construction year, energy efficiency, smart home features, and room counts
* Minor factors: architectural style, materials quality, landscaping, and garage capacity

## Dataset Description

This unique dataset combines structured, unstructured, and image data:

### Structured Data
* Physical characteristics (area, bedroom count, bathroom count)
* Construction details (year built, materials, energy rating)
* Property features (garage capacity, smart home level)

### Unstructured Data
* Detailed textual descriptions of interior and exterior features
* Architectural style descriptions
* Landscape and environment descriptions

### Image Data
* Exterior photographs of properties (Note: not all properties may have associated images)

![Example House](https://s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/data.heatonresearch.com/kaggle/images/2025_fall/10.jpg)

## Approach Opportunities

The competition offers multiple paths to success:

* Deep Learning students may focus on combining structured data with computer vision approaches using the property images
* Generative AI students may emphasize natural language processing of the detailed property descriptions
* The most competitive solutions will likely integrate all three data types: structured, unstructured, and image data

## Target Variable

The target variable is a property value score ranging from 0 to 100, where higher scores indicate more valuable properties. 

While this competition is primarily for students in the specified Washington University courses, it remains open to the broader Kaggle community.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation

## Goal
The goal of this competition is to predict property value scores for residential houses based on their characteristics and features. Your task is to build a model that can accurately estimate these scores using the provided features.

## Metric
This competition is evaluated using Root Mean Square Error (RMSE). The RMSE is calculated as:

$$ RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2} $$

Where:
- $n$ is the number of houses in the test set
- $y_i$ is the actual property value score for house $i$
- $\hat{y}_i$ is the predicted property value score for house $i$

Lower RMSE values indicate better performance. The RMSE particularly penalizes large prediction errors due to the squared term in the formula.

## Submission Format
For each house in the test set, you must predict a property value score. The file should contain exactly two columns:

- `id`: The ID of the house
- `property_value_score`: Your predicted value (between 0 and 100)

## Timeline
The competition is split into two stages:
1. Public leaderboard (30% of test data)
2. Private leaderboard (70% of test data)

Your final rank will be determined by your score on the private leaderboard.

## Notes
- Predictions should be between 0 and 100
- The evaluation metric (RMSE) penalizes larger prediction errors more heavily than smaller ones
- Multiple submissions per day are allowed
- The final winners will be selected based on their private leaderboard scores

Remember that the goal is to create a robust model that generalizes well to unseen data, rather than one that simply memorizes the training set patterns.",,False,True
10197,datacamp-cources-rating,"# Overview

Welcome to the **DataCamp Courses Rating Prediction** competition! 

Online learning platforms like DataCamp offer thousands of courses in Python, R, SQL, data science, and machine learning. Each course comes with rich metadata such as its title, description, difficulty level, programming language, number of chapters, exercises, videos, and number of subscribers.

**Goal:** Your task is to predict the average rating of a course (`course_rating`) using its metadata. This is a regression problem. Successfully predicting ratings can help e-learning platforms understand what drives course quality and learner satisfaction.

Participants are encouraged to explore the dataset, engineer meaningful features, and experiment with a variety of machine learning approaches, including classical models, ensemble methods, and text-based features from course titles and descriptions.

The dataset is split into three files: 
`train.csv` ¨C Contains course metadata and the target variable `course_rating` .
`test.csv` ¨C Contains course metadata without`course_rating`; participants must predict it.
`sample__submission.csv` ¨C Template showing the required submission format.

**Evaluation Metric:** Submissions will be evaluated using **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**. For interpretability, leaderboard scores will be reported as **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)**, which is the square root of the MSE.

Good luck, and may the best model win! 
----------------------
# Description

## About the Domain
Online learning platforms, such as DataCamp, provide thousands of courses covering programming, data science, and machine learning. Each course is designed with metadata that captures its structure, topic, difficulty, and learner engagement. Understanding which factors influence course quality can help platforms improve content and optimize learning experiences.

## Competition Details
In this competition, your goal is to predict the **average rating of a course** (`course_rating`) based on its metadata. This is a **regression problem**, where higher ratings indicate greater learner satisfaction.

The dataset is split into three files for this competition:
`train.csv` ¨C Contains course metadata and the target variable `course_rating` .
`test.csv` ¨C Contains course metadata without`course_rating`; participants must predict it.
`sample__submission.csv` ¨C Template showing the required submission format.

The dataset includes the following features:
- `title` and `description` (text features)
- `difficulty_level` and `programming_language` (categorical features)
- `num_chapters`, `num_exercises`, `num_videos`, `nb_of_subscriptions`, `xp`, `time_needed_in_hours` (numeric features)

Participants are encouraged to explore feature engineering, text processing, and a variety of machine learning models, including linear regression, ensemble methods, and NLP-based approaches on the course text.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**. For interpretability, leaderboard scores are reported as **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)**:

$$
RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2}
$$

where \(y_i\) is the true course rating and \(\hat{y}_i\) is your predicted rating.

## Submission File
Your submission file should be a CSV with the following format:

id,course_rating
1001,4.5
1002,3.8
1003,4.2
## Tips
- Use metadata creatively: difficulty, topic, instructor reputation, and engagement metrics can all be predictive.
- Text features from titles and descriptions can significantly improve your model.
- Preprocessing, feature engineering, and ensemble models are encouraged.",,False,True
10198,etiq-roadsense,"# Overview

Every year, millions of lives are impacted by road accidents. For public safety officials, insurance companies, and automotive engineers, understanding the factors that contribute to the severity of an accident is crucial. Early identification of high-risk accidents can help allocate emergency resources more effectively, inform safer road design, and ultimately save lives.
----------------------
# Description

In this competition, you are challenged to predict the **gravity** of road accidents¡ªclassifying them as either **Lethal** or **NonLethal**. You'll work with a challenging and realistic dataset that has been intentionally altered to mimic the messy, inconsistent data that data scientists often face in the real world. Your task is to clean, merge, and engineer features from four different sources to build a powerful predictive model.

This is more than just a modelling challenge; it's a test of your full data science skill set, from data wrangling to insightful feature engineering, and finally model optimization.

As a bonus challenge we want to see your best notebooks using Etiq Copilot ([Quick-Start Guide](https://docs.etiq.ai/quick-start)); not only will it give you a headstart in the challenge by effortlessly understanding your data and code, but we will also be awarding a secondary prize for the ¡°best use of Etiq¡±. Here we want to see you make great use of the data science copilot and avoid wasting your time with data pre-processing! (Bonus points for any bugs found).
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Main Contest

This competition is evaluated on the **macro F1 score**. 

F1 Score is calculated as:

$$ F_{1} = 2 \times \frac{precision \times recall}{precision + recall}$$

In the event of a tie, the first to submit their response will be awarded the prize.

### Etiq Contest

The most points win given the following scoring criteria:

* 1 point for running lineage
* 1 point for a clean lineage (No hanging nodes)
* 1 point for running tests (include results in comments or markdown cells for validation)
* 1 point for using RCA (include results in comments or markdown cells for validation)
* 1 point per new bug found per submission (given what Etiq supports, see below)

In the event of a tie, the first to submit their response will be awarded the prize.


**Etiq Supports**:

* Python 3.10-3.13
* Mac OS - ARM
* Windows - Intel/AMD64
* Linux - Intel/AMD64
* Libraries:
 * Pandas
 * Polars
 * Scikit-Learn
 * Numpy
 * Spark


## Submission File

### Main Contest
Submissions must be in a CSV file with two columns: ```AccidentId``` and ```Gravity```. The Gravity column should contain your predictions, either ""Lethal"" or ""NonLethal"".

    AccidentId,Gravity
    517754,""Lethal""
    517755,""NonLethal""
    517756,""NonLethal""
    etc.

### Etiq Contest

Start a discussion with the title Etiq contest submission, please include a list of the bugs you have found and upload the notebook with your lineage, tests and RCA, for the bug consideration upload the script/notebook and data you used.

You will not be able to use Etiq directly in the Kaggle Notebook, but if you are interested in this side competition you can use it as a jupyter or vscode extension. 
----------------------
# Prizes

### Main Contest

Total Prizes Available: $350
- **1st Place**?-?$200
- **2nd Place** - $150

### Etiq Contest:
Total Prizes Available: $150
- **1st Place**?-?$50
- **2nd Place** - $50
- **3rd Place** - $50",,False,True
10199,predicting-car-prices-from-text-descriptions-copy,"# Overview

# Case Study

## AutoList.ai: The Pricing Puzzle

Mira Das sat on the edge of her couch, one hand nursing a lukewarm mug of coffee, the other nervously flicking through lines of car listings. A gentle rain tapped against the windowpane of her San Francisco apartment, a soothing contrast to the storm inside her head. It was Thursday night, and she was staring down a Monday deadline that felt too close for comfort. Her assignment? Deliver a compelling product strategy to build AutoList.ai¡¯s flagship feature: an intelligent tool that would tell car sellers how much their vehicle was worth.

The idea sounded simple enough. AutoList.ai, a nimble startup in the used car marketplace space, wanted to provide private sellers with a suggested price when they listed their vehicle. Something fair, grounded, and ideally optimized for a quick sale. The feature would be a first step in a broader vision to become the most intelligent car listing platform in the country. Their investors had called it a ¡°game changer.¡± Mira called it a mess waiting to happen.

It wasn¡¯t the vision she doubted. In fact, she loved it. It was the data. Or rather, the chaos masquerading as data. Her team had scraped over 50,000 listings from various public sites. Each row was rich with detail: car makes, models, engine types, seller notes, accidents, colors, horsepower, transmission types. But rich didn¡¯t mean clean. The information was stitched together with inconsistent spelling, optional fields, and free-form entries that ranged from poetic to incomprehensible. One seller had described their car as ¡°Drives like a dream, never failed me, purple with gold trim, you won¡¯t find another like it,¡± while another wrote simply, ¡°Toyota. Used. 75k miles. Fair price only.¡±

Her task was to make sense of it all, or at least convince leadership that a plan to do so existed. The CEO, a visionary with a penchant for asking ""what if?"", had painted a future where a seller typed in their car details and got back a suggested price and an explanation. Not just a number, but a transparent, believable one. ""Something like Zillow for cars,"" he had said. Mira smiled politely at the time. Now she winced.

## Tension in the Product Room

The week prior, Mira had met with key stakeholders to clarify expectations. Engineering asked for edge cases. Marketing asked how it would look. The design team asked if they could make it feel ""delightful."" But no one had asked the one thing Mira kept asking herself: how will it work?
They weren¡¯t being naive. Everyone assumed someone would figure it out. Mira was that someone.
She opened the spreadsheet again. It was noisy, inconsistent, and beautifully chaotic. A Ford F-150 Lariat from 2018, 74,000 miles, gasoline, blue exterior, gray interior, clean title. Listed at $11,000. Below it: a BMW 335i, 2007, 80,000 miles, 6-speed manual, black-on-black, also clean title. $8,250. The rows flowed on like a river of stories.

She tried to trace patterns. Were BMWs always more expensive? Did clean titles consistently fetch higher prices? Was mileage the deciding factor, or did color combinations sway buyer sentiment? If only she could define a few core rules, a structure might emerge. But for every pattern she spotted, she found an exception.

At a recent team brainstorm, one engineer had suggested using recent listings of similar vehicles as a baseline. ""Like comps in real estate,"" he offered. Another proposed a scoring rubric based on brand, age, condition, and sentiment. The CTO smiled. ""Or,"" he said, ""we just build a model. Let the data speak.""
But models needed ground truth. This dataset was scraped, not verified. Was the listed price the final sale price? No one knew. Mira wondered whether she was being asked to build a compass using a magnetic field that constantly shifted. If only Mira knew that this is how most companies operate anyways.

## The Illusion of Simplicity

Friday morning, Mira sat with Charlie, a UX designer, to sketch out the user experience. ""It has to feel magical,"" he said. ""Someone just types in their car details and bam ¡ª we give them a price they can trust.""
Mira nodded. Trust. That word again.

""How accurate does it need to be?"" she asked.
Charlie shrugged. ""Accurate enough that people believe it. And consistent enough that we don't lose them.""

They agreed on an interface that offered both structured form fields and an open text box. Mira called it the illusion of choice. It gave users the feeling of flexibility, while nudging them toward clarity. She proposed a simple response: the price, a short explanation, and a button to list the car. Charlie loved it.
Back at her desk, Mira knew what she had to do. She would create a narrative. Not of how the tool worked, but how it could. She would propose a roadmap that balanced imagination with feasibility, suggest multiple paths forward, and subtly push toward one.

## Forks in the Road

Over the weekend, she drafted the strategy document. It laid out three approaches:

- Similarity-Based Pricing: Use existing listings to find comparable vehicles and suggest a price range based on recent data. Pros: intuitive. Cons: brittle, especially with incomplete descriptions.

- Rules-Based Engine: Define price adjustments based on known factors: subtract for accidents, add for rare trims, reduce for high mileage. Pros: interpretable. Cons: manual and hard to scale.

- Black Box AI: Feed in all the data and let a model learn the relationships. Pros: flexible and adaptive. Cons: opaque and potentially untrustworthy.

She didn¡¯t pick one. She presented all three. She included diagrams. She labeled them A, B, and C. And in the final section, she added a fourth option: the hybrid. A simple model supported by handcrafted rules. Transparent enough to explain, powerful enough to improve.

She titled the deck: Finding Value: Building Trust Through Transparent Pricing.

## Monday

The room was packed. Slides flicked by. Heads nodded. The CEO paused at the fork-in-the-road slide. ""Interesting,"" he said. ""Which one do you prefer?""
Mira smiled. ""I think we start with the hybrid.""
He smiled back. ""Smart. Give people a story they can believe in.""
And just like that, they believed.
Mira exhaled. The real work was just beginning.

## Recap:

You¡¯re helping a marketplace that lets sellers list their cars for sale. The data was gathered by a data analyst who scraped websites and prices. So the details are in free-form text and the platform wants to predict a recommended listing price automatically based on this text.
Your task is to read the above story and decide on how you will implement the following:
Take unstructured vehicle descriptions as input

- Predict a realistic price output with a model (define the word model as you see fit)

- Make your model robust to variations in phrasing

- Explain which features matter most for pricing

- [IF POSSIBLE] They also want a mock website where a user can input data and get a response with the above - this is not required but those who have this usually appear more credible

## Benchmark:

Your colleague has given you supplemental information for benchmarking your own models: ¡°We¡¯ve tried a simple model using TF-IDF + linear regression, but it's not very reliable - its predictions have an MAE of $30,000 which means that our predictions for the car¡¯s value are off, on average, by $30K which is too much. We are hoping to get to 20K MAE with your help, but anything better than that would be amazing such as 17K for instance. We think the model is missing context ¡ª like how hybrid systems or cylinder count should affect price. We¡¯ve also heard about model explainability tools like SHAP or LIME - we don¡¯t really understand those but could you help with that too? We¡¯d like to be able to understand why our models are making the predictions they make.¡±

# Presentation:

In a presentation, you should:

## Evaluate your model using MAE (mean absolute error) and create an approach such as (but NOT LIMITED TO):

- TF-IDF and Linear Regression

- Deep Learning

- LLMs

## Discuss your preprocessing steps
NON-EXHAUSTIVE list:

- Use an LLM or a rules-based parser to extract structured features (make, year, engine, mileage, accidents)

- Train a model using embeddings (e.g., Sentence Transformers + XGBoost)

- Use fine-tuned LLM regression (e.g., with LoRA or GPT adapters)

- Improve robustness to variation (e.g., horsepower formats, transmission naming)

## Demonstrate interpretability

- SHAP or LIME or something else

- And/or highlight how the model¡¯s attention (or embedding space) reflects importance of mileage, brand, etc.

## Create a 7-10 min presentation deck that will convince your CEO that your solution is reliable.

- Explain findings to a non-technical CEO

- Recommend next steps

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error) between the predicted value and the actual target value. MAE measures the average magnitude of the errors in a set of predictions, without considering their direction. It is calculated as:

$$
\text{MAE} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left| \hat{y}_i - y_i \right|
$$

where $$\hat{y}_i$$ is the predicted value and $$y_i$$ is the true value.

Lower MAE indicates better performance.

## Submission File

For each ID in the test set, you must predict a numeric value for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
ID,price
2,134.56
3,98.10
4,145.00
etc.
```

Make sure the `ID` values match those provided in the test set and that each ID appears exactly once. Predictions should be real numbers; integer rounding is not required.","# Dataset Description

Thanks for the clarification. Here's the updated **Dataset Description** with **MAE (Mean Absolute Error)** as the evaluation metric:

---

## Dataset Description

This dataset is designed for a **text regression** task: predicting the **price of an item** based on its textual description. It contains a collection of item listings, each with a natural language description and a corresponding numerical price.

Participants are challenged to build machine learning models that can understand the underlying patterns in language and accurately estimate item prices.

---

### Files

* **`train_text.csv`**
  Contains the training data with item descriptions and their known prices.

  | Column Name   | Type   | Description                                  |
  | ------------- | ------ | -------------------------------------------- |
  | `description` | string | A free-text description of the item listing. |
  | `price`       | float  | The item's price in USD. This is the target. |

---

*   **test_text.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_upload_for_students.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format; download and modify and then reupload as a submission

### Target Variable

* `price`
  A continuous variable representing the market value of an item. Models will be evaluated based on their ability to predict this value accurately.

---

### Evaluation Metric

* **Mean Absolute Error (MAE)**

  $$
  MAE = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} |\hat{y}_i - y_i|
  $$

Lower MAE indicates better model performance.",False,True
