10215,your-first-ml-competition,"# Overview

## ?? Predicting Bank Term Deposit Subscription

In this educational competition, you will build a **machine learning model** to predict whether a bank client will subscribe to a **term deposit** after a marketing campaign.

The task is based on the [Bank Marketing Dataset](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Bank+Marketing)  
originally described by *Moro et al. (2014)*.  
It contains detailed information about clients, previous marketing interactions, and macroeconomic indicators collected between 2008 and 2010.

Your goal is to use these features to classify each client as either **¡°likely to subscribe¡±** or **¡°not likely to subscribe¡±**, helping the bank focus on the most promising potential customers.

---

### ?? Objective
Develop a binary classification model that predicts the likelihood of a client subscribing to a term deposit.  
The competition focuses on:
- feature engineering and interpretability,  
- working with imbalanced data,  
- and model evaluation using **ROC-AUC**.

---

### ?? Citation
If you use this dataset, please cite:  
> Moro, S., Cortez, P., & Rita, P. (2014).  
> *A Data-Driven Approach to Predict the Success of Bank Telemarketing.*  
> Decision Support Systems. [DOI:10.1016/j.dss.2014.03.001](http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.dss.2014.03.001)

---

### ?? Context
This is a **student competition hosted by Ural University of Science and Technology (UUST)**.  
There are no monetary prizes ¡ª the goal is to help participants practice real-world data analysis, feature selection, and classification on a structured dataset.

----------------------
# Description

In this competition, you will work with real-world marketing data from a Portuguese banking institution.  
Your task is to predict whether a client will **subscribe to a term deposit** after being contacted as part of a marketing campaign.

---

## ?? Background

Marketing campaigns are often conducted through phone calls, where potential clients are asked whether they would like to open a term deposit account.  
Predicting the likelihood of a positive response helps the bank **optimize its efforts**, saving time and resources.

This dataset was originally introduced in:

> Moro, S., Cortez, P., & Rita, P. (2014).  
> *A Data-Driven Approach to Predict the Success of Bank Telemarketing.*  
> Decision Support Systems. [DOI:10.1016/j.dss.2014.03.001](http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.dss.2014.03.001)

---

## ?? Dataset Overview

The dataset contains information about:
- **Client attributes** (age, job, education, marital status, etc.)
- **Campaign data** (contact type, last contact day/month, number of contacts)
- **Previous outcomes** of earlier marketing campaigns
- **Macroeconomic indicators** (employment variation, consumer confidence, euribor rate)

The target variable is:
- `y` ¡ª *Did the client subscribe to a term deposit?*  
  (`yes` = 1, `no` = 0)

---

## ?? Task Details

You will build a binary classification model that outputs a **probability** (`y ¡Ê [0,1]`)  
representing how likely each client is to subscribe.

Submissions will be evaluated using the **ROC-AUC** metric.  
A higher ROC-AUC indicates better ability to distinguish between subscribers and non-subscribers.

---

## ?? Educational Focus

This is a **non-commercial educational competition** organized by **Ufa University of Science and Technology (UUST)**.  
The primary goals are:
- Understanding data preprocessing and feature encoding  
- Handling imbalanced data  
- Evaluating models using ROC-AUC  
- Practicing reproducible ML experimentation

There are **no monetary prizes** ¡ª this challenge is designed to support learning and research.

---

## ?? Tips for Participants
- Try different encodings for categorical features (e.g., one-hot, target encoding).  
- Experiment with balanced sampling or class-weighted models.  
- Use models like Logistic Regression, Random Forest, or Gradient Boosting as baselines.  
- Track your experiments and document your approach clearly.

Good luck, and happy modeling! ??

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using the **Area Under the ROC Curve (ROC-AUC)** metric.

ROC-AUC measures how well your model ranks positive examples (clients who subscribed) higher than negative ones (those who did not).  
A model that randomly guesses scores around 0.5 will have an ROC-AUC of **0.5**, while a perfect classifier achieves **1.0**.

You can learn more about the ROC-AUC metric here:  
[Receiver Operating Characteristic (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic)

---

## ?? Submission File Format

For each `id` in the **test.csv**, you must predict the probability that the client will subscribe to a term deposit (`y = 1`).

Your submission file must be in **CSV format** and include a header row:

    id,y
    1,0.472
    2,0.103
    3,0.981
- `id` ¡ª unique identifier from the test dataset  
- `y` ¡ª predicted probability of subscription (between **0** and **1**)

---

## ?? Scoring

During the competition:
- The **Public Leaderboard** score is calculated on ~30% of the test data.  
- The **Private Leaderboard** score (revealed after the competition ends) is calculated on the remaining ~70%.

Your final ranking will be determined based on the **Private Leaderboard ROC-AUC score**.","# Dataset Description

This dataset is based on the **Bank Marketing dataset** introduced by Moro et al. (2014), collected from a Portuguese banking institution.  
It contains demographic, financial, and macroeconomic features describing each client contacted as part of a marketing campaign.

The goal is to predict whether a client will **subscribe to a term deposit** (`y = 1`) or **not** (`y = 0`).

---

