# Evaluation

The evaluation section describes how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.

---

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    station_name,predict_delay_time
    ±±¾©,0
    ±±¾©Î÷,0
    Ê¯¼Ò×¯,0
    ...

    etc.",,False,True
10228,ITS_Challenge_Game2_Collaborative_Perception_LiDAROnly,"# Overview

The aim of this Vehicle-Infrastructure-Air?Cross-Domain Collaborative Perception Algorithm Competition is to significantly enhance the environmental perception capabilities of intelligent unmanned systems in intricate, highly dynamic, and unstructured road scenarios.  Participants in this challenge must create clever algorithms for effective cross-domain data fusion and collaborative perception.  Multi-source, heterogeneous data from connected-autonomous vehicles,?mobile roadside infrastructure?units, and aerial platforms?must be integrated into these algorithms.  The objective is to accomplish cross-domain data fusion and collaborative perception in complicated settings by overcoming the constraints of single-platform perception.  In the end, this will give autonomous unmanned systems stronger assistance for job planning and wise decision-making by improving their environmental sensing capabilities.
----------------------
# Description

Competition Rules

--Competition Schedule--

Deadline for submission: October 20, 2025

--Task Description--

This challenge focuses on key technologies such as multi-source sensing data processing, information fusion, and collaborative perception across vehicles, infrastructure, and aerial platforms. The main goals are to address issues of spatio-temporal alignment and calibration of multi-agent perception data, precise fusion of heterogeneous sensors, and cross-view spatial relationship modelling of detected targets.

Participants are required to consider the complex and highly dynamic nature of the physical world, as well as the heterogeneity of multi-agent data. Using the provided vehicle-infrastructure-air (VIA) collaborative perception dataset, teams should design models capable of adapting to various conditions (e.g., lighting, weather, viewing angles). They must train and validate their models using the supplied aerial¨Cground cooperative perception dataset and submit the testing code and documentation accordingly.

The expected submissions should demonstrate algorithmic innovation, high perception accuracy, and robust cross-view alignment, thereby advancing research and applications in the field of vehicle¨Cinfrastructure¨Caerial collaborative perception.

--Problem Modelling--

Input: Vehicle-mounted LiDAR point clouds, roadside LiDAR point clouds, and scene target annotation files.
Output: Target categories and poses within the scene.

--Other Rules--

(1) Originality: Entries must be original and comply with all relevant laws of the People¡¯s Republic of China. They must not infringe on any third-party intellectual property or other rights. Once plagiarism or rights violations are verified, the organizing committee will disqualify the team and revoke the results. The same work may not be submitted for multiple tasks¡ªany repetition will be deemed invalid.

(2) Fair Competition: Participants are prohibited from exploiting rules or technical loopholes to gain unfair advantages, from copying others¡¯ work, exchanging answers, or using multiple accounts. Any such misconduct will result in disqualification and disciplinary action.

(3) Organizer¡¯s Statement: The organizing committee reserves the right to modify the competition rules, determine and handle cases of cheating, and withdraw or withhold awards from teams whose behaviour compromises fairness or organizational integrity.

(4)  Intellectual Property: The intellectual property of all submitted works (including but not limited to algorithms, models, and solutions) shall be jointly owned by the participants and the organizing committee. The committee reserves the right to use the submissions, competition information, and team details in promotional materials, publications, authorized media releases, official website displays and downloads, exhibitions, or roadshows. The committee also retains priority rights for investment facilitation and product incubation based on submitted works.

(5)  Platform Security: Any attacks on the competition platform will result in immediate disqualification and forfeiture of awards. The committee reserves the right to pursue legal responsibility for such actions.

(6)  Special Rules: Participants must submit two algorithms separately¡ªone based on camera data and one based on LiDAR point cloud data¡ªvia the designated submission links. Any use of mixed or additional modalities at the input stage will result in disqualification once detected.

----------------------
# Evaluation

For the evaluation of LiDAR-based models, the Average Precision (AP) metric for object detection is used.
The detection accuracy is calculated under two different Intersection over Union (IoU) thresholds: 0.5 and 0.7.
The final AP score is then obtained as a weighted combination of the two results, as follows:

AP=40%*AP@50+60%*AP@70¡£","# Dataset Description

The vehicle¨Cinfrastructure¨Cair (VIA) collaborative perception dataset, collected through the simulation platform, contains multi-view images and point cloud data from connected autonomous vehicles, roadside infrastructure, and aerial platforms. The dataset provides annotated ground-truth bounding boxes, sensor calibration parameters, and temporal sequence information.
Participants are required to write the inference results for each frame in the test set into a CSV file (see example below) following the specified format. The submitted CSV will be compared with the ground-truth (GT) data in the system to compute the final score.
Submission Format Instructions
The CSV file must contain only two columns:

First column: ID¡ªthe sequential index of each test frame (a total of 2170 frames).


Second column: all vehicle detection results for that frame (one or more targets).

For each frame, the predicted bounding boxes from the object detection model should be listed in order within the second column.
Each predicted bounding box must include the following values in the specified order:
vehicle score center_x center_y center_z length, width, height, rotation_x, rotation_y, rotation_z

where each item represents:

vehicle¡ªobject category
score¡ªconfidence score
center_x, center_y, center_z ¡ª 3D bounding box center coordinates
length, width, height¡ªbounding box dimensions
rotation_x, rotation_y, rotation_z¡ªroll, yaw, and pitch angles

?? Important:
The CSV file must contain exactly two columns.
If there are multiple targets in a frame, all should be listed sequentially within the same cell of the second column.
Any formatting errors in the CSV file will result in failure to obtain a score.",False,True
10229,bs-6204-classroom-hackathon-on-deep-learning,"# Description


<p>Welcome! This notebook contains everything you need for the hackathon.

You¡¯ll start with a simple CNN baseline trained on medical images. Then, you can modify the code to improve performance.

Your instructor provided a data_public-3.zip file containing train, val, and test folders. Upload it before running Step 1.


----------------------
# Evaluation

<h3>Goal</h3>
The goal in this competition is to take an image of a fundus photo, and determine the category of this image.<br>For every image in the test set, you should predict the correct label. 

<h3>Metric</h3>
<p>This competition is evaluated on the AUC of your model prediction.</p>

<h3>Submission File Format</h3>
<p>Just submit the submission.csv generated by your code</p>","# Dataset Description

<p> Training, validation and testing images are listed in the 3 folders train/ val/ test/ </p>
<p>The data files train.csv and test.csv contain image file name and the corresponding labels (0 or 1).</p>
<p>Each image is 512 pixels in height and 512 pixels in width. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255, inclusive.</p>",False,True
10230,the-imagine-decoding-challenge,"# Overview

Use Machine Learning to decode what participants are thinking about!
---
Can you decode which images people were thinking of from data recorded while they were visually seeing these images?

In memory research, decoders are often trained on localizer data to extract neural activation patterns of specific stimuli (e.g., ¡°what pattern occurs when we see a clown¡±). Taken as evidence for reprocessing, these decoders are then used to detect pattern presence in other parts of the experiment (e.g., ¡°after learning something about clowns, we can see the activation pattern of ¡®clown¡¯ being more active than expected; that means knowledge is being consolidated¡±). But how well does this actually work?

We recorded a simple paradigm in the Magnetoencephalograph (MEG) in which we showed [ten different visual items](https://github.com/skjerns/imagine-decoding-challenge/blob/main/md_assets/stimuli.png) many times to participants. Later, we played back spoken words describing the items while they had their eyes closed and asked them to mentally visualize the items associated with the word as vividly as possible. Can you train a decoder on the visual presentation (localizer) and decode which item people imagined?

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F529561%2F0c53dab5f69a8e823e0e58fcb0689354%2FClipboard_10-14-2025_01.png?generation=1760458539963286&alt=media)
----------------------
# Experiment

We recorded a **localizer** in the [MEG](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnetoencephalography) with 30 participants, presenting one of [ten different items](https://github.com/skjerns/imagine-decoding-challenge/blob/main/md_assets/stimuli.png) in random order. [In each trial](https://github.com/skjerns/imagine-decoding-challenge/tree/main/md_assets/diagram_localizer.png), participants first heard a word describing the item (e.g., ¡°clown¡±) for ~1 second, then saw an image that either matched (i.e., was a clown) or did not, in which case they had to press a button. After that, they performed the study reported in Kern et al. ([2024](https://elifesciences.org/articles/93357) & [2025](https://elifesciences.org/reviewed-preprints/108023)), which is not of interest right now. Directly after the main experiment ended, [they closed their eyes](https://github.com/skjerns/imagine-decoding-challenge/tree/main/md_assets/diagram_imagine.png) and once again **heard the words** describing the stimuli. They were asked to **imagine the cued image** as vividly as possible whenever they heard its word.

![](https://github.com/skjerns/imagine-decoding-challenge/blob/main/md_assets/stimuli.png?raw=true)

**Goal:** The epochs have been scrambled! Can you figure out in which epoch we played which word as an auditory stimulus by training a decoder on visual images of the words?

Example of the localizer trials (tip: unmute audio).  
Instructions: *¡°Press a button if there is a mismatch between word and image.¡±*

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d093c932-83a1-435b-920a-1e89369ac48d


Example of the imagine trials (tip: unmute audio).  
Instructions: *¡°Close your eyes and mentally imagine the image belonging to the word you hear as vividly as possible.¡±*

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7a6b6ba3-c3f0-4c2f-ace2-3a9cb33e2a62

See a schematic of the two trial types here: [trial schematic](https://github.com/skjerns/imagine-decoding-challenge/tree/main/md_assets/diagram_trials.png)
----------------------
# Background

In human memory research, we typically train decoders on the visual peak of a localizer and then apply them to, e.g., resting-state data, expecting a learned sequence to be decodable. However, it is questionable to what extent the replayed representations correspond to the visual peak patterns the decoder extracted. Evidence suggests that memory representations - or even sensory stimulation from different modalities - have overlap with visually evoked stimulus processing patterns, but this overlap is slightly smaller than expected ([Bezsudnova et al., 2024](https://direct.mit.edu/jocn/article/36/8/1760/121050); [Dijkstra et al., 2019](https://www.cell.com/trends/cognitive-sciences/abstract/S1364-6613(19)30059-2); [Dijkstra et al., 2021](https://www.eneuro.org/content/8/5/ENEURO.0228-21.2021.abstract)). Memory decoding research relies on this overlap. Failure to transcode from visual presentation to imagined visual stimuli would challenge the current interpretation of decoders in many memory-processing studies.

While we know in principle that decoding mental imagery is possible and has been shown before (e.g. [Shatek et al. 2019](https://www.mdpi.com/2411-5150/3/4/53) or [Dijkstra et al. 2018](https://cdn.elifesciences.org/articles/33904/elife-33904-v1.pdf)), using the default settings used in many memory papers (i.e. training on a fixed timepoint of visual decoding peak) seems not to work well. While we're able to decode conscious retrieval of items after a visual cue (see [Kern et al. (2020)](https://elifesciences.org/articles/93357)), it does not seem to work cross-modality, if the cue is coming from a different modality (e.g. auditorily). Besides the theoretical implications we first want to make sure that we're not missing a crucial step in our decoder training that would allow for such a transfer from visual to imagery to be possible.

As a sanity check we tried to train a decoder on the auditory cues in the localizer and then decode the auditory cues in the IMAGINE condition. This works as expected! See https://github.com/skjerns/imagine-decoding-challenge/blob/main/md_assets/cross-decoding-auditory-cue.png


----------------------
# Data

The data can be downloaded here: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17351690 or from Kaggle.

For more information, see the `Data` tab
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [accuracy](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy) of the predicted trial labels. Each trial of the imagine epochs belongs to one of each class present in the localizer (`'apple', 'bicycle', 'brush', 'cake', 'clown', 'cup', 'desk', 'foot', 'mountain', 'zebra'`, enumerated alphabetically).


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a label describing the word that has been played. The labels are equivalent to the labels in the `localizer` epochs (see above). `ID` is a combination of subject id and epoch number, as appearing in the file and in the event label of each imagine epoch, starting with one. E.g. The first entry will be `sub-01_1`, the last one `sub27-50`, with 680 rows. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,label
    sub-01_1, clown
    sub-01_2, zebra
    sub-01_3, cake
    ...     etc.
    sub-27_50, brush


This challenge does not have a private test set, only the public test set is available.
----------------------
# Things to keep in mind

- Your task is to train a classifier on the data after stimulus onset of the localizer. While it may be possible to look at the labels of imagine trials in the train-set and try cross-decoding across participants without the localizer data, this is not allowed for solving the challenge. However, we'll be equally impressed if you achieve this.
- The `imagine` trials were recorded after participants learned some image associations (i.e., image sequences). Subconscious reactivation of associated stimuli after the word cue is possible, though we expect the instructed imagery pattern to **supersede** these reactivation effects in decoding strength. If you can any effect of the learned sequences in the data, we'll be more than impressed.
----------------------
# Prizes

Prices will be awarded for the best three solutions. However, prices will only be awarded for solutions reaching at least 20% accuracy.

Total Prizes Available: $1000
- **1st Place**?-?$600
- **2nd Place** - $300
- **3rd Place** - $100

Additionally, price winners are invited to collaborate on a publication describing the results and process.",,False,True
10231,vesuvius-challenge-surface-detection,"# Overview

Some ancient scrolls are too fragile to open, but that doesn¡¯t mean we can¡¯t read them. Can your model help?

Before we can recover what¡¯s written between the lines, we first need to find the lines. You¡¯ll work with CT scans from the Villa dei Papiri to train a model that follows the scroll¡¯s surface, one of the trickiest and most essential parts of virtually unwrapping the text.
----------------------
# Description

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1537731%2F14b3f064916a7808e50a4db947db56eb%2FPHerc0139-CT.jpg?generation=1762380856124206&alt=media)The library at the Villa dei Papiri is one-of-a-kind: it¡¯s the only classical antiquity known to survive. But when Mount Vesuvius erupted in AD 79, most of its scrolls were turned into carbonized bundles of ash. Nearly 2,000 years later, many are still sealed shut, too delicate to unroll and too complex to decode. In this competition, you¡¯ll build a model to segment the scroll¡¯s surface in CT scans, a critical step in revealing the texts hidden inside.

Physical unrolling would destroy the scrolls, and today¡¯s digital methods can handle only the easy parts, like clean, well-spaced layers. But the tightest, most tangled areas are often where the real discoveries hide. To recover those, we need better segmentation to handle noise and compression without distorting the scroll's shape.

You¡¯ll work with real 3D CT data from the previous Vesuvius Challenge, which uncovered passages from sealed scrolls, including an ancient book title revealed for the first time. Your model¡¯s job is to trace the scroll¡¯s surface as it winds through folds, gaps, and distortions so the next stage of virtual unwrapping can do its magic.

Your efforts could help unlock works of philosophy, poetry, and history that haven¡¯t been read in centuries. Texts that might have been lost forever¡ªuntil now.

Let¡¯s bring them back, one layer at a time.

*To learn about the previous Vesuvius Challenge, please follow [this link](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/vesuvius-challenge-ink-detection/overview).*
----------------------
# Dataset Description

The dataset includes 3D chunks of binary labeled CT scans of the closed and carbonized Herculaneum scrolls. Data was acquired at the ESRF synchrotron in Grenoble, on beamline BM18 and at the DLS synchrotron in Oxford, on beamline I12.

The dimension of a sample chunk is not fixed, and can vary across the dataset.

![Image showing the software used to virtually unwrap the scrolls](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F19579895%2F0293df31f3a2ca1c4255adfdd41dc955%2Fdragonfly-screenshot.png?generation=1762469029833434&alt=media)

A papyrus sheet is composed of two layers, the recto and the verso, one with horizontal fibers and one with vertical fibers on top of each other.

The ideal solution is detecting the recto surface ¨C the surface of the papyrus sheet that faces the umbilicus (the center of the scroll). The recto surface lies on the layer composed of horizontal fibers. Since the scrolls survived a carbonized eruption, the sheets can be partially damaged and frayed. Detecting, within a reasonable approximation, just the position of a sheet, no matter whether the segmentation surface encompasses both the recto and the verso, is also fine for the  purpose of virtually unwrapping the scrolls. 

Avoid topological mistakes: artificial mergers between different sheets and holes that split a single entity in several disconnected components.

The output of the machine learning model can be directly used in the Vesuvius Challenge pipeline to digitally unwrap (and read) the Herculaneum scrolls. Curious? Have a look at the latest tutorial on their digital unwrapping pipeline: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yHbpVcGD06U

Because the host team is laser-focused on unwrapping these scrolls, their research will continue throughout the Kaggle competition. Their annotation team will semi-manually unwrap additional portions of papyrus, and they expect to release both more labeled data AND ancient text as the competition progresses. Unlike the original training set, which underwent careful proofreading and refinement, these new labels will be less curated. Please use your judgment to decide how (and whether) each additional chunk should be incorporated into model training.
----------------------
# Evaluation

This competition uses a weighted average of three different segmentation metrics: Surface Dice, TopoScore, and VOI. See [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/sohier/vesuvius-2025-metric-demo/) for a working copy of the metric and [this dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/sohier/vesuvius-metric-resources) for the implementation details of each sub-metric. For general advice on how to optimize your submissions for the metric, see the Metric Summary section below. Note that this metric takes a relatively long time to run; you may need to wait several hours for scoring to complete.

## Submission File

You must submit a zip containing one .tif volume mask per test image. Each mask must be named `[image_id].tif`, match the dimensions of the source image exactly, and use the same data type as the train mask.

----------------------
# Timeline

* **November 13, 2025** - Start Date. 
* **February 6, 2026** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete. 
* **February 6, 2026** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams. 
* **February 27, 2026** - Final Submission Deadline. 

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

- 1st place: $60,000
- 2nd place: $40,000
- 3rd place: $30,000
- 4th place: $20,000
- 5th place: $15,000
- 6th place: $10,000
- 7th place: $10,000
- 8th place: $5,000
- 9th place: $5,000
- 10th place: $5,000
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met: 
- CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time 
- GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time 
- Internet access disabled 
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models 
- Submission file must be named `submission.zip` 

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.
----------------------
# Metric Summary

# **How We Score: Topology?Aware Metrics for 3D Surface Segmentation**

We reward surface proximity ([SurfaceDice@¦Ó](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.04430)), instance consistency ([VOI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variation_of_information) split/merge), and shape correctness (TopoScore) so good unwrapping is not only accurate but topologically right.

## **Why topology matters here**

To virtually unwrap the Herculaneum scrolls we must find the ink?bearing papyrus surface¡ªand keep it as a single, clean sheet as it winds through the volume. Beyond voxel?wise accuracy, we must avoid mergers across adjacent wraps and splits within the same wrap, and we must preserve high?level surface traits (no spurious holes/handles). Classic region?overlap metrics (e.g., [Dice](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dice-S%C3%B8rensen_coefficient)) can look great even when these topology errors are severe. That¡¯s why the leaderboard blends surface similarity, instance?level consistency, and topological correctness.

## **Leaderboard formula (higher is better)**

We linearly combine three bounded scores in \[0,1\]:  
**Score \= 0.30 ¡Á TopoScore \+ 0.35 ¡Á SurfaceDice@¦Ó \+ 0.35 ¡Á VOI\_score**

Leaderboard ranking uses the average score over all test volumes.

? All terms are in \[0, 1\].  
? The tolerance ¦Ó for Surface Dice is in the same physical units as the case spacing, and it¡¯s set to ¦Ó=2.0.  
? Weights are fixed for the competition unless announced otherwise.

## **1\) SurfaceDice@¦Ó ¡ª Are the two surfaces within tolerance?**

A surface?aware variant of Dice: it scores the fraction of surface points (both prediction and ground truth) that lie within a spatial tolerance ¦Ó of each other. This respects that both labels and predictions may be off by a voxel or two while still capturing the right geometry.

* Computation essentials: extract voxelized surfaces of prediction (P) and ground truth (G); compute nearest?surface distances with case spacing; count matches when distance ¡Ü ¦Ó; average matches both ways (P?G).  
* Edge cases: both empty ¡ú 1.0; exactly one empty ¡ú 0.0.  
* Defaults: ¦Ó \= 2.0 (in spacing units) unless specified.

## **2\) VOI\_score ¡ª Do instances split/merge correctly?**

[Variation of Information (VOI)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variation_of_information) compares the connected?component labelings of prediction and ground truth. It decomposes into VOI\_split \= H(GT | Pred) and VOI\_merge \= H(Pred | GT), which track over?segmentation (splits) and under?segmentation (merges), respectively. We convert the lower?is?better VOI\_total \= VOI\_split \+ VOI\_merge into a bounded score: VOI\_score \= 1 / (1 \+ ?? VOI\_total), with ?? \= 0.3.

* Computation essentials: run 3D connected components (default 26?connectivity) on union foreground; compute VOI on these integer labelings; map to VOI\_score as above.  
* Interpretation: VOI responds strongly to component splits/merges and complements the shape?level guarantees of TopoScore, described in the next section.

## **3\) TopoScore ¡ª Are the topological features preserved?**

Using [Betti (number](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betti_number)) [matching](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.04683) from algebraic topology, we compare the topological features present in prediction vs. ground truth in each homology dimension k: k=0 (components), k=1 (tunnels/handles), k=2 (cavities). We compute a per?dimension Topological?F1 based on matched features, then take a weighted average over active dimensions.

* Default weights: w0=0.34, w1=0.33, w2=0.33; inactive dimensions (no features in both) are skipped with renormalization.  
* Why this helps: detects bridges across neighboring wraps (extra k=1), within?wrap breaks (extra k=0), and spurious cavities (extra k=2).

## **What each metric ¡°sees¡± (cheat sheet)**

SurfaceDice@¦Ó focuses on boundary proximity; VOI captures instance split/merge behavior; TopoScore captures shape?level topology.  
See the table below for a quick mapping of common error patterns to metric responses.

| Error pattern | SurfaceDice@¦Ó | VOI\_score (split/merge) | TopoScore (Betti) |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| Slight boundary misplacement (¡Ü ¦Ó) | Tolerant (often unchanged) | Usually unaffected | Unaffected |
| Artificial bridge between parallel layers | Local effect ¨C May stay high | Merge ¡ý (under-segmentation) | k=0 / k=1 (components and tunnels) ¨C  penalized ¡ý |
| Split of the same wrap into pieces | Local effect ¨C May stay high | Split ¡ý (over-segmentation) | k=0 components ¨C penalized ¡ý |
| Spurious holes/handles in the sheet | Local effect ¨C May stay high | Small or none | k=1/k=2 ¨C penalized ¡ý |

## **Evaluation details & edge cases**

* Binarization: foreground is non?zero by default (or x \> threshold if a threshold is specified).  
* Ignore regions: voxels marked ignored (via GT label or explicit mask) are set to background in both prediction and GT before all computations.  
* Connectivity (VOI only): default 26 in 3D.  
* Spacing & tolerance: all distances use per?case spacing (sz, sy, sx); ¦Ó is in the same physical units.  
* Empty?mask conventions: both empty ¡ú SurfaceDice=1, VOI\_score=1, TopoScore=1; one empty ¡ú SurfaceDice=0; VOI\_score near 0; TopoScore=0 on k=0.

## **Practical tips for competitors**

* Favor continuity within a wrap; avoid bridges across wraps (thin spurious connections are costly in VOI and TopoScore).  
* Mind connectivity: thinning/dilation can alter counts; check results under 26?connectivity.

## **References**

1. [Pitfalls of topology-aware image segmentation](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.14619v1), *Berger et al.*, IPMI 2025 (2025)  
2. [Deep learning to achieve clinically applicable segmentation of head and neck anatomy for radiotherapy](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.04430), *Nikolov et al*., J Med Internet Res. 2021 Jul 12;23(7):e26151 (2021)  
3. [Efficient Betti Matching Enables Topology-Aware 3D Segmentation via Persistent Homology](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.04683), *Stucki et al.*, arXiv preprint (2024)  
4. [clDice \- a Novel Topology-Preserving Loss Function for Tubular Structure Segmentation](https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2021/papers/Shit_clDice_-_A_Novel_Topology-Preserving_Loss_Function_for_Tubular_Structure_CVPR_2021_paper.pdf), *Shit et al.*, CVPR 2021 (2021)  
5. [Efficient Connectivity-Preserving Instance Segmentation with Supervoxel-Based Loss Function](https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/32326/34481), *Grim et al.*, AAAI-25 (2025)  
6. [Skeleton Recall Loss for Connectivity Conserving and Resource Efficient Segmentation of Thin Tubular Structures](https://www.ecva.net/papers/eccv_2024/papers_ECCV/papers/09904.pdf) , *Kirchhoff et al.*, ECCV 2024 (2024)",,False,True
10232,chat-gpt-assisted-modelling-ws-test,"# Overview

Build a predictive model to estimate the sale price of houses using the Ames Housing dataset. The goal is to minimize RMSLE on a hidden test set.
----------------------
# Description

Predict home prices with the Ames housing dataset, connect with actuaries worldwide, and apply data science skills with AI tools such as ChatGPT, Gemini, etc. Top submissions stand out not only in performance, but also in storytelling and insight.

The dataset used in this competition contains detailed information about residential homes in Ames, including various property features such as lot size, number of rooms, year built, and other characteristics. For this hackathon, we have randomized and sampled the original data to create a unique training and test set, ensuring a fair and challenging experience for all participants.

## Duration
3 days (72 hours) - Friday 10 October 00:00 EDT to Sunday 12 October 23:55 EDT
----------------------
# Judging Criteria

This hackathon is all about **creativity and smart use of Generative AI tools**. Instead of focusing solely on model accuracy, we want to see how you **leverage AI to think, explore, and deliver insights**. Your ability to integrate AI tools like ChatGPT for analysis, automation, and storytelling will matter more than just leaderboard scores.

Your submission will be evaluated on a composite score across these dimensions:

| Criteria | Weight | Details |
| --- | --- |
| Model Performance (RMSLE) | 30% | Accuracy on the private leaderboard using a hidden test set |
| Creative Use of AI Tools | 30% | Effective and original use of AI (e.g., ChatGPT) for:  EDA and feature understanding, Prompt engineering for modeling strategies or insight discovery, Automating or simplifying tedious tasks (e.g., formatting submissions), Explaining or validating outputs |
| Interpretability & Explanation | 20% | Clear, logical, explanation of your model, features, and results |
|Code/Prompt Documentation & Reproducibility| 20%| Well-documented notebooks and prompt transcripts that make your workflow reproducible |


?? Notes for Participants

- You don¡¯t need to write every line of code from scratch.
- Show us how **AI helped you think, explore, and build your solution**.
- Submit your final work as a **notebook in the Code section**. Include your findings, prompts, visualization, or summaries of AI usage.
- A template notebook is provided in the Code section to guide you.

----------------------
# Model evaluation metric

Your model performance will be evaluated using **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE)**.

The formula is:

$$
\text{RMSLE} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( \log(\hat{y}_i + 1) - \log(y_i + 1) \right)^2 }
$$

Where:
$$
\hat{y}_i = \text{predicted value} \\
y_i = \text{actual value} \\
n = \text{number of observations}
$$

**Example:**  
- Actual price = 200,000  
- Predicted price = 180,000  

$$
\log(180{,}000 + 1) \approx 12.10,\ \log(200{,}000 + 1) \approx 12.21
$$

Difference = 0.11 ¡ú Squared = 0.0121  
If this were the only observation, RMSLE ¡Ö **0.11**.********

**Why RMSLE:**
RMSLE is used instead of RMSE because it reduces the impact of large outliers and emphasizes relative errors, making it more suitable for skewed price data

**Public Leaderboard:**
Based on a subset of the test set, updated during the competition. This gives you an idea of your relative performance but is not final.

**Private Leaderboard (Final Score):**
Calculated on a separate hidden subset of the test set. This determines the final ranking after the competition ends.
----------------------
# Prize (TBC)

Top 3 teams will receive subscription to preferred GenAI platform
- First Prize: 2 month subscription (max $40)
- Second Prize: 1 month subscription (max $20)

They will also be invited to share their experience and learnings via subsequent IAA webinars.",,False,True
