10239,ai-generated-computation,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the quality of wine based on various properties. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored based on the?quadratic weighted kappa, which measures the agreement between two outcomes. This metric typically varies from 0 (random agreement) to 1 (complete agreement). In the event that there is less agreement than expected by chance, the?metric may go below 0.

The quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as follows. First, an N?x?N histogram matrix <em>O</em> is constructed, such that <em>O<sub>i,j</sub></em> corresponds to the number of `Id`s <em>i</em> (actual) that received a predicted value <em>j</em>.?An <em>N-by-N </em>matrix of weights, <em>w</em>, is calculated based on the difference between actual and predicted values:

$$w_{i,j} = \frac{\left(i-j\right)^2}{\left(N-1\right)^2}$$

An <em>N-by-N</em> histogram matrix of expected outcomes, <em>E</em>, is calculated assuming that there is no correlation between values.? This is calculated as the outer product between the actual histogram vector of outcomes and the predicted histogram vector, normalized such that <em>E</em> and <em>O</em> have the same sum.

From these three matrices, the quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as:?

$$\kappa=1-\frac{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}O\_{i,j}}{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}E\_{i,j}}.$$


## Submission File
For each `Id` in the test set, you must predict the value for the target `quality`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,quality
    2056,5
    2057,7
    2058,3
    etc.",,False,True
10240,the-frequency-quest,"# Overview

Welcome to **The Decibel Duel**!
This is Task 1 of the **Cynaptics Induction Challenge 2025**. In this exciting competition, you¡¯ll tune into the fascinating world of sound waves and frequencies, putting your audio processing and deep learning skills to the test.

Your mission: develop an ML algorithm capable of classifying short audio clips into their correct sound categories.

To assist you, we¡¯ve provided a dataset featuring a diverse collection of real-world sounds ¡ª including everyday noises, environmental ambience, musical tones, and human-made sounds.

This project is not only fun but also highly relevant. As audio-based AI systems power applications like virtual assistants, surveillance, and accessibility tools, the ability to accurately recognize and interpret sounds is becoming increasingly important. A robust algorithm in this domain could help build smarter, more responsive, and more inclusive AI systems.
----------------------
# Description

# Audio Classification Challenge
## Problem Statement
Develop an algorithm to classify and distinguish between five different real-world audio categories using machine learning techniques. The core objective is to accurately predict the class of audio samples in the unlabeled test set, leveraging the provided labeled training set for model development and validation.

## Dataset
The dataset consists of the following structure:
#### Train/
- dog_bark/
- drilling/
- engine_idling/
- siren/
- street_music/

#### Test/
The Test directory contains audio files of unknown class labels. Your task is to predict the correct category for each audio file in the test set.

## Submission Format
Submissions must be made in a .csv file containing two columns:
|     ID     |     Class |
|     ---     |     ---     |
|     test001.wav     |     dog_bark     |
|     test002.wav     |     siren     |
|     ....................     |     ........     |

The filename column should contain the exact name of each test audio file, and the label column should specify your predicted class for that audio file (must be one of the five provided categories).


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using classification accuracy on the test set.
Accuracy measures the proportion of test samples that are correctly classified into their true categories. Your submission .csv will be compared against the hidden ground-truth labels, and your score will be the percentage of correct predictions.

**Accuracy = Number of Correct Predictions/Total Number of Test Samples**

## Submission File
You must submit a .csv file with two columns: filename and label. The label must be one of the five class names provided in the training set. Only one prediction per file is allowed.
In case of a tie, submissions with the same accuracy will be ranked based on the lowest number of submissions.

    ID, Class
    test001, dog_bark
    test002, siren
    test003, drilling
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of two folders :
- **Train** folder has 5 subfolders, each consisting of audio files belonging to a certain category.
- **Test** folder contains random audio files. U have to predict which audio file belongs to which category.

sample_submission.csv is a sample file showing how to submit your predictions.",False,True
