10264,we-do-wind-energy-fault-detector-challenge,"# Overview

Overview section
----------------------
# Description

This Challenge is a WeDoWind Challenge focusing on early fault detection in wind turbine SCADA-data. You can find more information [here](https://community.wedowind.ch/posts/the-energyfaultdetector-space-the-energyfaultdetector-challenge). If you enter through Kaggle please [register](https://community.wedowind.ch/posts/the-energyfaultdetector-space-challenge-registration) yourself for the challenge via WeDoWind. [Interim webinars](https://community.wedowind.ch/events/interim-webinar-2-energyfaultdetector-challenge) and the [final result presentation](https://community.wedowind.ch/events/final-event-energyfaultdetector-challenge) will be coordinated there.

# Background and Motivation
Early fault detection optimizes wind farm operations by using automated evaluations to flag potential anomalies before they lead to a fault. This enables predictive maintenance, which leads to lower maintenance costs. Also it increases operational efficiency by reducing downtime, and improving the reliability of plant operations, which lowers the cost of electricity generation.

Evaluating the quality of early fault detection software is a non-trivial problem, and publications to date have focused primarily on case studies in which successful error detection was possible. A standardized benchmark for such approaches does not yet exist. For this reason, participants in this challenge will be provided with data from different types of turbines in different wind farms.

A solution template based on the EnergyFaultDetector framework is provided in the code section. This framework is available as open source software on GitHub. Participants are welcome to build their solutions on this framework and also merge them into GitHub. However, completely independent solutions may also be submitted.

# Challenge
The aim of the challenge is to detect actionable anomalies before faults in sensor data (from SCADA systems) from wind turbines.

# Important Links

- You can register for the challenge by [here](https://community.wedowind.ch/posts/the-energyfaultdetector-space-challenge-registration)
- Recordings of the Kickoff Meeting, interim Webinars and the final meeting can be found [here](https://community.wedowind.ch/posts/the-energyfaultdetector-space-webinar-recordings)
- A Solution template can be found [here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/we-do-wind-challenge-energy-fault-detector/code)

----------------------
# Evaluation

This challenge uses the CARE score to evaluate anomaly detection models for early fault detection. CARE combines four dimensions: Coverage, Accuracy, Reliability, and Earliness, following the CARE to Compare benchmark for wind turbine data. It measures how well a model separates normal behavior from actionable anomalies while not accounting for the detection of non-actionable anomalies. Coverage is expressed through the pointwise F-beta-score on anomaly events. Accuracy is the classification accuracy on normal events. Reliability is the eventwise F-beta-score across all evaluated events, where an event counts as detected if the criticality measure exceeds 72 for the given event. More infos on the criticality measure can be found in the CARE-Paper (algorithm 1). Earliness rewards alarms raised earlier within anomaly event windows by applying weights to every detected data point within an anomaly event and computing the sum over all weighted detections relative to the total number of possible detections. The final score is a weighted combination of these components. The beta parameters of the F-beta-scores is set to 0.5, rewarding low false positive rates.
 

More detail on the CARE-Score refer to the [paper](https://doi.org/10.3390/data9120138).


## Submission File
For each event ID in the whole dataset, you must predict a boolean value for each timestamp in the prediction section of the event-dataset. For an example evaluation see the [solution template](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/we-do-wind-challenge-energy-fault-detector/code). The submission file should have the following format:

    event_id,prediction,prediction_index
    1,[True, False, True, False,...],[42, 43, 44, 45,...]
    2,[False, False, True, False,...],[52, 53, 54, 55,...]
    3,[True, True, True, False,...],[101, 102, 103, 104,...]
    etc.

Note that each entry in the columns 'prediction' and 'prediction_index' is a list. The prediction_index can optionally be a list of timestamps instead of a list of integer IDs.","# Dataset Description

The dataset and a description can be found on [zenodo](https://zenodo.org/records/15846963). An automatic download function is contained in the EnergyFaultDetector Package alongside with classes to handle the [dataset ](https://github.com/AEFDI/EnergyFaultDetector/blob/main/energy_fault_detector/evaluation/care2compare.py)and the evaluation metric - the [CARE-Score](https://github.com/AEFDI/EnergyFaultDetector/blob/main/energy_fault_detector/evaluation/care_score.py).
See also the [dataset description](https://community.wedowind.ch/posts/the-energyfaultdetector-space-scada-dataset) on WeDoWind.",False,True
