10270,animation,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the quality of wine based on various properties. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored based on the?quadratic weighted kappa, which measures the agreement between two outcomes. This metric typically varies from 0 (random agreement) to 1 (complete agreement). In the event that there is less agreement than expected by chance, the?metric may go below 0.

The quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as follows. First, an N?x?N histogram matrix <em>O</em> is constructed, such that <em>O<sub>i,j</sub></em> corresponds to the number of `Id`s <em>i</em> (actual) that received a predicted value <em>j</em>.?An <em>N-by-N </em>matrix of weights, <em>w</em>, is calculated based on the difference between actual and predicted values:

$$w_{i,j} = \frac{\left(i-j\right)^2}{\left(N-1\right)^2}$$

An <em>N-by-N</em> histogram matrix of expected outcomes, <em>E</em>, is calculated assuming that there is no correlation between values.? This is calculated as the outer product between the actual histogram vector of outcomes and the predicted histogram vector, normalized such that <em>E</em> and <em>O</em> have the same sum.

From these three matrices, the quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as:?

$$\kappa=1-\frac{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}O\_{i,j}}{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}E\_{i,j}}.$$


## Submission File
For each `Id` in the test set, you must predict the value for the target `quality`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,quality
    2056,5
    2057,7
    2058,3
    etc.",,False,True
10271,ufc-fight-outcome-prediction-challenge,"# Overview

# ?? UFC Fight Outcome Prediction Challenge

Welcome to the UFC Fight Outcome Prediction Challenge, where your goal is to predict the outcome of MMA fights using real-world data. Each row in the dataset represents a single UFC fight, and the features describe career-level averages for each fighter ¡ª not their stats in that specific bout.
----------------------
# Description

# ?? Overview

This competition is designed for machine learning enthusiasts and sports analytics fans who want to apply predictive modeling to combat sports.

Your task is to build a model that predicts whether Fighter A wins the fight (Winner_A = 1) or not (Winner_A = 0).

The dataset contains over 8,500 UFC fights from 1994¨C2025, including aggregated fighter-level statistics such as striking accuracy, grappling efficiency, and control time.

# ?? Objective

Given the training data with known outcomes, participants must predict the whether Fighter A wins for each fight in the test set.
Your final submission should include two columns: `Fight_ID` and `Winner_A`.

*Fight_ID* comes from the same column available in the test set.
*Winner_A* must have boolean values (1 if fighter A won, 0 otherwise).

# ?? Files

**train.csv** ¡ª Training data including the target column Winner_A

**test.csv** ¡ª Test data without the target

**sample_submission.csv** ¡ª Example submission format

# ?? Timeline

Start date: October 2025

End date: December 31, 2025 (23:55 CET)

Final results revealed: January 1, 2026 (14:00 CET)

# ?? Rules

- Participants may only use the data provided in this competition.

- External data sources are not allowed.

- The competition is for educational and research purposes only.

- Submissions must be generated by your own code and models.

# ?? Prizes

No monetary prizes are awarded. Winners will receive recognition on the leaderboard and in the competition overview.

# ?? Tags

- Sports Analytics
- Machine Learning 
- Binary Classification
- Feature Engineering
- Gradient Boosting

# ?? Author

Juan L¨®pez ¡ª Valencia, Spain
Created for educational and research purposes.
LinkedIn profile: https://www.linkedin.com/in/juanlopez1590/
----------------------
# Evaluation

# ?? Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the **accuracy** parameter between the predicted winner and the true outcome.

The higher your accuracy score is, the better your model¡¯s ability to discriminate between Fighter A wins and losses.","# Dataset Description

Participants need to download **train.csv** to make the model, while also needing to download **test.csv** for making the prediction. After that, they will need to save a new dataframe made by *Fight_ID* (column available in *test.csv*) and *Winner_A* (the boolean values for the prediction: 1 if fighter A is predicted to win, 0 otherwise).

All the data is **already clean from missing values**.

The objective is to **predict the column *Winner_A*** (boolean) to optimize the accuracy score.

If there is any doubt about what each column means, they appear at each fight from ufcstats.com, as the data is webscrapped from there (for example, *Sig_Str* means Significant Strikes).

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `example_id` - definition of example_id
*   `feature_1`?- definition of feature_1
*   etc.

* `Fight_ID`: Unique identifier for each fight.

* `Date`: Date of the event.

* `Weight_Class`: Weight division of the bout (e.g., lightweight, welterweight).

* `Title_Bout`: Boolean: 1 if it was a title fight, 0 otherwise.

* `Season`: Quarter of the year when the fight took place (1-4).

* `Fighter_A_Name` and `Fighter_B_Name`: Fighters' names.

* `Fighter_A_* and Fighter_B_*`: Aggregated career statistics (averages in their previous career). For example, *Fighter_A_Sig_Str_Landed* is the mean number of significant strikes landed per fight across the fighter's career.

* `Fighter_A_Is_Debut` and `Fighter_B_Is_Debut`: Boolean: 1 if it was the fighter's debut.

* `Winner_A`: Boolean target variable: 1 if Fighter A won, 0 if Fighter B won.",False,True
10272,nuume-2025-ai-midterm-exam,"# Overview

NUUME 2025 AI Midterm ¡ª Deep Neural Network Flower Classification

Build a deep learning model that classifies flower images into the correct species. Your objective is simple: maximize classification accuracy on the hidden test set (final ranking uses the private leaderboard).
Train a neural network (e.g., CNN/Transformer) to predict the flower class for each test image and submit a CSV of predictions.
----------------------
# Description

The description section is used to further explain the details of your competition. You could explain more about the domain or go into more depth about the problem. You can upload images using the ""Insert Image"" icon on the bar that displays above this textbox when in edit mode.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Accuracy-Score.

---

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files
train file - the training set
test file - the test set
sample_submission2025.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
label mapping.csv** - supplemental information to answer about the data

Predicting definition of example label mapping to answer
Daisy(class:0)
Dandelion(class:1)
Rose(class:2)
Sunflower(class:3)
Tulip(class:4)",False,True
10273,bio-signal-intelligence-smoker-status-prediction,"# Overview

**Welcome to the Bio-Signal Intelligence Challenge.**

In the evolving landscape of medical diagnostics and preventative healthcare, the ability to accurately identify lifestyle risk factors purely from physiological data is a critical frontier.

**The Challenge** Your task in this competition is to build a robust binary classification model capable of predicting a patient's smoking status (Smoker vs. Non-Smoker).

You have been provided with a high-dimensional dataset comprising physiological bio-signals and general health indicators. Unlike simple datasets where correlations are obvious, this challenge requires participants to employ advanced feature engineering, dimensionality reduction, and modeling techniques to discern the subtle, often noisy patterns that distinguish a smoker's physiology from a non-smoker's.

Can your model separate the biological signal from the noise? Good luck.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the probability for the target variable `smoking`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,smoking
    159256,0.5
    159257,0.5
    159258,0.5
    etc.",,False,True
10274,predicting-smoking-behavior-from-physiological-signals,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use binary classification to predict a patient's smoking status given information about various other health indicators. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the probability for the target variable `smoking`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,smoking
    159256,0.5
    159257,0.5
    159258,0.5
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Smoker Status Prediction using Bio-Signals](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/gauravduttakiit/smoker-status-prediction-using-biosignals) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `smoking` is the binary target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of positive `smoking`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
