10276,crop-yield-prediction-challenge,"# Overview

Welcome to the Crop Yield Prediction Challenge!
Your mission is to build a regression model that predicts the crop yield per hectare based on environmental and agricultural features such as soil quality, rainfall, temperature, and fertilizer usage.

Accurate yield prediction is vital for sustainable agriculture, helping farmers optimize resources, reduce waste, and improve productivity.
----------------------
# Description

Crop yield depends on many environmental and management factors ¡ª from rainfall to fertilizer application.
In this competition, you¡¯ll use real-world inspired synthetic data to estimate how much yield (in tons per hectare) a given field will produce.

## You¡¯ll get:

- `train.csv` ¡ª containing features and yield labels.

- `test.csv` ¡ª with the same features, but without yield labels.

You¡¯ll predict yield_tpha (tons per hectare) for each test record.
----------------------
# ?? Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).
Lower RMSE = better performance.","# Dataset Description

This dataset is provided for the **Crop Yield Prediction Challenge** and contains the necessary files to train and evaluate your model. Below is an overview of the available files and their format.

Files
- **crop_yield_train.csv** - The training dataset containing input features and corresponding labels.
- **crop_yield_test.csv** - The test dataset for final evaluation.
- **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file showing the correct format for submitting predictions.
Columns

## Columns
| id                   | Unique identifier                                                |
| soil_ph              | Soil pH value                                                    |
| soil_moisture        | Soil moisture percentage                                         |
| avg_temperature      | Average temperature during growing season (¡ãC)                   |
| total_rainfall       | Total rainfall (mm)                                              |
| fertilizer_amount    | Fertilizer used (kg/ha)                                          |
| pesticide_usage      | Pesticide used (L/ha)                                            |
| sunlight_hours       | Total sunlight exposure (hours)                                  |
| nitrogen_content     | Nitrogen concentration (%)                                       |
| phosphorus_content   | Phosphorus concentration (%)                                     |
| potassium_content    | Potassium concentration (%)                                      |
| irrigation_frequency | Watering frequency (times/week)                                  |
| crop_type            | Type of crop (categorical)                                       |
| region               | Geographic region                                                |
| season               | Growing season                                                   |
| yield_tpha           | Yield (tons per hectare) *(target variable ¡ª only in train.csv)* |",False,True
10277,introduction-to-data-secience-competition-ttmatch,"# Overview

Table tennis is one of the fastest and most dynamic racket sports in the world.
A single rally often unfolds within just a few seconds, with each stroke requiring players to rapidly assess the ball¡¯s landing point, spin, speed, and the opponent¡¯s position ¡ª all while executing a precise return in an extremely short reaction time.

Such a high-tempo competitive environment makes predicting the next shot or the rally¡¯s eventual winner not only valuable for players and coaches in developing more effective strategies, but also an excellent application scenario for sports analytics and artificial intelligence research.

In this competition, your task is to build a model that predicts the type of the next shot (e.g., topspin, counter-loop, smash, etc.).

The dataset provided for this challenge is collected from real-world table tennis matches, and includes:

 - The landing coordinates of each shot on the table.

 - The shot type and related performance data of each player.

 - The final rally outcome indicates which player won the point.

Participants must leverage these temporal and categorical features to learn the evolution of shot sequences and player decision-making patterns, and make predictions about how each rally will unfold before the ball actually lands.

This challenge combines sports data analysis, sequence modeling, and real-time prediction. It encourages competitors to explore advanced techniques such as Sequence-to-Sequence models, RNNs, or Transformers for modeling the temporal and event dynamics of sports competitions.

The targets you are required to predict are as follows:

### Next stroke (n-th shot) action type prediction(actionId)
 - Predict which action type the opponent will use for the next return.
### Next stroke (n-th shot) landing location prediction(pointId) 
 - Predict where the ball will land after the striker¡¯s shot.
### Current rally outcome prediction
 - Giving the shots already played in the current rally, predict which side will ultimately win the point.


----------------------
# Evaluation

## Next stroke (n-th shot) action type prediction(actionId)
     Macro F1-Score

## Next stroke (n-th shot) landing location prediction(pointId) 
     Macro F1-Score

## Current rally outcome prediction(ServerGetPoint)
     AUC-ROC (Area Under the ROC Curve)

## Final score 
    Final score = 0.4 * action type result + 0.4 * landing point result + 0.2 * rally outcome result","# Dataset Description

The following are the details of the TTMATCH dataset.
You might have to take a deep look at the reference of each ID for what they present and choose the right embedding method for them.
Furthermore, you can also choose the features you think are important for training and drop those which you think are useless.
## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


