10282,foot-traffic-wuerzburg-retail-forecasting-2-0,"# Overview

Can you forecast hourly sales and customer counts for two retailers in Wuerzburg¡¯s city center? In this private competition, your goal is to develop predictive models that leverage historical sales and customer data together with pedestrian foot traffic to anticipate store-level activity.

By combining multimodal time series data, participants will tackle real-world forecasting challenges relevant to retail operations, marketing, and resource planning. This task builds on the insights of the previous [Foot Traffic Wuerzburg](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/foot-traffic-wue/) competition and invites more advanced exploration of data-driven retail analytics.
----------------------
# Description

Participants will work with hourly time series data that includes in-store transactions from two retailers located in W¨¹rzburg¡¯s historic city center. The prediction task is to forecast the **hourly customer counts** for **Company A** and **hourly sales across three product categories** for **Company B** over a two-week test period.

To support more accurate forecasting, participants are provided with pedestrian foot traffic data from three nearby streets: Schoenbornstrasse (green), Kaiserstrasse (red), and Spiegelstrasse (blue). These streets are equipped with laser-based counters that continuously measure pedestrian movement and were featured in the previous [Foot Traffic W¨¹rzburg](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/foot-traffic-wue/) competition.

While the previous competition focused on developing accurate models to forecast the hourly number of pedestrians in W¨¹rzburg¡¯s city center, this challenge takes the next step: foot traffic now serves as auxiliary information to support the prediction of customer counts and sales. This reflects the broader vision from the outset of this project ¡ª to ultimately use pedestrian data to improve forecasting for retailers and thereby support more effective operational and planning decisions.

To help participants, it is specified which of the three foot traffic counters is closest to each company. However, for privacy and legal reasons, we are not permitted to publish detailed information about the businesses involved. Accordingly, the data has been anonymized to protect the identity of both companies.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F22956516%2Fa561867dbe1950c848a32932bf359aba%2FStraenmessungen_Wurzburg_resized.png?generation=1746522933173881&alt=media)

The foot traffic data includes hourly pedestrian counts split by direction:

* `n_pedestrians` ¨C total pedestrian count
* `n_pedestrians_towards` ¨C pedestrians walking **towards** the city center
* `n_pedestrians_away` ¨C pedestrians walking **away** from the city center

**Important:** For the **test period**, the actual foot traffic data is **not provided**¡ªneither for the public leaderboard nor the private leaderboard test sets. Instead, only forecasts of the foot traffic can be used during this period to simulate the real-world setting, where the true foot traffic of future time points is also not available for planning purposes. Instead of requiring participants to build their own foot traffic forecasting models, we provide the **predictions generated by the best-performing model** from the previous [Foot Traffic W¨¹rzburg](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/foot-traffic-wue/) competition. These forecasts can be used directly as auxiliary inputs to support sales and customer count predictions. Participants may also choose to generate their own foot traffic forecasts if they believe they can improve upon the provided baseline.

In addition, participants have access to **contextual information** such as:

* **Weather data**, including temperature, rainfall, and other conditions, is provided together with the foot traffic data.
  ¡ú To simplify the forecasting task, **weather data is provided even for the test period**, removing the need to independently predict future weather.
* A curated list of **local events** (e.g., festivals, markets, city-wide celebrations)
* **Public holidays** and **school holidays** in Bavaria
* Data on the **lecture periods** of the University of Wuerzburg.

These features are expected to play a key role in explaining short-term deviations in customer activity.

Participants may incorporate additional **external data**, as long as it was publicly available **before the start of the test period**. In particular, **economic indicators**¡ªsuch as inflation data¡ªmay offer valuable signals to improve forecasting performance.

The locations of the foot traffic counters are also included in the data. For interactive exploration, see the [Open Data platform of the City of W¨¹rzburg](https://opendata.wuerzburg.de/explore/dataset/pedestrians_counter_geojson/map/?location=16,49.79659,9.93497&basemap=jawg.streets), where the measurement zones are visualized.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The performance of the submitted models will be evaluated using the Root [Mean Squared Error (MSE)](https://scikit-learn.org/1.5/modules/model_evaluation.html#mean-squared-error), computed across **all prediction targets and all hours** of the two-week test period.

In this competition, there are a total of **four target variables**:

* **Customer count** for **Company A**
* **Hourly sales** for **three product categories** of **Company B**

The final score is calculated as the **RMSE** over all observations in the test set:
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F22956516%2Ff34a31eb509bfdaf967b31abc78ca5ec%2FBildschirmfoto%202025-05-06%20um%2013.24.03.png?generation=1746530681835870&alt=media)

