## Practice Project : Car Color Classification
|**Day-Time**	 | **Item** |
| --- | --- |
| Tuesday 18 November 2025 at 9:00 AM| Open |  |
| Tuesday 18 November 2025 at 11:20 AM| Close |  |


## Member

This competition is an individual format. All competitors must join the competition first.

## Leaderboards

Each group is allowed to send results 50 times per day. The score will be displayed into 2 parts:
- 50 percents of dataset will be displayed in the Public Leaderboard where the contestants can check their scores at any time.
- 50 percents of dataset will be stored and displayed in the Private Leaderboard which will be used to calculate the score to determine the result of the competition. The results will be displayed after the competition is over.


## Evaluation Metric
Metric: Categorization Accuracy

## Submission
To submit the results, the test set should be predicted class result and collected into "".CSV"" file as shown in the example file ""sample_submission.csv"", otherwise Kaggle will not be able to calculate submission score.
----------------------
# Description

<p>The Dataset consists of xxx high-quality images of vehicles, spanning multiple models and six distinct color categories: yellow, blue, orange, red, green, and pink, with each image carefully labeled to indicate its corresponding color class, making the dataset highly suitable for the development and evaluation of advanced vehicle color recognition systems that rely on accurate color classification. The dataset provides a diverse set of examples across different car models and colors, allowing researchers and developers to train machine learning models that can generalize well to real-world vehicle images in various environments and lighting conditions.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions are evaluated using categorization accuracy. Each image has been labeled with one true class. For each image, you must submit a set of the predicted labels.</p>

<p>Before we delve into the details of each of the accuracy score, it is important to cover the four building blocks used to define the evaluation metrics:</p>

- True Positive (TP) ¨C Actual label is positive and prediction is also positive
- True Negative (TN) ¨C Actual label is negative and prediction is also negative
- False Positive (FP) ¨C Actual label is negative but prediction is positive
- False Negative (FN) ¨C Actual label is positive but prediction is negative

![test](https://i.ibb.co/HnvkpfQ/Classification-Image.png)

<p>Accuracy is the most basic version of evaluation metrics. It is calculated as the ratio of correct predictions (TP + TN) over all the predictions made (TP + TN + FP + FN).</p>

![test](https://miro.medium.com/max/1210/1*N4Lo9Miw397g3XpX7o0CDw.png)

<h3>Submission File</h3>
You must submit a csv file with the image file name and class.

The 6 classes to predict are:

- yellow
- blue
- orange
- red
- green
- pink

<p>The order of the rows does not matter. The file must have a header and should look like the following:</p>

<pre>id,classes
1.jpg,blue
2.jpg,red
3.jpg,yellow
...
</pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>This is a datasets of car images  with labeled. This Data contains around 5k images distributed under 3 categories (yellow, blue, orange, red, green, pink).</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>sample-submission.csv</b> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><b>starter_code.ipynb</b> - a python code to process a feature extraction, model and submission file</li>
<li><b>./car_color/train</b> - the training set contain 6 folders (yellow, blue, orange, red, green, pink) contains 4k images</li>
<li><b>./car_color/test</b> - the test set image contains around 1.5k images</li>
</ul>",False,True
10285,vignette-ocr,"# Description

#??  Pharmacovigilance: Drug Sticker Text Recognition Challenge

**Please note: The project submission deadline has been extended to 4-1-2026.**

Do you know how much time is wasted every day by pharmacy staff trying to manually input details from tiny, sometimes poorly printed **drug stickers**? They are the frontline against human error, tasked with identifying crucial information like batch number and expiration date to ensure patient safety and inventory accuracy. This time-consuming and error-prone process is ripe for an innovative **Artificial Intelligence** solution.

The core challenge of this competition is to build a robust **Object Detection** and **Optical Character Recognition (OCR) **pipeline to automatically extract and standardize critical text from images of drug stickers. Your data science skills will be directly applied to creating a tool for **Point-of-Sale (POS)** systems to instantly recognize and verify drug information. The dataset provided is an object detection dataset of drug stickers with **COCO JSON** annotation.

Currently, the major obstacle to automation is the **two-step complexity**: first, accurately **cropping the small, varied sticker** from the larger drug packaging, and second, performing reliable **text recognition** on the often low-quality, curved, or partially obscured sticker text. While deep learning is powerful, slight variations in lighting, packaging, and sticker placement prevent current methods from being entirely effective.

This competition is a project for the **Master 2 Machine Learning for IoT Course** at the **University of El Oued**, hosted by **Dr. Ahmed GHENABZIA**. Your contribution to this problem will have a direct and tangible impact, allowing pharmacy staff to save significant time and money. More importantly, your solution will help **prevent the accidental dispensing or purchasing of drugs with end expiration dates** or other critical mismatches, bolstering consumer safety and inventory management.

In this academic competition, you will apply your machine learning expertise to build a model that predicts and extracts the standardized text from drug stickers. The applications extend beyond the classroom; your work contributes to a future of streamlined, safer, and more efficient pharmacovigilance processes.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on the Character Error Rate (CER). A lower CER indicates better performance.

The CER is calculated as:

$$ \mathrm{CER} = \frac{S + D + I}{N} $$

Where:
- ***S***: number of substitutions
- ***D***: number of deletions
- ***I:*** number of insertions
- ***N***: total number of characters in the reference (ground truth) text.

Equivalently, CER can also be computed as the **Levenshtein distance** between the prediction and the reference, divided by the length of the reference text:

$$ \mathrm{CER} = \frac{LevenshteinDistance(pred,?ref)}{len(ref)} $$
	?
A perfect prediction yields **CER = 0**, meaning no character-level errors.

----


## Submission File
You must create a two-column CSV file with the sticker id and the predicted standardized text.

### Pediction Format Rules:
The predicted text must adhere to the following strict format:

- It must be **UPPERCASE**.

- It may only contain **Capital Letters, Numbers, /, and -**.

- **All spaces and new line characters must be removed.**

The file should have a header, be named submission.csv, and look like the following:

<div style=""background-color:#f8f8f8; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:6px; padding:12px; font-family:monospace; font-size:14px; color:#222;"">
  <pre style=""margin:0;"">
id,text
0016f1ad9e7f16aa6dd2b8c9c13cb922,A23B/45-C99
005e28f75a93bc4de0e9e11373bdca47,102456-D/7890
007f7b00eb0a66cabcd72007358e3660,AX-400/XY-900
etc.
  </pre>
</div>
----------------------
# Timeline

- **October 25, 2025** - Competition Start Date

- **November 15, 2025** - Entry and Team Merger deadline.

- **December 15, 2025** - Final submission deadline.


All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted.
----------------------
# Code Requirements

This is a **Code Competition**. Submissions must be made through Notebooks. The following constraints apply:

- **CPU/GPU Notebook Run Time:** Your total code execution must be much faster than standard competitions. Optimize your code for speed.

- **Internet access disabled**

- **Freely & publicly available external data is allowed**, including pre-trained models, provided they are loaded as part of your committed notebook.

- Submission file must be named `submission.csv` .",,False,True
10286,stanford-rna-3d-folding-2,"# Overview

If you were handed only a melody and asked to compose a full symphony, you¡¯d need to rely on patterns, structure, and musical intuition to build the rest. In the same way, predicting Ribonucleic acid (RNA)¡¯s 3D structure means using its sequence to determine how it folds into the shapes that define its function.

In this part 2 of the original Stanford RNA 3D Folding competition, help uncover how RNA molecules fold and function at the molecular level by developing machine learning models that predict the 3D structure of RNA molecules using only their sequences.
----------------------
# Description

RNA is essential to life¡¯s core functions, but predicting its 3D structure remains difficult. Unlike proteins, where models like AlphaFold have made major progress, RNA modeling is still held back by limited data and the complexity of RNA folding.

This is the second Stanford RNA 3D Folding Challenge. The first marked a major milestone, where fully automated models matched human experts for the first time. Now, you¡¯ll face even more complex targets, including ones with no structural templates, and a new evaluation metric designed to reward greater accuracy.

Your work could solve a key challenge in molecular biology. Better RNA structure prediction could unlock new treatments, accelerate research, and deepen our understanding of how life works. This competition runs on a roughly two-month timeline, aiming to surface new breakthroughs ahead of the 17th Critical Assessment of Structure Prediction (CASP17) in April 2026.

This competition is made possible through a worldwide collaborative effort including the organizers, experimental RNA structural biologists, NVIDIA Healthcare, the AI@HHMI initiative of the Howard Hughes Medical Institute, and Stanford University School of Medicine.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored using [TM-score](https://www.kaggle.com/code/metric/ribonanza-tm-score) (""template modeling"" score), which goes from 0.0 to 1.0 (higher is better):

$$
\text{TM-score} = \max \left( \frac{1}{L_{\text{ref}}} \sum_{i=1}^{L_{\text{align}}} \frac{1}{1 + \left( \frac{d_i}{d_0} \right)^2 } \right)
$$

where:
- L<sub>ref</sub> is the number of residues solved in the experimental reference structure (""ground truth"").
- L<sub>align</sub> is the number of aligned residues.
- d<sub>i</sub> is the distance between the i<sub>th</sub> pair of aligned residues, in Angstroms.
- d<sub>0</sub> is a distance scaling factor in Angstroms, defined as:

  $$
  d_0 = 0.6 (L_{\text{ref}} - 0.5)^{1/2}  - 2.5
  $$

for L<sub>ref</sub> &ge; 30; and d<sub>0</sub> = 0.3, 0.4, 0.5, 0.6, or 0.7 for  L<sub>ref</sub> <12, 12-15, 16-19, 20-23, or 24-29, respectively. 

The rotation and translation of predicted structures to align with experimental reference structures are carried out by [US-align](https://dx.doi.org/10.1038/s41592-022-01585-1). To reward high accuracy modeling, the alignment will reward only residues in the prediction that align with the reference residues with the same numbering. 

For each target RNA sequence, you will submit 5 predictions and your final score will be the average of best-of-5 TM-scores of all targets. For a few targets, multiple slightly different structures have been captured experimentally; your predictions' scores will be based on the best TM-score compared to each of these reference structures. 

The scoring metric is publicly available at [this link](https://www.kaggle.com/code/rhijudas/tm-score-permutechains). 

## Submission File
For each sequence in the test set, you can predict five structures. Your notebook should look for a file `test_sequences.csv` and output `submission.csv`. This file should contain x, y, z coordinates of the C1' atom in each residue across your predicted structures 1 to 5:

    ID,resname,resid,x_1,y_1,z_1,... x_5,y_5,z_5
    R1107_1,G,1,-7.561,9.392,9.361,... -7.301,9.023,8.932
    R1107_2,G,1,-8.02,11.014,14.606,... -7.953,10.02,12.127
    etc.

You must submit five sets of coordinates.

----------------------
# Timeline

* **January 7, 2026** - Start Date.
* **${competition.ProhibitNewEntrantsExplicitDeadline}**  - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete. 
* **${competition.TeamMergerExplicitDeadline}** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams. 
* **${competition.Deadline}** - Competition End Date & Final Submission Deadline. 
* **Week of March 30, 2026** - Private leaderboard release.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary. 
----------------------
# Prizes

### Leaderboard Prizes

* 1st Place -   $ 50,000
* 2nd Place - $ 15,000
* 3rd Place -  $ 10,000

### Paper Authorship
Top performing participants on the Private Leaderboard rankings will be invited to contribute their code and model descriptions to a scientific paper summarizing the competition's scientific outcome.


----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

### This is a Code Competition

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

- CPU Notebook <= 8 hours run-time
- GPU Notebook <= 8 hours run-time
- Internet access disabled
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
- Submission file must be named `submission.csv`
- Submission runtimes have been slightly obfuscated. If you repeat the exact same submission you will see up to 5 minutes of variance in the time before you receive your score.

### Future Data Evaluation Phase
The run-time limits for both CPU and GPU notebooks will be extended to during the future data evaluation period proportional to the number of future samples. You must ensure your submission completes within that time. The extra runtime will enable us to use a substantially larger test set as the basis for ranking submissions on the final private leaderboard.

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.
----------------------
# Additional Resources

**What's the state-of-the-art in RNA 3D structure prediction?**

[2024 CASP16 challenge ¨C 2025 paper from assessors](https://www.doi.org/10.1002/prot.70072)

**Part 1 of the Stanford RNA 3D Folding challenge**

[Outcome of Part 1, Kaggle Post](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/stanford-rna-3d-folding/discussion/609187)

[""Template-based RNA structure prediction advanced through a blind code competition"", Preprint summarizing Part 1](https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2025.12.30.696949v1.full)

**How to think about RNA structure**

[A perspective from domain experts](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2112677119)",,False,True
10287,the-great-monster-candy-heist,"# Overview

#### A Plea for Help from Detective Duffy: The Candy Heist Crisis

Attention, Sleuths!

This is Detective Duffy, and boy, do I need your help. Turns out, every five years, the Monster Candy Heist¡ªyeah, the thing from that famous urban legend that everyone knows¡ªactually happens! And guess what? This year¡¯s the year. Monsters are getting ready to descend on Halloween night, and we¡¯re not talking about kids in costumes¡ªreal ghosts, zombies, vampires, you name it! And these creepy critters are after one thing: candy. Lots of it.

Now, here¡¯s where you come in. I¡¯ve been tasked with stopping this outrageous travesty during each of the last three runnings, but¡­ well, let¡¯s just say profiling monsters isn¡¯t exactly in my wheelhouse. Who am I kidding...I have been a spectacular failure. I need some smart brains on this case. We¡¯ve got to figure out the characteristics of the monsters that are likely to steal the most candy. I hear you're good at modeling and predicting behaviors¡ªperfect! I need you to save my jo...errr, the children's candy!

Ghosts might be sneaky, zombies are slow but relentless, and vampires¡ªwho knows what they're up to with all that hair and charm. But we need cold, hard predictions.

Help me out here, sleuths. Time's running out, and I can¡¯t let another candy crisis hit this town on my watch. Let¡¯s catch these monsters before they ruin Halloween!

Yours in (mild) desperation,
Detective Duffy
----------------------
# Description

Help Detective Duffy by building the best predictive model for the amount of Halloween candy a monster will steal from unsuspecting kids. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your models will be assessed via Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE). Where

$$\texttt{RMSE} = \sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i = 1}^{n}{\left(y_{\text{observed}} - y_{\text{pred}}\right)^2}}{n}}$$","# Dataset Description

## Files

+ **HalloweenTrain.csv** - all of the available observations for model training -- you may want to split these into a `train`ing and `test` set so that you can validate your model performance before submitting to Kaggle.
+ **HalloweenComp.csv** - the data used to score models in the competition (the `stolen_candy_lbs` column is missing for these monsters)
+ **SampleSubmission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

+ `ID` - A row number, identifying each monster
+ `Height` - The height of the monster in cm
+ `HairLength` - The average length of the monster's hair in cm
+ `Color` - The primary noticeable color of the monster when viewed from afar
+ `RottingFleshPct` - The proportion of the monster's flesh which appears to be rotten
+ `Aura` - The intensity of a halo of light emitting from the monster
+ `BloodCoverage` - The proportion of the monster's body which is covered with blood
+ `MonsterClass` - The type of monster
+ `stolen_candy_lbs` - The amount of Halloween Candy stolen by the monster (in lbs)

Note that `stolen_candy_lbs` is your response variable for this competition.",False,True
