10289,turkey-pardoning-classification,"# Description

?? Introducing the Great Turkey Pardon Prediction Extravaganza! ??

Join us in the zaniest modeling competition of the year, where students become the clairvoyants of the turkey world! Sponsored by the *National Turkey Coalition* ¨C a conglomerate of gobbling enthusiasts, and its poster bird, Nervous Ned the Turkey ¨C a feathered dreamer with ambitions of presidential pardon.

Every Thanksgiving, as families prepare to gather for feasts, turkeys everywhere ponder two crucial questions:

+ What does it take to earn the Presidential Turkey Pardon?
+ Who will be pardoned from an undesirable seat at the dinner table?

This is where you step in! Unleash your data prowess and predictive skills to craft models that will predict the fate of these galloping gobblers.

Is it the mystical strut of a turkey, the aura of cranberry sauce, or perhaps a gravy-induced premonition that guides the President's choice? Dive into the quirkiest dataset you may ever encounter. Will your model unravel the mysterious patterns that determine which turkey gets a presidential pardon?

Whether you're a fledgling data wizard or an ML master, this competition welcomes all to compete, learn, and laugh! Prepare for feathered frenzy, statistical silliness, and predictive poultry in the most anticipated event of the season ¨C the Great Turkey Pardon Prediction Extravaganza!

(**Note:** The data for this competition is completely fabricated.)
----------------------
# Evaluation

You'll be evaluated using classification log-loss as your model's performance metric. This means that your model's level of certainty is being taken into account. Misclassifying a record where your model assigned a class probability of 53% is penalized less than misclassifying a record where your model assigned a class probability of 78%.

The public leaderboard will be displayed using 30% of the competition observations and the final leaderboard will be computed using the remaining 70%. Beware the dreaded shakeup -- just because you go into the final minute ranked first, doesn't necessarily mean you'll finish at the top!","# Dataset Description

## Files

You have access to the following files

+ `turkeys_train.csv` - the training set
+ `turkeys_comp.csv` - the competition set
+ `turkeys_sample_sub.csv` - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

+ `ID` is a unique identifier for the turkey within each data set.
+ `primary_color` is the primary feather color of the turkey. 
+ `secondary_color` is the secondary feather color of the turkey, located on the breast of the bird.
+ `height` measures the standing height of the turkey in inches.
+ `wingspan` measures the wingspan of the turkey in inches.
+ `weight` measures the weight of the turkey in pounds.
+ `hobby` indicates the turkey's favorite past-time (you didn't know turkeys have hobbies?).
+ `dsb` is the number of days since the most recent biting incident involving the turkey.
+ `pardoned` is your target/response variable, indicating whether the turkey was pardoned or passed over during a previous pardoning opportunity.",False,True
10290,farmyard-flex-and-gobbling-gains-ag-2025,"# Overview

**Calling All Mighty Turkeys!** ????

Do you have what it takes to be crowned the Strongest Turkey on the Farm? Farmer Greene's Annual Turkey Bulking....errrr Weightlifting Contest(!) is here, and we¡¯re on the hunt for the mightiest gobblers in the barnyard!

This year¡¯s contest promises to be bigger and better than ever, with turkeys from all species competing to lift weights and win eternal farmyard glory (and some extra feed pellets)! Whether you're an Osceola powerhouse, a Rio Grande sprinter, or a Merriam with majestic wings, your strength will be put to the test.

**Competition Details:** 

+ Turkeys will showcase their strength by lifting as much weight as possible.
+ Judging will be based on a mix of strength, technique, and sheer gobbling determination.
+ Categories include Best in Species and Overall Champion!

Don¡¯t delay¡ªstart your training today! Enlist now at Farmer Greene's barn, where champions are made. ????¡á? Let¡¯s see who will rise to the top of the pecking order!

Sincerely,
Farmer Greene
----------------------
# Description

Welcome to the **Turkey Weightlifting Regression Challenge!**

Your task is to build a predictive model to determine the maximum weight a turkey can lift based on a variety of physical and personality features. Farmer Greene has carefully documented the characteristics and training regimens of each turkey in this year¡¯s competition, and your goal is to use these data points to uncover what makes a turkey strong!

The dataset includes:

+ **Physical attributes** such as turkey weight, wingspan, and age.
+ **Training factors** like weeks spent in training, practice sessions, and feed protein content.
+ **Unique quirks** like feather shine score, barnyard rival count, and gobble style.

As the competition progresses, you¡¯ll get to explore the relationships between these variables and predict the champion gobbler¡¯s lifting abilities! Use your skills in feature engineering, regression modeling, and creativity to uncover patterns in this whimsical world of turkey athletics.

Will you find the secret recipe to turkey strength? Gobble up the data, and let¡¯s find out! ????
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between the predicted weights and the observed weights.


## Submission File

For each ID in the test set, you must predict a value for the Max_Weight_Lifted variable. The file should be a csv file containing **only** the `ID` and `Max_Weight_Lifted` columns. Additionally, the file should have 7500 rows.","# Dataset Description

## Files

+   **turkey_train.csv** - the training set
+   **turkey_test.csv** - the test set
+   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

+ `Turkey_Weight`: Turkey's weight (lbs).
+ `Turkey_Age`: Age in months.
+ `Training_Weeks`: Duration of training in weeks.
+ `Protein_Content`: Protein intake (grams/day).
+ `Practice_Sessions`: Practice sessions per week.
+ `Species`: Turkey species (categorical).
+ `Wingspan`: Turkey wingspan (cm).
+ `FiveK_Time`: Most recent 5k finish time (minutes).
+ `Glide_Distance`: Maximum glide distance (meters).
+ `Feather_Shine`: Feather shine score.
+ `Training_Jams`: Favorite music genre during training.
+ `Barnyard_Rivals`: Number of barnyard enemies made.
+ `Confidence_Score`: Turkey confidence level.
+ `Gobble_Style`: Gobble style (categorical).
+ `Feather_Pattern`: Feather complexity score.
+ `Gobble_Volume`: Gobble volume (decibels).
+ `Max_Weight_Lifted`: Response variable (maximum weight lifted by the turkey in lbs).",False,True
10291,spdp,"# Overview

**Welcome to the Royale 2025** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** Predict the probability of various defects on steel plates. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) using the predicted probabilities and the ground truth targets.

To calculate the final score, AUC is calculated for each of the 7 defect categories and then averaged. In other words, the score is the average of the individual AUC of each predicted column.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the probability for each of 7 defect categories: `Pastry`, `Z_Scratch`, `K_Scatch`, `Stains`, `Dirtiness`, `Bumps`, `Other_Faults`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Pastry,Z_Scratch,K_Scatch,Stains,Dirtiness,Bumps,Other_Faults
    19219,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
    19220,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
    19221,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
    etc.","# Dataset Description

Welcome to VITC SteelWorks Pvt. Ltd., a futuristic smart factory where every steel plate is forged by autonomous robots and inspected by AI.
Everything was running smoothly ¡ª until last week .
Suddenly, batches of plates started coming out scratched, stained, and oddly bumpy. Someone tampered with the inspection robots!
Your mission: retrain the AI Quality Inspector to detect which kind of defect each plate has before the factory opens for VITC¡¯s annual tech showcase!
Can you uncover who scratched the plates and restore order to the production line? 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; there are 7 binary targets: `Pastry`, `Z_Scratch`, `K_Scatch`, `Stains`, `Dirtiness`, `Bumps`, `Other_Faults`
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of each of the 7 binary targets
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
