----------------------
# Description

In today¡¯s data-driven financial landscape, understanding stock market behavior through time series and probabilistic models is essential. This assessment challenges students to apply advanced modeling techniques to real-world stock data, revealing patterns in price movement and investor behavior.
The Yahoo Finance dataset contains daily stock prices (Open, High, Low, Close, Volume) for select companies (e.g., Infosys, Reliance) from 2020¨C2024. It includes macroeconomic indicators (e.g., interest rates, inflation) to assess real-world confounding effects.

**Incase of any issues, please access the starter notebooks and the dataset using this drive link : [Orange-2](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1g4iordMB37_A-L1y6aeIJw5H6ZhMfL1J?usp=sharing)**
##Your Task:
- Decompose time series data to identify trend, seasonality, and residual components.  
- Test for stationarity using appropriate statistical methods (e.g., ADF test).  
- Apply ARIMA models for forecasting and interpret the model results.  
- Construct and analyze Markov chains, including identifying and interpreting absorbing states to study market transitions.  
- Model investor behavior and retention patterns using statistical or probabilistic approaches.  
- Control and manage confounding variables when performing regression analysis.  
- Evaluate model performance using metrics such as AIC, R^2. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric: Symmetric Mean Absolute Percentage Error (SMAPE)

The performance of the model in this competition will be evaluated using the **Symmetric Mean Absolute Percentage Error (SMAPE)** metric.  
SMAPE measures the accuracy of forecasts by comparing the predicted values to the actual values, expressed as a percentage.  
It is a variation of the Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) that treats overestimation and underestimation symmetrically.

**SMAPE Formula**

$$
\text{SMAPE} = \frac{100\%}{n} \sum_{t=1}^{n} \frac{|F_t - A_t|}{\frac{|A_t| + |F_t|}{2}}
$$

### Interpretation

- **SMAPE = 0%** ¡ú Perfect prediction (no error).  
- **Lower SMAPE values** ¡ú Better model performance.  
- **Higher SMAPE values** ¡ú Larger deviation between predicted and actual values.  
- Unlike MAPE, SMAPE is **bounded between 0% and 200%**, making it more robust when actual values are close to zero.

---

### Python Code to Compute SMAPE

```python
smape = np.mean(200 * np.abs(y_pred - y_true) / (np.abs(y_true) + np.abs(y_pred)))
```
