10304,machine-learning-for-io-t-2025-lab-2,"# Description

### Introduction 
Epilepsy affects more than 64 million people worldwide. Despite substantial progress in the efficacy of anti-epileptic drugs, one-third of the epileptic patients continue to have seizures despite anti-epileptic medication. The unpredictable nature of seizures can be life-threatening, causing epilepsy patients to have a 2-3 times higher mortality rate than healthy control groups. 

Ideally, we would like to warn patients that a seizure is going to occur, so the patient can make sure they are in a safe environment (e.g. not near sharp objects) and can get emergency medication administered by their caregiver. At the moment, the standard procedure for such epileptic patient monitoring is based on simultaneous video recordings and multiple electroencephalogram electrodes (video-EEG). However, video recordings seriously impact the patient¡¯s privacy, and EEG measurements are invasive, requiring multiple electrodes to be attached to the patient¡¯s head at all times. Therefore, it is not possible to monitor patients on a long-term basis and in ambulatory settings based on such video-EEG. 

Wearable devices are light and only minimally invasive, so they do facilitate remote patient monitoring. For example, the e-Glass [1] is a pair of glasses that uses four EEG electrodes embedded and hidden in the temples of the glasses for real-time unobtrusive epilepsy monitoring. 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11405508%2F7432020e9cd9e8cdb4605b9b45ca62b6%2FScreenshot%20from%202023-10-31%2014-21-59.png?generation=1698758571972239&alt=media)

If we want to use the e-Glass to warn patients and caregivers, it needs to have a piece of software that analyzes the incoming EEG signals and determines whether or not the patient has a seizure at a given time. 
In the three labs of this course, you will implement a machine learning system for this purpose. The labs are built up in the following way: 
1.	In lab 1, you will use a Convolutional Neural Network
2.	In lab 2, you will use a Lightweight Machine Learning algorithm
3.	In lab 3, you will use a low-power machine learning network suitable to run on wearable devices. 

[1] Sopic, D., Aminifar, A., & Atienza, D. (2018, May). e-glass: A wearable system for real-time detection of epileptic seizures. In 2018 IEEE International Symposium on Circuits and Systems (ISCAS) (pp. 1-5). IEEE.

----------------------
# Evaluation

If you use the provided file 'Lab2-wo-answers.ipynb', the output file submission.csv should already be formatted in the correct way: It should have two columns, one with IDs, the other one with your answers, separated by commas. 

We compare your submission with our solution file (that contains the answers to the multiple choice/open questions and the ground truth labels) using the categorization accuracy metric in Kaggle. The results you will see are the results about the prediction of the labels only: They do not tell you anything about your score for the rest of the questions.","# Dataset Description

The dataset contains the only file you will need for this lab: patient.mat . This is a 4-lead EEG recording (similar to the E-glass setup) of a single patient that has been preprocessed for you.",False,True
10305,cs-506-fall-2025-technical-midterm,"# Overview

 
----------------------
# Description

Please accept the [GitHub Classroom Assignment](https://classroom.github.com/a/gJ7smGl3).

The goal of this competition is to predict reviewer ratings of music releases (albums, EPs, etc.). You should make these predictions using the set of various features provided. This is an individual competition. AI tools such as ChatGPT are allowed.

You may use any classical method except neural networks / deep learning on this assignment - it is up to you to research whether a library makes use of deep learning (**please do not ask on discord if a given library is allowed**). Any methods that you use that were not covered in class must be fully cited and you must explain how you used all external and online resources.

Additionally, no boosting methods (including XGBoost, LGBM etc.) or libraries are allowed.

In addition to submitting your fully reproducible solution to your github repo, your repo should also contain a README.md explaining what you did for each of the data science steps:

1.    Data Exploration
2.   Feature Extraction / Engineering
3.   Model Creation and Assumptions
4.   Model Tuning
5.   Model Evaluation / Performance
6.   Struggles / Issues / Open Questions

This competition ends at 4:30pm EST on Monday, Nov 3rd. The private leaderboards will be made available during class where we will announce winners and prizes. You can finish up your README after class until 11:59pm when you must submit it to gradescope. **Please include your kaggle username in the README**

This competition uses **Macro Averaged F1-Scoring** as the evaluation metric. You only get 5 submissions per day because it's expected you do your own local evaluation instead of overfitting to the kaggle leaderboard. This cannot be changed - make sure to start early.

The top 2 public submissions will be used for your final score. If you want to select a different two submissions (that aren't your best public scores) you can but I don't recommend it. If you overwrite this default, you understand that these are the submissions that will be used instead - no changes to these can be made after the competition ends.

Grading will be based on the benchmark you hit, and the closeness you achieve towards the next benchmark. Points may be awarded or deducted based on the state of the repo, the steps / process you followed to get to the benchmark, and the reproducibility of one of your best scores (which should be the only code in your repo once the competition ends).

Note: Gradescope does not automatically sync with GitHub. If you submitted your repo to gradescope and made changes to the repo these will not be reflected in gradescope. You must re-submit. Expect this submission process to take some time so please make sure you give yourself at least an hour before the deadline.

**Note: DO NOT GIT ADD / COMMIT CSVs or DATA TO GITHUB.**
----------------------
# Evaluation

Each review has a unique identifier associated with it. **This competition uses Macro Averaged F1 Scoring.** Every label will have its F1 scores averaged equally, regardless of their independent frequencies. 
<https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html>","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - Contains 447,583 unique reviews. 80,000 of these reviews will have a missing score column. These are the scores you want to predict
*   **test.csv** - A two-column table, where the first column is the 80k reviews mentioned above with a missing score column, and the second column is the Score column you are intended to make predictions with. You will use train.csv to make these predictions
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `id` - Unique identifier for every individual review
*   `reviewerID`?- Unique identifier for the reviewer who wrote the review
*   `album_mbid` - Unique identifier for the specific album being reviewed
*   `artist_mbid` - Unique identifier for the artist that made the album being reviewed
*   `unixReviewTime` - The time the review was made, described in Unix Time
*   `VotedHelpful` - The number of people who voted on a review, and marked it as helpful
*   `TotalVotes` - The total number of people who voted on the review, whether voted helpful or not
*   `summary` - A brief summary of the review
*   `reviewText` - The pure text content of the review
*   `genres` - A comma-separated list of the genres the album could be classified under
*   `Score` - An integer rating in between 1 and 5. Blank for the fields you are intended to predict

Note: Some of the rows of the table may have some of these values missing.",False,True
