10307,qcd-tt-jet-tagging-hsf-india-hackathon-hyd,"# Overview

Welcome to this beginner-to-advanced friendly machine learning competition on Kaggle!

In this challenge, you¡¯ll use simulations of real-world collider physics data to classify jets as either:
	?	QCD jets (background)
	?	Top quark (tt?) jets (signal)

Whether you¡¯re just starting out with ML or you¡¯re looking to sharpen your skills on a scientific dataset, this is a great opportunity to explore particle physics, experiment with models, and learn to handle structured and image-based data alike.

The data is from the 2011 CMS Open Simulation, and has been made using the data published by Javier and Harrison [1]. 

[1]Javier Duarte and Harrison Prosper, ¡®Fermilab LHC Physics Center Machine Learning Hands-On Advanced Tutorial Session Datasets¡¯. Zenodo, Jun. 20, 2020. doi: 10.5281/zenodo.3901869.
----------------------
# Description

**?? What Are Jets?**

At the Large Hadron Collider (LHC), protons collide at nearly the speed of light. These collisions can create quarks and gluons, the fundamental particles in Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD). But here¡¯s the catch: quarks and gluons don¡¯t appear alone ¡ª they quickly transform (a process called hadronization) into sprays of particles called jets.

Jets are one of the most common things seen in collider experiments like CMS or ATLAS, and they can come from many sources. Physicists need to tell apart ¡°ordinary¡± QCD jets from jets that come from interesting particles like top quarks.


**?? QCD Jets vs. tt? Jets**

Here¡¯s a simplified breakdown of the differences between the jets you will classify:
| Feature       | QCD Jets                    | Top Quark (tt?) Jets         |
|---------------|-----------------------------|------------------------------|
| Source        | Quarks or gluons            | From decay of a top quark    |
| Energy        | Often lower                 | Tend to be higher            |
| Structure     | Simpler, fewer particles    | More complex, often 3 sub-jets |
| Physics Role  | Background                  | Signal (interesting physics) |


Top quark jets are more structured and energetic, while QCD jets are more frequent and noisy.



**??  Starter Notebooks Provided**

To help you get started, we¡¯ve provided notebooks with different model types:
	?	 BDT (Gradient Boosting Trees)
	?	DNN (Dense Neural Network)
	?	CNN (Convolutional Neural Network)
	?	GNN (Graph Neural Network)

Feel free to use, modify, and combine them to build your own approach!



**?? Tips and Suggestions**

If you¡¯re new to physics or ML, don¡¯t worry. The important part is having a play around and see if you can build some intuition for how the data and models and have fun! 

You might try:
	?	Feature engineering
	?	Data augmentation (e.g., rotating jet images)
	?	Ensemble methods (e.g., voting across multiple models)
	?	Hyperparameter tuning

There's a whole document with suggestions in the data tab. 

Ready to classify some jets?
?? Good luck and have fun! 

----------------------
# Evaluation

**?? Understanding the ROC Curve**

You can see a detailed explanation of ROC and AUC [here](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/roc-and-auc)
When you¡¯re training a model to distinguish between QCD jets and top quark jets, it¡¯s important to understand not just how accurate your predictions are ¡ª but how well your model separates the two classes across different thresholds.

ROC stands for Receiver Operating Characteristic. The ROC curve is a plot that shows the tradeoff between:
	?	True Positive Rate (TPR) ¡ª also known as recall:
The fraction of actual tt jets correctly identified.
	?	False Positive Rate (FPR):
The fraction of QCD jets incorrectly identified as tt jets.

Each point on the curve corresponds to a different threshold for classifying a jet as either QCD or tt. A good model will have a curve that bows toward the top-left corner (depending on how you make your plot), which means it correctly identifies most true jets while avoiding false alarms.


**?? AUC**

The AUC (Area Under the Curve) is a single number that summarizes the ROC curve.
	?	AUC = 1.0 means perfect separation of the classes.
	?	AUC = 0.5 means the model is guessing randomly.
	?	AUC closer to 1 means better performance.


Accuracy only tells you how many predictions were correct at one specific threshold (usually 0.5).
ROC AUC shows how well your model ranks true positives higher than false positives regardless of the threshold.
This is especially helpful if the real-world data has more QCD than tt jets ¡ª a common case in particle physics. *The ROC AUC is the metric we will score you on*.  


## Submission File
Your submission must be in the correct format, otherwise you won't be able to submit. Also remember you will only see your results on part of the test set until the end of the competition. 

Your submission file should be a csv in this format where tt events are labelled 1 and QCD are 0 (this is also how the training set it, so you don't have to worry about this unless you change it in the training set). You want to submit the probability of the event being a tt event, so a decimal number between 0 and 1. You need the header names `id` and `label`, and you need to remote the index, so save with e.g. ```df.to_dataframe('submission_name.csv', index=False)```. The ids must match the ids in given in the test file, so don't change them. This is the format, and there is also a sample submission file in the data folder. 

    id,label
    2,0.1
    5,0.9
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

**?? About the Dataset**

We provide two formats:
	1.	Image Data: Each jet is converted into a 2D image (¦Ç-¦Õ plane), where the intensity reflects the energy deposits ¡ª perfect for CNNs or GNNs 
	2.	Data Frame: Jets are also represented as structured data (features like number of clusters, energy deposited, etc.), derived using the anti-kT algorithm ¡ª a standard method in collider physics for clustering particle tracks into jets. This is more apt for BDTs or DNNs. Could also be used for GNNs, but this is not included in the sample notebooks. 


**How was the data made?**
The images are made by recording energy deposited in a [calorimeter](https://lhcb-outreach.web.cern.ch/detector/calorimeters/). When a particle hits it, it dumps some of its energy and we record where and how much energy was deposited.Now, imagine slicing the detector like a cylindrical cake and unwrapping it into a flat surface. That¡¯s basically what a jet image is. These are the images in the dataset. The dataframes were made by processing the images with the anti-kT algorithm. 

**The anti-kT algorithm**
The anti-kT algorithm gathers nearby energy deposits (like particles close together) into a single ¡°jet¡±, based on their proximity and momentum. This gives us a clean and consistent definition of what we call a jet. 
You can read more about it [here](https://indico.cern.ch/event/1112883/contributions/4677589/attachments/2395256/4095349/Matteo_Cacciari_2008_J._High_Energy_Phys._2008_063.pdf)
You'll find the code I used in the util script under the code tab. Feel free to modify it to create new features if you'd like! 
The code I use is a simplified version of the full algorith. 

**Nomenclature** 

?? ¦Õ (Phi): The Compass Direction Around the Beam
	?	¦Õ (phi) is the angle around the beamline, like on a compass.
	?	It goes from 0 to 2¦Ð (or -¦Ð to ¦Ð).
	?	If a particle flies to the right, it might have ¦Õ = 0. If it flies behind you, it might be ¦Ð.

?? Analogy: Think of ¦Õ like rotating around the edge of a pizza.

?

?? p? (Transverse Momentum): Sideways Punch Power
	?	p? is how much momentum a particle has perpendicular to the beamline.
	?	Particles with high p? went flying sideways, which is interesting because:
	?	They are less likely to come from the boring remnants of the proton.
	?	They are more likely to come from interesting physics (like a top quark decay).

Please note that in our dataset we don't have the true particle p?, but we use the approximation $$p_T \approx E \cdot \sin(\theta) \approx E$$, which might not always be accurate. 

?? Analogy: p? is how hard the particle got ¡°punched sideways.¡±

?

?? ¦Ç (Eta): How Far Forward a Particle Went (Pseudo-Rapidity)
	?	¦Ç describes how forward or backward a particle flew ¡ª how close it stayed to the beamline.
	?	Big ¦Ç = particle went almost along the beam.
	?	Small ¦Ç = particle went out the side.
	?	It¡¯s a transformed version of angle (¦È) that makes math nicer when dealing with very fast particles.

?? Analogy: ¦Ç tells you if a particle flew out the side of the detector (low ¦Ç) or down the tunnel along the beamline (high ¦Ç).

?

?? Why Use ¦Ç and ¦Õ in ML or Physics?
	?	CMS is symmetric in ¦Õ, so patterns are easier to study there.
	?	¦Ç is more useful than ¦È for boosted collisions (think: fast-moving particles).
	?	Jet and event shapes are often described using (¦Ç, ¦Õ) maps ¡ª like heatmaps over the detector.
![The CMS coordinate system](https://tikz.net/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/axis3D_CMS-004.png)


What files do I need?  
What should I expect the data format to be?  
What am I predicting?  


## Files
There are three data directories, train, val and test. You can train your data on train and test your performance easily on the validation data without making a submission. If you want to test your models differently, you are also free to train on both train and val. The test data is what you make your submission on, which is why this directory does not have labels. 

Otherwise in each of those directories, you'll find 
*   **features/cluster_features.csv** - the dataframes made with the anti-Kt algorithm 
*   **ids/ids.npy** - unqiue IDs for each image/row of data 
*   **images/jet_images.h5** - the images
*   **labels/labels.npy** - the true labels 

You will also find some extra materials: 
* **supplementary/jet_kaggle_slides.pdf** - the slides shown in class 
* **supplementary/jet_competition_ideas.pdf** - ideas to try 
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns in the data frame 
The data contains the following information: 

**Basic Cluster Properties**

`n_clusters`: The total number of distinct clusters identified in the jet. This indicates how fragmented the jet's energy is distributed.
`total_pt`: The sum of transverse momentum (pT) across all clusters. This represents the total energy of the jet.

**Cluster Size Features**
`max_cluster_size`: The number of particles in the largest cluster. Larger values indicate more concentrated energy deposits.
`mean_cluster_size`: Average number of particles per cluster. Helps understand the typical cluster granularity.
`std_cluster_size`: Standard deviation of cluster sizes. High values indicate uneven distribution of particles across clusters.
**Cluster Energy (pT) Features**

`max_cluster_pt`: The highest transverse momentum among all clusters. Identifies the most energetic subjet.
`mean_cluster_pt`: Average transverse momentum of clusters. Indicates typical cluster energy.
`std_cluster_pt`: Standard deviation of cluster pT. High values suggest significant energy imbalance between clusters.

**Spatial Distribution Features**
`max_cluster_eta`: Maximum absolute pseudorapidity (¦Ç) of any cluster. Indicates how far clusters spread in the ¦Ç direction.
`max_cluster_phi`: Maximum absolute azimuthal angle (¦Õ) of any cluster. Shows angular spread in the ¦Õ direction.
`mean_cluster_eta`: Average absolute ¦Ç of clusters. Indicates typical ¦Ç spread of the jet's substructure.
`mean_cluster_phi`: Average absolute ¦Õ of clusters. Shows typical angular spread of the jet's substructure.

**Cluster Hierarchy Features**
`cluster_pt_ratio`: Ratio of highest to second-highest cluster pT. Values close to 1 indicate balanced energy distribution, while higher values suggest a dominant subjet.
`cluster_size_ratio`: Ratio of largest to second-largest cluster size. Similar to pT ratio but based on particle count rather than energy.",False,True
