_Santa has tree toys, tiny tree toys,_ <br>
_To mail from shore to shore._ <br>
_He needs the smallest box, indeed a square box,_<br>
_To fit them all inside,_<br>
_So he can mail these stocking stuffers_<br>
_On his big long Christmas ride!_ <br>

_Here comes the problem, here comes the problem._<br>
_We need the smallest size!_<br>
_For one to two hundred trees in shipments,_<br>
_We need your expert eyes._<br> 
_Can you find the best solution to help us pack_<br> 
_All the tiny trees inside?_<br>
_We must find the optimal packing to help Santa Claus_<br> 
_And win a prize!_<br>


**The Challenge**

In this re-defined optimization problem, help Santa fit Christmas tree toys into the smallest (2-dimension) parcel size possible so that he can efficiently mail these stocking stuffers around the globe. Santa needs the dimensions of the smallest possible square box that fits shipments of between 1-200 trees. 

**The Goal**

Find the optimal packing solution to help Santa this season and win Rudolph's attention by being one of the first to post your solution! 

Happy packing!  
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on sum of the normalized area of the square bounding box for each puzzle. For each `n`-tree configuration, the side `s` of square box bounding the trees is squared and divided by the total number `n` of trees in the configuration. The final score is the sum of all configurations. Refer to the [metric notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/metric/santa-2025-metric) for exact implementation details.

$$ \text{score} = \sum_{n=1}^{N} \frac{s_{n}^2}{n}$$

## Submission File
For each `id` in the submission (representing a single tree in a `n`-tree configuration), you must report the tree position given by `x`, `y`, and the rotation given by `deg`. To avoid loss of precision when saving and reading the files, the values must be converted to a string and prepended with an `s` before submission. Submissions with any overlapping trees will throw an error. To avoid extreme leaderboard scores, location values must be constrained to \\(-100 \le x, y \le 100\\).

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,x,y,deg
    001_0,s0.0,s0.0,s20.411299
    002_0,s0.0,s0.0,s20.411299
    002_1,s-0.541068,s0.259317,s51.66348
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **November 17, 2025** - Start Date.

* **January 23, 2026** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.

* **January 23, 2026** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.

* **January 30, 2026** - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

- First Prize: $12,000
- Second Prize: $10,000
- Third Prize: $10,000
- Fourth Prize: $8,000

**Rudolph Prize** - $10,000: Awarded to the team holding 1st place on the leaderboard for the longest period of time between November 17, 2025 12:00 AM UTC and January 30, 2026 11:59 PM UTC. In the event the competition needs to be restarted, the Rudolph Prize dates shall be the new start and deadline of the competition.

As a condition to being awarded a Prize, a Prize winner must provide a detailed write-up on their solution in the competition forums within 14 days of the conclusion of the competition.",,True,True
10318,colombian-ai-olympiad-2025-pr-1-heart-attack,"# Overview

 **Heart Disease Risk Prediction**

---

**Context**
Heart disease is the leading cause of death worldwide, and early detection of risk factors is crucial to saving lives. This synthetic (yet realistic) dataset simulates medical and lifestyle characteristics of patients, with the goal of predicting whether an individual has a **high risk (1)** or **low risk (0)** of developing heart disease within the next 5 years.

The dataset was programmatically generated in Python, where variable distributions depend on the *target* to replicate causal relationships observed in real medical studies. 

---

**Objective:** Use the data in `train.csv` to train a model capable of generating predictions for the data in `test.csv`. These predictions will be evaluated using the **F1-score**.

**Challenges for Competitors**

* **Complex interactions:** Risk does not depend on a single variable, but on combinations (e.g., smoker + gender + age).
* **Ordinal categorical variables:** Require proper encoding (e.g., `stress_level`).
* **Noise:** The distributions overlap, preventing perfect linear separation.

---

**Evaluation**

* *Main metric:* **F1 Score**

The **F1 Score** measures a model¡¯s performance by balancing two key aspects:

1. **Precision:** The proportion of correctly predicted positive cases (avoiding false alarms).
   `Precision = True Positives / (True Positives + False Positives)`

2. **Recall (Sensitivity):** The proportion of actual positive cases correctly identified (avoiding missed risks).
   `Recall = True Positives / (True Positives + False Negatives)`

**F1 Score formula:**
F1 = 2 ¡Á (Precision ¡Á Recall) / (Precision + Recall)

**Why is it important?**

* **Contexts with class imbalance** (e.g., 60% ¡°low risk¡±, 40% ¡°high risk¡± in your dataset).
* **Problems where both types of errors matter:**

  * *False positives:* Classifying healthy people as at-risk (unnecessary stress/treatment).
  * *False negatives:* Failing to detect those truly at risk (serious consequences).

**In this competition**, a good F1 Score means your model:
? Detects most high-risk cases (high Recall).
? Minimizes wrong diagnoses (high Precision).

This metric seeks a practical balance between prediction quality and coverage.

---

**Files**

1. `train.csv`: Training dataset (3,500 records).
2. `test.csv`: Dataset for generating predictions (1,500 records).
3. `sample_submission.csv`: Required submission format.


* The submission file must contain **exactly** the following columns: `id` and `heart_disease_risk`.
* Example: (see the sample_submission.csv as well)

  ```
  id,heart_disease_risk
  ID_35001, 1
  ID_35002, 0
  ...
  ```

---

# **Limits:**

* Maximum of **10** submissions per day.
* **Deadline:** December 7, 2025
---

**Acknowledgements**
*Dataset generated by Emerson Le¨®n ¨C Universidad Antonio Nari?o for educational purposes. Inspired by findings from the Framingham Heart Study.*",,False,True
10319,ll-ms-minds-machines,"# Overview

Hackathon pr¨¢ctica de LLMs en la ESEI-Universidade de Vigo donde cada participante construye su propio nanoGPT, lo entrena con un dataset elegido o curado, y compite maximizando una m¨¦trica compuesta 1¨C100 que premia calidad, eficiencia, regularizaci¨®n y frugalidad de datos. El evento favorece buenas pr¨¢cticas de entrenamiento reproducible, medici¨®n rigurosa y comparabilidad entre runs con una evaluaci¨®n autom¨¢tica tipo ¡°Kaggle metric¡± y leaderboard local.?

----------------------
# Description

Cada alumno implementa y entrena un nanoGPT minimalista con un pipeline claro (datos, tokens, entrenamiento, evaluaci¨®n) y registra sus runs en una hoja/tablero con m¨¦tricas estandarizadas. Se aceptan optimizaciones de arquitectura, regularizaci¨®n, curr¨ªculos de datos y trucos de rendimiento, siempre que se reporten los valores requeridos por la m¨¦trica y se pueda replicar el mejor run enviado.?
La din¨¢mica incluye una sesi¨®n de arranque, mentoring t¨¦cnico y cierre con anuncio del leaderboard, en el campus de la Escola Superior de Enxe?ar¨ªa Inform¨¢tica, con horario y log¨ªstica publicados en los canales de GDG Ourense/ESEI.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La puntuaci¨®n final es un ¨²nico n¨²mero entre 1 y 100 obtenido como el m¨¢ximo entre todas las filas de tu submission, es decir, se toma tu mejor run registrado en el CSV. Cada run aporta las siguientes columnas num¨¦ricas obligatorias: val_perplexity, num_parameters, tokens_per_sec, training_time_minutes, val_minus_train, total_tokens_processed. Cualquier columna faltante, no num¨¦rica o con valores no positivos (salvo val_minus_train, que debe ser ¡Ý 0) invalida la submission con un error visible para participantes.?
La m¨¦trica compuesta suma 4 componentes min¨Cmax normalizados con anclas ¡°good/bad¡± opcionales que pueden definirse en una fila de solution, o se usan valores por defecto robustos si faltan. Los pesos tambi¨¦n son ajustables y se renormalizan a 1 si est¨¢n presentes, con defaults w_quality=0.6, w_efficiency=0.2, w_regularization=0.1, w_data=0.1.?

Calidad (quality): se incentiva perplexity de validaci¨®n baja; se normaliza entre p_bad y p_good, y se recorta a. La perplexity es una medida est¨¢ndar basada en log-likelihood promedio por token y su exponente, ampliamente usada para LMs.?

Eficiencia (efficiency): promedio de tres subcomponentes normalizados y recortados a : menos par¨¢metros (num_parameters), m¨¢s velocidad de generaci¨®n/entrenamiento (tokens_per_sec) y menor tiempo total de entrenamiento (training_time_minutes).?

Regularizaci¨®n (regularization): gap de generalizaci¨®n bajo, medido como val_minus_train; se normaliza entre vtgap_bad y vtgap_good, favoreciendo menores gaps.?

Frugalidad de datos (data_eff): menos tokens totales procesados (total_tokens_processed) medidos en escala logar¨ªtmica y normalizados entre tokens_bad y tokens_good; promueve aprender m¨¢s con menos.","# Dataset Description

TinyShakespeare es un corpus de ~40.000 l¨ªneas de obras de William Shakespeare en texto plano, popularizado por los tutoriales de modelos de lenguaje a nivel car¨¢cter de Andrej Karpathy; su tama?o total ronda 1¨C1.1 MB, lo que permite entrenar prototipos de nanoGPT r¨¢pidamente en CPU/GPU modestas. Suele distribuirse como un ¨²nico archivo input.txt con el texto completo, y muchas implementaciones lo exponen en tres ¡°splits¡± simb¨®licos (train/validation/test) con una ¨²nica entrada por split que contiene todo el texto.?

Contenido y formato?
El archivo incluye di¨¢logos y acotaciones de m¨²ltiples obras, con nombres de personajes seguidos de sus l¨ªneas y saltos de l¨ªnea que preservan estructura teatral; un ejemplo inicial es ¡°First Citizen:\nBefore we proceed any further, hear me ¡­¡±. El dataset se entrega como una columna ¡°text¡± de tipo string, sin etiquetas adicionales, pensado para modelado autoregresivo de siguiente car¨¢cter o siguiente token tras tokenizaci¨®n.?

Tareas recomendadas?

Modelado a nivel car¨¢cter: dividir el texto en secuencias y entrenar a predecir el siguiente car¨¢cter, lo que simplifica vocabulario y facilita prototipos educativos.?

Modelado a nivel subpalabra/palabra: aplicar un tokenizador BPE o similar para entrenar un GPT peque?o con bloques de contexto fijos y evaluaci¨®n por perplexity en validaci¨®n.?

Tama?o y splits?
El tama?o de descarga y del dataset es aproximadamente 1.06¨C1.11 MB, con un total ocupado en disco cercano a 2.23 MB en algunos empaquetados. En cat¨¢logos comunes se ofrece con tres splits (train/validation/test), cada uno con una ¨²nica ¡°ejemplo¡± que contiene todo el texto, pr¨¢ctica que facilita pipelines simples de corte en ventanas.?

Ventajas para la hackathon?

Ciclos r¨¢pidos: su tama?o peque?o permite iterar hiperpar¨¢metros, arquitecturas y regularizaci¨®n con entrenos de minutos, ideal para comparar runs en un leaderboard.?

Reproducibilidad: al ser un est¨¢ndar de facto en demos de nanoGPT/char-rnn, existen scripts y repositorios de referencia que facilitan baselines y comparaciones justas.?

Limitaciones y buenas pr¨¢cticas?

Escala y diversidad: el corpus es peque?o y estil¨ªsticamente homog¨¦neo (ingl¨¦s isabelino), por lo que conviene evitar conclusiones generales sobre escalado o capacidades fuera del dominio.?

Evaluaci¨®n: usar un split de validaci¨®n consistente y registrar la perplexity de validaci¨®n y el gap val?train para medir generalizaci¨®n; documentar c¨®mo se cuentan tokens si se emplea tokenizaci¨®n subpalabra.?",False,True
