10331,spr-2026-mammography-report-classification,"# Overview

The [Radiology Society of S?o Paulo (SPR - Sociedade Paulista de Radiologia)](http://www.spr.org.br) is organizing an Artificial Intelligence Challenge for Breast Cancer. All healthcare professionals and data scientists worldwide are invited to participate.

The challenge is to create a solution that accurately predicts the BI-RADS Category from the findings section of mammography reports. The mammography report usually contains three sections: indication (reason the exam was performed), findings (description of the images read by radiologists), and impression (the BI-RADS is stated in this section). The dataset will not contain the impression, as it defines the target. To support this initiative, SPR has partnered with institutions that have donated thousands of mammography reports, enabling participants to develop and test artificial intelligence algorithms to enhance breast cancer care.

This initiative aims to advance AI-driven mammography analysis, foster scientific and educational activities in the Radiology and Diagnostic Imaging community, and encourage research on imaging biomarkers.

The top three winners of the competition will be acknowledged during Jornada Paulista de Radiologia (JPR) 2026. All participants are invited to [register ](https://www.jpr.org.br/) and participate in JPR.

## About SPR

For 57 years, SPR has promoted scientific updating and continuing education within the highest principles of professionalism.

With an eye on the future, the organization is often looking to bring dynamism to its work and great prominence to everyone in its community, aiming to be well-recognized nationally and intentionally.

SPR is responsible for the S?o Paulo Radiological Meeting (JPR), its most significant event, and is also considered the largest congress of the specialty in Latin America.

JPR2026 will take place in the Transamerica Expo Center in S?o Paulo - SP, on April 30-May 3.




----------------------
# Description

# Description

Mammography is one of the most effective tools for the early detection of breast cancer. Radiologists describe mammographic findings in structured narrative reports, and assign a BI-RADS? (Breast Imaging Reporting and Data System) category to indicate the level of suspicion for malignancy and the recommended clinical management.

In this competition, you will develop models that predict the BI-RADS classification given the mammography report text, excluding the impression/conclusion section. The goal is to teach models to infer the underlying diagnostic reasoning from the descriptive content of the report ¡ª simulating a system that can classify cases based on textual findings alone.

# What is BI-RADS?

The Breast Imaging Reporting and Data System (BI-RADS) was developed by the American College of Radiology (ACR) to standardize mammography reporting and reduce ambiguity between radiologists and referring clinicians. The BI-RADS category reflects both imaging appearance and clinical context, ranging from negative exams to confirmed malignancies.

The standard BI-RADS categories are:
| Category | Description                                     | Typical Management                 |
| -------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| **0**    | Incomplete ¨C Need additional imaging evaluation | Recall for additional imaging      |
| **1**    | Negative                                        | Routine screening                  |
| **2**    | Benign finding(s)                               | Routine screening                  |
| **3**    | Probably benign                                 | Short-interval follow-up suggested |
| **4**    | Suspicious abnormality                          | Consider biopsy                    |
| **5**    | Highly suggestive of malignancy                 | Appropriate action should be taken |
| **6**    | Known biopsy-proven malignancy                  | Surgical or oncologic treatment    |

Reference: [https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Reporting-and-Data-Systems/BI-RADS](url)

# The Challenge

Your task is to predict the BI-RADS category for each mammogram exam using only the text of the radiology report (excluding the impression section).

Each report includes descriptive findings such as breast composition, presence of calcifications or masses, asymmetries, and associated features. The impression or assessment section ¡ª which explicitly contains the BI-RADS category ¡ª has been removed to ensure the model must reason from the findings themselves.

Participants will receive a training set of reports with their corresponding BI-RADS labels and a test set without labels.
Your model should output a predicted BI-RADS category for each test report.

# Why This Matters

Predicting BI-RADS from descriptive findings is a step toward developing decision-support systems that can:
- Assist radiologists in maintaining consistency across reports
- Enable automated quality control of breast imaging reports
- Provide educational feedback for trainees
- Support large-scale research in breast imaging and natural language processing (NLP)







----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [F1-score with average='macro'](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html) between the predicted class and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the `target` variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,target
    1,6
    2,4
    3,1
    4,2
    etc.

The target is the BI-RADS Category (possible values range from 0 to 6)
----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: $3,000.00.
- **1st Place**?-?$1,500.00.
- **2nd Place** - $1,00.00.
- **3rd Place** - $500.00.
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met: 
- CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time 
- GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time 
- Internet access disabled 

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.
