10338,emotion-classification-challenge,"# Overview

Understanding emotions expressed in customer feedback is critical for companies to tailor responses and improve service quality. You have to develop an efficient and explainable NLP system that can accurately interpret the emotional tone of short text messages.

You will work with the Emotion dataset, which contains short text sentences labeled with diverse emotion classes including joy, anger, sadness and more. Your goal is to build models that not only predict these emotions accurately but also provide interpretable explanations regarding how they arrive at their decisions.

To simulate real-world constraints, your solutions must be optimized to run within the resources available, ensuring practicality for deployment in production environments.

You are encouraged to explore approaches that carefully balance predictive performance, model interpretability, and resource efficiency, including methods for handling class imbalance and minimizing inference latency.
----------------------
# Description

The dataset consists of the following columns:

- **Text**: A short text sentence expressing an emotion (e.g., customer feedback or message).
- **Emotion**: The emotion label associated with the text. The possible classes are:
  - anger
  - fear
  - joy
  - love
  - sadness
  - surprise
  - thankfulness
  - neutral


### Output Format

Your submission file should be a CSV containing:

| Column  | Description                                      |
|---------|------------------------------------------------|
| ID      | Unique identifier for each test data sample     |
| Predicted | Predicted emotion label from the set of classes |
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on accuracy, which measures the proportion of correctly predicted emotion labels out of all test samples.","# Dataset Description

### Content
List of documents with emotion flag, Dataset is split into train, test & validation for building the machine learning model

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **val.csv** - the validation set
*   **test.csv** - the test set

## Columns
*   **id** - unique identifier of the data sample
*   **text** - text field containing the message/text
*   **sentiment** - text field descirbing the type/category of the emotion",False,True
10339,fake-news-detection-projectx,"# Overview

In the year 2027, misinformation has reached epidemic levels across India¡¯s digital platforms. From viral political rumors to fake health advisories, millions of posts spread daily, blurring the line between fact and fiction.

The Ministry of Digital Trust (MDT) has launched Project *TruthLens*, a national challenge to develop an AI system capable of identifying fake news from real ones, based solely on textual content, even when the data is messy, noisy, and incomplete.

As a data scientist on the TruthLens team, your task is to build a model that predicts whether a given article or social media post is Fake or Real.

*But beware, the data you¡¯ve received has been scraped from multiple sources.*

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the **Weighted F1-Score** between the predicted and actual labels.

---
The F1-Score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, effectively balancing both false positives and false negatives.

$$
F1 = 2 \times \frac{(\text{Precision} \times \text{Recall})}{(\text{Precision} + \text{Recall})}
$$","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set

## Columns

*   `id` - Unique identifier for each record in the dataset.
*   `title`?- The headline or title of the article/post.
*   `text` - The full body content of the article or post.
*   `subject` - The main topic or category of the news
*   `date` - The published or posted date of the article
*   `type` - Target variable (0=True, 1=Fake)

## Output Format

* `id` - Unique identifier from test.csv
* `type` - Predicted output of news **(0 or 1)**",False,True
10340,poker-hand-classification-challenge,"# Overview

# Poker Hand Classification Challenge

### Overview
Each record represents a 5-card poker hand described by 10 integers ¡ª 5 suits (1¨C4) and 5 ranks (1¨C13).  
Your task: classify each hand into one of 10 poker categories, from ""Nothing in hand"" to ""Royal Flush.""

---

### Goal
Build a model that accurately predicts the poker hand type, despite a massively imbalanced dataset.

---

### Class Distribution

| Class | Hand Type         | Train | Test |
|:-----:|:------------------|------:|----:|
| 0 | Nothing in hand | 12,493 | 501,209 |
| 1 | One pair | 10,599 | 422,498 |
| 2 | Two pairs | 1,206 | 47,622 |
| 3 | Three of a kind | 513 | 21,121 |
| 4 | Straight | 93 | 3,885 |
| 5 | Flush | 54 | 1,996 |
| 6 | Full house | 36 | 1,424 |
| 7 | Four of a kind | 6 | 230 |
| 8 | Straight flush | 5 | 12 |
| 9 | Royal flush | 5 | 3 |

---

### Data Format
- **Features:**  
  `S1¨CS5` ¡ú suits (1¨C4)  
  `R1¨CR5` ¡ú ranks (1¨C13)  
- **Target:** `class` ¡Ê {0¨C9}

---

### Evaluation
Submissions are evaluated using **Accuracy** on the test set.

---

### Tips
- Handle class imbalance with resampling or class weights.  
- Try tree-based models or neural networks.  
- Engineer features (e.g., count of ranks/suits, sequential ranks).

---

### Objective
Detect every hand type ¡ª even the rare Royal Flushes ¡ª and build the ultimate poker-hand predictor.

----------------------
# Description

Welcome to the Poker Hand Classification Challenge, where your mission isn¡¯t to play poker ¡ª it¡¯s to teach machines how to win at it. Each record in this dataset represents a 5-card poker hand, described by the suit and rank of every card. Your task? Build a model that can look at those 10 numbers and instantly recognize what kind of hand it is ¡ª from a simple ¡°Nothing in hand¡± to the elusive Royal Flush.

Sounds easy? Think again. Just like in real poker, the rare hands are incredibly hard to find ¡ª the dataset is massively imbalanced, with millions of ¡°Nothing¡± hands and just a few lucky Straight Flushes and Royal Flushes sprinkled in. Your model will need to spot patterns in chaos, deal with rare events, and still keep its accuracy sky-high.

The dataset comes with 25,010 training samples and a massive 1,000,000-record test set. Here¡¯s the breakdown of how often each hand appears:

Class 0 (Nothing in hand): 12,493 train / 501,209 test

Class 1 (One pair): 10,599 train / 422,498 test

Class 2 (Two pairs): 1,206 train / 47,622 test

Class 3 (Three of a kind): 513 train / 21,121 test

Class 4 (Straight): 93 train / 3,885 test

Class 5 (Flush): 54 train / 1,996 test

Class 6 (Full house): 36 train / 1,424 test

Class 7 (Four of a kind): 6 train / 230 test

Class 8 (Straight flush): 5 train / 12 test

Class 9 (Royal flush): 5 train / 3 test

That¡¯s right ¡ª out of a million test hands, only three are Royal Flushes! Your model will need to handle everything from common junk hands to those nearly mythical combinations.

So, shuffle your features, deal out your training data, and build the best poker-hand predictor you can.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the accuracy score of the submitted model.",,False,True
10341,sentence-order-prediction,"# Overview

**Task:** Predict whether a pair of sentences are in the correct order or reversed.
**Type:** Binary classification
**Evaluation Metric:** Accuracy
**Submission Format:**
`submission.csv` ¡ú columns: `id`, `predicted_label`
**Goal:** Build a model that understands sentence flow and coherence.
----------------------
# Description

Every great story has a beginning, a middle, and an end ¡ª but what happens when it¡¯s all scrambled?
In **SentenceOrder++**, you¡¯ll train a model to determine if two consecutive sentences appear in the right order (`1`) or have been swapped (`0`).

This challenge tests your model¡¯s ability to reason about **contextual continuity**, **semantic flow**, and **temporal logic** in natural language.

#### ?? What You¡¯ll Get

* `sentenceorder_train.csv`: columns ¡ú `sentence_1`, `sentence_2`, `label`
* `sentenceorder_test.csv`: columns ¡ú `sentence_1`, `sentence_2`
* `sentenceorder_sample_submission.csv`: submission example
----------------------
# Evaluation

Accuracy:

>Accuracy= Correct Predictions/Total Samples

#### ?? Tips

* Start with **TF-IDF + Logistic Regression**.
* Experiment with **BERT**, **RoBERTa**, or **Sentence-BERT** embeddings.
* Semantic similarity + coherence scoring works surprisingly well.",,False,True
10342,sensor-swap-find-the-faulty-bot,"# Overview

**Task:** Detect and classify sensor faults in robotic arm readings.
**Type:** Multi-class classification + auxiliary prediction
**Evaluation Metric:** Custom scoring (fault-type + sensors)
**Submission Format:**
`submission.csv` ¡ú columns: `window_id`, `fault_type`, `sensor_1`, `sensor_2`
**Goal:** Identify faulty sensors and their type of fault from time-series readings.
----------------------
# Description

Our robotic arm has attitude problems ¡ª sometimes its sensors swap signals, drift, or just malfunction randomly.
Your challenge in **SensorSwap++** is to detect the type of fault and locate which sensors are responsible.

Each test sample contains readings from 20 sensors over 24 timesteps.
You must predict:

* `fault_type` ¡Ê {`Swap`, `Glitch`, `None`}
* `sensor_1`, `sensor_2` (if applicable)

This task reflects **real-world robotics diagnostics**, where signal inconsistencies can cascade into catastrophic errors ¡ª unless you detect them in time.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Each window is scored as:

* +1.0 for correct `fault_type`
* +0.5 for correct `sensor_1`
* +0.5 for correct `sensor_2` (only for `Swap`)

Final score = average across all test windows.
Higher is better.",,False,True
10343,power-pulse-predict-the-pulse-of-power,"# Overview

**Task:** Predict future power consumption (12 hours) based on past readings (84 hours).
**Type:** Multivariate time series forecasting
**Evaluation Metric:** SMAPE (Symmetric Mean Absolute Percentage Error)
**Submission Format:**
`submission.csv` ¡ú columns: `id`, `t_0`, `t_1`, ¡­, `t_83`
**Goal:** Accurately forecast the next 12 hours of energy usage.
----------------------
# Description

Power grids fluctuate like living systems ¡ª steady, spiky, and sometimes unpredictable.
Your mission in **PowerPulse++** is to predict upcoming power consumption from historical data.
Each sample is a sequence of 84 hourly readings, and you must forecast the next 12.

Think of it as teaching your model to **feel the rhythm of the grid** ¡ª forecasting trends, patterns, and shifts that mirror real-world behavior.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**SMAPE (lower is better):**


![https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/how-to-calculate-smape-in-python/]



#### ?? Tips

* Try **GRU**, **LSTM**, or **Temporal CNN** architectures.
* Classical baselines like **ARIMA** and **Prophet** are strong too.
* Visualize data to detect periodic or seasonal behaviors.",,False,True
10344,ecuador-infant-malnutrition-risk-prediction-mcd,"# Overview

This competition challenges participants to develop a machine learning model that predicts whether a child under 2 years old is at risk of malnutrition.  

The dataset is synthetic but designed to reflect realistic health and demographic patterns from Ecuadorian regions.  

Your goal is to build an accurate and reliable classification model.
----------------------
# Description

Malnutrition in early childhood can have serious long-term effects on growth and development.  
In this task, you will analyze and model data related to infants¡¯ weight, height, age, sex, and socioeconomic background to predict a binary outcome:

- `1` ¡ú child at risk of malnutrition  
- `0` ¡ú child not at risk  

The dataset contains missing values, outliers, and some inconsistent entries to simulate real-world data challenges. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using **Accuracy**.  
The formula is:

$$ Accuracy = \frac{Correct Predictions}{Total Predictions} $$

Each submission must include predictions for all rows in `X_test.csv`, formatted exactly as in `sample_submission.csv`.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains synthetic records of children under 2 years old in Ecuador.  
It is designed to resemble real-world public health data, including demographic, anthropometric, and socioeconomic features.

### Features
- `edad_meses` ¡ú Age of the child in months (can include some inconsistencies)
- `sexo` ¡ú Sex of the child (`M` or `F`)
- `region` ¡ú Region in Ecuador (`Costa`, `Sierra`, `Amazon¨ªa`, `Insular`)
- `nivel_educacion_madre` ¡ú Mother's education level (`Sin escolaridad`, `Primaria`, `Secundaria`, `Superior`)
- `peso_kg` ¡ú Weight of the child in kilograms (includes outliers and missing values)
- `talla_cm` ¡ú Height of the child in centimeters (includes outliers and missing values)
- `alerta_desnutricion` ¡ú Target variable (1 if at risk of malnutrition, 0 otherwise)",False,True
