10347,dat-a-thon-group-2,"# Overview

Welcome to the CampusPulse Data-thon

Welcome to the CampusPulse Relationship Prediction challenge, hosted by IIT Jammu! This competition is designed to test your skills not just in modeling, but in your entire data science workflow¡ªfrom exploration and cleaning to building a high-performance predictive model.","# Dataset Description

This page appears alongside the data files. It describes what files have been provided and the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitions, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.

Participants should be able to answer these types of questions after reading the data description:

What files do I need?

What should I expect the data format to be?

What am I predicting?

What acronyms will I encounter?

Files

train.csv - The training set. This file contains all student attributes, including the target variable, romantic.

test.csv - The test set. This file contains all student attributes except the target variable. Your task is to predict the romantic status for each id in this file.

sample_submission.csv - A sample submission file in the correct format. It shows the required two-column format: id and romantic.

Columns

This dataset contains demographic, social, and academic information for a set of students.

id - A unique identifier for each student. This is used for matching your predictions.

school - Student's school (binary: 'GP' - Gabriel Pereira or 'MS' - Mousinho da Silveira)

sex - Student's sex (binary: 'F' - female or 'M' - male)

address - Student's home address type (binary: 'U' - urban or 'R' - rural)

famsize - Family size (binary: 'LE3' - less or equal to 3 or 'GT3' - greater than 3)

Pstatus - Parent's cohabitation status (binary: 'T' - living together or 'A' - apart)

Medu - Mother's education (numeric: 0 - none, 1 - primary (4th grade), 2 ¨C 5th to 9th grade, 3 ¨C secondary or 4 ¨C higher education)

Fedu - Father's education (numeric: 0 - none, 1 - primary (4th grade), 2 ¨C 5th to 9th grade, 3 ¨C secondary or 4 ¨C higher education)

Mjob - Mother's job (nominal: 'teacher', 'health' care related, civil 'services', 'at_home' or 'other')

Fjob - Father's job (nominal: 'teacher', 'health' care related, civil 'services', 'at_home' or 'other')

reason - Reason to choose this school (nominal: close to 'home', school 'reputation', 'course' preference or 'other')

guardian - Student's guardian (nominal: 'mother', 'father' or 'other')

traveltime - Home to school travel time (numeric: 1 - <15 min., 2 - 15 to 30 min., 3 - 30 min. to 1 hour, or 4 - >1 hour)

failures - Number of past class failures (numeric)

schoolsup - Extra educational support (binary: yes or no)

famsup - Family educational support (binary: yes or no)

paid - Extra paid classes within the course subject (binary: yes or no)

activities - Extra-curricular activities (binary: yes or no)

nursery - Attended nursery school (binary: yes or no)

higher - Wants to take higher education (binary: yes or no)

internet - Internet access at home (binary: yes or no)

famrel - Quality of family relationships (numeric: from 1 - very bad to 5 - excellent)

freetime - Free time after school (numeric: from 1 - very low to 5 - very high)

goout - Going out with friends (numeric: from 1 - very low to 5 - very high)

Dalc - Workday alcohol consumption (numeric: from 1 - very low to 5 - very high)

health - Current health status (numeric: from 1 - very bad to 5 - very good)

absences - Number of school absences (numeric)

G1 - First period grade (numeric)

G2 - Second period grade (numeric)

G3 - Final grade (numeric)

Feature_1 - Anonymized Feature 1.

Feature_2 - Anonymized Feature 2.

Feature_3 - Anonymized Feature 3.

romantic - (Target Variable) Whether the student is in a romantic relationship (binary: 'yes' or 'no'). This is the column you must predict.",False,True
10348,dat-a-thon-group-1,"# Overview

Welcome to the IIT Jammu Real Estate Challenge

Welcome to the IIT Jammu Junior ML Dat-a-thon! This challenge is designed to test your intuition, your analytical skills, and your ability to build a high-quality regression model from the ground up.


----------------------
# Description

The Goal

Can you analyze a property's features to accurately estimate its market value?

Your goal is to build a regression model to predict the sale price of a house. The target column you are predicting is Price_in_Lakhs, which is a continuous numerical value.

The Challenge

This is a classic and highly practical machine learning problem. Your task is to perform a complete data science workflow:

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA): Understand the features. Which ones are most correlated with price? How are features like Location or Num_Bedrooms distributed?

Feature Engineering: Create new, predictive features from the data provided. For example, does Area_sq_ft per bedroom matter?

Modeling: Build and train a regression model (like Linear Regression, Decision Trees, Gradient Boosting, etc.) to minimize the error of your price predictions.

How to Get Started

Participation: This is an individual competition.

Explore the Data: Download train.csv and test.csv. Check the Data tab for a full description of all columns.

Build Your Model: Start your .ipynb notebook.

Submit Your Predictions: When you're ready, submit your predictions for test.csv. Your submission must be a .csv file with two columns: ID and Price_in_Lakhs. The sample_submission.csv file shows the exact format required.

Final Ranking & Deliverables

Your ranking on the Public Leaderboard is for your reference (scored on a portion of the test data). Your final, official score will be calculated on the Private Leaderboard (scored on the rest of the test data), which will be revealed only after the competition deadline.

To be eligible for final rankings and prizes, you must submit two items:

Kaggle Submission: Your prediction .csv file submitted to the Kaggle leaderboard.

Code Notebook (.ipynb): You must also submit your final Jupyter Notebook, named with your name, to the official submission link (to be provided by organizers). Your final rank is conditional on submitting a working notebook that can reproduce your leaderboard score.

Good luck!","# Dataset Description

Data Description

This dataset contains housing data for a real estate challenge. The goal is to predict the price of a house based on its features.

Files

train.csv - The training set, containing all features and the target variable, price.

test.csv - The test set, containing all features except the target variable. Your task is to predict the price for these properties.

sample_submission.csv - A sample submission file in the correct format. It must contain an id and your predicted price.

Columns

id - (Generated) A unique identifier for each property.

price - (Target Variable) The sale price of the house in raw currency (e..g, 13300000).

area - The total area of the property in square feet.

bedrooms - The number of bedrooms in the house.

bathrooms - The number of bathrooms in the house.

stories - The number of stories (floors) in the house.

mainroad - (Binary) 1 if the house is connected to the main road, 0 otherwise.

guestroom - (Binary) 1 if the house has a guest room, 0 otherwise.

basement - (Binary) 1 if the house has a basement, 0 otherwise.

hotwaterheating - (Binary) 1 if the house has hot water heating, 0 otherwise.

airconditioning - (Binary) 1 if the house has air conditioning, 0 otherwise.

parking - The number of parking spots available.

prefarea - (Binary) 1 if the house is located in a preferred area, 0 otherwise.

furnishingstatus - (Categorical) The furnishing status of the house.",False,True
10349,ag-ml-leipzig-2025-future-crop,"# Overview

**Can we learn from the recent past to predict climate impacts in the future?**

Machine learning models are frequently trained on observed data from the last decades and then used to make projections of future climate change impacts. However, the ability of such models to generalise to these unseen conditions outside of the observed distribution is not guaranteed. How far into the future can we make good predictions? Which types of models or training methods do better or worse? Can domain generalisation strategies help, and if so, how much? We have created a benchmark dataset to help answer these questions, using simulated agricultural maize and wheat yields from biophysical crop models.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18516286%2F383a8e3a35350c73a9aff9139c94714d%2Fmainfig.png?generation=1718072981145491&alt=media)

Your mission is to predict end-of-season wheat and maize yields for each grid cell and year, using the daily weather experienced during the growing season (and just before), along with the soil texture and ambient CO<sub>2</sub> level. The challenge is that you must predict yields in the future - from 2021 to 2100 - under a high-emissions climate change scenario, but will only receive training data from the last few decades (1980 - 2020).
----------------------
# Description

**You are going to predict the yield of wheat and maize under climate change conditions.** Projected mean growing-season radiation and temperature are increasing each decade, and precipitation patterns are changing. The CO<sub>2</sub> level is also rising, which can have a fertilisation effect on yields - but the effect of this depends on the crop¡¯s photosynthesis pathway, which is different for maize and wheat.

In reality, irrigation and fertilisation would also vary over time, adding complication. However, in these simulations, this doesn¡¯t need to be considered. We work only with water-limited yields, which means all grid cells are rainfed, and crops are fertilised at the same rate for all years, varying by country.

We have restricted the grid cells to areas which are heavily harvested (at least 2000 hectares), and we are providing 30 days of climate data before sowing at each grid cell and 210 days after to use as predictive features. The growing season will not necessarily last exactly 210 days. Crops are harvested when they reach maturity, but the maturity process is influenced by the effects of the weather, fertilization, CO<sub>2</sub> level and soil type.

We included below a few visualisations of the behaviour of the climate variables, so that these variabilities and distribution shifts become more clear.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F231819%2F3a3debe3949a2918f3dfe1db48be2fa1%2Fsrad.png?generation=1718051104190516&alt=media)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F231819%2Fd1ef93d2c43ddc04a2b8c1b34f704a6a%2Ftmean.png?generation=1718051120405214&alt=media)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F231819%2Fa5c40477b17e1cd48fdcbd127393b129%2Fprec.png?generation=1718051160937916&alt=media)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F231819%2F6608dc05a2d66a29240c5ba750701a79%2Fco2.png?generation=1717778155327128&alt=media)

We include some maps of the management forcing data (nitrogen fertilization rate and soil texture). These vary spatially, based on recent observation datasets, and are held constant over time for these simulations.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18516286%2F39cea96781f8d8bad03153f265cf0909%2Fnfertmaize.png?generation=1718070092795384&alt=media)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18516286%2F213d60bd034751553154057c287a59e4%2Fnfertwheat.png?generation=1718070116951250&alt=media)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18516286%2F097599884c394bf4a4cf853d2b841500%2Fsoiltexture.png?generation=1718070941712785&alt=media)

Finally, we are only using for training and testing pixels where >2000 hectares are harvested (as of the year 2000). To illustrate this, we provide an example map of these pixels, coloured according to the yields in the second year of the training set for each crop.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18516286%2F63f16b370de98bcf5aef2aaf222e9ee0%2Fmaizeyield.png?generation=1718071139199576&alt=media)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18516286%2F2b346e4b4dc703cec969f852106bfb52%2Fwheatyield.png?generation=1718071151914570&alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

Since we can't measure the actual values of crop yields in the future, we are using simulations obtained by a crop model that has been thoroughly studied and validated. This model was used to generate the yields of the training and test data, driven by the climate data that we have provided for you as predictor data.

## Metrics and leaderboards

Submissions are evaluated using the root mean squared error (RMSE) between the predicted yield and the true simulated yield from the crop model over all datapoints (unique combinations of grid cell, year and crop). A RMSE of 0 would mean a perfect emulator of the crop model.

During the competition, the RMSE as calculated on the validation set (years 2021-2050) will be used for the Kaggle leaderboard. On November 10th, the challenge will close and model scores on the final decades (2051 - 2100) will be shown.
----------------------
# Submission File

For each ID in the test set (which represents a unique combination of crop, gridcell and year), you must predict a yield. An example file is available to download from the Data section. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,yield
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.
----------------------
# Who are we?

We are members of the Agricultural Model Intercomparison and Improvement Project (AgMIP) Machine Learning team ([AgML](https://www.agml.org/)), which brings together crop modellers, machine learning experts and other relevant scientists and stakeholders to conduct intercomparison studies and create essential resources such as benchmark datasets. [Join our mailing list](https://mail.agml.org/mailman/listinfo/agml) to stay up-to-date on this and our other activities!","# Dataset Description

Input data is provided in the form of two-dimensional files such that each row corresponds to a unique combination of latitude, longitude, year and crop. We provide separate files for each climate variable (precipitation, shortwave radiation, minimum and maximum daily temperature and mean daily temperature), which contain 240 columns reflecting daily values for 30 days before sowing and 210 days after. An additional file contains the dominant USDA soil texture class (constant over time), the ambient CO<sub>2</sub> concentration (spatially constant), the planting date and the nitrogen application rate (constant over time). Each input datapoint therefore consists of a multivariate time series of shape 5x240, plus several auxiliary variables. The target variable is a single value (end-of-season crop yield in tons per hectare) per unique gridcell, year and crop combination.

All the data is in .parquet format, and the predictive features have been split across several files due to their size to make it easier to work with. Each row corresponds to a unique datapoint (one growing season at one 0.5 degree grid cell), with columns corresponding to different variables, and the column ID as a unique identifier.

The training set consists of the first 39 years, covering 1982 to 2020; this can be used for training your models. The resulting yields are provided in the files `train_solutions_maize.parquet` and `train_solutions_wheat.parquet`.

The test set is twice as big as the training set, covering the 78 years from 2021 to 2098. The first 30 of those years (2021-2050) will be used to measure your models' performance (using RMSE as scoring metric) on the public leaderboard during the challenge, and model performance on the rest of the years will be revealed after the challenge ends.

## Files

* Training data (1982-2020)
    - For each crop (maize and wheat)
        - Five files containing climate variables from 30 days before and 210 days after planting date
            + rsds - daily short-wave radiation
            + pr - daily precipitation
            + tas - daily mean temperature
            + tmax - daily maximum temperature
            + tmin - daily minimum temperature
        - One ¡°solutions¡± file containing the target variable (yield)
    - Soil
        - One file containing soil texture, real year, nitrogen fertilization rate and CO<sub>2</sub> concentration
* Test data (2021-2098)
    - For each crop (maize and wheat)
        - Five files containing climate variables from 30 days before and 210 days after planting date
            + rsds - daily short-wave radiation
            + pr - daily precipitation
            + tas - daily mean temperature
            + tmax - daily maximum temperature
            + tmin - daily minimum temperature
    - Soil
        - One file containing soil texture, real year, nitrogen fertilization rate and CO<sub>2</sub> concentration
* sample_submission.csv - an example of how your submitted predictions file should look.

**Important note:** To make it easier for participants to work with, we have split most of the data files between the two crops (maize and wheat). However, submission files should include predictions for **both** crops.

## Columns

All files contain an `ID` column, which is a unique ID for each datapoint (crop, gridcell and year). Apart from **train_crop_solutions.csv**, all other files also contain columns `lat`, `lon`, `year` and `crop`. Note that `year` is **not** the 'real year' but reflects the number of growing seasons since the start of the simulation run. However, the 'real year' can be found in the **soil_co2_*.parquet** files in the column `real_year`. In these files, `texture_class` contains the dominant USDA soil texture class (categorical variable coded as 1 to 13) and `co2` contains the ambient CO<sub>2</sub> concentration in parts per million. Finally, `nitrogen` describes the annual fertilization rate in tons per hectare per growing season.

Additional units: short-wave radiation [W m<sup>-2</sup>], precipitation [kg m<sup>-2</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>], temperatures [<sup>o</sup> C], yield [t ha<sup>-1</sup>]].

Because the dataset is very large, each climate variable is in a separate file to make it easier to work with - you might not want to use all of the variables at first. Each file contains 240 columns reflecting one daily value, from 30 days before sowing until 210 days after, as well as a column called `variable` with the name of the variable being measured (e.g. pr, tas or rsds), and columns describing the year, latitude, longitude and crop, as well as the unique `ID` to facilitate joining the datasets.",False,True
10350,k-us-artificial-intelligence-competition-2025,"# Overview

**Welcome to the KU Programming Competition 2025** 
This time featuring Kaggle Playground

**Your Goal:** The goal of this competition is to predict frequency given some features
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Getting Started
Please use the data in the kpc directory. **Please do not use the archive files!**
On the Kaggle page, you launch a new Jupypter Notebook, it might take a while to set up, as this creates container.
If you work with colab, you can link to there, see the File menu.
Once a notebook is created, you see a cell that should execute as is and show you the available data.

A natural next step is to do something like:

```
train = '/kaggle/input/k-us-artificial-intelligence-competition-2025/kpc/train.csv'
df = pd.read_csv(train)
df.head()
```

Submissions are evaluated using the [Root Mean Squared Error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_mean_square_deviation) between the predicted and the observed target.



## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict a continuous-valued `Frequency`. The csv file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,Frequency
    524164,119.5
    524165,127.42
    524166,111.11
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - ${competition.DateEnabled}
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  ${competition.Deadline}

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Dataset

The challenge uses fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `Frequency` is the continuous target ground truth
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the `Frequency` for each row
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10351,forecasting-the-future-of-urban-mobility-ai-model,"# Overview

Based on data about the number of trips and other relevant features, you need to build a model that can accurately predict the expected demand for bus rides in a future period.
----------------------
# Description

Every day, thousands of buses operate across Russia, transporting millions of passengers. Efficient public transport planning requires a clear understanding of **where and when** the demand for bus rides will be highest.

In this competition, your task is to develop a **model to forecast bus travel demand**. Using historical data from **2021¨C2022**, participants must predict the **demand for the following month**.

Accurate forecasts will help transportation companies to:

* estimate route loads in advance,
* optimize schedules and fleet sizes,
* prepare the necessary equipment and personnel.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the **Symmetric Mean Absolute Percentage Error (SMAPE)** between the predicted and actual demand values.

The SMAPE metric measures the accuracy of your forecasts as a percentage and is defined as:

$$
SMAPE = \frac{100%}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{|y_{pred,i} - y_{true,i}|}{(|y_{true,i}| + |y_{pred,i}|)/2}
$$

A **lower SMAPE** indicates a more accurate forecast.

---

## Submission File

For each date in the test set, you must predict the expected **bus demand (`target`)**.
The file must include a header and follow this format:

```
date,target
2021-03-25,0.0
2021-03-26,23.0
2021-03-27,567.0
2021-03-28,644.0
2021-03-29,1182.0
2021-03-30,1345.0
2021-03-31,2547.0
2021-04-01,2465.0
2021-04-02,2175.0
2021-04-03,2258.0
```

Each row should contain the **forecasted demand** for the corresponding **date** in the test dataset.","# Dataset Description

## Data Description

In this competition, participants are provided with a dataset of bus travel demand in Russia for 2021¨C2022. Using this data, you need to build a model to forecast the number of bus trips for the upcoming month.

---

## Files

* **train.csv** ¡ª training dataset containing historical bus demand. Use this file to train your forecasting models.
* **test.csv** ¡ª test dataset containing the dates for which predictions are required.
* **sample_submission.csv** ¡ª a sample submission file showing the correct format for predictions:

```
date,target
2021-03-25,0.0
2021-03-26,23.0
2021-03-27,567.0
...
```

---

## Columns

**train.csv**

* `date` ¡ª the date of the record (YYYY-MM-DD format)
* `target` ¡ª the observed number of bus trips on that date

**test.csv**

* `date` ¡ª the date for which the forecast is required

**sample_submission.csv**

* `date` ¡ª the date corresponding to the prediction
* `target` ¡ª the predicted number of bus trips for that date",False,True
10352,ai-hackaton-code-create-conquerrr,"# Overview

In many industries, predicting the direction of price changes is crucial for planning and decision-making.

In this competition, your task is to develop a model to predict whether the price will increase (1) or decrease (0) on the next day based on historical daily price data.

Accurate predictions will help:

Make better short-term decisions based on price trends

Reduce risk in sequential price predictions

Optimize operational planning
----------------------
# Description

## Description

In many industries, predicting the direction of price changes is crucial for planning and decision-making.

In this competition, your task is to develop a model to predict whether the price will **increase (`1`)** or **not (`0`)** on the next day based on historical daily price data.

Accurate predictions will help:

* Make better short-term decisions based on price trends
* Reduce risk in sequential price predictions
* Optimize operational planning

---
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the **Gini coefficient**, calculated as:

$$
\text{Gini} = 2 \cdot \text{AUC} - 1
$$

Higher Gini indicates better ranking of positive vs negative cases.

Participants should submit **predicted probabilities for the price increase (target = 1)**.

---

## Submission File

For each date in the test set, you must predict a probability for the **target** variable. The file must contain a header and follow this format:

```
date,target
2025-10-02,0
2025-10-03,0
2025-10-04,0
...
2025-10-31,0
```

Each row should contain the predicted probability that the price will **increase** on that date.

**File name:** `submission.csv`","# Dataset Description

This page describes the files provided for the competition and the format of each. Participants should be able to understand what files are needed, the data format, and what they are predicting.

---

## Files

* **train.csv** ¨C the training set containing historical daily prices and targets.
* **test.csv** ¨C the test set for which participants must predict the probability of price increase.
* **sample_submission.csv** ¨C a sample submission file in the correct format.

---

## Columns

* `date` ¨C the date of the observation in YYYY-MM-DD format.
* `price` ¨C the observed price on that date (used to calculate features).
* `target` ¨C the binary target variable indicating price direction:

  * `1` if the price increased the next day
  * `0` if the price did **not** increase the next day

Participants are predicting the **probability that `target = 1`** for each row in `test.csv`.",False,True
10353,klasifikasi-lab-5-linear-model,"# Overview

Kompetisi Kaggle Lab 5 ini menguji pemahaman mahasiswa mengenai Linear Model pada Data Science dan Machine Learning.

Tujuan dari kompetisi ini adalah untuk membangun model machine learning yang dapat memprediksi nilai dari Stage untuk setiap pasien yang terkena Sirosis. Ini adalah task klasifikasi, dengan nilai Stage adalah:
1: Sudah terjangkit Sirosis
0: Tidak terjangkit

Dataset: Anda akan menggunakan gaming_behaviour.csv yang berisi berbagai statistik seorang gamer.

Fitur (X): Kolom-kolom seperti Drug, Age, Sex, Ascites, etc akan menjadi fitur input Anda.

Target (y): Kolom yang harus Anda prediksi adalah Stage.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric that will be used in the leaderboard is F1 Score.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```md
ID,Stage
1,1
2,0
3,1
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

Sirosis adalah stadium akhir dari jaringan parut (fibrosis) pada hati yang disebabkan oleh berbagai bentuk penyakit dan kondisi hati, seperti hepatitis dan kecanduan alkohol kronis. Data berikut berisi informasi yang dikumpulkan dari uji coba Mayo Clinic pada sirosis bilier primer (PBC) hati yang dilakukan antara tahun 1974 dan 1984.

# Attribute Information

- **ID**: unique identifier
- **Drug**: type of drug. Value: D-penicillamine or placebo 
- **Age**: age in [years] 
- **Sex**: M (male) or F (female) 
- **Ascites**: presence of ascites. Value:  N (No) or Y (Yes) 
- **Hepatomegaly**: presence of hepatomegaly. Value:  N (No) or Y (Yes) 
- **Spiders**: presence of spiders. Value:  N (No) or Y (Yes) 
- **Edema**: presence of edema. Value:  N (no edema and no diuretic therapy for edema), S (edema present without diuretics, or edema resolved by diuretics), or Y (edema despite diuretic therapy) 
- **Bilirubin**: serum bilirubin in [mg/dl] 
- **Cholesterol**: serum cholesterol in [mg/dl] 
- **Albumin**: albumin in [gm/dl] 
- **Copper**: urine copper in [ug/day] 
- **Alk_Phos**: alkaline phosphatase in [U/liter] 
- **SGOT**: SGOT in [U/ml] 
- **Triglycerides**: triglicerides in [mg/dl] 
- **Platelets**: platelets per cubic [ml/1000] 
- **Prothrombin**: prothrombin time in seconds [s] 
- **Stage**: patients' liver stage. Value: 1: Cirrhosis, 0: not cirrhosis",False,True
10354,news-classification-challenge,"# Overview

The India News Headline Classification Challenge invites participants to develop machine learning and deep learning models that can automatically identify the category of a news headline. With the explosion of digital media, automated news classification plays a crucial role in organizing, recommending, and analyzing information efficiently.

In this challenge, you¡¯ll work with a dataset of Indian news headlines spanning diverse domains such as Politics, Sports, Technology, Business, Science, Entertainment, Education, Health, Environment, and Society.

Your goal is to train a model capable of understanding the linguistic nuances and contextual cues in short text snippets to predict the correct news category accurately.

This competition provides an opportunity to explore Natural Language Processing (NLP), text embedding techniques, and sequence models like LSTM and BERT, combining theory with real-world problem-solving skills relevant to AI applications in media analytics.
----------------------
# Description

In this competition, your task is to **build a deep learning model that can automatically classify news articles** based on their content.
With the rapid growth of digital journalism and online news platforms, **text classification** has become a powerful tool for filtering, organizing, and understanding large volumes of textual information.

You will be working with an **India-focused news dataset** containing thousands of real-world articles across multiple categories such as **Politics, Sports, Technology, Entertainment, Business, and Health**.
Your goal is to train a model ¡ª for example, an **LSTM**, **GRU**, or **Transformer-based network** ¡ª that can accurately predict the **category** of an unseen news headline or article.

This challenge helps participants explore key **Natural Language Processing (NLP)** and **Deep Learning** techniques, including:

* Text preprocessing (tokenization, stopword removal, stemming/lemmatization)
* Word embeddings (Word2Vec, GloVe, or BERT embeddings)
* Sequence modeling using LSTMs or advanced architectures
* Model evaluation and optimization

This competition is **for practice and learning only**, designed to help students and enthusiasts:

* Gain hands-on experience with text data and neural networks
* Understand real-world challenges of NLP tasks
* Learn how to structure and evaluate a Kaggle-style ML competition

---

### ?? **Objective**

Develop a machine learning model that classifies each piece of text into the correct **news category label** using the provided training dataset.

---

### ?? **Dataset Overview**

* **Train Data:** Contains news text and corresponding category labels (e.g., *Sports*, *Politics*, *Tech*).
* **Test Data:** Contains only news text without labels ¡ª your model must predict the correct category.
* **Evaluation:** Based on model **accuracy** (or **macro F1-score**, depending on configuration).

---

### ?? **Learning Goals**

By participating in this challenge, you will:

* Learn to clean, analyze, and preprocess textual data
* Apply sequence-based deep learning models (LSTM, BiLSTM, or RNN variants)
* Evaluate NLP models effectively using standard classification metrics
* Gain experience in setting up and managing a competition workflow similar to Kaggle

---

### ?? **Example Use Case**

Imagine a news aggregator app that automatically organizes new articles into relevant topics ¡ª this competition simulates that scenario on a smaller scale, allowing you to build your own intelligent text classifier.
----------------------
# Evaluation

### ?? **Evaluation Metric**

Your model will be evaluated based on its ability to correctly predict the **news category** for each headline in the test dataset.

The primary evaluation metric for this competition is:

> **Accuracy**

Accuracy is defined as the percentage of correct predictions over the total number of samples.

[
\text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{Number of Correct Predictions}}{\text{Total Number of Predictions}} \times 100
]

As an additional optional metric, you can also compute **Macro F1-score**, which gives equal weight to all categories (useful if the dataset is slightly imbalanced):

[
\text{Macro F1} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{2 \times (\text{Precision}_i \times \text{Recall}_i)}{\text{Precision}_i + \text{Recall}_i}
]

Where **N** is the number of classes.

---

### ?? **Submission Format**

Each participant must submit a **CSV file** (`submission.csv`) with the following three columns:

| Id | Headline                                         | Predicted_Category |
| -- | ------------------------------------------------ | ------------------ |
| 1  | ""Government launches new digital payment scheme"" | Business           |
| 2  | ""India wins gold medal in Asian Games hockey""    | Sports             |
| 3  | ""ISRO announces next lunar mission timeline""     | Science            |
| 4  | ""Sensex rises 500 points amid investor optimism"" | Business           |
| 5  | ""New education reforms announced by Ministry""    | Politics           |

**Rules for submission:**

* The **Id** must match the IDs in the test dataset.
* The **Headline** must remain unchanged.
* The **Predicted_Category** must be one of the valid category labels (e.g., *Politics, Sports, Business, Technology, Health,* etc.).
* The file should be in **UTF-8 CSV format** with no extra spaces or missing columns.
* Maximum of **3 submissions per day** are allowed.

---

### ?? **Example Code for Creating Submission**

```python
import pandas as pd

# Load test data
test_data = pd.read_csv(""india_news_test_dataset.csv"")

# Generate predictions using your trained model
predictions = model.predict(test_data[""Headline""])

# Prepare submission DataFrame
submission = pd.DataFrame({
    ""Id"": test_data[""Id""],
        ""Predicted_Category"": predictions
})

# Save as CSV
submission.to_csv(""submission.csv"", index=False)
print(""? Submission file created successfully!"")
```

---

### ?? **Leaderboard**

* The leaderboard will be based on **test set accuracy**.
* Only the **final submission** will be considered for ranking.
* As this is a **practice competition**, the leaderboard is for learning and self-assessment purposes only.","# Dataset Description

---

## ?? **Dataset Description**

This dataset contains thousands of **news headlines from Indian media sources**, categorized into multiple topics such as **Politics, Sports, Technology, Business, Entertainment, and Health**.

It is designed for **Natural Language Processing (NLP)** and **Text Classification** tasks ¡ª specifically to train and evaluate models that can automatically predict the **category of a news headline** based on its content.

---

### **?? Files Included**

1. **india_news_dataset.csv** ¡ª Training data containing 5,000 labeled rows.

   * Columns:

     * `Headline`: The text of the news headline.
     * `Category`: The category label (Politics, Sports, Technology, Business, Entertainment, Health, etc.).

2. **india_news_test_dataset.csv** ¡ª Test data containing 2,000 unlabeled rows (for model evaluation).

   * Columns:

     * `Headline`: The text of the news headline (no category provided).

---

### **?? Dataset Statistics**

| Split | Rows  | Columns | Description         |
| ----- | ----- | ------- | ------------------- |
| Train | 5,000 | 3       | Id+Headline + Category |
| Test  | 2,000 | 2       | Id+ Headline       |

---

### **?? Intended Use**

* Building and training **LSTM**, **RNN**, **CNN**, or **Transformer-based** models for text classification.
* Practicing preprocessing techniques such as tokenization, stopword removal, and vectorization (TF-IDF, Word2Vec, etc.).
* Evaluating classification performance using accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score.

---

### **?? Source and Relevance**

The dataset is **synthetically generated and curated for educational purposes**, reflecting typical styles and topics of Indian news headlines to mimic real-world data distribution.

---

### **?? Example**

| Headline                                      | Category   |
| --------------------------------------------- | ---------- |
| ""Government passes new bill on data privacy""  | Politics   |
| ""Virat Kohli leads India to series victory""   | Sports     |
| ""TCS announces new AI hiring initiative""      | Business   |",False,True
10355,la-amenaza-del-hongo-jedi-o-sith-en-el-reino-mic,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2024 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** The goal of this competition is to predict whether a mushroom is edible or poisonous based on its physical characteristics.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the [Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phi_coefficient).

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict target `class`, whether the observation is editable (`e`) or poisonous (`p`). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,class
    3116945,e
    3116946,p
    3116947,e
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - August 1, 2024
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  August 31, 2024

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the UCI [Mushroom](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/73/mushroom) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

**Note:** Unlike many previous Tabular Playground datasets, data artifacts _have not_ been cleaned up. There are categorical values in the dataset that are not found in the original. It is up to the competitors how to handle this.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `class` is the binary target (either `e` or `p`)
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict target `class` for each row
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
