10359,rental-product-recommendation-system,"# Overview

Predicting the next product page a user will visit based on their previous actions during the same browsing session.

This is a session-based next-item prediction task built on real user behavior from an online rental marketplace. More precisely, the platform focuses on children¡¯s products, including strollers, car seats, toys, and other baby and toddler items that families rent for short- or long-term use. As a result we want to improve our perfomance metrics: primary, number of daily orders.

----------------------
# Description

Your task is to build a model that, given the metadata of a browsing session and the sequence of visited pages, predicts which product pages the user will visit next.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using [Recall](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evaluation_measures_(information_retrieval)#Recall)@6

For each session in the test dataset, your task is to predict which product_id values will appear in the user¡¯s browsing history after the last recorded event in the test session fragment.

Your model must output exactly 6 different product IDs per session.

### Important Notes on Evaluation

All predictions in the test set are made **exclusively for sessions from the new site**.  
Although the dataset includes historical data from both the old and the new site, only sessions from the **new site** appear in the test portion used for scoring.

Therefore:

- Your model must generate predictions **based on the item catalog of the new site**.
- All product identifiers used in your submission must be **`product_id` values from the new site**.
----------------------
# Code and Model Requirements

All models submitted to this competition must be prepared and executed **entirely offline**, without relying on any external services or network connectivity.

Participants must ensure that:

- All computations used to generate the submission file are performed **locally** or on the participant¡¯s own hardware.
- The solution does **not require access to the Internet** at any stage of inference or submission preparation.
- No external APIs, cloud services, hosted models, online databases, remote content, or remote feature stores may be queried.
- All resources required for generating predictions (models, embeddings, vectorizers, lookup tables, etc.) must be included in the participant¡¯s own environment and must not depend on external downloads.
- The submission file must be fully reproducible from the data provided in the *Data* section of the competition.

Solutions that depend on Internet access or external compute resources while producing the submission file may be disqualified according to the competition rules.

----------------------
# Submission Format

Your submission must contain a header:

visit_id,product_ids

Each row must contain:

- **visit_id** ¡ª session identifier
- **product_ids** ¡ª exactly 6 different product IDs, space-delimited

Example:

    visit_id,product_ids
    6970478551166091000,463480210 463480211 463480999 463480998 463480997 463480996


Only `visit_id` values from the test set should appear in your file.

----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: $10,000
- **1st Place**?-?$10,000
----------------------
# Fair Play and Rule Enforcement

Any form of cheating, including participation using multiple accounts, is a violation of both the competition rules and Kaggle platform policies.

The public leaderboard should be considered provisional. Before any prizes are awarded, top submissions will be manually reviewed, including checks for duplicate models, suspicious account activity, and overall compliance with the competition rules. Submissions found to violate the rules **will be disqualified**.

In cases of suspected abuse or suspicious patterns, we will escalate them to Kaggle through the appropriate support channels.

Our goal is to ensure a fair and meaningful competition.
----------------------
# Questions and Discussion

If you have any questions about the dataset, the task, the evaluation, or encounter issues while preparing your solution, feel free to reach out to us.

We host an open community where participants can ask questions, report problems, and discuss approaches with organizers and other competitors.

Join our Discord server:

https://discord.gg/dsycKMBHKr

We actively monitor the channel and will do our best to respond quickly.",,False,True
10360,hse-itmo-acoustic-event-detection-2025,"# Description

### Event classification competition of the course ""Acoustic Events Detection"".

### Baseline: https://github.com/Tvicker/itmo-kaggle-inclass

You will achive scores: 
 5 : beat random baseline 

get 'passed' grade when you beat the :
5: when you beat 0.1 F1-score          //It is strongly better then random (1/42) - you on the right way!
10: when you beat 0.4 F1-score       //It is usualy achivable if you do everything correct
15: when you beat 0.5 F1-score       // It is achvable if you pay attention to both: preprocessing and good training process  
20: when you beat the F1-baselines  //Excellent! Now try to win others ;)

Your solutions will be scored with average class-based [ F1-score ](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_score).

Please, ensure to set the seed when you do any randomness in your notebook. These lines of code must help:

```
import random
import numpy as np

random.seed(42)
np.random.seed(42)
torch.manual_seed(42)
torch.cuda.manual_seed(42)
torch.backends.cudnn.deterministic = True
```
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have included below:

 ---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `AuthorId` and `DuplicateAuthorIds`. `DuplicateAuthorIds` should be a space-delimited list. Every `AuthorId` counts as its own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids. For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list `A,A B C`, `B,B A C`, `C,C A B`.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

Files `train.csv` and `sample_submission.csv` contain `label` and `fname`  as columns.

`fname` is filename, and `train.csv` describes the files in `audio_train` folder, while `sample_submission.csv`contains all the names from the test set. 

To make a submission you should change zeroes in `sample_submission.csv` to meaningful class names as in `train.csv`.",False,True
10361,topics-in-statistics-data-competition,"# Overview

Here is the data competition for the course Topics in Statistics. 

Please follow all the instructions in PDF file and submit the prediction



----------------------
# Description

The presence of sprouted or diseased kernels in wheat can reduce the value of a wheat producer's entire crop. It is important to identify these kernels after being harvested but prior to sale. To facilitate this identification process, automated systems have been developed to separate healthy kernels from the rest. Improving these systems requires better understanding of the measurable ways in which healthy kernels differ from kernels that have sprouted prematurely or are infected with a fungus. To this end, Martin et al. (1998) conducted a study examining numerous physical properties of kernels - density, hardness, size, weight, and moisture - measured on a sample of wheat kernels from two different classes of wheat, hard red winter (hrw) and soft red winter (sw) (represented by the categorical variable ""class"") in a dataset. Each kernel's condition was also classified as ""Healthy"" or ""Unhealthy"" by human visual inspection (represented by the variable ""Type"" in the data, ""Healthy""-1 and ""Unhealthy""-0).

Now you are in this data competition. The training dataset is ""Train.csv"" with 100 observations. The response of the other 100 observations are on hold by Kaggle as the test dataset, which will be further used to assess your prediction performance.

Please attempt different statistical learning models for the response variable ""Type"" based on those data released by Kaggle. You should use all six explanatory variables in the model building process. Then determine one model by yourself to predict the test data and submit to Kaggle to evaluate your performance.
----------------------
# Evaluation

We use ``accuracy"" of your prediction results as the evaluation.","# Dataset Description


## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format 
 
We focus on the prediction of categorical response ``Type"".",False,True
