10383,ramadan-global-data-science-challenge-2026,"# Overview

 Overview:

Ramadan Global Data Science Challenge 2026 is a knowledge-driven machine learning competition designed to foster learning, innovation, and technical excellence during the month of Ramadan.

This competition focuses on real-world data science problem solving, encouraging participants from around the world to apply analytical thinking, feature engineering, and modeling skills in a structured and ethical environment.

Rather than emphasizing prizes, this challenge highlights intellectual growth, global collaboration, and the pursuit of knowledge ¡ª reflecting the spirit of Ramadan as a time of reflection, discipline, and self-improvement.

Participants will work with a structured dataset to build predictive models, optimize performance, and demonstrate sound machine learning practices. The leaderboard will recognize excellence, but the true reward is mastery and learning.
----------------------
# Evaluation

##Evaluation Metric

Submissions are evaluated using the Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (ROC-AUC).
ROC-AUC measures the model¡¯s ability to distinguish between customers who churn (Exited = 1) and customers who remain (Exited = 0).

- A score of 0.5 represents random guessing.

- A score of 1.0 represents perfect classification.

Higher scores indicate better model discrimination performance.
Participants must submit predicted probabilities, not class labels.
Final rankings will be determined by the Private Leaderboard ROC-AUC score.

##Submission Format

For each id in the test dataset, participants must predict the probability that the customer will churn (Exited = 1).

- The submission file must:
- Be in CSV format
- Contain a header
- Include exactly two columns: id and Exited
- Provide probability values between 0 and 1

   ```python
 id,Exited
165034,0.9123
165035,0.0845
165036,0.5031
```
...","# Dataset Description

### Ramadan Global Data Science Challenge 2026

**Bank Customer Retention Prediction**

The dataset used in this competition is synthetically generated from a deep learning model trained on a real-world bank customer churn dataset. While the feature distributions closely resemble authentic banking data, the dataset has been generated to ensure privacy, reproducibility, and safe experimentation.

The objective of this challenge is to predict whether a customer will leave the bank (Exited) based on structured demographic and financial features.

This competition is designed to be accessible to both beginners and experienced practitioners. Participants are encouraged to apply structured data science workflows, including:

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

Data preprocessing and feature engineering

Model selection and validation

Probability-based prediction

Performance evaluation using Log Loss

For beginners, this challenge provides an opportunity to practice fundamental machine learning concepts in a guided and structured environment.

For advanced participants, the dataset offers room for deeper optimization strategies, cross-validation techniques, and ensemble modeling approaches.

Rather than focusing solely on leaderboard ranking, this competition emphasizes clarity of methodology, analytical rigor, and continuous improvement ¡ª aligning with the spirit of discipline and growth during Ramadan.

?? Files

train.csv
Contains the training dataset.
Target variable:

Exited = 1 ¡ú Customer churned

Exited = 0 ¡ú Customer retained

test.csv
Contains the test dataset.
Participants must predict the probability of Exited = 1.

sample_submission.csv
Provides the correct submission format.",False,True
10384,data-science-and-ai,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the quality of wine based on various properties. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored based on the?quadratic weighted kappa, which measures the agreement between two outcomes. This metric typically varies from 0 (random agreement) to 1 (complete agreement). In the event that there is less agreement than expected by chance, the?metric may go below 0.

The quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as follows. First, an N?x?N histogram matrix <em>O</em> is constructed, such that <em>O<sub>i,j</sub></em> corresponds to the number of `Id`s <em>i</em> (actual) that received a predicted value <em>j</em>.?An <em>N-by-N </em>matrix of weights, <em>w</em>, is calculated based on the difference between actual and predicted values:

$$w_{i,j} = \frac{\left(i-j\right)^2}{\left(N-1\right)^2}$$

An <em>N-by-N</em> histogram matrix of expected outcomes, <em>E</em>, is calculated assuming that there is no correlation between values.? This is calculated as the outer product between the actual histogram vector of outcomes and the predicted histogram vector, normalized such that <em>E</em> and <em>O</em> have the same sum.

From these three matrices, the quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as:?

$$\kappa=1-\frac{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}O\_{i,j}}{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}E\_{i,j}}.$$


## Submission File
For each `Id` in the test set, you must predict the value for the target `quality`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,quality
    2056,5
    2057,7
    2058,3
    etc.",,False,True
