# Evaluation

- A : FID Score by student's generated Image 
- B : Latency Score by student model's total time 

- Public_score = 1000 * (0.7 * (1 / 1+0.02A) + 0.3 * 1/(1+0.01B))
- Score Range = [0.0 ~ 1000.0] (Higher is better!)

**Teams ranked in the top 10 on the public leaderboard will have their final ranking determined by combining their public and private scores.**

- C : FID Score by student's generated Image using private Image
- D : Latency Score by student model's total time using private Image

- Private_score = 1000 * (0.7 * (1 / 1+0.02C) + 0.3 * 1/(1+0.01D))

- Final Score = Public_Score + Private_Score

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

- Dataset : TinyImageNet (200 Classes)
- Public data : Train dataset (100,000) + Val dataset(10,000)
- Private data : -
- Image Size : 64x64
- Teacher model weight : Pretraiend on TinyImageNet 100 epoch DDPM model  (FID : 59.00)

Below contents are the example of the format of the sets.",False,True
10387,physical-retail-ai-workshop-track-3-wacv-2026,"# Overview

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F319017%2Fcf4754aa8d6b68b4e5e208660245054e%2FRetailAction%20Challenge.png?generation=1762443553677455&alt=media)
Welcome to the **RetailAction challenge**!
This challenge is Track 3 of the Physical Retail Challenges, which will be presented at the WACV 2026 conference within the Physical Retail AI Workshop (PRAW). The dataset and challenge are provided by Standard AI.

## Useful Links

- [Physical Retail Workshop at WACV 2026](https://grocery-vision.github.io/index.html)
- [Dataset on Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/datasets/standard-cognition/RetailAction)
- [About Standard AI](#standard-ai)

The goal of this competition is to develop AI models that can accurately detect and localize shopper-shelf interactions in top-view retail videos. Your model must identify **when** actions occur (temporal localization) and **where** they happen (spatial localization) as shoppers interact with items on shelves.

**What makes this challenging?**
- Videos may contain one, multiple, or no interactions
- Each sample provides two synchronized camera views of the same scene
- Spatial precision is measured in real-world meters, not just pixels
- Actions can be subtle and occur at various temporal scales

This competition advances computer vision research in retail analytics, with applications in inventory management, customer behavior analysis, and automated checkout systems.

If this is your first time participating in a Kaggle competition, please refer to our [Getting Started Guide](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions) for help. For instructions on submitting predictions, check the [submission documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

**Key Competition Details:**
- **Evaluation Metric:** mAP Global (mean Average Precision combining spatial and temporal accuracy)
- **Dataset:** 2,501 dual-view video samples in the test set
- **Submission Format:** CSV file with JSON-formatted predictions
----------------------
# Description

### Task Overview

In this challenge, you'll work with short video clips from overhead cameras in retail environments. Each clip is centered around potential shopper-shelf interactions and captured from two synchronized viewpoints (**rank0** and **rank1** cameras).

Your model must:
1. **Detect** whether any interactions occur in the video
2. **Temporally localize** the start and end frames of each action
3. **Spatially localize** the interaction point between the shopper and the shelf
4. **Provide predictions for both camera views**

> **Note:** Action classification (take, put, touch) is **not** part of the evaluation. Focus on detecting and localizing interactions, not classifying them.

### Action Types

The interactions in the dataset include:
- **Taking** items from shelves
- **Putting** items back on shelves  
- **Touching** items on shelves

### Dataset Characteristics

Videos in this dataset may contain:
- **One interaction** (most common case)
- **No interaction** (shopper passes by without touching items)
- **Multiple interactions** (less frequent, but possible)

Each video sample includes:
- Two synchronized video files from different viewpoints
- Normalized temporal range: `[0, 1]` (0 = video start, 1 = video end)
- Normalized spatial coordinates: `[0, 1]` for both x and y dimensions

### Understanding Spatial Localization

A unique aspect of this challenge is that **spatial accuracy is measured in meters**, not pixels. This accounts for:
- Different camera perspectives
- Varying distances from the camera
- Real-world physical accuracy

The evaluation uses pose keypoint data to compute meter-to-pixel conversion factors, ensuring that predictions are evaluated based on real-world distance rather than pixel counts.

### Training Data

While this competition focuses on the test set, the full dataset includes:

| Split | Video Pairs | Purpose |
|-------|-------------|---------|
| **Training** | 17,222 | Model training |
| **Validation** | 1,277 | Model validation |
| **Public Test** | 2,501 | Suggested test set (labels released) |
| **Private Test** | 2,500 | Competition evaluation (labels not released) |

**Important:** The private test set is split into two subsets of equal size. The first subset is used to compute the scores displayed on the public leaderboard, and the second subset is reserved for the final competition ranking on the private leaderboard.

### Example Scenarios

**Scenario 1: Single Action**
```
A shopper approaches a shelf, reaches out at time 0.2-0.4, 
picks up an item, and walks away.
¡ú Predict: 1 action with temporal bounds [0.2, 0.4] and spatial location
```

**Scenario 2: No Action**
```
A shopper walks past a shelf without stopping or touching anything.
¡ú Predict: Empty action list
```

**Scenario 3: Multiple Actions**
```
A shopper picks up an item at time 0.15-0.35, examines it, 
then picks up another item at time 0.65-0.85.
¡ú Predict: 2 actions with respective temporal and spatial information
```
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Evaluation Metric

Submissions are evaluated using **mAP Global** (mean Average Precision), which combines both temporal and spatial accuracy across multiple threshold values.

The metric computation involves:

1. **Temporal Matching:** Actions are matched using Temporal Intersection over Union (tIoU)
   - Default thresholds: 10 values from 0.05 to 0.5
   - Higher tIoU indicates better temporal alignment

2. **Spatial Matching:** Spatial locations are compared using distance in meters
   - Default thresholds: 20 values from 1.0m to 0.05m  
   - Distance computed from pose keypoint data

3. **Confidence Scoring:** Predictions are evaluated at multiple confidence thresholds
   - Default: 101 linearly spaced values from 1.0 to 0.0
   - Enables precision-recall curve computation

A prediction matches a ground truth action if:
- Temporal IoU ¡Ý temporal threshold **AND**
- Spatial distance ¡Ü spatial threshold **AND**  
- Prediction confidence ¡Ý confidence threshold

The final **mAP Global** score is the mean Average Precision across all threshold combinations.

### Score Interpretation

- **0.0:** No correct predictions
- **0.3-0.5:** Moderate performance
- **0.7+:** Strong performance
- **1.0:** Perfect predictions (all actions correctly detected and localized)

### Submission File Format

You must submit a CSV file with exactly **two columns**: `id` and `prediction`.

**CSV Structure:**
```csv
id,prediction
video_id_1,""{""""actions"""": [...]}""
video_id_2,""{""""actions"""": []}""
```

The `prediction` column must contain a **JSON string** with the following structure:

#### Example: Single Action

```json
{
  ""actions"": [
    {
      ""score"": 0.85,
      ""start"": 0.1,
      ""end"": 0.4,
      ""spatial"": {
        ""action_cam"": {
          ""rank0"": {
            ""x"": 0.5,
            ""y"": 0.6
          },
          ""rank1"": {
            ""x"": 0.7,
            ""y"": 0.4
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

#### Example: Multiple Actions

```json
{
  ""actions"": [
    {
      ""score"": 0.89,
      ""start"": 0.1,
      ""end"": 0.35,
      ""spatial"": {
        ""action_cam"": {
          ""rank0"": {""x"": 0.45, ""y"": 0.62},
          ""rank1"": {""x"": 0.52, ""y"": 0.58}
        }
      }
    },
    {
      ""score"": 0.95,
      ""start"": 0.65,
      ""end"": 0.85,
      ""spatial"": {
        ""action_cam"": {
          ""rank0"": {""x"": 0.38, ""y"": 0.71},
          ""rank1"": {""x"": 0.61, ""y"": 0.43}
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

#### Example: No Actions Detected

```json
{
  ""actions"": []
}
```

### Field Descriptions

| Field | Type | Range | Description |
|-------|------|-------|-------------|
| **score** | float | [0, 1] | Confidence score for the action detection. Higher values indicate greater confidence in the action's presence. |
| **start** | float | [0, 1] | Normalized start time of the action (0 = video beginning, 1 = video end) |
| **end** | float | [0, 1] | Normalized end time of the action. Must satisfy: `start < end` |
| **x** | float | [0, 1] | Normalized x-coordinate (0 = left edge, 1 = right edge) |
| **y** | float | [0, 1] | Normalized y-coordinate (0 = top edge, 1 = bottom edge) |

### Important Submission Requirements

? **Required:**
- Include predictions for **all** test set video IDs
- Provide spatial coordinates for **both** camera views (`rank0` and `rank1`)
- Ensure all values are within valid ranges
- Use proper JSON formatting with escaped quotes in CSV

? **Common Mistakes to Avoid:**
- Missing video IDs from the test set
- Incorrect JSON formatting (causes parsing errors)
- Values outside valid ranges (e.g., start > end, coordinates > 1.0)
- Missing camera views in spatial predictions

### Sample Submission

Download the `sample_submission.csv` file from the Data tab to see the correct format. You can use it as a template by replacing the predictions with your model's output.

### Submission Tips

1. **Validate locally first:** Ensure your JSON is properly formatted before submission
2. **Handle edge cases:** Some videos may have no actions - predict empty action lists
3. **Balance precision and recall:** The metric evaluates across all confidence thresholds
4. **Consider both views:** Spatial predictions should be accurate for both camera perspectives
5. **Test your code:** Verify that all video IDs from `test.csv` are included in your submission

### Getting Help

- **Forum:** Post questions in the competition discussion forum
- **Sample Code:** Check the competition kernels for baseline implementations
- **Documentation:** Review the detailed README in the competition data download
