10392,breast-cancer-us-classification-ml-4-im-2025-2026,"# Description

This academic competition has the aim to support students through the *Machine Learning for Imaging Data* course at the University School for Advanced Studies IUSS Pavia.

The target of the copmetition is to implement a model able to automatically discriminate *malignant* from *benign* lesions in breast ultrasound (US) studies.

The dataset and rules of submission are briefly described in the corresponding sections of this hosting page.
Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

Good luck and have fun!
----------------------
# Evaluation

User submissions are evaluated by comparing their Submission CSV to the ground truth Solution CSV with respect to the <a href=""https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Area_Under_the_Curve"" target=""blank"">Area Under the ROC Curve</a> (AUC) metric.
<br><br>
The Submission CSV file must contain a prediction score or probability for each classified study, corresponding to the probability of that lesion(s) to belong to class ""Malignant"" or ""Benign"".
<br><br>
It is expected that the probability to belong to class ""Benign"" ranges from 0 to 0.5, and that the probability to belong to class ""Malignant"" ranges from 0.5 to 1 (p &lt; 0.5 -&gt; ""Benign"", p &gt; 0.5 -&gt; ""Malignant"").","# Dataset Description

The training set contains two folders, corresponding to the ""benign"" and ""malignant"" classes of interest. This folder separation provides information regarding the label of each study (which is also the target of our predictions).

Each breast ultrasound study is referred to with a numerical *Id* (e.g. ""benign (25)"") and has a corresponding lesion segmentation (e.g. ""benign (25)_mask""). Each study has at least one corresponding segmentation file, containing the mask of a lesion.
However, there are studies with more than one segmented lesion, and each segmentation mask is reported in a different file (e.g. ""benign (25)_mask"" and ""benign (25)_mask_1""). In these cases, all the lesions belonging to the same study share the same class (e.g. both lesions of study ""benign (25)"" are benign). 
Studies and masks are provided in *png* format.",False,True
10393,flood-image-segmentation-ml-4-im-2025-2026,"# Overview

This academic competition has the aim to support students at the University School for Advanced Studies IUSS Pavia.

The target of the competition is to implement a model able to automatically segment visible aerial flood images.
----------------------
# Description

The dataset and rules of submission are briefly described in the corresponding sections of this hosting page.
Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

Good luck and have fun!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on the DICE score between predicted and target segmentations (higher score is better).",,False,True
10394,cat-or-dog-image-classification-challenge,"# Overview


# ?? Cat or Dog Image Classification Challenge

Welcome to the **Cat or Dog Image Classification Challenge** ¡ª a fun and practical computer vision competition where your goal is to teach machines to **differentiate between images of cats and dogs**.

This challenge will test your ability to work with image data, design and train classification models, and optimize their performance for real-world accuracy. It¡¯s a great opportunity to sharpen your deep learning and data preprocessing skills while exploring one of the most classic problems in image recognition.

---

## ?? Competition Overview

In this competition, you are provided with a labeled dataset of cat and dog images for training. Your task is to build a model that can correctly identify whether an unseen image contains a **cat (0)** or a **dog (1)**.

You¡¯ll submit your predictions in a `.csv` file format that matches the provided `sample_submission.csv` structure. Submissions will be evaluated based on accuracy ¡ª how well your predictions match the ground truth labels.

---

## ?? What You¡¯ll Learn

By participating in this competition, you¡¯ll gain hands-on experience in:

* Image preprocessing and augmentation
* Neural network architecture design (CNNs, transfer learning, etc.)
* Model evaluation and tuning for higher accuracy
* Managing large-scale image datasets efficiently
* Submitting and scoring your results on a live leaderboard

Whether you¡¯re a beginner exploring computer vision or an experienced ML developer looking to improve model performance, this challenge will help you build strong foundations and showcase your skills.

---

## ?? Goal

The main objective of this challenge is simple yet powerful:
**Build an AI model that can automatically distinguish between cats and dogs with high accuracy.**

Participants are encouraged to experiment, learn, and share their insights with the community. Collaboration, creativity, and experimentation are key to mastering this task!

---

## ?? Helpful Resources

If you¡¯re new to Kaggle competitions or machine learning, here are a few links to get you started:

* [Getting Started Guide](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions-setup)
* [Kaggle Competition Documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions)
* [Showdown Markdown Syntax](https://github.com/showdownjs/showdown/wiki/Showdown's-Markdown-syntax)


----------------------
# Description


### **Description**

Can your model tell the difference between a **cat ??** and a **dog ??**?

In this competition, participants are challenged to build an image classification model that can accurately identify whether a given image contains a **cat** or a **dog**.
This task is a fundamental problem in **computer vision** and a great starting point for anyone exploring **deep learning** and **image recognition**.

---

## **About the Problem**

Image classification is a key task in the field of machine learning.
Given a set of labeled images, the goal is to train a model to automatically predict the label of a new, unseen image.
In this case, your model should predict whether an image belongs to the **cat** class or the **dog** class.

You can use traditional ML algorithms or train **convolutional neural networks (CNNs)** for higher accuracy.

---

## **Dataset Overview**

The dataset consists of a single folder containing mixed images of cats and dogs.
Each image is named as an integer (e.g., `0.jpg`, `1.jpg`, `2.jpg`, ...).

Participants must use these images to train and evaluate their models.
No labels are provided ¡ª you¡¯ll have to infer them using your own model training and experimentation.


## **Mathematical Representation**

You can include equations in LaTeX format.
For instance, the prediction confidence of your model can be represented as:

$$ \epsilon = \sqrt{x_n^2 + 1} $$

This shows how LaTeX renders math expressions beautifully in your competition description.


----------------------
# Evaluation



Submissions are evaluated based on the [Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the actual `is_dog` target.

Your goal is to predict how likely each image contains a **dog**.
Higher AUC scores indicate better model performance at distinguishing between cats and dogs.

---

### **Submission File**

For each image in the dataset, you must predict the probability that it is a dog (`is_dog = 1`).
Your submission file should include a header and follow this format:

```
img,is_dog
0,0
1,1
2,1
3,0
...
```

Where:

* `img` ¡ª corresponds to the image filename without extension (e.g., `0` for `0.jpg`)
* `is_dog` ¡ª is the **predicted probability** (a float between 0 and 1) that the image is a dog.","# Dataset Description

### **Data Description**

The dataset for this competition consists of images of **cats** and **dogs**.
Your task is to build a model that can correctly identify whether a given image is a **dog (1)** or a **cat (0)**.

No labels are provided in the dataset ¡ª participants must train their own models and generate predictions.
Your submissions will be evaluated against hidden ground truth labels.

---

## **Files**

* **data/** ¡ª contains all unlabeled images used for both training and testing.
  Each file is named as an integer, e.g., `0.jpg`, `1.jpg`, `2.jpg`, etc.
  The images are mixed; there are no separate folders for cats or dogs.

* **sample_submission.csv** ¡ª a sample file showing the required submission format.

---

## **Columns**

* **`img`** ¡ª the image filename (integer only, without `.jpg` extension).
  Example: `0` corresponds to `0.jpg`.

* **`is_dog`** ¡ª your model¡¯s prediction:

  * `1` ¡ú Dog
  * `0` ¡ú Cat

---

## **Example**

A correct submission file should look like this:

```
img,is_dog
0,0
1,1
2,1
3,0
```

---

## **Summary**

**What you¡¯re predicting:**
Whether each image contains a dog (`1`) or a cat (`0`).

**Data format:**

* Input: JPEG images (`.jpg`) of cats and dogs
* Output: A CSV file with predictions in the format shown above",False,True
