10407,ames-house-pricing-interval-prediction-2025,"# Overview

This competition introduces a twist to the traditional approach of machine learning contests involving regression. Typically, such contests focus on *point predictions*¡ªthe expected values $\hat{y}$. However, in this competition, the focus shifts to *prediction intervals*. These intervals are often of greater practical interest than point predictions, as they provide a fuller picture of uncertainty in the model's predictions.  

In this challenge, participants will predict the intervals associated with a modified version of the **Ames housing prices dataset**.  

### Goal  
The objective is to achieve the smallest average prediction interval while maintaining accurate coverage levels.
----------------------
# Description

We will be working with a modified version of the Ames Housing Dataset. The original dataset contains 79 independent variables for modeling house prices. However, in this version, only 31 variables are included to simplify the modeling process. Additionally, house prices have been adjusted to make the data more conducive to linear regression.

The objective is to provide interval estimates for house prices. These intervals will be evaluated based on two factors: the length of the interval (shorter is better) and their accuracy in covering the actual house prices. This dual penalty ensures a balance between precision and reliability.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions
* `train_ames.csv` - the training set
* `test_ames.csv` - the test set
* `sample_submission.csv` - an example of a valid submission

## About Dataset
The Ames Housing Dataset is a well-known dataset in the field of machine learning and data analysis. It contains various features and attributes of residential homes in Ames, Iowa, USA. The dataset is often used for regression tasks, particularly for predicting housing prices.

We will be working with a modified version of this dataset that contains the following variables.

* LotFrontage: Linear feet of street connected to property
* LotArea: Lot size in square feet
* Street: Type of road access
* OverallQual: Overall material and finish quality
* OverallCond: Overall condition rating
* YearBuilt: Original construction date
* YearRemodAdd: Remodel date
* MasVnrArea: Masonry veneer area in square feet
* TotalBsmtSF: Total square feet of basement area
* CentralAir: Central air conditioning
* 1stFlrSF: First Floor square feet
* 2ndFlrSF: Second floor square feet
* LowQualFinSF: Low quality finished square feet (all floors)
* BsmtFullBath: Basement full bathrooms
* BsmtHalfBath: Basement half bathrooms
* FullBath: Full bathrooms above grade
* HalfBath: Half baths above grade
* Bedroom: Number of bedrooms above basement level
* Kitchen: Number of kitchens
* TotRmsAbvGrd: Total rooms above grade (does not include bathrooms)
* Fireplaces: Number of fireplaces
* GarageYrBlt: Year garage was built
* GarageCars: Size of garage in car capacity
* GarageArea: Size of garage in square feet
* WoodDeckSF: Wood deck area in square feet
* OpenPorchSF: Open porch area in square feet
* EnclosedPorch: Enclosed porch area in square feet
* 3SsnPorch: Three season porch area in square feet
* ScreenPorch: Screen porch area in square feet
* PoolArea: Pool area in square feet
* YrSold: Year Sold
* SalePrice - the property's sale price in dollars. This is the target variable that you're trying to predict.",False,True
10408,deseret-alphabet-manuscript-hackathon,"# Overview

In this competition participants are tasked with developing models that can read in lines of [Deseret Alphabet Scripture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deseret_alphabet) and transcribe the corresponding unicode representation. This hackathon is hosted by [the BYU AI Association](https://aia.byu.edu/) and is available to any BYU undergraduate or graduate student as long provided they are available to present their solution in on December 3rd, 2025. 

Total cash prizes will be around $500. The details will be added here later.

The kickoff for this competition will be on November 19th 2025 in 2111 JKB
----------------------
# Description

The Deseret alphabet was created in the 1850s by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints as a phonetic writing system designed to make English spelling more logical and easier to learn. Its purpose was to promote literacy among church members of various nationalities and help standardize written communication, though it ultimately fell out of use as the standard English alphabet remained the dominant writing system.

Despite the alphabet's lack of use it is still [of interest to researchers](http://faculty.las.illinois.edu/rshosted/deseret.html) to understand the linguistic history of the American frontier as accents are fundamentally encoded into the phonetic characters. 

Currently there are a large number of untranscribed documents written in Deseret. This hackathon aims to develop models that will automate this process to ultimately accelerate Deseret's research.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Levenshtein distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Levenshtein_distance) or the number of single-character edits required to make the predicted representation match the true representation. For this hackathon, capitalization and spaces are not relevant to scoring.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the Deseret alphabet unicode representation. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
id,label
003062561,?????????????? ??????????????
0061360b6,?????????????? ??????????????
00ff2b57f,?????????????? ??????????????
```",,False,True
10409,data-science-24-hour-challenge,"# Overview

Restaurants across the world receive ratings from thousands of customers on Zomato, reflecting factors like cuisine type, price range, delivery options, and overall experience.
Understanding what drives these ratings can help businesses enhance customer satisfaction and improve their performance.

In this competition, your task is to predict the average restaurant rating (Aggregate rating) based on various features such as location, cuisines, price range, country, and services offered.
Participants will use the provided dataset (Zomato + Country Code) to build a machine learning regression model capable of accurately estimating restaurant ratings.

The competition is open exclusively to ABES Engineering College students and faculty and will run for 24 hours.
After the competition ends, all participants¡¯ performances will be displayed on a Kaggle leaderboard, which faculty members can review to assess results.

Objective:
Build a model to predict the Aggregate rating of a restaurant using features like Cuisines, Price range, Votes, and service availability.

Evaluation Metric:
Submissions are evaluated based on Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between predicted and actual ratings.
----------------------
# Description

Restaurants worldwide receive ratings from customers on Zomato, influenced by multiple factors such as cuisine type, price range, delivery options, and overall experience.
Understanding what drives these ratings is valuable for restaurant owners, analysts, and machine learning enthusiasts.

In this competition, your task is to predict the average restaurant rating (Aggregate rating) based on restaurant attributes such as cuisine, city, country, price range, and customer votes.

This is a regression problem, where you must build a model capable of estimating the rating value as accurately as possible.

The challenge is part of the ABES 24-Hour Data Science Challenge, designed to help students practice data cleaning, feature engineering, and predictive modeling on a real-world dataset.

Build a machine learning regression model to predict the Aggregate rating for each restaurant in the test dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between predicted and actual ratings.

A lower RMSE indicates a better model performance.","# Dataset Description

File Name                  Description                                                                           
 **train.csv**              Contains restaurant data and their `Aggregate rating` (target variable).             
  **test.csv**               Contains the same columns as train, except `Aggregate rating` is removed.             
**sample_submission.csv**  Example of the submission file format.                                                
 **solution.csv**           (Hidden) file used by Kaggle to evaluate predictions and generate leaderboard scores.",False,True
10410,rent-prediction-2025,"# Overview

This competition is an in-class competition for the course, ""Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning (Fall 2025)"", in Renmin University of China.

The competition is to predict the rental prices for listings on the Airbnb website.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation) between the predicted prices and the observed prices.


## Submission File
For every listing in the test dataset, the submission file should contain two columns: id and price, i.e. the id and the predicted price for the listing.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,price
    257384,206.85
    209273,16.8
    164016,185
    140574,28
    etc.","# Dataset Description

Airbnb, Inc. is an American company operating an online marketplace for short- and long-term homestays and experiences. The dataset comprises more than 200,000 individual Airbnb listings across multiple cities, described by 52 distinct features. It captures a snapshot of properties available, details about their hosts, location specifics, pricing structures, availability, and comprehensive review scores. 

## Files

*   **airbnb_train.csv** - the training set
*   **airbnb_test.csv** - the test set
*   **airbnb_sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `id` : Unique identifier for each listing.
*   `name`, `description` : Textual descriptions of the listing.
*   `property_type` : Type of property (e.g., apartment, house, private room).
*   `room_type` : The type of room offered (e.g., Entire home/apt, Private room).
*   `accommodates` : Number of guests the listing can accommodate.
*   `bathrooms`, `bedrooms`, `beds` : Details on the property's physical attributes.
*   `amenities` : A list of amenities provided by the listing.
*   `host_id`, `host_name` : Unique identifiers and names for hosts.
*   `host_since` : Date when the host joined Airbnb.
*   `host_location`, `host_about` : Information about the host's location and self-description.
*   `host_response_time`, `host_response_rate`, `host_acceptance_rate` : Metrics on host responsiveness and booking acceptance.
*   `host_is_superhost` : Indicates if the host is a ""Superhost.""
*   `host_neighbourhood` : The neighborhood where the host resides (if provided).
*   `host_listings_count`, `host_total_listings_count` : Number of listings by the host.
*   `host_verifications`, `host_has_profile_pic`, `host_identity_verified` : Verification status of the host.
*   `latitude`, `longitude` : Geographical coordinates of the listing.
*   `neighbourhood_overview` : Information about the listing's neighborhood.
*   `city` : The city where the listing is located.
*   `price` : The nightly price of the listing.
*   `has_availability` : Indicates if the listing has any availability.
*   `availability_30`, `availability_60`, `availability_90`, `availability_365` : Number of available days in various future periods.
*   `availability_eoy` : Availability at the end of the year.
*   `number_of_reviews`, `number_of_reviews_ltm`, `number_of_reviews_l30d` : Total reviews and reviews in the last 12 months (ltm) and 30 days (l30d).
*   `number_of_reviews_ly` : Number of reviews in the last year.
*   `first_review`, `last_review` : Dates of the first and last reviews.
*   `review_scores_rating`, `review_scores_accuracy`, `review_scores_cleanliness`, `review_scores_checkin`, `review_scores_communication`, `review_scores_location`, `review_scores_value` : Detailed breakdown of review scores.
*   `reviews_per_month` : Average number of reviews per month.
*   `estimated_occupancy_l365d`, `estimated_revenue_l365d` : Estimated occupancy and revenue over the last 365 days.",False,True
10411,dummy-competition-aicvs-2025-26-introductory,"# Overview

This is the home page of the competition. In this section, you should aim to succinctly introduce the competition.

If this is your first time creating a competition, please refer to our [Getting Started Guide](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions-setup) for help. Here is a handy link to [Kaggle's competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on submitting predictions.

**Goal:** When you write this section, try to summarize your competition¡¯s goal. It will help your participants quickly understand their objective.
----------------------
# Competition Overview

This is a dummy Competition designed to demonstrate the working of AICVS's kaggle contest
----------------------
# Description

# ?? Sweet Fruit Classification Challenge

Fruits can vary widely in sweetness based on their physical properties.  
Your task in this competition is to predict whether a fruit is **sweet (1)** or **not sweet (0)** using its characteristics.

## Dataset
Each fruit is described by the following features:

- **id** ¨C unique identifier  
- **weight** ¨C numeric weight of the fruit  
- **color** ¨C categorical color label  
- **sweet** ¨C binary target label (0 = not sweet, 1 = sweet)

## Objective
Build a machine learning model that accurately predicts sweetness from the given features.



----------------------
# Evaluations

Submissions are evaluated using **Accuracy Score**.

Your submission file should have the format:



# Submission CSV File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET (Sweet) variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id, Sweet
    2,0
    5,0
    6,1
    etc.


The accuracy is computed as the percentage of correctly predicted sweetness labels.

Higher accuracy means better performance.

----------------------
# Scoring 

Scoring will be based on the Accuracy scores",,False,True
