#  #KaggleCommunityOlympiad 
# ?? **Predict Stress from Conversation Text**

### IMP NOTE: 
**Please make sure to submit atleast one prediction to make your participation count. Joining competition will not be considered as participation. A tutorial on how to make a submission and an approach to get better score is already made watch it here on YouTube. [Click here to watch tutorial](https://www.youtube.com/live/vGNtWj1mntI?si=No1cN9n9uyDHz8sL&t=940)**

### **A bit about the problem**

Everyday conversations can reveal far more than what¡¯s explicitly said ¡ª they carry subtle emotional cues, intent, and stress patterns.
This competition challenges participants to decode these signals from text alone and **predict the underlying stress level or emotional category. (Multiclass Prediction)**.

This competition is part of the **Kaggle Community Olympiad**, empowering learners to explore applied NLP through a real-world mental wellness scenario.

---

# ? **Why Participate?**

### **?? Build Strong NLP Expertise**

Work hands-on with text preprocessing, emotion mining, linguistic patterns, and multi-class classification using real conversational data.

### **?? Understand Human-Centric AI**

Help shape models that can detect stress, overwhelm, or emotional tone ¡ª foundational for assistants, well-being tools, and empathetic AI systems.

### **?? Showcase Your ML Engineering**

Feature engineering, embeddings, transformer models, classical baselines ¡ª this challenge welcomes all approaches to shine on the leaderboard.

### **?? Community Recognition**

Top contributors receive certificates, shout-outs, and a chance to be featured in Kaggle Community events.

---

# ?? **Goal**

The goal is to build a machine learning model that can accurately **classify a piece of human conversation text into its corresponding stress-related emotional category**.
Your task is to use the provided conversational dataset ¡ª enhanced with metadata and annotated labels ¡ª to predict:

* **Joy, Fear, Sadness, or other emotional cues from the dataset**

This challenge strengthens your skills in **text classification, linguistic analysis, feature engineering**, and modern **NLP modeling**.

---

# ??? **Join the Olympiad**

Be part of a global AI learning movement.
Create, compete, collaborate ¡ª and help advance empathetic AI one conversation at a time.

**#KaggleCommunityOlympiad**

---

----------------------
# Description

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F9327258%2F88b53258e7bf5522021ed60ca2f72cd4%2FKCO_landscape_1200x630.png?generation=1763331283823570&alt=media)



---
Stress and emotion shape the way people speak ¡ª even in short conversations or simple statements. Subtle patterns in wording, tone, and structure can signal calmness, positivity, overwhelm, or stress.
In Kaggle Community Olympiad ¨C StressSense, your mission is to analyze short conversational texts and predict their underlying emotional or stress-related category.

The dataset blends real human-written expressions with carefully generated counterparts to ensure balanced labels across emotional states.

Your job is to use the dataset to build an accurate classification model that can identify the stress category for new, unseen text samples.

This challenge is designed for all skill levels ¡ª easy enough for beginners to get started, yet rich enough for experienced data scientists to experiment with feature engineering, transformers, embeddings, or conversational NLP strategies.

Whether you're just beginning your ML journey or refining your NLP craft, StressSense is an opportunity to develop meaningful AI systems that interpret human emotion through language.

Join the Kaggle Community Olympiad and help advance human-aware, emotionally intelligent machine learning.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Submissions are evaluated on [F1 Score - micro]
Compute the score using the metric. 

The ""F1 Score micro beta"" is a metric that combines the concepts of micro-averaging, the general F¦Â score, and the F1 score to evaluate classification models. It calculates the F-beta score by first summing up all true positives, false positives, and false negatives across all classes, and then computing the harmonic mean to give it a global F-score that can be adjusted with the 'beta' parameter to weigh precision versus recall. 



## ?? Submission format and Uploading Output

Make sure you have a sample_submission.csv CSV file containing **2 columns** only as output. 
The output can be a prediction for `test.csv` **and must have same number of rows as test csv** or can be your dataset as well from real sources( can be social media comments or any excerpt from any conversation or dialogue).

**Example (sample_submission.csv) format:**

ID  |  category |
| --- | --- |
1 | sadeness  | 
2 | fear  |
3 | joy |
... | ... |","# Dataset Description

## All about the dataset

### As usual this contains `train.csv` and `test.csv`

This dataset contains short human-written conversational texts prepared for stress/emotion analysis.  
Each row is one text entry with lightweight metadata and an emotion label. 
The training set includes a stress_level target for supervised learning.

Participants should be able to answer these after reading this page:

Which files to use and how they are structured

What each column contains and which is the prediction target

How submissions should be formatted

## ?? Files

**train.csv** ¡ª Labeled training set. 
Contains all input features plus the target column stress_level.



## ?? Columns (exact names present in the data)

`example_id` ¡ª Unique identifier for each example (present in train and test).

`original_text` ¡ª The original human conversational text (raw), taken from source + AI-transformed examples.

`cleaned_text` ¡ª Preprocessed / normalized text ready for NLP (lowercased, stripped of noise, punctuation normalized). Use this column for reproducible baselines.

`is_modified` ¡ª Boolean flag indicating whether the record was AI-generated or transformed (TRUE = modified, FALSE = original). Useful for analysis and domain-shift checks.

`num_sentence` ¡ª Line count of sentences in original_text.

`category` ¡ª Emotion label assigned to the entry (examples: affection, achievement, sadness, stress, neutral). This is an auxiliary feature and may help models learn context.

## ?? What are you predicting?

Predict the **""category""** for every row in test.csv using the provided textual and metadata features. The leaderboard and evaluation will use F1 Micro beta Score (multiclass classification).

## ?? Submission format and Uploading Output

Make sure you have a sample_submission.csv CSV file containing **2 columns** only as output. 
The `id` col is already given in the `test.csv`
The output must be a prediction for all the texts in test data or same number of texts from real sources( can be social media comments or any excerpt from any conversation or dialogue).

**Example (sample_submission.csv) format:**

ID |  category |
