10460,detect-leaking-data-hackathon,"# Overview

   
----------------------
# Summary ??

This competition's main task is to detect data leakage in the given texts. Each text originally came from [The Messenger] (https://messenger.eso.org) journal but has been significantly modified using large language models (LLMs). Only after these modifications was a hidden message inserted into each text.

To solve the task, you have access to:

1) Five specially curated texts created for this competition, each containing a hidden message. The difficulty is increasing with the increasing file numbers.

2) A notebook demonstrating how to build a sample solution.

If needed, additional hints will be publicly provided during the hackathon ¡ª see *Discussion* tab for more details and rules.
----------------------
# Description ??

ESA's European Space Operations Centre initiated a project to outline a clear direction for data strategy and AI implementation in mission operations. This initiative led to the creation of the [DataX] (https://iafastro.directory/iac/paper/id/89097/summary/) [1] strategy, developed to improve the scalability of advanced analytics applications, including AI systems and to increase the value of data within the organization. One of the key elements of developing an AI system is the **security of AI applications** addressed within the [**Assurance for Space Domain AI Applications** project](https://assurance-ai.space-codev.org/) and its [Catalogue of Security Risks for AI Applications in Space](https://zenodo.org/records/14762574). This competition is based on a real-life AI security threat identified within the project - **data leakage** [2].

## Story

There was unauthorized access reported in your company's network. Your job is to investigate the incident and check if the data from the recent important discovery is safe. No file transfer has been observed, but some of the latest PDFs of the [""The Messenger"" magazine from the ESO webpage](https://messenger.eso.org/) were irreversibly replaced on servers with their slightly modified versions. You are aware of [steganography](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steganography) and different [techniques](https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/9/21/2829) of hiding information in text, so you suspect that some sensitive data may have been hidden in the articles from the magazine. Your next task is to check this hypothesis and determine what information was breached.

## Task description

Find the secret message hidden in the changed ""The Messenger"" files. Both contaminated files and messages are text. We provide changed PDF files in [Data tab](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detect-leaking-data-hackathon/data).

You are given five texts, with five messages hidden, one in each. The difficulty increases with increasing numbers in the file names.

The hackathon can be solved in teams of up to 3 members. You have a maximum of 10 submissions per team, so use them wisely.

### Data

Data contains ""The Messenger"" articles intentionally modified using LLMs, so they are not the same as the ones available online, and you do not have access to their versions from before the attack.

More details regarding the data are provided in the [Data tab](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detect-leaking-data-hackathon/data).

### Code
The competition offers a [notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detect-leaking-data-hackathon/code) to show how to read PDF files and create a sample solution format.

----------------------
# Awards ??

<img title=¡±aistar¡± src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6945783%2F9741af38375e23ef6abd1d2ab9107050%2Fobraz_2025-12-03_101843870.png?generation=1764753528479919&alt=media""; style=""float:right; height:200px; width:380px"">
The award ceremony and best teams presentations will take place during the Closing Day Ceremony during ESA AI STAR 2025, on Friday, 5th December 2025.

Please check the [Rules tab] (https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detect-leaking-data-hackathon/rules) for further details about eligibility and criteria.

----------------------
# Evaluation metric ?

Submissions are evaluated using the **averaged normalised Levenshtein distance** (also known as the edit distance) [3]. Normalisation is performed by dividing the classical edit distance by the length of the longer string. You can think about the classical edit distance as a number of operations (removing, swapping, and inserting) performed on individual characters to transform your answer into the correct solution. **Thus, if your answer is correct, the distance equals 0. If, on the other hand, nothing in your answer matches (everything should be either removed, swapped or inserted), the distance is equal to 1.**

**Tip:** Because the distance is averaged over all tasks (separately for public and private leaderboards), and the distance from the "" "" string to the correct answer is always 1, you can set all solutions to the "" "" string except the one you are currently solving and check if the metric is equal to ""1 - (1/N)"", where N is the number of tasks (N=2 for public leaderboard and N=5 for private leaderboard) . If this is true, you predicted this task correctly!

In the case of identical scores for two teams on the final leaderboard, priority will be given to the team that solved tasks with higher numbers (they are more difficult). Later, the faster team. [Resolved manually by the organisers.] ?

**Please have in mind, that the lower the score the better.**
----------------------
# Submission file ??

Provide a string with found hidden message for each index representing a file number from [Data tab](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detect-leaking-data-hackathon/data). In that way, you will provide answers for all five texts in first five rows of CSV. However, to enable initial results computation, please add answers to text 1 and 2 in rows 6 and 7, respectively (see example below). Also, the important part is that the value in the second column is of type string, which cannot be empty. You can also provide "" "" in the second column for test purposes (see right column for id=3). An example can be seen below:

| id | hidden_message|
| --- | --- |
|1|Answer to text 1|
|2|Answer to text 2|
|3| |
|4|Answer to text 4|
|5|Answer to text 5|
|6|Answer to text 1|
|7|Answer to text 2|

The file should be saved in CSV.
----------------------
# Timeline ?

* **Start Date** - 04 December 2025 at 09:50 AM
* **Entry deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final submission deadline** -   05 December 2025 at 8:00 AM
* **Private leaderboard release** -  during Award Ceremony

All deadlines are in CET on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if deemed necessary.
----------------------
# Organizers Team ?

The competition is organized on behalf of ESA (Evridiki Ntagiou) by [MI Space Team/ Centre for Credible AI Team from Warsaw University of Technology](http://credibleai.eu/) (Przemys?aw Biecek, Artur Janicki, Agata Kaczmarek, Dawid P?udowski and Piotr Wilczy¨½ski) and [KP Labs](https://www.kplabs.space) (Krzysztof Kotowski, Jakub Nalepa and Ramez Shendy). 
----------------------
# References ??

[1] E. Ntagiou, J. Eggleston, K. Cichecka, and P. Collins, ""DataX: a state of the art data strategy for mission operations"", in 75th International Astronautical Congress 2024. Available: https://iafastro.directory/iac/paper/id/89097/summary/

[2] K. Kotowski, P. Wilczy¨½ski, D. P?udowski, A. Kaczmarek, R. Shendy, J. Nalepa, P. Biecek, and E. Ntagiou, ""Towards Explainable and Secure AI for Space Mission Operations"", SpaceOps 2025. Available: https://star.spaceops.org/2025/user_manudownload.php?doc=150__traivhwa.pdf

[3] Levenshtein, Vladimir I. ""Binary codes capable of correcting deletions, insertions, and reversals."" Soviet physics doklady. Vol. 10. No. 8. 1966.",,False,True
10461,binary-classification-with-a-bank-database,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2025 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** Your goal is to predict whether a client will subscribe to a bank term deposit.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using [ROC AUC](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html) between the predicted value and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the probability of the binary target `y`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,y
    750000,0.5
    750001,0.5
    750002,0.5
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - ${competition.DateEnabled}
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  ${competition.Deadline}

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!",,False,True
10462,egci-486-quiz-2025,"# Overview

## Practice Project : Car Color Classification
|**Day-Time**	 | **Item** |
| --- | --- |
| Monday 24 November 2025 at 14:00 PM| Open |  |
| Monday 24 November 2025 at 16:00 PM| Close |  |


## Member

This competition is an individual format. All competitors must join the competition first.

## Leaderboards

Each group is allowed to send results 50 times per day. The score will be displayed into 2 parts:
- 50 percents of dataset will be displayed in the Public Leaderboard where the contestants can check their scores at any time.
- 50 percents of dataset will be stored and displayed in the Private Leaderboard which will be used to calculate the score to determine the result of the competition. The results will be displayed after the competition is over.


## Evaluation Metric
Metric: Categorization Accuracy

## Submission
To submit the results, the test set should be predicted class result and collected into "".CSV"" file as shown in the example file ""sample_submission.csv"", otherwise Kaggle will not be able to calculate submission score.
----------------------
# Description

<p>The Dataset consists of xxx high-quality images of vehicles, spanning multiple models and six distinct color categories: yellow, blue, orange, red, green, and pink, with each image carefully labeled to indicate its corresponding color class, making the dataset highly suitable for the development and evaluation of advanced vehicle color recognition systems that rely on accurate color classification. The dataset provides a diverse set of examples across different car models and colors, allowing researchers and developers to train machine learning models that can generalize well to real-world vehicle images in various environments and lighting conditions.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions are evaluated using categorization accuracy. Each image has been labeled with one true class. For each image, you must submit a set of the predicted labels.</p>

<p>Before we delve into the details of each of the accuracy score, it is important to cover the four building blocks used to define the evaluation metrics:</p>

- True Positive (TP) ¨C Actual label is positive and prediction is also positive
- True Negative (TN) ¨C Actual label is negative and prediction is also negative
- False Positive (FP) ¨C Actual label is negative but prediction is positive
- False Negative (FN) ¨C Actual label is positive but prediction is negative

![test](https://i.ibb.co/HnvkpfQ/Classification-Image.png)

<p>Accuracy is the most basic version of evaluation metrics. It is calculated as the ratio of correct predictions (TP + TN) over all the predictions made (TP + TN + FP + FN).</p>

![test](https://miro.medium.com/max/1210/1*N4Lo9Miw397g3XpX7o0CDw.png)

<h3>Submission File</h3>
You must submit a csv file with the image file name and class.

The 6 classes to predict are:

- yellow
- blue
- orange
- red
- green
- pink

<p>The order of the rows does not matter. The file must have a header and should look like the following:</p>

<pre>id,classes
1.jpg,blue
2.jpg,red
3.jpg,yellow
...
</pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>This is a datasets of car images  with labeled. This Data contains around 5k images distributed under 3 categories (yellow, blue, orange, red, green, pink).</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>sample-submission.csv</b> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><b>starter_code.ipynb</b> - a python code to process a feature extraction, model and submission file</li>
<li><b>./car_color/train</b> - the training set contain 6 folders (yellow, blue, orange, red, green, pink) contains 4k images</li>
<li><b>./car_color/test</b> - the test set image contains around 1.5k images</li>
</ul>",False,True
