10477,tnm-112-lab-2-2025,"# Overview

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F17779492%2F31e532b5c6b81d1da9b31c106debcacb%2Fpcam.jpg?generation=1700166771794534&alt=media)
The PCam (PatchCamelyon) medical image classification is a challenging task focused on the analysis of histopathologic scans of lymph node sections. The primary objective is to develop an automated method to detect metastatic tissue in these scans, which is a critical task in the diagnosis of breast cancer.

Participants are provided with a large dataset of small patches extracted from digital scans of lymph node sections. Each patch is labeled as either containing metastatic tissue or not. The challenge involves creating a model that can accurately classify these patches. This task is vital because detecting metastatic cancer in lymph nodes is a key factor in determining the stage of breast cancer, which directly influences treatment decisions and patient outcomes. In summary, the goal of the competition is to harness the power of machine learning in medical image analysis to improve the accuracy and efficiency of breast cancer diagnosis. 
----------------------
# Description

This section will introduce some general rules and terms. In this task, you will train a CNN to predict whether each image patch contains metastatic tissue. Of course, you can also predict them by yourself ??.

#**Requirements**
You need to have at least 5 valid submissions (i.e. better than randomly guessing) to pass this task, please describe what you did to improve your CNN on your lab report.

#**Discussion chanel**

You could discuss your solutions with others and ask questions in the Discussion section.

#**Leaderboard**

**Public leaderboard**: Public leaderboard provides a real-time ranking of participants based on the score, it allows participants to gauge their model's performance relative to others. However, your CNN model might ""overfit"" on Public leaderboard, it could be different that your rank on Public leaderboard and Private leaderboard. 30% of test set are used for Public leaderboard.

**Private leaderboard**: Private leaderboard in a machine learning competition provides a more accurate and unbiased assessment of the participants' models, ensuring that the winning solutions are robust, generalized, and effective on unseen data. 70% of test set are used for Private leaderboard.

The final rank will only depend on private leaderboard, you will not know private leaderboard rank until the date of private leaderboard released, but the public leaderboard is always visible.

#**Winners**
It¡¯s not mandatory for you to submit more than 5 valid submissions, but it could always be fun to explore more! Feel free to try, also top-3?????? teams will get some goodies!!! You are encouraged to use more techniques such as transfer learning, regularization, data augmentation, more sophisticated architectures, ensemble learning and whatever you can find from papers or internet. 


#**Beyond this competition...**

There are a lot of open competitions in different domains you could find in Kaggle, and you can win Kaggle Points or bounties from some competitions to become a [Kaggle Grand master or Master](https://www.kaggle.com/progression).  

----------------------
# Evaluation

Your submissions will be evaluated on [Balanced Accuracy Score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#balanced-accuracy-score) between the predicted results from your CNN and the actual labels.

Think about why [Balanced Accuracy Score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#balanced-accuracy-score) is more proper than [Accuracy Score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score)?

There is a script in your jupyter notebook to predict test set and generate a **.csv** file, you only need to upload the **.csv** to kaggle.

---




## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the **Class** variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,Class
    1,0
    2,1
    3,0
    
----------------------
# References

PCam dataset is from: Rotation Equivariant CNNs for Digital Pathology. 

Biblatex entry:
```
@ARTICLE{Veeling2018-qh,
  title         = ""Rotation Equivariant {CNNs} for Digital Pathology"",
  author        = ""Veeling, Bastiaan S and Linmans, Jasper and Winkens, Jim and
                   Cohen, Taco and Welling, Max"",
  month         =  jun,
  year          =  2018,
  archivePrefix = ""arXiv"",
  primaryClass  = ""cs.CV"",
  eprint        = ""1806.03962""
}
```","# Dataset Description

The PatchCamelyon (PCam) benchmark is a notable dataset in the field of medical image analysis, particularly for cancer diagnosis. It was derived from the Camelyon16 Challenge, which involved the detection of cancer metastases in lymph node sections using whole-slide images of breast cancer patients. The PCam dataset simplifies this task by providing a large set of small patches (96x96 pixels), each extracted from the whole-slide images.

Note that, we selected only a subsets from PCam benchmark and crop them to 32x32 pixels, which makes the dataset less computational demanding and more suitable for this lab.

## Files

*   **train.h5** - the training set contains 100000 patch images (32x32x3) and corresponding labels.
*   **valid.h5** - the validation set contains 32768 patch images (32x32x3) and corresponding labels.
*   **test_x.h5** - the test set contains 32768 patch images (32x32x3) and no labels.

How to open your data sets:
```
import h5py

with h5py.File('./patchcam/train.h5','r') as f:
                x_test = f['x'][:]
                y_test = f['y'][:]
```",False,True
10478,submission-2,"# Overview

Every Christmas Eve, Santa and his team of reindeer must deliver millions of gifts in a single night. But conditions vary from house to house: weather changes, chimney widths differ, GPS precision fluctuates and reindeer get tired.

To keep operations running smoothly, Santa¡¯s Workshop has launched its first machine learning initiative: a model that predicts whether a gift delivery attempt will succeed.

Your task is to build a classification model that predicts if the presents have been delivered for a house. 
Example : 
delivered_successfully ¡ú 1 = delivery success
                                            0 = delivery failed
----------------------
# Evaluation

Using features such as:

distance_km ¡ª travel distance to the home
chimney_width ¡ª how tight the landing zone is
weather_score ¡ª conditions like snow, wind, and visibility
reindeer_fatigue ¡ª how tired the reindeer team is
start_hour ¡ª the hour of delivery attempt
cookie_bribe_quality ¡ª quality of the treats left out
gps_precision ¡ª accuracy of sleigh navigation

?? Goal

Predict the probability of successful delivery. The competition¡¯s evaluation metric is:
 Correct predictions/Total predictions



	?","# Dataset Description

Dataset Columns: 
- distance_km: travel distance to the home
- chimney_width: how tight the landing zone is
- weather_score: conditions like snow, wind, and visibility
- reindeer_fatigue: how tired the reindeer team is
- start_hour:  the hour of delivery attempt
- cookie_bribe_quality: quality of the treats left out
- gps_precision:  accuracy of sleigh navigation

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


Sample_submission.csv should be in the form of: 
row_id,delivered_successfully
0,1
1,0
2,1
...
Predictions must be binary (0 or 1).
Probabilities will not be evaluated: round or threshold your model outputs.
The order of rows in your submission must match the row_id order in test.csv.",False,True
10479,the-gps-blackout-cv-challenge,"# Overview


>**March, 2125. RoboDeliver Inc.**
<br><p></p>

A catastrophic solar storm has devastated the global GPS, leaving navigation systems worldwide completely inoperative. The estimated recovery time is around **6-12 months minimum.** <br>
<p></p>

?? **Current infrastructure of city**
<br>
City planners have rapidly deployed a network of directional arrows across urban areas for easier navigation:
- **Daylight visibility:** Bright orange arrow clearly visible in sunlight
- **Night visibility:** The same arrow glows brilliant neon green in darkness for 24/7 operation
<p></p>

?? **The Mission**
As the Computer Vision Researcher at RoboDeliver Inc., you've been tasked with adjusting the robot navigation system for autonomous delivery by detecting the arrow directions. Although, the solve requires pretty challenging tasks mentioned in **Description**. Failure could leave millions without critical deliveries during this global crisis.<br>
>**The clock is ticking. The world is depending on you.**


----------------------
# Description

?? **Dataset**<br>
Your team has collected **20 reference images** of these navigation arrows under perfect daylight conditions and positions. Unfortunately, citywide curfews have prevented collection of nighttime training images.


![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5645642%2F91bb601f80db7fc6391d3aeb3584389a%2Farrow.png?generation=1758718842331837&alt=media)
Figure: *Sample of Training(DAY) and Testing(Day, Night, Road) images*

>**Real-World Complications:** Remember the training set contains perfect images. Real life arrows maybe a bit off-angled<br>

??**Extension:**
Classifying arrow images in day and night correctly results in 80% with 40% accuracy each. The rest 20% unlocks by correctly classifying images with no arrows as `road`.
<br><br>

?? **Task:**
Develop a robust image classification model that can:
* **Input:** 20 reference images of navigational arrows in perfect daylight conditions.
* **Output:** Directional classification into **8 directional categories and 1 conditional category:**
   * `north`, `south`, `east`, `west`
   * `north-east`, `south-east`, `north-west`, `south-west`
   * `road` (no arrow present)<br> <br>

??**Limitations:**
- Your solution cannot use extra images to train model. You are limited to 20 reference image.
- No manual data labeling and incorporation is allowed.

Also any pretrained models are allowed.

>May the AI forces be with you.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The public leader-board is based on the 30% of the test set and the private leaderboard is based on the rest 70% of the test set. Submissions are evaluated on F-1 score. According to metric documentation of Kaggle - 

```python
**F1 score**: The harmonic mean of the precision and recall, where an F1 score reaches its best value at 1 and worst score at 0.

The formula for the F1 score is::

F1 = 2 * (precision * recall) / (precision + recall)
```

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id, label
    1,south
    2,north-west
    3,road
    etc.",,False,True
