10488,2025-competition-apprentissage-i-3-d,"# Description

# Classification d'images biologiques FIB/SEM

## Challenge 
L'imagerie de microscopie ¨¦lectronique ¨¤ balayage ¨¤ faisceau d'ions focalis¨¦, ou FIB/SEM, est une modalit¨¦ permettant d'obtenir des images 3D ¨¤ tr¨¨s haute r¨¦solution. Elle combine un faisceau ionique focalise? (FIB) qui de?coupe une couche mince a? la surface d'un e?chantillon et un faisceau d¡¯e?lectrons a? balayage (SEM) qui image la face du bloc. Dans le contexte biologique, cette modalit¨¦ permet d¡¯imager des zones de 40 x 40 ¦Ìm avec une re?solution spatiale isotrope de 3 nm sur une profondeur de plusieurs dizaines de microme?tres, ce qui offre la possibilit¨¦ de visualiser de nombreux objets cellulaires. L'imagerie FIB/SEM produit des images de grande taille, de l'ordre de 2048 x 1536 x 500. L'annotation manuelle des diff¨¦rents objets biologiques contenus dans une telle image est tr¨¨s longue et fastidieuse : il est donc crucial de s'appuyer sur des techniques automatis¨¦es.

*Coupe d'une image FIB/SEM d'un ¨¦chantillon biologique. Les mitochondries sont annot¨¦es en trait noir*
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2812581%2F84af9f212e6ea9e8467e0a6dd0e6dc64%2Fmito.png?generation=1573221721186729&alt=media)

*En rouge : mitochondries. En vert : reticulum endoplasmique. En bleu : membrane nucl¨¦aire. En jaune : membrane cellulaire*
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2812581%2F11eea0e7245a9d59a4d19f0b2156198e%2Fall_classes.png?generation=1573236554246132&alt=media)


Dans ce contexte, ce challenge propose de classifier des imagettes 2D d'images FIB/SEM selon 5 classes : {autre, mitochondrie, r¨¦ticulum endoplasmique, membrane nucl¨¦aire, membrane cellulaire}. Plus pr¨¦cis¨¦ment, il s'agit d'identifier la classe du **pixel central** de l'imagette.

Les 5 classes ont pour identifiants :
- classe 0 : le pixel central n'appartient ¨¤ aucune classe annot¨¦e
- classe 1 : le pixel central appartient ¨¤ une mitochondrie
- classe 2 : le pixel central appartient ¨¤ un reticulum endoplasmique
- classe 3 : le pixel central appartient ¨¤ la membrane nucl¨¦aire (membrane d¨¦limitant l'int¨¦rieur du noyau)
- classe 4 : le pixel central appartient ¨¤ la membrane cellulaire (membrane d¨¦limitant l'int¨¦rieur de la cellule)


## Rendu
- notebook Kaggle ou notebook Colab : pensez ¨¤ me donner les droits
- votre code doit ¨ºtre comment¨¦ 
- compte-rendu de projet (int¨¦gr¨¦ dans le notebook) justifiant la m¨¦thode utilis¨¦e et les choix effectu¨¦s
### Remerciements
Nous remercions Patrick Schultz, responsable de l'¨¦quipe de biologie structurale de l'IGBMC, pour fournir ces images et accepter leur diffusion dans le cadre de ce challenge.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Score

Le score de votre soumission est ¨¦valu¨¦ en calculant le pourcentage d'imagettes classifi¨¦es correctement.

## R¨¨gles

- soumission d'un fichier **resultat.csv** donnant les r¨¦sultats de votre m¨¦thode de classification sur les 6000 images de l'ensemble de test
- le classement ""publique"" ¨¦value votre score ¨¤ partir de 30% des donn¨¦es de test
- le classement ""priv¨¦""  ¨¦valuera votre score ¨¤ partir de 70% des donn¨¦es restantes : vous n'aurez acc¨¨s ¨¤ ce classement qu'¨¤ la fin du challenge
- vous n'avez droit qu'¨¤ **deux** soumissions quotidiennes.

## Format de soumission

Le fichier contient une en-t¨ºte et 6000 lignes.
Il a le format suivant :
```
ID, Category
image_id1, category1
image_id2, category2
...
```","# Dataset Description

## Description des fichiers :
- **train.zip**  - archive contenant les 14000 images de l'ensemble d'entra?nement </li>
- **train.csv**  - fichier associant pour chaque image_id de l'ensemble d'entra?nement sa classe  </li>
- **test.zip** - archive contenant les 6000 images de l'ensemble de test </li>
- **sampleSubmission.csv** - un exemple de soumission dans le format correct</li>

## Champs
- *ID* - identifiant de l'image (associ¨¦ au nom du fichier <ID>.png)
- *Category* - classe de l'image {0,1,2,3,4}

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10489,Thrombus_challenge_1_1,"# Overview


# Introduction
Venous thromboembolism conditions, including deep vein thrombosis (DVT), are the third most common cause of vascular mortality worldwide after heart attack and stroke. Prompt diagnoses of DVT is essential to decrease the risk of fatal complications. Machine learning (ML) models have emerged as a valuable tool for assisting prompt diagnoses. These models, by performing pixel-wise segmentation in ultrasound videos, assess vein compressibility, an indicator of DVT or no-DVT.
Training such models, requires an enormous amount of effort for creating the ground truth datasets. Thereby, this **Data Challenge** aims to foster development of AI models for DVT detection using ultrasound videos without the need for exhaustive pixel-wise annotations, reducing the burden of manual labeling, while also enabling robust and clinically relevant predictions.    

This work is co-funded by the European Union, under the Horizon Europe Innovation Action ThrombUS+ (Grant Agreement No. 101137227). Views and opinions expressed are however those of the authors only and do not necessarily reflect those of the European Union or HADEA as the granting authority. Neither the European Union nor the grant-ing authority HADEA can be held responsible for them. 
----------------------
# Description

Challenge participants are invited to submit their responses to one or both of the respective data challenges in Kaggle, and submit a paper in the AI4DVT workshop in BIOSTEC conference (https://biostec.scitevents.org/AI4DVT.aspx).

BIOSTEC Important Dates:   
Paper submission: 8 January 2026.  
Author notification: 14 January 2026.  
Conference registration: 22 January 2026.  

Top 3-scoring submissions that will also submit a paper with their findings and methodology will be awarded a prize:    
**1st Place: 1500€**    
**2nd Place: 1000€**       
**3rd Place: 500€**   

The dataset consists of **compression ultrasound video scannings** of lower limbs collected during a multi-center cohort study in European Hospitals (Greece, France, Italy and Lithuania ([https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06989255](https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06989255)). Patients suspected of DVT are scanned using conventional ultrasound machines, after they had given informed consent form, according to a dedicated scanning protocol. All hospitals have been granted ethics approvals from their respective local ethics committees, prior enrollment and data collection.   

The first anatomical site refers near the inguinal ligament, where the common femoral vein (CFV) and the common femoral artery (CFA) are visualized. The second compression video is recorded a few centimeters distally, where the great saphenous vein is visible, branching off of the common femoral vein (CFV). The third compression video is acquired in the middle of the thigh, where the femoral vein is visible. The video from the fourth anatomical site is recorded below the knee, where compression of the popliteal vein (PV) is recorded. Videos were collected from the left (L), right (R) or both limbs, based on clinical symptoms.   

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7621325%2F37cdbaeec06e55eedaae3fcc5bf78064%2FPicture%201.svg?generation=1763636802684529&alt=media)    
Figure: The four anatomical sites of the right limb where ultrasound compression videos were recorded. Ultrasound probe (red rectangle) was positioned perpendicular to the respective veins and used for compression. CFV: common femoral vein, GSV: great saphenous vein, FV: femoral vein, Pop: popliteal vein.   

All videos have been anonymized prior uploading to the eCRF platform using an open-source software. All personal-related DICOM attributes were removed, videos were cropped to desired dimensions, and finally, for each DICOM file, a filename-based tag were added, for representing the anatomical site (CFV, GS, FV, PV) visualized and the laterality of the limb (L, R).   

All DICOM files and a structure report filled by the medical expert were stored in the eCRF platform, per patient. The structure report is based on the compression interpretation and diagnosis made by the medical expert for each available video recording. In the structure report medical experts rate the compressibility of the visualized veins (as No (0), Partial (1), Yes (2)) and the presence of thrombosis (as Yes (1) or No (0)). Consequently, the provided dataset for the Data Challenge is created by combining the filename-based tags with the structured report which assesses the compressibility and the presence of thrombosis. This information will serve as ground truth annotations of the compression ultrasound recordings to train artificial intelligence and machine learning models.   

The Data Challenge focuses on one clinically relevant task based on compression ultrasound recordings:   
**Predict DVT presence based on vein compressibility**.  

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Log Loss](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.log_loss.html) between the predicted probability and the observed target.

## Submission File
For each Id in the test set, you must predict a probability for the Thrombosis variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,Thrombosis
    file1.dcm,1
    file2.dcm,0.5
    etc.",,False,True
10490,Thrombus_challenge_1_2,"# Overview


# Introduction
Venous thromboembolism conditions, including deep vein thrombosis (DVT), are the third most common cause of vascular mortality worldwide after heart attack and stroke. Prompt diagnoses of DVT is essential to decrease the risk of fatal complications. Machine learning (ML) models have emerged as a valuable tool for assisting prompt diagnoses. These models, by performing pixel-wise segmentation in ultrasound videos, assess vein compressibility, an indicator of DVT or no-DVT.
Training such models, requires an enormous amount of effort for creating the ground truth datasets. Thereby, this **Data Challenge** aims to foster development of AI models for DVT detection using ultrasound videos without the need for exhaustive pixel-wise annotations, reducing the burden of manual labeling, while also enabling robust and clinically relevant predictions.    

This work is co-funded by the European Union, under the Horizon Europe Innovation Action ThrombUS+ (Grant Agreement No. 101137227). Views and opinions expressed are however those of the authors only and do not necessarily reflect those of the European Union or HADEA as the granting authority. Neither the European Union nor the grant-ing authority HADEA can be held responsible for them. 
----------------------
# Description

Challenge participants are invited to submit their responses to one or both of the respective data challenges in Kaggle, and submit a paper in the AI4DVT workshop in BIOSTEC conference (https://biostec.scitevents.org/AI4DVT.aspx).

BIOSTEC Important Dates:   
Paper submission: 8 January 2026.  
Author notification: 14 January 2026.  
Conference registration: 22 January 2026.  

Top 3-scoring submissions that will also submit a paper with their findings and methodology will be awarded a prize:    
**1st Place: 1500€**    
**2nd Place: 1000€**       
**3rd Place: 500€**

The dataset consists of **compression ultrasound video scannings** of lower limbs collected during a multi-center cohort study in European Hospitals (Greece, France, Italy and Lithuania ([https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06989255](https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06989255)). Patients suspected of DVT are scanned using conventional ultrasound machines, after they had given informed consent form, according to a dedicated scanning protocol. All hospitals have been granted ethics approvals from their respective local ethics committees, prior enrollment and data collection.   

The first anatomical site refers near the inguinal ligament, where the common femoral vein (CFV) and the common femoral artery (CFA) are visualized. The second compression video is recorded a few centimeters distally, where the great saphenous vein is visible, branching off of the common femoral vein (CFV). The third compression video is acquired in the middle of the thigh, where the femoral vein is visible. The video from the fourth anatomical site is recorded below the knee, where compression of the popliteal vein (PV) is recorded. Videos were collected from the left (L), right (R) or both limbs, based on clinical symptoms.   

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7621325%2F37cdbaeec06e55eedaae3fcc5bf78064%2FPicture%201.svg?generation=1763636802684529&alt=media)    
Figure: The four anatomical sites of the right limb where ultrasound compression videos were recorded. Ultrasound probe (red rectangle) was positioned perpendicular to the respective veins and used for compression. CFV: common femoral vein, GSV: great saphenous vein, FV: femoral vein, Pop: popliteal vein.   

All videos have been anonymized prior uploading to the eCRF platform using an open-source software. All personal-related DICOM attributes were removed, videos were cropped to desired dimensions, and finally, for each DICOM file, a filename-based tag were added, for representing the anatomical site (CFV, GS, FV, PV) visualized and the laterality of the limb (L, R).   

All DICOM files and a structure report filled by the medical expert were stored in the eCRF platform, per patient. The structure report is based on the compression interpretation and diagnosis made by the medical expert for each available video recording. In the structure report medical experts rate the compressibility of the visualized veins (as No (0), Partial (1), Yes (2)) and the presence of thrombosis (as Yes (1) or No (0)). Consequently, the provided dataset for the Data Challenge is created by combining the filename-based tags with the structured report which assesses the compressibility and the presence of thrombosis. This information will serve as ground truth annotations of the compression ultrasound recordings to train artificial intelligence and machine learning models.   

The Data Challenge focuses on clinically relevant task based on compression ultrasound recordings:   
**Identify the anatomical site**(CFV, GS, FV, PV).

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Accuracy Log](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score.html) between the predicted probability and the observed target.

## Submission File
For each Id in the test set, you must predict a label for the Anatomical Site variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,Anatomical Site
    file1.dcm, CFV
    file2.dcm, PV
    file3.dcm, FV
    file4.dcm, GS
    etc.",,False,True
10491,dsmu-kaggle-competition-prolonged,"# Overview

Every Christmas Eve, Santa and his team of reindeer must deliver millions of gifts in a single night. But conditions vary from house to house: weather changes, chimney widths differ, GPS precision fluctuates and reindeer get tired.

To keep operations running smoothly, Santa¡¯s Workshop has launched its first machine learning initiative: a model that predicts whether a gift delivery attempt will succeed.

Your task is to build a classification model that predicts if the presents have been delivered for a house. 
Example : 
delivered_successfully ¡ú 1 = delivery success
                                            0 = delivery failed


# Code to read in data 
train <- read.csv(""/kaggle/input/dsmu-kaggle-competition/train.csv"")
test <- read.csv(""/kaggle/input/dsmu-kaggle-competition/test.csv"")


Submission format : 
submission <- data.frame(
  row_id = 0:(nrow(test)-1), # has to be from 0 
  delivered_successfully = model output 
)
# write it to the output, row.names = FALSE is needed to get rid of the extra row names 1:2000
write.csv(submission, ""/kaggle/working/submission.csv"", row.names = FALSE) 
then download the csv file and upload on the right hand side section
----------------------
# Evaluation

Using features such as:

distance_km ¡ª travel distance to the home
chimney_width ¡ª how tight the landing zone is
weather_score ¡ª conditions like snow, wind, and visibility
reindeer_fatigue ¡ª how tired the reindeer team is
start_hour ¡ª the hour of delivery attempt
cookie_bribe_quality ¡ª quality of the treats left out
gps_precision ¡ª accuracy of sleigh navigation

?? Goal

Predict the probability of successful delivery. The competition¡¯s evaluation metric is:
 Correct predictions/Total predictions



	?","# Dataset Description

Dataset Columns: 
- distance_km: travel distance to the home
- chimney_width: how tight the landing zone is
- weather_score: conditions like snow, wind, and visibility
- reindeer_fatigue: how tired the reindeer team is
- start_hour:  the hour of delivery attempt
- cookie_bribe_quality: quality of the treats left out
- gps_precision:  accuracy of sleigh navigation

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


Sample_submission.csv should be in the form of: 
row_id,delivered_successfully
0,1
1,0
2,1
...
Predictions must be binary (0 or 1).
Probabilities will not be evaluated: round or threshold your model outputs.
The order of rows in your submission must match the row_id order in test.csv.",False,True
