10493,neural-machine-translator,"# Overview

The goal will be to train a deep learning network to translate from French to English.

We will use a dataset of French-English pairs from the nonprofit Tatoeba.

1. Either use RNN only (LSTM or GRU)
2. Or RNN and an attention + RNN decoder
3. Or just attention (GPT)

One of 2. or 3. is necessary for this homework.
----------------------
# Description

You can get inspiration from [NLP from scratch PyTorch tutorial part 3](https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/intermediate/seq2seq_translation_tutorial.html).

Check the [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/neural-machine-translator/data) tab for details.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Compute [BLEU score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BLEU) using torcheval's [bleu_score](https://meta-pytorch.org/torcheval/main/generated/torcheval.metrics.functional.bleu_score.html#torcheval.metrics.functional.bleu_score) with 4 n-grams.
    It is a weighted geometric mean of all the modified n-gram precisions, multiplied by the brevity penalty.

    >>> import pandas as pd
    >>> row_id_column_name = ""id""
    >>> y_pred = [""the squirrel is eating the nut"", ""the cat is on the mat""]
    >>> y_pred = pd.DataFrame(y_pred)
    >>> y_pred[""id""] = range(len(y_pred))
    >>> y_true = [""a squirrel is eating a nut|the squirrel is eating a tasty nut"", ""there is a cat on the mat|a cat is on the mat""]
    >>> y_true = pd.DataFrame(y_true)
    >>> y_true[""id""] = range(len(y_true))
    >>> score(y_true.copy(), y_pred.copy(), row_id_column_name)
    0.6534189

---

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
ID,TARGET
0,he s as strong as his teacher
1,he is what we call a pioneer
2,you re fussy i ve one
3,he is to working with you with you
4,she is no longer today
5,he s the man i want to be
6,you re the first i remember
7,he is the tallest one night early
8,she s my friend s friend
...
```","# Dataset Description

There is a training dataset `eng-fra.txt` with English-French pairs (no header, separated by tabs), and a test dataset `fra.csv` with only French sentences (header contains ""ID"" and ""fra"").
You have to provide a new submission file, comma-separated values, that contains two columns, ID and TARGET, with the translated sentences into English.

## Files

*   **eng-fra.txt** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `ID` - an ID of sentence
*   `fra`?- a sentence in French
*   `TARGET` - a sentence in English",False,True
10494,hourly-streamflow-forecasting-hackathon,"# Overview

Reliable short-term streamflow forecasts are critical for flood preparedness, reservoir operations, and water management. In this competition, your task is to build machine-learning models that predict hourly river discharge for six basins in central Texas using meteorological forcings and static physical attributes from the CAMELS-US Hourly dataset.

You will generate predictions for four lead times ¡ª
Q(t), Q(t+1), Q(t+2), and Q(t+3) ¡ª
and submit your results for evaluation on the leaderboard.

This challenge is part of the University of Texas at El Paso¡¯s Streamflow Forecasting Hackathon, designed to give participants hands-on experience with real hydrologic data, time-series modeling, and transfer learning.

Your objective is simple:
build the most accurate streamflow forecasting model.
Leaderboard rankings are based on Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).

**This material is based upon work supported by the National Science Foundation (NSF) under Grant No. 2239550 CAREER award, and the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) Cooperative Science Center Educational Partnership Program with Minority Serving Institutions (MSI) award NA22SEC4810016. The views and conclusions contained in this document are those of the authors and do not reflect the views of NSF and NOAA.**
----------------------
# Description

Accurate short-term streamflow forecasting is critical for flood early warning, reservoir management, urban stormwater control, and hydropower operations. In this competition, participants will work with hourly discharge data from USGS gauges and meteorological forcings from NLDAS to predict river flow in six basins in central Texas.

Streamflow is generated by complex hydrologic processes, which can be summarized by the continuity (mass-balance) equation:

$$
\frac{dS}{dt} = P - ET - Q - G
$$

here:
- ?? = water storage (soil, snow, groundwater, reservoirs, etc.)
- ?? = precipitation input
- ???? = evapotranspiration losses
- ?? = streamflow (surface runoff)
- ??= deep percolation or groundwater exchange

Traditionally, physics-based models simulate these processes, but machine learning offers an alternative by learning nonlinear rainfall¨Crunoff relationships directly from data.

Participants will build models to predict hourly streamflow for four lead times:

- Q(t) ¡ª current hour
- Q(t+1) ¡ª 1 hour ahead
- Q(t+2) ¡ª 2 hours ahead
- Q(t+3) ¡ª 3 hours ahead
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be scored using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE):

$$
RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \left( \hat{Q}_i - Q_i \right)^2 }
$$
 where,

- \(N =\) number of observations  
- \(\hat{Q}_i =\) predicted discharge for the \(i^{\text{th}}\) observation  
- \(Q_i =\) observed discharge for the \(i^{\text{th}}\) observation

Lower RMSE indicates better short-term streamflow predictions. Participants are encouraged to explore different model architectures, calibration strategies, and transfer learning techniques to maximize performance.",,False,True
10495,orbyx-ml-challenge-star-system-classification,"# Overview

## ?? Galaxy Explorer: Star System Classification

How well can you classify entire star systems using just 10 astronomical measurements?

Welcome to a synthetic yet ultra-realistic universe of **7,779 star systems** within 500 light-years ¡ª built from Kepler + Gaia physics rules.  
Every row is a complete stellar neighborhood: its star, planets, age, location in the Milky Way, and final scientific classification.

Your mission: **multi-class classification** ¡ª predict if a system is  
**Habitable** ???**Young** ???**Old** ??or **Exotic** ?

No images. No names. Just pure astrophysical parameters.

Accuracy scoring ? 4 balanced classes ? Perfect for feature engineering, categorical encoding, and tree-based models.

Are you ready to map the galaxy, one star system at a time?
----------------------
# Description

# ?? ORBYX ML Challenge: Star System Classification

## Background
Star systems display remarkable diversity, influenced by stellar mass, brightness, composition, and planetary architecture. These properties are often interdependent: massive stars tend to be brighter and hotter, metal-rich stars are more likely to host rocky planets, and galactic location affects stellar population age and composition.

This dataset captures a simplified yet structured version of these astrophysical relationships through **12 features**, including:

- **Stellar parameters:** size, brightness, mass, metallicity, spectral class, and stellar activity  
- **Planetary system properties:** dominant orbital configuration  
- **Galactic context:** galaxy region, distance from Earth, and galaxy morphology  

The goal is to predict the **type of star system** (`system_type`, 0¨C3) based solely on these measurable physical and categorical properties.

While synthetic, the dataset encodes **realistic correlations inspired by stellar evolution and galaxy formation**. Perfect accuracy is unlikely ¡ª only models that uncover the hidden structure will perform well.

---

## Task
**Multiclass classification:** Predict the `system_type` for each star system.

| Value | Meaning (abstracted) |
|-------|---------------------|
| 0     | Low-mass, simple planetary system |
| 1     | Medium-mass, mixed planetary system |
| 2     | High-mass or dynamically active system |
| 3     | Exotic or irregular system |

---



---

## Files
- **train.csv** ¨C Labeled star systems (includes `system_type`)  
- **test.csv** ¨C Unlabeled star systems for prediction  
- **sample_submission.csv** ¨C Example submission file showing correct format  

---
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric
**Accuracy** ¡ª the fraction of correctly classified star systems.  

All classes are reasonably represented, but the relationships between features and system type are **nonlinear and interdependent**, requiring models that can capture complex patterns.

## Submission File
```csv 

id,system_type

0,2

1,0

2,3
...","# Dataset Description

## Files
- **train.csv** ¨C 6456 labeled star systems (includes target)
- **test.csv** ¨C 1323 unlabeled star systems for prediction
- **sample_submission.csv** ¨C correct submission format

## Target
| Column        | Description                            | Values                     |
|---------------|----------------------------------------|----------------------------|
| `system_type` | Final scientific classification       | 0 = Habitable<br>1 = Young<br>2 = Old<br>3 = Exotic |

## Features (10 total)

### Stellar Characteristics
| Column                | Description                                                                 | Type      |
|-----------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|
| `star_size`| Relative radius of the star (normalized). Larger stars tend to have higher mass and brightness.| Float |                                       
| `star_brightness`     | Approximate luminosity measure of the star. | Float     |
| `stellar_activity_class`            | Activity level of the star (Low, Medium, High).                                         | Categorical|
| `star_spectral_class` | Simplified spectral class of the star (e.g., O, B, F, G).     | Categorical|
| `star_mass` | Stellar mass (synthetically correlated with size and brightness).      | Float |
| `metallicity`    | Abundance of heavy elements ¡ª critical for planet formation.                            | Float     |

### Planetary System Features
| Column                 | Description                                                    | Type      |
|------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|
| `planet_configuration` | Global structure of the planetary system (Rocky-dominated, Gas-giant-dominated, Compact, etc.)| Categorical |

### Galactic Context
| Column              | Description                                           | Type      |
|---------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|-----------|
| `galaxy_region`     |Encoded region index (0¨C2) representing varying population ages and metallicities.  | Integer   |
| `distance_from_earth`|Distance to the system (arbitrary units). Introduces observational variance and missingness.            | Float     |
| `galaxy_type`       | Morphological type of the host galaxy (0 = Spiral, 1 = Elliptical, 2 = Irregular). | Integer   |

Total: **7,779 realistic star systems** ¡ª perfectly balanced across the 4 classes.",False,True
10496,gnss-r-ddm-oil-spill-detection-thickness-inv,"# Overview

Marine oil spills pose major risks to ocean ecosystems and offshore operations. Traditional monitoring relies on optical or SAR remote sensing, but performance is limited by weather, illumination, and wind conditions.

GNSS-R (Global Navigation Satellite System Reflectometry) is a passive, all-weather microwave remote sensing technique that has rapidly advanced in ocean observation. Reflected GNSS signals contain information on sea-surface roughness and dielectric changes, making them highly sensitive to the smoothing effect caused by surface oil films.

This competition provides simulated and experimental GNSS-R Delay-Doppler Maps (DDMs) to advance automated oil-spill detection. Participants must determine the presence of oil and estimate film thickness using DDM images and limited environmental parameters.
----------------------
# Description

Participants must use DDM images and observation parameters to complete two tasks:

**Task 1: Ocean Oil Spill Detection (Classification)**

Determine whether the sea surface corresponding to the given DDM image contains an oil spill:

oil_exist = 0 (no oil)
oil_exist = 1 (oil present)

**Task 2: Oil Film Thickness Estimation (Regression)**

If an oil spill is detected, further predict the oil film thickness (unit: mm):

**Range: 0¨C5 mm
Thickness is 0 when no oil is present.**

This competition adopts a joint classification¨Cregression framework to better reflect real-world application scenarios.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The task uses a combined classification and regression score:
**Classification Metrics:**

1.F1 -score
F1 = 2 * Precision * Recall / (Precision + Recall)

2.Regression Metrics£ºMAE£¨Mean Absolute Error£©
MAE = average( abs(y_true - y_pred) )

3.Final Combined Score£º
The competition uses the following formula:
Score = 0.4 * F1 + 0.6 * (1 - MAE / 5)
Here, 5 is the maximum oil film thickness (0¨C5 mm).
A higher Score indicates better model performance.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains CYGNSS satellite GNSS-R observation data during the ""Symphony"" ship oil spill incident on April 30, 2021. The dataset is specifically designed for marine oil spill detection and oil film thickness estimation tasks, containing Delay-Doppler Map (DDM) observation data from 8 CYGNSS satellites throughout the day.

**Each NetCDF file contains:**
1. Raw DDM images: Power measurements of delay-Doppler matrix

2.Bistatic radar cross-section: Sea surface scattering characteristics

3.Geometric parameters: Satellite positions, incidence angles, reflection point coordinates, etc.

4.Timestamps: Precise observation time information

5.Quality flags: Data quality control parameters",False,True
10497,google-dev-fest-ourense-2025-llm-workshop,"# Overview

Hackathon pr¨¢ctica de LLMs en el Google DevFest Ourense donde cada participante construye su propio nanoGPT, lo entrena con un dataset elegido o curado, y compite maximizando una m¨¦trica compuesta 1¨C100 que premia calidad, eficiencia, regularizaci¨®n y frugalidad de datos. El evento favorece buenas pr¨¢cticas de entrenamiento reproducible, medici¨®n rigurosa y comparabilidad entre runs con una evaluaci¨®n autom¨¢tica tipo ¡°Kaggle metric¡± y leaderboard local.?

----------------------
# Description

Cada alumno implementa y entrena un nanoGPT minimalista con un pipeline claro (datos, tokens, entrenamiento, evaluaci¨®n) y registra sus runs en una hoja/tablero con m¨¦tricas estandarizadas. Se aceptan optimizaciones de arquitectura, regularizaci¨®n, curr¨ªculos de datos y trucos de rendimiento, siempre que se reporten los valores requeridos por la m¨¦trica y se pueda replicar el mejor run enviado.?
La din¨¢mica incluye una sesi¨®n de arranque, mentoring t¨¦cnico y cierre con anuncio del leaderboard, en el campus de la Escola Superior de Enxe?ar¨ªa Inform¨¢tica, con horario y log¨ªstica publicados en los canales de GDG Ourense/ESEI.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La puntuaci¨®n final es un ¨²nico n¨²mero entre 1 y 100 obtenido como el m¨¢ximo entre todas las filas de tu submission, es decir, se toma tu mejor run registrado en el CSV. Cada run aporta las siguientes columnas num¨¦ricas obligatorias: val_perplexity, num_parameters, tokens_per_sec, training_time_minutes, val_minus_train, total_tokens_processed. Cualquier columna faltante, no num¨¦rica o con valores no positivos (salvo val_minus_train, que debe ser ¡Ý 0) invalida la submission con un error visible para participantes.?
La m¨¦trica compuesta suma 4 componentes min¨Cmax normalizados con anclas ¡°good/bad¡± opcionales que pueden definirse en una fila de solution, o se usan valores por defecto robustos si faltan. Los pesos tambi¨¦n son ajustables y se renormalizan a 1 si est¨¢n presentes, con defaults w_quality=0.6, w_efficiency=0.2, w_regularization=0.1, w_data=0.1.?

Calidad (quality): se incentiva perplexity de validaci¨®n baja; se normaliza entre p_bad y p_good, y se recorta a. La perplexity es una medida est¨¢ndar basada en log-likelihood promedio por token y su exponente, ampliamente usada para LMs.?

Eficiencia (efficiency): promedio de tres subcomponentes normalizados y recortados a : menos par¨¢metros (num_parameters), m¨¢s velocidad de generaci¨®n/entrenamiento (tokens_per_sec) y menor tiempo total de entrenamiento (training_time_minutes).?

Regularizaci¨®n (regularization): gap de generalizaci¨®n bajo, medido como val_minus_train; se normaliza entre vtgap_bad y vtgap_good, favoreciendo menores gaps.?

Frugalidad de datos (data_eff): menos tokens totales procesados (total_tokens_processed) medidos en escala logar¨ªtmica y normalizados entre tokens_bad y tokens_good; promueve aprender m¨¢s con menos.","# Dataset Description

TinyShakespeare es un corpus de ~40.000 l¨ªneas de obras de William Shakespeare en texto plano, popularizado por los tutoriales de modelos de lenguaje a nivel car¨¢cter de Andrej Karpathy; su tama?o total ronda 1¨C1.1 MB, lo que permite entrenar prototipos de nanoGPT r¨¢pidamente en CPU/GPU modestas. Suele distribuirse como un ¨²nico archivo input.txt con el texto completo, y muchas implementaciones lo exponen en tres ¡°splits¡± simb¨®licos (train/validation/test) con una ¨²nica entrada por split que contiene todo el texto.?

Contenido y formato?
El archivo incluye di¨¢logos y acotaciones de m¨²ltiples obras, con nombres de personajes seguidos de sus l¨ªneas y saltos de l¨ªnea que preservan estructura teatral; un ejemplo inicial es ¡°First Citizen:\nBefore we proceed any further, hear me ¡­¡±. El dataset se entrega como una columna ¡°text¡± de tipo string, sin etiquetas adicionales, pensado para modelado autoregresivo de siguiente car¨¢cter o siguiente token tras tokenizaci¨®n.?

Tareas recomendadas?

Modelado a nivel car¨¢cter: dividir el texto en secuencias y entrenar a predecir el siguiente car¨¢cter, lo que simplifica vocabulario y facilita prototipos educativos.?

Modelado a nivel subpalabra/palabra: aplicar un tokenizador BPE o similar para entrenar un GPT peque?o con bloques de contexto fijos y evaluaci¨®n por perplexity en validaci¨®n.?

Tama?o y splits?
El tama?o de descarga y del dataset es aproximadamente 1.06¨C1.11 MB, con un total ocupado en disco cercano a 2.23 MB en algunos empaquetados. En cat¨¢logos comunes se ofrece con tres splits (train/validation/test), cada uno con una ¨²nica ¡°ejemplo¡± que contiene todo el texto, pr¨¢ctica que facilita pipelines simples de corte en ventanas.?

Ventajas para la hackathon?

Ciclos r¨¢pidos: su tama?o peque?o permite iterar hiperpar¨¢metros, arquitecturas y regularizaci¨®n con entrenos de minutos, ideal para comparar runs en un leaderboard.?

Reproducibilidad: al ser un est¨¢ndar de facto en demos de nanoGPT/char-rnn, existen scripts y repositorios de referencia que facilitan baselines y comparaciones justas.?

Limitaciones y buenas pr¨¢cticas?

Escala y diversidad: el corpus es peque?o y estil¨ªsticamente homog¨¦neo (ingl¨¦s isabelino), por lo que conviene evitar conclusiones generales sobre escalado o capacidades fuera del dominio.?

Evaluaci¨®n: usar un split de validaci¨®n consistente y registrar la perplexity de validaci¨®n y el gap val?train para medir generalizaci¨®n; documentar c¨®mo se cuentan tokens si se emplea tokenizaci¨®n subpalabra.?",False,True
