     alt=""MAMBO Logo"" height=""50""/ style=""margin-right: 20px;""><img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2005261%2Ffc625ee9a660525331f6783b5e913368%2Finbox_1518097_5d2e4ae70449c12176fd37926fb5e852_guarden_logo.png?generation=1736699711069598&alt=media"" 
     alt=""Guarden Logo"" height=""50""/ style=""margin-right: 20px;""><img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2005261%2F628711fef0289bb601ca15db9d5c52a6%2Finbox_1518097_c44b08f2cabdfc04178c475dada7dbe5_logo-ec--en.svg?generation=1736699740361087&alt=media"" 
     alt=""UE Logo"" height=""50"" style=""margin-right: 20px;""/>","# Dataset Description

Note on Data Versions: To allow for historical comparison, this 2026 edition uses the same datasets as the previous rounds. Consequently, files and folders retain their original ""2024"" or ""2025"" names.

Please note: Only the test set and metadata are hosted directly on Kaggle (~8.3 GB). The images of the training sets (>160 GB) must be downloaded via the external links provided below.

**Training set: individual plant images**

The training set is conventionally composed of observations of individual plants, such as those used in previous editions of PlantCLEF. More precisely, it is a [subset of the Pl@ntNet training data](https://identify.plantnet.org/k-southwestern-europe/species) focusing on south western Europe and covering 7.8k plant species. It contains about 1.4 million images extended with some images with trusted labels aggregated from the [GBIF platform](https://www.gbif.org/) to complete the less illustrated species. This dataset was used in the [PlantCLEF 2024 competition](https://www.imageclef.org/PlantCLEF2024) and [PlantCLEF 2025 competition](https://www.imageclef.org/PlantCLEF2025), and no additional data has been added for this year (the dataset remains named 2024).

In the corresponding metadata file (PlantCLEF2024singleplanttrainingdata.csv), the ¡®url¡¯ column contains links to the original images. The images can also be downloaded via the two links below for your convenience. The images have a relatively high resolution (minimum or maximum of 800 pixels on the biggest side) to allow the use of classification models that can handle relatively large resolution inputs and may reduce the difficulty of predicting small plants in large vegetative plot images.

[Training set (images) - Min side size - 800px [~281GB]](https://lab.plantnet.org/LifeCLEF/PlantCLEF2024/single_plant_training_data/PlantCLEF2024singleplanttrainingdata.tar)

[Training set (images) - Max side size - 800px [~160GB]](https://lab.plantnet.org/LifeCLEF/PlantCLEF2024/single_plant_training_data/PlantCLEF2024singleplanttrainingdata_800_max_side_size.tar)

It should be noted that these images are pre-organized into subfolders by class (i.e., by species_id) and split into train/validation/test sets to facilitate the training of individual plant classification models, particularly those shared with the participants below. The images can be gathered into a single 'train' directory if a participant wishes to use all the training images, or they can perform their own train/validation/test splits of courses

The species_ids.csv file contains the official list of all taxon IDs.


**Complementary training set: high-resolution pseudo quadrat images without labels**

The motivation behind this second dataset is to help models better adapt to multi-species vegetation quadrat images. Ideally, a large number of annotated multi-species quadrat images would be available for training, but such data is scarce. Instead, we provide a large, unannotated dataset of pseudo-quadrat images¡ªground vegetation images framed similarly to vertical quadrats, but not necessarily covering a precise 50 cm ¡Á 50 cm area.  

Despite the lack of annotations, this dataset can still be valuable for self-supervised pre-training, helping models become more familiar with the pseudo-quadrat domain before fine-tuning on labeled individual plant images. While integrating it effectively remains challenging, we hypothesize that it can help reduce the domain shift between training and test data in the challenge.  

This second dataset is derived from the [LUCAS Cover Photos 2006¨C2018 collection](https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/14/4463/2022/) and consists of **212,782 pseudo-quadrat images**, selected using a dedicated model trained to identify such views (listed in the pseudoquadrats_without_labels_complementary_training_set_urls.csv file). For your convenience the images can be directly downloaded from this [remote tar file - [~170GB]](https://lab.plantnet.org/LifeCLEF/PlantCLEF2025/pseudoquadrats_without_labels_complementary_training_set/PlantCLEF2025_pseudoquadrats_without_labels_complementary_training_set.tar)


**Test set: vegetation quadrat images**
The test set is a compilation of several image datasets of plots in different floristic contexts, including Pyrenean and Mediterranean floras. These datasets were all produced by experts and consist of a total of 2105 high-resolution images. The shooting protocol can vary significantly from one context to another: the use of wooden frames or measuring tape to delimit the plot or not, angles of view more or less perpendicular to the ground. Additionally, the quality of the images may vary depending on the weather, which can result in more or less pronounced shadows, blurry areas, etc. The test set remains the same during the last year challenge.
The file PlantCLEF2025_test.csv gives some metadata related to the quadrat images inside the PlantCLEF2025test directory. Alternatively, the test dataset can be found [here](https://lab.plantnet.org/LifeCLEF/PlantCLEF2025/vegetation_plot_test_data/PlantCLEF2025test.tar)


**Pre-trained models**
For participants who may have difficulty finding the computational power necessary to train a plant image identification model on such a large volume of data, or to enable direct work on a multi-class method, we have trained 2 state-of-the-art models using the renowned 'timm' library, now hosted on Hugging Face. Both pre-trained models are based on a Vit base architecture pre-trained with the self-supervised learning approach DINO V2 on 142M images.

[Available on zenodo](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.10848263) or directly from the Models tab of the competition, respectively named:
+ dinov2_patch14_reg4_onlyclassifier_then_all
+ dinov2_patch14_reg4_dinov2_onlyclassifier

