10537,riva-cervical-cytology-challenge-track-b-isbi,"# Overview

### FINAL EVALUATION CHALLENGE LAUNCHED ??
The final evaluation phase has launched. Participants will have from February 13th to February 20th to submit their predictions on the final test sets. Please remember that participation in this phase and paper submission are mandatory for presentation at ISBI 2026 and prize awards.
You can access the new challenge through <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/riva-cervical-cytology-challenge-track-b-isbi-final-evaluation"">this</a> link.

Early and accurate detection of abnormal cervical cells is essential for preventing cervical cancer in women, particularly in regions where access to expert cytotechnologists is limited. The RIVA Cervical Cytology Challenge invites the global machine learning community to advance automated analysis of Pap smear images using a large, expert-annotated dataset. On this page, you will find the description and leaderboard for Competition **Track B:Cell detection**. For more information, or to view the Track A leaderboard, please refer to the challenge website: [https://lia-ditella.github.io/rivachallenge/](https://lia-ditella.github.io/rivachallenge/)
----------------------
# Description

In Track B, participants must develop models capable of localizing cells in Pap smear images using bounding boxes. Only cells belonging to the Bethesda categories (NILM, ASCUS, LSIL, HSIL, ASC-H, SCC, INFL, ENDO) will be considered, although class labels are not evaluated in this track.

All images must be downloaded from the **Data Section**.

---

## ?? Submission Rules

Each team may submit **up to 5 prediction files per day**.

All submissions must be provided in **CSV format** and must contain **every detected cell for the entire test set**.  
Your CSV must follow the structure described below.

---

## ?? Submission File Format

Your submission must include the following columns:

- **id**: A unique identifier for each prediction.  
  This value is not matched against the ground truth; it only needs to be unique within the file.

- **image_filename**: Name of the test image to which the detection belongs.

- **x**: x-coordinate of the **center** of the bounding box.

- **y**: y-coordinate of the **center** of the bounding box.

- **width**: Width of the bounding box.

- **height**: Height of the bounding box.

- **conf**: Confidence score assigned to the detection.

- **class**: This track is **single-class**.  
  The `class` column **must be included and set to 0 for *all* predictions**, even though it is not used for scoring.

> Your file may contain **any number of rows**, depending on how many detections your model outputs.

---

## ?? Example Submission

A valid submission file should look like this:

| id | image_filename | class | x     | y     | width | height | conf |
|----|----------------|-------|-------|-------|--------|--------|------|
| 0  | test_001.png   | 0     | 512.0 | 430.0 | 120.0  | 140.0  | 0.92 |
| 1  | test_001.png   | 0     | 300.0 | 200.0 | 80.0   | 90.0   | 0.78 |
| 2  | test_002.png   | 0     | 256.0 | 256.0 | 100.0  | 110.0  | 0.85 |
| 3  | test_003.png   | 0     | 600.0 | 350.0 | 150.0  | 160.0  | 0.67 |
| 4  | test_003.png   | 0     | 420.0 | 500.0 | 90.0   | 95.0   | 0.74 |
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on mean Average Precision ([mAP@0.50:0.95](https://lightning.ai/docs/torchmetrics/stable/detection/mean_average_precision.html)). Leaderboard rankings are determined solely based on this metric.

The full implementation of the evaluation metric is available in [this Github repository](https://github.com/LIA-DiTella/RIVA-CCC-ISBI2026)

To experiment with the metric on your own validation data, you can refer to the `demo.ipynb` notebook  
or review the sample evaluations provided.","# Dataset Description

Below you will find all the information you need regarding the data used in this competition.

---

## ?? Data Overview

All the data required to participate in this competition is available directly in this section.  
Participants must download the files provided here to train their models and generate predictions.

There are **two files to download**:

- `annotations.zip`
- `images.zip`

---

## ?? `annotations.zip`

This file contains **three CSV files**:

- `train.csv`
- `val.csv`
- `sample_submission.csv`

### **`train.csv` and `val.csv` ¡ª Ground Truth Annotations**

These files contain the **bounding box annotations from the official RIVA Dataset**, and should be used for **training and validation**.

Each row corresponds to a detected cell, including its image identifier and bounding box coordinates.  
Participants are free to preprocess or convert these annotations to any format required by their models.

---

### **`sample_submission.csv` ¡ª Submission Format Example**

This file provides a **minimal example** of the required submission format.

It contains a small number of **dummy bounding box predictions**, intended solely to illustrate:

- the expected column names  
- the structure of a valid submission file  

Your actual submission:

- may contain **more or fewer rows** than this example  
- but must follow **exactly the same column structure**

We strongly recommend using this file as a starting point when generating your final submission.

---

## ??? `images.zip`

This file contains **all the images** used in the competition, organized into three folders:

- `train/` ¡ª images corresponding to `train.csv`
- `val/` ¡ª images corresponding to `val.csv`
- `test/` ¡ª images **without annotations**, used for inference

Participants must run their trained models on the images in the `test/` folder and submit their predictions following the format specified in `sample_submission.csv`.",False,True
10538,iris-classification-challenge,"# Overview

Welcome to the **Iris Species Classification Challenge!**
This challenge is designed for beginners and intermediate learners exploring Decision Trees and Random Forests, two of the most important algorithms in machine learning.

Your task is simple but fundamental:

Predict the species of Iris flowers based on their physical measurements.

You will use four numerical features¡ªsepal length, sepal width, petal length, and petal width¡ªto classify each flower into one of three species:

- Iris setosa
- Iris versicolor
- Iris virginica

This dataset is widely used for teaching and benchmarking classification models. It is small, clean, and interpretable¡ªperfect for experimenting with:

- Decision Trees (Entropy, Gini)
- Random Forests
- Model tuning (max_depth, min_samples_split, etc.)
- Visualization of decision boundaries

Whether you are new to ML or revisiting the basics, this competition is a great way to sharpen your skills while enjoying
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using **Accuracy**:

Accuracy = Number of correct predictions/Total predictions


## Submission File
The submission file must contain two columns::

    Id,species
    0,setosa
    1,virginica
    2,versicolor
    etc.


- `Id` must match the `Id` in `test.csv`
- `species` must be one of: `setosa`, `veriscolor`, `virginica`

A sample submission file is provided","# Dataset Description

The dataset includes training and test sets, plus a submission example.

## Files

**train.csv**: Includes features and the target label species.

**test.csv**: Includes only features; your model must predict species.

**sample_submission.csv**: Shows the required submission structure.

| Column         | Description                                                        |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `sepal_length` | Sepal length in centimeters                                        |
| `sepal_width`  | Sepal width in centimeters                                         |
| `petal_length` | Petal length in centimeters                                        |
| `petal_width`  | Petal width in centimeters                                         |
| `species`      | Target class (only in train) ¡ª `setosa`, `versicolor`, `virginica` |




All features are numeric.


There are no missing values.",False,True
10539,binary-machine-learning-contest,"# Overview

**Welcome to the Binary Machine Learning Contest organized by Machine Learning club - Uttara University !** 

**Your Goal:** The goal of this competition is to predict whether a mushroom is edible or poisonous based on its physical characteristics.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the [Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phi_coefficient).

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict target `class`, whether the observation is editable (`e`) or poisonous (`p`). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,class
    3116945,e
    3116946,p
    3116947,e
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the UCI [Mushroom](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/73/mushroom) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

**Note:** Unlike many previous Tabular Playground datasets, data artifacts _have not_ been cleaned up. There are categorical values in the dataset that are not found in the original. It is up to the competitors how to handle this.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `class` is the binary target (either `e` or `p`)
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict target `class` for each row
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10540,stat-learning-multivariate-linear-regression-mpg,"# Overview

Predict MPG using linear regression based on vehicle technical specifications. This competition uses noise-augmented data derived from the Auto dataset from the UCI Machine Learning Repository.
----------------------
# Description

You are given vehicle specifications such as cylinders, displacement, horsepower, weight, acceleration, model year, and origin. Your task is to build a linear regression model to predict continuous MPG values. Missing horsepower values in the evaluation set were filled using researched approximations.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).

RMSE = sqrt((1/n) * ¦²(predicted - actual)^2). Lower scores indicate better predictive accuracy.

----------------------
# Section 3

Submit a CSV file with the following structure:

```
ID,mpg
1,23.5
2,31.2
3,18.9
```

`ID` must match the provided identifiers. `mpg` should contain your predicted continuous values.","# Dataset Description

# Data Description

This competition uses a modified version of the **Auto MPG** dataset from the UCI Machine Learning Repository.  
The data consists of technical specifications of cars along with fuel efficiency.  
To prevent overfitting, the competition test set includes **noise-added values**, and any originally missing horsepower values have been replaced using **researched approximations**.

Participants should be able to answer after reading this page:

- What files do I need?
- What is the data format?
- What am I predicting?
- What acronyms will I encounter?

---

## Files

- **train.csv** ¡ª training set with features and the regression target (`mpg`)
- **test.csv** ¡ª test set with the same features but without the target
- **sample_submission.csv** ¡ª example submission in the correct format

---

## Columns

### Identifier
- `ID` ¡ª unique identifier for each row; required in submissions

### Features
- `cylinders` ¡ª number of engine cylinders  
- `displacement` ¡ª engine displacement (cubic inches)  
- `horsepower` ¡ª horsepower; missing values imputed using researched approximations  
- `weight` ¡ª vehicle weight (lbs)  
- `acceleration` ¡ª 0¨C60 mph acceleration time (seconds)  
- `year` ¡ª model year (two-digit)  
- `origin` ¡ª origin code: 1 = USA, 2 = Europe, 3 = Japan  
- `name` ¡ª car name (string; often unique), ideally not used in training only for identification

### Target
- `mpg` ¡ª miles per gallon (continuous value)


---

## Acronyms

- **MPG** ¡ª Miles Per Gallon  
- **RMSE** ¡ª Root Mean Squared Error (evaluation metric)",False,True
