10548,binary-classification-of-insurance-cross-selling,"# Overview

We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** The objective of this competition is to predict which customers respond positively to an automobile insurance offer.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) using the predicted probabilities and the ground truth targets.

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict the probability of the target, `Response`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Response
    11504798,0.5
    11504799,0.5
    11504800,0.5
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - November 30, 2025
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  December 31, 2025

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Health Insurance Cross Sell Prediction Data](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/annantkumarsingh/health-insurance-cross-sell-prediction-data) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

This [notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/annantkumarsingh/health-insurance-cross-sell-prediction/notebook) gives more details about the dataset used for this competition.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `Response` is the binary target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of `Response` for each row
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10549,onia-2,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the quality of wine based on various properties. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored based on the?quadratic weighted kappa, which measures the agreement between two outcomes. This metric typically varies from 0 (random agreement) to 1 (complete agreement). In the event that there is less agreement than expected by chance, the?metric may go below 0.

The quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as follows. First, an N?x?N histogram matrix <em>O</em> is constructed, such that <em>O<sub>i,j</sub></em> corresponds to the number of `Id`s <em>i</em> (actual) that received a predicted value <em>j</em>.?An <em>N-by-N </em>matrix of weights, <em>w</em>, is calculated based on the difference between actual and predicted values:

$$w_{i,j} = \frac{\left(i-j\right)^2}{\left(N-1\right)^2}$$

An <em>N-by-N</em> histogram matrix of expected outcomes, <em>E</em>, is calculated assuming that there is no correlation between values.? This is calculated as the outer product between the actual histogram vector of outcomes and the predicted histogram vector, normalized such that <em>E</em> and <em>O</em> have the same sum.

From these three matrices, the quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as:?

$$\kappa=1-\frac{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}O\_{i,j}}{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}E\_{i,j}}.$$


## Submission File
For each `Id` in the test set, you must predict the value for the target `quality`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,quality
    2056,5
    2057,7
    2058,3
    etc.",,False,True
10550,martingale-hacks-winter-2025,"# Overview

#### **The Signal in the Noise**
Wall Street doesn't run on gut feeling. It runs on unstructured data, stochastic calculus, and massive compute.

**The Martingale** is the first dedicated Quantitative Finance competition for elite high school engineers. We are looking for the signal in the noise. Your objective is not to guess if the market will go up or down, but to predict **Magnitude**¡ªthe raw energy of the market.

#### **The Challenge: Volatility Forecasting**
Volatility is the price of risk. When the market panics, volatility spikes. When the market sleeps, volatility decays.
You will be provided with a 10-year dataset containing **S&P 500 price history** and **financial news headlines**.

**Your Directive:**
Build a model that digests this information to predict the **Absolute Percentage Return** of the SPY ETF for the *next* trading session.

#### **The Data**
To prevent look-ahead bias, we have engineered an institutional-grade dataset:
*   **Time-Series:** 100 days of pre-computed price lags.
*   **NLP:** 10+ years of financial headlines, time-shifted to ensure no data leakage.
*   **The Trap:** The dataset spans three distinct market regimes:
    *   **The Playground (Train):** Post-Recession Recovery.
    *   **The Bull (Public Test):** Low volatility, steady growth.
    *   **The Crash (Private Test):** High volatility, crisis events (COVID, Flash Crashes).

### **Strategy**
Use custom torchfbm pip package: https://github.com/Coder9872/torchfbm
to increase robustness and learn market dynamics
*Warning: A model that overfits to the Bull Market will fail during the Crash. Build for robustness.*

#### **Awards & Career Capital**
*   **The Dossier:** The top 10 finalists will have their code and resumes compiled into an Investment Memo forwarded to our university and industry partners.
*   **The Compute:** Winners receive institutional-grade API access from *Wolfram Alpha*.


----------------------
# Description

### **. Rules **

#### **Eligibility**
*   Open to all high school students (or international equivalents, approx. ages 13-19).
*   Teams of 1-4 members.
*   Undergraduates may participate as ""Mentors"" but are ineligible for the Top 10 Dossier.

#### **Code & Integrity**
*   **No Manual Trading:** All predictions must be generated by an automated algorithm.
*   **Open Source:** To claim a leaderboard spot, you must make your Kaggle Kernel **Public** at the end of the competition.
*   **External Data:** You may use external pre-trained models (e.g., BERT, FinBERT) but **you may not use external price/news data**. The provided dataset is the only source of truth.

#### **The Leaderboard Split**
*   **Public Leaderboard (30%):** Visible during the competition.
*   **Private Leaderboard (70%):** Hidden until the competition closes.
*   *Strategy Note:* The Private leaderboard contains the most volatile market periods. Optimize for this.

The use of external market data (e.g., downloading SPY history from Yahoo Finance during inference) is prohibited. Your model must generate predictions solely based on the features provided in test.csv (Headlines + Lags).
---


### **. Dataset Description**


*   `Date`: The trading session date (YYYY-MM-DD).
*   `Headlines`: The top 20 news headlines available *before* market open.
*   `Return_Lag_1` ... `Return_Lag_100`: The daily percentage return of SPY for the previous 100 days.
*   `Target`: The Absolute Percentage Return of the *current* session. (Target = `100 * |(Close-Open)/Open|`).

---
----------------------
# Evaluation

#### **The Metric**
Submissions are evaluated on **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)**.
This metric penalizes large errors heavily. In volatility forecasting, missing a massive crash is far worse than missing a quiet day.

$$RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2}$$

*   **y**: The Actual Volatility (Absolute % Return).
*   **y_hat**: Your Predicted Volatility.

#### **Submission File**
For each `Date` in the test set, you must predict the `Target` variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```csv
Date,Target
2013-01-02,0.85
2013-01-03,1.20
2013-01-04,0.45
...
```
*   `Target` represents the absolute percentage move. (e.g., `1.5` means the market moved 1.5% that day).",,False,True
10551,gene-activation-anova-2025,"# Overview

Wakanda has become the world¡¯s first fully gene-regulated nation. Using futuristic biotechnology, its scientists can activate or suppress individual genes in living humans. However, the country has limited funding and cannot run large-scale experiments on every gene.

As the Chief Data Scientist of Wakanda, your mission is to **predict which genes meaningfully improve human health** ¡ª before the nation commits to costly experiments. Your compensation is determined entirely by the accuracy of your recommendations, so statistical rigor is not optional.

Your task is to assign a probability to each gene representing how likely it is that activating or suppressing the gene improves the wellbeing index by **at least 1 point**.
----------------------
# Description

Wakanda has conducted a preliminary observational study on 1,000 citizens. For each individual, you are given:

* A wellbeing index score
* Activation status for 500 different genes (0 = inactive, 1 = active)
* Demographic variables: sex, age, height, and weight

Unfortunately, Wakanda cannot experiment on all genes. Instead:

* They will run targeted experiments later.
* Each gene will be labeled as:

  * 1 if intervention changes wellbeing by ¡Ý 1
  * 0 if intervention changes wellbeing by < 1

Please note that while combining the suppression or activation of multiple genes can impact the well-being index, Wakanda's government focuses solely on the effects of activating or suppressing one gene at a time.

Your job is to **forecast these outcomes now**.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Predictions are evaluated using **Mean Absolute Error (MAE)** between your submitted probabilities and the true experimental outcomes.

For each gene:

* The true label is:

  * 1 if the gene¡¯s experimental effect is ¡Ý 1
  * 0 if the effect is < 1
* Your prediction is a probability between 0 and 1.

The evaluation metric is:

$$ MAE = \frac{1}{\text{# of Genes}} \sum_{i=1}^{\text{# of Genes}} |\text{True-Label}_i - \text{Probability}_i| $$

This scoring rule is a **strictly proper scoring rule**, meaning that you minimize expected loss by reporting your true estimated probability. Overconfident or underconfident predictions are penalized equally.

Your grade will be computed using the following formula:

$$\max\left(0, \left(2 - \frac{\text{Your Score}}{\text{Minimum Score}}\right) * 100\right)$$

## Submission File

You must submit a CSV file with exactly two columns:

* `colID`: The gene identifier.
* `probability`: Your predicted probability that the gene improves wellbeing by at least one point.

Each row corresponds to one gene.

Example submission:

    ColID, probability
    1, 0.83
    2, 0.12
    3, 0.56
    etc.",,False,True
