10557,ioai-2025-gaite-task-5,"# Description

Make a classifier to distinguish between synthetic (AI-generated) and actual (human-recorded) audio files.

In this dataset, the features of the audio files have already been extracted into spectrogram images (so no need to worry about audio feature extraction). So it comes down to an image classification task.

This task was one of the tasks in the 2025 GAITE contest, which is the secondary contest in IOAI 2025 in Beijing from August 2 to 9, 2025. Data source: [https://github.com/IOAI-official/IOAI-2025/blob/main/GAITE-Contest/Synthetic_Speech_Detector](https://github.com/IOAI-official/IOAI-2025/blob/main/GAITE-Contest/Synthetic_Speech_Detector)  
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [F1 score] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score) between the predicted class label and the actual class label.

The F1 score is defined as 

$${{F1\ score}} = \frac{2}{\frac{1}{Precision} + \frac{1}{Recall}}$$

where precision and recall are taken over the detection of the ""spoof"" (synthetic speech) class with class label 1.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the label for the target class. Example submission (format shown in sample_submission.csv in the data)

    id,label
    0,0
    1,1
    2,0
    etc.",,False,True
10558,pest-clef-2026,"# Overview

PestCLEF is a knowledge graph extraction challenge framed as a document-level relation extraction. Grounding of entities on the text (entities offsets) is not required though they are provided in the training set.

Predictions are evaluated using the F1 metric.
----------------------
# Description

#### Motivation

- Extract knowledge on crop disease biology from text.
- Help crop scientists to discover new ways to detect outbreaks, reduce pesticide usage, and prevent food insecurity.

#### Information Extraction Task

Extract knowledge in the form of a graph from text documents harvested from the Web. Participants are provided the text content of the documents along with the original HTML layout. Ancillary sections --such as navbars, menus, ads-- were removed automatically. For training, participants are provided the gold knowledge graph for each document.

Participants predict a graph of the knowledge mentioned in the document. The graph must conform to the schema below.



----------------------
# Evaluation

#### Metric

The metric computes:
1. the F-score for each document by comparing the predicted KG with the reference KG;
2. the mean of F-scores across documents (macro-average).

The F-score is defined as:

$$F = 2\times \frac{Recall * Precision}{Recall + Precision}$$

$$Recall = \frac{TP}{T}$$ $$Precision = \frac{TP}{P}$$

- *T* is the number of edges in the reference KG.
- *P* is the number of edges in the predicted KG.
- *TP* is the number of edges in the predicted KG that match an edge in the reference KG.

#### Edge matching algorithm

Let *E<sub>p</sub> = (S<sub>p</sub>, P<sub>p</sub>, O<sub>p</sub>)*, an edge of the predicted KG, and its subject, predicate and object.

Let *E<sub>r</sub> = (S<sub>r</sub>, P<sub>r</sub>, O<sub>r</sub>)*, an edge of the reference KG, and its subject, predicate and object.

*E<sub>p</sub>* matches *E<sub>r</sub>*, if and only if their respective subject, predicate, and object match.

Predicate matching is a string comparison with case folding, and ignoring whitespaces and underscores.

Subject and object matching",,False,True
10559,egci-486-final-exam-2025-1,"# Overview

## Final Exam : Image Classification
|**Day-Time**	 | **Item** |
| --- | --- |
| Monday 8 December 2025 at 13:30 PM| Open |  |
| Monday 8 December 2025 at 15:00 PM| Close |  |


## Member

- This competition is an individual format. 
- All competitors must join the competition first.
- After joining competition, all competitors must change their **Team Name** to **6XXXXXX-Name** in the Team tab.

## Leaderboards

Each group is allowed to send results 6 times per day. The score will be displayed into 2 parts:
- 50 percents of dataset will be displayed in the Public Leaderboard where the contestants can check their scores at any time.
- 50 percents of dataset will be stored and displayed in the Private Leaderboard which will be used to calculate the score to determine the result of the competition. The results will be displayed after the competition is over.


## Evaluation Metric
Metric: Categorization Accuracy

## Submission
To submit the results, the test set should be predicted class result and collected into "".CSV"" file as shown in the example file ""sample_submission.csv"", otherwise Kaggle will not be able to calculate submission score.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions are evaluated using categorization accuracy. Each image has been labeled with one true class. For each image, you must submit a set of the predicted labels.</p>

<p>Before we delve into the details of each of the accuracy score, it is important to cover the four building blocks used to define the evaluation metrics:</p>

- True Positive (TP) ¨C Actual label is positive and prediction is also positive
- True Negative (TN) ¨C Actual label is negative and prediction is also negative
- False Positive (FP) ¨C Actual label is negative but prediction is positive
- False Negative (FN) ¨C Actual label is positive but prediction is negative

![test](https://i.ibb.co/HnvkpfQ/Classification-Image.png)

<p>Accuracy is the most basic version of evaluation metrics. It is calculated as the ratio of correct predictions (TP + TN) over all the predictions made (TP + TN + FP + FN).</p>

![test](https://miro.medium.com/max/1210/1*N4Lo9Miw397g3XpX7o0CDw.png)

<h3>Submission File</h3>
You must submit a csv file with the image file name and class.


The 10 classes to predict are:

- Black
- Blue
- Brown
- Green
- Grey
- Orange
- Red
- Violet
- White
- Yellow

<p>The order of the rows does not matter. The file must have a header and should look like the following:</p>

<pre>id,classes
1.jpg, Green
2.jpg, Yellow
3.jpg, Violet
...
</pre>","# Dataset Description

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2561061%2Fb1a80731d2ff42814844ff5f4dbfb3da%2Fexample_image.png?generation=1764677402343477&alt=media)
<p>The dataset consists of ten color classes, each representing a single color: Black, Blue, Brown, Green, Grey, Orange, Red, Violet, White, and Yellow. Each class contains exactly 25 images, resulting in a perfectly balanced dataset. The main purpose of this dataset is to support the training and evaluation of image classification models, enabling them to correctly predict and classify images by color. This dataset is also suitable for developing and testing machine learning techniques in a balanced classification scenario. Students can refer to the original dataset on Kaggle for more details.</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>sample-submission.csv</b> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><b>starter_code.ipynb</b> - a python code to process a feature extraction, model and submission file</li>
<li><b>train</b> - the training set contain 10 folders (Black, Blue, Brown, Green, Grey, Orange, Red, Violet, White, Yellow) contains 170 images</li>
<li><b>test</b> - the test set image contains around 80 images</li>
</ul>",False,True
10560,dojo-hackathon-2025-are-you-done-yet,"# Overview

Welcome to the Dojo Hackathon 2025! In this exciting cross-functional challenge, participants will compete to solve a real-world computer vision challenge.

## **?? Competition Overview**
The challenge is a binary image classification task: building models to accurately determine whether a given image contains an empty plate or a not-empty plate.

With a dataset of over 350 labeled plate images, competitors will explore the data, experiment with preprocessing techniques, and train machine learning models to achieve the highest accuracy.

The competition's scoring metric is accuracy (how often are your predictions correct), but there will be a special place in my heart reserved for the individual or team with the most inventive solution!

## **?? Goal**
Develop a predictive model that can reliably classify plates as empty or not-empty. Success will depend on creative problem-solving, effective data preprocessing, and innovative model tuning.
----------------------
# Description

## Problem Domain
In this competition, you will work on a computer vision task, which involves training a model to recognize patterns in images. Specifically, the model needs to distinguish between two classes: plates that are empty (i.e., no food or objects on them) and plates that are not empty (i.e., plates with food or other items). This problem is part of a broader domain of image classification, where the goal is to teach a machine to understand visual content and make predictions based on the features present in an image.

## Key Challenge
The key challenge is data variability, as images can vary in lighting, angles, and the amount of food or objects on the plates. To build a robust model, you¡¯ll need to:

- Preprocess and augment the images to improve model generalization.
- Train a classifier that can identify subtle features between empty and not-empty plates.
- Handle data imbalance, as the number of images in each class may not be perfectly equal.

## Problem Setup
Participants will have access to a labeled dataset of images of plates, where each image is tagged as either containing an empty plate or a not-empty plate. You'll use this dataset to train your models and make predictions.

## Key Requirements:

- Preprocessing steps such as resizing, normalization, or augmentation.
- Selection of the appropriate machine learning or deep learning model (e.g., CNNs).
- Fine-tuning of hyperparameters to improve accuracy.

## Objective

Build a model that predicts, for each image, whether the plate is empty or not. The model will be evaluated based on its accuracy, with more complex solutions and creative approaches receiving higher marks.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The primary evaluation metric for this competition is accuracy ¡ª  the percentage of correct predictions made by the model on a test set.

---
## Submission File
For each image_id in the test set, you must predict a class (empty or not_empty) for the class_predictions variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

| image_id   | class_predictions |
|------------|-------------------|
| eimage_55   | empty             |
| zimage_64   | not_empty         |
| himage_12   | not_empty         |
| etc.   | etc.         |",,False,True
