10562,testingggthanjjandrx,"# Overview



##  **Title**

**Histopathology 7-Class Tissue Classification Challenge**

---

##  **Description**

Early and accurate detection of tissue abnormalities is essential for guiding diagnosis and treatment. This competition focuses on automated classification of **histopathology image patches** into **seven diagnostic categories**, ranging from normal tissue to invasive carcinoma.

Participants are provided with a labeled training set of microscopy image patches and a hidden-label test set. The goal is to develop a model that can accurately predict the correct tissue class for each test image:

* **0 ¡ª Normal (N)**
* **1 ¡ª Papillary Benign (PB)**
* **2 ¡ª Usual Ductal Hyperplasia (UDH)**
* **3 ¡ª Flat Epithelial Atypia (FEA)**
* **4 ¡ª Atypical Ductal Hyperplasia (ADH)**
* **5 ¡ª Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS)**
* **6 ¡ª Invasive Carcinoma (IC)**

This dataset captures subtle morphological differences across benign, atypical, and malignant categories, making the challenge both medically relevant and technically demanding. By participating, you¡¯ll contribute to advancing machine learning methods for digital pathology and helping explore solutions to real-world diagnostic problems.

Your task is simple:
**Build the most accurate 7-class classifier for tissue histopathology images.**

----------------------
# Description


##  **Submission Format**

Your submission must be a **CSV file** with **two columns**:

* **`image`** ¡ª the filename of each test image
* **`label`** ¡ª your predicted class ID (integer 0¨C6)

###  Example (`submission.csv`)

```
image,label
0.png,0
1.png,1
3.png,5
4.png,6
...
```

###  Rules

* Every test image must appear exactly once.
* Column names must match **exactly**.
* Labels must be integers corresponding to the table below.

---

##  **Label Mapping (Class ¡ú ID)**

Participants must predict **one of the following 7 categories**:

| Label ID | Diagnosis Class | Description                 |
| -------: | --------------- | --------------------------- |
|    **0** | **N**           | Normal tissue        |
|    **1** | **PB**          | Benign proliferative lesion |
|    **2** | **UDH**         | Usual ductal hyperplasia    |
|    **3** | **FEA**         | Flat epithelial atypia      |
|    **4** | **ADH**         | Atypical ductal hyperplasia |
|    **5** | **DCIS**        | Ductal carcinoma in situ    |
|    **6** | **IC**          | Invasive carcinoma          |

All predictions must be one of these integers **0¨C6**.

----------------------
# Evaluation


#  **Evaluation**

This competition uses **Quadratic Weighted Cohen¡¯s Kappa** as the official evaluation metric.
Cohen¡¯s Kappa is designed for **ordinal classification**, where the classes have a natural order ¡ª making it ideal for medical severity grading.

Unlike standard accuracy, Cohen¡¯s Kappa:

* Rewards correct predictions
* Penalizes predictions **more heavily when they are far from the true class**
* Adjusts for the possibility of random chance agreement

For example, predicting **IC (6)** when the true class is **N (0)** results in a much stronger penalty than predicting **ADH (4)** for **DCIS (5)**.

---

##  **Metric Definition: Quadratic Weighted Kappa**

The formula computes the agreement between predictions and ground truth while weighting errors quadratically:

* A difference of 1 class ¡ú small penalty
* A difference of 2 classes ¡ú larger penalty
* A difference of 6 classes ¡ú maximum penalty

This ensures the scoring reflects the **clinical relevance of mistakes**.

---

##  **Leaderboard Scoring**

Your submissions are evaluated against:

* A **Public** test subset (visible during the competition)
* A **Private** test subset (hidden, used for final ranking)

Only the **Public** portion is used to update the live leaderboard, while the **Private** portion determines the final standings at the end of the competition.","# Dataset Description



###  Dataset Description

This dataset contains digitized histopathology image patches representing **seven tissue categories**, ranging from normal tissue to invasive carcinoma. The images originate from microscopy scans of biopsies and have been organized into well-defined classes:

* **N** ¨C Normal
* **PB** ¨C Papillary Benign
* **UDH** ¨C Usual Ductal Hyperplasia
* **FEA** ¨C Flat Epithelial Atypia
* **ADH** ¨C Atypical Ductal Hyperplasia
* **DCIS** ¨C Ductal Carcinoma In Situ
* **IC** ¨C Invasive Carcinoma

Each image is a small, uniformly processed patch extracted from whole-slide histopathology images. The dataset has been partitioned into:

* **Training set** (with labels for model development)
* **Test set** (labels hidden from participants)
* **Sample submission** (format guide for participants)

The task is a **7-class classification problem**, where participants must predict the correct tissue category for each test image. This dataset is designed to evaluate performance on real-world medical imaging classification under class imbalance, subtle morphological differences, and multi-class diagnostic complexity.",False,True
10563,data-cept,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2024 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** The goal for this competition is to predict whether an applicant is approved for a loan.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) using the predicted probabilities and the ground truth targets.

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict target `loan_status`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,loan_status
    58645,0.5
    58646,0.5
    58647,0.5
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - October 1, 2024
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  October 31, 2024

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Loan Approval Prediction](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/chilledwanker/loan-approval-prediction) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `loan_status` is the binary target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict probability of the target `loan_status` for each row
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10564,gsb-544-fall-2025-regression,"# Description

## Data

This dataset consists of information about properties sold in Ames, Iowa.
You may have seen this data before, and you may find examples of it being used elsewhere online.  The version in this competition has many of the variables removed, for uniqueness and simplicity.

## Data fields

Here's a brief description of the variables:

* SalePrice - the property's sale price in dollars. This is the target variable that you're trying to predict.
* PID - An identifying number of the property
* LotFrontage: Linear feet of street connected to property
* LotArea: Lot size in square feet
* Street: Type of road access
* Neighborhood: Physical locations within Ames city limits
* BldgType: Type of dwelling
* HouseStyle: Style of dwelling
* OverallQual: Overall material and finish quality
* OverallCond: Overall condition rating
* YearBuilt: Original construction date
* RoofStyle: Type of roof
* Heating: Type of heating
* CentralAir: Central air conditioning
* Electrical: Electrical system
* GrLivArea: Above grade (ground) living area square feet
* FullBath: Full bathrooms above grade
* HalfBath: Half baths above grade
* Bedroom AbvGr: Number of bedrooms above basement level
* TotRmsAbvGrd: Total rooms above grade (does not include bathrooms)
* Functional: Home functionality rating
* ScreenPorch: Screen porch area in square feet
* PoolArea: Pool area in square feet
* YrSold: Year Sold
* SaleType: Type of sale

## GOAL
Your mission today is to use the available variables to predict the sales price of homes.

## Only your first two submissions will be graded!

## Acknowledgments
The Ames Housing dataset was compiled by Dean De Cock for use in data science education. It's an incredible alternative for data scientists looking for a modernized and expanded version of the often cited Boston Housing dataset. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

It is your job to predict the sales price for each house. For each `PID` in the test set, you must predict the value of the `SalePrice` variable. 

## Submission File Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

| PID | SalePrice |
| --- | --- |
|907135180  | 169000.1 |
|903206120 | 187724.1233 |

etc.

You can download an example submission file (`sample_submission.csv`) on the Data page.

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on **Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE)** between the *logarithm* of the predicted value and the *logarithm* of the observed sales price. (Taking logs means that errors in predicting expensive houses and cheap houses will affect the result equally.)

 Note that although this **metric** is calculated on log price, your **submitted predictions** need to be regular prices.
----------------------
# Grading

Your grade on this portion of the Final will be determined by the metric achieved by your submitted predictions.

---

## GRADE TIERS

*Note: these may be subject to change depending on outcomes.  If they are changed, they will only change in the direction of your advantage; i.e., your grade will be higher than expected.*

**0.21 or above:** F

**0.21 - 0.17:** D range

**0.17 - 0.16:** C range

**0.16 - 0.15:** B range

**0.15 - 0.14:** A range

**0.14 and below:** A+","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train_new.csv** - The training set.  There may be missing values.
*   **test_new.csv** - The test set, which you will make predictions on.  There are no missing values.
*   **sample_submission_new.csv** - A sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns for Submission

*   `PID` - The property ID
*   `SalePrice`?- The predicted price of the property


## IMPORTANT

Remember that your predictions are being assessed based on the **log** RMSE, but that your submissions should have the sale price predictions on the **original** scale.",False,True
10565,function-approximation-using-multilayer-perceptron,"# Description

The dataset contains the data which are the outputs from an unknown function 
$$ y=f(x\_1,x\_2). $$
Note that the given training dataset has been disturbed by some noise. You are now required to design a network to remove the introduced noise. After finishing your network training, you need to produce the outputs for a given testing dataset. Kaggle will compute the **mean square error** (MSE) between your outputs and the true outputs for the performance evaluation of your network.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The criterion used to evaluate your submission is the MSE loss. 
$$L=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n(\hat{y}_i-y)^2$$

----------------------
# File Submission Requirements

0. **Follow every rule below without any violation. Otherwise, your work will not be scored.**
1. Write the code with clear comments/documentations.
2. Write a report on HackMD platform and make it SHARED so that the teacher has the permission to read your report. Store the shared link in a text file ""REPORT_LINK.txt"".
3. The report had better enclose the following:
    * Detailed descriptions about the model you have design
    * The novelty of your design
    * What impressive results you have achieved
    * The difficulty you encountered during the work
    * What have you learned from the work
2. Pack all source code files and the ""REPORT_LINK.txt"" (not including the data files) into one zip file (use winzip, not winrar) named with **""XXXXXX_HW1.zip"" where XXXXXX is your username on Kaggle**.  (** ###### VERY IMPORTANT! ###### **)
3. Submit the files to –|ÈAeŒWÔ·.","# Dataset Description

Two datasets are given:
* **train.csv**: the training dataset which contains three columns of data. Column 1 and Column 2 are inputs x1 and x2, while Column 3 is the target value y.
* **test.csv**: the testing dataset for evaluating your model. Only two columns of data, i.e., x1 and x2, are given.

You need to produce the estimated value for every sample in the testing dataset using your network and save all estimated values into a file (**submission.csv**). Do not change the order of samples in the testing dataset; otherwise, your submission may get a low score due to the mismatch between your answers and the true answers.",False,True
10566,music-scale-recognition-by-cnn,"# Description

# Music Scale Recognition Using Mel-ceptstrum

In this competition, you are required to recognize the scales (ÒôëA) of musical sounds. By some sound signal processing method, each sound sample has been converted into the **mel-cepstrum** feature, which can be represented as a 2-D image. Using this image, you need to build a CNN model to recognize it into a music scale. 
[![](https://i.imgur.com/Z7kk7e3l.jpg)](https://i.imgur.com/Z7kk7e3.png)

# How to Retrieve the Dataset for Model Training?
You can get one image dataset of the extracted mel-cepstrum features from [http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/music\_train.zip](http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/music_train.zip) for training your model. Associated with the dataset, you are given an annotation file [http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/truth.txt](http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/truth.txt) which contains the label of each training image. Each line in this file has two fields, i.e., the image filename and the label of this image which is a number ranging between 0 and 87. 

# Testing
 To test your network model, here is the test dataset ([http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/music\_test.zip](http://web.csie.ndhu.edu.tw/ccchiang/Data/music_test.zip). All you need to do is to use your trained CNN to recognize the images in this dataset. You have to output the recognized scale for each testing image and prepare the outputs as an 'output.csv' file according to the required format for submission to Kaggle. 

&gt; P.S.: You may need to learn how to read each image as a numpy array and then convert it into a PyTorch tensor. To this end, you should ask the ChatGPT about how to do it.

----------------------
#  ## 2. File Submission Requirements 

0. **Follow every rule below without any violation. Otherwise, your work will not be scored.**
1. Write the code with clear comments/documentations.
2. Write a report on HackMD platform and make it SHARED so that the teacher has the permission to read your report. Store the shared link in a ""REPORT_LINK.txt"".
3. The report had better enclose the following:
    * Detailed descriptions about the model you have design
