10568,24-hrs-hackathon,"# Overview

Develop a machine learning model that can accurately predict permeability from the basic petrophysical logs and evaluate the performance of the model on the blind well.
----------------------
# Description

 ## Expected outputs:
	- Plots showing quality control (i.e. outlier removal)
	- A trained ML model for permeability prediction 
	- Model performance metrics (e.g., RMSE, MAE, R^2,etc.)
	- Predicted permeability vs. Actual permeability plots
	- Feature importance / sensitivity interpretation
## To be submitted: 
	- The trained model along with a user guide text file (this file should clearly mention the necessary input well logs along with the input file format)
	- A summary report, and 
	- A slide deck for presentation showcasing all the bullet points within ¡®Expected Outcomes¡¯. 
## Dataset Provided:
 - 4 well log files including basic petrophysical logs: Gamma Ray, Resistivity, Porosity, Permeability, etc. 

For evaluation, the trained model will be tested on a test dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation section describes how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.

---

Submissions are evaluated on [Mean Absolute Error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error)


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Permeability
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains detailed well log data from a compact reservoir, focusing on key petrophysical parameters necessary for reservoir characterization and simulation studies. The dataset provides high-resolution data from five critical well logs, specifically: Resistivity, Gamma Ray, Porosity, Permeability, and Lithology. These logs are invaluable for interpreting subsurface geology, estimating reservoir quality, and supporting hydrocarbon exploration and production activities.

## Dataset Contents

- Resistivity Log: Provides information about the resistivity of the reservoir rock, crucial for determining fluid saturation and identifying hydrocarbon-bearing zones.
- Gamma Ray Log: Measures natural radioactivity in the formation and is used for lithology identification, distinguishing between shale-rich and clean formations.
- Porosity Log: Offers an estimation of the porosity within the formation, indicating the void spaces that can hold hydrocarbons.
- Permeability Log: Describes the ability of fluids to flow through the rock, a key factor in determining reservoir performance and productivity.
- Lithology Log: Defines the type of rock present at various depths, based on petrophysical characteristics and geophysical measurements.

## Dataset fields:

- Depth (m): The measured depth for each data point.
- Resistivity (Ohm.m): Formation resistivity values at different depths.
- Gamma Ray (API): Gamma ray values to assist in lithology identification.
- Porosity (fraction): Log-derived porosity values.
- Permeability (mD): Permeability readings indicating fluid flow potential.
- Lithology: Classification of the rock types in the reservoir.

###  Applications
This dataset can be used in various applications, including:
Petrophysical analysis for subsurface formation evaluation.
Reservoir characterization to determine hydrocarbon potential.
Simulation modeling for flow and production forecasting.
Geological interpretations to assist in identifying structural and stratigraphic traps.
Source and Collection Method
The logs were collected from high-resolution well logging tools deployed in a compact reservoir. The measurements were obtained at various intervals to capture detailed petrophysical characteristics throughout the formation.",False,True
