10591,crop-classification-with-polarimetric-sar-data,"# Overview

Crop classification with polarimetric SAR data
----------------------
# Description

## 
This competition focuses on land cover classification using polarimetric synthetic aperture radar (PolSAR) images from the AgriSAR 2009 campaign conducted over the Flevoland region in the Netherlands.

**Challenge**: Classify image pixels into **15 distinct land cover types** using polarimetric scattering features.

**Why This Matters**: 
- PolSAR provides all-weather, day-and-night imaging capabilities.
- Agricultural monitoring and land cover mapping are crucial for agricultural land management. 
- Machine learning techniques hold the potential to significantly enhance the accuracy of automated classification.

**Key Dataset Characteristics**:
- Source: AgriSAR 2009 Campaign (Flevoland)
- Features: 10 polarimetric scattering features extracted from quad-polarimetric SAR data
- Classes: 15 different land cover types

----------------------
# Reference

European Space Agency. (2011). AgriSAR 2009 Campaign Dataset. ESA Earth Online.1 Available from [https://doi.org/10.5270/esa-jdg5o4g].","# Dataset Description

# 
## File Structure

```
©À©¤©¤ train_input.npy
©À©¤©¤ test_input.npy
©¸©¤©¤ sample_submission.csv
```

## Detailed File Descriptions

### 1. **train_input.npy**
**Purpose**: Train the classification model 

**Structure**:
- **Format**: NumPy array (.npy)
- **Shape**: (N_train, 11) where N_train is the number of training pixels
- **Columns**:
  - **Columns 1-10**: Polarimetric scattering features (see **Feature Descriptions** below)
  - **Column 11**: Ground truth land cover label (integer: 1-15)


---

### 2. **test_input.npy**
**Purpose**: Test dataset for prediction

**Structure**:
- **Format**: NumPy array (.npy)
- **Shape**: (N_test, 10) where N_test is the number of test pixels
- **Columns**:
  - **Columns 1-10**: Polarimetric scattering features (identical feature set as training data)


---

### 3. **sample_submission.csv**
**Purpose**: Submission template

**Structure**:
- **Format**: CSV file
- **Columns**:
  - **Column 1 (ID)**: Pixel index (1 to N_test)
  - **Column 2 (Label)**: Predicted land cover class (integer: 1-15)

**Example**:
```csv
ID,Label
0,3
1,7
2,12
...
```


---

## ?? Feature Descriptions

The 10 polarimetric scattering features are extracted from quad-polarimetric SAR data:

| Feature Index | Feature Name | Description |
|--------------|--------------|-------------|
| 1 | C11 | Covariance matrix element (1,1) - HH intensity |
| 2 | C12 | Covariance matrix element (1,2) |
| 3 | C13 | Covariance matrix element (1,3) |
| 4 | C22 | Covariance matrix element (2,2) - HV intensity |
| 5 | C23 | Covariance matrix element (2,3) |
| 6 | C33 | Covariance matrix element (3,3) - VV intensity |
| 7 | SPAN | Total scattered power (C11+C22+C33) |
| 8 | Alpha (¦Á) | Average scattering angle |
| 9 | Anisotropy (A) | Polarimetric anisotropy |
| 10 | Entropy (H) | Randomness of scattering mechanisms |


---",False,True
10592,cyprus-ai-camp-2026-tasks,"# Overview

This competitions will be a selection that defines candidates to the Cyprus team at IOAI 2026. The problems will cover a wide range of AI topics, including different domains in machine learning and math, testing students¡¯ practical and theoretical abilities.

### Dates and Fromat

**Dates:** Jan 31¨CFeb 8, 2026

**Format:** In-person participation

### Competition Structure

Tasks will be gradually released according to the following schedule:

| Task             | Available From   | Available Until  | Links|
|------------------|------------------|------------------|------|
| ML Tables 1  and 2      | Feb 2, 09:00 EET | Feb 2, 12:00 EET | [Space](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cyprus-ai-camp-binary-classification-in-space), [WineQuality](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cyprus-ai-camp-the-enigma-of-wine-quality) |
| ML Images 1 and 2 | Feb 3, 09:00 EET | Feb 3, 12:00 EET | [FashionClassifier](https://www.kaggle.com/t/50d7475c6b6b4557accaabd2a46105ab), [Galaxy10Classifier](https://www.kaggle.com/t/640910f341924fe993edba1e04916e3d) |
| ML Tables 3 and Multimodal | Feb 4, 09:00 EET | Feb 4, 12:00 EET | [Cars](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cyprus-cars-price-oracle), [SkinClass](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/multimodal-skin-classification)|
| Maths Tasks      | Feb 5, 09:00 EET | Feb 5, 12:00 EET | Pen and paper format |
| NLP | Feb 6, 09:00 EET | Feb 6, 12:00 EET | [BERT](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cyprus-ai-camp-broken-bert), [The Last Frequency](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/the-last-frequency) |
| Segmentation | Feb 7, 09:00 EET | Feb 7, 12:00 EET | [Cuties](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cyprus-ai-camp-cuties-segmentation), [Document Layout Analysis](https://www.kaggle.com/t/7f910d27a7a640d5a3997c0152e24a12)|

## Rules

Carefully read ""Rules"" section before joining the competitions.","# Dataset Description

This a test competition to help you familiarize yourself with Kaggle platform. Links to real competitions will be published in time in ""Overview"" tab of the page.

The data is quite simple: you have a set of images in `test` folder that you need to classify into 6 classes. In `train` folder there are certain examples of each class. Read ""Rules"" tab to learn more about Cyprus AI competitions rules - they're going to be the same (minor changes are possible) for the rest competitions you're going to participate in.

You need to write your predictions into `sample_submission.csv` file and submit it on Kaggle.

**Note that this is a competition where you have a small amount of samples and you can get the ideal accuracy of 1. Try to achieve that in the least amount of submissions possible! This is the real challenge here.**

We suggest you to use Kaggle notebooks which you can create in the ""Code"" tab.",False,True
10593,land-parcel-segmentation-with-remote-sense-images,"# Overview

Semantic segmentation of land parcels based on optical remote sensing images
----------------------
# Description

Parcel segmentation of remote sensing images aims to perform pixel-level content analysis, extracting and classifying categories of interest. This competition seeks to leverage artificial intelligence to fully utilize heterogeneous multi-source data across diverse scenarios to develop efficient and practical algorithms to improve segmentation performance.  

**Why This Matters**
- Semantic segmentation of remote sensing images holds significant practical value for fields such as urban planning, flood monitoring, and disaster relief.
- Existing segmentation algorithms suffer from limited applicability and insufficient accuracy for practical applications.
- Manual parcel segmentation is time-consuming and labor-intensive, making it difficult to apply on a large scale.  

**Task**  
The task is to segment 256¡Á256 optical remote sensing image tiles into 7 land cover categories:  
| value | class | 
| --- | --- | 
| 0 | building | 
| 1 | crop land | 
| 2 | forest | 
| 3 | water | 
| 4 | road |
| 5 | grassland |  
| 6 | others | 

**Data Introduction**
- Source: 2020 CCF BDCI Remote Sensing Image Parcel Segmentation Dataset
- Format: 256¡Á256 size JPG file
- Category: 7 types of land parcels

----------------------
# Submission Instructions

The segmentation results need to be converted to a CSV file using the following standard procedure:
```Python
import os
import os
import csv
import random
from PIL import Image

random.seed(42)

image_folder = r""""  # path to your results
output_csv = r""xxx.csv"" # path to your CSV file

image_files = sorted([f for f in os.listdir(image_folder)
                      if f.lower().endswith(('.png', '.jpg', '.jpeg', '.tif', '.bmp'))])

print(f""Found {len(image_files)} images"")

total_pixels = len(image_files) * 256 * 256


with open(output_csv, 'w', newline='') as csvfile:
    csv_writer = csv.writer(csvfile)
    csv_writer.writerow(['ID', 'Label'])

    processed = 0

    for img_name in image_files:
        print(f""processing: {img_name}"")

        
        img_path = os.path.join(image_folder, img_name)
        img = Image.open(img_path)

        
        pixels = list(img.getdata())
        width, height = img.size

        
        base_name = os.path.splitext(img_name)[0]

        
        pixel_index = 1
        for row in range(height):
            for col in range(width):
                pixel_id = f""{base_name}_{pixel_index}""
                pixel_value = pixels[pixel_index - 1]

                csv_writer.writerow([pixel_id, pixel_value])

                pixel_index += 1
                processed += 1

        img.close()

print(f""Completed! {processed:,} pixels have been processed"")
print(f""CSV has been saved: {output_csv}"")

```
----------------------
# Reference

China Computer Federation. (2020). 2020 CCF BDCI. Available from [https://www.datafountain.cn/competitions/475].","# Dataset Description

# 
## File Structure
```
©À©¤©¤ train_image.zip
©À©¤©¤ train_label.zip
©À©¤©¤ test_image.zip
©¸©¤©¤ sample_submission.csv
```
## Detailed File Descriptions
### **1. train_image.zip**
**Purpose**£ºImages for training the model  
**Structure**£º
- **Format**£ºJPG images
- **Shape**£º256¡Á256

### **2. train_label.zip**
**Purpose**£ºGround truth labels corresponding to `train_image.zip`  
**Structure**£º
- **Format**£ºPNG images
- **Shape**£º256¡Á256
- **Classes**£º

| value | class | 
| --- | --- | 
| 0 | building | 
| 1 | crop land | 
| 2 | forest | 
| 3 | water | 
| 4 | road |
| 5 | grassland |  
| 6 | others | 
| 255 | no label | 

### **3. test_image.zip**
**Purpose**£ºImages for testing semantic segmentation models  
**Structure**£º
- **Format**£ºJPG images
- **Shape**£º256¡Á256

### **4. sample_submission.csv**
**Purpose**£ºSubmission template  
**Structure**£º
- **Format**£ºCSV file
- **Columns**£º
    - **Column 1 (ID)**£ºPixel index (1 to N_test)
    - **Column 2 (Label)**£ºPredicted land cover class (integer: 0-6)
- **Example**£º
```
ID,Label
T005001-1,3
T005001-2,2
T005001-3,6
¡¤¡¤¡¤
```",False,True
10594,automated-url-discovery-semantic-relevance-filter,"# Overview

The objective of this challenge is to develop an automated solution for discovering high-quality, relevant pages from a raw list of domains. We have a database of approximately 3,000 potential websites, but the data is ""noisy"" and limited exclusively to root domains (homepages). We need a method to crawl these domains and identify specific deep pages (URLs) that match a provided set of niche keywords.
----------------------
# Description

The Problem
We have a raw list of 3,000 root domains (e.g., `example-site.com`). However, we need to find specific articles or sub-pages (e.g., `example-site.com/specific-topic-article`) that are relevant to our client's niche.

The solution must be capable of taking a homepage as input, ""expanding"" the site structure to find deep content, and filtering out irrelevant pages (e.g., ""About Us"", generic news, or off-topic content).

Input Data
You are provided with two datasets:
- 'Input_Domains.csv`: A raw export of 3,000 root domains. Note: This is ""messy"" real-world data containing both high-relevance sites and irrelevant ""junk"" sites.
Target_Keywords.csv: A list of specific keywords that define ""Relevance"" for this project.

The Task
Your solution should process the inputs and execute the following pipeline:
1.  Expansion & Crawling: Iterate through the domains and crawl the site to discover relevant deep pages (articles, guides, reviews).
2.  Semantic Filtering: Score each identified page against the `Target_Keywords.csv` list. A page is a ""Match"" if it contains target keywords in its core metadata (Title, H1, URL).
3. Exclusion: Automatically discard ""utility"" pages (e.g., Privacy Policy, Login) and irrelevant topics.

Deliverables
1.  Source Code / Methodology: The complete code, script, or workflow used to process the list.
2.  Final Spreadsheet (CSV): The output file generated by your solution using our provided sample data.

Success Metrics
- Precision: Does the output successfully exclude the irrelevant domains included in the input?
- Granularity: Does the output provide specific deep URLs rather than just homepages?
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on the quality and precision of the final output CSV.

Precision (50%)

Does the filtered list exclude the irrelevant or off-topic domains that were included in the input sample?

Are the identified pages genuinely relevant to the provided keyword list?

Granularity (30%)

Does the solution provide specific deep URLs, for example domain.com/specific-guide?

Solutions that only return the homepage, for example domain.com, will be penalized.

Code Quality (20%)

Is the crawling and filtering logic reproducible?

Can the solution scale to larger datasets, for example 100,000 domains?

Submission Format
Participants must upload a CSV file with the following columns:
Source Domain
Target URL
Detected Keyword","# Dataset Description

DATASET DESCRIPTION

This competition provides two input files. Participants will use these files
to crawl, filter, and identify relevant deep URLs from noisy root domains.

FILES
1. Input_Domains.csv        ¨C Approximately 3,000 root domains 
2. Target_Keywords.csv      ¨C A list of niche-specific keywords defining the target topics

INPUT_DOMAINS.CSV
- domain                 Root domain or homepage URL to be expanded.

TARGET_KEYWORDS.CSV
- keyword                Keyword defining the niche or topic of interest.

NOTES
- The final output (submission) should be a CSV file containing the following columns:
  Source Domain, Target URL, Detected Keyword, Relevance Score",False,True
