10596,quitting-smoking-bgu-2026,"# Overview


Smoking has been proven to negatively affect health in a multitude of ways.Smoking has been found to harm nearly every organ of the body, cause many diseases, as well as reducing the life expectancy of smokers in general. As of 2018, smoking has been considered the leading cause of preventable morbidity and mortality in the world, continuing to plague the world¡¯s overall health.

According to a World Health Organization report, the number of deaths caused by smoking will reach 10 million by 2030.

Evidence-based treatment for assistance in smoking cessation had been proposed and promoted. however, only less than one third of the participants could achieve the goal of abstinence. Many physicians found counseling for smoking cessation ineffective and time-consuming, and did not routinely do so in daily practice. To overcome this problem, several factors had been proposed to identify smokers who had a better chance of quitting, including the level of nicotine dependence, exhaled carbon monoxide (CO) concentration, cigarette amount per day, the age at smoking initiation, previous quit attempts, marital status, emotional distress, temperament and impulsivity scores, and the motivation to stop smoking. However, individual use of these factors for prediction could lead to conflicting results that were not straightforward enough for the physicians and patients to interpret and apply. Providing a prediction model might be a favorable way to understand the chance of quitting smoking for each individual smoker. Health outcome prediction models had been developed using methods of machine learning over recent years.

A group of scientists are working on predictive models with smoking status as the prediction target.Your task is to help them create a machine learning model to identify the smoking status of an individual using bio-signals

Dataset Description -
age : 5-years gap
height(cm)
weight(kg)
waist(cm) : Waist circumference length
eyesight(left)
eyesight(right)
hearing(left)
hearing(right)
systolic : Blood pressure
relaxation : Blood pressure
fasting blood sugar
Cholesterol : total
triglyceride
HDL : cholesterol type
LDL : cholesterol type
hemoglobin
Urine protein
serum creatinine
AST : glutamic oxaloacetic transaminase type
ALT : glutamic oxaloacetic transaminase type
Gtp : ¦Ã-GTP
dental caries
smoking

Your task is to use binary classification to predict a patient's smoking status given information about various other health indicators. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the probability for the target variable `smoking`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,smoking
    159256,0.5
    159257,0.5
    159258,0.5
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - December 12, 2025
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline (18/1/2026)
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline (18/1/2026)
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  19/1/2026

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Smoker Status Prediction using Bio-Signals](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/gauravduttakiit/smoker-status-prediction-using-biosignals) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `smoking` is the binary target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of positive `smoking`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10597,nst-placement-prediction-challenge-2025,"# Overview

?? The NST Placement Prediction Challenge
The Scenario
It is the year 2027. The NST Placement Cell has digitized the entire hiring process. Instead of manually reviewing resumes, they now rely on an AI Assessment Engine to predict the market value of every graduating student.

The system analyzes a student's entire 4-year journey¡ªfrom their first ""Hello World"" in Python to their final mock interview scores¡ªto forecast their Final CTC (Cost to Company).

Your mission is to build the machine learning model that powers this engine.
----------------------
# Description

?? Welcome to the Future of HiringIt is the year 2027,2028,2029. The Placement Cell at NST has evolved. They no longer manually screen thousands of resumes. Instead, they use a centralized AI Assessment Engine to predict the market value of every graduating student.Your task? Build the brain of this engine.You have been given access to the (anonymized) academic and professional history of the graduating batch. This data tracks their journey from their very first ""Problem Solving with Python"" class in Year 1 to their final ""Mock Interviews"" in Year 4.Your goal is to analyze this data and predict the Final CTC (Cost to Company) for the test set.?? The GoalYou are provided with a training dataset containing the profiles of 3,200 students, including their grades, internship history, technical skills, and their final job offers.You must predict the Final_CTC_LPA for the remaining 800 students in the test.csv file.The stakes are high: In the real world, an accurate prediction ensures students get fair offers. In this competition, it ensures you top the leaderboard!?? Understanding the Data ContextThe dataset is not a simple ""clean"" file. It mirrors real-world data collection challenges. It captures the Four Pillars of an NST Student's Journey:The Foundation (Year 1):Performance in core subjects like Problem Solving with Python (PSP) and electives like Geo Politics.The Grind (Year 2 & 3):Skill metrics such as Competitive Programming Ratings and the specific Tech Stack (MERN, Next.js, Java, etc.) the student specializes in.Note: Some data fields like 'Tech Stack' are self-reported by students, so treat them with caution (expect inconsistencies).The Career Arc (Internships):The link between 5th Semester performance and 7th Semester outcomes.Indicators of whether a student showed ""loyalty"" to a company (returning for a second internship).The Final Sprint (Year 4):Placement preparation metrics, including Aptitude Test error rates and Mock Interview scores.?? Evaluation MetricSubmissions are evaluated on Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE) between the predicted value and the observed target.$$RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2}$$What this means:We penalize large errors heavily.If a student actually got 45 LPA and you predicted 6 LPA, your score will suffer significantly.Goal: Minimise this error score. A score of 0.0 means a perfect prediction.?? Rules & TimelineTimeline: This is a 3-Day Sprint.Submission Limit: You may submit up to 10 prediction files per day. Use them wisely!Team: Individual participation is encouraged, but learning together is better.External Data: Use only the provided datasets.?? Getting StartedGo to the Data tab to download the files.Read the Data Description carefully (Context is King!).Open a Notebook, clean the data, and build your first model.Submit your submission.csv and check the Leaderboard.Good luck, Class of 2027,2028,2029!
----------------------
# Evaluation

?? Evaluation MetricThe evaluation metric for this competition is Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).The RMSE is commonly used in regression problems to measure the differences between values predicted by a model and the values observed.$$RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2}$$Where:$n$ is the number of predictions.$y_i$ is the actual value (True CTC).$\hat{y}_i$ is the predicted value (Your Model's CTC).What does this mean for you?Lower is Better: A score of 0.0 means a perfect prediction.Penalty for Outliers: RMSE punishes large errors heavily. If your model predicts a 40 LPA package for a student who actually got 6 LPA, your score will suffer significantly more than if you were off by a small amount for many students.?? Submission FormatFor every student in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: NST_Student_ID and Final_CTC_LPA.
NST_Student_ID,Final_CTC_LPA
20250001,10.5
20250002,6.5
20250003,45.0
20250004,12.0
etc.
Note:

The NST_Student_ID must match the IDs in the test.csv file exactly.

The Final_CTC_LPA should be a numerical value (float).",,False,True
10598,eacl-2026-abjad-nlp-shared-task-medical-text-classification-in-arabic,"# Overview

Welcome to the medical text classification shared task at the Abjad NLP workshop in EACL 2026! Further details about the workshop can be found at https://wp.lancs.ac.uk/abjad/ . Further details about the shared task can be found at https://balajinaga.github.io/EACL2026-Abjad-NLP-SharedTask/ .

You DO NOT need to register for the conference to participate in the shared task. There are no fees involved in this shared task for participants. We expect all participating teams to submit a 4-page system description paper as well. Most submissions that adhere to the shared task guidelines will be accepted into the workshop proceedings. **This can be a great way to enter AI research and publish something at one of the leading NLP venues!**

Each row in the dataset will have a question-answer pair in Arabic under the column named ""text"", along with a category under the ""category"" column (the class to be predicted). There are 82 categories in total, and there is considerable class imbalance in the dataset across these 82 categories, which makes the problem interesting. 

Note that you need to predict the integer label corresponding to the ""category"" column given under the ""label"" column. These category names were originally in Arabic, but we translated them into English using an LLM in order to aid in modeling. 

The link to the training dataset is here: https://drive.google.com/file/d/19bBhM9Eir6DnLijm90QHwELgGrG2wtZQ/view?usp=sharing

The link to the evaluation dataset (no labels provided) is:
https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_uXX2ILzhf9aUGos3WPWu6K7dDp4yJoe/view?usp=drive_link

You will submit your predictions as a csv file with 2 columns, ""Id"" and ""Predicted"". For example,
Id, Predicted

0, 34

1, 76

2, 43

Note that if you are using Pandas to generate your submission csv file, you need to use `df.to_csv(...., index=False)`, otherwise Kaggle will throw errors during submission because of the unnamed index column. 

These question-answer pairs are from the health care domain, and given the importance of NLP applications for healthcare in Abjad script languages such as Arabic, we are confident that this shared task will attract significant interest and have a positive impact on the community.

The scoring metric will be macro averaged F1 for all the 82 classes. To read up more about the macro average F1 metric, you can refer to https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html . 

To help you get started, we have shared a sample Colab notebook, where we finetune CamelBERT: https://colab.research.google.com/drive/10LAGmJZhoocW2N19lX3jRg49BWLJOYBF?usp=sharing . 


| Date | Milestone |
|------|-----------|
| December 8, 2025 | Release of training data |
| December 25, 2025 | Release of test data |
| December 31, 2025 | End of evaluation cycle (test submissions close) |
| January 2, 2026 | Final results released |
| January 13, 2026 | Shared task papers due date |
| January 20, 2026 | Notification of acceptance |
| February 3, 2026 | Camera-ready versions due |
| March 24¨C29, 2026 | Workshop Dates [TBD] |

*(All deadlines in AoE)*

For any questions, please contact abjadnlpmedicaltextclassificat@gmail.com .","# Dataset Description



## Files (look at the Google Drive links in the task overview page- they are the most up to date)

- The link to the training dataset is here: https://drive.google.com/file/d/19bBhM9Eir6DnLijm90QHwELgGrG2wtZQ/view?usp=sharing


- The link to the evaluation dataset (no labels provided) is: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_uXX2ILzhf9aUGos3WPWu6K7dDp4yJoe/view?usp=drive_link",False,True
10599,mercor-cheating-detection,"# Overview

Welcome to the Mercor Cheating Detection Competition, a Kaggle community event hosted by Mercor. Your goal in this challenge is to build a model that predicts whether a candidate is engaging in cheating behavior during an interview. The dataset includes anonymized behavioral features, platform activity signals, and a social graph that captures relationships between users across the network.

Cheating detection in real interview environments is challenging because most labeled cases come from candidates who were already flagged by internal systems or human reviewers, rather than from the broader population. To mirror these conditions, this competition provides both manually reviewed ground-truth labels and a large unlabeled sample drawn from the wider candidate pool. This structure encourages participants to address class imbalance, sampling bias, semi-supervised learning, and graph-based modeling techniques.

To be eligible for prizes, participants must also apply through the Mercor registration form linked below. This application ensures that your Kaggle account is associated with your submission and allows Mercor to contact and pay winners after the competition concludes.

https://work.mercor.com/jobs/list_AAABmuZ8XM29hAHcM8NNgLXq/mercor-cheating-detection-kaggle-contest
----------------------
# Description

You are tasked with predicting whether a candidate in an online talent marketplace is engaging in cheating behavior during an interview, using anonymized activity features and a social graph.

Submissions are evaluated using a cost-based metric that reflects real-world operational impact: false negatives (missing confirmed cheating) incur the highest penalty, false positives in high-confidence auto-blocking lead to candidate loss costs, manual review operations incur a smaller review cost, and correct decisions incur no cost. Your model¡¯s predicted probabilities are translated into three operational decision regions (auto-pass, manual review, auto-block), and the leaderboard ranks models by the minimum achievable total cost across optimal thresholds.

Data fields: Each candidate record includes anonymized behavioral features (feature_001 through feature_018), a high_conf_clean flag identifying high-confidence not cheating unlabeled examples, and is_cheating labels for manually reviewed cases.

Train/test setup: The training set contains manually reviewed labeled candidates as well as a large set of unlabeled high-confidence examples. Test and holdout sets include only labeled candidates for evaluation. A complete social graph is also provided for graph-based detection and relational modeling.

Submission format: Predict a cheating probability (0.0 to 1.0) for each candidate in the test set. The evaluation function will automatically search for the optimal decision thresholds that minimize total operational cost.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using a **cost-based metric** that reflects real-world operational costs in cheating detection. The evaluation function automatically searches for optimal decision thresholds that minimize total cost across three decision regions:

## Decision Regions
- **Auto-pass** (low cheating risk): Candidate proceeds without review  
- **Manual review** (medium risk): Candidate is flagged for human review  
- **Auto-block** (high cheating risk): Candidate is removed from consideration without review  

## Cost Structure
- **False Negative** (cheating passes through): $600  
- **False Positive in auto-block region**: $300  
- **False Positive in manual review region**: $150  
- **True Positive requiring manual review**: $5  
- **Correct auto-pass or auto-block**: $0  

Your score on the leaderboard is the negative of the minimum total cost found across all threshold combinations (higher scores are better). This metric rewards models that make confident, correct decisions while penalizing costly operational errors.

Eval code here:
https://www.kaggle.com/code/drewkringel/cheating-detection-eval

## Submission File

For each `user_hash` in the test set, you must predict a probability that the candidate is cheating. The file should contain a header and follow this format:

```python
user_hash,prediction
a3f2e1b92c8d4567,0.05
b8d4c2a71f9e3a82,0.87
c9e5d3f12a4b7c89,0.42
etc.
```

----------------------
# Prizes

**Kaggle Prizes Available: $5,000**
- First Prize: $2,500  
- Second Prize: $1,400  
- Third Prize: $700  
- Fourth Prize: $300  
- Fifth Prize: $100  

These prizes are open to everyone in the Kaggle community. 

Note that we will have a separate **identical prize pool** with the same structure reserved for students of undergraduate and graduate economic institutions in North America.
**Campus Prizes Available: $5,000**
- First Prize: $2,500  
- Second Prize: $1,400  
- Third Prize: $700  
- Fourth Prize: $300  
- Fifth Prize: $100  

Notes:
- It is possible to win awards in both tracks. 
- Eligibility for the campus prize will be determined via proof of student status during form submission. 
- For any questions, please leave a note in the discussion!","# Dataset Description

This competition provides real-world cheating detection data from a production AI interviewing system. All candidate identifiers and feature names have been anonymized to protect proprietary detection methods and user privacy.

## Files

**train.csv** ¨C Training set with labels and optional unlabeled examples  
**test.csv** ¨C Test set without labels  
**sample_submission.csv** ¨C Example submission file  
**social_graph.csv** ¨C Complete social graph as an edge list  
**feature_metadata.json** ¨C Feature types, value ranges, and missingness statistics  

## Data Format

### Main Dataset Files (train.csv, test.csv, holdout.csv)

Each row represents a single candidate with the following columns:

**user_hash** ¨C Anonymized candidate identifier  

**feature_001 through feature_018** ¨C Anonymized behavioral and interview-related features.  
Feature meanings are intentionally hidden to prevent reverse-engineering of cheating detection methods.  
See `feature_metadata.json` for data types, value ranges, and missingness percentages.

**high_conf_clean** ¨C Binary flag (`1` or `NaN`) indicating high-confidence **not-cheating** predictions from the production model. Candidates with `high_conf_clean = 1` do not have manual review labels and represent a broader sample of the population.

**is_cheating** ¨C Target variable  
- `0` = Not cheating (legitimate candidate)  
- `1` = Cheating (confirmed during manual review)  
- `NaN` = Unlabeled (only for rows where `high_conf_clean = 1`)  

---

### Social Graph (social_graph.csv)

An edge list representing the complete social network:

**user_a** ¨C Anonymized ID of one user in the connection  
**user_b** ¨C Anonymized ID of the other connected user  

The graph includes all relationship edges in the system.  
Many nodes in the graph do not appear in the main dataset (these are ""ghost users"").  

---

### Feature Metadata (feature_metadata.json)

Provides metadata for each anonymized:

- `type`: numeric or binary  
- `range`: min¨Cmax values or number of categories  
- `missing_%`: percentage of missing values in the training set  

---

## What You're Predicting

For each `user_hash` in the test set, predict the probability (`0.0` to `1.0`) that the candidate is cheating.

---

## Important Notes

**Missing Data:**  
Features contain realistic missingness patterns. Some checks or signals are only available for certain candidates.  
`NaN` values are informative and should be modeled carefully.

**Unlabeled Data:**  
The training set includes candidates with `high_conf_clean = 1` and `is_cheating = NaN`.  
These unlabeled candidates reflect a broader slice of the population.

**Graph Structure:**  
The social graph is complete but sparse.  
Not all users in the graph appear in the main dataset, and not all users in the dataset appear in the graph.",False,True
10600,machinelearningevent,"# Overview

The Machine Learning Hackathon at Pragati Engineering College challenges participants to build a supervised machine learning model for a real-world text classification problem.

Participants will analyze email data and develop models to classify emails as spam or not spam. The competition is hosted on Kaggle to give hands-on experience with data preprocessing, model training, evaluation, and leaderboard-based submissions.
**Goal**
Build a machine learning model to predict whether an email is spam (1) or not spam (0) for unseen data.
----------------------
# Description

The Machine Learning Hackathon at Pragati Engineering College challenges participants to apply data science techniques to solve a real-world predictive modeling problem. You will be given a dataset containing input features and a target variable. The objective is to understand the data, preprocess it properly, build suitable machine learning models, and generate accurate predictions for the test dataset.

All participants must submit their results through Kaggle in the correct format so that they can be scored and ranked on the leaderboard. The competition helps students gain hands-on experience with data analysis, supervised learning, model evaluation, and versioned submissions using Kaggle.

## What you will do

* Load and inspect the dataset using the files provided.
* Perform Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) to understand the data better.
* Handle missing values, categorical data, and other required preprocessing steps.
* Train and evaluate machine learning models.
* Select the best performing model and generate predictions for the test dataset.
* Submit your predictions to Kaggle in the required CSV format.

## Files

The following files are included in this competition:

`train.csv` ¡ª Contains features and target column used for training the model
`test.csv` ¡ª Contains the same features, but without the target
`sample_submission.csv` ¡ª Shows the exact format of the output you must submit

## Submission Format

Your output file must contain two columns named exactly as:

```
id,target
```

Example:

```
id,target
1,0
2,1
3,0
```

If formatting or column names differ, your submission may receive an error.

## Team and Evaluation Details

Teams must consist of exactly three participants.
Your model will be evaluated using **Accuracy Score** on hidden labels.
Only the best score per team will be used for final ranking.

---



----------------------
# Problem Statement & Objective

Build a machine learning model to classify emails as spam or not spam.
Predict the label (1 = Spam, 0 = Not Spam) for unseen email.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using Accuracy, which measures how many emails are correctly identified as either spam or not spam.

Higher accuracy means better performance in filtering unwanted email while retaining legitimate communication.

Submission File

For each id in the test set, you must predict whether the email is ¡°spam¡± or ¡°not spam¡±.
The file must include a header row and have the following format:

id,type
1,spam
2,not spam
3,not spam
4,spam
etc.


Requirements:

Predictions should only contain the labels spam or not spam

The id column must match the IDs in the test dataset exactly

Additional columns or formatting changes may result in a scoring error
----------------------
# Eligibility and Conduct

This is a student learning competition. All code must be written originally by team members. External datasets, pretrained models, public Kaggle notebooks, and internet-sourced code are not allowed.

Any plagiarism or rule violation will result in disqualification.
----------------------
# Prizes & Communication

First Prize: ?1,000
Second Prize: ?500
Third Prize: Certificate of Appreciation All Participants: E-Certificates
Any clarifications should be posted in the Kaggle Discussion section for this competition or directly to the organizing team.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains email messages for building a model that detects spam emails.

Files provided

train.csv ¨C Email text with labels

test.csv ¨C Email text without labels

sample_submission.csv ¨C Example submission format

Data format

CSV files

Main column: text (email content)

Target column: label (1 = Spam, 0 = Not Spam)

Prediction task

Predict whether an email is Spam or Not Spam

Acronyms

Spam ¨C Unwanted email

Ham ¨C Legitimate email

NLP ¨C Natural Language Processing",False,True
10601,cyprus-ai-camp-binary-classification-in-space,"# Overview

Binary classification.
----------------------
# Description

### Binary Classification in Space (100 points)

The score in this problem will be fractional, from 0 to 100 points.

*Year 2222*. The modern cosmologist Arkadiya knows for sure that theoretically there should be a balance of *ordinary* and *dwarf* galaxies in the Universe. 
However, the problem of the shortage of dwarf galaxies, known since the 20th century, is that the number of observed dwarf galaxies is many times less than predicted by theory.

Arkadiya has a collected dataset of **1000 observed galaxies**, which are described by **13 numerical features**; the class label is known for them (0 - dwarf, 1 - ordinary).

Help Arkadiya train a **binary classifier** that will work as accurately as possible on real data ¡ª all galaxies.

*P.S. The real data is a thousand times larger and confirms the theoretical distribution of ordinary and dwarf galaxies.*

#### About dataset
All data can be downloaded [here](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1NAuX7-UGr-phATwB-wN4w6E74lnHBkth?usp=sharing), or from the Kaggle competition.

You are provided with three files:

- The `train.csv` file contains data on 1000 observed galaxies and their classes in the target column. Each line is a set of real numbers (decimal separator is dot) separated by commas
- The `test.csv` file contains data on unobserved galaxies that you have to label
- The `sample_submission.csv` file is an example of a correct submission

#### What should be done
Upload the `submission.csv` file to the [Kaggle Competition](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cyprus-ai-camp-binary-classification-in-space), each line of which contains the class (in the `target` column) and the `Id` of the galaxy, separated by a comma.

Your score on this problem will be calculated as:

$$\min(100, (\text{accuracy} - 0.55)*294),$$

where `accuracy` is the proportion of correctly classified galaxies, according to your predictions.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Accuracy metric.","# Dataset Description

All the data in files.",False,True
