## ENGLISH 
**Metric**
This competition is evaluated on the categorization accuracy of your predictions (the percentage of images you get correct).","# Dataset Description

The data files train.csv (80213 rows) and test.csv (8904 rows) contain gray-scale images of hand-drawn letters of kyrgyz language, from '§¡' through '§Á'.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1664341%2F016e14a026ad84119abbb1c9246a5a12%2FUntitled-1.jpg?generation=1678707253530804&alt=media)

Each image is 50 pixels in height and 50 pixels in width, for a total of 2500 pixels in total. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255, inclusive.

**The training data set**, (train.csv), has 2501 columns. The first column, called ""label"", is the letter that was drawn by the user. The rest of the columns contain the pixel-values of the associated image.

Label is an integer between 1 and 36, inclusive (1 - §¡, 2 - §¢, 3 - §£, ..., 36 - §Á).

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1664341%2F6ddb64f08e1d949194085285cc8dd81b%2Ftrain_pic.png?generation=1678708137902596&alt=media)

Each pixel column in the training set has a name like pixel_x, where x is an integer between 0 and 2499, inclusive. To locate this pixel on the image, suppose that we have decomposed x as x = i * 50 + j, where i and j are integers between 0 and 49, inclusive. Then pixel_x is located on row i and column j of a 50 x 50 matrix, (indexing by zero).

For example, pixel_53 indicates the pixel that is in the fourth column from the left, and the second row from the top, as in the ascii-diagram below.


**The test data set**, (test.csv), is the same as the training set, except that it does not contain the ""label"" column.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1664341%2Fec2d307b94c043d62af4836482fa0201%2Ftest_pic.png?generation=1678708240825333&alt=media)

Your submission file should be in the following format: For each of the 8904 images in the test set, output a single line containing the ImageId and the letter you predict. For example, if you predict that the first image is of a 3, the second image is of a 7, the third image is of a 8, and the fourth image is 36, then your submission file would look like:
```
id,label
1,3
2,7
3,8 
4,36
(8900 more lines)
```

**The evaluation metric for this contest is the categorization accuracy**, or the proportion of test images that are correctly classified. For example, a categorization accuracy of 0.97 indicates that you have correctly classified all but 3% of the images.

## File

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **submission_sample.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10623,emo-decode-7000,"# Overview

?Sue?an los Algoritmos con Contar Ovejas de Datos Felices? ?Desaf¨ªo Emocional para Descifrar si Estamos 'Felices', 'Tristes' o Solo So?ando con un Buen Hiperpar¨¢metro!


