# Description

<section id=""ps1-problem-description"">


  <p>
    Stark Industries operates autonomous shipment routes across three primary environments:
    high-security <strong>labs</strong>, dense <strong>forests</strong>, and harsh <strong>desert</strong> corridors.
    A recent failure has knocked out the high-level navigation logic. What remains are rendered HUD-style
    map images and per-tile ¡°velocity boost¡± fields.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your task is to:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Reconstruct a <strong>20 ¡Á 20 tile grid</strong> from each map image (semantic perception).</li>
    <li>Plan a <strong>low-cost path</strong> from the start tile to the goal tile, using tile costs and boosts.</li>
    <li>Submit the path as a sequence of moves (<code>l</code>, <code>r</code>, <code>u</code>, <code>d</code>) for each map.</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h3>1. Map Structure & Tile Classes</h3>

  <p>
    Each map is a <strong>20 ¡Á 20 grid</strong>. Every tile belongs to one of the following classes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>0</code> ¨C Walkable path (normal floor / traversable ground).</li>
    <li><code>1</code> ¨C Wall (impassable obstacle).</li>
    <li><code>2</code> ¨C Hazard (walkable but more expensive: glue, puddle, quicksand, etc.).</li>
    <li><code>3</code> ¨C Start tile (shipment origin).</li>
    <li><code>4</code> ¨C Goal tile (shipment destination).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Class <code>1</code> is <strong>never traversable</strong>. All other classes are walkable but incur different
    base costs depending on the (hidden) terrain.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h3>2. Base Costs per Terrain</h3>

  <p>
    Internally, each map is one of three terrains: <strong>lab</strong>, <strong>forest</strong>, or <strong>desert</strong>.
    You are not given this label explicitly, but the sprite style and colors are different for each terrain.
    The evaluation script uses the following base costs:
  </p>

  <table border=""1"" cellpadding=""6"" cellspacing=""0"">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Terrain</th>
        <th>Class</th>
        <th>Description</th>
        <th>Base Cost</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <!-- Lab -->
      <tr>
        <td rowspan=""4"">Lab</td>
        <td><code>0</code></td>
        <td>Lab floor / walkable path</td>
        <td>1.0</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>2</code></td>
        <td>Hazard (glue)</td>
        <td>3.0</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>3</code></td>
        <td>Start tile</td>
        <td>1.0</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>4</code></td>
        <td>Goal tile</td>
        <td>2.0</td>
      </tr>

      <!-- Forest -->
      <tr>
        <td rowspan=""4"">Forest</td>
        <td><code>0</code></td>
        <td>Dirt path</td>
        <td>1.5</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>2</code></td>
        <td>Hazard (puddle)</td>
        <td>2.8</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>3</code></td>
        <td>Start tile</td>
        <td>1.5</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>4</code></td>
        <td>Goal tile</td>
        <td>2.5</td>
      </tr>

      <!-- Desert -->
      <tr>
        <td rowspan=""4"">Desert</td>
        <td><code>0</code></td>
        <td>Sand </td>
        <td>1.2</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>2</code></td>
        <td>Hazard (quicksand)</td>
        <td>3.7</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>3</code></td>
        <td>Start tile</td>
        <td>1.2</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>4</code></td>
        <td>Goal tile</td>
        <td>2.2</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    You may choose to treat all terrains the same (simple baseline) or explicitly infer terrain from visuals and
    model terrain-dependent costs.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h3>3. Velocity Boost & Cost Logic</h3>

  <p>
    Each tile <code>(i, j)</code> has an associated <strong>velocity boost</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>boost[i][j]</code> is a real number in <code>(?0.99, 0.99)</code>.</li>
    <li>Positive boost &rarr; cheaper to pass (tailwind / helpful flow).</li>
    <li>Negative boost &rarr; more expensive (headwind / resistance).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    When you step into tile <code>(i, j)</code> during evaluation, the true cost added is:
  </p>

  <pre><code>class_ij = grid[i][j]                     # true class (0,1,2,3,4)
terrain = {lab, forest, desert}           # hidden
base    = BASE_COST[terrain][class_ij]
b       = boost[i][j]

step_cost(i, j) = base - b
</code></pre>

  <p>
    The total path cost for a path <code>P</code> is:
  </p>
  <pre><code>total_cost(P) = ¦² step_cost(i, j) over all tiles (i, j) visited along P</code></pre>

  <p>
    Because base costs are at least 1 and <code>boost ¡Ê (?0.99, 0.99)</code>, each step has a strictly positive cost,
    and an optimal (minimum-cost) path is guaranteed to exist.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h3>4. Submission Format (High-Level)</h3>

  <p>
    You must submit a CSV file named:
  </p>
  <pre><code>submission.csv</code></pre>

  <p>with header:</p>
  <pre><code>image_id,path</code></pre>

  <p>and rows of the form:</p>
  <pre><code>0001,rrddrruuld...
0002,ddrruulld...
...</code></pre>

  <ul>
    <li><code>image_id</code> ¨C ID of the test image (e.g., <code>0001</code>).</li>
    <li>
      <code>path</code> ¨C sequence of moves using:
      <ul>
        <li><code>r</code> = move right (column + 1)</li>
        <li><code>l</code> = move left  (column ? 1)</li>
        <li><code>d</code> = move down  (row + 1)</li>
        <li><code>u</code> = move up    (row ? 1)</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The evaluation system starts from the (hidden) start tile, applies your moves, accumulates the true
    <code>step_cost</code> at each valid step, and compares your final cost to the optimal cost to compute your score.
    See the Evaluation section for the exact scoring formula.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h3>5. Sample Code</h3>

  <p>
    A baseline notebook <strong>(sample_code)</strong> is provided. It:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Loads the train and test folders.</li>
    <li>Implements a very crude perception pipeline.</li>
    <li>Uses a naive planner.</li>
    <li>Writes a valid <code>submission_baseline.csv</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Recommended first step:</strong>
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Run the sample code once end-to-end.</li>
    <li>Submit the generated CSV to Kaggle.</li>
    <li>Confirm that it appears on the leaderboard with a non-zero score.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Once you know the pipeline and format work, start improving the perception model and planning logic.
  </p>
</section>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<section id=""ps1-evaluation"">
  <h3>Evaluation Metric (Score out of 100)</h3>

  <p>
    For each test map, the evaluation server knows the <strong>true optimal cost</strong> 
    <code>min_cost</code> (computed using Dijkstra on the hidden grid and boost field).
  </p>

  <p>
    From your <code>submission.csv</code>, we simulate your <code>path</code> on the true map:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>If your path goes out of bounds, enters a wall, or never reaches the goal, that map is treated as <strong>invalid</strong> and scored ~0.</li>
    <li>If your path is valid, we compute the true <strong>path_cost</strong> by summing
      <code>step_cost = base_cost(terrain, class_ij) ? boost[i][j]</code> along the path.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For a valid map, we define:
  </p>
  <pre><code>ratio   = path_cost / min_cost         (ratio ¡Ý 1)
score_i = max(0, 100 / ratio)</code></pre>

  <p>
    So:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>path_cost = min_cost</code> &rarr; <code>score_i = 100</code> (perfect path).</li>
    <li><code>path_cost = 2 ¡Á min_cost</code> &rarr; <code>score_i = 50</code>.</li>
    <li>Worse/invalid paths &rarr; score approaches <code>0</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Your final competition score is the <strong>average of <code>score_i</code> over all test maps</strong>. 
    The leaderboard is sorted by this final score (higher is better, maximum 100).
  </p>
</section>",,False,True
10625,the-volatile-cargo-synapse-drive-ps-2,"# Overview

<section id=""ps2-overview"">
  <h2>Problem Statement 2 ¨C Semi-Active Suspension Control</h2>

  <p>
    Stark convoy vehicles carrying fragile lab cargo are still moving, but their active dampers are offline.
    Every bump on the road risks destroying the payload. In this problem statement, you will design a
    <strong>semi-active suspension controller</strong> for a quarter-car model and evaluate it on
    <strong>five</strong> different road profiles.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your job is to take the provided <strong>road displacement signals</strong> (time¨Cseries inputs),
    run them through a <strong>quarter-car dynamics model</strong> with your own control law for the
    damper coefficient <code>c(t)</code>, and then compute performance metrics that quantify:
    <strong>ride comfort</strong>, <strong>tire contact</strong>, and <strong>suspension travel</strong>.
    Kaggle will compare your reported metrics against a hidden Stark reference controller and score how
    close your controller performance is to the reference.
  </p>

  <h3>Hackathon Format</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Duration:</strong> 15 days from the official start of the hackathon.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Platform:</strong> This problem is hosted as a <strong>Kaggle competition</strong> with a live leaderboard.
      All scoring is based on submissions to Kaggle.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Submissions:</strong> You submit a <code>submission.csv</code> file to Kaggle.
      Each row corresponds to a road profile (<code>profile_1</code> to <code>profile_5</code>) and contains the
      required metrics:
      <code>rms_acc</code>, <code>rms_tire</code>, <code>max_susp</code>,
      <code>rms_jerk</code>, <code>comfort</code>, and overall <code>cost</code>
      computed from <em>your</em> simulation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Daily limit:</strong> Maximum <strong>5 submissions per day</strong> per team/account (Kaggle-enforced).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Leaderboard:</strong> Kaggle shows a public leaderboard during the competition.
      Final rankings are based on a hidden/private evaluation of the same metrics against Stark¡¯s
      reference controller.
    </li>
<li>
      <strong>IMPORTANT:</strong> Ensure your Kaggle Username follows this exact format <code>NAME_ENROLMENTNO</code>. Any entries not following this format will be removed immediately!
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Deliverables</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>During the competition:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Upload <code>submission.csv</code> files to Kaggle and track your performance on the leaderboard.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>At the end of the competition:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Code Repository (GitHub):</strong> containing:
          <ul>
            <li>Your quarter-car simulation and semi-active controller implementation.</li>
            <li>Metric computation and CSV generation code matching the required schema.</li>
            <li>Clear instructions to reproduce your final submission from scratch.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Short Technical Report:</strong> explaining:
          <ul>
            <li>Your controller design (skyhook, groundhook, hybrid, LQR, MPC, RL, custom logic, etc.).</li>
            <li>Your modelling and integration choices for the quarter-car system.</li>
            <li>
              How you tuned and evaluated performance across all five road profiles,
              including trade-offs between comfort, tire contact, and suspension travel.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A separate submission link / form for code and reports will be shared
        <strong>after the competition ends</strong>. Keep your repo clean and reproducible.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Important Instructions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Read everything carefully:</strong> The quarter-car equations, definitions of
      <code>rms_acc</code>, <code>rms_tire</code>, <code>max_susp</code>, <code>rms_jerk</code>,
      <code>comfort</code>, and <code>cost</code>, plus the CSV format, are all critical.
      If you get them wrong, your score will tank.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Computation:</strong> Most controllers will run fine on CPU. Use GPU only if you go for
      learning-based controllers (e.g. RL or neural networks).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Submission format:</strong> Follow the exact column names and ordering specified in the
      problem description. Wrong headers, wrong profile names, or missing rows will lead to failed
      submissions or zero scores.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Don¡¯t panic about low scores initially:</strong> A basic skyhook or hand-tuned controller
      will not match the Stark reference immediately. Use the 15 days to iterate, tune gains,
      and test different control strategies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Experiment freely:</strong> You can try skyhook, groundhook, hybrid logic, neural or
      reinforcement learning controllers, or any other physically sensible semi-active strategy,
      as long as you respect damping limits and basic stability.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How Kaggle Works (Recommended Reading)</h3>
  <p>
    If you are new to Kaggle competitions, we strongly recommend going through the following resource before you start:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href=""https://youtu.be/djbjDOBkz1k"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">
        Kaggle Competition Basics (how to join, create notebooks, and submit)
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Under the Stark storyline, this is a focused controls challenge:
    build a semi-active suspension controller, simulate it on five realistic road profiles,
    and quantify how well you protect the cargo compared to Stark¡¯s reference controller.
  </p>
</section>

----------------------
# Description

<section id=""ps2-problem-description"">

  <p>
    Stark convoy vehicles are moving fragile lab cargo from a compromised facility to a secure bunker. The
    <strong>active suspension system has degraded</strong>, leaving only onboard sensing and a constrained actuator command interface.
    Your job is to plug into the provided quarter-car simulation template, design an <strong>active suspension control law</strong>
    that outputs <code>c(t)</code>, and prove (numerically) that you can move the cargo without turning Pepper¡¯s coffee into a ceiling stain.
  </p>

  <p>
    In this version, you do <strong>not</strong> receive accelerometer signals in the dataset.
    <strong>You have to calculate acceleration profiles for each road and must take them as controller inputs for both sprung and unsprung mass</strong>.
    That means: after you solve the quarter-car dynamics at each time step, compute <code>a_s(t)</code> and <code>a_u(t)</code> from your simulated states
    (do not assume they are provided as CSV columns).
  </p>

  <p>
    In this problem, you will:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Simulate a <strong>2-DOF quarter-car model</strong> on five road profiles (from a CSV file).</li>
    <li>Design an <strong>active suspension controller</strong> that commands <code>c(t)</code> within limits.</li>
    <li>
      Use only <strong>vehicle constraints/parameters</strong> and the <strong>two acceleration profiles</strong>
      (<code>a_s(t)</code> and <code>a_u(t)</code>, computed by you during simulation) as controller inputs.
    </li>
    <li>
      Generate the displacement responses <code>z_s(t)</code> (required) and <code>z_u(t)</code> (bonus) from your simulated physics.
    </li>
    <li>Compute the required per-profile metrics and submit them via CSV to Kaggle.</li>
    <li>Include required displacement plots in a mandatory report submission.</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h3>1. Quarter-Car Approximation & Given Parameters</h3>

  <p>
    The system you work with is a standard <strong>quarter-car approximation</strong>.
    Conceptually, it has:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sprung mass</strong> ¨C vehicle body + cargo supported by the suspension.</li>
    <li><strong>Unsprung mass</strong> ¨C wheel + hub + part of suspension.</li>
    <li><strong>Suspension spring</strong> ¨C between sprung and unsprung mass.</li>
    <li><strong>Active suspension command channel</strong> ¨C controller command <code>c(t)</code> applied between sprung and unsprung mass.</li>
    <li><strong>Tire stiffness</strong> ¨C between wheel and road surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Road input</strong> ¨C vertical displacement <code>r(t)</code> from the dataset.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Acceleration profiles</strong> ¨C you must compute both mass accelerations from your simulation:
      sprung mass <code>a_s(t)</code> and unsprung mass <code>a_u(t)</code>. These are mandatory controller inputs.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <a href=""https://ibb.co/nq1qCCPd""><img src=""https://i.ibb.co/GfMfTTHY/unnamed.jpg"" alt=""unnamed"" border=""0""></a>

  <p>
    The numerical values used in the reference/starter code are:
  </p>

  <table border=""1"" cellpadding=""6"" cellspacing=""0"">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Parameter</th>
        <th>Symbol / Name</th>
        <th>Value</th>
        <th>Units</th>
        <th>Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Sprung mass</td>
        <td><code>m_s</code></td>
        <td>290.0</td>
        <td>kg</td>
        <td>Approx. quarter of vehicle body + payload mass (cargo sits on this mass).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Unsprung mass</td>
        <td><code>m_u</code></td>
        <td>59.0</td>
        <td>kg</td>
        <td>Wheel, hub, and associated hardware.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Suspension stiffness</td>
        <td><code>k_s</code></td>
        <td>16,000.0</td>
        <td>N/m</td>
        <td>Main suspension spring between body and wheel.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Tire stiffness</td>
        <td><code>k_t</code></td>
        <td>190,000.0</td>
        <td>N/m</td>
        <td>Effective stiffness of tire¨Croad contact.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Minimum command</td>
        <td><code>c_min</code></td>
        <td>800.0</td>
        <td>(as defined)</td>
        <td>Lower bound on the active suspension command interface.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Maximum command</td>
        <td><code>c_max</code></td>
        <td>3,500.0</td>
        <td>(as defined)</td>
        <td>Upper bound on the active suspension command interface.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Sampling frequency</td>
        <td>f<sub>s</sub></td>
        <td>200</td>
        <td>Hz</td>
        <td>Signals are sampled at 200 Hz.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Duration</td>
        <td>T</td>
        <td>20</td>
        <td>s</td>
        <td>Length of each road profile.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Actuator delay (to be accounted)</td>
        <td>¦Ó</td>
        <td>¡Ö 20</td>
        <td>ms</td>
        <td>
          Evaluation code models a simple 4-step delay buffer on <code>c(t)</code>.
          You must incorporate this real-world limitation to improve your score.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <hr />

  <h3>2. Dataset Format (<code>road_profiles.csv</code>)</h3>

  <p>
    You are given <strong>only</strong> the road displacement inputs. You must load all road columns for each profile,
    apply the same parameters/constraints, solve the quarter-car physics, and compute the full state evolution.
    As part of that simulation, you must compute <code>a_s(t)</code> and <code>a_u(t)</code> and use them inside your controller.
  </p>

  <p>
    The CSV header format is exactly:
  </p>

  <pre><code>t,profile_1,profile_2,profile_3,profile_4,profile_5</code></pre>

  <ul>
    <li><code>t</code> ¡ª time in seconds</li>
    <li><code>profile_k</code> ¡ª road displacement input <code>r_k(t)</code> (meters)</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h3>3. Controller Inputs & Required Outputs</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Controller inputs:</strong> Your controller must base its decision only on:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Known vehicle parameters and constraints (<code>m_s</code>, <code>m_u</code>, <code>k_s</code>, <code>k_t</code>, <code>c_min</code>, <code>c_max</code>, delay ¦Ó)</li>
    <li>Sprung-mass acceleration profile <code>a_s(t)</code> (computed from your simulated dynamics)</li>
    <li>Unsprung-mass acceleration profile <code>a_u(t)</code> (computed from your simulated dynamics)</li>
    <li>Any causal internal memory/state you maintain (filters, buffers, observers)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Outputs you must generate during simulation:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Control command</strong> <code>c(t)</code> (bounded by <code>c_min</code> and <code>c_max</code>)</li>
    <li><strong>Sprung-mass displacement</strong> <code>z_s(t)</code> (required; cargo is mounted here)</li>
    <li><strong>Unsprung-mass displacement</strong> <code>z_u(t)</code> (bonus/optional)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The intent is to keep the cargo/body displacement response <code>z_s(t)</code> as small and steady as possible, while keeping the ride smooth.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h3>4. Metrics to Submit</h3>

  <p>
    For each profile, compute the following from your simulated signals:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>RMS sprung-mass displacement</strong> ¡ª <code>rms_zs</code><br />
      <pre><code>z_s_rel(t) = z_s(t) - z_s(0)
rms_zs = RMS(z_s_rel)</code></pre>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Maximum sprung-mass displacement</strong> ¡ª <code>max_zs</code><br />
      <pre><code>max_zs = max_t | z_s(t) - z_s(0) |</code></pre>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>RMS jerk (sprung mass)</strong> ¡ª <code>rms_jerk</code><br />
      <pre><code>jerk(t) ¡Ö (a_s(t) - a_s(t-¦¤t)) / ¦¤t
rms_jerk = RMS(jerk)</code></pre>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Compute peak jerk internally as well:
  </p>

  <pre><code>jerk_max = max_t | jerk(t) |</code></pre>

  <p>
    The submitted <strong>comfort_score</strong> is defined as:
  </p>

  <pre><code>comfort_score = 0.5 * rms_zs + max_zs + 0.5 * rms_jerk + jerk_max</code></pre>

  <hr />

  <h3>5. Submission Guidelines (Mandatory)</h3>

  <h4>5.1 Kaggle CSV Submission (<code>submission.csv</code>)</h4>

  <p>
    You must submit a CSV file named <code>submission.csv</code> with <strong>one row per profile</strong>
    and the following header exactly:
  </p>

  <pre><code>profile,rms_zs,max_zs,rms_jerk,comfort_score</code></pre>

  <p>
    The <code>profile</code> column must use these exact identifiers (case-sensitive):
    <code>profile_1</code>, <code>profile_2</code>, <code>profile_3</code>, <code>profile_4</code>, <code>profile_5</code>.
    All numeric columns must be valid numbers (no blanks, no text, no units in the CSV).
  </p>

  <p>
    Example format:
  </p>

  <pre><code>profile,rms_zs,max_zs,rms_jerk,comfort_score
profile_1,0.0000,0.0000,0.0000,0.0000
profile_2,0.0000,0.0000,0.0000,0.0000
profile_3,0.0000,0.0000,0.0000,0.0000
profile_4,0.0000,0.0000,0.0000,0.0000
profile_5,0.0000,0.0000,0.0000,0.0000</code></pre>

  <h4>5.2 Report Submission (Graphs Required)</h4>

  <p>
    A short report (PDF or equivalent) is <strong>mandatory</strong>. For each road profile, include:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Required:</strong> Sprung-mass displacement plot ¡ª <code>z_s(t)</code> vs time (0¨C20 s). This is the primary motion used for evaluation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bonus (optional):</strong> Unsprung-mass displacement plot ¡ª <code>z_u(t)</code> vs time (0¨C20 s).
      Bonus points if the wheel assembly displacement is also well-controlled.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <ul>
    <li>Axes must be labeled: time (s) and displacement (m)</li>
    <li>Plots must cover the full run (0¨C20 s) at the original sampling rate (200 Hz)</li>
    <li>Each plot title must include the profile name (e.g., <code>profile_4: z_s(t)</code>)</li>
  </ul>

</section>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<section id=""ps2-evaluation"">
  <h3>Evaluation Metric (Accuracy out of 100)</h3>

  <p>
    For each road profile, Stark already has a <strong>reference controller</strong> that has been
    run on the same quarter-car model. Its results are stored internally as
    <code>reference_metrics.csv</code> and contain the ground-truth values:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>rms_zs_ref</code> ¨C RMS sprung-mass displacement (cargo/body motion), computed from <code>z_s(t)</code></li>
    <li><code>max_zs_ref</code> ¨C maximum absolute sprung-mass displacement (peak cargo/body excursion)</li>
    <li><code>rms_jerk_ref</code> ¨C RMS jerk (rate of change of sprung-mass acceleration)</li>
    <li><code>comfort_score_ref</code> ¨C combined comfort score (definition below)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Your <code>submission.csv</code> must provide the same four scored metrics for each profile:
    <code>rms_zs</code>, <code>max_zs</code>, <code>rms_jerk</code>, <code>comfort_score</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The evaluator compares your submitted values to the reference values using a
    <strong>relative-error accuracy</strong>:
  </p>

  <pre><code>Accuracy_m = ( 1 ? |pred_m ? ref_m| / |ref_m| ) ¡Á 100%</code></pre>

  <p>
    where <code>m ¡Ê {rms_zs, max_zs, rms_jerk, comfort_score}</code>.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Perfect match: <code>pred_m = ref_m ? Accuracy_m = 100%</code>.</li>
    <li>10% relative error: <code>|pred_m ? ref_m| = 0.1 ¡¤ |ref_m| ? Accuracy_m = 90%</code>.</li>
    <li>Large errors drive the accuracy towards 0 (values below 0 are clamped to 0 in the final score).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The <strong>comfort_score</strong> is defined as:
  </p>

  <pre><code>comfort_score = 0.5 * rms_zs + max_zs + 0.5 * rms_jerk + jerk_max</code></pre>

  <p>
    where <code>jerk_max = max_t |jerk(t)|</code> and <code>jerk(t) ¡Ö (a_s(t) ? a_s(t?¦¤t)) / ¦¤t</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For each profile, the per-metric accuracies are combined into a single
    <strong>overall accuracy</strong> using fixed weights:
  </p>

  <pre><code>overall_accuracy(profile) =
    0.35 ¡Á Accuracy_rms_zs
  + 0.30 ¡Á Accuracy_max_zs
  + 0.20 ¡Á Accuracy_rms_jerk
  + 0.15 ¡Á Accuracy_comfort_score</code></pre>

  <p>
    This weighting keeps the focus on <strong>cargo/body displacement control</strong> (RMS and peak displacement),
    while still rewarding smoothness via jerk. The comfort score is included as an aggregate consistency check.
  </p>

  <p>
    Finally, your <strong>competition score</strong> is the mean of
    <code>overall_accuracy(profile)</code> over all five road profiles,
    reported on a 0¨C100 scale (higher is better).
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>NOTE:</strong> If any metric¡¯s accuracy becomes negative due to extreme relative error, it is clamped to 0 before aggregation.
    To avoid pathological scoring, aim for a minimum of <strong>10% overall accuracy</strong>.
  </p>
</section>",,False,True
10626,aqc-midnight-roast-logistic-regression,"# Overview

Can you predict whether a customer will enjoy the new ¡°Midnight Roast¡± dark blend? Use logistic regression on 500 taste-test records and climb the AUC leaderboard.
----------------------
# Description

### Story
The specialty caf¨¦ Bean & Bone is launching a bold ¡°Midnight Roast¡± and wants to send coupons only to people likely to love it. They collected 500 anonymous taste-tester ratings. Your job: build an interpretable logistic-regression model that outputs P(Likes_Coffee = 1) so marketing can target the right customers.
### Data
All synthetic, but realistic.
train.csv ¨C 350 customers (target revealed)
test.csv  ¨C 150 customers (target hidden)
sample_submission.csv

| Column                            | Meaning                   | Type  | Missing    |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ----- | ---------- |
| Id                                | observation id (index)    | int   | no         |
| Age                               | customer age (years)      | float | **5 %**    |
| Prefer\_Dark\_Chocolate           | 1 = yes                   | int   | no         |
| Usually\_Drinks\_Espresso         | 1 = yes                   | int   | no         |
| Coffee\_Acidity\_Rating           | 1¨C10 scale                | float | **5 %**    |
| Roast\_Level                      | 1=Light, 2=Medium, 3=Dark | int   | no         |
| Sugar\_Preference                 | grams typically added     | float | **5 %**    |
| Previous\_Dark\_Roast\_Experience | 1 = yes                   | int   | no         |
| Likes\_Coffee                     | target (0/1)              | int   | train only |


### Task
Clean / impute missing values.
Encode categorical variables (Roast_Level).
Train logistic regression (plain, L1, or L2).
Produce predicted probabilities for the 150 test customers.
Share a short kernel showing coefficient interpretation & ROC curve.
### Rules
Only logistic-regression family allowed (no trees, no boosting).
No external data, no target leakage.
Kernel must be public to appear on leaderboard.
### Metric
ROC-AUC (higher is better).
Public / private split: 30 % / 70 % of test rows.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve:
**$$\mathrm{AUC}=\int_{0}^{1}\mathrm{TPR}(T)\,d(\mathrm{FPR}(T))$$**


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

(midnight-roast-challenge / Kaggle-ready Markdown)
### Files
- `train.csv` ¨C 350 taste-tester records with ground-truth label
- `test.csv` ¨C 150 customers; labels hidden
- `sample_submission.csv` ¨C correct upload format


## Columns

| Column                            | Meaning                                   | Values | Missing    |
| --------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------ | ---------- |
| Id                                | unique observation id (index)             | int    | no         |
| Age                               | customer age (years)                      | float  | 5 %        |
| Prefer\_Dark\_Chocolate           | likes dark chocolate                      | 0, 1   | no         |
| Usually\_Drinks\_Espresso         | usually drinks espresso                   | 0, 1   | no         |
| Coffee\_Acidity\_Rating           | perceived acidity 1¨C10                    | float  | 5 %        |
| Roast\_Level                      | roast darkness: 1=Light, 2=Medium, 3=Dark | int    | no         |
| Sugar\_Preference                 | grams sugar typically added               | float  | 5 %        |
| Previous\_Dark\_Roast\_Experience | has tried dark roast before               | 0, 1   | no         |
| Likes\_Coffee                     | target: 1 = enjoyed ¡°Midnight Roast¡±      | 0, 1   | train only |


#### What am I predicting?
A single binary label Likes_Coffee for each customer in test.csv.
#### Acronyms / Terms
MCAR ¨C Missing Completely At Random (small fraction in Age, Acidity, Sugar)",False,True
