10629,beyond-visible-spectrum-ai-for-agriculture-2026p2,"# Overview

# ICPR 2026 Competition on ¡°Beyond Visible Spectrum: AI for Agriculture¡±

## Abstract

The ICPR 2026 Competition on ¡°Beyond Visible Spectrum: AI for Agriculture¡± offers a unique opportunity for researchers to advance computer vision techniques in agricultural crop disease monitoring. Building on a successful series of competitions from 2022 to 2025, which attracted over 1000 participants from over 10 countries across Europe, Asia, and North America.

The challenge focuses on developing innovative deep learning algorithms using extensive multi/hyperspectral and satellite remote sensing datasets across two main tasks:

*   **Task 1: Automated Multimodal Crop Disease Diagnosis from Multimodal Remote Sensing Imagery**: Participants will be provided with RGB, multispectral (G/R/RE/NIR), and hyperspectral imagery. They will be encouraged to develop multimodal models capable of adapting to remote sensing data of varying spectral and spatial information to classify the data into three categories: 'rust,' 'healthy,' and 'other.'
*   **Task 2: Boosting Automatic Crop Diseases Classification using Sentinel Satellite Data and Self-Supervised Learning (SSL)**: Addressing the critical issue of limited labelled data in agriculture, this task requires participants to employ self-supervised learning (SSL) to learn meaningful representations from large, unlabelled Sentinel satellite datasets before fine-tuning on limited labelled data.

The primary goal is to enhance the accuracy and efficiency of identifying crop diseases, contributing to significant advancements in precision farming and sustainable agricultural practices to support global food security.


### Background and Task
Remote sensing technology offers continuous global monitoring of vast agricultural areas in real-time, enabling early identification and mitigation of threats. Advances in digital imaging have led to various image-based methods for automated plant management, showing great potential for crop disease diagnosis using RGB, thermal, multi-spectral, and hyperspectral sensors. Hyperspectral imagery (HSI), with its numerous narrow spectral bands, provides detailed spectral¨Cspatial information, offering enhanced diagnostic accuracy for disease infestation. However, the high data dimensionality of HSI can also present challenges, leading to research into new methods like super-resolution and automated detection models.

Deep learning methods have shown promising results in remote sensing imagery analysis. Identifying relevant spectral features and extracting meaningful representations from remote sensing data are crucial for effective deep learning. However, the high number of spectral bands and complex relationships in remote sensing images pose significant challenges for feature selection and extraction, necessitating the development of new deep learning algorithms.

Building on our research in precision agriculture and extensive experience, we are hosting The ICPR 2026 Competition on ¡°Beyond Visible Spectrum: AI for Agriculture.¡± This is our fifth consecutive year running this competition. We have seen a remarkable and sustained increase in community engagement, demonstrating the critical importance of this research area.


## Competition Schedule
*   **Launch Date**: December 15, 2025
*   **Submission Opening**: December 30, 2025
*   **Deadline for Competition Participants**: March 1st, 2026
*   **Winner Announcement**: March 15, 2026
*   **Initial Submission of Competition Reports Deadline**: April 1st, 2026
*   **Presentation at ICPR Conference**: August 17¨C21, 2026

----------------------
# Description

 **Task 2: Boosting Automatic Crop Diseases Classification using Sentinel Satellite Data and Self-Supervised Learning**
    *   Participants are challenged to employ self-supervised learning (SSL) techniques to identify various types of crop diseases from remote sensing imagery.
    *   Addresses the issue of limited labelled data in remote agricultural regions.
    *   Evaluation will be based on transfer performance, measuring how well the learned representations generalize to new tasks after fine-tuning on a labelled dataset.

----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: 3
- **1st Place**?-?¡ê100
- **2nd Place** - ¡ê60
- **3rd Place** - ¡ê 40

## Organizers
*   **Prof. Liangxiu Han**, Manchester Metropolitan University, United Kingdom
*   **Prof. Wenjiang Huang**, Aerospace Information Research Institute, Chinese Academy of Sciences

**Technical Committee**:
*   Dr. Xin Zhang, Manchester Metropolitan University, UK
*   Dr. Yue Shi, Manchester Metropolitan University, UK
*   Dr. Yingying Dong, Aerospace Information Research Institute, Chinese Academy of Sciences","# Dataset Description

# Competition: Beyond Visible Spectrum - AI for Agriculture 2026

## Overview
Participants are challenged to develop robust **Self-Supervised Learning (SSL)** methods to extract meaningful representations from satellite imagery, which will then be fine-tuned for **Crop Disease Prediction**.

Innovative approaches that leverage **Multimodal** data are highly encouraged to improve classification accuracy under varying environmental conditions.

## 1. Self-Supervised Training Data (Unlabeled)
The core dataset consists of unlabeled Sentinel-2 (S2A) time-series imagery, organized by location and acquisition time. This data is intended for pre-training SSL models (e.g., MoCo, SimCLR, MAE) to learn temporal and spectral features of agricultural land.

**Download:** https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/d7cmjdd10qlkp9w895me2/s2a.tar.gz?rlkey=xk1pka3q4nokpmj7dilfys5lw&st=xn8g7k53&dl=0)

If you encounter issues downloading the full `s2a.tar.gz` file due to network instability or size limits, you can download the dataset in split parts using the links below.

We have updated the link.

Please download all the following parts:

- [s2a.tar.gz.partaa](https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fo/0ca5g354y1tn8cw7dwk85/AITOGzyxyqCoKJjhzXbuOH4/s2a.tar.gz.partaa?rlkey=h8yirspxk6r312634qabi412m&st=x90erzjv&dl=0)
- [s2a.tar.gz.partab](https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fo/0ca5g354y1tn8cw7dwk85/AN5iLpgIOwJ6TWNhqAzuDcw/s2a.tar.gz.partab?rlkey=h8yirspxk6r312634qabi412m&st=v6m8n9w7&dl=0)
- [s2a.tar.gz.partac](https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fo/0ca5g354y1tn8cw7dwk85/AJutAYqAJ8hQ69aIL4RXZyQ/s2a.tar.gz.partac?rlkey=h8yirspxk6r312634qabi412m&st=ed7eqamq&dl=0)
- [s2a.tar.gz.partad](https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fo/0ca5g354y1tn8cw7dwk85/AP0sa-5mR8mzBoYBN10sa9w/s2a.tar.gz.partad?rlkey=h8yirspxk6r312634qabi412m&st=p0bii2m4&dl=0)
- [s2a.tar.gz.partae](https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fo/0ca5g354y1tn8cw7dwk85/AG3d3HP7pOgePdY4ni2yJ9s/s2a.tar.gz.partae?rlkey=h8yirspxk6r312634qabi412m&st=odnubqp0&dl=0)


### Data Structure
The data is organized hierarchically:
Band_File.tif

*   **Band Files**: Individual GeoTIFF files for each spectral band.

### Available Bands (Sentinel-2)
Each acquisition folder contains the following 12 spectral bands:
*   **Coastal/Aerosol**: `B1.tif`
*   **Visible**: `B2.tif` (Blue), `B3.tif` (Green), `B4.tif` (Red)
*   **Red Edge**: `B5.tif`, `B6.tif`, `B7.tif`
*   **NIR (Near Infrared)**: `B8.tif`, `B8A.tif`
*   **Water Vapour**: `B9.tif`
*   **SWIR (Short-Wave Infrared)**: `B11.tif`, `B12.tif`


## 2. Supervised Training Data (Labeled)
This dataset contains labeled samples for the downstream classification task.

##Submission Columns

*   `Id` - file name
*   `Category`?- Aphid, Rust, RPH, Blast",False,True
