10641,tda-aiml-cosmic-stability-problem-0f3ebc,"# Overview

# Cosmic Stability Index Challenge

## Predict Spacecraft Stability in Deep Space

Welcome to the Cosmic Stability Index competition! In this challenge, you'll predict the operational stability of spacecraft navigating through deep space environments.

### Goal

Your task is to build a model that predicts the **Cosmic Stability Index** - a critical metric measuring spacecraft operational stability - based on telemetry data and environmental measurements from deep space sensors.

You'll be working with 12 features including:
- Space environment data (cosmic radiation, star luminosity, plasma density)
- Spacecraft systems (hull temperature, engine thrust, magnetic field strength)
- Deep space phenomena (dark matter flux, nebula density, photon noise)

### Evaluation

Submissions are scored using **R2 (R-squared)**, where higher is better. A perfect score is 1.0.

### Data

- **Training set:** 10,000 observations with target values
- **Test set:** 3,000 observations to predict

This is an educational competition designed to develop practical machine learning skills in regression modeling.

Good luck! ??

----------------------
# Description

# ?? Cosmic Stability Index - Regression Challenge

## Overview
Welcome to the Cosmic Stability Index prediction competition! Your mission is to predict the stability of spacecraft operating in deep space environments based on telemetry data and environmental measurements.

## Dataset Description

### Files
- **train.csv** - Training set with features and target (10,000 rows)
- **test.csv** - Test set with features only (3,000 rows)
- **sample_submission.csv** - Sample submission file in the correct format


### Target Variable
`cosmic_stability_index` - A continuous value (0-100+) representing the stability rating of the spacecraft

### Features (12 total)

| Feature | Description | Unit/Range |
|---------|-------------|------------|
| `id` | Unique identifier | 0 to 12,999 |
| `star_luminosity` | Nearby star brightness | Solar luminosity units (0.1-10) |
| `cosmic_radiation` | Radiation exposure level | mSv/year (50-500) |
| `orbital_velocity` | Spacecraft velocity | km/s (5-52) |
| `plasma_density` | Surrounding plasma concentration | particles/cm3 (0.1-20) |
| `solar_wind_pressure` | Solar wind force | nPa (0.5-10) |
| `hull_temperature` | External hull temperature | Kelvin (200-400) |
| `magnetic_field_strength` | Local magnetic field | nT (1-100) |
| `dark_matter_flux` | Dark matter interaction rate | arbitrary units (-5 to 5) |
| `nebula_density` | Surrounding nebula material | g/cm3 (0.001-0.1) |
| `photon_noise_level` | Background radiation noise | dB (10-102) |
| `engine_thrust` | Propulsion system output | kN (100-1000) |
| `gravity_well_depth` | Gravitational field strength | arbitrary units (1-20) |

## Tips & Hints

Sharam nahi ati wese bhi GPT karne wale ho aur hint le rahe ?!



## Good Luck! ??

May your models achieve stellar performance across the cosmic void!

---


----------------------
# Evaluation and Submission


## Evaluation Metric
**R2 Score (Coefficient of Determination)**

Your predictions will be evaluated using the R2 score on the test set. Higher is better, with 1.0 being perfect prediction.



## Submission Format
Your submission file should contain exactly 3,000 rows (excluding header) with two columns:

```csv
id,cosmic_stability_index
10000,5.23
10001,8.91
10002,3.45
...
```
----------------------
# Prizes

