10676,face-matching-aicc-round-2,"# Overview

This competition is organized by the **IOAI Community Contest** group (not affiliated with IOAI), which is made up of IOAI alumni. It is meant to **prepare** students for the IOAI (International Olympiad in Artificial Intelligence).

Deep inside the North-Pole workshop, Santa¡¯s portrait-elves have painted 100 tiny festive portraits of 15 very special guests¡ªcelebrity carolers who will lead the midnight choir. Each guest sat only once, yet their likeness appears again and again among the stacked canvases, hidden by holly, candle-glow, and ribbon. Mrs. Claus hands you a single golden frame: the reference portrait for each of the 15 stars. Your mission, before the sleigh bells ring, is to wander the candlelit gallery and pick out every other canvas that bears the same face, even if a Santa hat or string of lights partly disguises it. Find them all and the album will be complete, ensuring every singer sees themselves reflected in the Christmas Eve concert program.

Formally, you are given a collection of 100 images of 15 different celebrities. Your task is to identify which other images in the dataset show the **same celebrity** as a given reference image.

You will be provided with:

- **`ref_img.csv`**: Contains one reference image ID per celebrity (15 rows total)
- **`images/`**: Directory containing all 108 images as `.jpg` files, named with 3-digit IDs (e.g., `000.jpg`, `001.jpg`, etc.)

Baseline: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1cj_3KEJamQaJ2cc-lVTjn-VXkxsyLmTN/view?usp=sharing
To use the baseline, copy it to your Google Drive. We will also upload soon a notebook on Kaggle's Code section.
----------------------
# Description

For each reference image in `ref_img.csv`, you must find all images from the dataset that **show the same celebrity** as the reference image. The reference image itself should **not** be included in your results.

Note: Each celebrity appears a variable number of times in the dataset.

Constraints:
- **No face-specific models**: You cannot use pretrained models specifically designed for face recognition or face feature extraction (e.g., FaceNet, ArcFace, VGGFace, DeepFace)
- **General pretrained models allowed**: You may use other general-purpose pretrained models (e.g., CLIP, ResNet, ViT, etc.)
- **No face-specific libraries**: You cannot use libraries specifically built for face detection/recognition (e.g., face_recognition, dlib, MediaPipe Face). However you are allowed to use anything included in OpenCV, without additional downloads.
- **No manual labeling**: You cannot manually group images and you also cannot manually write text labels for each image
- **Memory limit**: Solutions must run with less than 16GB VRAM
- **Time limit**: The entire notebook execution must complete in under 30 minutes

## Evaluation

Your submission will be evaluated using **F1 Score**, which measures how well you balance finding all matching faces while avoiding false matches.

- **F1**: The harmonic mean of precision and recall. A good F1 means you found most of the matching faces without making too many mistakes.

The final score is the average F1 across all 15 reference images.

## Submission Format

Create a `submission.csv` file with the following format:

| Column    | Description                                                            |
| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `ref_img` | 3-digit ID of the reference image                                      |
| `photos`  | Pipe-separated (`\|`) list of image IDs that match the reference image |

**Example:**
```
ref_img,photos
042,001|002|003
089,001|002
031,001|002|003
```

**Important:**
- Each submission must have exactly 15 rows (one per reference image)
- Do not include the reference image in your predictions
- IDs must be formatted as 3-digit strings (e.g., `005` not `5`)
- Each celebrity has at least 5 photos",,False,True
10677,essay-gap-aicc-round-2,"# Overview

This competition is organized by the **IOAI Community Contest** group (not affiliated with IOAI), which is made up of IOAI alumni. It is meant to **prepare** students for the IOAI (International Olympiad in Artificial Intelligence).

Santa¡¯s elves were racing to finish the annual Christmas storybook for children worldwide, but one page stayed stubbornly blank. In the tale, a quiet snowfall had just begun outside the workshop windows; the next scene needed to explain how the gentle hush led the reindeer to gather at the stable door, eager for the long flight. Mrs. Claus read the four candidate sentences aloud beside the crackling fireplace: one spoke of jingling bells, another of sugar-cookie crumbs, a third of glowing northern lights, the last of a distant choir. Only the sentence that wove the stillness of the snow into the reindeers¡¯ anticipation would let the story glide forward like a sleigh on fresh powder. The elves¡¯ task¡ªidentical to yours¡ªwas to choose the single line that kept the narrative glow alive so the book could be wrapped and tucked into every stocking by dawn.

In this task, you are given essays with a missing sentence in the middle. Your goal is to identify which of the four provided options best completes the gap. This tests your understanding of textual coherence, context, and semantic relationships between sentences.

Constraints:
- **Memory limit**: Solutions must run with less than 16GB VRAM
- **Time limit**: The entire notebook execution must complete in under 30 minutes
- **General pretrained models allowed**: You may use other general-purpose pretrained models (e.g., CLIP, ResNet, ViT, etc.)
- **no code written by an LLM**: since this is an educational contest, you will win most by writing the code on your own. Do not vibe code.

Baseline: https://drive.google.com/file/d/14MRVq9KsgFIinUDZobP3YqxFDDmIIYld/view?usp=sharing
To use the baseline, copy it to your Google Drive. We will also upload soon a notebook on Kaggle's Code section.
----------------------
# Description

## Dataset

You have access to two files:
- **train.csv**: Contains labeled examples with complete sentence mappings
- **test.csv**: Contains unlabeled examples where you must predict the correct option

`train.csv`:
- `sampleID`: Unique identifier for each essay
- `before`: Text preceding the gap (lorem ipsum in previews)
- `after`: Text following the gap (lorem ipsum in previews)
- `opt_0`, `opt_1`, `opt_2`, `opt_3`: Four candidate sentences to fill the gap
- `label`: Correct option index (0, 1, 2, or 3)

`test.csv`:
- `sampleID`: Unique identifier for each essay
- `before`: Text preceding the gap
- `after`: Text following the gap
- `opt_0`, `opt_1`, `opt_2`, `opt_3`: Four candidate sentences to choose from

## Evaluation
Submissions are evaluated using **F1 Score** (macro-averaged across all four classes):

$$F1 = 2 \times \frac{\text{Precision} \times \text{Recall}}{\text{Precision} + \text{Recall}}$$

Macro-averaging ensures each option class (0, 1, 2, 3) is weighted equally regardless of frequency in the test set.

## Submission
Submit a CSV file:

```
sampleID,answer
100,0
101,1
102,2
103,3
```

- `sampleID`: ID from test.csv
- `answer`: Predicted option index (0, 1, 2, or 3)

All test samples must be included with valid indices.",,False,True
