10682,track1-operator-development-and-optimization-flagos-challenge,"# Overview

Main Competition Page: [FlagOS Open Computing Global Challenge](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/flag-os-open-computing-global-challenge/overview)

---

With the development of large models, operator performance has become a key factor affecting model throughput and latency. As an open-source GPU programming language and compiler, Triton combines cross-hardware compatibility with excellent usability,  becoming one of the mainstream choices for developing custom operators in both industry and academia gradually.

FlagGems, an open-source operator library focused on the Triton ecosystem, aims to provide efficient, universal, and scalable operator solutions for large model training and inference, building an active and competitive open-source ecosystem. With the core tenet of ""Empowering the Open-Source Ecosystem and Attracting Core Contributors"", this operator development competition serves as a platform for developers to exchange technologies and showcase their capabilities. Centered on actual open-source needs, the competition features 20 operator development topics of varying difficulty, covering three categories: basic mathematical operations, high-frequency deep learning operators, and cutting-edge complex scenario operators. It not only offers a low-threshold entry path into open-source for novice developers but also reserves room for senior technical experts to expand technical boundaries.

All participating solutions will undergo strict community review. Winning works will be directly integrated into the FlagGems open-source library, and participants will simultaneously become official community contributors, enjoying exclusive contributor benefits (such as the right to participate in ecosystem roadmap planning and preferential access to technical resources). Through the model of ""promoting construction through competitions"", the competition aims to gather the wisdom of global developers, enrich the functional coverage of the operator library, improve core performance, strengthen cross-platform adaptability, and promote FlagGems to become a highly influential open-source operator ecosystem in the AI field.
----------------------
# Description

The competition includes 20 operators, categorized by difficulty and technical assessment dimensions to accommodate developers of different skill levels, encouraging more participants to join.

- Difficulty Distribution: 8 easy operator topics (basic mathematics / general basic operators), 8 medium operator topics (classic deep learning operators), 4 difficult operator topics (cutting-edge complex scenario operators);

- Design Logic: Following the core thread of ""Basic Implementation ¡ú Performance Optimization ¡ú Innovative Breakthrough"", it forms a comprehensive capability assessment system¡ªfrom the functional implementation of basic mathematical operators, to performance tuning and back propagation adaptation of high-frequency deep learning operators, and further to algorithm innovation and scenario landing of cutting-edge complex operators;

- Core Orientation: All topics are derived from the actual needs and technical gaps of the FlagGems open-source ecosystem, focusing on core scenarios such as general mathematical computing, computer vision, speech recognition, and deep learning framework adaptation, ensuring that participating results have open-source application value;

- Assessment Dimensions: Covering multiple dimensions including ""functional correctness, performance competitiveness, open-source adaptability, cross-platform compatibility, test case completeness, and code readability"", it emphasizes both engineering implementation capabilities and encourages technical innovation exploration.


## Competition Timeline

- 01 Registration 

Jan 9 ¨C May 20 Register via the official portal to secure your spot.

- 02 Development 

Self-hosted Environment: Jan 9 ¨C May 20

Official Environment: Feb 24 ¨C May 20 (Access to sponsor-provided resources)

- 03 Evaluation: Rolling Review

We implement a ""Live Review & Merge"" system for instant feedback:

Real-time Review: PRs are reviewed and merged immediately upon qualification.

Bounty Claim: Once a PR is merged, the task is closed and the prize is claimed.

Dynamic Update: New operator tasks will be added continuously to the task pool.

- 04 Final Results

Early June Official announcement of all winners and final rankings.


---

## 3. Details

### Topic List

The list of 20 topics is as follows:

| No. | Operator | Difficulty | Schema | Category | Reference |
|-----|----------|------------|--------|----------|-----------|
| 1 | log10 | Easy | `log10(Tensor self) -> Tensor` | Pointwise | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.log10.html) |
| 2 | logaddexp | Easy | `logaddexp(Tensor self, Tensor other) -> Tensor` | Pointwise | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.logaddexp.html) |
| 3 | cosh | Easy | `cosh(Tensor self) -> Tensor` | Pointwise | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.cosh.html) |
| 4 | gcd | Easy | `gcd(Tensor self, Tensor other) -> Tensor` | Pointwise | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.gcd.html) |
| 5 | tril | Easy | `tril(input, diagonal=0, *, out=None) ¡ú Tensor` | Pointwise | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.tril.html) |
| 6 | roll | Easy | `torch.roll(input, shifts, dims=None)` | Layout | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.roll.html) |
| 7 | Leaky ReLU | Easy | `torch.nn.LeakyReLU(negative_slope=0.01, inplace=False) ¡ú Tensor` | Pointwise | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.LeakyReLU.html) |
| 8 | asinh | Easy | `asinh(input: Tensor, *, out: Optional[Tensor]) ¡ú Tensor` | Pointwise | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.asinh.html) |
| 9 | upsample_nearest2d | Medium | `upsample_nearest2d(Tensor self, SymInt[2] output_size, ...)` | Upsampling | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.UpsamplingNearest2d.html) |
| 10 | scatter_reduce | Medium | `scatter_reduce.two(Tensor self, int dim, Tensor index, ...)` | Index | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.scatter_reduce.html) |
| 11 | median | Medium | `median.dim(Tensor self, int dim, bool keepdim=False) -> (Tensor values, Tensor indices)` | Reduction | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.median.html) |
| 12 | smooth_l1_loss | Medium | `smooth_l1_loss(Tensor self, Tensor target, int reduction=Mean, float beta=1.0) -> Tensor` | Loss | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.smooth_l1_loss.html) |
| 13 | pixel_shuffle | Medium | `torch.nn.functional.pixel_shuffle(input, upscale_factor) ¡ú Tensor` | Layout | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.pixel_shuffle.html) |
| 14 | conv_transpose2d | Medium | `torch.nn.functional.conv_transpose2d(input, weight, ...)` | Conv | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.conv_transpose2d.html) |
| 15 | avg_pool3d | Medium | `avg_pool3d(input, kernel_size, stride, padding, ...) ¡ú Tensor` | Reduction | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.avg_pool3d.html) |
| 16 | max_pool3d | Medium | `max_pool3d(input, kernel_size, stride, padding, ...) ¡ú Tensor` | Reduction | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.max_pool3d.html) |
| 17 | chunk_gated_delta_rule | Difficult | / | Fused | No separate API; applied to Qwen3-Next model. [Qwen Models](https://huggingface.co/Qwen) / [Reference Implementation](https://github.com/NVlabs/GatedDeltaNet/blob/main/lit_gpt/gated_delta_rule_ops/chunk.py#L616) |
| 18 | svd | Difficult | `svd(Tensor self, bool some=True, bool compute_uv=True) -> (Tensor U, Tensor S, Tensor V)` | Linalg | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.svd.html) |
| 19 | ctc_loss | Difficult | `torch.nn.functional.ctc_loss(log_probs, targets, ...)` | Loss | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.ctc_loss.html) |
| 20 | grid_sample | Difficult | `grid_sample(input, grid, mode, padding_mode, align_corners) ¡ú Tensor` | Special | [PyTorch Docs](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.grid_sample.html) |

All topics are designed based on the actual application needs of the FlagGems open-source ecosystem. The core requirements are the correctness of operator functions, competitive performance, and adaptability to the open-source ecosystem. All winning works must meet the FlagGems open-source contribution standards and can be directly integrated into the community open-source library.

---

### 3.1.1 Functional Correctness

The output results of all operators must align with official benchmarks:

- Cover boundary use cases (such as extreme values, zero values, negative numbers, empty tensors, dynamic shapes)
- Abnormal inputs (such as illegal dtypes, dimension mismatches) must have clear error handling logic

**Integer/logical operators** (e.g., `gcd`, `tril`):  
Output results must be completely consistent with benchmarks without deviations.

**Floating-point operators** (e.g., `log10`, `cosh`, `svd`):  
The error with PyTorch's official implementation must be within an acceptable range.

#### Accuracy Evaluation Criteria

For computation-type operators, the accuracy acceptance standard adopts the `torch.allclose` interface:

```python
torch.allclose(input: Tensor, other: Tensor, rtol: float = 1e-05, atol: float = 1e-08, equal_nan: bool = False) ¡ú bool
```

This function checks if `input` and `other` satisfy the condition:

  $$| \text{input}_i - \text{other}_i | \leq \text{atol} + \text{rtol} \times | \text{other}_i |$$

If the operator (or data type) can achieve bit-level consistency relative to the reference, both `rtol` and `atol` are set to 0; otherwise, `rtol` is `1e-4`, and `atol` is related to the computation data type as follows:

| Computation Data Type | atol |
|-----------------------|------|
| torch.bool | 0 |
| torch.uint8 | 0 |
| torch.int8 | 0 |
| torch.int16 | 0 |
| torch.int32 | 0 |
| torch.int64 | 0 |
| torch.float8_e4m3fn | 1.00E-03 |
| torch.float8_e5m2 | 1.00E-03 |
| torch.float8_e4m3fnuz | 1.00E-03 |
| torch.float8_e5m2fnuz | 1.00E-03 |
| torch.float16 | 1.00E-03 |
| torch.float32 | 1.30E-06 |
| torch.bfloat16 | 0.016 |
| torch.float64 | 1.00E-07 |
| torch.complex32 | 1.00E-03 |
| torch.complex64 | 1.30E-06 |

---

### 3.1.2 Open-Source Adaptation Requirements

- Code must comply with FlagGems community coding standards (variable naming, function comments, code layering, etc.), with no redundant code or hardcoding
- Operator interfaces must align with PyTorch's homonymous operator interfaces (parameter names, input/output formats, default values) to reduce the usage cost for community users
- Complete unit test cases (based on pytest) must be provided, and the code coverage must meet the requirements of the FlagGems repository

---

### 3.1.3 Compatibility Requirements

**Data Types:** Support at least `float32` / `float16`

**Hardware Adaptation:**
-  Participants are allowed to use AI accelerators provided by themselves or AI computing  accelerators provided by the organizer. Additional points may be awarded for successfully adapting to at least one type of AI computing accelerator provided by the organizer.

> **Note:** The organizer will provide computing power support. Those in need of computing power and development environment may apply via the ¡°Track 1¡± section on the official FlagOS platform at https://flagos.io/RaceDetail?id=296flq8k&lang=cn.

---

### 3.1.4 Test Case Completeness Requirements

The completeness of test cases and operator functions is one of the core assessment objectives. Test cases for all topics must fully cover the following dimensions:

**Input Shape:**
- Small sizes (e.g., 1¡Á1, 8¡Á8)
- Regular sizes (e.g., 64¡Á64, 256¡Á256)
- Large sizes (e.g., 1024¡Á1024, 4096¡Á4096)

**Input Dimensions:**
- Cover the entire range of legal dimensions defined by the operator
- Example: `tril` must cover 2D matrices and 4D batch matrices; `roll` must cover 1-5D tensors

**Parameter Modes:**
- Cover default values, boundary values, and special value modes of all optional parameters

**Functional Completeness:**
- Test cases must verify all functional branches of the operator
- Example: `log10` must cover in-place/non-in-place operations; `conv_transpose2d` must cover combinations of different padding/stride/dilation parameters

**Submission Requirements:**
- A test case coverage list must be provided, clearly indicating the shape, dimensions, and parameter modes corresponding to each test case
- A comparison table of test case execution result speedups (compared with PyTorch's native implementation case by case) must be provided

---

### 3.2 Submission Specifications

- The code structure must strictly follow the FlagGems directory specifications  
  ?? https://github.com/flagos-ai/FlagGems/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md

- The code style must strictly meet the coding style specifications of the FlagGems operator library:
  - C++ follows the **Google C++ Style Guide**
  - Python follows **PEP 8**  
  ?? https://github.com/flagos-ai/FlagGems/blob/master/docs/code_contribution.md

- Code must be **original**

- Participants must commit that winning works can be incorporated into the FlagGems open-source library under the **Apache 2.0 license**, with themselves credited as core contributors

---

### 3.2.1 Submission Results

Participants must submit their source code by initiating a **Pull Request (PR)** to the **FlagGems repository** ([https://github.com/flagos-ai/FlagGems/pulls](https://github.com/flagos-ai/FlagGems/pulls)).

> **Note:** The PR title must include the prefix: `[FlagGems Operator Development Competition]`


### a. Participants with Self-Provided Computing Resources

Upon the release of the competition tasks, participants using their own hardware and development environments may begin operator development immediately.

* **Submission & Reward:** Once development is complete, participants can submit their PR.
* **Review Process:** If the submission meets the evaluation requirements, the PR will be accepted and merged.
* **Task Completion:** The specific operator task will then be closed, and the corresponding prize money will be awarded.
* **Task Updates:** The organizing committee will periodically add new operator tasks to the pool as existing ones are completed.

### b. Participants Using Provided Computing Resources

Participants who require the official unified computing environment must visit the **""FlagOS Official Website - Track 1 - Bounty Hunter""** ([https://flagos.io/RaceDetail?id=296flq8k&lang=cn](https://flagos.io/RaceDetail?id=296flq8k&lang=cn)) after **February 24, 0:00:00 (UTC+8)** to claim a task and gain access to the environment.

The duration of access to the official development environment depends on the task difficulty:

| Operator Difficulty | Access Duration (From successful claim) |
| --- | --- |
| **Simple** | 5 Days |
| **Medium** | 10 Days |
| **Difficult** | 15 Days |

---

### 3.2.2 Submission Address

Participants must submit the corresponding code to the following FlagGems operator library GitHub address and initiate a pull request. The PR title must include **[FlagGems Operator Development Competition]** to indicate that the PR is from the competition.

**Pull Request Address:** https://github.com/flagos-ai/FlagGems/pulls


----------------------
# Evaluation

This evaluation plan is formulated around the core principles of ""fairness, impartiality, and transparency"", aiming to select high-quality operator solutions that meet technical standards, adapt to the open-source ecosystem, and have practical application value, and to attract core contributors who meet the requirements of the FlagGems community. The entire evaluation process is based on the competition topic requirements and submission specifications, combined with the open-source landing value of the operators for comprehensive evaluation.

**Evaluation Principles:**
1. Technology-Oriented: Prioritize the functional correctness, performance competitiveness, and engineering landing capabilities of the operators, aligning with the actual application needs of the FlagGems open-source library;

2. Open-Source Adaptation Priority: Focus on code specifications, CI compatibility, and community usability to ensure that the solutions can be directly incorporated into the open-source library.


---

### 3.3.1 Evaluation Process and Basis

#### (1) Evaluation Process

| Evaluation Subject | Core Actions |
|--------------------|--------------|
| FlagGems Core Developers + Algorithm/Engineering Experts | 1. Formal Review: Verify the completeness of submitted code and compliance of code structure |
| | 2. Automated Verification: Execute the official FlagGems CI pipeline to verify code style, unit tests, and TT1 |
| | 3. Verify whether the operator's function, performance, and test cases meet the standards item by item |
| | 4. Determine winners and merge into the repository |

#### (2) Evaluation Basis

**Core Basis:**
- ""FlagGems Community Operator Development Competition Topic Requirements"" (including topic requirements and submission specifications)

**Reference Basis:**
- FlagGems open-source library coding standards and CI pipeline test standards
- PyTorch official operator interface definitions and performance benchmarks
- Universal standards for industrial-grade operator development (such as numerical stability, cross-hardware adaptation requirements)
- FlagGems community open-source contributor admission standards

---

### 3.3.2 Review Criteria

The total evaluation score is **100 points**. Quantitative scoring is carried out across multiple dimensions:

| Evaluation Dimension | Weight | Core Scoring Rules |
|---------------------|--------|-------------------|
| **Functional Correctness** | 30% | 1. Result Accuracy: Refer to the functional correctness description for the absolute error of floating-point operators compared with benchmarks; integer/logical operators must be completely consistent with benchmarks |
| | | 2. Boundary Use Case Handling: Cover boundary scenarios such as extreme values, zero values, NAN, and INF |
| | | 3. Exception Handling: Clear error prompts for illegal inputs |
| **Performance Competitiveness** | 20% | 1. Operators must use the benchmark framework built into the FlagGems repository for device-side performance testing |
| | | 2. The device-side speedup of the operator compared with the native implementation of PyTorch and other frameworks must be **0.9 or higher** |
| **Open-Source Adaptability** | 10% | 1. Code Style: Comply with FlagGems coding standards and pass pre-commit verification (required) |
| | | 2. CI Testing: Pass all necessary FlagGems CI pipeline tests (required) |
| | | 3. Interface Adaptation: Fully align with the interfaces of PyTorch, vllm, and Transformer Engine frameworks |
| **Cross-Platform Compatibility** | 10% | 1. Compatible with at least one type of AI hardware accelerator provided by the organizer. |
| | | 2. Must use the FlagTree compiler with version number 0.3.0rc1+ascend3.2 . |
| | | 3. Need to provide the data of hardware computing power and bandwidth utilization rate for operator implementation. |
| **Test Case Completeness** | 20% | 1. Comprehensive coverage of test case shape, dimensions, data types, and interface parameters |
| | | 2. High-quality test cases that can efficiently achieve code coverage |
| | | 3. Test case development shall refer to: https://github.com/flagos-ai/FlagGems/blob/master/tests |
| **Code Readability** | 10% | 1. Complete comments |
| | | 2. Standardized naming of variables and functions |

---

### 3.4 Winning Solutions

Centered on the core of ""hierarchical incentives and focus on open-source"", the competition sets three levels of awards (First Prize, Second Prize, Third Prize) based on operator difficulty. All winning works will be incorporated into the FlagGems open-source library, and participants will simultaneously become community contributors.

**Award Rules:**
- Each topic has **one winning quota**
- If no developer meets the winning standards for a certain topic, the corresponding award will be vacant and not filled by lower-level candidates

**Prize Structure:**

| Award Level | Corresponding Topic Difficulty | Core Benefits |
|-------------|-------------------------------|---------------|
| **First Prize** | Difficult | 1. Bonus of **3,000 RMB** |
| | | 2. Priority access to community technical resources (such as accelerator resources) |
| | | 3. Priority participation in FlagGems ecosystem roadmap planning |
| | | 4. Solution incorporated into the open-source library with author credited |
| **Second Prize** | Medium | 1. Bonus of **2,000 RMB** |
| | | 2. Priority access to community technical resources (such as accelerator resources) |
| | | 3. Solution incorporated into the open-source library with author credited |
| **Third Prize** | Easy | 1. Bonus of **1,000 RMB** |
| | | 2. Solution incorporated into the open-source library with author credited |


## Results Announcement and Notification

The organizers will promptly announce the list of winners on the FlagOS Official Website (https://flagos.io/events?lang=cn&tab=competition).

