10693,titanic-ml-introduction-seminar-2-1-xpand-olympiad,"# Description

> ####??&nbsp;  Recommended Competition
> We highly recommend [Titanic - Machine Learning from Disaster](https://kaggle.com/c/titanic/overview) to get familiar with the basics of machine learning and Kaggle competitions.

Welcome to the year 2912, where your data science skills are needed to solve a cosmic mystery. We've received a transmission from four lightyears away and things aren't looking good.

The *Spaceship Titanic* was an interstellar passenger liner launched a month ago. With almost 13,000 passengers on board, the vessel set out on its maiden voyage transporting emigrants from our solar system to three newly habitable exoplanets orbiting nearby stars.

While rounding Alpha Centauri en route to its first destination¡ªthe torrid 55 Cancri E¡ªthe unwary *Spaceship Titanic* collided with a spacetime anomaly hidden within a dust cloud. Sadly, it met a similar fate as its namesake from 1000 years before. Though the ship stayed intact, almost half of the passengers were transported to an alternate dimension!

<img title=¡±ID 52¡± src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/Spaceship%20Titanic/joel-filipe-QwoNAhbmLLo-unsplash.jpg"" style=""float:center; height:320px; width:404px"">

To help rescue crews and retrieve the lost passengers, you are challenged to predict which passengers were transported by the anomaly using records recovered from the spaceship¡¯s damaged computer system.

Help save them and change history!

> ####??&nbsp;  Getting Started Notebook
> To get started quickly, feel free to take advantage of [this starter notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/gusthema/spaceship-titanic-with-tfdf/notebook).

**If you want to talk with other users about this competition, come join our Discord! We've got channels for competitions, job postings and career discussions, resources, and socializing with your fellow data scientists. Follow the link here:** https://discord.gg/kaggle

### Acknowledgments

Photos by <a href=""https://unsplash.com/@joelfilip?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText"">Joel Filipe</a>, <a href=""https://unsplash.com/@uncle_rickie?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText"">Richard Gatley</a> and <a href=""https://unsplash.com/@actionvance?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText"">ActionVance</a> on Unsplash.
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Metric

Submissions are evaluated based on their [classification accuracy](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy), the percentage of predicted labels that are correct.

### Submission Format

The submission format for the competition is a csv file with the following format:

```
PassengerId,Transported
0013_01,False
0018_01,False
0019_01,False
0021_01,False
etc.
```
----------------------
# Frequently Asked Questions

<h3>What is a Getting Started competition?</h3>
<p>Getting Started competitions were created by Kaggle data scientists for people who have little to no machine learning background. They are a great place to begin if you are new to data science or just finished a MOOC and want to get involved in Kaggle.</p>
<p>Getting Started competitions are a non-competitive way to get familiar with Kaggle¡¯s platform, learn basic machine learning concepts, and start meeting people in the community. They have no cash prize and are on a rolling timeline.</p>
<h3>How do I create and manage a team?</h3>
<p>When you accept the competition rules, a team will be created for you. You can invite others to your team, accept a merger with another team, and update basic information like team name by going to the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/team"" target=""_blank"">Team</a> page.</p>
<p>We've heard from many Kagglers that teaming up is the best way to learn new skills AND have fun. If you don't have a teammate already, consider asking if anyone wants to team up in the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/discussion"" target=""_blank"">discussion forum</a>.</p>
<h3>What are Notebooks?</h3>
<p>Kaggle Notebooks is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Notebooks support scripts in Python and R, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. You can visit the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/notebooks"" target=""_blank"">Notebooks</a> tab to view all of the publicly shared code for the Spaceship Titanic competition. For more on how to use Notebooks to learn data science, check out our <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/learn/overview"" target=""&quot;_blank"">Courses</a>!</p>
<h3>Why did my team disappear from the leaderboard?</h3>
<p>To keep with the spirit of getting-started competitions, we have implemented a two month rolling window on submissions. Once a submission is more than two months old, it will be invalidated and no longer count towards the leaderboard.</p>
<p>If your team has no submissions in the previous two months, the team will also drop from the leaderboard. This will keep the leaderboard at a manageable size, freshen it up, and prevent newcomers from getting lost in a sea of abandoned scores.</p>
<p><i>""I worked so hard to get that score! Give it back!""</i> Read more about our decision to implement a rolling leaderboard <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/discussion/6240"" target=""_blank"">here</a>.</p>
<h3>How do I contact Support?</h3>
<p>Kaggle does not have a dedicated support team so you¡¯ll typically find that you receive a response more quickly by asking your question in the appropriate forum. (For this competition, you¡¯ll want to use the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/discussion"" target=""_blank"">Spaceship Titanic discussion forum</a>).</p>
<p>Support is only able to help with issues that are being experienced by all participants. Before contacting support, please check the discussion forum for information on your problem. If you can¡¯t find it, you can post your problem in the forum so a fellow participant or a Kaggle team member can provide help. The forums are full of useful information on the data, metric, and different approaches. We encourage you to use the forums often. If you share your knowledge, you'll find that others will share a lot in turn!</p>
<p>If your problem persists or it seems to be effective all participants then please <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/contact"" target=""_blank"">contact us</a>.</p>","# Dataset Description

Dans cette comp¨¦tition, votre tache est de pr¨¦dire si un passager a ¨¦t¨¦ transport¨¦ dans une dimension alternative lors de la collision du Spaceship Titanic avec une anomalie spatio-temporelle. Pour vous aider ¨¤ faire ces pr¨¦dictions, vous disposez d'un ensemble de dossiers personnels r¨¦cup¨¦r¨¦s dans le syst¨¨me informatique endommag¨¦ du vaisseau.

# Descriptions des fichiers et des champs de donn¨¦es #

- **train.csv** - Dossiers personnels pour environ deux tiers (~8700) des passagers, ¨¤ utiliser comme donn¨¦es d'entra?nement.
    - `PassengerId` - Un ID unique pour chaque passager. Chaque ID prend la forme gggg_pp o¨´ gggg indique le groupe avec lequel le passager voyage et pp est son num¨¦ro au sein du groupe. Les personnes d'un m¨ºme groupe sont souvent des membres d'une m¨ºme famille, mais pas toujours.
    - `HomePlanet` - La plan¨¨te de d¨¦part du passager, g¨¦n¨¦ralement sa plan¨¨te de r¨¦sidence permanente.
    - `CryoSleep` - Indique si le passager a choisi d'¨ºtre mis en animation suspendue pour la dur¨¦e du voyage. Les passagers en sommeil cryog¨¦nique sont confin¨¦s dans leurs cabines.
    - `Cabin` - Le num¨¦ro de cabine o¨´ s¨¦journe le passager. Prend la forme pont/num/c?t¨¦, o¨´ c?t¨¦ peut ¨ºtre soit P pour Babord (Port) ou S pour Tribord (Starboard).
    - `Destination` - La plan¨¨te o¨´ le passager d¨¦barquera.
    - `Age` - L'age du passager.
    - `VIP` - Si le passager a pay¨¦ pour un service VIP sp¨¦cial pendant le voyage.
    - `RoomService`, `FoodCourt`, `ShoppingMall`, `Spa`, `VRDeck` - Montant factur¨¦ par le passager pour chacune des nombreuses prestations de luxe du Spaceship Titanic.
    - `Name` - Le pr¨¦nom et le nom du passager.
    - `Transported` - Si le passager a ¨¦t¨¦ transport¨¦ dans une autre dimension. C'est la cible (target), la colonne que vous essayez de pr¨¦dire.

- **test.csv** - Dossiers personnels pour le tiers restant (~4300) des passagers. Votre tache est de pr¨¦dire la valeur de Transported pour les passagers de ce jeu de donn¨¦es. 

- **sample_submission.csv** - Un fichier de soumission au format correct.
    - `PassengerId` - ID de chaque passager du jeu de test.
    - `Transported` - La cible. Pour chaque passager, pr¨¦disez soit True (Vrai), soit False (Faux).",False,True
10694,hw-2-p-2-spring-2026-sandbox-testing,"# Overview

This is the Face Verification Kaggle TA Sandbox competition for 11-785 HW2P2, Spring 2026
----------------------
# Description

In this homework, you will work on pattern recognition problems that require position invariance. Specifically, you will work on the problem of verifying faces in images. In typical pictures of faces, the face is rarely perfectly centered. Different pictures of the same person may have the face shifted by varying amounts. The classifier must recognize the face regardless of this indeterminacy of position. This calls for position-invariant models, specifically Convolutional Neural Networks, or CNNs.

Face verification refers to the task of determining whether two face images are of the same person, without necessarily knowing who the person is. Face verification is an instance of a larger class of problems where we attempt to determine if two data instances belong to the same class without necessarily knowing (or having a model for) the class itself.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation Metric: EER (Equal Error Rate)","# Dataset Description

The verification dataset consists of images and a verification pair list (for validation and testing) in a txt file.",False,True
10695,urban-flood-modelling,"# Overview

**A new version of the dataset has been released. Please download and use the updated version.**

Urban flooding arises from complex interactions between underground drainage networks (1D) and surface overland flow (2D) upon the occurrence of rainfall forcing, making water level prediction a challenging task. This competition challenges participants to build models that accurately **predict water levels for all 1D and 2D nodes**, across multiple events and two different urban flood models.

**Please download the full dataset [HERE](https://drive.google.com/file/d/18XT8NlOfWOnR64mi6faKOR2mVzqpaLSA/view?usp=sharing)**. The dataset uploaded to Kaggle is incomplete and is intended solely for data exploration and understanding.

To get an overview of the competition, please refer to the **video recording [HERE](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1pSBLpe16EWgEupIEOmiBoSNkv8pGEdPM/view?usp=sharing)**.

The participants aim to achieve the **best overall prediction performance** across all node types, events, and urban settings, as measured by the competition evaluation metrics based on the data given. Success requires models that generalize well across both 1D drainage nodes and 2D surface nodes.

Participants are ranked based on their evaluation scores, with cash prizes awarded to the top 5 teams. In addition, top-ranked participants will be invited to collaborate with the organizers as **co-authors on the contest paper** to be submitted to a reputed journal, recognizing outstanding contributions.

----------------------
# Description

This competition aims to advance urban flood forecasting in coupled 1D¨C2D hydraulic systems, where underground drainage networks interact dynamically with surface overland flow. Participants are challenged to **predict water levels at both 1D drainage nodes and 2D surface nodes**, using rainfall and hydraulic states.

The dataset consists of two different urban landscape, terrain, and hydraulic configurations. Although the spatial layouts and hydraulic properties differ across the two models, all share the same core task which is to **autoregressively forecast water levels at all 1D and 2D nodes throughout a rainfall event**. Each model includes multiple events, allowing participants to explore both temporal and spatial generalization.

To make the competition accessible, the organizers have carefully preprocessed the input and target variables and both static and dynamic features are provided in clean CSV formats. The geospatial shapefiles are included as supplementary resources for participants who wish to visualize the urban layouts. To understand further on the data provided, please refer to the **Data** tab.

Participants are **allowed** to use external data sources, pretrained models, and any modeling techniques. All approaches are permitted as long as they are clearly cited, reproducible, and comply with Kaggle¡¯s competition rules. Participants are encouraged to be creative in designing their pipelines, which may include training separate models for different urban landscapes, sharing representations across models, or using ensemble strategies.

The final submission must include **predictions for all two urban flood models**, covering all required test events, node types, and nodes. While participants may adopt different modeling strategies for each simulation, the submission must follow the unified format specified in the submission guidelines.

Evaluation is conducted using a public and private leaderboard with the standard metric of **Standardized Root Mean Squared Error (Standardized RMSE)**. The public leaderboard provides real-time feedback during the competition using a subset of the test data. Final rankings are determined by the private leaderboard, which is evaluated on the full test set and will be released a few days after the competition ends, following manual validation of the results. For more information, please refer to the **Evaluation** section.

This competition is designed as a research-driven challenge. Top-ranked participants will be required to submit the code, models and a detailed solution write-up explaining their methodology and design choices. They will be invited to collaborate with the organizers as **co-authors on the contest paper** to be submitted to a reputed journal.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The competition uses two leaderboards for evaluation: a public and a private leaderboard. During the competition, submissions are evaluated on the public leaderboard, which is computed using 50% of the total test data and provides immediate feedback to participants. Final rankings are determined using the private leaderboard, which is evaluated on another 50% of the test set. The private leaderboard will be released a few days after the competition ends, following a manual validation process. As a result, participant rankings may change once the private leaderboard is announced.

Model performance is measured using the **Standardized Root Mean Squared Error (Standardized RMSE)**, a commonly used error-based metric for evaluating time-series predictions. RMSE directly measures the magnitude of prediction errors, where lower values indicate better performance. To ensure fair comparison across different urban models and node types with varying water-level scales, RMSE is standardized using the pre-computed standard deviation corresponding to each combination of urban model and node type. Specifically, separate standard deviation values are provided for each model and each node type. This standardization ensures that prediction errors are evaluated on a comparable scale and that 1D nodes and 2D nodes contribute equally to the final score, despite differences in node count and variance.

Furthermore, each urban model includes multiple independent test rainfall events. For each event, Standardized RMSE is first calculated at the node level, then averaged to produce a node-type-level score (1D and 2D separately). The node-type scores are then averaged to obtain an event-level score, ensuring equal contribution from each node type. Event-level scores are subsequently averaged across all test events within the same urban model to obtain a model-level score, giving equal weight to each rainfall event. Finally, the model-level scores from the two urban models are averaged to produce a single final Standardized RMSE score used for ranking. Submissions can be provided in **CSV** or **Parquet** format.

**Sample code** for computing the Standardized Root Mean Squared Error (Standardized RMSE) metric is provided at the following link: [LINK](https://www.kaggle.com/code/jiayulim/rmse-nodes-metrics).

The full evaluation process takes approximately **15 minutes** to complete.

Participants achieving top ranks on the private leaderboard are required to submit the code, models and a detailed solution write-up explaining their modeling approach, pipeline design, and key decisions. Participants who fail to submit this write-up within the specified timeframe will be disqualified, and their ranking will be forfeited. Outstanding solutions may also be invited to collaborate with the organizers as **co-authors on the contest paper** to be submitted to a reputed journal.

---

## Submission File
For each sample in the test set, participants are required to predict the water level at every relevant node. Predictions must be submitted as a single **CSV** or **Parquet** file that consolidates results from all two urban flood models and all test events.

Each row in the submission file represents the predicted water level for one node, corresponding to a specific model, event, and node type. To uniquely identify each prediction, the submission file must contain six columns: row_id, model_id, event_id, node_type, node_id, and water_level.

The model_id column specifies which urban model the prediction belongs to, with valid values of 1, or 2. The event_id column identifies the rainfall event within that model. The node_type column indicates whether the prediction is for a 1D drainage node (1) or a 2D surface node (2). The node_id column specifies the unique identifier of the node, and the water_level column contains the predicted water level value produced by the model.

The submission file must include a header row and follow the exact column order shown below:
```row_id, model_id,event_id,node_type,node_id,water_level```

An example submission is shown below:
```
row_id, model_id, event_id, node_type, node_id, water_level
0, 1, 3, 1, 50, 233.3301
1, 2, 1, 2, 90, 254.7810
2, 2, 4, 1, 100, 210.9821
3, 1, 2, 2, 88,0 200.7012
```

Participants must submit one consolidated **CSV** or **Parquet** file containing predictions for all required nodes, all events, and all two models. Although participants may train separate models or pipelines for different urban models or events, all predictions must be combined into a single file for submission.

Before evaluation, a validation script will verify that all expected model IDs, event IDs, node types, and node IDs are present and correctly formatted. Submissions that are incomplete or incorrectly structured may fail validation or be excluded from scoring.

----------------------
# Prizes

**TOTAL PRIZES AVAILABLE: 9000 SGD**

- First Prize: 3500 SGD
- Second Prize: 2500 SGD
- Third Prize: 1500 SGD
- Fourth Prize: 1000 SGD
- Fifth Prize: 500 SGD

Winners will be determined based on the highest evaluation scores achieved on the private test set. 

**Top-performing participants will be invited to collaborate as co-authors with the organizers on the contest paper to be submitted to a reputed journal.**
----------------------
# Reference

Acosta, C. M., Herath, H. M. V. V., Lim, J. Y., Saha, A., Rasnayaka, S., & Marshall, L. (2025). Physics-informed Graph Neural Networks for Operational Flood Modeling. arXiv [Cs.LG]. Retrieved from http://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23964",,False,True
