10697,i-2526-ise-defect-detection-challenge,"# Overview

Welcome to the Defect Code Detection Challenge!
----------------------
# Description

In this competition, your task is to develop a model that can accurately classify source code snippets as either secure or insecure. With the rise of software vulnerabilities like resource leaks, use-after-free vulnerabilities, and denial-of-service (DoS) attacks, identifying insecure code is crucial for maintaining robust software systems.
Participants will be provided with a dataset containing labeled code snippets. The labels indicate whether the code is secure (0) or insecure (1). Your goal is to create an effective machine learning model that can predict these labels with high accuracy.
Key Objectives:
- Analyze code snippets for potential vulnerabilities.
- Develop models to automate the classification of secure and insecure code.
- **Ensure the ROC score exceeds 0.63.**
----------------------
# Evaluation


Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the Label variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,Label
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description


The participant need to classify each code snippet into 2 class: secure (0) or insecure (1)

The submission file should include 2 column: index and label.
The full description of files and column in each files is presented below.

## Files
*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `ID` - index of example
*   `code`? - source code of example
*   `Label`  - label of example",False,True
10698,riva-cervical-cytology-challenge-track-a-isbi-final-evaluation,"# Overview

Early and accurate detection of abnormal cervical cells is essential for preventing cervical cancer in women, particularly in regions where access to expert cytotechnologists is limited. The RIVA Cervical Cytology Challenge invites the global machine learning community to advance automated analysis of Pap smear images using a large, expert-annotated dataset. On this page, you will find the description and leaderboard for Competition **Track A:Cell detection + Classification (FINAL EVALUATION)**. For more information, or to view the Track B leaderboard, please refer to the challenge website: [https://lia-ditella.github.io/rivachallenge/](https://lia-ditella.github.io/rivachallenge/)



----------------------
# Description

In Track A, participants must develop models capable of localizing and classifying cells in Pap smear images. Each detected cell should be annotated with a bounding box and assigned to one of the eight Bethesda categories (NILM, ASCUS, LSIL, HSIL, ASC-H, SCC, INFL, ENDO).

All images must be downloaded from the **Data Section**.

---

## ?? Submission Rules

Each team may submit **up to 15 prediction files per day**.

All submissions must be provided in **CSV format** and must contain **every detected cell for the entire test set**.  
Your CSV must follow the structure described below.

---

## ?? Submission File Format

Your submission must include the following columns:

- **id**: A unique identifier for each prediction.  
  This value is not matched against the ground truth; it only needs to be unique within the file.

- **image_filename**: Name of the test image to which the detection belongs.

- **x**: x-coordinate of the **center** of the bounding box.

- **y**: y-coordinate of the **center** of the bounding box.

- **width**: Width of the bounding box.

- **height**: Height of the bounding box.

- **conf**: Confidence score assigned to the detection.

- **class**: Predicted class label for the detection.

### Class Label Mapping

| Class ID | Label   |
|----------|----------|
| 0        | NILM     |
| 1        | ENDO     |
| 2        | INFL     |
| 3        | ASCUS   |
| 4        | LSIL     |
| 5        | HSIL     |
| 6        | ASCH    |
| 7        | SCC      |

> Your file may contain **any number of rows**, depending on how many detections your model outputs.

---

## ?? Example Submission

A valid submission file should look like this:

| id | image_filename | class | x     | y     | width | height | conf |
|----|----------------|-------|-------|-------|--------|--------|------|
| 0  | test_001.png   | 6     | 512.0 | 430.0 | 120.0  | 140.0  | 0.92 |
| 1  | test_001.png   | 1     | 300.0 | 200.0 | 80.0   | 90.0   | 0.78 |
| 2  | test_002.png   | 2     | 256.0 | 256.0 | 100.0  | 110.0  | 0.85 |
| 3  | test_003.png   | 5     | 600.0 | 350.0 | 150.0  | 160.0  | 0.67 |
| 4  | test_003.png   | 4     | 420.0 | 500.0 | 90.0   | 95.0   | 0.74 |
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on mean Average Precision ([mAP@0.50:0.95](https://lightning.ai/docs/torchmetrics/stable/detection/mean_average_precision.html)). Leaderboard rankings are determined solely based on this metric.

The full implementation of the evaluation metric is available in [this Github repository](https://github.com/LIA-DiTella/RIVA-CCC-ISBI2026)

To experiment with the metric on your own validation data, you can refer to the `demo.ipynb` notebook  
or review the sample evaluations provided.","# Dataset Description

Below you will find all the information you need regarding the data used in this competition.

---

## ?? Data Overview

All the data required to participate in this competition is available directly in this section.  
Participants must download the files provided here to train their models and generate predictions.

There are **two files to download**:

- `annotations.zip`
- `images.zip`

---

## ?? `annotations.zip`

This file contains **three CSV files**:

- `train.csv`
- `val.csv`
- `sample_submission.csv`

### **`train.csv` and `val.csv` ¡ª Ground Truth Annotations**

These files contain the **bounding box annotations from the official RIVA Dataset**, and should be used for **training and validation**.

Each row corresponds to a detected cell, including its image identifier and bounding box coordinates.  
Participants are free to preprocess or convert these annotations to any format required by their models.

---

### **`sample_submission.csv` ¡ª Submission Format Example**

This file provides a **minimal example** of the required submission format.

It contains a small number of **dummy bounding box predictions**, intended solely to illustrate:

- the expected column names  
- the structure of a valid submission file  

Your actual submission:

- may contain **more or fewer rows** than this example  
- but must follow **exactly the same column structure**

We strongly recommend using this file as a starting point when generating your final submission.

---

## ??? `images.zip`

This file contains **all the images** used in the competition, organized into three folders:

- `train/` ¡ª images corresponding to `train.csv`
- `val/` ¡ª images corresponding to `val.csv`
- `test/` ¡ª images **without annotations**, used for inference

Participants must run their trained models on the images in the `test/` folder and submit their predictions following the format specified in `sample_submission.csv`.",False,True
10699,riva-cervical-cytology-challenge-track-b-isbi-final-evaluation,"# Overview

Early and accurate detection of abnormal cervical cells is essential for preventing cervical cancer in women, particularly in regions where access to expert cytotechnologists is limited. The RIVA Cervical Cytology Challenge invites the global machine learning community to advance automated analysis of Pap smear images using a large, expert-annotated dataset. On this page, you will find the description and leaderboard for Competition **Track B:Cell detection (FINAL EVALUATION)**. For more information, or to view the Track A leaderboard, please refer to the challenge website: [https://lia-ditella.github.io/rivachallenge/](https://lia-ditella.github.io/rivachallenge/)
----------------------
# Description

In Track B, participants must develop models capable of localizing cells in Pap smear images using bounding boxes. Only cells belonging to the Bethesda categories (NILM, ASCUS, LSIL, HSIL, ASC-H, SCC, INFL, ENDO) will be considered, although class labels are not evaluated in this track.

All images must be downloaded from the **Data Section**.

---

## ?? Submission Rules

Each team may submit **up to 15 prediction files per day**.

All submissions must be provided in **CSV format** and must contain **every detected cell for the entire test set**.  
Your CSV must follow the structure described below.

---

## ?? Submission File Format

Your submission must include the following columns:

- **id**: A unique identifier for each prediction.  
  This value is not matched against the ground truth; it only needs to be unique within the file.

- **image_filename**: Name of the test image to which the detection belongs.

- **x**: x-coordinate of the **center** of the bounding box.

- **y**: y-coordinate of the **center** of the bounding box.

- **width**: Width of the bounding box.

- **height**: Height of the bounding box.

- **conf**: Confidence score assigned to the detection.

- **class**: This track is **single-class**.  
  The `class` column **must be included and set to 0 for *all* predictions**, even though it is not used for scoring.

> Your file may contain **any number of rows**, depending on how many detections your model outputs.

---

## ?? Example Submission

A valid submission file should look like this:

| id | image_filename | class | x     | y     | width | height | conf |
|----|----------------|-------|-------|-------|--------|--------|------|
| 0  | test_001.png   | 0     | 512.0 | 430.0 | 120.0  | 140.0  | 0.92 |
| 1  | test_001.png   | 0     | 300.0 | 200.0 | 80.0   | 90.0   | 0.78 |
| 2  | test_002.png   | 0     | 256.0 | 256.0 | 100.0  | 110.0  | 0.85 |
| 3  | test_003.png   | 0     | 600.0 | 350.0 | 150.0  | 160.0  | 0.67 |
| 4  | test_003.png   | 0     | 420.0 | 500.0 | 90.0   | 95.0   | 0.74 |
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on mean Average Precision ([mAP@0.50:0.95](https://lightning.ai/docs/torchmetrics/stable/detection/mean_average_precision.html)). Leaderboard rankings are determined solely based on this metric.

The full implementation of the evaluation metric is available in [this Github repository](https://github.com/LIA-DiTella/RIVA-CCC-ISBI2026)

To experiment with the metric on your own validation data, you can refer to the `demo.ipynb` notebook  
or review the sample evaluations provided.","# Dataset Description

