10710,echoes-of-silenced-genes,"# Overview

Modelling cell behavior in response to stimuli and perturbations has been an increasingly popular topic. The so-called *virtual cell* models promise to navigate, or even replace lab experiments and thus significantly contribute to advances in medical research. However, we are yet to see such a capable model. In fact, we are yet to see a definition of what a good model is - the evaluation of prediction quality itself is not a simple task. 

Your objective in this challenge is to predict how a human cancer cell line responds to perturbations induced by CRISPR-interference. We organize this challenge to: 

* See how capable current models of cell response are.
* Evaluate a new metric of prediction quality.
* Have fun.
----------------------
# Description

Single-cell RNA-seq has been used to catalog mRNA expression levels of almost all accessible cell types. Summarized in large atlases like [CELLxGENE](https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/) or [scBaseCount](https://github.com/ArcInstitute/arc-virtual-cell-atlas/tree/main/scBaseCount), this type of data attracts data scientists eager to mine it for biological insights. While the bulk of these datasets contains only information on steady-state cells, the advent of techniques like CROP-seq and pertrub-seq has enabled the generation of very large datasets containing transcriptomes of cells after a CRISPR perturbation. Some of the largest public CROP-seq/perturb-seq datsets include: 

[Replogle et al., 2022](https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(22)00597-9)  
[Nourreddine et al., 2024](https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.11.03.621734v2.full)  
[Huang et al., 2025](https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.06.11.659105v1.full)  
[Jiang et al., 2025](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41556-025-01622-z)  
[Nadig et al., 2025](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41588-025-02169-3)  
[Song et al., 2025](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41556-025-01626-9)  
[Zhu et al., 2026](https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2025.12.23.696273v1)  

The increasing abundance of such datasets naturally triggers ideas like:
*Having seen what the perturbation does in one cell type, can we predict what it will do in another?*  

Initial works in this area suggest it might be possible, but arguably, a model generating predictions actually useful in practice is yet to be published. 

But what makes a prediction useful in practice? The sparse nature of scRNA-seq data, together with mostly sparse effects of perturbations make model evaluation a topic on its own. Several publications, even challenges similar to this one, have introduced different metrics, putting emphasis on different features of the predictions and generating vastly different quality measures. 

In this challenge, we want to evaluate how well do current models master the *perturbation transfer* task: Based on unperturbed transcriptomes of *many* cell types (publicly available), perturbed transcriptomes of *some* cell types (publicly available), and *a few* perturbations in the target cell type (provided here), can you predict the outcomes of arbitrary gene perturbations? 

To this end, we have designed a new evaluation metric, which should capture the important information in the sparse and noisy datasets. However, since the question of the ideal metric is far from universally answered, after the end of the challenge, we will publish the best submitted predictions together with ground truth data, so that the community can evaluate the current best predictions using any metric. 

Winners will be announced on the [High-Content CRISPR Screening Conference](https://www.perturb2026.bio/) in March 2026 in Vienna, Austria.

##### About the organizer
[Myllia Biotechnology](https://myllia.com/) is a biotech company based in Vienna, Austria. Its technology platform combines CRISPR perturbation screens with single-cell RNA sequencing in primary human cells to deliver deeply physiologic, mechanistic insights. By generating large-scale, custom perturbation datasets, we enable AI-driven foundation models of virtual cells that infer causal relationships, reveal mechanisms of action, and accelerate unbiased target discovery.

##### Acknowledgements
Big thanks to members of the Virtual Cell Research Community for valuable feedback - Kendall, Philipp, Mauro and others, as well as to Giovanni for coming up with the name.
To discuss everything virtual cell, [join the community discord channel](https://discord.gg/ksptEXV2r).

----------------------
# Evaluation

The metric used in this challenge consists of 2 parts: Weighted Mean Absolute Error Ratio and Weighted Cosine Similarity. Importantly, WMAE is normalized against a baseline prediction (average of the training data). Submissions need to perform better than the baseline in terms of WMAE to get a positive score.

## Weighted Mean Absolute Error (WMAE)
Inspired by [Mejia et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22641), we define the WMAE for a single perturbation as: 

$$
\mathrm{WMAE}(true, pred)
= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i \,\left|\, true_i - pred_i \,\right|.
$$

where \\(true\\) and \\(pred\\) are the vectors of ground-truth and predicted delta expression values across \\(n\\) genes and \\(w = (w_1,\ldots,w_n) \in \mathbb{R}^n\\) is a vector of nonnegative weights with the same length as \\(true\\) and \\(pred\\), satisfying

$$
w_i \ge 0 \quad \text{for } i=1,\ldots,n,
\qquad \sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i = n.
$$

The weights vector is calculated using the moderated t-statistic from the [limma](https://www.bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/limma.html) package. 

Let \\(t = (t_1,\ldots,t_n) \in \mathbb{R}^n\\) be the vector of moderated t-statistics for a single perturbation. Define

$$
c_i = \min\bigl(|t_i|+0.1,\;10\bigr), \qquad i=1,\ldots,n.
$$

If the perturbation targets gene with index \\(g \in \{1,\ldots,n\}\\), set \\(c_g = 0\\).

Let 
$$
M = \max_{1\le j \le n} c_j
$$

We define the weight vector \\(w = (w_1,\ldots,w_n) \in \mathbb{R}^n\\) as

$$
w_i
= n \cdot
\frac{\left(\dfrac{c_i}{M}\right)^2}{\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left(\dfrac{c_k}{M}\right)^2},
\qquad i=1,\ldots,n.
$$


Finally, to aggregate over multiple perturbations, we define the WMAE ratio score \\(W\\) over \\(L\\) perturbations as

$$
W
= \sum_{l=1}^{L}
\min\left(
5,\;
\log_2 \left( \frac{WMAE^{base}_l}{WMAE^{pred}_l} \right)
\right).
$$

Here, \\(\mathrm{WMAE}^{\mathrm{base}}_l\\) is the WMAE for perturbation \\(l\\) of the baseline prediction, and \\(\mathrm{WMAE}^{\mathrm{pred}}_l\\) is the WMAE for perturbation \\(l\\) of the model prediction. The threshold of 5 serves only to safeguard against outlier cases. 

In this instance, the baseline prediction is the same for all perturbations and is defined as the arithmetic mean of the log fold-change vectors across the 80 training perturbations. Let \\(x^{(j)} \in \mathbb{R}^n\\) denote the log fold-change vector for training perturbation \\(j\\), for \\(j=1,\ldots,80\\). The baseline prediction vector is

$$
\bar{x} = \frac{1}{80}\sum_{j=1}^{80} x^{(j)}.
$$


## Weighted Cosine Similarity (Wcos)

We define the weighted cosine similarity score \\(Wcos\\) between two vectors \\(a,b \in \mathbb{R}^m\\). In our setting, \\(a\\) is the single concatenated ground-truth delta vector across all genes in all perturbations of length \\(m = L \times n \\), and \\(b\\) is the corresponding concatenated predictions vector.

Let the smoothstep function be defined as
$$
s(t) = t^2(3-2t).
$$

We fix the gating constants \\(left = 0\\) and \\(right = 0.3\\). For each coordinate in the concatenated vectors \\(i=1,\ldots,m\\), we define
$$
x_i = \max(|a_i|,\;|b_i|),
\qquad
t_i = \frac{x_i - left}{right - left} = \frac{x_i}{0.3},
\qquad
\tilde t_i = \min\bigl(1,\max(0,t_i)\bigr),
$$
and the (smooth) gate weight
$$
w_i = s(\tilde t_i).
$$

Let \\(w_i^2\\) denote the squared weights. Then the weighted cosine similarity is

$$
Wcos(a,b) = \frac{\sum\_{i=1}^{m} w\_i^{2}\, a\_i b\_i}{\left(\sum\_{i=1}^{m} w\_i^{2}\, a\_i^{2}\right)^{1/2}\left(\sum\_{i=1}^{m} w\_i^{2}\, b\_i^{2}\right)^{1/2}}.
$$

(If the denominator is zero, define \\(Wcos(a,b)=0\\).)

## The scoring metric
The final score used in this is the multiplication of the aggregated WMAE ratio scores and the weighted cosine.  

$$
W \times \max(0,Wcos)
$$

See [the implementation](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mylliabiotechnology/relative-wmae-multiplied-by-wcosine)

----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: 3500 EUR
- **1st Place**?-?2000 EUR
- **2nd Place** - 1000 EUR
- **3rd Place** - 500 EUR

Winners will be announced on the [High-Content CRISPR Screening Conference](https://www.perturb2026.bio/) in March 2026 in Vienna, Austria.
----------------------
# FAQs

## Can I use external datasets? 
Yes, without limits. See the [rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/echoes-of-silenced-genes/rules) for details.

## What about private datasets? 
Private datasets are allowed. See the [rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/echoes-of-silenced-genes/rules) for details.

## Do I need to submit/licence/publish my code/model/data ? 
No. You only grant us the permission to use the predicted values you submit. See the [rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/echoes-of-silenced-genes/rules) for details.

## Even if I'm eligible for a prize? 
Still no. Winners are only asked to submit a high-level description of the methods and data used. See the [rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/echoes-of-silenced-genes/rules) for details.

## How does the public/private leaderboard work? 
The public leaderboard is based on predictions of 60 perturbations. The private leaderboard (final evaluation) will be based on 60 different perturbations, whose gene symbols will be released one week before the end of the challenge. See the [data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/echoes-of-silenced-genes/data) section for details.

## What type of values should I submit?
Delta expression on log2 scale. See the [data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/echoes-of-silenced-genes/data) section for details.

## How are the training and ground-truth datasets normalized?
Log1p-normalization, followed by simple arithmetic mean per perturbation. See the [data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/echoes-of-silenced-genes/data) section for details.

## Will you release the ground-truth data after the end of the contest? 
Yes, we will release the test dataset ground-truth, as well as the winning predictions. 

