10735,tesla-directional-move-prediction-from-technical-indicators,"# Overview

In this competition, you¡¯ll build a multi-class classification model that predicts the next-period directional move of stock price of a US traded equity using a set of technical indicator features computed from historical price and volume data. Your goal is to learn how combinations of indicators map to short-term direction labels, and to build models that generalize well despite noisy financial signals.

This is intentionally framed as a realistic quant-style ML problem: lots of correlated indicators, non-stationarity, and limited sample size. 

**Target:**  The target column is y, a categorical label with three classes:

    down
    neutral
    up

The class distribution in this dataset is fairly balanced (good for macro metrics), but not perfectly even. 
----------------------
# Description

Train a model that predicts y from the 36 feature columns. This is a multi-class classification problem.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Use Macro F1-score as the primary leaderboard metric.

Macro F1 treats each class equally, which is important here because predicting only the majority class (if any) shouldn¡¯t win. It also reflects the practical need to perform reasonably across down, neutral, and up.


## Submission File
Participants submit a CSV with two columns:

    id: row identifier from the test set (or index)
    y: predicted label (down, neutral, up)

Example:

id, y

0, neutral

1, up

2, down",,False,True
