10740,asia-2026-longitudinal-isncsci-imputation,"# Overview

Spinal cord injury (SCI) can result in a sudden and substantial loss of function and independence. The standard for measuring injury severity is the [International Standards for Neurological Classification of Spinal Cord Injury (ISNCSCI) assessment](https://asia-spinalinjury.org/international-standards-neurological-classification-sci-isncsci-worksheet/). This is a comprehensive examination that measures strength and sensory function and different levels across the nervous system.

As an alternative to the full ISNCSCI examination, there is also an expedited version ([E-ISNCSCI](https://asia-spinalinjury.org/expedited-isncsci-exam/)). This can be performed in some circumstances to determine the neurological level of injury (NLI) and the American Spinal Injury Association (ASIA) Impairment Scale (AIS). 

The full ISNCSCI is typically performed early after an injury, with the E-ISNCSCI being more appropriate for subsequent examinations. However, it is uknown exactly how much information about assessing sensory function is lost using the E-ISNCSCI and how much can be recovered through data-driven imputation methods. 

This medical science data challenge, hosted by the [American Spinal Cord Injury Association (ASIA)](http://asia-spinalinjury.org/), aims to improve performance on this task and to invite data scientists into the field of spinal cord injury research.

**Goal:** This challenge aims to impute sensory scores from both a complete baseline ISNCSCI and subsequent E-ISNCSCI scores.

**Dual Track:** This competition includes both this track and imputating full ISNCSCI scores from E-ISNCSCI without a baseline ISNCSCI [which can be found here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/asia-2026-e-isncsci-imputation). Participants can submit to one or both.

**Participation Rules:**  Please carefully check the participation rules, which also indicate that in order to win the competition, the team must provide their code for review and execution.
----------------------
# Description

The [International Standards for Neurological Classification of SCI (ISNCSCI)](https://asia-spinalinjury.org/international-standards-neurological-classification-sci-isncsci-worksheet/) evaluates motor and sensory impairment across the body and helps localize the neurologic level of the spinal cord injury and grade the severity of that injury. However, conducting a full ISNCSCI assessment is time-consuming, leading to the recent proposal of the expedited ISNCSCI (E-ISCNSCI) assessment, focusing on key muscle function. In this challenge, participants will attempt to impute full ISNCSCI scores from E-ISNCSCI examinations by analyzing a subset of the data from the [Sygen multicenter clinical trial](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11805614/). Additional data includes demographic information, comorbidities, surgical interventions, and medical complications.

This data challenge involves two tracks addressing the imputation task from two distinct angles. [The first track](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/asia-2026-challenge-e-isncsci-imputation) investigates the inference of full ISNCSCI assessments directly from the shortened examination. However, it is likely that an earlier full ISNCSCI assessment may offer essential information for this imputation task. Thus, this track aims to provide the same imputation task, based on E-ISNCSCI scores and additional data of an earlier full ISNCSCI assessment (and the respective assessment time). **We will score performance based on a regression metric, the coefficient of determination for the imputed sensory scores**.

Why these outcomes? Essentially all functions of the body are influenced by spinal cord injury and are important to predict, including arm and hand function, bowel and bladder function, respiratory function, blood pressure control, and skin integrity. However, there are no available datasets that capture these rich aspects of function. Our goal with this challenge is to assess the possibility of E-ISNCSCI to recapitulate full assessments.

A representative from the winning team for each track will be invited to present their work at the ASIA society annual meeting, with travel support available for the representatives.

There are additional open datasets available with data for spinal cord injury. Participants are invited to also use these data if it improves their prediction performance on the Sygen challenge data but need to provide information and the respective data upon request to the organizers in order to verify the performance reported truly stems truely from a learning task - teams using datasets which are not publicly available, including private datasets from their health care system will not be eligible. Any teams with access to the complete Sygen dataset are prohibited from using this data when training their algorithms. Leading teams will be asked to submit a write-up and code to reproduce their solution.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Scoring and test dataset details**: For this track, 20% of the Sygen data will be held back as test data and used in the evaluation process. The Kaggle public leaderboard is computed on a subset of the test data for the functional prediction task, so while it will give you a sense of your relative performance, it may not exactly match the final evaluation. This prevents overfitting through submissions. Also note that the leaderboard is based on a simple root mean squared error across all entries, including assessment above the NLI. 

**Imputation performance**: For imputation of the ISNCSCI scores, for each test-set sample, the final prediction will be scored by first measuring the error between the predicted and true scores. Then, the sum of those residuals will be computed to provide a score for each individual. This will be averaged across individuals in the test data set to determine the final score. In short, we will base evaluations on a root mean squared error between true and predicted scores.

We provide a template for the submission file in the Data section.",,False,True
10741,asia-2026-e-isncsci-imputation,"# Overview

Spinal cord injury (SCI) can result in a sudden and substantial loss of function and independence. The standard for measuring injury severity is the [International Standards for Neurological Classification of Spinal Cord Injury (ISNCSCI) assessment](https://asia-spinalinjury.org/international-standards-neurological-classification-sci-isncsci-worksheet/). This is a comprehensive examination that measures strength and sensory function and different levels across the nervous system.

As an alternative to the full ISNCSCI examination, there is also an expedited version ([E-ISNCSCI](https://asia-spinalinjury.org/expedited-isncsci-exam/)). This can be performed in some circumstances to determine the neurological level of injury (NLI) and the American Spinal Injury Association (ASIA) Impairment Scale (AIS). However, it is unknown how well the full sensory scores from the ISNCSCI can be recovered from the expedited examination.

This medical science data challenge, hosted by the [American Spinal Cord Injury Association (ASIA)](http://asia-spinalinjury.org/), aims to improve performance on this task and to invite data scientists into the field of spinal cord injury research.

**Goal:** This challenge aims to impute sensory scores of the ISNCSCI from E-ISNCSCI scores.

**Dual Track:** This competition has a track to impute scores from a single snapshot (this track) and in the context of longitudinal recovery [which can be found here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/asia-2026-longitudinal-isncsci-imputation). Participants can submit to one or both.

**Participation Rules:**  Please carefully check the participation rules, which also indicate that in order to win the competition, the team must provide their code for review and execution.
----------------------
# Description

The [International Standards for Neurological Classification of SCI (ISNCSCI)](https://asia-spinalinjury.org/international-standards-neurological-classification-sci-isncsci-worksheet/) evaluates motor and sensory impairment across the body and helps localize the neurologic level of the spinal cord injury and grade the severity of that injury. However, conducting a full ISNCSCI assessment is time consuming leading to the recent proposal of the expedited ISNCSCI (E-ISCNSCI) assessment focussing on key muscle function. In this challenge, participants will attempt to impute full ISNCSCI scores from E-ISNCSCI examinations by analyzing a subset of the data from the [Sygen multicenter clinical trial](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11805614/). Additional data includes demographic information, comorbidities, surgical interventions, and medical complications.

This data challenge involves two tracks addressing the imputation task from two distinct angles. The first construct addressed here is the inference of full ISNCSCI assessments from the shortened examination. **We will score performance based on a regression metric, the coefficient of determination for the imputed sensory scores**.
It is likely, that an earlier full ISNCSCI assessment may offer essential information for this imputation task. Thus [the second track](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/asia-2026-challenge-longitudinal-isncsci-imputation) aims to provide the same imputation task, however includes data on an earlier full ISNCSCI assessment as well (and the respective assessment time).

Why these outcomes? Essentially all functions of the body are influenced by spinal cord injury and are important to predict, including arm and hand function, bowel and bladder function, respiratory function, blood pressure control, and skin integrity. However, there are no available datasets that capture these rich aspects of function. Our goal with this challenge is to assess the possibility of E-ISNCSCI to recapitulate full assessments.

A representative from the winning team for each track will be invited to present their work at the ASIA society annual meeting, with travel support available for the representatives.

There are additional open datasets available with data for spinal cord injury. Participants are invited to also use these data if it improves their prediction performance on the Sygen challenge data but need to provide information and the respective data upon request to the organizers in order to verify the performance reported stems truely from a learning task - teams using datasets which are not publicly available, including private datasets from their health care system will not be eligible. Any teams with access to the complete Sygen dataset are prohibited from using this data when training their algorithms. Leading teams will be asked to submit a write-up and code to reproduce their solution.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Scoring and test dataset details**: For this track, 20% of the Sygen data will be held back as test data and used in the evaluation process. The Kaggle public leaderboard is computed on a subset of the test data for the functional prediction task, so while it will give you a sense of your relative performance, it may not exactly match the final evaluation. This prevents overfitting through submissions. Also note that the leaderboard is based on a simple root mean squared error across all entries, including assessment above the NLI. 

**Imputation performance**: For imputation of the ISNCSCI scores, for each test-set sample, the final prediction will be scored by first measuring the error between the predicted and true scores. Then, the sum of those residuals will be computed to provide a score for each individual. This will be averaged across individuals in the test data set to determine the final score. In short, we will base evaluations on a root mean squared error between true and predicted scores.

We provide a template for the submission file in the Data section.",,False,True
