10772,vista26,"# Overview

Vista is a 7-day online computer vision hackathon conducted as part of Codefest¡¯26 by Department of Computer Science and Engineering, IIT (BHU) Varanasi.

The competition focuses on solving real-world computer vision problems using modern AI techniques. Participants will work on a single task of automating checkout process in Malls and supermarkets. We encourage participants to study the dataset well and think of developing models progressively according to the image difficulty (refer the dataset description).

A live leaderboard will track team performance throughout the event. **Also, do read the rules as it has information about the eligibility criterion for prizes as well as other contact information.**

**We wish you luck! Trust me all model designers need it??**
----------------------
# Description

CodeFest is the annual technical festival of Computer Science and Engineering department at IIT BHU. Vista has been a part of CodeFest since 2016. We aim to develop and nurture CV community and solve real world challenges. 

The challenge for this year is an automation to the long queues in supermarkets and the tedious process of scanning each item individually in self-checkouts. This challenge involves making a CV model smart enough to identify the objects placed on the table. Although missing the component of validating proper object placement and overlapping objects, the model is still practical if used at checkouts by the staff. 

We expect participants to not just develop a model with good scores but also a practical low to mid resource solution for enterprises. The final judgements will be made based on the practicality of the solution and not only the evaluation parameter.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using an automated scoring system based on the number of objects correctly identified from the image. In case the count of objects generated by the model differs from truth, that entry will be scored 0. In case of detection with correct number of total objects, score for that row is calculated as:

$$
\text{Row Score} = \frac{\text{Number of correctly identified objects}}{\text{Total number of objects}}
$$
The total score of the participant will be the average row score.
A public leaderboard will display scores computed on the test data during the competition.
Final rankings will be determined using a private leaderboard, evaluated on a hidden validation data.

Only the top-scoring submissions, as defined by the platform settings, will be considered for the final evaluation.

In case of a tie on the final leaderboard, the team with the earlier valid submission will be ranked higher.
----------------------
# Submission format

## Submission Format

Participants must submit predictions as a **CSV file** containing model outputs for all images in the validation dataset.

---

### File Format

The submission file must be a **CSV** with the following structure:

```text
image_id,categories
```

* **`image_id`**: Unique identifier of the image (integer).
* **`categories`**: A JSON-formatted string representing the list of predicted category IDs for that image.

---

### Example Submission

```csv
image_id,categories
1,""[12,13,16]""
2,""[2,4,10,11]""
3,""[5]""
```

---

## Sorting Requirements (Strict)

Submissions **must satisfy all of the following sorting constraints**:

1. **Category IDs must be sorted in ascending order within each row**.

   * Incorrect: `[4, 2, 10]` ?
   * Correct: `[2, 4, 10]` ?

2. **Rows must be sorted by `image_id` in ascending numerical order**.

Failure to follow the required sorting order may result in **incorrect evaluation or submission rejection**.

---


### Notes on Submission & Evaluation Behavior

* The submission file **must contain exactly the same set of `image_id`s** as the solution file.
  Kaggle aligns rows using the `image_id` **before** calling the metric.

* If an `image_id` is **missing** from the submission or the solution, the evaluation **will fail** rather than assigning a score of `0`. Same thing happens in case of duplicate `image_id`

---

### Important for Participants

Participants must ensure that:

* Every `image_id` in the validation set appears **exactly once** in the submission.
* Category lists are well-formed and semantically correct.
* No assumptions are made about Kaggle automatically filling, ignoring, or forgiving missing or malformed entries.


---

## Leaderboard

* The **Public Leaderboard** reflects scores computed on the public portion of the validation data during the competition.
* Final rankings will be determined using a **Private Leaderboard**, evaluated on a hidden validation set.
* In case of a tie on the final leaderboard, the team with the **earlier valid submission** will be ranked higher.



## Important Notes

* Each image must contain **exactly the predicted number of objects**. If the number of predicted objects does not match the ground truth count, the score for that image will be **zero**.
* Missing image IDs in the submission will be treated as **zero score** for that image.
* Extra image IDs (not part of the validation set) will be **ignored**.
* The submission file must be a **valid JSON file**.","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition comprises images captured in **retail checkout¨Clike environments**, designed to closely simulate real-world computer vision challenges encountered in **automated product recognition systems**.

---

### Training Data

The training set consists of images containing **a single retail product**, captured from multiple viewpoints and orientations. These images are collected under controlled conditions to represent the canonical appearance of each product.

The figure below illustrates the training data collection process:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F15846380%2F58467d81b206680357be8dc071710e33%2FTraining_process.png?generation=1769396584658768&alt=media)

---

### Test Data

The test set contains images with **one or more retail products** drawn from the training set, placed under a variety of challenging conditions, including:

* Background clutter
* Partial occlusion
* Viewpoint variations
* Lighting changes

Based on the **number of objects present** and **packing density**, each test image is categorized into one of three difficulty levels:

* **Easy**
* **Medium**
* **Hard**

We have provided 2 empty backgrounds for your reference in `background` folder.

---

### Annotations

Both training and test images are annotated to support multiple computer vision tasks such as:

* Image classification
* Object detection
* Image segmentation

Annotations are provided in **JSON format**. Train and test instance Json file includes:

* An `images` section describing image metadata (e.g., image dimensions, file name, difficulty level)
* An `annotations` section specifying object-level details such as bounding boxes and corresponding `category_id`

The `category_id` values can be mapped to real-world product names using the provided `category.json` file.

---

### Task Objective

The objective of the competition is to **accurately identify all objects present in an image** and **classify them into their respective product categories**.

---

### Evaluation Protocol

The dataset is intentionally designed to promote the development of **robust and generalizable models**, rather than solutions optimized for idealized conditions.

* **Training and test datasets** are provided with annotations.
* The **validation dataset** is **unlabeled** and will be used for evaluation through the competition¡¯s automated scoring system.

---

### Support

For updates, clarifications, or questions regarding the dataset or competition, please join the official Discord server:

[https://discord.gg/4j9duQ94](https://discord.gg/4j9duQ94)",False,True
