10774,binary-battle-ml-bank-customer-churn-challenge,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2026 Kaggle Playground**  We plan to continue in the spirit of providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month. 

**Your Goal:** For this Competition, your task is to predict whether a customer continues with their account or closes it (e.g., churns). Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the probability for the target variable `Exited`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Exited
    165034,0.9
    165035,0.1
    165036,0.5
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - January 27, 2026
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  January 31, 2027

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Bank Customer Churn Prediction](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shubhammeshram579/bank-customer-churn-prediction) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `Exited` is the binary target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of `Exited`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
10775,radar-ioai-2025,"# Overview

# BASELINE - https://www.kaggle.com/code/hnbk54/radar-baseline
# OFFICIAL SOLUTION - https://www.kaggle.com/code/hnbk54/radar-official-solution

Radar is a key sensing technology widely used in wireless communication and perception systems such as **autonomous driving**, **robotics**, and **indoor monitoring**.

A radar system emits electromagnetic signals and receives reflections from objects in the environment. By processing these reflections, the radar can estimate:

- **Range** (distance)
- **Azimuth** (horizontal angle)
- **Elevation** (vertical angle)
- **Velocity** (relative motion)

Real-world radar data is noisy and contains reflections from irrelevant objects (e.g. walls, furniture).  
The goal of this competition is to apply **AI-based semantic segmentation** to radar heatmaps in order to **detect and classify objects**, with a special focus on **human detection**.

----------------------
# Description

## Dataset

### Radar Parameters

Radar measurements are represented using:

- **Range** ¨C distance from the radar
- **Azimuth** ¨C horizontal angle (?90¡ã to +90¡ã)
- **Elevation** ¨C vertical angle (?90¡ã to +90¡ã)
- **Velocity** ¨C radial velocity (remapped to ?90¡ã to +90¡ã for visualization)

<img src=""./figs/Radar Fig 1.png"" width=""300"">

---

### Radar Heatmaps

Each radar sample consists of multiple **heatmaps** encoding received signal strength:

- **Static heatmaps** emphasize stationary objects
- **Dynamic heatmaps** emphasize moving objects

Objects appear as high-intensity regions, while empty areas mostly contain noise.

---

### Data Format

Each sample is stored as a `.mat.pt` file with shape:

\[
7 \times 50 \times 181
\]

- **7** ¨C number of maps  
- **50** ¨C range bins  
- **181** ¨C angle / velocity bins (?90¡ã to +90¡ã)  
- All values are normalized to **[0, 1]**

#### Heatmap Channels

| Index | Description |
|------:|------------|
| 0 | Static range¨Cazimuth |
| 1 | Dynamic range¨Cazimuth |
| 2 | Static range¨Celevation |
| 3 | Dynamic range¨Celevation |
| 4 | Static range¨Cvelocity |
| 5 | Dynamic range¨Cvelocity |
| 6 | Semantic label map (range¨Cazimuth) |

---

### Semantic Labels

| Label | Meaning |
|------:|--------|
| -1 | Background |
| 0 | Suitcase |
| 1 | Chair |
| 2 | Human |
| 3 | Wall |

<img src=""./figs/Radar Fig 2.png"" width=""675"">
----------------------
# Metric

### CSV Format

| filename | pixel_0 | pixel_1 | ... | pixel_9049 |
|---------:|--------:|--------:|-----|-----------:|
| 1.mat.pt | -1 | -1 | ... | -1 |
| ... | ... | ... | ... | ... |

- Pixels correspond to flattened **50 ¡Á 181** output
- Values must be integers in **[-1, 3]**

---

## Evaluation

Pixel-wise accuracy with higher weight for object pixels.

### Scoring Rules

- Correct background pixel (?1): **1 point**
- Correct object pixel (0¨C3): **50 points**

Final score is normalized to **[0, 1]**.

---

### Scoring Formula

\[
Score = \frac{|C_{0,correct}| \times 1 + |C_{1,correct}| \times 50}
{|C_0| \times 1 + |C_1| \times 50}
\]

Where:

- \(C_0\): background pixels  
- \(C_1\): non-background pixels  

---

### Example

Ground Truth:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
-1 & -1 & -1 \\
1 & 2 & 3 \\
-1 & -1 & -1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Prediction:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
-1 & 1 & -1 \\
-1 & 2 & -1 \\
-1 & 3 & -1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Score:

\[
\frac{4 \times 1 + 1 \times 50}{6 \times 1 + 3 \times 50} = 0.346
\]

---",,False,True
10776,the-decision-tree-dilemma-tune-or-clean,"# Overview

### Welcome to the Challenge!

In this competition, your goal is to predict a binary target using a decision tree (or other models). However, this is not just a standard ""fit and predict"" problem. The dataset contains specific hurdles that mimic real-world messiness.

### The Story You will likely experience three distinct phases of performance:

The Baseline Trap: If you simply drop missing rows and run a default Decision Tree, your model will overfit the noise. Expect low accuracy.

The Tuning Jump: If you tune your hyperparameters (max_depth, min_samples_leaf), your model will generalize better. Accuracy will improve, but it hits a ceiling.

The Preprocessing Peak: To truly win, you must address the data quality itself. Handling missing values (Imputation) and outliers is the key to unlocking the highest score.

### The Goal Can you identify which features are corrupted? Can you tune your tree to ignore the noise? The leaderboard awaits!","# Dataset Description

### Dataset Description The dataset is synthetic but challenging. It consists of 45 numerical features and a binary target.

train.csv: The training set, containing features and the target.

test.csv: The test set. You must predict the target for these rows.

sample_submission.csv: A sample submission file in the correct format.

### Column Descriptions

id: Unique identifier for each row.

feature_00 - feature_44: Numerical input features.

Hint: Some of these columns contain missing values. How you handle them (dropping vs. imputing) will drastically change your training set size.

Hint: One feature contains extreme outliers that may skew distance-based metrics or scalers.

target: The binary classification target (0 or 1).",False,True
