| Column        | Type   | Description                                       | Example   |
|---------------|--------|---------------------------------------------------|-----------|
| date          | date   | Transaction date                                  | 2019-11-28 |
| store_id      | int    | Foreign key ¡ú stores.store_id                     | 1         |
| category_id   | int    | Foreign key ¡ú categories.category_id              | 3         |
| sales_qty     | int    | Quantity of items sold                            | 450       |
| sales_revenue | float  | Total revenue for that store ¡Á category ¡Á date    | 3725.50   |


### Embedded Seasonal/Holiday Signals
- **Weekly seasonality**: weekends ~15¨C20% higher  
- **Annual cycle**: sinusoidal (summer peak, winter holiday peak)  
- **Holiday uplift (not pre-labeled):**
  - Easter (Bakery, Beverages)
  - July 4 (Meat, Beverages)
  - Thanksgiving (Meat, Bakery)
  - Christmas/New Year (Bakery, Beverages)
- **Trend**: ~2% growth per year  
- **Weather effect**: simplified (hot-day BBQ/beverages, cold-day soups)",False,True
10782,urban-elements-re-id-challenge-2026,"# Overview

The previous edition of the challenge included a dataset with objects such as trash bins, containers, and pedestrian crossings, consisting of around 5,000 images and 397 different identities. This new edition includes the traffic sign object, with around 10,000 images and 1,000 identities along with a baseline based on Part-aware Transformer [1]. ([Click here to visit the official Challenge website](http://www-vpu.eps.uam.es/UrbanReIDChallenge2026/))


![](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/129270/images/header)
----------------------
# Description

# Motivation

Building on the foundations of our previous work, the 2026 edition of the Urban Elements ReID Challenge 2026 addresses the growing need for intelligent, automated infrastructure management. While traditional Re-Identification has focused on humans and vehicles, our mission is to decode the complex visual identity of the objects that actually define our cities.

This year, we are introducing a significant layer of difficulty: Traffic Sign Re-Identification. Unlike larger urban assets, traffic signs demand a much more granular analysis, challenging algorithms to distinguish between objects that are designed to look identical.

# Urban elements database

The proposed database is a continuation of the work done for the UrbAM-ReID dataset [2] and [URVAM-ReID2025] (https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/urban-reid-challenge/data) by our colleagues at VPULab. The previous edition of the challenge included a dataset with objects such as trash bins, containers, and pedestrian crossings, consisting of around 5,000 images and 397 different identities. This new edition includes the traffic sign object, with around 10,000 images and 1,000 identities. To ensure that this database is as realistic as possible and provides sufficient variability for this type of task, the videos were recorded over a period of four months. In total, the database comprises 14,947 images from 1,453 different identities. Among these, 11,175 images representing 1,088 identities are utilized for the training set, while 3,772 images from 365 distinct identities are reserved for evaluation (2,844 images and 363 identities for the 'gallery' split, and 928 images of 215 identities for the 'query' split). Further details about the database, its distribution, and capture can be found in ""[Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/urban-elements-re-id-challenge-2026/data)"" .

# Baseline code

As baseline  Part-aware Transformer [[1]](https://github.com/liyuke65535/Part-Aware-Transformer) has been used for the development of new algorithms for urban elements ReID. It has been decided to employ this algorithm due to its great generalization capacity compared to other state-of-the-art algorithms.
The code and instructions for setting up the system is publicly available in GitHub [[click here to access](https://github.com/vpulab/Urban-Elements-ReID---baseline/tree/main)].
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Task
The task for the evaluation is to match, for each query, the top-100 most similar images of the test set.

## Evaluation metric
For the competition, the **mean Average Precision** (mAP) for the top-100 predictions for each query  has been established as the evaluation metric for the generated track file. However, we highly encourage the use of **Cumulative Match Characteristic** for the first K predictions (CMC-K) for the development of proposed algorithms.

## Submission File
The submission format must be a .csv file consisting of two columns. The first column, *'imageName'*, serves as the identifier for the query images in descending numerical order. The second column,*'corresponding Indexes'*, should contain, for each row, the top-100 identifiers of the elements in the test set (test_labels.csv) sorted in descending order (with the most confident assignments first). An example submission can be found in ['Data'](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/urban-elements-re-id-challenge-2026/data).
----------------------
# Timeline

11/02/2026 - Launch of the competition

13/05/2026- Final submission deadline

13-17/09/2026 - Leaderboard Announcement

Submissions made after the final deadline will not be considered in determining the winning team.
----------------------
# Doubts and discussion

If you have any questions, suggestions, or need clarification to participate in the competition, please use the [Discussion](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/urban-elements-re-id-challenge-2026/discussion) section, and we will try to respond as quickly as possible.
----------------------
# License

Urban Elements ReID dataset is licensed under CC BY-ND 4.0. This enables reusers to copy and distribute the material in any medium or format in unadapted form only, and only so long as attribution is given to the creator. Reference this work as shown in section 'Cite this proyect'. This license allows for commercial use. CC BY-ND includes the following elements:
BY: credit must be given to the creator
ND: no derivates or adaptions of the work are permited.
----------------------
# Cite this proyect

If this work has been useful for your research, you are welcome to cite it as:

Javier Gal¨¢n, ¨¢lvaro Garc¨ªa-Mart¨ªn . Kaggle. Urban Elements ReID Competition. VPULab, Universidad Aut¨®noma de Madrid (2024). URL: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/urban-reid-challenge/overview
----------------------
# Registration to ICIP 2026 Challenge

Each team is required to register their participation in kaggle also via EMAIL to urban.reid.challenge@uam.es. The email should contain:

? Challenge name and team short-name Participation as the SUBJECT LINE.

? Team name in Kaggle and team member names and affiliation in the BODY OF THE EMAIL.

? Contact number and email address in the BODY OF THE EMAIL.
----------------------
# Acknowledgments

This work has been partially supported by the Regional Government of Madrid of Spain under project IDEAL-CV-CM (TEC 2024/COM-322)
----------------------
# Related References

[1]. Ni, Hao & Li, Yuke & Shen, Heng & Song, Jingkuan. (2023). Part-Aware Transformer for Generalizable Person Re-identification. 

[2]. Paula Moral, Alvaro Garc¨ªa-Mart¨ªn, Jose M. Mart¨ªnez. (2024). Long-term geo-positioned re-identification dataset of urban elements. IEEE International Conference on Image Processing (ICIP), pp. 124-130, doi: 10.1109/ICIP51287.2024.10647759.

[3]. Javier Gal¨¢n, Miguel Gonz¨¢lez, Paula Moral, ¨¢lvaro Garc¨ªa-Mart¨ªn, Jos¨¦ M. Mart¨ªnez. (2025). Transforming urban waste collection inventory: AI-Based container classification and Re-Identification. Waste Management, Volume 199, pp. 25-35, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.wasman.2025.02.051.
----------------------
# PAPER SUBMISSION 

The two-page extended abstract format for the challenge, including all relevant information and submission instructions, can be found at the following link:

*To be announced*","# Dataset Description

For this competition, the traffic signal category has been added to the previous categories of crosswalks, trash bins, and containers. These images were extracted from four different videos capturing the same route in two directions through an unspecified city. To ensure a truly representative long-term reidentification database, the videos were recorded over a period of four months. Each recording has been randomly divided into a training set (~80%) and an evaluation set (~20%). Due to the possibility of multiple identities appearing within the same bounding box due to occlusion, a filtering process based on GPS coordinates has been applied to ensure that there are no shared identities between the training and evaluation sets. This edition, the classes for each category are provided.

## Train set
As the training set, the 'train' subsets from the three recordings of the trajectory that share the same direction (cameras c001, c002, and c003) have been used. These images are available in the 'train_images' folder, and their corresponding labels can be found in the file 'train_labels.csv'.

## Evaluation set
For the evaluation, two sets have been created following the methods established in the literature. The 'queries' set consists of images from the test subset of the video with the reverse trajectory (camera c004), while the 'test' (also know as 'gallery') set is formed by the test subsets extracted from the three videos that share the trajectory.

## Dataset distribution

|  **Full dataset**<br>Containers + Rubbish Bins + Crosswalks+Traffic Signs</div> |   |   |
|:---------------------------------------------:|---|---|
| **Subset** | **# images** | **# identities** |
| Train  | 11175| 1088|
| Test:Query  | 928| 215|
| Test:Gallery   | 2844| 363|
| **Overall** | **14947** | **1453** |
## Labels format

*   `cameraID` - The identifier for the recording for each image. Cameras 1, 2, and 3 (c001, c002, and c003) correspond to the recordings that share the same direction in the trajectory and they are used for train split. Camera 4 (c004) is the identifier for the recording in the reverse direction and it is exclusively employed during the evaluation.
*   `imageName`?- Name of the image within the corresponding folder.
*   `objectID` - Available only for the training set. Indicates the identifier of the object.

# Submission format
The submissions must be sent in CSV format and have one row per image in the query set, a header row, and two columns with the following information:

* `imageName` - Name of the image of the query set.
* `Correponding Index` - Top-100 indexes from the 'image_test' folder corresponding to each query. (Separated by a single space).

# Files
* **image_train** - folder containing the images for training.
*   **train.csv** - csv file with the training set labels.
*   **train_classes.csv** -csv file with the training set labels, including the class information.
* **image_query** - folder containing the query images.
*  **query.csv** - csv file with the query set names.
*  **query_classes.csv** - csv file with the query set names, including the class information.
* **image_test** - folder containing the test iamges.
*   **test.csv** - csv file with que test set names.
*   **test_classes.csv** - csv file with que test set names, including the class information.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format.

# External data
As external data, only the use of the UrbAM-ReID dataset [1] is allowed to train models participating in the challenge.
To facilitate the use of external data, we provide a ZIP file [click here](http://www-vpu.eps.uam.es/challenges/UrbanReIDChallenge2026/files/UAM_Unified.zip) that unifies the three object categories into a single split, organized in the same structure as the official challenge dataset.

# References
[1]. Paula Moral, Alvaro Garc¨ªa-Mart¨ªn, Jose M. Mart¨ªnez. (2024). Long-term geo-positioned re-identification dataset of urban elements. IEEE International Conference on Image Processing (ICIP), pp. 124-130, doi: 10.1109/ICIP51287.2024.10647759.",False,True
10783,dl-sprint-4-0-bengali-speaker-diarization-challenge,"# Overview

**Speaker diarization** is the task of determining ""**Who spoke when?**"" in an audio recording. Unlike speech recognition (which transcribes *what* is said), diarization identifies *when* each speaker talks and groups segments belonging to the same person.

In this competition, you will build a model that performs **Bengali (Bangla) speaker diarization** on long-form audio ¡ª lectures, interviews, conversations, and more. Given audio recordings with multiple speakers, predict time-stamped segments identifying when each speaker talks.

**Goal**: Produce speaker-wise time segments with `start_time`, `end_time`, and `speaker_id` for each test audio file.

This competition is  part of **BUET CSE Fest 2026**.

Detailed Rulebook: https://tinyurl.com/DLSprint4Rulebook
----------------------
# Description


### What is Speaker Diarization?

Speaker diarization segments audio by speaker identity ¡ª determining *when* each person speaks, not *what* they say.

| Task | Output |
|------|--------|
| Speech Recognition (ASR) | Transcript of words |
| Speaker Identification | Identity of known speakers |
| **Speaker Diarization** | Time segments per speaker |

### The Challenge

Given **long-form Bangla audio** (lectures, interviews, conversations), output speaker-wise segments:

```
Audio Input: conversation.wav
     ¡ý
Model Output:
  [0.0 - 5.2]   ¡ú SPEAKER_1
  [5.3 - 12.8]  ¡ú SPEAKER_2  
  [13.0 - 18.5] ¡ú SPEAKER_1
  [18.6 - 25.0] ¡ú SPEAKER_3
```



### Competition Phases

| Phase | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| **Phase I (Online)** | Submit on Kaggle, evaluated on Public/Private test sets |
| **Phase II (Final)** | Hidden test set + On-site presentation for top teams |

### Scoring Breakdown

```
Total Score = 0.70 ¡Á Online Score + 0.30 ¡Á Offline Score

Online Score  = 0.30 ¡Á Inference Time Score + 0.70 ¡Á Model Performance
Offline Score = 0.20 ¡Á Presentation + 0.20 ¡Á Paper + 0.60 ¡Á Novelty
```

### Key Rules

| Allowed  | Prohibited  |
|-----------|--------------|
| Open-source pretrained models | External APIs (OpenAI, Claude, etc.) |
| Fine-tuning on competition data | Cloud-based inference services |
| External public datasets (cited) | Manual test set annotation |
| External compute for training | Private code sharing outside team |

**Requirements**: Inference notebook (Kaggle-runnable), IEEE research paper, max 4 members/team, 5 submissions/day.

----------------------
# Evaluation


### Diarization Error Rate (DER)

Submissions are evaluated using **Diarization Error Rate (DER)**:

$$\text{DER} = \frac{\text{False Alarm} + \text{Missed Speech} + \text{Speaker Confusion}}{\text{Total Reference Speech}}$$

| Error Type | What It Means |
|------------|---------------|
| **False Alarm** | Predicted speech where there was silence |
| **Missed Speech** | Missed actual speech (predicted silence) |
| **Speaker Confusion** | Correct speech detection, wrong speaker assigned |

**Score** = 100 ¡Á (1 ? DER), clipped to 0¨C100. Lower DER ¡ú Higher score.
### DER Worked Example

**Reference**  
- 0¨C4 s ¡ú Speaker A  
- 4¨C7 s ¡ú Speaker B  
- 7¨C10 s ¡ú Silence  

**TOTAL = 7 s**
---



**Hypothesis**
- 0¨C5 s ¡ú Speaker A  
- 5¨C6 s ¡ú Silence  
- 6¨C10 s ¡ú Speaker B  


| Span (s) | Ref | Hyp | Counts |
|---------|-----|-----|--------|
| 0¨C4 | A | A | Correct (4 s) |
| 4¨C5 | B | A | CONF = 1 s |
| 5¨C6 | B | Silence | MISS = 1 s |
| 6¨C7 | B | B | Correct (1 s) |
| 7¨C10 | Silence | B | FA = 3 s |


****

Totals:
---
- FA = 3  
- MISS = 1  
- CONF = 1  
- TOTAL = 7  
- TOTAL = 7  

$$
\text{DER} = \frac{3 + 1 + 1}{7} = \frac{5}{7} \approx 0.714 \; (71.4\%)
$$

### Important clarifications (read this!)
- **Speaker IDs do NOT have to match the ground-truth IDs.**  
  Your `speaker_id` values are treated as **labels** (strings). The evaluator automatically finds the **best mapping** between your predicted speakers and the reference speakers to minimize errors.  
  **Example:** if the ground truth uses `""1""` and `""2""`, your system can output `""11""` and `""A""` ¡ª that¡¯s fine.

- **Consistency matters.**  
  Even though IDs don¡¯t need to match, you should keep the **same label for the same person across time**. 


### Inference Time Score

Inference efficiency is evaluated using **Real-Time Factor (RTF)**. Faster models receive significantly higher scores.

**RTF Calculation**:
- RTF is computed for every test case per participant
- Final RTF = average RTF across all test cases

**Scoring**:
- Participants are ranked by average RTF (lower = better)
- Percentile ranks map directly to scores:
  - 1st percentile ¡ú Score 100
  - 2nd percentile ¡ú Score 99
  - Higher percentiles (slower) ¡ú proportionally lower scores

This percentile-based scoring rewards meaningful improvements in inference speed.",,False,True
10784,dl-sprint-4-0-bengali-long-form-speech-recognition,"# Overview

Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) is the task of converting spoken audio into written text. ASR focuses on ¡°what was said¡± by producing the correct transcript. 

In this competition, you will build a model that performs Bangla long-form ASR on real long-form audio¡ªlectures, interviews, conversations, and more. Given Bangla audio recordings, predict the corresponding Bangla text transcript. 

Goal: Produce an accurate Bangla transcript for each test audio file. 

Note: Participants do not need to handle punctuation explicitly. 

This competition is part of BUET CSE Fest 2026 and is hosted on Kaggle. 

Detailed Rulebook: https://tinyurl.com/DLSprint4Rulebook
----------------------
# Description

## What is Bangla Long-Form ASR?

Bangla Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) is the task of converting spoken Bangla audio into Bangla text ¡ª determining what was said in an audio recording.

## The Challenge

Given long-form Bangla audio (lectures, interviews, conversations, etc.), generate the Bangla transcript for each test audio file.

## Example

**Audio Input:** `test_001.wav`

¡ý

**Model Output (Transcript):**
```
?? ???? ????? ???? ?????????????? ????? ?????? ??? ...
```

## Note

You do not need to handle punctuation explicitly.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Evaluation Metric**

Submissions are evaluated using the **mean Word Error Rate (WER)**, computed as follows:

1. The WER is calculated for each instance in the test set.
2. Each WER is weighted by the number of words in the corresponding sentence.
3. The weighted mean WER is reported as the final score.

**Unicode Normalization Notice**

To ensure evaluation consistency, all text comparisons are performed on **Unicode NFC-normalized strings**.

Visually identical Bengali text may have multiple valid Unicode representations (composite vs decomposed forms). Without normalization, this can introduce artificial mismatches and unfair scoring penalties.

Participants are therefore strongly encouraged to submit predictions using **NFC-normalized CSV files**.",,False,True
