10788,gdgoc-telkom-university-2026-mask-classification,"# Overview

Dataset **Face-Mask-Detection** dibuat sebagai respons terhadap pandemi COVID-19, di mana penggunaan masker menjadi salah satu langkah penting dalam menjaga kesehatan publik. Dataset ini ditujukan untuk mendukung pengembangan sistem klasifikasi citra otomatis yang dapat mendeteksi apakah seseorang menggunakan masker atau tidak.

Permasalahan pada dataset ini adalah **klasifikasi biner**, dengan dua kelas utama: with_mask dan without_mask. Dataset ini sangat cocok digunakan untuk eksperimen **Convolutional Neural Network** (CNN) serta transfer learning, karena setiap citra direpresentasikan sebagai tensor 3 dimensi (tinggi ¡Á lebar ¡Á channel) yang dapat diproses langsung oleh model deep learning.

Dataset ini dapat dimanfaatkan untuk membangun sistem pemantauan kepatuhan penggunaan masker secara real-time, seperti pada lingkungan rumah sakit, bandara, institusi pendidikan, dan transportasi publik.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric ¡ª F1-Score (Macro)

Pada kompetisi ini, performa model dievaluasi menggunakan **F1-Score Macro**. Metrik ini menghitung F1-Score **untuk setiap kelas secara terpisah**, kemudian mengambil **rata-rata aritmatika** dari seluruh kelas. Dengan pendekatan ini, setiap kelas memiliki **bobot yang sama**, terlepas dari jumlah sampelnya.

Pendekatan **macro** sangat sesuai untuk tugas klasifikasi citra karena mendorong model untuk memiliki performa yang seimbang pada seluruh kelas, tidak hanya fokus pada kelas yang dominan.

### Definisi Metrik

Untuk setiap kelas \( c \):

$$
Precision_c = \frac{TP_c}{TP_c + FP_c}
$$

$$
Recall_c = \frac{TP_c}{TP_c + FN_c}
$$

$$
F1_c = 2 \times \frac{Precision_c \times Recall_c}{Precision_c + Recall_c}
$$

F1-Score Macro didefinisikan sebagai:

$$
F1_{macro} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{c=1}^{N} F1_c
$$

dengan:
- \( N \) : jumlah kelas  
- \( TP_c, FP_c, FN_c \) : nilai confusion matrix untuk kelas \( c \)

Nilai **F1-Score Macro** berada pada rentang **0 hingga 1**, di mana nilai yang lebih tinggi menunjukkan performa model yang lebih baik dan seimbang pada seluruh kelas.

Implementasi Kode
```python
from sklearn.metrics import f1_score

# y_true: label ground truth
# y_pred: hasil prediksi (0 atau 1)
f1 = f1_score(y_true, y_pred, average=""macro"")
print(""F1-Score:"", f1)

```

---

## Submission Format

Peserta diwajibkan mengirimkan file submission dalam format **CSV** dengan dua kolom, yaitu `filename` dan `label`.

### Deskripsi Kolom
- **filename**: nama file citra uji (`.jpg`) yang terdapat pada direktori `Dataset/test/`
- **label**: hasil prediksi model untuk setiap citra  
  - `0` ¡ú without_mask  
  - `1` ¡ú with_mask  

### Contoh File Submission
```csv
filename,label
test_1.jpg,0
test_2.jpg,0
test_3.jpg,0
test_4.jpg,0
test_5.jpg,0
test_6.jpg,0
test_7.jpg,0
test_8.jpg,0
```","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Description

Dataset **Face-Mask-Detection** merupakan dataset citra untuk tugas **klasifikasi biner** yang bertujuan mengidentifikasi apakah seseorang menggunakan masker wajah atau tidak. Dataset ini dikembangkan untuk mendukung penelitian dan eksperimen di bidang **computer vision**, khususnya klasifikasi citra berbasis **deep learning**.

Dataset terdiri dari citra wajah dalam format `.jpg` yang terbagi ke dalam dua kelas utama, yaitu `with_mask` dan `without_mask`. Setiap citra direpresentasikan sebagai **tensor 3 dimensi (H ¡Á W ¡Á C)** sehingga dapat langsung digunakan sebagai input pada model **Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)** maupun pendekatan **transfer learning**.

### Distribusi Data
- **with_mask**: 4776 citra  
- **without_mask**: 4852 citra  

Distribusi kelas relatif seimbang, sehingga dataset ini cocok untuk eksperimen klasifikasi tanpa penanganan *class imbalance* yang kompleks.

### Struktur Dataset
- `Dataset/train/`
  - `with_mask/` : citra wajah dengan masker  
  - `without_mask/` : citra wajah tanpa masker  
- `Dataset/test/`
  - Berisi citra uji tanpa label  
  - 371 citra evaluasi",False,True
10789,thrombus_chalenge_2_1,"# Overview


# Introduction
Venous thromboembolism conditions, including deep vein thrombosis (DVT), are the third most common cause of vascular mortality worldwide after heart attack and stroke. Prompt diagnoses of DVT is essential to decrease the risk of fatal complications. Machine learning (ML) models have emerged as a valuable tool for assisting prompt diagnoses. These models, by performing pixel-wise segmentation in ultrasound videos, assess vein compressibility, an indicator of DVT or no-DVT.
Training such models, requires an enormous amount of effort for creating the ground truth datasets. Thereby, this **Data Challenge** aims to foster development of AI models for DVT detection using ultrasound videos without the need for exhaustive pixel-wise annotations, reducing the burden of manual labeling, while also enabling robust and clinically relevant predictions.    

This work is co-funded by the European Union, under the Horizon Europe Innovation Action ThrombUS+ (Grant Agreement No. 101137227). Views and opinions expressed are however those of the authors only and do not necessarily reflect those of the European Union or HADEA as the granting authority. Neither the European Union nor the grant-ing authority HADEA can be held responsible for them. 
----------------------
# Description

Challenge participants are invited to submit their responses to one or both of the respective data challenges in Kaggle, and submit a full paper in the respective workshop conference describing their methodology and their results (https://cloudcomp.eai-conferences.org/2026/call-for-workshop-papers/).

EAI CloudComp Workshop Important Dates:   
Submission deadline 30th April 2026.   
Notification deadline  15th May 2026.   
Camera-ready deadline 30th May 2026.      
  
## Dataset description
The dataset consists of **compression ultrasound video scannings** of lower limbs collected during a multi-center cohort study in European Hospitals (Greece, France, Italy and Lithuania ([https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06989255](https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06989255)). Patients suspected of DVT are scanned using conventional ultrasound machines, after they had given informed consent form, according to a dedicated scanning protocol. All hospitals have been granted ethics approvals from their respective local ethics committees, prior enrollment and data collection.   

The first anatomical site refers near the inguinal ligament, where the common femoral vein (CFV) and the common femoral artery (CFA) are visualized. The second compression video is recorded a few centimeters distally, where the great saphenous vein is visible, branching off of the common femoral vein (CFV). The third compression video is acquired in the middle of the thigh, where the femoral vein is visible. The video from the fourth anatomical site is recorded below the knee, where compression of the popliteal vein (PV) is recorded. Videos were collected from the left (L), right (R) or both limbs, based on clinical symptoms.   

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7621325%2F37cdbaeec06e55eedaae3fcc5bf78064%2FPicture%201.svg?generation=1763636802684529&alt=media)    
Figure: The four anatomical sites of the right limb where ultrasound compression videos were recorded. Ultrasound probe (red rectangle) was positioned perpendicular to the respective veins and used for compression. CFV: common femoral vein, GSV: great saphenous vein, FV: femoral vein, Pop: popliteal vein.   

All videos have been anonymized prior uploading to the eCRF platform using an open-source software. All personal-related DICOM attributes were removed, videos were cropped to desired dimensions, and finally, for each DICOM file, a filename-based tag were added, for representing the anatomical site (CFV, GS, FV, PV) visualized and the laterality of the limb (L, R).   

All DICOM files and a structure report filled by the medical expert were stored in the eCRF platform, per patient. The structure report is based on the compression interpretation and diagnosis made by the medical expert for each available video recording. In the structure report medical experts rate the compressibility of the visualized veins (as No (0), Partial (1), Yes (2)) and the presence of thrombosis (as Yes (1) or No (0)). Consequently, the provided dataset for the Data Challenge is created by combining the filename-based tags with the structured report which assesses the compressibility and the presence of thrombosis. This information will serve as ground truth annotations of the compression ultrasound recordings to train artificial intelligence and machine learning models.   

The Data Challenge focuses on one clinically relevant task based on compression ultrasound recordings:   
**Predict DVT presence based on vein compressibility**.  

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Log Loss](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.log_loss.html) between the predicted probability and the observed target.

## Submission File
For each Id in the test set, you must predict a probability for the Thrombosis variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,Thrombosis
    file1.dcm,1
    file2.dcm,0.5
    etc.",,False,True
