10803,cyprus-cars-price-oracle,"# Overview

## The Cyprus Cars Price Oracle

A local car dealership in Limassol, Cyprus is struggling to price used cars fairly. They've been relying on gut feelings and random market checks, but inconsistencies are losing them both customers and profits. They need a **data-driven pricing model** that can estimate a car's market value from its listing characteristics.
----------------------
# Description

### Goal

Build a regression model that predicts the **asking price** (in EUR) of a used car listed on a popular Cyprus classifieds website, based on features like year, mileage, engine size, fuel type, body type, power, location, and extras.

### Context

The dataset contains **~730 real car listings** from Cyprus. Car prices span a huge range ¡ª from under 1,000 to over 200,000 EUR ¡ª so your model will be evaluated on **Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE)**, which treats a 50% error on a cheap car and a 50% error on an expensive car equally.

The data is messy: some columns have missing values, mixed formats (e.g. engine size as `""1,6L""`), and free-text fields that need parsing. Feature engineering will be key to a strong solution.

### Evaluation

Submissions are scored on **MAPE** (lower is better). See the Evaluation tab for the full scoring formula.

### Acknowledgements

Data sourced from publicly available car listings on [Bazaraki](https://www.bazaraki.com/), a popular classifieds website in Cyprus. This is a learning competition ¡ª no monetary prizes are awarded.
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Metric

Submissions are evaluated on **MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error)**:

$$\text{MAPE} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left| \frac{y_i - \hat{y}_i}{y_i} \right|$$

where $y_i$ is the actual price and $\hat{y}_i$ is your predicted price.

**Lower MAPE is better.** A MAPE of 0.25 means your predictions are off by 25% on average.

### Scoring

Your competition points (0¨C100) are calculated as:

$$\text{points} = \max(0, \min(100, (0.65 - \text{MAPE}) \cdot 200))$$

| MAPE | Points |
|------|--------|
| >= 0.65 | 0 |
| 0.55 | 20 |
| 0.45 | 40 |
| 0.35 | 60 |
| 0.25 | 80 |
| <= 0.15 | 100 |

### Submission Format

For each row in the test set, you must predict the `price`. Your submission file should have exactly 2 columns:

```
Id,price
0,15000.00
1,32500.00
2,8700.00
...
```

The file must contain a header and have the same number of rows as the test set.",,False,True
10804,dy-gs-bench-dynamic-3-dgs-compression-icme-2026-grand-challenge,"# Overview

<div align=""center"">
<h1><strong>IEEE ICME 2026 Grand Challenge</strong></h1>
</div>
<h2>&nbsp;</h2>
<h1>DyGS-Bench: Dynamic Gaussian Splatting Compression</h1>

<h2>Important Dates</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>2026.02.05</strong> Starter kit + baseline + dataset release (scripts, format checks)</li>
  <li><strong>2026.04.01</strong> Stage 1 Deadline</li>
  <li><strong>2026.04.25</strong> Final submission deadline</li>
  <li><strong>2026.05.01</strong> Results announcement </li>
</ul>

<h2>Challenge Overview</h2>
<p align=""justify"">
Dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting (DyGS) enables real-time, high-fidelity rendering of dynamic scenes and is increasingly used in AR/VR/XR and volumetric video. However, naively storing a time-varying set of Gaussian primitives leads to prohibitive storage and bandwidth costs. While many compression approaches have been proposed, progress is slowed by the lack of a standardized benchmark that evaluates <em>rate</em>, <em>rendering quality</em>, and <em>online decoding cost</em> under a unified protocol.
</p>

<p align=""justify"">
<strong>DyGS-Bench</strong> focuses on a realistic deployment setting where only the reconstructed <strong>native Gaussian sequence</strong> is available (no original multi-view training images/videos). The goal of this Grand Challenge is to advance practical codecs that compress dynamic Gaussian assets into a compact bitstream and reconstruct renderable Gaussians efficiently.
</p>

<h2>Task</h2>
<p align=""justify"">
Given a native dynamic Gaussian sequence {G_t} and a fixed camera set C, design an <strong>encoder</strong> (offline) and <strong>decoder</strong> (online, measured) that:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Produces a compact <strong>bitstream</strong> from {G_t} (offline encoding is not time-constrained).</li>
  <li>Determine code stream size for coded Gaussian.</li>
  <li>Achieves high rendering quality when rendering and temporal consistency Ghat_t under C compared with renderings of {G_t}.</li>
</ul>



