10808,kirinyaga-university-computer-science-community-beginner-regression,"# Overview

Welcome to the Kirinyaga University (CSK) Machine Learning Challenge!

This competition is designed as a hands-on introduction to applied machine learning, focusing on a regression problem using realistic, structured data. It is especially suitable for beginners in AI and ML who want to learn how to build end-to-end models the right way.
----------------------
# Description

Mobile money platforms generate large volumes of transactional and behavioral data every day. Understanding how users interact with these platforms makes it possible to estimate future usage patterns, assess engagement, and support better financial decision-making.

In this challenge, you will work with aggregated mobile money behavioral data representing user activity over recent time windows. The dataset is designed to reflect realistic patterns while remaining accessible to beginners in machine learning.

All data provided in this competition is synthetic and created strictly for educational purposes.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between the predicted transaction value and the true observed value.

RMSE measures the average magnitude of prediction errors and penalizes larger errors more heavily. Lower RMSE values indicate better model performance.

Formally, RMSE is defined as:
$$
RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2}
$$

Submission File

For each id in the test set, you must predict the corresponding value of the target variable next_30d_txn_value.

The submission file must:

Be in CSV format

Contain a header row

Include predictions for all rows in the test set","# Dataset Description

?? Files

The following files are provided:

train.csv ¨C The training dataset.
Contains feature columns and the target variable.

test.csv ¨C The test dataset.
Contains the same feature columns as train.csv except the target variable.

sample_submission.csv ¨C A sample submission file.
Shows the correct format required for Kaggle submissions.

?? What Are You Predicting?

The goal of this competition is to predict the column:

next_30d_txn_value

This represents the total transaction value (KES) a user is expected to generate in the next 30 days, based on historical behavioral data.

?? Data Format

Each row represents a single user

The id column uniquely identifies each user

The target variable appears only in train.csv

All values are already aggregated (no raw transaction logs)

?? Columns
Identifier

id
Unique identifier for each user.
This column must be included in the submission file.

Account & Profile Features

account_age_days
Number of days since the user¡¯s account was created.

kyc_level
Know-Your-Customer (KYC) verification level.
Possible values: low, medium, high.

is_business_account
Indicates whether the account is a business account.

0 = Personal account

1 = Business account

region_code
User¡¯s region.
Example values: Central, Nairobi, Eastern, Western.

Transaction Behavior Features

tx_count_30d
Total number of transactions made in the last 30 days.

tx_amount_mean_30d
Average transaction amount over the last 30 days.

tx_amount_std_30d
Standard deviation of transaction amounts over the last 30 days.

Activity & Temporal Features

active_days_30d
Number of days the user was active in the last 30 days.

weekend_tx_ratio
Proportion of transactions that occurred on weekends.
Value between 0 and 1.

night_tx_ratio
Proportion of transactions that occurred at night.
Value between 0 and 0.5.

Additional / Noise Feature

promo_messages_received
Number of promotional messages sent to the user.
This feature may have weak or no predictive power and is included intentionally.

Target Variable (train.csv only)

next_30d_txn_value
Total transaction value (KES) generated by the user in the next 30 days.
This is the value your model must predict.",False,True
