10818,dy-gs-bench-dynamic-3-dgs-compression,"# Overview

<div align=""center"">
<h1><strong>IEEE ICME 2026 Grand Challenge</strong></h1>
</div>
<h2>&nbsp;</h2>
<h1>DyGS-Bench: Dynamic Gaussian Splatting Compression &amp; Reconstruction</h1>

<h2>Important Dates (tentative)</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>2026.01.21</strong> Website information due to ICME&#39;26 (Word document)</li>
  <li><strong>2026.02.10</strong> Starter kit + baseline release (scripts, format checks)</li>
  <li><strong>2026.02.15</strong> Development phase opens (online submissions)</li>
  <li><strong>2026.04.15</strong> Development phase submission deadline</li>
  <li><strong>2026.05.05</strong> Testing phase opens</li>
  <li><strong>2026.05.10</strong> Final submission deadline</li>
  <li><strong>2026.05.15</strong> Results announcement (recommended by ICME&#39;26 GC chairs)</li>
  <li><strong>2026.05.30</strong> ICME&#39;26 Grand Challenge author registration deadline</li>
</ul>

<h2>Challenge Overview</h2>
<p align=""justify"">
Dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting (DyGS) enables real-time, high-fidelity rendering of dynamic scenes and is increasingly used in AR/VR/XR and volumetric video. However, naively storing a time-varying set of Gaussian primitives leads to prohibitive storage and bandwidth costs. While many compression approaches have been proposed, progress is slowed by the lack of a standardized benchmark that evaluates <em>rate</em>, <em>rendering quality</em>, and <em>online decoding cost</em> under a unified protocol.
</p>

<p align=""justify"">
<strong>DyGS-Bench</strong> focuses on a realistic deployment setting where only the reconstructed <strong>native Gaussian sequence</strong> is available (no original multi-view training images/videos). The goal of this Grand Challenge is to advance practical codecs that compress dynamic Gaussian assets into a compact bitstream and reconstruct renderable Gaussians efficiently.
</p>

<h2>Task</h2>
<p align=""justify"">
Given a native dynamic Gaussian sequence {G_t} and a fixed camera set C, design an <strong>encoder</strong> (offline) and <strong>decoder</strong> (online, measured) that:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Produces a compact <strong>bitstream</strong> from {G_t} (offline encoding is not time-constrained).</li>
  <li>Reconstructs renderable Gaussians Ghat_t from the bitstream (online decoding time and memory are measured).</li>
  <li>Achieves high rendering quality when rendering Ghat_t under C compared with renderings of {G_t}.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Evaluation (high-level)</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Quality:</strong> PSNR / SSIM / LPIPS on rendered images under the fixed camera set.</li>
  <li><strong>Rate:</strong> total bitstream size (and derived metrics such as MB/frame or bpp).</li>
  <li><strong>Efficiency:</strong> online decoding FPS and peak GPU memory (measured on decoded Gaussians).</li>
</ul>

<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Please refer to the Data and Evaluation pages for details on dataset access, formats, and submission rules.</strong></p>

<h2>Contact</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Forum:</strong> please use the competition forum for Q&amp;A (recommended).</li>
  <li><strong>Email:</strong> see the Organizers page for contact emails.</li>
</ul>

----------------------
# Description

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F14564763%2F7127122281456a6570e1d17e1eace58d%2Fdunhuang.png?generation=1770047891983328&alt=media)

 Dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting (DyGS) enables real-time, high-fidelity rendering of dynamic scenes and is increasingly used in AR/VR/XR and volumetric video. However, naively storing a time-varying set of Gaussian primitives leads to prohibitive storage and bandwidth costs. While many compression approaches have been proposed, progress is slowed by the lack of a standardized benchmark that evaluates rate, rendering quality, and online decoding cost under a unified protocol.

DyGS-Bench focuses on a realistic deployment setting where only the reconstructed native Gaussian sequence is available (no original multi-view training images/videos). The goal of this Grand Challenge is to advance practical codecs that compress dynamic Gaussian assets into a compact bitstream and reconstruct renderable Gaussians efficiently. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

<div align=""center"">
<h1><strong>IEEE ICME 2026 Grand Challenge</strong></h1>
</div>
<h2>&nbsp;</h2>
<h1>DyGS-Bench Evaluation</h1>

<h2>What is evaluated?</h2>
<p align=""justify"">
Submissions are evaluated by comparing your rendered images with hidden GT images under the same views:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Quality</strong>: image-to-image metrics on aligned frames/cameras.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Image submission track (current)</h2>
<p align=""justify"">
For online testing convenience, this Codabench bundle currently evaluates an <strong>image submission track</strong>:
participants upload their locally rendered test-view images. The scorer compares them against hidden GT images.
</p>

<h2>Metrics</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>PSNR</strong> (higher is better)</li>
  <li><strong>SSIM</strong> (higher is better)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Submission Package (template)</h2>
<p align=""justify"">Unless otherwise stated, please prepare a single zip file with the following structure:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;scene_name&gt;/
  frame000000/
    images/
      cam00.png
      cam01.png
      ...
  frame000001/
    images/
      cam00.png
    ...
readme.txt (optional)
</code></pre>
<p align=""justify"">
<strong>Note:</strong> Your submission must contain the required images for each GT frame/camera you want to be evaluated on.
</p>

<h3>How Codabench runs your submission</h3>
<p align=""justify"">
Codabench typically executes two steps:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ingestion</strong>: copies your submission into the scoring input folder.</li>
  <li><strong>Scoring</strong>: compares your uploaded images with hidden GT images and writes <code>scores.json</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Baseline</h2>
<p align=""justify"">
Baseline results (representative dynamic reconstruction methods) will be provided in the starter kit, together with scripts for rendering and metric computation.
</p>

<h2>Contact</h2>
<p>For questions about evaluation rules or submission formatting, please contact the organizers listed on the Organizers page.</p>","# Dataset Description

<div align=""center"">
<h1><strong>IEEE ICME 2026 Grand Challenge</strong></h1>
</div>
<h2>&nbsp;</h2>
<h1>DyGS-Bench Data</h1>

<h2>Data Access</h2>
<p align=""justify""><strong>By accessing the data, participants agree to the dataset license and the challenge Terms &amp; Conditions.</strong></p>

<h2>Dataset: Native Dynamic Gaussian Sequences</h2>
<p align=""justify"">
DyGS-Bench focuses on a <strong>native Gaussians-only</strong> setting: the input is a reconstructed dynamic 3D Gaussian sequence (no original multi-view training RGB/video data). Each sequence includes:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Native Gaussian assets</strong>: time-varying Gaussians (e.g., per-frame PLY or equivalent).</li>
  <li><strong>Camera set</strong>: calibrated camera parameters for rendering-based evaluation.</li>
  <li><strong>Rendered reference images</strong>: renderings produced from the <em>uncompressed</em> Gaussians under the fixed camera set (used as supervision and as ground truth for scoring).</li>
</ul>



<h2>Recommended Local Folder Structure (example)</h2>
<pre><code>DyNativeGaussian_sequence/
  &lt;scene_name&gt;/
    cameras.json
    gaussians/
      000000.ply
      000001.ply
      ...
    renders_ref/
      cam00/
        000000.png
        000001.png
      cam01/
        ...
</code></pre>

<h2>Splits</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Training</strong>: full access (for developing codec components and analyzing statistics).</li>
  <li><strong>Validation</strong>: used for online leaderboard evaluation (references are hidden on the platform).</li>
  <li><strong>Test</strong>: used for final ranking (references are hidden).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Citation</h2>
<p align=""justify"">
If you use the dataset and benchmark in a publication, please cite the DyGS-Bench paper (citation will be updated here once the preprint is public).
</p>

<h2>Contact</h2>
<p>For questions about data format or access, please contact the organizers listed on the Organizers page.</p>",False,True
10819,humidity-prediction-given-weather-data,"# Overview

Hello and welcome!

This competition has been designed as a fun programming (and also machine learning) exercise for you for the current lab (lab 1). In this task you're given a fun dataset on the weather conditions of the region Szeged in Hungary. The conditions include temperature, apparent temperature, wind speed etc. You are expected to experiment and come up with a **regression model**, based on what we've covered in the lab today and also in the lectures, which will predict the humidity given the weather conditions mentioned in the dataset. 


----------------------
# Description

For your convenience, here are the details regarding the task:

## Expectation and the dataset

Train a regression model to predict humidity given the weather conditions in the dataset. It's provided as csv files in a tabular format. There are the following columns in the training set: 

```
Idx, Summary, Precip Type, Temperature (C), Apparent Temperature (C), Humidity, Wind Speed (km/h), Wind Bearing (degrees), Visibility (km), Loud Cover, Pressure (millibars), Daily Summary
```

For this task, your target column is `Humidity`.


## Files 

| File Name | Description  |
| --- | --- |
|  train.csv | Contains the training data  |
| test.csv | Contains the test data and you'll be using it to measure the performance of your model |
| sample_submission.csv | A sample example on file submission format |


### Data splitting?

To make things easier for you within the short timeframe of the lab, we've taken care of data splits (as you can see above) and picked out some noisy elements from it. Yet not every nuisance is gone though. There are also more features in the data then you may need for your task. We hope that you'll be able to explore the data and the nuisances around it and also train your models with well performing scores.


## Leaderboard

As we've designed this to be a fun competition, we'll be asking the top few performers to come forward and explain their solution, including but limited to their choice of model, learning parameters, regularisation methods, choice of features etc. Please feel free to be creative and ask questions to the TAs if something is not clear.

----------------------
# Evaluation


We will be judging your models based on their R-squared score or coefficient of determination. You can use the scoring function available in scikit-learn. For example:

```python
from sklearn.metrics import r2_score


score = r2_score(y_pred=predicitions, y_true=targets)
```

## Submitting solutions

After training, you'll have to run the predictions on your model with the test data, and the test data won't have the target (Humidity) column in them. Store the predictions and write them into a csv file with the `Idx` column. Then submit it to this competition. 

So your submitted file will look like this:

| Idx | Humidity |
| --- | --- |
|  1 | 0.55 |


### Note for pandas and polars

If you're using pandas or polars to create csv files, the default behavior is to include the indexes. You'll have to turn them off. For example: 
```python

# assuming predicitions is a data frame here:
predicitions.to_csv(path, index=False)
```



Best of luck!",,False,True
10820,icdar-2026-circleid-writer-identification,"# Overview

CircleID is the ICDAR 2026 competition on identifying who drew a circle using only a scanned image of a hand drawn circle.
A circle looks simple, yet it contains subtle cues from human motor behavior. However, appearance is also affected by pen/ink, which makes writer identification challenging.

This competition focuses on **Writer Identification**.

The test set includes circles from writers that do not appear in the training set. For these unknown writers you must output `-1`.

A second Kaggle competition covers **Pen Classification** and uses the same dataset:
https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/icdar-2026-circleid-pen-classification

**Starter baseline:** Can be found under Code > CircleID Writer Baseline and on [GitHub](https://github.com/ThomasGorges/icdar2026-circleid-baseline).

For questions and announcements please use the Discussion tab.
More information and contact details are available on the [CircleID project page](https://thomasgorges.github.io/icdar2026-circleid/)

**Note**: As mentioned in the rules, all teams agree to provide a method description and training details sufficient to understand the approach.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Metric: Top-1 accuracy.

Evaluation is computed on the full test set (known + unknown writers). Known writers must be predicted with the correct writer ID. Unknown writers must be predicted as `-1`.

**Leaderboard protocol:** During the competition, the leaderboard is computed using only 30% of the test set annotations (public leaderboard). The remaining 70% of the test set annotations are held back (private leaderboard) and are used to update the final rankings at the end of the challenge.",,False,True
10821,icdar-2026-circleid-pen-classification,"# Overview

CircleID is the ICDAR 2026 competition on classifying which pen was used to draw a circle using only a scanned image of a hand drawn circle.

This competition focuses on **Pen Classification**.

A pen prediction is required for every test image, including images drawn by writers that do not appear in the training set.

A second Kaggle competition covers **Writer Identification** and uses the same dataset:
https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/icdar-2026-circleid-writer-identification

**Starter baseline:** Can be found under Code > CircleID Pen Baseline and on [GitHub](https://github.com/ThomasGorges/icdar2026-circleid-baseline).

For questions and announcements please use the Discussion tab.
More information and contact details are available on the [CircleID project page](https://thomasgorges.github.io/icdar2026-circleid/)

**Note**: As mentioned in the rules, all teams agree to provide a method description and training details sufficient to understand the approach.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Metric: Top-1 accuracy.

Evaluation is computed on the full test set. We will also report macro-averaged F1 in the final competition report.

**Leaderboard protocol:** During the competition, the leaderboard is computed using only 30% of the test set annotations (public leaderboard). The remaining 70% of the test set annotations are held back (private leaderboard) and are used to update the final rankings at the end of the challenge.",,False,True
10822,no-code-spatial-omics-using-fusion,"# Overview

Join this no-code competition to explore the intersection of histology and molecular data using FUSION. Participants will utilize curated 10x Visium kidney datasets to analyze tissue morphology and molecular composition at the Functional Tissue Unit (FTU) level.

Using interactive tools like HistomicsUI, you will examine 72 quantitative morphometric features alongside cell-type proportions. Leverage UMAP and violin plots to uncover relationships between disease states and structural changes. Your goal is to identify unique biological patterns and submit a testable hypothesis that links tissue morphology to spatial-omics signals.
----------------------
# Description

Submissions will be scored by the FUSION developers and subject-matter experts. Top 3 winning teams will be invited to develop their submission into a manuscript and will receive technical and editorial support.

You can learn about the FUSION platform through [an asynchronous training course](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1p6ivJdyrrrxGcANAERSLIdbYPx-xYlLF/view).

You can assess FUSION directly [here](https://athena.rc.ufl.edu/). You can use the login and password below if you don't wish to create a personal workbench.
Login: public
Password: public
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Submission File: Multimodal Hypothesis Generation in Kidney Pathology

The objective of this no-code competition is to utilize the **FUSION 1.0 platform** to generate a testable biological hypothesis by performing a multimodal analysis of kidney tissue. You are expected to investigate the intersection between tissue morphology (pathomics) and molecular composition (spatial transcriptomics) within specific Functional Tissue Units (FTUs).

#### 1. Data Exploration and Selection
*   **Access the Dataset**: Navigate to the Human Kidney Atlas v2 collection and select a dataset from either the Visium FFPE or Visium Frozen directories.
*   **Identify Conditions**: Compare different tissue states, such as Reference (healthy), Acute Kidney Injury (AKI), Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD), or Diabetic Kidney Disease (DKD).
*   **Select FTUs**: Focus your analysis on specific kidney compartments, such as *on-sclerotic vs. sclerotic glomeruli, tubules, or the interstitium.

#### 2. Multimodal Analysis Workflow
Your analysis must bridge the gap between visual pathology and molecular signals using the following tools:
*   **Quantitative Morphometrics**: Use the Feature Extraction data to analyze 72 distinct metrics, including the size, shape, color, and texture of tissue structures.
*   **Molecular Cell Composition**: Inspect cell deconvolution results to determine the abundance of major cell types, such as podocytes, fibroblasts, and immune cells, within your selected structures.
*   **Integrated Visualization**: Use Metadata Plots (Violin, Scatter, or UMAP) to identify clusters or gradients. Utilize the Lasso Select tool within these plots to link numerical outliers or groups directly back to their spatial context on the Whole Slide Image (WSI).

#### 3. Hypothesis Generation Requirement
Your final submission must state a hypothesis that links a specific morphological change to a molecular shift. For example, you might investigate:
*   How glomerular hypertrophy (eosinophilic area) in diabetic samples correlates with a reduction in podocyte transcript expression.
*   The relationship between interstitial fibrosis and the localization of immune cell infiltration in AKI vs. CKD.
*   How the nuclear aspect ratio changes in glomerular cells relates to increased fibroblast content.

### Submission Requirement
Submit a written hypothesis accompanied by a FUSION screenshot showing the specific data distribution (e.g., a Scatter or UMAP plot) and the corresponding highlighted tissue structures that support your observation.","# Dataset Description

Data is available online within the platform.
[https://athena.rc.ufl.edu/#collections](https://athena.rc.ufl.edu/#collections)",False,True
10823,ai-grand-prix-task1,"# Overview

*Each task represents a different real-world racing decision problem and is hosted as a separate Kaggle competition, all based on the same underlying dataset but with different prediction objectives.*
----------------------
# Description

### ***TASK 1:***
To prepare the car for the grid, you must first conduct a rigorous structural inspection of the dataset to ensure it can withstand the pressures of the race. Begin by checking the air pressure in the tires by identifying and resolving all missing values, either by refilling gaps with statistical averages or replacing incomplete records entirely. Next, strip away unnecessary weight by scanning for damaged or duplicate spare parts, ensuring that no redundant data rows compromise the car¡¯s efficiency. To complete the initial setup, upgrade the onboard electronics by verifying that all technical readings are correctly formatted, converting every relevant performance metric into a precise numeric value to ensure the telemetry systems are fully operational. Once the foundation is secure, focus on performance optimization to ensure the car handles perfectly at high speeds. Fine-tune the aerodynamics by detecting and mitigating extreme outliers that could destabilize the vehicle¡¯s performance, carefully smoothing out anomalies without discarding essential structural components that define the car¡¯s integrity. Be cautious not to over-clean, as maintaining the core features is vital for the car¡¯s overall strength. Finally, balance the engine power by normalizing the data, bringing all performance metrics into a stable, comparable range. With these calibrations complete, the dataset is refined, balanced, and ready to be submitted for final race approval.

You are part of a Formula-1 race engineering team tasked with evaluating a car¡¯s on-track performance. Using the provided race data, select key features that represent driver skill such as starting grid, qualifying position, and driver experience, car quality such as constructor rank, and race conditions such as total laps, laps completed, laps led, pit stops, and race outcomes. Add additional features that you think are relevant to predicting the car¡¯s average speed based on logical reasoning and domain understanding. Train a machine learning regression model to learn the relationship between these factors and predict the car¡¯s average speed. The model is evaluated on unseen race data using the R2 score, where a higher value reflects a more accurate and better-engineered predictive model.
----------------------
# Evaluation

For each id in the test set, you must predict the average speed of the car.

***Submissions are evaluated using Mean Absolute Error (MAE) between the predicted average speed and the true average speed.***

MAE measures the average magnitude of the errors in a set of predictions, without considering their direction. Lower MAE values indicate better performance.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:


id,avg_speed
1,245.6
2,231.4
3,260.1

etc.",,False,True
10824,ar-xiv-papers-classification,"# Overview

Scientific research papers on arXiv span a wide range of domains, each with distinct terminology, structure, and mathematical style. Correctly identifying the primary research category of a paper is critical for indexing, search, recommendation systems, and large-scale academic discovery.

In this competition, participants are challenged to build a machine learning model that can accurately classify full-length arXiv research papers into their correct arXiv subject category, using only the paper text as input.

Unlike typical short-text classification tasks, the documents in this dataset are long-form scientific papers, often containing tens of thousands of characters. This requires participants to think carefully about document representation, long-context handling, and robust evaluation strategies.

The goal of this competition is to develop/fine-tune a model that can:

Understand long, structured scientific text
- Capture domain-specific language and concepts


- Correctly assign each paper to one of 11 arXiv subject categories

- Achieve balanced performance across all categories, rather than overfitting to the most common ones

Submissions are evaluated using Macro F1-Score, ensuring that all categories are treated equally and encouraging models that generalize well across diverse research areas. . .
----------------------
# Description

## ?? Competition Description

Modern scientific research spans a wide range of highly specialized domains. Platforms such as **arXiv** organize papers into subject categories to support indexing, discovery, and recommendation. However, manually classifying long and technical research papers is time-consuming and does not scale with the growing volume of submissions.

This competition focuses on **automatic classification of full-length arXiv research papers** into their correct subject categories using machine learning. Unlike short-text classification problems, this task involves **long-form scientific documents**, often containing mathematical notation, domain-specific terminology, and structured arguments.

Participants are expected to design models that go beyond surface-level keyword matching and demonstrate an understanding of **document-level semantics**.

---

## ?? Problem Details

Each paper in the dataset belongs to **exactly one arXiv subject category**.

- **Input:** Full text of an arXiv research paper  
- **Output:** One arXiv subject category label  
- **Task Type:** Single-label, multi-class classification  
- **Number of Classes:** 11

Formally, the goal is to learn a function  
Formally, the goal is to learn a function f(text) ¡ú category.


that generalizes well across different scientific domains.

---

## ??? arXiv Category Mapping

The dataset uses standard arXiv subject identifiers.  
The mapping from category codes to their full names is provided below:

| Category Code | Full Name |
|--------------|-----------|
| `math.AC` | Commutative Algebra |
| `cs.CV` | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition |
| `cs.AI` | Artificial Intelligence |
| `cs.SY` | Systems and Control |
| `math.GR` | Group Theory |
| `cs.CE` | Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science |
| `cs.PL` | Programming Languages |
| `cs.IT` | Information Theory |
| `cs.DS` | Data Structures and Algorithms |
| `cs.NE` | Neural and Evolutionary Computing |
| `math.ST` | Statistics Theory |

Each label represents the **primary research area** of the paper.

---

## ?? Dataset Characteristics

Important properties of the dataset include:

- Papers are **not truncated** and may exceed tens of thousands of characters
- Text may contain:
  - Mathematical expressions
  - Formal definitions
  - Citations and references
- Classes are **not perfectly balanced**

As a result, standard short-text NLP pipelines are often insufficient.

---

## ?? Modeling Considerations

Participants are free to use any approach that can run on Kaggle backend. There are no constraints on the approach.

Participants may need to consider approaches such as:
- Transformer-based models with long-context handling
- Chunking or hierarchical representations for long documents
- Aggregation of chunk-level representations
- Classical baselines (e.g., TF-IDF with linear classifiers)

Evaluation is performed using **Macro F1-Score**, encouraging balanced performance across all categories rather than dominance by frequent classes.

---

## ?? Notes

- Spreadsheet software (e.g., Excel or Google Sheets) cannot reliably open these files due to document length.
- Use Python (`pandas`), R, or similar tools for data loading and analysis.
- The goal is robust document understanding, not heuristic or keyword-based classification.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Macro F1 Score",,False,True
