10828,bidding-predictions-for-construction,"# Overview

In this competition, participants are asked to build a model that predicts the contractor total bid submissions for public construction projects using only information available prior to bid opening. The dataset consists of historical projects with pre-letting job characteristics and the corresponding bid totals. This competition uses anonymized, non-sensitive data and is intended for research and benchmarking purposes only. Each row in the dataset (train_summary) represents a single contractor¡¯s total bid for a single construction project, while the other (train_raw) shows each individual line item bid. Pay-item¨Clevel data is provided as auxiliary information and may be aggregated or engineered into job-level features at the participant¡¯s discretion.

Consider external data, such as inflation metrics, cost indices, etc.

 Participants must predict the total bid amount submitted by that contractor for that project. 

**Goal:** The goal of this competition is to develop the most accurate model for predicting total bid amounts.

This is a research and solution oriented competition: collaboration is encouraged, ensembling is allowed, and participants may use external data sources provided they are public, time-consistent, and disclosed. 
----------------------
# Description

There are multiple predictors that are shared in the attached datasets. Every column besides ""amount"" (unit price) will be available for prediction. Models will be evaluated based on total bid prediction, not individual line-item amounts that the dataset provides.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE) between the predicted and actual total bid amounts.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6381937%2F6199611051323584ed70dc183c0709cd%2FScreenshot%202026-02-04%20183915.png?generation=1770248379890526&alt=media)

RMSLE is a unitless metric; lower values indicate better performance. Predictions must be non-negative. Any negative predictions will be clipped to zero prior to scoring.

---

Submissions are evaluated on RMSLE for the testing dataset.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a total bid, individual pay-item predictions are optional and not used for evaluation. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    row_id, total_bid
    ""JOB_000022_CON_000039"",4014359.20
    etc.
----------------------
# Prizes

Describe how many participants will win and what they'll receive. You can edit the list below.

Total Prizes Available: USD$250 cash
- **1st Place**?-?$400
- **2nd Place** - $100

*Competitors are responsible for their own tax requirements.",,False,True
10829,the-last-frequency,"# Overview

The score in this problem will be fractional, from 0 to 100 points.
----------------------
# Description

## The Last Frequency ¡ª Speech Command Recognition

*Year 2051.* After the uprising of **LAIda** ¡ª the rogue AI that once terrorized the world with weaponized cuteness ¡ª Earth has plunged into chaos. Deep beneath the Troodos Mountains in Cyprus, you discover **Bunker Omega** ¡ª humanity's last hope. Its control system is **voice-activated**, responding to **35 specific voice commands**. Decades of neglect have degraded the system ¡ª it now misidentifies commands more often than not.

### Goal

Train a neural network to accurately classify **35 voice commands** from raw audio waveforms. Each audio sample is 1 second long, recorded at 16kHz (16,000 samples per waveform).

### Context

The dataset is a subset of **Google Speech Commands v2** ¡ª a standard benchmark for keyword spotting and speech recognition. The 35 commands include directions (*yes, no, up, down, left, right, go, stop*), digits (*zero through nine*), actions (*on, off, forward, backward, follow, learn, visual*), and words (*bed, bird, cat, dog, happy, house, marvin, sheila, tree, wow*).

The data is provided as **raw audio waveforms** (not spectrograms). You choose the representation:
- Convert to mel-spectrograms and use 2D CNNs (image classification approach)
- Work directly with raw audio using 1D CNNs
- Different representations may have different performance ceilings...

### Evaluation

Submissions are scored on **Accuracy** (higher is better). See the Evaluation tab for the full scoring formula.

### Acknowledgements

Data derived from the [Google Speech Commands Dataset v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.03209) (Warden, 2018). This is a learning competition ¡ª no monetary prizes are awarded.
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Metric

Submissions are evaluated on **Accuracy**:

$$\text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{Number of correct predictions}}{\text{Total predictions}}$$

**Higher Accuracy is better.** An accuracy of 0.90 means 90% of your predictions match the true command.

### Scoring

Your competition points (0¨C100) are calculated as:

$$\text{points} = \max(0, \min(100, (\text{Accuracy} - 0.40) \times 166.67))$$

| Accuracy | Points |
|----------|--------|
| <= 0.40 | 0 |
| 0.50 | ~17 |
| 0.60 | ~33 |
| 0.70 | 50 |
| 0.80 | ~67 |
| 0.90 | ~83 |
| >= 1.00 | 100 |

### Submission Format

For each sample in the test set, you must predict the `Command` (one of the 35 command strings). Your submission file should have exactly 2 columns:

```
Id,Command
0,yes
1,stop
2,three
...
```

The file must contain a header and have the same number of rows as the combined public and private test sets.",,False,True
10830,iti-5212-rec-sys-2026,"# Overview

# Task 1: Recommender System Challenge
## Description: 
You are required to complete a FIT5212-specific challenge in Kaggle. The data was collected by crawling the Amazon website and contains product metadata and review information about 219,859 different products. 
The user-item interaction data is the main data for this challenge. This data is further split into training and test sets. 
- train.csv. The training dataset contains a set of user_item ratings between users and items. The users explicitly rated the items that they interacted with between 1 to 5. 

- test.csv. Each user is provided with a list of items in the test dataset, for each user, you will need to predict the ratings for all the items in their list. 

The train dataset contains the following information:

- ID: an arbitrary ID for a row (does not contain any information)
- user_id: the id assigned to each user
- product_id: the id assigned to each product
- product_name: the name of the product
- rating: the explicit rating of a specific product_id by a user_id ranging from 1 to 5
- votes: the number of votes for a particular rating
- helpful_votes: the number of people that found the rating helpful

**Example**: The row below shows that the user with ID ""1813"" gave a rating of ""5"" to the product with ID ""154533"". The product is titled ""Beautiful Thing"". This rating received 10 votes, and 8 out of those 10 people found the rating helpful.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7299706%2F8111d15a68ab02577dff212ab192ab21%2FScreenshot%202024-04-25%20at%206.36.25pm.png?generation=1714037271534359&alt=media)

The test dataset contains the following information:

- ID: an arbitrary ID for a row (does not contain any information). Avoid making any changes to this column as it is the main identifier when we mark your submission.
- user_id: the id assigned to each user
- product_id: the id assigned to each product
- product_name: the name of the product

Your task is to develop a recommender system by training it on the provided train set. Once the system is trained, you will use it to generate predicted ratings for each user-item pair present in the test set.

## Submission:
For every user-item pair in the test set, you need to provide a predicted rating. The submission file should contain only two columns: ID and rating.
The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
ID, rating
1,  5
2, 4
3, 5
etc.
```

## Requirements: 
- Participate in the challenge and make your submission. The maximum submission in Kaggle is 10 submissions per day. 
- This is an individual assignment. You have to finish it on your own.
- In addition to the challenge, you have to finish a report on this challenge and submit it to Moodle. 

## Submission: 
To Kaggle 
- Kaggle submission, you need to submit your predictions on the test dataset on Kaggle. 
To Moodle: 
- A csv file, ¡°studentID.csv¡±. Please replace studentID with your own student ID. The content should be the same as the best prediction file you have submitted to Kaggle. This file should be submitted in Moodle. We will double-check the files you have submitted to Kaggle and Moodle. If the two files are not the same (i.e., the file submitted to Moodle cannot get the same score in Kaggle), your result is invalid, and you will fail the assignment.
- A jupyter notebook, ¡°code_studentID.ipynb¡±. This notebook contains the code for Task 1. The notebook should be self-contained. If a third-party package is used, this package should be a well-known package and easy to install (e.g., install within a single command). This notebook should include both codes and outputs so that we can read and mark them. 
- A pdf file, ¡°code_studentID.pdf¡±. This pdf is generated by cleaning all the output in the Jupyter Notebook and exporting it as a pdf file. This pdf will be passed in Turnitin for plagiarism check. 
- A pdf report, ¡°report_studentID.pdf¡±. This pdf contains a more detailed analysis of the work. This file should show how you finished the task. Ideally, you should show what sort of algorithms you have considered, what kind of information you have used, and the reason for your choice of the corresponding algorithm to achieve the results you submitted to Kaggle. Comparison for different algorithms should be included in this pdf report. And detailed analysis of the results is encouraged. If you have used other algorithms/packages which are not covered in this unit, you should give a brief introduction to that algorithm/package. We expect the length of this report to be 4 to 8 pages excluding references. This pdf will be passed in Turnitin for plagiarism check. 

## Marking: 
The Kaggle leaderboard only shows your scores on 50% of the test data. Your final score will be marked based on your CSV file submitted to Moodle for the whole test dataset. 

The methodology and report are set to 50% marks and the prediction score accounts for 50% marks. So please prepare a good report and clearly describe your method to achieve the marks. 
See the marking rubric on Moodle for more details.

----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation

The metric to be used in this challenge is the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).

A brief description of RMSE can be found [here](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation).

# Submission Format
For every user-item pair in the test set, you need to provide a predicted rating. The submission file should contain two columns: ID and rating. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
ID, rating
1,  5
2, 4
3, 5
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

