10832,predicting-employee-burnout-score-using-machine-learning,"# Overview

Employee burnout is a growing challenge in modern workplaces, affecting productivity, well-being, and employee retention. Factors such as workload, work pressure, poor work¨Clife balance, and mental fatigue play a significant role in burnout.

In this competition, participants will build a machine learning regression model to predict the burnout risk score of employees using anonymized workplace and well-being data. The dataset includes features related to job roles, workload, work¨Clife balance, job satisfaction, stress levels, and mental health indicators.

This competition is suitable for beginner to intermediate practitioners and provides hands-on experience in feature engineering, regression modeling, and performance evaluation using real-world inspired data.

----------------------
# Description

Employee burnout is a critical issue faced by organizations worldwide. Long working hours, high work pressure, lack of work¨Clife balance, and mental fatigue can significantly impact an employee¡¯s performance and overall well-being. Identifying burnout at an early stage can help organizations take preventive measures and promote healthier work environments.

In this competition, participants are provided with an anonymized dataset containing information related to employee demographics, job roles, workload, work¨Clife balance, job satisfaction, stress levels, sleep patterns, and mental health indicators. The objective is to use this data to build a machine learning model that accurately predicts the burnout risk score of employees.

This is a regression problem, where the target variable is a continuous burnout score ranging between 0 and 1. Participants are encouraged to perform data preprocessing, feature engineering, and experiment with different regression algorithms to improve model performance.

The competition is designed for beginner to intermediate machine learning practitioners and aims to provide hands-on experience with a realistic HR analytics problem while reinforcing core machine learning concepts such as regression modeling, evaluation metrics, and model generalization.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions in this competition are evaluated using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between the predicted burnout scores and the actual burnout scores.

RMSE measures the average magnitude of prediction errors by calculating the square root of the average squared difference between the predicted and true values. A lower RMSE indicates better model performance.

The target variable, burnout_score, is a continuous value ranging between 0 and 1. Participants are required to predict the burnout score for each employee in the test dataset.","# Dataset Description

**Dataset Description**

The dataset provided for this competition is designed to simulate a real-world employee well-being and workplace analytics scenario. It contains anonymized information related to employee demographics, job roles, workload, work¨Clife balance, and mental health indicators.
The dataset is synthetically generated to reflect realistic patterns observed in organizational settings. No personally identifiable information is included.

Each row in the dataset represents one employee, and the features collectively capture factors that influence employee burnout severity.

Files Included

**1. train.csv**

Contains employee-related features along with the target variable.

Used to train and validate machine learning models.

Target column:

burnout_score ¨C A continuous value between 0 and 1, representing the severity of employee burnout.

Higher values indicate higher burnout levels.

**2. test.csv**

Contains the same set of input features as train.csv.

Does not include the target variable.

Participants must predict the burnout score for each employee in this file.

**3. sample_submission.csv**

Demonstrates the correct submission format required for Kaggle evaluation.

Format:

employee_id,burnout_score

**Feature Overview**

The dataset includes a mix of numerical and categorical features, broadly categorized as:

Employee Information: Age, gender, education level, job role, experience, and company type

Workload & Job Pressure: Working hours, number of projects, overtime, work pressure score, and deadline frequency

Work¨CLife Balance & Support: Work¨Clife balance rating, remote work status, team size, manager support, and job satisfaction

Mental & Physical Well-Being: Stress level, mental fatigue score, sleep hours, physical activity, and health issues

Prediction Task

Participants are required to build a regression model to predict the burnout_score for each employee in the test dataset. The objective is to minimize the prediction error as measured by Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).",False,True
10833,advanced-insurance-forecasting-competition,"# Overview

Working with your learning team, revisit the Advanced Insurance case. Using the Training data, generative AI resources of your choice, and the visualizations we explored for class 12, build the best model you can to predict the missing premiums in the Test Data. Submit your predictions for the missing premiums on Kaggle by 11:59 PM on Sunday evening. You should come to class on Monday prepared to discuss your model and approach.

When you submit a set of predictions, you will get feedback on your model in the form of the RMSE (root mean squared error). The smaller the error, the better!

You are welcome to use ANY technology to complete this task. In fact, non-excel tech is encouraged -- have fun with it!  Please be prepared to share if/how generative AI was helpful. If you use non-excel tools for your analysis (e.g., python, R, etc.), we would love for you to demonstrate your approach in class. (All such tools are enrichment and, for the avoidance of doubt, will not be necessary on the exam).
----------------------
# Description

In this competition you will be using the data from the case Advanced Insurance. You will be predicting the annual rate that Competitor A charges to insure one vehicle for one year based on various attributes.

The training set contains information on 7000 accounts. You will be predicting the prices of 1500 other accounts. We will use mean absolute percentage error (MAPE), to evaluate your submissions.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is MAPE</a>. The MAPE is a measure of prediction accuracy, where the forecast error is measured relative to the magnitude of the quantity of interest.  MAPE can be expressed as:  </p>
<p>\[ MAPE = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (\frac{\left|A_{t}-F_{t}\right|}{A_{t}})  \]</p>

<p>where At is the actual value and Ft is the forecast value.</p>","# Dataset Description

Data used here came from the Darden Case Study: ""Advanced Insurance"". 

Working with your learning team, using the training data , generative AI resources of your choice, and the visualizations we explored in class, build the best model you can to predict the missing premiums in the Advanced Test tab . Submit your predictions for the missing premiums by 11:00 PM on Tuesday (Feb 17). You should come to our final class prepared to discuss your model and approach.

When you submit a set of predictions, you will see where your teams ends up on the leaderboard and get feedback on your model in the form of the RMSE (root mean squared error). The smaller the error, the better!

You are welcome to use ANY technology to complete this task. In fact, non-excel tech is encouraged -- have fun with it!  Please be prepared to share if/how generative AI was helpful. If you use non-excel tools for your analysis (e.g., python, R, etc.), we would love for you to demonstrate your approach in class. (All such tools are enrichment and, for the avoidance of doubt, will not be necessary on the exam).

**File descriptions**
- training.csv - the training set of 7500 rows and 18 columns
- test.csv - the test set of 1500 rows and 18 columns. The column of 'Comp_A_Premium' has no values
- Submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format

**Data fields**
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F228069%2F28b99133a267d31c702051056eadc905%2FScreenshot%202026-02-08%20175823.gif?generation=1770591523538523&alt=media)
- ID: A unique customer ID to identify accounts
- Comp_A_Premium:  The annual rate that Competitor A charges to insure one vehicle for one year
- SafeDrive_Score: The output of Advanced Insurance's in-house prediction model to assess the safety of a driver. Higher scores indicate a safer driver
- Age: 	The age of the insured driver
- Years_Driving:  The number of years of driving experience of the insured driver
- Gender	The reported gender of the insured driver
- Vehicle_Type	A code for the type of vehicle insured
- SC_or_MC?	An indication of whether the insured driver purchases insurance for a single vehicle (SC) or multiple vehicles (MC)
- Points	The number of penalty points on the insured driver's record. Higher points indicate more frequent or severe driving violations. 
- County_Residence	A categorical variable indicating the county of residence of the insured driver
- PIF?	A categorical variable indicating whether the insured driver's account with Competitor A has been paid in full. (1= paid in full)
- Homowner	A categorical variable indicated whether the insured driver is a homeowner (1 = homeowner)
- Education	A categorical variable indicating the highest level of education achieved by the insured driver.
- Marital_Status	A categorical variable indicating whether the insured driver is married (1 = married)
-  Organ_Donor?	A categorical variable indicating whether the insured driver is a registered organ doner (1 = organ doner)
- Months_Since_Policy_Inception	The number of months that have elapsed since the insured driver initiated a policy with Competitor A
- Log(SafeDrive_Score)	The natural logarithm of Advance Insurance's in-house prediction model to assess the safety of a driver",False,True
10834,evaluating-ai-decision-correctness,"# Overview

**Welcome to AI Decision Correctness: Business Scenarios** - a community-hosted machine learning challenge focused on decision quality, not just prediction.

This competition is designed to serve as a **benchmark-style evaluation** for multi-class decision correctness under uncertainty, emphasizing probability calibration and realistic business constraints.

In real organizations, the most important question is often: ¡°What should we do first?¡±
This competition simulates realistic incidents and business scenarios across domains like banking, SaaS, operations, security, and healthcare.

**Goal**

Predict the correct first_action (A/B/C/D) for each scenario in the test set using the provided tabular features.

**What you¡¯ll practice**

By participating, you¡¯ll gain hands-on experience with:

- Multi-class classification on structured, business-oriented data

- Probability-based evaluation using Multi-class Log Loss

- Feature engineering for decision-driven problems

- Handling uncertainty, trade-offs, and imbalanced outcomes

- Building interpretable and robust ML baselines

This challenge is well-suited for:

- Data Scientists

- ML Engineers

- Applied AI practitioners

- Anyone interested in decision-centric AI systems
----------------------
# Description

Modern AI systems are increasingly used not just to predict outcomes, but to support or automate decisions under uncertainty, risk, and operational constraints. In real-world business environments, the most critical question is often:

¡°What is the correct first action to take?¡±

This competition focuses on AI decision correctness ¡ª evaluating whether a model can identify the most appropriate initial action in complex, high-stakes scenarios where trade-offs exist between urgency, risk, cost, compliance, and uncertainty.
----------------------
# Problem Context

Participants are given a set of simulated but realistic business scenarios drawn from domains such as:

- Banking

- SaaS platforms

- Operations & infrastructure

- Security incidents

- Healthcare systems

Each scenario is described using structured, tabular features that represent:

- Severity and urgency

- Uncertainty in diagnosis

- Regulatory and compliance risk

- User and data impact

- Operational constraints (cost, recoverability, rollback feasibility)

- Stakeholder visibility

For every scenario, there is a single correct first action, determined using transparent, rule-based business logic that mirrors real decision frameworks used by organizations.
----------------------
# Objective

Your task is to predict the correct first_action for each scenario in the test set.

The target variable is:

`first_action ¡Ê {A, B, C, D}`


Each label represents a distinct decision strategy, such as:

- Immediate intervention or reset

- Further investigation or diagnostics

- Stakeholder notification

- Temporary mitigation or controlled response

The goal is not only to predict the correct class, but to produce well-calibrated probabilities that reflect decision confidence - a key requirement for real-world AI systems.

**Why This Challenge Is Different**

Unlike many traditional classification problems, this competition emphasizes:

- Decision quality, not just accuracy

- Probability calibration, not hard labels

- Constraint-aware reasoning, not purely statistical patterns

A confident but wrong decision can be more harmful than a cautious, well-calibrated one. The evaluation metric reflects this reality.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Metric**

- Submissions are evaluated using Multi-class Log Loss

- Lower scores are better

- Models are rewarded for assigning high probability to the correct action

- Over-confident incorrect predictions are penalized more heavily

- Log Loss rewards well-calibrated probabilities, not just correct labels, reflecting real production requirements

**Submission Format**

For each scenario_id in the test set, submit predicted probabilities for each action.

Your submission file must contain the following columns:

- scenario_id

- A, B, C, D (probabilities)

*Example:*

```python
scenario_id,A,B,C,D
165034,0.05,0.70,0.10,0.15
165035,0.25,0.25,0.25,0.25
165036,0.80,0.10,0.05,0.05
```

**Tips**

Each row should satisfy:

- Probabilities should be between 0 and 1

- Row values should sum close to 1.0

- Use validation carefully to avoid overfitting to the public leaderboard


----------------------
# Probability Calibration

In this competition, predicting the correct class is not enough.

Because submissions are evaluated using **Multi-class Log Loss**, models are rewarded for:
- Assigning higher probability to the correct action
- Avoiding overconfident wrong predictions

### Best Practices
- Use validation sets to evaluate calibration
- Avoid hard 0 or 1 probabilities
- Consider techniques such as:
- Temperature scaling
- Platt scaling
- Regularization and early stopping

In real decision systems, **confidence quality matters as much as accuracy**.

----------------------
# Learning-First Competition

This is a community-hosted, educational competition designed to promote practical machine learning skills and responsible AI decision-making.

- No monetary prizes

- Open participation

- Focus on learning, benchmarking, and discussion

We encourage participants to:

- Start here: Baseline - CatBoost (Multiclass Log Loss) (https://www.kaggle.com/code/sweetyseelam/baseline-catboost-multiclass-log-loss)

- Share insights and approaches

- Publish notebooks and baselines

- Discuss trade-offs and modeling decisions

Good luck, and welcome to AI Decision Correctness: Business Scenarios.
----------------------
# Related Benchmark

This competition has a companion Kaggle Benchmark: ¡°AI Decision Correctness ¨C First Action Selection¡±.

The benchmark provides a model-centric evaluation of the same decision problem, allowing direct comparison of LLMs and ML models on calibrated probability outputs.

Link: [https://www.kaggle.com/benchmarks/sweetyseelam/ai-decision-correctness-first-action-selection]","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains synthetic, realistic business scenarios where an AI system must choose the correct first action under operational constraints, ambiguity, and risk.

**Goal**

Build a model that predicts the correct first_action (A/B/C/D) for each scenario in the test set.

**Files**

train.csv ¡ª training data containing input features + target column first_action

test.csv ¡ª test data containing the same input features (no target)

sample_submission.csv ¡ª example submission format (probabilities for A/B/C/D)

metaData.csv ¡ª feature and label definitions

**Key Columns**

scenario_id: unique identifier for each scenario

domain: scenario category (banking/saas/ops/security/healthcare)

severity, time_pressure, uncertainty: core decision drivers (0¨C10)

recoverable, rollback_possible: binary constraints (0/1)

compliance_risk, user_impact, data_integrity_risk, stakeholder_visibility, cost_of_action: business risk indicators (0¨C10)

first_action (train only): correct first move (A/B/C/D)

**Actions (Target Meaning)**

A: Restart / hard reset first

B: Run diagnostics / investigation first

C: Notify stakeholders first

D: Apply temporary mitigation/patch first",False,True
10835,ai-grand-prix-task-2,"# Overview

*Each task represents a different real-world racing decision problem and is hosted as a separate Kaggle competition, all based on the same underlying dataset but with different prediction objectives.*
----------------------
# Description

### ***TASK 2:***
To prepare the car for the grid, you must first conduct a rigorous structural inspection of the dataset to ensure it can withstand the pressures of the race. Begin by checking the air pressure in the tires by identifying and resolving all missing values, either by refilling gaps with statistical averages or replacing incomplete records entirely. Next, strip away unnecessary weight by scanning for damaged or duplicate spare parts, ensuring that no redundant data rows compromise the car¡¯s efficiency. To complete the initial setup, upgrade the onboard electronics by verifying that all technical readings are correctly formatted, converting every relevant performance metric into a precise numeric value to ensure the telemetry systems are fully operational. Once the foundation is secure, focus on performance optimization to ensure the car handles perfectly at high speeds. Fine-tune the aerodynamics by detecting and mitigating extreme outliers that could destabilize the vehicle¡¯s performance, carefully smoothing out anomalies without discarding essential structural components that define the car¡¯s integrity. Be cautious not to over-clean, as maintaining the core features is vital for the car¡¯s overall strength. Finally, balance the engine power by normalizing the data, bringing all performance metrics into a stable, comparable range. With these calibrations complete, the dataset is refined, balanced, and ready to be submitted for final race approval.

You are a Formula-1 race strategist responsible for deciding the optimal pit stop strategy during a race. Using the provided dataset, select relevant features such as starting grid, qualifying position, driver experience or driver experience update, constructor rank, total laps, race time, laps completed, laps led, average speed, finish position, and championship to represent driver skill, car performance, and race dynamics. Additionally, add additional features that you think are relevant to predicting pit stops based on logical reasoning and domain understanding. Train a machine learning classification model to predict the pit stops, for example whether a driver follows a 1-stop, 2-stop, or 3-stop strategy. The model is evaluated on unseen race data using the F1 score, where a higher score indicates a more effective and competitive pit stop strategy.
----------------------
# Evaluation

For each id in the test set, you must predict the pit stop strategy class.

***Submissions are evaluated using the F1 score.***

The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, and is well suited for classification problems where class imbalance may exist. A higher F1 score indicates better performance.


## Submission File
The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    1,2
    2,1
    3,0
    etc.",,False,True
10836,ai-grand-prix-task-3,"# Overview

*Each task represents a different real-world racing decision problem and is hosted as a separate Kaggle competition, all based on the same underlying dataset but with different prediction objectives.*
----------------------
# Description

### ***TASK 3:***
To prepare the car for the grid, you must first conduct a rigorous structural inspection of the dataset to ensure it can withstand the pressures of the race. Begin by checking the air pressure in the tires by identifying and resolving all missing values, either by refilling gaps with statistical averages or replacing incomplete records entirely. Next, strip away unnecessary weight by scanning for damaged or duplicate spare parts, ensuring that no redundant data rows compromise the car¡¯s efficiency. To complete the initial setup, upgrade the onboard electronics by verifying that all technical readings are correctly formatted, converting every relevant performance metric into a precise numeric value to ensure the telemetry systems are fully operational. Once the foundation is secure, focus on performance optimization to ensure the car handles perfectly at high speeds. Fine-tune the aerodynamics by detecting and mitigating extreme outliers that could destabilize the vehicle¡¯s performance, carefully smoothing out anomalies without discarding essential structural components that define the car¡¯s integrity. Be cautious not to over-clean, as maintaining the core features is vital for the car¡¯s overall strength. Finally, balance the engine power by normalizing the data, bringing all performance metrics into a stable, comparable range. With these calibrations complete, the dataset is refined, balanced, and ready to be submitted for final race approval.

You are a Formula-1 race analyst responsible for identifying the championship contender and predicting the ultimate race winner. Using the provided dataset, select relevant features such as starting grid, qualifying position, driver experience update, constructor rank, total laps, race time, laps completed, laps led, average speed, finish position, and championship points to represent driver skill, car performance, and race dynamics. Additionally, add additional features that you think are relevant to predicting the race winner based on logical reasoning and domain understanding. Train a machine learning model to predict the race winner, for example whether a driver takes first place or not. The model is evaluated on unseen race data using the accuracy score, where a higher score indicates a more accurate and reliable system for spotting the driver who takes the checkered flag.
----------------------
# Evaluation

For each id in the test set, you must predict whether the driver wins the race or not.

***Submissions are evaluated using Accuracy.***

Accuracy measures the proportion of correct predictions out of the total number of predictions. It is appropriate for binary classification problems where correctly classifying both positive and negative cases is important. A higher accuracy score indicates better performance.

## Submission File

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    1,1
    2,0
    3,0
    etc.",,False,True
10837,ai-wweek,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2025 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** Your objective is to predict whether a person is an Introvert or Extrovert, given their social behavior and personality traits.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using [Accuracy Score](https://www.kaggle.com/code/metric/accuracy-score) between the predicted value and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `Personality`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Personality
    18524,Extrovert
    18525,Introvert
    18526,Introvert
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - ${competition.DateEnabled}
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  ${competition.Deadline}

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!",,False,True
10838,stock-pledge-financing-default-prediction-2026,"# Overview

Equity pledges by listed companies are a prevalent method of raising capital; however, they come with inherent risks that can lead to significant financial distress. In light of this challenge, our competition invites participants to develop predictive models for equity pledge financing defaults based on judicial freeze events involving controlling shareholders from 2017 to 2022.Participants will have the opportunity to explore and compare the effectiveness of different modeling approaches in predicting defaults.
----------------------
# Description

Equity pledging is a common financing method for listed companies in China. This approach was particularly active between 2015 and 2018 due to the clear ownership, high liquidity, and fair pricing of shares, making them appealing to financial institutions.

While equity pledging helps shareholders raise capital, it also comes with significant credit risks. A key concern is when controlling shareholders default on their pledges. These defaults can destabilize a company's equity structure, disrupt normal operations, and shake investor confidence. In serious cases, they can affect entire industries and harm the economy.

Controlling the risks of equity pledge financing is challenging, and resolving issues can take a long time. Therefore, proactive risk management is essential. This competition aims to develop effective models for predicting and mitigating these risks in equity pledge financing.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The AUC (Area Under the ROC Curve) is a performance metric used to evaluate the effectiveness of a classification model, particularly in scenarios with imbalanced datasets. It measures the model's ability to distinguish between positive and negative classes across all classification thresholds, providing a single score that reflects overall ranking quality regardless of class distribution.
----------------------
# Prizes

The competition period coincides with the Chinese Spring Festival, and it will conclude on the Lantern Festival (Yuanxiao Festival). We wish all participants a joyful holiday season!

Total Prizes Available: 3

1st Place ¨C 500 CNY (¡Ö70 USD)

2nd Place ¨C 300 CNY (¡Ö40 USD)

3rd Place ¨C 200 CNY (¡Ö30 USD)

Prizes will be paid in Chinese Yuan (CNY) via WeChat Pay or Alipay only.

Winners based in Mainland China must provide a valid WeChat or Alipay account to receive the prize.

Winners outside Mainland China must also register and verify a WeChat or Alipay account capable of receiving payments in CNY.

Unfortunately, we cannot guarantee payment through any other method (e.g., PayPal, bank transfer, etc.). By participating, winners agree to accept the prize via one of the supported platforms above.",,False,True
10839,uji-coba,"# Overview

Kota Adalah Pokoknya, sebagai salah satu kota yang terus berkembang dan berkomitmen pada peningkatan kesejahteraan masyarakat, secara rutin menyalurkan berbagai program bantuan sosial bagi rumah tangga berpenghasilan rendah. Program-program ini bertujuan untuk mengurangi ketimpangan sosial, meningkatkan kualitas hidup warga, serta memastikan pemenuhan kebutuhan dasar masyarakat yang paling rentan.

Namun, dalam praktiknya, penyaluran bantuan sosial masih menghadapi sejumlah tantangan, seperti keterbatasan anggaran, potensi ketidaktepatan sasaran, serta kurang optimalnya pemanfaatan data dalam proses penentuan penerima bantuan. Perbedaan kondisi sosial-ekonomi antarwilayah dan karakteristik rumah tangga yang beragam menuntut pendekatan yang lebih sistematis dan berbasis bukti.

Seiring dengan meningkatnya ketersediaan data kependudukan, sosial, dan ekonomi yang dikelola oleh pemerintah daerah, Kota Adalah Pokoknya melihat peluang untuk menerapkan pendekatan berbasis data dalam pengambilan keputusan. Dengan memanfaatkan data historis penerima bantuan serta karakteristik rumah tangga, pemerintah kota berupaya membangun model prediktif yang mampu mengidentifikasi rumah tangga yang paling layak menerima bantuan sosial secara objektif dan transparan.

Oleh karena itu, Pemerintah Kota Adalah Pokoknya mengundang para analis data untuk membangun model klasifikasi yang dapat memprediksi kelayakan rumah tangga sebagai penerima bantuan sosial berdasarkan data demografis, ekonomi, dan kondisi lingkungan yang tersedia.

Tujuan utama dari pemodelan ini adalah memberikan wawasan berbasis data bagi pemerintah kota dalam mengevaluasi dan menyempurnakan kebijakan bantuan sosial, meningkatkan ketepatan sasaran penyaluran bantuan, serta memastikan bahwa program bantuan benar-benar berkontribusi pada keadilan sosial dan peningkatan kesejahteraan masyarakat Kota Adalah Pokoknya.
----------------------
# Description

Metrik evaluasi dalam kompetisi ini adalah F1 Score, yaitu rata-rata harmonis antara precision dan recall:

F1 Score:
$$F1 = 2 ¡Á \frac {(Precision ¡Á Recall)}{(Precision + Recall)}$$
Dengan:
$$Precision = \frac{TP}{(TP + FP)},  Recall = \frac{TP}{(TP + FN)}$$
Keterangan:

TP: True Positives (Benar Positif)
FP: False Positives (Positif Palsu)
FN: False Negatives (Negatif Palsu)","# Dataset Description

# **Files**
- **train.csv** : Dataset yang digunakan untuk melatih model
- **test.csv** : Dataset yang diprediksi menggunakan model yang telah dilatih menggunakan dataset training
- **sample_submission.csv** : File contoh untuk mengilustrasikan format pengiriman prediksi.
- **metadata.csv** : Berisikan informasi dari peubah yang ada pada dataset

# **Variables**
