10842,motion-s-hierarchical-text-to-motion-generation-for-sign-language,"# Overview

Communication is a fundamental human right, but for over 430 million deaf individuals worldwide, the digital divide remains vast. Can your model help bridge it?
Before we can make AI truly accessible, we first need to teach machines to sign. You'll work with professionally captured sign language motion data to train a model that generates fluid, culturally authentic 3D gestures¡ªone of the most challenging and impactful problems in accessibility technology. This have been our mission at Signvrse where we envision communication with no barriers.

## Goal
Build a model that translates English/glossified text into realistic 3D sign language animations by generating hierarchical motion tokens that can be decoded into fluid avatar animations.

<div style=""text-align: center;"">
  <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11979020%2F724ae66b09afbe1c4424205b0f76f44e%2Fnew.gif?generation=1770628747750743&alt=media"" alt=""Sample GIF"">
</div>


----------------------
# Description

TL;DR: Generate 6 layers of motion tokens (base_tokens + residual_1-5) 
for 3,000 test samples. Each token ¡Ê [0-511]. Use provided VAE model.

### **Understanding the Problem**

Sign languages have fundamentally different grammatical structures than spoken languages. They require:
- **Precision and naturalness:** Movements must be precise yet natural¡ªhands and body working in concert
- **Smooth transitions:** Transitions between signs must flow smoothly while preserving meaning
- **Cultural authenticity:** Movements must respect the cultural nuances that make sign language come alive

### **Understanding Glossification**

Glossification is the process of converting natural language into sign language grammar. Sign languages don't follow spoken language word order¡ªthey have their own linguistic rules.

**Examples:**

| Natural English | Glossified (Sign Grammar) |
|----------------|---------------------------|
| ""I am going to the store tomorrow"" | ME GO STORE TOMORROW |
| ""The cat is sleeping on the bed"" | CAT SLEEP BED ON |
| ""What time is the meeting?"" | MEETING TIME WHAT |
| ""I don't understand"" | ME UNDERSTAND NOT |

## Dataset

**Training Data:** ~15,000 text-motion pairs from Kenyan Sign Language
- **Text Input**: Both natural English and glossified versions
- **Motion Output**: 6 layers of RVQ tokens (pre-tokenized using `rvq_vae_best.pth`)
- **Metadata**: Signer ID, sequence length, sign complexity

**Files:**
- `train.csv` - Training pairs with pre-computed tokens
- `test.csv` - Test prompts (id, sentence, gloss) - tokens withheld
- `sample_submission.csv` - Correct format example

### **Technical Framework (Fixed Ecosystem)**

To ensure consistency across submissions and maintain the high fidelity required for sign language, this competition utilizes a fixed motion-tokenization pipeline.



#### **1. Fixed Motion Backbone (RVQ-VAE)**

**Purpose:** Converts continuous motion into discrete tokens that can be generated by language models.

**Key Specifications:**
- **Pre-trained model:** `rvq_vae_best.pth` (provided - do not modify)
- **Input:** 30 FPS motion sequences
- **Output:** 6 layers of tokens, each token ¡Ê [0-511]
- **Compression:** 4¡Á temporal reduction (30 fps ¡ú 7.5 tokens/second)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11979020%2F365d4bec6d4c92c572e84a92a0b76a81%2FScreenshot%202026-02-04%20194922.png?generation=1770223783648624&alt=media)

**Critical:** You must use the provided VAE exactly as-is. Custom modifications will result in incompatible tokens and a score of 0.

**How it works:**
1. **Encoding:** Motion sequence ¡ú 6 token layers (hierarchical from coarse to fine)
2. **Decoding:** 6 token layers ¡ú Reconstructed motion sequence

Your task is to generate the 6 token layers given glossified text input.





----------------------
# Getting Started



**Your Task:** Generate 6 layers of motion tokens from glossified text input.

**Starter Code Provided:**
- `rvq_vae_best.pth` - Pre-trained motion tokenizer (DO NOT MODIFY)
- `length_estimator.pth` - Optional sequence length predictor
- `baseline_notebook.ipynb` - Simple baseline to get you started
- `evaluation_script.py` - Local evaluation before submission

**Recommended Approach:**
1. Start with the baseline notebook
2. Experiment with different text encoders (BERT, CLIP, T5, etc.)
3. Try autoregressive vs. non-autoregressive generation
4. Fine-tune on the provided training data
5. Validate locally before submitting
#### **2. Length Estimator (Text-to-Duration)**

**Purpose:** Predicts appropriate motion sequence length for given text.

**Specifications:**
- Architecture: CLIP Text Embedding (512-dim) ¡ú MLP ¡ú Length Bin
- Function: Predicts motion sequence length ranging from 40 to 800 tokens
- Input: Linguistic complexity of the input text
- Output: Number of tokens to generate

----------------------
# Submission Format

Your notebook must output a `submission.csv` file with the following structure. To ensure compatibility with the evaluation script, token indices within each cell must be **space-separated**.

**submission.csv Format:**

```csv
id,base_tokens,residual_1,residual_2,residual_3,residual_4,residual_5
0,""45 127 88 234"",""201 88 456 12"",""88 234 12 456"",""456 78 90 12"",""12 345 67 89"",""89 12 345 67""
1,""23 456 78 90"",""89 12 34 56"",""56 78 90 12"",""12 34 56 78"",""78 90 12 34"",""34 56 78 90""

```

**Requirements:**

* **6 Columns:** You must provide all 6 RVQ layers.
* **Space-Separated:** Each cell must contain token indices separated by a single **space** (do not use commas).
* **Token Values:** Every token must be an integer .
* **Sequence Length:** Each sequence must be between **40 and 800 tokens** long.
* **Layer Consistency:** All 6 layers must have the **exact same sequence length** for any given row.

### Submission Workflow

1. Load the test data from test.csv
2. Generate motion token predictions for all test samples
3. Save predictions to `submission.csv` in the correct format
4. Ensure the notebook completes within the 9-hour time limit
----------------------
# Evaluation



Submissions are evaluated using a weighted composite of three metrics, calculated in a learned feature space to ensure semantic meaningfulness.

### **Scoring Formula**
*All metrics are normalized to [0,1] range before weighting. **Higher Final Scores are better**.*

```markdown
Score = 0.30 ¡Á FID_norm + 0.50 ¡Á R_Precision_norm + 0.20 ¡Á Diversity_norm
```

### **Metric Breakdown**

| Metric | Weight | Direction | Description |
|--------|--------|-----------|-------------|
| **R-Precision** | 50% | ¡ü Higher | Measures text-motion alignment: given a generated motion and 32 text descriptions (1 correct + 31 random), can we retrieve the correct description in the top 3 matches? |
| **FID** | 30% | ¡ý Lower | Measures physical realism by comparing the distribution of generated motions to real KSL motions. |
| **Diversity** | 20% | ¡ü Higher | Ensures the model generates a varied range of signs and avoids ""mode collapse."" |


----------------------
# Code Submission Requirements


This is a **Code Competition**. Submissions must be made through Kaggle Notebooks.

### Runtime Requirements

- **Time Limit**: 9 hours total to process all 3,000 test samples
- **Hardware**: Kaggle GPU (T4 or P100)
- **Internet Access**: Disabled during inference
- **Memory**: Standard Kaggle notebook memory limits apply

### External Resources Allowed

- Pre-trained language models (BERT, GPT, CLIP, T5, etc.)
- Publicly available sign language datasets (with proper citation)
- General motion/gesture datasets
- Standard ML libraries (PyTorch, TensorFlow, Hugging Face, etc.)

### External Resources NOT Allowed

- Private/proprietary sign language data
- Data scraped without consent
- Modified versions of `rvq_vae_best.pth`
- Any custom motion tokenizers or VAE models

### Validation Rules

**CRITICAL**: All submissions must use the codebook indices from the provided `rvq_vae_best.pth` model. Custom codebooks or modified tokenizers will fail to decode properly and will receive a score of 0.

**Your submission will be rejected if:**
- Missing any of the 6 required token columns
- Token values outside the range [0, 511]
- Sequence lengths outside the range [40, 800]
- Different sequence lengths across the 6 layers for the same sample ID
- Incorrect number of rows (must equal test set size)
- Non-integer token values or incorrect formatting


----------------------
# Privacy & Ethics



* **Informed Consent:** All motion data was captured with the explicit informed consent of signers from the KSL community.
* **De-identification:** All personally identifiable information has been removed. Data consists strictly of 3D joint coordinates and expression parameters.
* **Community Partnership:** This project is a collaboration with the **Kenya National Association of the Deaf (KNAD)** and the **Africa Institute for Deaf Studies and Research**. 
----------------------
# Sponsors & Partners

Supported by Google.org as a primary sponsor and partner

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11979020%2F82c7cbedeef54175b180c6b350f9e848%2FScreenshot%202026-02-09%20120211.png?generation=1770714323568464&alt=media)","# Dataset Description

![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-CC%20BY--NC%204.0-blue)
![Version](https://img.shields.io/badge/version-1.0-green)
![Format](https://img.shields.io/badge/format-BVH%20%2B%20NPY-orange)

## Overview
**Motion-S** is a comprehensive sign language motion dataset designed for text-to-motion generation research. The dataset contains high-quality 3D motion capture data paired with English sentences and their corresponding sign language glosses, enabling the development of systems that can automatically generate sign language animations from text input.

<div>
  <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11979020%2Fdb8bcd9123abffe7a7e64a0799ca3669%2FScreenshot%202026-01-23%20123837.png?generation=1769593603263056&alt=media"">
</div>

## Dataset Statistics
| Metric | Value |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Total Samples** | 12,468 |
| **Total Frames** | 5,372,828 |
| **Average Motion Duration** | 431.1 frames |
| **Max Motion Duration** | 12,261 frames |
| **Vocabulary Size (Gloss)** | 4,212 unique glosses |
| **Motion Feature Dimension** | 668 |

## Data Collection Pipeline

### Motion Capture Technology
The motion data was captured using **Meshcapade's MoCapade** technology, a state-of-the-art markerless motion capture system. MoCapade enables the capture of 3D human motion from any video source without requiring specialized suits or markers, allowing for natural and authentic sign language gesture recording.

**Key features of MoCapade:**
- Markerless motion capture from standard video
- High-fidelity 3D body, hand, and facial motion extraction
- Natural movement preservation without motion artifacts from markers

### SMPL-X Body Model
The captured motions were processed using the **SMPL-X** (SMPL eXpressive) body model, also developed by Meshcapade. SMPL-X is an advanced parametric 3D human body model that provides:
- **Full body articulation**: 22 body joints
- **Detailed hand modeling**: 15 joints per hand (30 total)
- **Facial expressions**: 10 expression parameters
- **Body shape**: 10 shape parameters

SMPL-X is particularly well-suited for sign language as it captures the nuanced hand movements and facial expressions essential for accurate sign representation.

### BVH Conversion
The SMPL-X motion data was converted to the **BVH (Biovision Hierarchy)** format for broad compatibility with animation tools and research frameworks. Each BVH file contains:
- Hierarchical skeletal structure
- Per-frame joint rotation data (168 channels)
- Root translation and orientation

## Dataset Structure
```
©À©¤©¤ Motion-Features/         # Likely for pre-extracted features or submission prep
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ {sample_id}/
©¦       ©À©¤©¤ metadata.txt     # SENTENCE and GLOSS annotations
©¦       ©À©¤©¤ {sample_id}.bvh  # Raw motion sequence
©¦       ©¸©¤©¤ {sample_id}.npy  # Processed feature array
©¦
©À©¤©¤ Train/                   # Primary training data (12,468 samples)
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ {sample_id}/
©¦       ©À©¤©¤ metadata.txt     # Textual ground truth
©¦       ©À©¤©¤ {sample_id}.bvh  # Skeleton hierarchy and motion data
©¦       ©¸©¤©¤ ...
©¦
©À©¤©¤ sample_submission.csv
©À©¤©¤ test.csv
©¸©¤©¤ train.csv
```

### File Counts
- **Total files**: 37,400+
- **BVH files**: 12,500 (motion sequences)
- **NPY files**: 12,500 (pre-extracted motion features)
- **Metadata files**: 12,500 (text annotations)
- **CSV files**: 3 (train, test, sample_submission)

### File Descriptions
| File | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `train.csv` | Training set with columns: `id`, `sentence`, `gloss`, `layered motion tokens` |
| `test.csv` | Test set with columns: `id`, `sentence`, `gloss` (no motion tokens) |
| `sample_submission.csv` | Example submission with 5 samples in tokenized format |
| `metadata.txt` | Contains the English sentence and corresponding gloss annotation |
| `{sample_id}.bvh` | Complete motion sequence for the full sentence in BVH format |
| `{sample_id}.npy` | Pre-extracted motion features (numpy array: frames ¡Á 668 dimensions) |
| `{gloss}.bvh` | Individual sign motion for each gloss in the sentence |

### Metadata Format (`Motion-Features/Train/{sample_id}/metadata.txt`)
Each sample folder contains a `metadata.txt` file with the following format:
```text
SENTENCE: {The spoken language sentence}
GLOSS: {The sign language gloss annotation}
```



## Motion Feature Format

### NPY Files
Each `.npy` file contains a numpy array of shape `(T, 668)` where:
- `T` = number of frames in the motion sequence
- `668` = motion feature dimension per frame

The 668-dimensional feature vector represents the complete body state including joint positions, rotations, and SMPL-X parameters.

### BVH Files
BVH files follow the standard Biovision Hierarchy format with:
- **Skeleton hierarchy**: 55 joints (body + hands + face)
- **Channels per joint**: 3 rotation channels (XYZ Euler angles)
- **Root joint**: 6 channels (3 position + 3 rotation)
- **Frame time**: 0.0333 seconds (30 FPS)

## Usage

For a detailed walkthrough on how to load, visualize, and preprocess the motion data, please refer to the official exploration notebook:

**[Motion-S Dataset Exploration Notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/antonygithinji/motion-s-dataset-exploration-notebookc29543514f)**

This notebook covers:

* **Data Loading:** Efficiently accessing `.bvh` and `.npy` files from the hierarchical structure.
* **Metadata Parsing:** Extracting `GLOSS` and `SENTENCE` tokens from `metadata.txt`.
* **Visualization:** Rendering the motion sequences to understand the 668 feature dimensions.",False,True
10843,data-seeker-selection-copy,"# Overview

In many real-world production environments, **product quality** is driven by a complex interaction of chemical, physical, and process variables. Breweries are no exception: subtle changes in acidity, sugar content, alcohol level, or fermentation conditions can dramatically alter the final sensory quality of a beer.  

This competition uses a **real laboratory dataset** of physicochemical measurements that has been adapted into a craft brewery setting. The original measurements come from actual fermentation samples; they have been combined, relabeled, and lightly transformed to represent batches of beer evaluated by a quality panel. Each row corresponds to one production batch and includes numeric features such as acidity, residual sugar, sulphates, alcohol content, and a proxy for fermentation temperature.  

Your task is to use these real measurement profiles to predict how an expert tasting panel would score each batch on a **1¨C5 quality scale**, where 1 represents poor quality and 5 represents excellent quality.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Primary metric: Macro F1?Score**

Submissions are evaluated using **Macro F1?Score**. This metric is the harmonic mean of Precision and Recall, computed independently for each class and then averaged with equal weight, regardless of how many samples each class has.

For a given class \(k\):

- Precision\(_k\) is:

$$
\text{Precision}_k = \frac{TP_k}{TP_k + FP_k}
$$

- Recall\(_k\) is:

$$
\text{Recall}_k = \frac{TP_k}{TP_k + FN_k}
$$

The F1?Score for class \(k\) is defined as:

$$
F1_k = \frac{2 \cdot \text{Precision}_k \cdot \text{Recall}_k}{\text{Precision}_k + \text{Recall}_k}
$$

If there are \(K\) classes (here \(K = 6\)), the **Macro F1?Score** is:

$$
F1_{\text{macro}} = \frac{1}{K} \sum_{k=1}^{K} F1_k
$$

In Python / scikit?learn, this is computed as:

```python
from sklearn.metrics import f1_score
import numpy as np

def score(y_true: np.ndarray, y_pred: np.ndarray) -> float:
    """"""
    Calculate the Macro F1-Score.

    Parameters:
    y_true (array-like): True class labels.
    y_pred (array-like): Predicted class labels.

    Returns:
    float: The calculated macro F1 score.
    """"""
    return f1_score(y_true, y_pred, average=""macro"")
```

**Why Macro F1?Score?**

Macro F1?Score balances Precision and Recall across all classes, treating each class as equally important. Since the target labels in this competition are imbalanced, accuracy alone could be misleading. Macro F1?Score encourages models that perform well on both majority and minority quality categories.","# Dataset Description

## Craft Brewery Panel Scoring Dataset

This dataset contains physicochemical measurements from a simulated craft brewery quality control program.  
Each row represents a beer batch evaluated by an expert sensory panel on a 0¨C5 quality scale.  
The data is intentionally **noisy, imbalanced, and partially missing** to mimic real-world production data.

***

### Files

- **train.csv** ¨C training set with features and target `panel_score`  
- **test.csv** ¨C test set with the same features (no `panel_score`)  
- **sample_submission.csv** ¨C example submission file (`id`, `panel_score`)  

***

### Columns

All files share the same feature columns.  
Only `train.csv` contains the target `panel_score`.

**Identifiers**

- `id` ¨C unique batch ID (e.g., `BR0001`, `BR0002`, ¡­)

**Physicochemical features**

These are continuous numeric features derived from lab measurements:

- `base_malt_acidity` ¨C fixed acidity from malt (g/L)  
- `fermentation_volatiles` ¨C volatile acidity from fermentation by?products (g/L)  
- `citric_additive` ¨C citric acid additive level (g/L)  
- `post_ferment_sugar` ¨C residual sugar after fermentation (g/L)  
- `salt_content` ¨C salt (chloride) concentration (g/L)  
- `free_so2_protectant` ¨C free SO? used as antioxidant (mg/L)  
- `total_so2_stabilizer` ¨C total SO? used as preservative (mg/L)  
- `final_gravity` ¨C final density of the beer (g/cm3)  
- `final_ph` ¨C final pH of the beer  
- `sulfate_ion_level` ¨C sulfate ion concentration (g/L)  
- `abv_alcohol_content` ¨C alcohol by volume (% ABV)  
- `brew_temp_c` ¨C fermentation temperature in ¡ãC  


**Target**

- `panel_score` ¨C expert panel quality score in the range **0¨C5** (integer, ordinal)   
  - 1: poor  
  - 2: below average  
  - 3: average  
  - 4: good  
  - 5: excellent",False,True
10844,sanfrancisco-aiorg,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2025 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** Your objective is to predict whether a person is an Introvert or Extrovert, given their social behavior and personality traits.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using [Accuracy Score](https://www.kaggle.com/code/metric/accuracy-score) between the predicted value and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `Personality`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Personality
    18524,Extrovert
    18525,Introvert
    18526,Introvert
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - ${competition.DateEnabled}
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  ${competition.Deadline}

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!",,False,True
10845,march-machine-learning-mania-2026,"# Overview

You will be forecasting the outcomes of both the men's and women's 2026 collegiate basketball tournaments, by submitting predictions for every possible tournament matchup.
----------------------
# Description

Another year, another chance to predict the upsets, call the probabilities, and put your bracketology skills to the leaderboard test. In our *twelfth* annual March Machine Learning Mania competition, Kagglers will once again join the millions of fans who attempt to predict the outcomes of this year's college basketball tournaments. Unlike most fans, you will pick the winners and losers using a combination of rich historical data and computing power, while the ground truth unfolds on television.

You are provided data of historical NCAA games to forecast the outcomes of the Division 1 Men's and Women's basketball tournaments. This competition is the official 2026 edition, with points, medals, prizes, and basketball glory at stake.

We are continuing the format [from last year](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/march-machine-learning-mania-2025) where you are making predictions about every possible matchup in the tournament, evaluated using the Brier score. See the [Evaluation Page](overview/evaluation) for full details.

Prior to the start of the tournaments, the leaderboard of this competition will reflect scores from 2021-2025 only. Kaggle will periodically fill in the outcomes and rescore once the 2026 games begin.

Good luck and happy forecasting!
![basketball graph](https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/kaggle/4862/media/bball-logo.png)

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on the [Brier score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brier_score) between the predicted probabilities and the actual game outcomes (this is equivalent to mean squared error in this context). 
### Submission File

As a reminder, the submission file format also has a revised format from prior iterations:

1. **We have combined the Men's and Women's tournaments into one single competition.** Your submission file should contain predictions for both.
2. **You will be predicting the hypothetical results for every possible team matchup, not just teams that are selected for the NCAA tournament.** This change was enacted to provide a longer time window to submit predictions for the 2026 tournament. Previously, the short time between Selection Sunday and the tournament tipoffs would require participants to quickly turn around updated predictions. By forecasting every possible outcome between every team, you can now submit a valid prediction at any point leading up to the tournaments.
3. You may submit as many times as you wish before the tournaments start, but **make sure to select the two submissions you want to count towards scoring**. Do not rely on automatic selection to pick your submissions.

As with prior years, each game has a unique `ID` created by concatenating the season in which the game was played and the two team's respective `TeamId`s. For example, ""2026\_1101\_1102"" indicates a hypothetical matchup between team 1101 and 1102 in the year 2026. You must predict the probability that the team with the lower `TeamId` beats the team with the higher `TeamId`. Note that the men's teams and women's `TeamId`s do not overlap.

The resulting submission format looks like the following, where `Pred` represents the predicted probability that the first team will win:

    ID,Pred
    2026_1101_1102,0.5
    2026_1101_1103,0.5
