10858,e-commerce-retention-roi-challenge,"# Overview

The global e-commerce sector loses over $100 billion annually to customer churn. While loyal customers generate 80% of revenue, at-risk customers often defect silently, reducing lifetime value by nearly 70%. Traditional churn prediction models generally optimize for generic accuracy or ROC-AUC. However, this competition focuses directly on the economic impact of probabilistic decisions, recognizing that every prediction carries a highly specific financial consequence.


----------------------
# Description

Businesses do not operate on generic metrics; they operate on **profit and loss**. This competition forces a shift in perspective by focusing directly on the *economic impact* of probabilistic decisions, recognizing that every prediction carries a highly specific financial consequence.

### **The Asymmetric Cost of Churn**

In this business context, all mistakes are not created equal.

* **Successfully saving a churner** via targeted intervention (a True Positive) yields a net gain of **¡ê122.50**.
* **Wasting campaign spend** on an already loyal customer (a False Positive) results in a **¡ê15.00** loss.
* **Completely missing a churner** (a False Negative) inflicts a massive **¡ê350.00** blow due to lost Customer Lifetime Value (CLV).

### **Your Mission**

Your objective is to move beyond standard binary classification and build a **profit-optimized churn probability model**.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation framework has been updated to prioritize calibrated probabilities and economic weighting over simple threshold-based classification.

Primary Metric: Profit-Weighted Log Loss (PWLL) ¡ª 80% Weight
Instead of thresholding, we evaluate the full probability distribution using a cost-sensitive log loss.
Mathematical Definition:
Given $y_i \in \{0,1\}$ as the true label and $p_i$ as the predicted probability:
Weight for Churn ($w_1$): 350
Weight for Active ($w_0$): 15

$$PWLL = -\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{n} [w_1 y_i \log(p_i) + w_0 (1 - y_i) \log(1 - p_i)]$$
Normalized Leaderboard Score:
To ensure intuitive tracking, the score is normalized against a baseline:

$$Score_{PWLL} = 1 - \frac{PWLL}{PWLL_{baseline}}$$

Secondary Metrics ¡ª 20% Weight
ROC-AUC (10%): Measures ranking quality. (Minimum threshold: 0.65).
Brier Score (10%): Measures probability calibration.
$$Brier = \frac{1}{N} \sum (p_i - y_i)^2$$
$$Score_{Brier} = 1 - Brier$$

Final Scoring Logic
The final leaderboard position is determined by the weighted sum of all metrics:

$$FinalScore = (0.80 \times Score_{PWLL}) + (0.10 \times ROC\_AUC) + (0.10 \times Score_{Brier})$$","# Dataset Description

The competition dataset is derived from **541,909 real-world transaction records** belonging to a UK-based, non-store online retail company, capturing customer activity between **01/12/2010 and 09/12/2011**.

Unlike sanitized academic datasets, this data intentionally preserves production-level noise. Your feature engineering pipeline will be just as critical as your model architecture.

### ?? **Files Provided**

* **`training_classification.csv`**: Your primary workspace. Contains the full transaction history for the training cohort, alongside the `HighChurnRisk` target labels. Use this for exploratory data analysis (EDA), feature engineering, and model validation.
* **`testing_classification.csv`**: The out-of-sample transaction history. The target labels are withheld and used internally to calculate your leaderboard score. You must generate churn probability predictions for every customer in this file.
* **`sample_submission_classification.csv`**: A blueprint of the required submission format. Your final submission must match this exact structure, including row count and customer order.

---

### ?? **Target Variable Definition**

Your models must predict the **`HighChurnRisk`** variable (available only in `train.csv`). It is framed as a binary classification problem based on a strict 90-day cutoff:

* **1 (Churned):** The customer made *zero* purchases in the final 90 days of the dataset period.
* **0 (Active):** The customer made at least one purchase during the final 90 days.

---

### ?? **The Engineering Challenge: Data Quality Issues**

To succeed in this competition, you cannot simply feed raw data into an algorithm. You must design robust preprocessing strategies to handle the following intentional data anomalies:

* **Transaction Anomalies:** You will encounter cancelled orders (indicated by an `InvoiceNo` beginning with the letter 'C') as well as negative transaction quantities representing returns and refunds.
* **Structural Noise:** The dataset contains duplicate invoices, inconsistent country formatting, and malformed free-text product descriptions that will require careful parsing.
* **Missingness:** Approximately **25% of the transaction records are missing a `CustomerID**`. How you choose to impute, group, or discard these orphaned transactions will be a key differentiator on the leaderboard.

---",False,True
10859,tams-admissions-plan,"# Overview

The Texas Asian Mathematics School (TAMS) is an early college program located at the Northern University of Technology (NUT). NUT isn't some second-rate university - it is recognized by the Carnegie Classification as a Tier 3 research institution and is known for its outstanding college food and world-class faculty. Each year, millions of students from across the state of Texas apply for admission to TAMS, with only about two hundred students being selected to enroll at this prestigious school. They are among the best and brightest minds in the world, and at NUT, they will go on to change the world! Students that are accepted rarely even apply to other institutions after their two years at the school because of the exciting TAMS-to-Hawks scholarship, which provides a staggering $20,000 scholarship to TAMS students that decide to continue at NUT, along with automatic admission to the Honors College.

Your job in this Kaggle competition is to help Dr. Hoover, the Associate Dean of Admissions for TAMS, calculate the admit chance for the applications he's received so far. Not only will it help him provide better admissions decisions, but it will also free up more time for him to help the TAMS population through his awe-inspiring seminars!

Sincerely,
the TAMS Artificial Intelligence Society

This description is purely fictional. Any resemblance to the real world, whether past, present, or future is purely coincidental.
----------------------
# Description

Every year, millions of students apply to join TAMS, and it is time-consuming and laborious to carefully review each student's situation. Dr. Hoover wants to use machine learning technology to save review time.
Dr. Hoover provided the competition team with score data for some students from previous years, and your model will be used to predict the scores of students this year.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Dr. Hoover doesn't care about the specific scores of students, he only cares about whether you can give good students a high score. Simply put, he cares about the ranking of students, so that good students can be screened out. So the evaluation metric chosen here is `Spearman correlation coefficient`.","# Dataset Description

The relevant introduction of this competition is completely fictional, and the original data of the competition is the training set of <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/tams-ais-winter-2022-competition/overview"">this competition</a>.

The training and testing data for the competition are synthesized through neural networks.

<b>Hint</b>

`Serial No.` is a feature rather than an index.

This competition needs to take into account the small number of test sets and this special evaluation metric, and be careful to avoid overfitting.",False,True
