10866,critical-object-grounding,"# Overview

Task. Critical Object Grounding: Given an accident video clip, our goal is to output bounding boxes associated with human driver gaze fixations, which are considered critical objects. Specifically, we encourage participants to use the DADA-2000 dataset (Train) as a benchmark and incorporate multimodal large models£¨MLLM£©for network modeling.
----------------------
# Description

To allow the research community to investigate safety-critical driving scene understanding for autonomous driving thoroughly, this workshop introduces the DADA-2000 dataset, a novel human-gaze-aligned egocentric accident video benchmark collected in driving scenes. DADA-2000 is a more diverse and larger video benchmark dataset annotated with the driver fixations for each video frame. Each gaze map is obtained by accumulating five subjects's fixation points and utilizing the Gaussian filter with (50¡Á50) pixel kernel.

DADA-2000 collects over 658, 476 frames of human driver gaze fixations for 2000 video clips with the resolution of 1584¡Á 660 on 54 kinds of accident scenarios. These clips are crowd-sourced and captured in various occasions (highway, urban, rural, and tunnel), weather (sunny, rainy and snowy) and light conditions (daytime and nighttime). Leveraging the features of the MM-AU dataset, each video is temporally aligned with the text descriptions of accident reasons, prevention solutions, accident categories, and object boxes manually annotated for video frames.

We propose to organize the Critical Object Grounding Task, supported by the DADA-2000 (train) and MM-AU (Partial Test) datasets.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The performance will be calculated at the image level. For the Critical Object Detection task, the performance is measured by Mean Average IoU (mIoU). It is computed by the Intersection over Union (IoU) detection threshold to 0.1, 0.3, and 0.5 (indicating that the overlap between predicted and the critical object ground truth (GT) bounding box observed by the human driver gaze exceeds 10%, 30%, and 50%, respectively). Because of the challenging nature of the data, the final performance will be determined by the equally weighted average of the performances at the three thresholds. The evaluation will focus on the video level only, to stress our focus on understanding the safety-critical driving scenes within dynamic evolution.","# Dataset Description

# DADA-2000 Dataset Guide (Training)

This document describes the directory structure and data formats of the **DADA-2000** dataset used in this competition.

---

## Training Split

[DADA-2000-Train (Google Drive)](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1l1_xOMWfs2eSoh0771ZJOcS2tcKwhh-C)
[DADA-2000-Train (Baidu Netdisk)](https://pan.baidu.com/share/init?surl=oxoQKYIaNCkLCxVCrOwgHw&pwd=ahyz)
[DADA-2000-Detection (Google Drive)](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rEDR3JHgiWt0oz_a7MNhOQsTRv1mzuxZ/view?usp=sharing)
More details about DADA-2000 can be found on the dataset [homepage/documentation](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.12634).

The training set includes:

- **Visual inputs**: RGB frames, driver gaze maps, and driver fixation points  
- **Bounding box annotations**: frame-level detections for common road participants  
- **Scenario metadata**: structured labels describing accident context and driving scene information

---

## 1) Multi-modal Data

Each accident video contains multi-modal data under the following layout:

```
DADA/
©¸©¤©¤ 1/
    ©¸©¤©¤ 001/
        ©À©¤©¤ images/     # RGB video frames
        ©À©¤©¤ maps/       # driver gaze maps
        ©¸©¤©¤ fixation/   # driver fixation points
©¸©¤©¤ ...
```

---

## 2) Frame-level Bounding Box Annotations

Frame-wise bounding box annotations are provided as text files:

```
DADA_Detection/
©¸©¤©¤ 2/
    ©¸©¤©¤ 002/
        ©À©¤©¤ 0001.txt
        ©À©¤©¤ 0002.txt
        ©¸©¤©¤ ...
©¸©¤©¤ ...
```

We generate these bounding boxes using a **YOLOX detector pre-trained on COCO**, run on the original DADA videos.

Each `.txt` file contains bounding boxes for **7 categories of road participants**:

- car
- traffic light
- pedestrian
- truck
- bus
- bicycle
- motorcycle

> Note: Some critical objects (e.g., certain obstacles) may still be missing from the detections.

---

## 3) Metadata Annotation (Excel)

Each training video is accompanied by a structured metadata file in **Excel** format, which describes driving scene attributes and accident-related information.

Below is a JSON-style interpretation of the fields (per video entry):

```json
{
  ""video_hashcode"": {
    ""video_name"": ""1_1"",
    ""id"": ""1"",
    ""type"": ""1"",
    ""weather"": ""1"",
    ""light"": ""1"",
    ""scenes"": ""4"",
    ""linear"": ""1"",
    ""accident occurred"": ""1"",
    ""abnormal_start_frame"": ""30"",
    ""abnormal_end_frame"": ""115"",
    ""accident_frame"": ""63"",
    ""total_frames"": ""440"",
    ""t_ai"": ""30"",
    ""t_co"": ""63"",
    ""t_ae"": ""115"",
    ""texts"": ""a pedestrian crosses the road"",
    ""causes"": ""Pedestrian does not notice the coming vehicles when crossing the street"",
    ""measures"": ""When passing the zebra crossing, drivers must slow down. When pedestrians or non-motor vehicles cross the zebra crossing, they should stop and give way to other normal running vehicles; When crossing the road, pedestrians must follow the zebra crossing, carefully observe the traffic, and do not cross the road in a hurry.""
  }
}
```

### Field Definitions

| Field Name | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `video_hashcode` | Unique identifier for each video entry |
| `video_name` | Video name composed of accident type and index (e.g., `type_id`) |
| `id` | Video index |
| `type` | Accident type (see the accident-type list provided with the dataset) |
| `weather` | Weather condition: `1=Sunny`, `2=Rainy`, `3=Snowy`, `4=Foggy` |
| `light` | Lighting condition: `1=Daytime`, `2=Nighttime` |
| `scenes` | Driving scene: `1=Highway`, `2=Tunnel`, `3=Mountain road`, `4=Urban`, `5=Rural` |
| `linear` | Road topology: `1=Straight`, `2=Curve`, `3=Cross intersection`, `4=T-intersection`, `5=Ramp` |
| `accident occurred` | Whether an accident occurs in the video (`1=Yes`, `0=No`) |
| `abnormal_start_frame` | Start frame index of the abnormal event |
| `abnormal_end_frame` | End frame index of the abnormal event |
| `accident_frame` | Frame index where the collision happens |
| `total_frames` | Total number of frames in the video |
| `t_ai` | Accident interval start frame (same as `abnormal_start_frame`) |
| `t_co` | Collision frame (same as `accident_frame`) |
| `t_ae` | Accident interval end frame (same as `abnormal_end_frame`) |
| `texts` | Natural-language description of the scene |
| `causes` | Description of the accident cause |
| `measures` | Preventive measures to avoid similar accidents |

---

## Test Split

In the test phase, **only RGB frames are provided**.  
No gaze maps, fixation points, or annotation files are included.

**MM-AU-Test**: 
[Google Drive](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CNYVFiDVLZuocp1d4vRG5zidzGPuC7SD/view?usp=sharing)
```
Test_Video/
©¸©¤©¤ 1/
    ©¸©¤©¤ 001537/
        ©À©¤©¤ images/     # RGB video frames
    ©¸©¤©¤ ...
©¸©¤©¤ ...
```

---

## Submission Format

Please download and follow **`sample_submission.csv`**. Make sure the `id` field matches correctly.

### Bounding Box Format

Each predicted box should be:

```text
[x1, y1, x2, y2]   # top-left and bottom-right coordinates (x_min, y_min, x_max, y_max)
```

### Important Constraint (One Box Per Frame)

?? **Each frame must contain exactly ONE key-object bounding box.**  
Submissions that output **multiple boxes per frame** will be treated as **invalid**.

---",False,True
