10875,robo-vision-challenge-data-hack-3-0,"# Overview

## ?? Why This Matters  

Every day, millions of hours are wasted on repetitive manual tasks in warehouses, factories, hospitals, and even homes.  
**Robotic manipulation** -the ability for machines to see, understand, and physically interact with the world -
is one of the most powerful technologies shaping the next industrial revolution.  

However, there is still a major challenge: **robots do not perceive the world the way humans do.**  

A human can look at a cluttered desk and immediately know how to organize it.  
A robot, on the other hand, sees only raw pixels.  
Closing this gap - enabling machines to predict the right actions directly from visual input -
is one of the most important frontiers in AI and robotics.  

---

### ?? Real-World Impact  

| Industry | Application |
|----------|-------------|
| ?? **Manufacturing** | Automated assembly, sorting, packaging |
| ?? **Healthcare** | Surgical assistance, medication sorting, lab automation |
| ?? **Home Robotics** | Elderly care, household chores, accessibility |
| ?? **Logistics** | Warehouse picking, order fulfillment, last-mile delivery |
| ?? **Sustainability** | Waste sorting, recycling automation |
| ?? **Agriculture** | Crop harvesting, fruit picking, precision farming |


**Your solution today could help build the robots of tomorrow.**

---

## ?? Challenge Overview

In this competition, you will develop computer vision models to predict the full robotic manipulation sequences from **raw perspective camera images**. Given an image of the initial scene configuration, your model must determine the optimal sequence of **pick-and-place coordinates** in world space to accomplish the manipulation task.
This challenge simulates real-world scenarios where robots need to understand 3D spatial relationships from 2D camera observations and plan manipulation actions based on visual input.

You receive realistic perspective camera images showing the workspace from an angled viewpoint. You must figure out how to map image observations to world coordinates yourself - this is a core part of the challenge!

This is a **computer vision** challenge that tests your ability to:
- Detect and localize objects in cluttered scenes
- Understand spatial relationships between objects
- Predict multi-step action sequences from static images

----------------------
# Description

# ?? Task 1: Diagonal Color Intensity Sorting Task (50% of Dataset)

## 1. Task Overview

This task requires organizing colored bowls based on their **brightness (luminance)**  
and then matching the corresponding blocks to them.

The solution follows a structured two-phase process:

- **Phase 1:** Arrange bowls on the main diagonal according to intensity  
- **Phase 2:** Match each block to its corresponding bowl  

---

## 2. Color Configuration

### 2.1 Available Colors (11 Total)

`red, blue, green, orange, yellow, purple, pink, cyan, brown, gray, navy`

---

## 3. Intensity Hierarchy (Dark ¡ú Bright)

Colors are ranked by brightness as follows:

- **Darkest:** navy, brown  
- **Medium:** blue, green, red, purple, cyan  
- **Brightest:** orange, yellow, pink  

This ranking defines the required diagonal placement.

**Reference file:**  
`dataset/color_intensity_reference.csv`

---

## 4. Diagonal Placement Rule

Bowls must be positioned on the **main diagonal** according to their brightness.

### 4.1 Positional Mapping

| Diagonal Position | Required Intensity |
|------------------|-------------------|
| Top-Left         | Darkest           |
| Center           | Medium            |
| Bottom-Right     | Brightest         |

### 4.2 Constraints

- The diagonal must strictly follow the defined intensity order  
- Any incorrect ordering invalidates the configuration  

---

## 5. Visual Transition Example

| INITIAL STATE |  | GOAL STATE (Diagonal Formation) |
|--------------|--|------------|
| ![initial](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F9509650%2Fda4073c57ced7df2eaaf00e4215af6a9%2Fexample_03_initial.png?generation=1770463200574674&alt=media) | ? | ![goal](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F9509650%2Fa6e0c829e1212adce457e18482d7855f%2Fexample_03_final.png?generation=1770463270476956&alt=media) |

---

## 6. Example Action Sequence

1. Move **navy bowl** ¡ú Top-Left  
2. Move **green bowl** ¡ú Center  
3. Move **yellow bowl** ¡ú Bottom-Right  
4. Move **navy block** ¡ú navy bowl  
5. Move **green block** ¡ú green bowl  
6. Move **yellow block** ¡ú yellow bowl  

---

# ?? Task 2: Color Matching with Triangle Intensity Placement (50% of Dataset)

## 1. Task Overview

This task requires organizing colored bowls into a **triangle formation**  
based on brightness (luminance), and then matching the corresponding blocks.

The solution follows a structured two-phase process:

- **Phase 1:** Arrange bowls at triangle vertices according to intensity  
- **Phase 2:** Match each block to its corresponding bowl  

---

## 2. Triangle Placement Rule

Bowls must be positioned at the triangle vertices according to brightness ranking.

### 2.1 Positional Mapping

| Triangle Position | Required Intensity |
|------------------|-------------------|
| Top-Left         | Darkest           |
| Top-Right        | Second Brightest  |
| Center           | Brightest         |

The triangle configuration must strictly follow this intensity hierarchy.

### 2.2 Constraints

- Bowls must be placed at the correct triangle vertex based on intensity  
- Any incorrect intensity-to-position mapping invalidates the configuration  

---

## 3. Visual Transition Example

| INITIAL STATE |  | GOAL STATE (Triangle Formation) |
|---------------|--|----------------------------------|
| ![INITIAL](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F9509650%2F1401e73191df1812c693115c4e7f7084%2Fexample_04_initial.png?generation=1770464963886001&alt=media) | ? | ![GOAL](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F9509650%2F3799dc16c130ee41d9aa5f6dc5771a69%2Fexample_04_final.png?generation=1770464735464394&alt=media) |


---

## 4. Example Action Sequence

1. Move the **darkest bowl** ¡ú Top-Left  
2. Move the **second brightest bowl** ¡ú Top-Right  
3. Move the **brightest bowl** ¡ú Center  
4. Move the matching block ¡ú corresponding bowl  
5. Repeat until all blocks are matched  

----------------------
# ?? Evaluation Metric

Submissions are scored using **Gaussian-weighted position accuracy** for pick-and-place actions.  
Distances are in meters; standard tolerance is **0.05 m (5 cm)**. Scores range from 0 (poor) to 1 (perfect).

---

## Score Formula

For each predicted action:

$$
\displaystyle
\text{distance} = 
\sqrt{(x_{\text{pick}}^{\text{pred}} - x_{\text{pick}}^{\text{true}})^2 + (y_{\text{pick}}^{\text{pred}} - y_{\text{pick}}^{\text{true}})^2} +
\sqrt{(x_{\text{place}}^{\text{pred}} - x_{\text{place}}^{\text{true}})^2 + (y_{\text{place}}^{\text{pred}} - y_{\text{place}}^{\text{true}})^2}
$$


$$
\text{score} = \exp\left(-\left(\frac{\text{distance}}{\text{tolerance}}\right)^2\right)
$$


---

## Task-Specific Evaluation

### Task 1: Diagonal
- **Bowls (60%)**: order-sensitive by intensity; 30% penalty if order is wrong  
- **Blocks (40%)**: order-insensitive (Hungarian matching)  

### Task 2: Triangle
- **Bowls (60%)**: order-sensitive by triangle vertex intensity; 35% penalty if order is wrong  
- **Blocks (40%)**: order-insensitive (Hungarian matching)  

---

## Penalties

- Missing bowl actions: multiply by 
$$
\displaystyle \left(n_{\text{pred}} / n_{\text{true}}\right)^{1.5}
$$

- Extra bowl actions: multiply by 
$$
\displaystyle \left(n_{\text{true}} / n_{\text{pred}}\right)^{0.8}
$$

- Position errors: reduce score via Gaussian decay
$$
\displaystyle \text{score} = \exp\Big(-( \text{distance} / \text{tolerance} )^2 \Big)
$$

- Block count mismatch: penalized proportionally  

----------------------
# ??Submission Format

## Required Columns
- `image_id`: Unique identifier (e.g., ""test_000000"") ?
- `action_0` ¡­ `action_5`: Actions as strings `""x_pick,y_pick,x_place,y_place""`

Where: 

- **x_pick**: X coordinate to pick up object (meters, range: -0.3 to 0.3)  
- **y_pick**: Y coordinate to pick up object (meters, range: -0.8 to -0.2)  
- **x_place**: X coordinate to place object (meters, range: -0.3 to 0.3)  
- **y_place**: Y coordinate to place object (meters, range: -0.8 to -0.2)  

## Action Rules
- Use **4 decimals** (e.g., `-0.1234`)  
- Each action must be a **single string** with commas  
- Leave unused actions empty  
- Maintain bowl¡úblock order  

## Important Notes
- Match `image_id`s exactly  
- Include all test images  
- Use **world coordinates**, not pixels  
- No extra spaces, zeros, or scientific notation  

## Example

```csv
image_id,action_0,action_1,action_2
test_000002,""-0.0165,-0.6338,-0.1979,-0.319"",""-0.1409,-0.584,0.0145,-0.5007"",""0.1425,-0.4877,0.1914,-0.6803""
test_000006,""-0.0415,-0.3164,0.0196,-0.5079"",""-0.1921,-0.6811,0.1898,-0.3346"",""-0.0674,-0.6656,-0.1644,-0.3236""
...etc
```

