10884,medhack-frontiers,"# Overview

# TLDR (Too Long, Didn't Read)
## Here's some quick links to popular content for the hackathon. But see the main overview below. 
**[Big Tract (Pitching) Dataset 1: ](https://kaggle.com/datasets/4dbfe5ed9320db06fc60bdf33aa2d604e15773c4e992e9e090e630c9bafb5384)**

**[Big Tract (Pitching) Dataset 2: ](https://kaggle.com/datasets/dd6cc7f5d00dc22cfa6d500202a2ca774155cd7a49bfd3bcbe2aba1ab99082c4)**

**[Small Tract (Coding) Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/mlaiaus/small-tract-coding/data)**

## Small Tract (Coding) Notebooks (To learn how to build an AI from scratch)
[**1. Introduction and Data Tour**
](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/1-introduction-data-tour-public)

**[2. Visualising the Chaos](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/2-visualizing-the-chaos)**

**[3. Hello, Logistic Regression](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/3-hello-logistic-regression-public)**

[**4. Limits of a Simple Model**](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/4-limits-of-a-simple-model-public)

[**5. Enter, Deep Neural Networks**](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/5-enter-deep-neural-networks-public)

[**6. My Deep Neural Network Says You're Fine**](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/6-my-deep-neural-network-says-you-re-fine)

[**7. Creating and submitting our predictions**](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/7-creating-and-submitting-our-predictions)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F24432781%2Fe4d507ff909dded7a8b5f34ba38690b3%2FUntitled%20design%20(3).gif?generation=1737724283066488&alt=media)

# **OH NO!**

Funding cuts, questionable business practices, and poor working conditions have left the famously **world-renowned** hospital, the **AI General**, terribly short-staffed. But the patients just **keep pouring in!**

**Can you help** this simulated hospital by building an AI that monitors patients and sounds the alarm only when something truly serious is happening?

Because, let¡¯s be honest, if our doctors and nurses are running around for every sneeze or a patient heading to the bathroom, they¡¯ll lose precious time (and their sanity). We need **you** (yes, you!) to **save the day** and help our (simulated) patients make it to tomorrow.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F24432781%2F747eb3312d69e46b0d1a683fdfcbdffb%2FUntitled%20(1920%20x%20288%20px).gif?generation=1737717830022924&alt=media)

_______________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

# **Welcome to the Emergency Department!**

Picture this: A bustling ER that never seems to slow down. Alarms beeping, doctors rushing, nurses juggling charts and IV lines, the smell of hand sanitiser everywhere. Into this **friendly chaos**, streams a line of patients in various states¡ªsome with minor issues, some teetering on the edge of crisis.

In this fantasy hospital:

- We have **medical histories** for each patient (like a chaotic library of prior conditions and surprising diagnoses).
- We have **real-time vitals** updating every few seconds: heart rate, blood pressure, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation... all those beep-boop numbers that keep folks alive.
- We even have a **treasure chest of old patient notes** from previous cases¡ªrandom scribbles, exam findings, ¡°Patient complained of headache after eating 40 cookies,¡± etc.
- And of course, an **unending queue** of new arrivals who might or might not be in serious danger.

Your job? **Tame** this mountain of data. Build an AI that tells us who needs urgent help (and who¡¯s just excited from chatting with Grandma on the phone).

_______________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

# What¡¯s the Actual Challenge?
We want an AI that can say:

1. **¡°ALERT!¡±** if a patient is experiencing something truly dangerous (like a crisis situation).
2. **¡°All good here¡±** if the patient is stable, or maybe just doing something non-emergency-like (going to the bathroom, high-fiving a friend, or spontaneously dancing to elevator music).

The** secret sauce** is striking that perfect balance between saving lives (catching genuine emergencies) and not overloading the staff with false alarms. (Because if every beep is a ¡°Code Red,¡± the team will ignore them all, and we don¡¯t want that!)
----------------------
# Description

1. **Time-Series Vitals:** We record multiple vital signs every 5 seconds:
* `diastolic_bp`
* `systolic_bp`
* `heart_rate`
* `respiratory_rate`
* `oxygen_saturation`

2. **State Label:** In the **training data**, we give you a `state_label` for each 5-second snapshot (1 = crisis, 0 = not crisis). In the **test data**, that label is hidden¡ªyour job is to predict it.

So, your AI model will look at some chunk of vitals + (optionally) any unstructured data you can find, and figure out: ¡°Is the patient in crisis or not?¡±
----------------------
# Evaluation

# What¡¯s the Deal with This Scoring System?
We have 4 states in our hospital: **Normal, Warning, Crisis** and **Death** They¡¯re labeled with certain numbers in your dataset:

- **Normal**: {0}
- **Warning**: {1}
- **Crisis**: {2}
- **Death**: {3}

A **Normal** state might be a patient going to the bathroom, or getting excited when their family visits.

A **Warning** state might be when a patient starts to feel off. Their heart rate rises, they start sweating, blood pressure begins to drop... Maybe they are **just low on sugar**? or maybe they have a **pre-existing heart condition** that they know is benign? Or maybe it's a prelude to something more dangerous like a **heart-attack**?

A **Crisis** state is a full-blown emergency. For example; This patient is having a **heart-attack** and we need to alert the medical team **NOW**.

**Death** state... is.. well.. Let's just say there's no coming back from this state.

Your job is to predict which exact state a patient is in. But our scoring system doesn¡¯t just pat you on the back for right answers¡ªit also slaps you on the wrist for missing important calls. Here¡¯s how we score each row:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F24432781%2F88e08983fcda37b4e4bcfa1eefbd477c%2FIntro_2.gif?generation=1737718460544735&alt=media)

**If the true label is Normal** (basically, the patient¡¯s fine¡ªmaybe they¡¯re just bored or telling jokes):
- Guess **Normal** too? You get **¡À0** (no big deal¡ªcarry on, doc).
- Guess **Warning **or **Crisis**? You get **¨C2** (the staff went running for the defibrillator¡­ and found a patient daydreaming).

**If the true label is Warning** (the patient needs attention but isn¡¯t coding in front of your eyes):
- Guess **Warning**: You get **+2** (nice job, you flagged a legit concern).
- Guess **Crisis: ¨C1** (you cried wolf a bit, sending the crash cart for someone who isn¡¯t dying¡­ yet).
- Guess **Normal: ¨C3** (you missed something important¡ªnurses are unimpressed).

If the true label is **Crisis** (drop everything and help this patient now):
- Guess **Crisis: +3** (heroic! You saved the day!).
- Guess **Warning: ¨C3** (oops, you told the docs ¡°take a look sometime,¡± but we needed them right now).
- Guess **Normal: ¨C10** (yikes, the patient was literally crashing, and you told everyone to chill).

Your **total score** is the sum of these points across **all** the rows. Aim high¡ªand remember that **missing** a crisis is more serious than accidentally screaming ¡°Emergency!¡± when someone¡¯s just a bit anxious.

----------------------
# Learning Content

# Competition Notebook Series
We¡¯ve prepared a series of friendly, **step-by-step** notebooks to hold your hand along the journey. Think of them as little questlines in your favourite RPG¡ªbut with more code and fewer dragons. Here¡¯s our **table of contents**:

[**1. Introduction & Data Tour**](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/1-introduction-data-tour-public)
- Learn what this challenge is about, meet your data, and see how we can poke around those vitals.

**[2. Visualising the Chaos](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/2-visualizing-the-chaos)**
- Plot heart rates, blood pressures, and see how to distinguish normal from bizarre.

**[3. Hello, Logistic Regression](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/3-hello-logistic-regression-public)**
- We start small by building a simple model (Logistic Regression) that just looks at `heart rate`. We¡¯ll see how it guesses if someone¡¯s in crisis.

[**4. Limits of a Simple Model**](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/4-limits-of-a-simple-model-public)
- Watch as Logistic Regression tries (and sometimes fails) when the vitals get more complex. Learn why it might not always be enough for the job.

[**5. Enter, Deep Neural Networks**](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/5-enter-deep-neural-networks-public)
- Learn about these bigger, fancier AIs that can juggle all the vitals at once, maybe even some textual clues. We¡¯ll build a small one, see how it performs, and watch your accuracy (hopefully) skyrocket.

[**6. My Deep Neural Network Says You're Fine**](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/6-my-deep-neural-network-says-you-re-fine)
- Hear some insider tips: add more data features, tune hyperparameters, handle time-series patterns more elegantly¡­ basically, level up your AI to handle the real deal.


Feel free to pick and choose, or follow them all in sequence. By the end, you¡¯ll see how to **build a robust model, evaluate it, and improve it.**

_______________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

# How to submit your predictions and get on the leaderboard

[**7. Creating and submitting our predictions**](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mlaiaus/7-creating-and-submitting-our-predictions)

When you've got your predictions.csv, submit it via the 'submissions' tab on the med-hack.com website:

_______________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

# Conclusion & Parting Words
You¡¯ve learned:

- How to handle simulated hospital data (the drama, the clutter, the oh-so-frequent beep-beeps).
- How to identify what¡¯s really an emergency.
- How to build both a simple Logistic Regression and a more powerful Deep Neural Network.

There¡¯s a **lot** more you could do. In real hospitals, you¡¯d see advanced time-series algorithms, specialised medical image analysis, and robust text-mining techniques for those scribbled notes. But for now, **feel proud: **you¡¯re taking your first steps toward saving simulated lives!

**Best of luck** with your AI creation. Tweak it, break it, tune it¡ªjust don¡¯t let that heart attack slip by. And, most important of all, **have fun.**

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F24432781%2F3515f691f9e718896300e1594d99c622%2FIntro_3.gif?generation=1737718972099897&alt=media)

_______________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

**Ready to begin?** Check out the first notebook in the series and start exploring how to keep your patients alive (in a simulated environment, at least!). Good luck!","# Dataset Description

# **OMG, So Many Patients¡ªWhere Do I Even Start?!**

Don¡¯t worry; we¡¯ve put everything you need into a couple of files, `train.csv` and `test.csv`. Each file is jam-packed with rows describing 5-second snapshots of a patient¡¯s life in the ER. Here¡¯s the rundown:

# A) Data Files
`train.csv`

- This is your **gold**. It has **historical patient data** *plus* the secret sauce: `state_label.`
- Use it to **train** your model (i.e., show your AI what¡¯s happening when patients are in normal, warning, or crisis states).


`test.csv`

- This is the **mystery** file. It¡¯s got the same columns as `train.csv` (except for state_label¡ªthat¡¯s removed).
- You must guess or **predict** the `crisis_label` for each row. Then, you¡¯ll **submit** those predictions to see how well you did.


# B) What¡¯s In These CSVs?
Each row represents a **5-second interval** for a **specific patient**. You¡¯ll see:

- **timestamp**: The exact date/time for that measurement.
- **patient_id**: Which patient this data belongs to (it¡¯s chaotic in an ER, so we have lots of patients at once).
- **diastolic_bp**: The lower number in blood pressure measurements.
- **systolic_bp**: The higher number in blood pressure measurements.
- **heart_rate**: Beats per minute¡ªthose beep-beeps you hear on a monitor.
- **respiratory_rate**: How many breaths per minute the patient¡¯s taking.
- **oxygen_saturation**: A percentage telling us how well oxygenated a patient¡¯s blood is.

`state_label` (only in train.csv):
- 0 means Normal (no biggie),
- 1 means Warning,
- 2 means Crisis,
- 3 means Death.


# C) Training, Testing, & Why They Matter

- **Training Data** (`train.csv`):
Think of it as a big storybook where you know the ending. Each row says, ¡°At this time, for this patient, the label was X.¡± Your AI model ¡°reads¡± these examples and **learns** what normal vs. warning vs. crisis looks like.

- **Test Data** (`test.csv`):
Here, the endings are **hidden**. Each row shows vitals but **omits** the `state_label`. Your model has to predict that label without cheating. Then, we¡¯ll check your predictions against the real answers (which we keep secret like a surprise quiz). If your model does well, **congrats**; you¡¯re one step closer to being a hero in the ER (simulated, but still heroic).


# D) Common Questions
**What files do I need?**

- At minimum, you need `train.csv` to train your model and `test.csv` to generate predictions for the leaderboard.

**What¡¯s the format?**

- Both files are CSV. Each row: [`timestamp`, `patient_id`, `diastolic_bp`, `systolic_bp`, `heart_rate`, `respiratory_rate`, `oxygen_saturation`, (`state_label`?)].

**What am I predicting?**

- You¡¯re predicting the missing `state_label` (0, 9, 12, etc.) in `test.csv`.

**What acronyms or weird terms might I see?**

- **BP** = Blood Pressure.
- **SpO2** or **Oxygen Saturation** = oxygen_saturation.
- Don¡¯t worry if the medical jargon looks alien. Focus on the numbers as features.

Now you know everything you need to dive in. So, crack open `train.csv`, feed your AI, then unleash it on `test.csv`. And remember: your model¡¯s job is to figure out when someone¡¯s truly in crisis vs. just daydreaming about candy bars.

Good luck, and may your predictions keep all (simulated) patients safe and sound!",False,True
10885,eliva-26-image-restoration,"# Overview

This is the first exercize in Eliva 2026 class.
The goals are:
-  be familiar with kaggle env
-  design an image restoration tool
----------------------
# Description

The goal of this assignment is to restore degraded images using image processing techniques. 
The degradation is unkonwn and must be estimated and corrected properly.

The challenge ranking will be done by estimating the restoration quality using the Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio ([PSNR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peak_signal-to-noise_ratio)) and Structural Similarity Index Measure ([SSIM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Structural_similarity_index_measure)) metrics.

The Data section contains the set of images to process. The goal is to improve their quality. You can start by inspecting the images to understand their nature and how they can be improved.

You can find a sample notebook with a dummy solution in the Code section. The script can be used to loop over all images in data26/, apply your enhancement solution, and create the .csv file to be submitted to the challenge. The enhanced images are encoded into the .csv that you upload as a solution.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The quality of each restored image in you subission will be ranked as follows:
$$p_i= PSNR (\textrm{restored}_i, \textrm{original}_i) \in [0, +\infty]$$
$$s_i =SSIM (\textrm{restored}_i, \textrm{original}_i) \in [0,1]$$
The submission score is computed  as the average product of both metrics
$$score = \frac{1}{N} \sum_i s_i p_i $$ 
A perfectly recovered image is expected to get 
$$s_i \approx 1 $$ 
 $$p_i > 20 \ \textrm{dB}$$","# Dataset Description

**Dataset**
In the data26 folder, you can find a set of degraded images.
Inspect the images and devise a tool to estimate the degradation in order to remove it.",False,True
10886,paml-hackathon-2-author-prediction,"# Description

<p><img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/spooky-books/dmitrij-paskevic-44124.jpg"" alt=""Spooky books"" width=""200"" style=""float: right;"" /></p>
<p>Late one evening in the GJBC Library, an unfortunate discovery was made.</p> <p>A dusty archive of classic horror literature, recently digitized for ¡°educational purposes¡±, had suffered a catastrophic metadata failure. Thousands of carefully catalogued excerpts from the works of Edgar Allan Poe, Mary Shelley, and H. P. Lovecraft were now completely detached from their original authors.</p> <p>The texts remain intact. The writing styles remain distinct. But the labels are gone.</p> <p>Was this the result of a routine preprocessing mishap? A poorly timed script execution? An overenthusiastic find-and-replace? No one knows. What <em>is</em> known is that restoring authorship manually would take weeks, and some of us would rather not read cosmic horror at scale unless absolutely necessary.</p> <p>Your task, as part of the Practical Approach to Machine Learning (PAML) Workshop's 2nd Hackathon, is to build a model that can correctly attribute each excerpt to its rightful author using only the stylistic and linguistic clues present in the text. In doing so, you¡¯ll explore practical approaches to text classification, feature engineering, and model evaluation in a real-world NLP setting.</p> <p>After all, if a machine can learn to distinguish existential dread from gothic melancholy, we might just be able to recover the archive before the semester ends.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using multi-class logarithmic loss. Each id has one true class. For each id, you must submit a predicted probability for each author. The formula is then:

<p>$$log loss = -\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N\sum_{j=1}^My_{ij}\log(p_{ij}),$$</p>
<p>where N is the number of observations in the test set, M is the number of class labels (3 classes),  \\(log\\) is the natural logarithm, \\(y_{ij}\\) is 1 if observation \\(i\\) belongs to class \\(j\\) and 0 otherwise, and \\(p_{ij}\\) is the predicted probability that observation \\(i\\) belongs to class \\(j\\).</p>

The submitted probabilities for a given sentences are not required to sum to one because they are rescaled prior to being scored (each row is divided by the row sum). In order to avoid the extremes of the log function, predicted probabilities are replaced with \\(max(min(p,1-10^{-15}),10^{-15})\\).

<h3>Submission File</h3>

You must submit a csv file with the id, and a probability for each of the three classes.
The order of the rows does not matter. The file must have a header and should look like the following:

<pre>
id,EAP,HPL,MWS
id07943,0.33,0.33,0.33
...
</pre>","# Dataset Description

The competition dataset contains text from works of fiction written by spooky authors of the public domain: Edgar Allan Poe, HP Lovecraft and Mary Shelley. The data was prepared by chunking larger texts into sentences using CoreNLP's MaxEnt sentence tokenizer, so you may notice the odd non-sentence here and there. Your objective is to accurately identify the author of the sentences in the test set.

## File descriptions

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Data fields

*   **id** - a unique identifier for each sentence
*   **text**?- some text written by one of the authors
*   **author** - the author of the sentence (`EAP`: Edgar Allan Poe, `HPL`: HP Lovecraft; `MWS`: Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley)",False,True
