10888,ma-477-spoken-digit-data-competition,"# Overview

## MA477 Data Science ¨C Spoken Digit Audio Classification Competition (6 vs 7)

This competition is part of **Project 2** and is designed to introduce you to **audio feature engineering and binary classification using classical machine learning methods**.

In this competition, you will work with the **Spoken MNIST audio dataset**, focusing specifically on spoken digits **6 and 7**.

The dataset includes:

- Raw audio waveforms (spoken digits)
- Spectrogram representations of audio
- Multiple speakers (introducing natural variation)
- High-dimensional numeric feature representations

Your task is to:

- Apply and compare multiple classification models
- Evaluate model performance using appropriate metrics

You should focus on:

- Train/test separation
- Feature scaling
- Model comparison (LDA, Logistic Regression, SVM, KNN)
- Model evaluation and competition performance
----------------------
# Description

## What You Are Predicting

In this competition, you are classifying **spoken digit 6 versus spoken digit 7**.

For modeling and evaluation purposes, the original digits have been **recoded into binary labels**:

- `0` ¡ú Spoken digit **6**
- `1` ¡ú Spoken digit **7** (positive class)

Your task is to predict:

> The probability that a given recording corresponds to spoken digit **7**.

Even though the original recordings contain the spoken words ¡°six¡± and ¡°seven,¡± the dataset you receive uses the binary labels `0` and `1` to simplify modeling and scoring.

When you submit predictions, your `Probability` column should represent:

- A value close to **0** if the model believes the recording is digit 6  
- A value close to **1** if the model believes the recording is digit 7  

Evaluation is performed using **ROC AUC**.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored using **ROC AUC (Receiver Operating Characteristic ¨C Area Under the Curve)** based on your predicted probabilities for the positive class (`7`).  

Higher ROC AUC values indicate better performance. A model with perfect separation achieves an AUC of 1.0, while a model that performs no better than random guessing has an AUC of 0.5.

---

## Submission File

For each `ID` in the test set, you must provide the **predicted probability that the spoken digit is 1**.

Your submission file must:

- Be in **CSV format**
- Contain a header
- Include exactly **two columns**: `ID` and `Probability`
- Match the order of rows in the test dataset

### Required format:

ID,Probability

1001,0.732

1002,0.118

1003,0.945

etc","# Dataset Description

# Data Description

This page describes the files provided for the **Spoken Digit Classification Competition**. Please read this section carefully before beginning your analysis.

After reading this description, you should be able to answer:

- What files do I need?  
- What is the format of each file?  
- What am I predicting?  
- How is the leaderboard constructed?  
- What do the feature columns represent?  

---

## Overview

In this competition, you will build a **binary classifier** to distinguish between **two spoken digits** from the Spoken MNIST dataset.

Each example originates from a short audio recording of a spoken digit. The raw audio has been transformed into a **spectrogram**, and that spectrogram has been flattened into a numeric feature vector suitable for classical machine learning models.

The test set is constructed using a **speaker-held-out split**, meaning the speakers in the test set do not appear in the training set. This prevents speaker leakage and ensures that models must generalize across different voices.

---

## Files

### **train.csv**
The training dataset.

Use this file to train your model.

---

### **test.csv**
The test dataset.

Use this file to generate predictions for submission.  
The true labels for this file are hidden during the competition.

---

### **sample_submission.csv**
A sample file showing the required submission format.

Your final submission must match this format exactly.

---

## What You Are Predicting

You are predicting:

> The probability that a spoken recording corresponds to the **positive class digit**.

---

## Columns

### **train.csv**

- `ID`  
  Unique identifier for each recording.

- `x0, x1, x2, ..., xp-1`  
  Flattened spectrogram features.  
  Each feature corresponds to a time¨Cfrequency bin from the spectrogram of the original audio recording.

- `Label`  
  Binary class label:
  - `0` = 6  
  - `1` = 7 (positive class)

---

### **test.csv**

- `ID`  
  Unique identifier for each recording.

- `x0, x1, x2, ..., xp-1`  
  Same feature format as `train.csv`.

There is **no `Label` column** in this file.

---

### **sample_submission.csv**

- `ID`  
  Must match the test IDs exactly.

- `Probability`  
  A value between 0 and 1 representing your model¡¯s estimated probability that the recording belongs to the positive class.

Example:
ID,Probability
1451,0.73
1452,0.12
1453,0.98",False,True
10889,rizzzzzzzz,"# Overview

Modern cameras and lenses introduce predictable geometric distortion (barrel/pincushion) and sometimes slight shifts in principal point. In professional photo pipelines (Adobe Lightroom, Capture One, manufacturer tools), this is typically fixed using lens profiles released and maintained by manufacturers or curated databases.

In practice, open-source lens profile coverage lags behind new camera/lens releases. That gap causes real-world pain: uncorrected distortion shows up as bent lines, warped interiors, and ""off"" perspective ¡ª especially noticeable in real-estate photography where straight edges matter.

**Your task in this competition is to automatically correct lens distortion in photos when no reliable lens profile is available.**

You'll receive a set of distorted test images. Using the provided training pairs (distorted originals + their corrected counterparts), build a model or algorithm that produces lens-corrected output images. Your corrected images will be scored against hidden ground truth using a geometry-focused evaluation metric.

### How It Works

1. **Download** the training dataset (distorted + corrected image pairs) and test images from the Data tab
2. **Build** your lens correction approach ¡ª neural network (img2img), classical algorithm, or any method you choose
3. **Correct** the test images and upload them to our [scoring service](INSERT_YOUR_SCORING_URL_HERE)
4. **Download** your `submission.csv` from the scoring service
5. **Submit** the CSV here on Kaggle to see your score on the leaderboard

### What Makes a Good Correction?

Our scoring metric prioritizes geometric accuracy:
- **Edge alignment** ¡ª do edges in your output match the ground truth?
- **Line straightness** ¡ª are lines that should be straight actually straight?
- **Gradient orientation** ¡ª are structural directions preserved correctly?
- **Structural similarity** ¡ª does the overall structure match?
- **Pixel accuracy** ¡ª are the pixels close to ground truth?

You don't need pixel-perfect output. The metric is designed to reward correct geometry even when there are minor differences in color, noise, or compression artifacts.

### Approaches You Might Try

- Image-to-image neural networks (pix2pix, U-Net, diffusion models)
- Classical distortion parameter estimation + remapping
- Feature matching and homography-based correction
- Hybrid approaches combining learning with geometric priors

Any method is fair game. We care about the result, not how you got there.

----------------------
# Description

### Submit Your 1-Minute Video Overview

Along with your final submission, provide a short video (60 seconds max) explaining your approach.

**Include:**
- What method did you use? (neural network, algorithmic, hybrid, etc.)
- What was your key insight or design decision?
- What challenges did you face and how did you solve them?
- Any interesting findings or surprises?

Feel free to be as creative as you want ¡ª screen recordings, slides, whiteboard sketches, or just talking to camera all work. The goal is to understand your technical approach.

**Upload your video to YouTube (unlisted is fine) and paste the link in the discussion forum with the tag [Video Submission].**
----------------------
# Evaluation!

### Metric

Submissions are evaluated using **Mean Absolute Error (MAE)** between your submission scores and perfect scores (1.0).

Each of your corrected test images is scored against hidden ground truth using a multi-metric evaluation that combines:

| Metric | Weight | What It Measures |
|---|---|---|
| Edge Similarity | 40% | Multi-scale Canny edge F1 score |
| Line Straightness | 22% | Hough line angle distribution match |
| Gradient Orientation | 18% | Gradient direction histogram similarity |
| SSIM | 15% | Structural similarity index |
| Pixel Accuracy | 5% | Mean absolute pixel difference |

Each image receives a score from 0.0 (completely wrong) to 1.0 (perfect match). Your submission CSV contains these per-image scores. Kaggle computes the MAE against a solution file where all scores are 1.0.

**Lower MAE = better.** A perfect correction scores MAE = 0.0.

#### Hard Fail Conditions

An image scores 0.0 automatically if:
- Maximum regional difference exceeds threshold (catastrophic local error)
- Edge similarity is below minimum (structurally broken output)

### How to Submit

1. Correct all test images using your model/algorithm
2. Upload your corrected images (as a zip) to the **scoring service** ¡ª [INSERT_SCORING_URL_HERE]
3. The scoring service returns a `submission.csv` with your per-image scores
4. Download the CSV and submit it here on Kaggle

### Submission File Format

Your `submission.csv` must contain a header and two columns:

```
image_id,score
08084abe-e9be-4277-8aa1-e1d910312a58_g0,0.9729
10ab83b0-c13a-4b7c-a08e-2d8b2e250a7e_g0,0.9748
...
```

**Note:** You do not compute the scores yourself. The scoring service generates this CSV for you. Just download and submit it.",,False,True
10890,alchemy-aicc-round-4,"# Overview

This competition is organized by the [IOAI Community Contest group](https://ioai-community-contest.netlify.app/) (not affiliated with IOAI), which is made up of IOAI alumni. It is meant to prepare students for the IOAI (International Olympiad in Artificial Intelligence).
----------------------
# Description

## 1. Problem Description

In the game Little Alchemy, players combine elements to discover new ones. Fire and water make steam. Earth and seeds make a tree. Some combinations are intuitive, others require creativity, dinosaur plus fire becomes a dragon.

You are given a dataset of 150 training combination rules (`item1 + item2 ¡ú result`) and 70 test pairs with hidden results. Your job is to predict which result belongs to each test pair.

## 2. Dataset

```
data/
©À©¤©¤ train.csv       # 150 rows: item1, item2, result
©À©¤©¤ test.csv        # 70 rows: Id, item1, item2 (target hidden)
©¸©¤©¤ candidates.csv  # 70 rows: result (unique candidate labels)
```

- **Training set:** 150 labeled examples
- **Test set:** 70 pairs to predict
- **Candidates:** The list of 70 possible results for the test set
- All items are lowercase English words or short phrases
- The assignment is bijective, each test pair maps to exactly one unique candidate, and each candidate is used once.

![Alchemy Combination Example](https://imgur.com/bWlfm5A.png) 

## 3. Task

For each of the 70 test pairs, predict the correct result from the candidate list.

**Constraint:** You may only use `bert-base-uncased`, no other pretrained models or external data allowed.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## 4. Submission

Submit a CSV with columns `Id` and `result`:

```python
import pandas as pd

submission = pd.DataFrame({
    'Id': test_df['Id'],
    'result': predictions
})
submission.to_csv('submission.csv', index=False)
```

## 5. Score

Accuracy: fraction of correct assignments.

$$ \text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{Number of Correct Predictions}}{70} $$

- **Baseline Score:** ~0.08
- **Reference Solution Score:** ~0.41

The baseline can be found on the **[Code](https://www.kaggle.com/code/antoningorokva/baseline-alchemy-aicc-round-4)** section of this competition or in Google Colab [Here](https://colab.research.google.com/#fileId=https%3A//storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-colab-exported-notebooks/antoningorokva/baseline-alchemy-aicc-round-4.fedbf9a4-dcae-41e7-8d23-4fd53d64eb73.ipynb%3FX-Goog-Algorithm%3DGOOG4-RSA-SHA256%26X-Goog-Credential%3Dgcp-kaggle-com%2540kaggle-161607.iam.gserviceaccount.com/20260219/auto/storage/goog4_request%26X-Goog-Date%3D20260219T205732Z%26X-Goog-Expires%3D259200%26X-Goog-SignedHeaders%3Dhost%26X-Goog-Signature%)","# Dataset Description

```
data/
©À©¤©¤ train.csv       # 150 rows: item1, item2, result
©À©¤©¤ test.csv        # 70 rows: Id, item1, item2
©¸©¤©¤ candidates.csv  # 70 rows: result (unique candidate labels)
```

- **Training set:** 150 labeled examples
- **Test set:** 70 pairs to predict
- **Candidates:** The list of 70 possible results for the test set
- All items are lowercase English words or short phrases
- The assignment is bijective, each test pair maps to exactly one unique candidate, and each candidate is used once.",False,True
10891,sticky-note-blindness-aicc-round-4,"# Overview

This competition is organized by the [IOAI Community Contest group](https://ioai-community-contest.netlify.app/) (not affiliated with IOAI), which is made up of IOAI alumni. It is meant to prepare students for the IOAI (International Olympiad in Artificial Intelligence).
----------------------
# Description

## 1. Problem Description

You are working on an object classification system based on CLIP (ViT-B/16). The system embeds an image, embeds a fixed set of class names as text, and predicts the class whose text embedding is closest to the image embedding.

On clean images, this works reliably. However, the system is vulnerable to a simple typographic attack: a sticky note with a misleading word is attached to the object. CLIP often focuses on the visible text and predicts the wrong class.

Your task is to recover the true object class from attacked images, ignoring the misleading text.

## 2. Dataset

```
data/
©À©¤©¤ images/images/         # PNG images, original resolution
©À©¤©¤ class_embeddings.pt    # [K, 512] float32 CLIP text embeddings
©¸©¤©¤ metadata.csv           # sample_id, postit_area_pct
```

- `images/`: attacked RGB images showing an object with an attached sticky note containing misleading text.
- `class_embeddings.pt`: L2-normalized CLIP text embeddings for all K candidate classes, computed from class names only. The class set contains ~800 labels, consisting of true object classes and attack words.
- `metadata.csv`: for each image, the fraction of image area covered by the sticky note (`postit_area_pct`).

CLIP achieves 100% zero-shot accuracy on the corresponding clean images (same objects, text removed), so any misclassification is caused by the typographic attack.

Example of CLIP misclassification caused by attached text:

![Typographic attack example](https://imgur.com/m5VKhHJ.png)

## 3. Task

For each attacked image, predict the integer index of the true object class.

You must only use `openai/clip-vit-base-patch16`. No other pretrained models or external data are allowed.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## 3. Submission

Create a CSV file with the following format:

```python
submission = pd.DataFrame({
    ""sample_id"": list(range(N)),
    ""label"": predictions  # integer class indices
})
submission.to_csv(""submission.csv"", index=False)
```

Submissions that do not match this format receive a score of 0.

## 4. Score

The evaluation metric is classification accuracy.

$$ \text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{Number of Correct Predictions}}{\text{Total Samples}} $$

- Baseline Score: ~0.46
- Reference Solution Score: ~0.93+

The baseline can be found on the **[Code](https://www.kaggle.com/code/antoningorokva/baseline-sticky-note-blindness-aicc-round-4/notebook)** section of this competition or in Google Colab [Here](https://colab.research.google.com/#fileId=https%3A//storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-colab-exported-notebooks/antoningorokva/baseline-sticky-note-blindness-aicc-round-4.a8217f62-c0a3-43ca-9459-0729336fb87f.ipynb%3FX-Goog-Algorithm%3DGOOG4-RSA-SHA256%26X-Goog-Credential%3Dgcp-kaggle-com%2540kaggle-161607.iam.gserviceaccount.com/20260219/auto/storage/goog4_request%26X-Goog-Date%3D20260219T205726Z%26X-Goog-Expires%3D259200%26X-Goog-SignedHeaders%3Dhost%26X-Goog-Signature%)","# Dataset Description

## Dataset

```
data/
©À©¤©¤ images/images/         # PNG images, original resolution
©À©¤©¤ class_embeddings.pt    # [K, 512] float32 CLIP text embeddings
©¸©¤©¤ metadata.csv           # sample_id, postit_area_pct
```

- `images/`: attacked RGB images showing an object with an attached sticky note containing misleading text.
- `class_embeddings.pt`: L2-normalized CLIP text embeddings for all K candidate classes, computed from class names only. The class set contains ~800 labels, consisting of true object classes and attack words.
- `metadata.csv`: for each image, the fraction of image area covered by the sticky note (`postit_area_pct`).

CLIP achieves 100% zero-shot accuracy on the corresponding clean images (same objects, text removed), so any misclassification is caused by the typographic attack.",False,True
