10893,stock-market-signal-predict-next-day-returns,"# Overview

Can you beat a coin flip at predicting the stock market?

This competition challenges you to build a machine learning model that predicts whether a stock will close **higher or lower** the next trading day, using 27 engineered technical indicators derived from real US equity market data.

You are given features for 100 anonymized stocks spanning over two decades of market history ¡ª including momentum signals, volatility measures, moving average ratios, volume statistics, and more. The training set covers 2000¨C2023, and you must predict next-day return direction for 2024¨C2026.

**Goal:** For each row in the test set, predict the probability that the stock closes higher the next trading day (`target = 1`).

This is a binary classification problem evaluated on **AUC-ROC**. The baseline (random guessing) scores 0.500. Can you do better?

## Timeline

- **Start Date** ¡ª February 21, 2026
- **Entry Deadline** ¡ª Same as the Final Submission Deadline
- **Team Merger Deadline** ¡ª Same as the Final Submission Deadline
- **Final Submission Deadline** ¡ª May 20, 2026

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizer reserves the right to update the competition timeline if deemed necessary.

## Prizes

This is a **knowledge-only** competition ¡ª no monetary prizes (but at the end of the day, knowledge is the real prize¡­ haha).

## Acknowledgments

This competition was created by [Jack Saleeby](https://www.kaggle.com/jacksaleeby). If you're new to stock market data, check out the [NASDAQ-100 Historical Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/jacksaleeby/nasdaq100-historical-data-2000-2026-upvote) and the accompanying [EDA & Visualization notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/jacksaleeby/nasdaq-100-stock-data-eda-visualization) for an introduction to how equity data works.

Finally, if you enjoy working with equity data, consider checking out and competing in my [Stock Market Signal: Predict Next-Day Returns](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/stock-market-signal-predict-next-day-returns) competition ¡ª can you beat a coin flip at predicting the stock market?
----------------------
# Description

## Background

Predicting short-term stock movements is one of the hardest problems in quantitative finance. Markets are noisy, largely efficient, and influenced by countless factors ¡ª earnings reports, macroeconomic shifts, geopolitical events, and investor sentiment. Yet even small edges ¡ª consistently predicting direction just slightly better than a coin flip ¡ª can be enormously valuable in real-world trading.

This dataset contains **27 engineered features** derived from real historical US equity data for **100 anonymized stocks**. The features are technical indicators commonly used by quantitative traders and systematic hedge funds. No raw prices, dates, or ticker names are included ¡ª only computed signals.

## What You're Predicting

The `target` column is binary:
- **1** = the stock's adjusted close price **increased** the next trading day
- **0** = the stock's adjusted close price **decreased** the next trading day

The baseline (random guessing) achieves ~50% accuracy and 0.500 AUC. The target is roughly balanced: 50.3% up-days in the training set, 51.6% in the test set.

## Dataset Files

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `train.csv` | 440,402 rows ¡ª features + target for 100 stocks (2000¨C2023) |
| `test.csv` | 53,276 rows ¡ª features only for 100 stocks (2024¨C2026) |
| `sample_submission.csv` | Example submission in the correct format |

## Feature Descriptions

All features are **normalized ratios or bounded indicators**, not raw prices. This prevents reverse-engineering of the underlying stocks.

| Feature | Description |
|---|---|
| `stock_id` | Anonymized stock identifier (stock_000 to stock_099) |
| `return_1d` | 1-day return |
| `return_5d` | 5-day return |
| `return_10d` | 10-day return |
| `return_20d` | 20-day return |
| `sma_ratio_5` | Price / 5-day simple moving average |
| `sma_ratio_10` | Price / 10-day SMA |
| `sma_ratio_20` | Price / 20-day SMA |
| `sma_ratio_50` | Price / 50-day SMA |
| `sma_ratio_200` | Price / 200-day SMA |
| `ema_ratio_12` | Price / 12-day exponential moving average |
| `ema_ratio_26` | Price / 26-day EMA |
| `macd_hist` | MACD histogram (normalized by price) |
| `rsi_14` | 14-day Relative Strength Index (0¨C100) |
| `bb_position` | Bollinger Band position (typically -1 to 1) |
| `volatility_10d` | 10-day annualized volatility |
| `volatility_20d` | 20-day annualized volatility |
| `volatility_60d` | 60-day annualized volatility |
| `volume_sma_ratio_10` | Volume / 10-day average volume |
| `volume_sma_ratio_20` | Volume / 20-day average volume |
| `daily_range` | (High ? Low) / Close |
| `avg_range_10d` | 10-day average daily range |
| `high_low_ratio` | High / Low |
| `momentum_10d` | Price / Price 10 days ago |
| `momentum_20d` | Price / Price 20 days ago |
| `roc_5` | 5-day rate of change |
| `roc_10` | 10-day rate of change |
| `atr_14` | 14-day Average True Range (normalized by price) |

## Key Challenges

- **Market efficiency:** Stock prices largely follow a random walk. Consistent alpha is hard to find.
- **Low signal-to-noise ratio:** Even professional quant funds achieve only small edges above 50%.
- **Regime changes:** Market behavior in the 2024¨C2026 test period may differ from historical patterns (COVID recovery, rate hikes, AI boom).
- **Feature correlation:** Many features are derived from overlapping price data and will be highly correlated. Feature selection matters.
- **Overfitting risk:** With 440K training rows and noisy targets, complex models can easily overfit to noise.

## Suggested Approaches

- **Gradient boosted trees** (XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost) ¡ª strong baselines for tabular data
- **Logistic regression** with careful feature selection ¡ª interpretable and resistant to overfitting
- **Neural networks** ¡ª can capture non-linear feature interactions
- **Feature engineering** ¡ª create interaction features, rolling statistics, or per-stock normalizations
- **Ensemble methods** ¡ª combine multiple models for robust predictions
- **Per-stock modeling** ¡ª train separate models or add stock-level features using `stock_id`

## Helpful Resources

- [NASDAQ-100 Historical Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/jacksaleeby/nasdaq100-historical-data-2000-2026-upvote) ¡ª Explore raw stock data to understand market dynamics
- [NASDAQ-100 EDA & Visualization Notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/jacksaleeby/nasdaq-100-stock-data-eda-visualization) ¡ª Visual guide to stock data patterns
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric

Submissions are evaluated on the **Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC-ROC)** between the predicted probability and the observed target.

AUC-ROC measures how well your model ranks positive cases (up-days) above negative cases (down-days). A score of 0.500 means your model is no better than random guessing; a score of 1.000 means perfect ranking.

### Performance Interpretation

| AUC-ROC | Interpretation |
|---|---|
| 0.500 | Random guessing (coin flip) |
| 0.500¨C0.510 | Marginal edge ¡ª noise or weak signal |
| 0.510¨C0.520 | Basic model with some predictive power |
| 0.520¨C0.540 | Solid model ¡ª competitive range |
| 0.540¨C0.560 | Strong model ¡ª difficult to achieve consistently |
| 0.560+ | Exceptional ¡ª would be valuable in real trading |

Note: In real-world stock prediction, even a 0.520 AUC is considered meaningful. Don't be discouraged by scores close to 0.500 ¡ª this is an inherently noisy prediction task.

## Submission Format

For each `id` in the test set, you must predict a **probability** (between 0 and 1) that the stock goes up the next day. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
id,target
440402,0.5
440403,0.5
440404,0.5
etc.
```

- **id** ¡ª matches the `id` column from `test.csv`
- **target** ¡ª your predicted probability (float, 0.0 to 1.0)

The sample submission file (`sample_submission.csv`) contains the correct format with all predictions set to 0.5 (random baseline).

### Validation Checklist

Before submitting, verify:

- ? File is CSV format with comma delimiter
- ? Header row contains exactly `id,target`
- ? Contains 53,276 rows (excluding header)
- ? `id` values match test.csv exactly
- ? `target` values are floats between 0.0 and 1.0
- ? No missing values (NaN)

### Common Errors

**Wrong number of rows** ¡ª Ensure you have predictions for all 53,276 test rows. Load `sample_submission.csv` as a template to guarantee correct IDs.

**Values outside [0, 1]** ¡ª If your model outputs logits or unbounded scores, apply a sigmoid function or clip to [0, 1]:
```python
import numpy as np
predictions = np.clip(predictions, 0, 1)
```

**Missing values** ¡ª Fill any NaN predictions with 0.5 (neutral baseline):
```python
submission['target'].fillna(0.5, inplace=True)
```
----------------------
# Timeline

- **Start Date** ¡ª February 21, 2026
- **Entry Deadline** ¡ª Same as the Final Submission Deadline
- **Team Merger Deadline** ¡ª Same as the Final Submission Deadline
- **Final Submission Deadline** ¡ª May 20, 2026

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizer reserves the right to update the competition timeline if deemed necessary.",,False,True
10894,fun-ml-hw-4-problem-5-sp-26,"# Overview

You are right! This is the place to submit your HW 4 Problem5. 

In previous part of HW 4 Problem 5, you learnt to process images in a form they could be used to train clustering algorithms (with each individual pixel being a 'training example'). For an RGB image, each training example would have at least 3 features (the Red, Green, and Blue values for the pixel). In addition, one could add the spatial positions of the pixel as another set of features. 

We are now going test what you have learnt in processing image by this?Kaggle?competition wherein you are asked to segment two RGB images. By submitting your result to Kaggle, you will see your multi-class classification accuracy and ranking on the leaderborad. 10 pts will depend on your ranking (top 10% get 10pts, top 11%-20% get 9pts, etc.), and another 5 pts depend on your method explanation and clear, well-labeled plots and images. 

Since this is an open-ended question, you should try to design your own features to achieve better classification results. Try different feature combinations, image processing techniques to get the best looking results. Refer to the homework notebook for details. 
----------------------
# Description

After having your ideal result, please save your result in `result_img1` and `result_img2` in the cell below.

We have defined the label for each class in each image. Make sure you use the same settings as us and feel free to use the provided swapping label code to swap labels if needed.

 - for `img1.png`, use k=3, background labels as 0, rectangle labels as 1 and triangle labels as 2
 - for `img2.png`, use k=2, background labels as 0, building labels as 1

After your `result_img1` and `result_img2` are ready, run the cell below to create a `submission.csv`. Please download it from this notebook and submit it in our [Kaggle](https://www.kaggle.com/t/107607dc5f7da97fdfa41d5870ec8bb2) competition.

Please remember to note your Kaggle competition nickname in this notebook. We will use your ranking to grade.

You have 10 submission quota each day to submit your result and get your multi-class classification accuracy and ranking.
----------------------
# Evaluation

We calculate the multi-class classification accuracy with ground-turth hand-crafted labels in both images by

$$\text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{# of correctly labeled pixels}}{\text{total # of pixels}}$$
