10905,9-grade-kaggle-case-competiton,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2025 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** Your goal is to predict how many calories were burned during a workout.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error**.

The **RMSLE** is calculated as:
$$
\textrm{RMSLE} =   \left( \frac{1}{n} \sum\_{i=1}^n \left(\log (1 + \widehat{y}\_i) - \log (1 + y\_i)\right) \right)^{\frac{1}{2}}
$$
where:
- \\(n\\) is the total number of observations in the test set,
- \\(\widehat{y}\_i\\) is the predicted value of the target for instance \(i\),
- \\(y\_i\\) is the actual value of the target for instance \(i\), and,
- \\(\log\\) is the natural logarithm.

<script type=""text/x-mathjax-config"">
MathJax.Hub.Register.StartupHook(""End Jax"",function () {
  var BROWSER = MathJax.Hub.Browser;
  var jax = ""SVG"";
  return MathJax.Hub.setRenderer(jax);
});
</script>

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict the continuous target, `Calories`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Calories
    750000,93.2
    750001,27.42
    750002,103.8
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - ${competition.DateEnabled}
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  ${competition.Deadline}

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!",,False,True
10906,ai-emergency-medicine-turkiye,"# Overview

AI in Emergency Medicine - Turkiye (AI-EMT) is a community competition organized by Informatiqs. The goal is to predict two clinically critical outcomes for emergency department encounters across Turkiye using a large-scale tabular clinical dataset of 1,000,000 records spanning 2019 through 2024.

**Primary Target:** adverse_outcome - composite of ICU admission or in-hospital death

**Secondary Target:** readmission_30d - unplanned ED re-presentation within 30 calendar days

**Evaluation Metric:** Macro-Averaged AUC-ROC across both targets

**Dataset Size:** 1,000,000 encounters | 135 features | 25 cities across all NUTS-2 regions of Turkiye

---

## Competition Organizers

This competition is organized by the **Informatiqs** community.

| Role | Name |
|------|------|
| Community President | Kutluk Atalay |
| Community Vice President | Ozan Mohurcu |
| Lead Competition Organizer | Olaf Yunus Laitinen Imanov |

---

## Timeline

| Milestone | Date |
|-----------|------|
| Competition Start | Set via launch checklist |
| Team Merger Deadline | 5 days before close |
| Final Submission Deadline | See competition header |
| Private Leaderboard Release | Up to 7 days after close |

---

## Quick Start

Download the data, run the official starter notebook `setup_solution.ipynb`, and submit `submission.csv`. The starter notebook provides a working LightGBM baseline with 5-fold cross-validation achieving a macro AUC of approximately 0.85 on the training set.
----------------------
# Description

## Background

Emergency departments in Turkiye process tens of millions of patient encounters annually across a geographically and demographically diverse national healthcare network. The system spans university hospitals, state hospitals, city hospitals, training and research hospitals, and private institutions distributed across 25 metropolitan and provincial centers covering all NUTS-2 statistical regions, from Istanbul and Izmir in the west to Diyarbakir, Erzurum, and Van in the east.

Early identification of patients at elevated risk of critical deterioration at the moment of ED presentation is one of the most clinically impactful unsolved problems in applied health informatics. Validated clinical scoring systems such as MEWS, NEWS2, and SOFA provide structured physiological summaries but are designed as threshold tools rather than probabilistic classifiers. Machine learning approaches offer the ability to integrate heterogeneous data types, model nonlinear interactions, and incorporate institutional and temporal context that rule-based scores ignore.

---

## Dataset

The dataset comprises **1,000,000 emergency department encounters** recorded between January 2019 and December 2024. Each encounter is characterized by **135 features** spanning:

- Patient demographics and anthropometrics
- Vital signs at initial triage assessment
- 29 laboratory values with clinically realistic missing data patterns
- 18 binary comorbidity indicators and computed Charlson Comorbidity Index
- 10 binary medication class indicators
- 14 binary ED procedure and intervention indicators
- Institutional features: hospital type, annual volume, bed category
- Geographic features: city, NUTS-2 region, urban/rural classification
- Temporal features: year, hour, season, night shift, weekend, COVID-19 period flag
- Clinical severity scores: MEWS, NEWS2, SOFA approximations
- Socioeconomic features: insurance type, SES index
- COVID-19 specific features: tested, positive, vaccinated status

The dataset is anchored in the clinical and epidemiological characteristics specific to Turkiye, incorporating SGK (Social Security Institution) insurance coding, nationally standardized Manchester Triage System levels, and regional hospital infrastructure variation across all NUTS-2 regions.

---

## Missing Data

Seven laboratory columns contain informative missingness (Missing Not At Random, MNAR) at rates of 25% to 42%. These tests are selectively ordered for higher-acuity patients, meaning the missingness indicator itself carries predictive signal. Participants are encouraged to engineer binary missingness indicator features prior to imputation.

| Column | Approximate Missing Rate |
|--------|--------------------------|
| troponin\_i\_ngml | 35% |
| bnp\_pgl | 40% |
| d\_dimer\_mgfl | 42% |
| lactate\_mmoll | 25% |
| lipase\_ul | 38% |
| procalcitonin\_ngl | 30% |
| albumin\_gdl | 28% |

---

## Target Construction

The `adverse_outcome` target was generated via a logistic regression model encoding established clinical risk relationships. The strongest predictors include triage level 1 (log-odds +1.2), GCS below 9 (+0.8), lactate above 4 mmol/L (+0.6), SBP below 90 mmHg (+0.5), and age as a continuous predictor (+0.05 per year above 40).

---

## Feature Engineering Directions

The following engineered features are demonstrated in the starter notebook and are provided as starting points:

$$\text{Shock Index} = \frac{\text{Heart Rate (bpm)}}{\text{Systolic BP (mmHg)}}$$

$$\text{Modified Shock Index} = \frac{\text{Heart Rate (bpm)}}{\text{MAP (mmHg)}}$$

$$\text{Anion Gap} = \text{Na}^+ - (\text{Cl}^- + \text{HCO}_3^-)$$

$$\text{eGFR} \approx \frac{120}{\text{Creatinine} + 0.1}$$

Additional high-value interaction terms include `age x charlson_comorbidity_index`, `sofa_score_approx x triage_level`, and `is_night_shift x is_weekend`.

---

## Dataset Construction Methodology

The dataset was generated using a mechanistic simulation framework (seed = 42) calibrated against published epidemiological parameters. City-level sampling is proportional to population across 25 cities. Hospital type and infrastructure features vary systematically by city size and region. The COVID-19 period (2020 to 2021) introduces structured distribution shift in testing patterns, presentation characteristics, and disposition rates. 

---

## Files

| File | Rows | Columns | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| train.csv | 700,000 | 135 | Training encounters with both target variables |
| test.csv | 200,000 | 132 | Test encounters with targets withheld |
| sample\_submission.csv | 200,000 | 3 | Submission format template |
| supplemental\_data.csv | 100,000 | 135 | Additional labeled data for training or validation |
| hospital\_reference.csv | 25 | 13 | Hospital infrastructure metadata by city |
| icd10\_reference.csv | 21 | 7 | ICD-10 diagnostic code reference table |
| setup\_solution.ipynb | ¡ª | ¡ª | Official starter notebook with baseline models |
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated using **Macro-Averaged Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC-ROC)**, computed independently for each binary target and averaged with equal weight.

$$\text{Score} = \frac{\text{AUC}(\hat{y}\_{\text{adverse\_outcome}},\ y\_{\text{adverse\_outcome}}) + \text{AUC}(\hat{y}\_{\text{readmission\_30d}},\ y\_{\text{readmission\_30d}})}{2}$$

where \(\hat{y}\) denotes predicted probabilities in \([0, 1]\) and \(y\) denotes the binary ground truth labels. AUC is computed using the trapezoidal rule as implemented in `sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score`.

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| Range | 0.0 to 1.0 |
| Direction | Higher is better |
| Random classifier baseline | 0.5000 |
| Perfect classifier | 1.0000 |

---

## Baseline Reference Scores

| Model | AUC adverse\_outcome | AUC readmission\_30d | Macro AUC |
|-------|--------------------|--------------------|-----------|
| Constant prediction | 0.5000 | 0.5000 | 0.5000 |
| Logistic Regression (vital signs only) | 0.8251 | 0.6842 | 0.7547 |
| LightGBM (all features, 5-fold CV) | 0.9123 | 0.7891 | 0.8507 |

Scores are computed via 5-fold stratified cross-validation on `train.csv` as reported in `setup_solution.ipynb`.

---

## Submission File

For each `encounter_id` in the test set, you must predict a probability for `adverse_outcome` and a probability for `readmission_30d`. Both values must be continuous floats in \([0, 1]\). The file must contain a header and have the following format:
```apache
encounter_id,adverse_outcome,readmission_30d
TR-ED-00700001,0.34218,0.09841
TR-ED-00700002,0.72193,0.23407
TR-ED-00700003,0.08552,0.05123
```

The submission file must contain exactly 200,000 rows. All `encounter_id` values must match the test set exactly with no duplicates or omissions. NaN, Inf, or out-of-range values will cause submission rejection.

---

## Leaderboard

The public leaderboard is evaluated on a randomly selected **50% of the test encounters** and is updated after each submission. The private leaderboard is evaluated on the remaining **50%** and is revealed after the Final Submission Deadline. Participants must select up to **2 Final Submissions** for private leaderboard scoring. If no selection is made, the system automatically selects the 2 submissions with the highest public leaderboard scores.

---

## Evaluation Code
```python
from sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score
import numpy as np

def competition_score(y_true_df, y_pred_df):
    targets = [""adverse_outcome"", ""readmission_30d""]
    merged = y_true_df.merge(y_pred_df, on=""encounter_id"", suffixes=(""_true"", ""_pred""))
    auc_scores = []
    for col in targets:
        auc = roc_auc_score(merged[f""{col}_true""], merged[f""{col}_pred""])
        auc_scores.append(auc)
        print(f""AUC {col}: {auc:.5f}"")
    macro_auc = np.mean(auc_scores)
    print(f""Macro AUC: {macro_auc:.5f}"")
    return macro_auc
```",,False,True
10907,stat-cse-416-26w-assignment-5,"# Overview

Will a student complete their online course? In this competition, you work solo or in teams of two to apply models from our course to answer that question.

  

The [edX](https://www.edx.org) higher education course platform offers online courses from MIT and Harvard, so you will analyze data from students who have enrolled in courses from those universities to build a model that predicts a student's completion in those courses.

  

Remember that any model produced in this competition is **not** necessarily evidence that some characteristics **cause** completion of the course since the data are observational. Nevertheless, the model you create will be able to make accurate predictions and lead to causal hypotheses.

  

**You will not be graded your ranking in this competition.** The Kaggle leaderboard is a fun way to compare your model against your classmates, but the rankings will not be used in grading. All that matters is the accuracy of your model in the end, not how it competes with everyone else's (more on grading in the Evaluation section below).
----------------------
# Description

# Building the Model

  

We provide you two data sets, but notice that the TEST set does not contain labels! This is designed to mimic what it's really like to deploy a model for use in the ""real world"" when you don't know performance of the model before launch. The competition results will be based on how your model performs on our labeled version of the test data.?

  

Kaggle is a good medium for rehearsing what it is like to deploy models in the real world since your final score comes from truly unseen examples. However, Kaggle will show you your accuracy on a portion of the test set when you submit early versions of your model. These are the scores you will see on the live ""leaderboard"" when you submit to give you a taste of how you might do. Remember that your final score will be updated against the unseen data, so take your leaderboard score with a grain of salt along the way -- it will change at the end!

  

The following files contain the data:

edx_train.csv - The file containing the labeled training data

edx_test.csv - The file containing the **unlabeled** test data that will be used to evaluate your submission (the labeled version of this file will be used to judge your model for the final competition results).

  

  

## Data Description

  

The data includes a sample of students who enrolled in online courses created by MIT and Harvard, and each entry in the data records a student's interactions in the specific course they enrolled in along with their demographics. From these predictors, you will try to predict the target variable of whether a student completed their course (Yes/No). In other words, you will predict the target column certified that indicates if a student received certification for their course.

  

Read through the full description of the data in `edX_column_description.pdf` below.

  

The userId_DI field is necessary for evaluating your results but is likely not useful for your model in any way. You are encouraged to exclude `userId_DI` as an input feature, but you **must** keep the `userId_DI` field so that you can properly identify the examples for evaluation.

  

Some categorical values can have the value `unk` or `nan` for unknown. In the numerical categories, like `YoB` which represents Year of Birth, `-1` is filled in for unknown values. Unknown values (missing data) will be present in the test set as well so your model will need to be able to handle them.

  

  

  

## Development Environment?

Do not use edStem for this assignment. We would like you to gain experience in different environments because platforms will likely evolve throughout your career, so go explore!

  

Instead, here are some recommended options for this assignment:

  

**Kaggle Notebook.** The starter notebook shows you the files available in the Kaggle system. For example, to refer to the training data you could use the file path /kaggle/input/csestat-416-23sp-assignment-5/edx_test.csv.

  

**Colab Notebook.** If you're working with Colab Jupyter notebooks, you can use the starter code linked from the overview page to download the files to your Colab instance.

  

save_file('https://courses.cs.washington.edu/courses/cse416/23sp/homeworks/hw5/edx_train.csv', 'edx_train.csv') save_file('https://courses.cs.washington.edu/courses/cse416/23sp/homeworks/hw5/edx_test.csv', 'edx_test.csv')

You can then refer to these files directly as edx_train.csv and edx_test.csv.

  

**Local Notebook.** If you set this up, you will need to download the data from Kaggle and then refer to the files in your code as you downloaded them on your computer.

  

  

  

  

  

## Starter Notebook

  

Find a starter notebook here: https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1_VcpxCBmOzqrHcqdCoKNuovcYqUh7F75?usp=sharing

  

We recommend getting used to a coding environment outside of EdStem for this environment. We recommend three possible options:

  

Use Google Collaboratory (or Colab for short). Colab is Google's ""Google Docs for Jupyter Notebooks"". It works quite well and is very popular. You can use this starter notebook here.

Use a ""kernel"" on Kaggle (click the Code tab above). This will be another Jupyter Notebook view that is built by the Kaggle team. This is another great option since it's integrated in Kaggle, but make sure you don't share your notebook publicly as that would be a violation of our academic honesty policy.

Download Python and set up Jupyter Notebook on your computer locally. We recommend installing Python with Anaconda and launching Jupyter Notebooks from there.

  

There is minimal starter code for this assignment here. Note that you will need to write everything based on what we have done in previous assignments. It is recommended to work off of this starter notebook as it has section headings and instructions for all necessary components of the programming report.

  

  

## Usernames

  

When you make a submission on Kaggle, by default it will use your username on the leaderboard. If you would like to use something else, you may change your ""team name"" in the Team tab which will change what shows up in the leaderboard.

  

Regardless if you change the name or not, you must include your username and/or team name in programming assignment 5 on Gradescope so we can identify which submission is yours. User/team names must not be offensive and should be appropriate. If we are not able to find which submission on Kaggle is yours, you will not receive credit for what you submit on Kaggle.

  

## Group Work

  

You will be allowed to form teams for this assignment! This assignment should be doable alone, but we thought this was a good opportunity to let you explore working on machine learning in a team if that interests you. We recommend grouping up with your peers since you will learn a lot more from the experience!

  

You can form teams of up to 2 students. Kaggle lets you make submissions as a team and Gradescope allows you to turn in your code/report as a group as well. See Gradescope for more.

  

When working with a group, you can fully collaborate on the programming portion (the report and the Kaggle competition), but you should do the concept portions individually like normal.

  

## Discussion and Asking Questions

  

Please do not use the Discussion tab on Kaggle, but instead, use EdStem so there is only one place to look. If you use Kaggle's Kernels to develop your code, please make sure they are private so that you are not in violation of our academic misconduct policy.

  

  

  

## What to Submit

  

For this assignment, you will use Kaggle to turn in your model predictions, but everything else (Notebook, programming, concept questions) should be submitted on Gradescope. **Do not submit code on EdStem.**

  

  

Submit the following:

