10908,kbtu-business-school-ai-engagement-challenge,"# Overview

# KBTU Business School: AI-Driven Client Engagement Challenge

## Welcome to the Prospective Client Engagement Forecasting Challenge

This competition is part of your **midterm assessment**. You are tasked with building a robust binary classification model to predict whether a prospective client will enrol in a structured long-term financial advisory programme, based on a variety of campaign engagement, financial standing, and demographic indicators.

In modern financial services, identifying clients most likely to commit to an advisory product allows firms to allocate outreach resources efficiently and improve campaign returns. Using a dataset of around **1 ,000,000 records** drawn from multiple outreach cycles, you will predict whether a client's `advisory_enrollment` outcome is `conversion` or `no_conversion`.

### The Goal
Your objective is to predict `advisory_enrollment` for each record in the test set based on client profile and outreach campaign data. This is a **supervised binary classification problem**.

### The Competition submissions deadline is on 03/09/2026 11:59 PM
----------------------
# Description

### Competition Context

The dataset reflects a comprehensive client outreach assessment conducted across multiple outreach cycles by a financial advisory firm. It captures features spanning client demographics (occupation tier, credential level, household status), financial standing (account equity, liability profile, prior delinquency), campaign mechanics (touchpoint count, outreach channel, contact timing), prior engagement history (days since prior contact, prior outcome).

### Task Description

Participants must develop a machine learning pipeline that includes:

- **Data Preprocessing:** Handling mixed-type categorical features (tier/band/cohort/channel labels), missing values, and the sentinel value `999` in `days_since_prior`.
- **Feature Engineering:** Identifying which indicators (e.g. `engagement_efficiency` vs. `liability_index`, or `prior_outcome` vs. `touchpoint_count`) could be most predictive of advisory enrolment.
- **Model Selection:** Training and tuning classifiers (e.g. Logistic Regression, Random Forests, Gradient Boosted Trees ¡ª XGBoost / LightGBM, or MLP Neural Networks, or any model types of your choice)
- **Evaluation:** Optimising for the F1-Score to maximise detection of the minority `conversion` class.

> ?? **Data Leakage Warning:** `engagement_seconds` encodes interaction duration and would not be available before a contact is made. You might choose to train and evaluate models **with and without** this feature and evaluate the difference in the performance of any of your models.

### Class Distribution

The dataset is imbalanced, reflecting the real-world reality of outreach campaign conversion rates:

| Class | Share |
|---|---|
| `no_conversion` | ~88.5% |
| `conversion` | ~11.5% |

Your model must be able to identify converting clients without being overwhelmed by the dominant `no_conversion` class.

---
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Primary Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is the **F1-Score (Binary)**. The positive label for this calculation is `conversion`.

The F1-Score is the harmonic mean of Precision and Recall, defined as:

**F1 = 2 ¡Á (Precision ¡Á Recall) / (Precision + Recall)**

We have chosen this metric because the classes are imbalanced ¡ª a high accuracy could be achieved by simply predicting `no_conversion` for every record, but that would fail entirely to identify the converting clients the firm is looking for.

### Secondary Metrics

While the leaderboard is ranked by F1-Score, we will also monitor:

- **Recall:** To measure what proportion of actual converting clients your model correctly identifies.
- **Precision:** To understand how many of your predicted conversions are correct.
- **Accuracy:** To illustrate the effect of class imbalance on naive models.
- **AUC:** To be able to rank the prospective clients with respect to the potential selling opportunities.

### Submission Format

You must submit a CSV file with the correct number of rows and a header. The file should contain two columns: `ID` and `advisory_enrollment`.

```
ID,advisory_enrollment
1,conversion
2,no_conversion
3,no_conversion
...
```","# Dataset Description

## File Descriptions

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `train.csv` | Training set (~500,000 rows). Includes the target `advisory_enrollment` and all features. |
| `test.csv` | Test set. You must predict `advisory_enrollment` for each row. |
| `sample_submission.csv` | A template showing the correct submission format. |

---

## Data Fields

| Column | Description |
|---|---|
| `ID` | Unique record identifier |
| `advisory_enrollment` | **Target.** Whether the client enrolled in the structured long-term advisory programme (`conversion` or `no_conversion`) |
| `client_age` | Age of the client in years at the time of contact (18+) |
| `occupation_tier` | Employment classification grouped into firm-defined tiers based on occupational income band and sector (`tier_A` ¡­ `tier_K`, `tier_U` for unclassified) |
| `household_status` | Household composition cohort used as a proxy for financial obligations and discretionary income (`cohort_M`, `cohort_S`, `cohort_D`) |
| `credential_level` | Highest formal qualification mapped to an ordinal band reflecting expected financial literacy (`band_0` none ¡­ `band_3` advanced) |
| `prior_delinquency` | Whether the client has a recorded payment default history with the firm (`clear`, `flagged`) |
| `account_equity` | Composite net account position index over the preceding 12 months; negative values indicate a net liability position |
| `property_liability` | Whether the client holds an active secured lending product with the firm (`active`, `inactive`) |
| `personal_liability` | Whether the client holds an active unsecured personal credit facility with the firm (`burdened`, `unburdened`) |
| `outreach_channel` | Communication channel used for this interaction (`channel_C`, `channel_T`, `channel_U` ¡ª not recorded) |
| `contact_day` | Calendar day of the month on which contact was made (1¨C31) |
| `contact_month` | Campaign reporting period (`P01` January ¡­ `P12` December) |
| `engagement_seconds` | ?? Normalised interaction duration score ¡ª higher values indicate a more substantive interaction. See leakage warning above. |
| `touchpoint_count` | Total number of outreach contacts made to this client during the current campaign wave |
| `days_since_prior` | Days elapsed since the client was last contacted in a previous outreach cycle. **999 = no prior engagement history** |
| `prior_contacts` | Total contacts made to this client across all previous outreach cycles (0 = no prior history) |
| `prior_outcome` | Result of the client's most recent prior engagement (`outcome_win`, `outcome_loss`, `outcome_other`, `outcome_pending`) |
| `engagement_efficiency` | **Engineered.** `engagement_seconds / (touchpoint_count + 1)` ¡ª proxy for lead quality and client receptiveness |
| `liability_index` | **Engineered.** Combined active liability burden (`index_low`, `index_mid`, `index_high`) |

---

## Technical Notes

1. **Sentinel value:** `days_since_prior = 999` indicates no prior engagement contact ¡ª it is not a numeric outlier. Consider encoding it as a separate binary flag.
2. **Unknown categories** are encoded as explicit labels (`tier_U`, `band_0`, `channel_U`, `outcome_pending`). Treat these as distinct valid categories rather than missing data.
3. **Missing values** exist in this dataset.",False,True
10909,ml-26-prediciendo-precios-de-casas,"# Description

<h3>Start here if...</h3>
<p>You have some experience&nbsp;with R or Python and&nbsp;machine learning basics. This is a perfect competition for data science students&nbsp;who have completed an online course in machine learning and are looking to expand their skill set before trying a featured competition.&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Competition Description</h3>
<p><img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/kaggle/5407/media/housesbanner.png""></p>
<p>Ask a home buyer to describe their dream house, and they probably won't begin with the height of the basement ceiling or the proximity to an east-west railroad. But this playground competition's dataset proves that much more influences price negotiations than the number of bedrooms or a white-picket fence.</p>
<p>With 79 explanatory variables describing (almost) every aspect of residential homes in Ames, Iowa, this competition challenges you to predict the final price of each home.</p>
<h3>Practice Skills</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pre procesamiento de datos y feature engineering&nbsp;</li>
<li>Regresi¨®n con MLPs (optimizando los hiperpar¨¢metros)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Acknowledgments</h2>
<p>The <a href=""http://www.amstat.org/publications/jse/v19n3/decock.pdf"" target=""_blank"">Ames Housing&nbsp;dataset</a>&nbsp;was compiled&nbsp;by Dean De Cock for use in data science education. It's an incredible alternative&nbsp;for data scientists looking for a modernized and expanded version of the often cited Boston Housing dataset.&nbsp;</p>

<p>Photo by <a href=""https://unsplash.com/@tthfilms"">Tom Thain</a> on Unsplash.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h3>Goal</h3>
<p>It is your job to predict the sales price for each house.&nbsp;For each Id in the test set, you must predict the value of the SalePrice variable.&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Metric</h3>
<p>Submissions are evaluated on <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation"" target=""_blank"">Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE)</a>&nbsp;between the logarithm of the predicted value&nbsp;and the logarithm of the observed sales price. (Taking logs means that errors in predicting expensive houses and cheap houses will affect the result equally.)</p>
<h3>Submission File Format</h3>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,SalePrice<br>1461,169000.1<br>1462,187724.1233<br>1463,175221<br>etc.</pre>
<p>You can download an example submission file (sample_submission.csv) on the&nbsp;<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/house-prices-advanced-regression-techniques/data"" target=""_blank"">Data page.</a></p>
----------------------
